// C/P Practice Test flashcards: original stand-alone cards that teach what the Chem/Phys questions of AAMC Practice
// Exams 1, 3, 4, 5 and 6 need (the pe1-cp … pe6-cp cards of data/qb-aamc-fl.js). TODAY's C/P flashcard step uses only
// these cards (masteryFlashPool in js/mastery.js).
// Every card names, in `from`, the Practice Exam card(s) whose question needs it; `aamc` and `disc` come from that card.
// Original wording, numbers and compounds only: no AAMC passage, question, number or answer choice is reproduced.
// Format: {id, s: topic tag, disc, aamc, q, choices: [4], ans: 0-3, exp, from: [src, …], how: {ask, know, steps, trap}, teach}
// One card per method: a card lists in `from` every spec item (and so every question) it prepares for, across the
// exams; the whole deck stays at 250 cards or fewer (tools/test-cp-test-cards.cjs).
const CP_TEST_CARDS = [
  // ── Practice Exam 1 · Chem/Phys ──
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-03a","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe1-cp:3#1","pe1-cp:1#1","pe3-cp:46#1","pe6-cp:23#1"],"q":"A TLC baseline is 1.0 cm above the plate's lower edge. Measured from that edge, the solvent front is at 9.0 cm and a spot's center is at 4.2 cm. What is the retention factor (Rf)?","choices":["0.36","0.40","0.47","2.5"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"impossible","why":"Rf can never exceed 1: a spot can't travel farther than the solvent front."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"That's 4.2 ÷ 9.0, the raw readings; both distances must be measured from the baseline, not the plate edge."},{"c":0,"tag":"skipped","why":"The baseline was taken off the spot but not the front; subtract it from both readings."}],"rule":"Rf is between 0 and 1, and both distances are measured from the baseline. Estimate: about 3 ÷ 8.","exp":"**Rf = spot distance ÷ solvent-front distance, both from the baseline.** Spot: 4.2 − 1.0 = 3.2 cm. Front: 9.0 − 1.0 = 8.0 cm. Rf = 3.2 ÷ 8.0 = **0.40**.","how":{"ask":"Measure both distances from the baseline, then divide.","know":"**Rf = distance moved by the compound ÷ distance moved by the solvent front**, both measured from the **baseline** where the sample was spotted. Rf is between 0 and 1.","steps":[{"text":"The distances are given from the edge, but the sample started at the baseline.","hl":"baseline is 1.0 cm above the plate's lower edge","fig":"cpt-tlc-plate"},{"text":"Subtract the baseline from both readings.","calc":["spot: 4.2 − 1.0 = **3.2 cm**","front: 9.0 − 1.0 = **8.0 cm**"]},{"text":"Divide.","calc":["Rf = 3.2 ÷ 8.0 = **0.40**"]},{"text":"0.47 skips the baseline (4.2 ÷ 9.0), 0.36 subtracts it from only the spot (3.2 ÷ 9.0), and 2.5 is inverted (8.0 ÷ 3.2).","strike":["0.47","0.36","2.5"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.40."}],"trap":"Dividing the raw readings (4.2 ÷ 9.0 = 0.47): both distances must start at the baseline."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"1.0 cm","note":"where the sample started"},{"t":"9.0 cm","note":"solvent front, from the edge"},{"t":"4.2 cm","note":"spot, from the edge"}]},{"text":"**Start at the baseline.** The race starts where the sample was spotted, not at the plate's edge.","calc":["spot: 4.2 − 1.0 = 3.2 cm","front: 9.0 − 1.0 = 8.0 cm"]},{"text":"**Divide spot by front.**","calc":["Rf = 3.2 ÷ 8.0 = 0.40"]},{"text":"**Also:** on silica, the compound with the strongest polar groups (O–H or N–H first) has the smallest Rf."},{"text":"**Also:** a low Rf means strong binding to the stationary phase; a high Rf means the compound prefers the mobile phase."},{"text":"**Also:** only N–H, O–H and F–H donate hydrogen bonds; C=O and ether oxygens can only accept them."},{"text":"**Also:** in chromatography, Rf is characteristic of the compound under fixed conditions; spot size only reflects how much was applied."},{"text":"**Also:** every Rf lies between 0 and 1; a larger value means the ratio is inverted."},{"text":"**Also:** sugar-based column materials (polysaccharides such as dextran, agarose and cellulose) are covered in –OH groups, so they interact with proteins mainly by hydrogen bonding."},{"text":"**Also:** in a binding pocket, sort each ligand atom by what it carries. An oxygen with lone pairs but no H of its own (a ketone or amide C=O, a carboxylate –COO⁻, an ether O) can only receive an H-bond, for instance from arginine's N–H; an O–H or N–H on the ligand can also give one; a C–H does neither. In acetic acid, drawn here, the C=O oxygen is a pure acceptor and the O–H is a donor.","fig":"acetic-acid","calc":["C=O oxygen: lone pairs, no H → acceptor only","O–H: H on O plus lone pairs → donor and acceptor","C–H: no lone pair, no polar H → neither"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Rf = (spot − baseline) ÷ (front − baseline), and it always falls between 0 and 1."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-02a","s":"Research Design & Reasoning","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe1-cp:2#1"],"q":"A team concludes that amino acids arose on the primitive Earth, since passing sparks through methane and ammonia gave them in the lab. The argument requires that:","choices":["those gases were present then.","the yield exceeded half the methane.","the products were optically pure.","sparks speed up every reaction."],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Any yield, even a trace, shows amino acids can form; the amount isn't what the conclusion rests on."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Optical purity isn't needed: the claim is only that amino acids formed, in whatever mix of forms."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Too sweeping: the argument concerns these gases only, so a claim about every reaction isn't needed."}],"rule":"A required assumption bridges the lab result to the claim's real setting; details of the experiment's yield or purity don't.","exp":"A lab synthesis shows what **can** happen. To conclude it **did** happen on the early Earth, the **starting materials and conditions** (here methane, ammonia and electrical discharge) must have existed then. Yield, purity and a claim about all reactions do not bear on that.","how":{"ask":"Find the unstated premise that must be true for the lab result to support the historical claim.","know":"• **Assumption**: a premise the conclusion needs but does not state; if it were false, the conclusion would fail.\n• A lab model of a past event supports the claim only if its **inputs and conditions** existed at that time.","steps":[{"text":"The conclusion is about the early Earth; the evidence is from a lab.","hl":"amino acids arose on the primitive Earth"},{"text":"Test each choice: if it were false, would the argument fail?","calc":["gases absent then → the lab result says nothing about then → fails","low yield → some still formed → stands"]},{"text":"Yield, optical purity and all reactions are not needed for the conclusion.","strike":["the yield exceeded half the methane.","the products were optically pure.","sparks speed up every reaction."]},{"text":"Answer: the gases must have been present on the early Earth."}],"trap":"Choosing a detail about the experiment's quality; the gap is between the lab and the early Earth."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"on the primitive Earth","note":"the conclusion"},{"t":"in the lab","note":"the evidence"},{"t":"requires","note":"find the assumption"}]},{"text":"**Split conclusion from evidence.** The evidence is a lab result; the conclusion is about the past."},{"text":"**Bridge the gap.** For the lab to stand in for the early Earth, the same ingredients had to be there."},{"text":"**Negate to test.** If the gases were absent, the argument collapses, so that is the assumption.","calc":["negate the choice → does the conclusion fail?"]},{"text":"**Also:** an assumption is the unstated premise whose denial would break the conclusion."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a lab model supports a claim about the past only if its starting materials and conditions existed then."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-04e","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe1-cp:4#1","pe1-cp:4#3","pe1-cp:4#4","pe1-cp:4#2"],"q":"Which statement about the acid-catalyzed dehydration of 1-methylcyclopentanol, shown, is true?","fig":"cpt-1-methylcyclopentanol","choices":["One water is lost, via a carbocation","Two waters are lost in one step","The alkene geometry is set by the alcohol","The C–H bond breaks before the C–O bond"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"impossible","why":"The alcohol has one OH, so only one water can leave."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Order flipped: in E1 water leaves first (C–O breaks), and only then is the C–H proton removed."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"The flat carbocation forgets the alcohol's shape, so the alcohol can't set the alkene geometry."}],"rule":"Alcohol + acid + heat is E1: water leaves first, a flat carbocation forms, then H⁺ is lost.","exp":"The OH is protonated and leaves as **one water molecule**, giving a **planar carbocation**; then H⁺ is lost from a neighboring carbon. Because the flat cation forgets the alcohol's 3D arrangement, the reaction is **not stereospecific**, and the C–O bond breaks first (it is E1, not a concerted E2).","how":{"ask":"Judge each statement against the E1 mechanism: what leaves, in what order, through what intermediate.","know":"• **E1 dehydration**: ROH + H⁺ → ROH₂⁺ → **R⁺ + H₂O** (one water) → alkene + H⁺.\n• The C–O bond breaks **first**; the C–H bond breaks last.\n• The carbocation is planar, so the alcohol's geometry is lost: **not stereospecific**.","steps":[{"text":"Write the E1 steps for this tertiary alcohol.","hl":"acid-catalyzed dehydration","calc":["R–OH + H⁺ → R–OH₂⁺","R–OH₂⁺ → **R⁺ + H₂O**","R⁺ → alkene + H⁺"]},{"text":"Only one water molecule leaves, and the steps are separate, not one concerted step.","strike":["Two waters are lost in one step"]},{"text":"The planar cation does not remember the alcohol's arrangement, so the alcohol does not set the geometry.","strike":["The alkene geometry is set by the alcohol"]},{"text":"Water leaves before H⁺ is removed, so the C–O bond breaks first.","strike":["The C–H bond breaks before the C–O bond"]},{"text":"Answer: one water is lost, via a carbocation."}],"trap":"Treating dehydration like E2 (one step, stereospecific): with acid and heat, alcohols go through a carbocation."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"acid-catalyzed dehydration","note":"E1"},{"t":"1-methylcyclopentanol","note":"tertiary alcohol"}]},{"text":"**Count the waters.** One OH leaves as one H₂O."},{"text":"**Order the bonds.** C–O breaks to make C⁺; the C–H breaks after.","calc":["C–O first → C⁺ → C–H last"]},{"text":"**Flat cation, no memory.** The planar C⁺ can lose an H from either side, so the starting geometry does not carry over."},{"text":"**Also:** in E1, the C=C always forms between the cationic carbon and a carbon directly bonded to it."},{"text":"**Also:** a direct E1 alkene always has the former C–OH carbon in its C=C."},{"text":"**Also:** E1 through a planar carbocation gives both E and Z alkenes, with the more stable E usually major."},{"text":"**Also:** tertiary alcohols dehydrate most easily because they form the most stable carbocation; primary alcohols dehydrate slowest."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** acid-catalyzed dehydration loses one water through a carbocation, so it is stepwise and not stereospecific."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-05a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:5#1","pe3-cp:27#3"],"q":"The molecule drawn, CH₃CONHCH₂COOCH₂CH₃, has two carbonyl groups. Which functional group is the highlighted one, whose carbonyl carbon is also bonded to an O–CH₂CH₃?","fig":"cpt-fix-acgly-ester-hl","choices":["Ester","Amide","Ether","Carboxylic acid"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"An amide's carbonyl carbon is bonded to N; the highlighted carbonyl carbon is bonded to O."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"An ether is C–O–C with no C=O; the highlighted O sits right on a carbonyl carbon."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"A carboxylic acid ends in O–H; this oxygen carries an ethyl group instead of H."}],"rule":"Name a carbonyl by the atom on its carbon: N makes an amide, O–H an acid, O–C the alkoxy kind; no C=O at all means an ether.","exp":"A carbonyl carbon bonded to an **–O–R** group is an **ester**, C(=O)–O–C: here an ethyl ester. The other carbonyl in the molecule is bonded to N, so that one is the **amide**. An ether has C–O–C with no carbonyl, and a carboxylic acid has C(=O)–O**H**.","how":{"ask":"Look at what the highlighted carbonyl carbon is bonded to besides its C=O oxygen, then name the group.","know":"• Name a carbonyl group by its other neighbor on the C=O carbon.\n• C(=O)–**O–C** → **ester**; C(=O)–**N** → **amide**; C(=O)–**OH** → carboxylic acid; C(=O)–C on both sides → ketone.\n• **Ether**: C–O–C with no C=O on either carbon.\n• An ester forms from a carboxyl + an alcohol; an amide from a carboxyl + an amine.","steps":[{"text":"Find the highlighted carbonyl carbon and its other neighbor.","hl":"bonded to an O–CH₂CH₃","fig":"cpt-fix-acgly-ester-hl","calc":["highlighted: –C(=O)–O–CH₂CH₃","C=O carbon + O–R → **ester**"]},{"text":"The amide is the other carbonyl, CH₃C(=O)–NH–, whose carbonyl carbon is bonded to N; the highlighted one is bonded to O.","strike":["Amide"]},{"text":"An ether's oxygen has no carbonyl next to it, and an acid's oxygen carries H, not an ethyl group.","strike":["Ether","Carboxylic acid"]},{"text":"Answer: an ester."}],"trap":"Calling every carbonyl next to a heteroatom an amide. Look at the atom on the carbonyl carbon: N makes an amide, O–R makes an ester."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"two carbonyl groups","note":"name each by its neighbor"},{"t":"O–CH₂CH₃","note":"an OR group"},{"t":"CH₃CONH","note":"carbonyl on N: amide"}]},{"text":"**Name a carbonyl by its other neighbor.**","calc":["C(=O)–O–R → ester","C(=O)–N → amide (peptide bond)","C(=O)–OH → carboxylic acid","C–O–C, no C=O → ether"]},{"text":"**Read the molecule.** The left carbonyl sits on N; the right one, highlighted, sits on O–ethyl.","fig":"cpt-fix-acgly-ester-hl","calc":["CH₃C(=O)–NH– → amide","–C(=O)–O–CH₂CH₃ → ester"]},{"text":"**Also:** a peptide bond is an amide, C(=O)–N, made when a carboxyl group condenses with an amino group and releases water; carboxyl + alcohol gives an ester instead.","calc":["R–COOH + H₂N–R′ → R–CO–NH–R′ + H₂O","R–COOH + HO–R′ → R–CO–O–R′ + H₂O"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a carbonyl carbon bonded to O–R is an ester; bonded to N it is an amide (the peptide bond); C–O–C with no C=O is an ether."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-06a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:6#1"],"q":"Which statement gives the order of SN2 reaction rates for alkyl halides with the same leaving group?","choices":["Methyl fastest, tertiary slowest","Tertiary fastest, methyl slowest","Secondary fastest, methyl slowest","Primary fastest, tertiary slowest"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"That's the SN1 order, reversed; SN2 backside attack is blocked by bulky groups, so tertiary is slowest."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"SN2 rate drops with each alkyl group added, so secondary can't beat primary or methyl."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Methyl has no alkyl groups at all, so it beats primary in backside attack."}],"rule":"SN2 is backside attack, so less crowding is faster: count the alkyl groups on the carbon, and fewer wins.","exp":"SN2 needs **backside attack** on the carbon bearing the leaving group. Each added alkyl group crowds that side: **methyl > 1° > 2° ≫ 3°** (tertiary essentially does not react by SN2).","how":{"ask":"Rank substrates by how crowded the backside of the reacting carbon is.","know":"• **SN2**: one step, the nucleophile attacks opposite the leaving group.\n• Steric order: **methyl > primary > secondary ≫ tertiary**.\n• The reverse order (3° fastest) belongs to SN1, which goes through a carbocation.","steps":[{"text":"SN2 rate depends on access to the carbon's backside.","hl":"order of SN2 reaction rates","fig":"cpt-sn2-backside"},{"text":"Tertiary-first is the SN1 order; secondary-first fits neither.","strike":["Tertiary fastest, methyl slowest","Secondary fastest, methyl slowest"]},{"text":"Primary is fast but methyl, with no alkyl groups, is faster still.","strike":["Primary fastest, tertiary slowest"]},{"text":"Answer: methyl fastest, tertiary slowest."}],"trap":"Using carbocation stability (3° > 2° > 1°); that ranks SN1 rates, not SN2."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"SN2","note":"backside attack, one step"},{"t":"same leaving group","note":"only sterics differ"}]},{"text":"**Picture the attack.** The nucleophile must reach the carbon from behind the leaving group."},{"text":"**Count the blockers.** Each alkyl group on that carbon gets in the way.","calc":["CH₃–X: 0 → fastest","1°: 1","2°: 2","3°: 3 → no SN2"]},{"text":"**Also:** SN2 slows with crowding at the carbon being attacked (methyl > 1° > 2°, no 3°), not at the leaving group."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** SN2 rates run methyl > 1° > 2° ≫ 3°, the reverse of SN1."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-07b","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:7#2","pe1-cp:7#1","pe1-cp:7#3"],"q":"3,3-Dimethylbutan-2-ol, shown, reacts with HBr. Which product can form without a methyl shift in the carbocation?","fig":"cpt-33-dimethylbutan-2-ol","choices":["2-Bromo-3,3-dimethylbutane","2-Bromo-2,3-dimethylbutane","2,3-Dimethylbut-2-ene","2,3-Dimethylbut-1-ene"],"choiceFigs":["cpt-2-bromo-33-dimethylbutane","cpt-2-bromo-23-dimethylbutane","cpt-23-dimethylbut-2-ene","cpt-23-dimethylbut-1-ene"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Its 2,3-dimethyl skeleton means a methyl moved from C3 to C2; with no shift the 3,3-dimethyl skeleton stays."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Again 2,3-dimethyl: a methyl had to migrate from C3 to C2, so this needs the shift."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Also a 2,3-dimethyl skeleton, so a methyl must have moved; an unshifted product keeps both methyls on C3."}],"rule":"No shift means an unchanged carbon skeleton: compare each name's methyl positions with the starting alcohol's.","exp":"Loss of water from C2 leaves a **secondary cation on C2**. Br⁻ adding there gives **2-bromo-3,3-dimethylbutane** directly. C3 is quaternary (no H), so a **1,2-methyl shift** from C3 makes a 3° cation at C3; that cation gives 2-bromo-2,3-dimethylbutane, 2,3-dimethylbut-2-ene and 2,3-dimethylbut-1-ene.","how":{"ask":"Find the product whose new group sits on the carbon that held the OH, with the carbon skeleton unchanged.","know":"• C⁺ forms where the OH was; Br⁻ there = direct product.\n• A methyl shift moves C⁺ to the next carbon and rearranges the skeleton (the methyl changes carbons).\n• Products with Br on a carbon that never held the OH, a C=C that does not include that carbon, or a changed branching pattern come from the shifted cation.","steps":[{"text":"The OH is on C2; C3 carries two methyls and no H.","hl":"3,3-Dimethylbutan-2-ol"},{"text":"Direct path: Br⁻ adds to C2, keeping both methyls on C3.","calc":["CH₃–CH(Br)–C(CH₃)₂–CH₃ = **2-bromo-3,3-dimethylbutane**"]},{"text":"A 2,3-dimethyl skeleton means one methyl moved from C3 to C2.","strike":["2-Bromo-2,3-dimethylbutane","2,3-Dimethylbut-2-ene","2,3-Dimethylbut-1-ene"]},{"text":"Answer: 2-bromo-3,3-dimethylbutane."}],"trap":"Choosing an alkene because elimination seems \"direct\"; here every listed alkene has the 2,3-dimethyl skeleton of the shifted cation."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"3,3-Dimethylbutan-2-ol","note":"OH on C2, quaternary C3"},{"t":"HBr","note":"cation path"},{"t":"without a methyl shift","note":"direct product"}]},{"text":"**Place the cation.** C⁺ forms on C2, where the OH was."},{"text":"**Compare skeletons.** Before the shift: two methyls on C3. After: one on C2 and one on C3.","calc":["3,3-dimethyl → unshifted","2,3-dimethyl → shifted"]},{"text":"**Pick the unshifted one.** Br on C2 with the 3,3-dimethyl skeleton."},{"text":"**Also:** a carbocation shifts H or CH₃ from a neighboring carbon whenever that makes a more stable cation."},{"text":"**Also:** a new group on a carbon that never held the leaving group is the fingerprint of a carbocation rearrangement."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a product whose groups or branches moved to a carbon that never held the OH came from a rearranged cation."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-08d","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:8#3","pe1-cp:8#1","pe1-cp:8#2","pe4-cp:9#1","pe4-cp:9#2","pe4-cp:9#3"],"q":"A mixture of pent-1-ene, 1-chloropentane and pentan-1-ol, shown, is run on a nonpolar gas chromatography column. In what order do they reach the detector?","fig":["cpt-pent-1-ene","cpt-1-chloropentane","cpt-pentan-1-ol"],"choices":["Alkene, then chloride, then alcohol","Alcohol, then chloride, then alkene","Chloride, then alkene, then alcohol","Alkene, then alcohol, then chloride"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Backwards: the hydrogen-bonding alcohol has the highest bp, so it elutes last, not first."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"The alkene is less polar and more volatile than the chloride, so it elutes before it."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"The alcohol hydrogen-bonds, so it must come out last; this order puts it in the middle."}],"rule":"On a nonpolar GC column, the lowest boiling point elutes first; an O–H (hydrogen bonding) raises the boiling point most.","exp":"Lowest boiling point first. Pent-1-ene has only London forces (bp ≈ 30 °C). 1-Chloropentane adds a C–Cl dipole and more mass (bp ≈ 108 °C). Pentan-1-ol **hydrogen-bonds** (bp ≈ 138 °C), so it comes out **last**.","how":{"ask":"Rank the three by intermolecular forces, which sets their boiling points and so their GC order.","know":"• GC elutes in order of increasing boiling point.\n• Alkene: London only → most volatile.\n• Alkyl halide: dipole–dipole plus London → middle.\n• Alcohol: hydrogen bonding → least volatile, last.","steps":[{"text":"All three have five carbons, so the functional group decides.","hl":"pent-1-ene, 1-chloropentane and pentan-1-ol","calc":["pent-1-ene: London → bp ≈ 30 °C","1-chloropentane: dipole → bp ≈ 108 °C","pentan-1-ol: H-bonding → bp ≈ 138 °C"]},{"text":"The alcohol hydrogen-bonds, so it cannot come out first or second.","strike":["Alcohol, then chloride, then alkene","Alkene, then alcohol, then chloride"]},{"text":"The alkene is more volatile than the chloride, so it comes out before it.","strike":["Chloride, then alkene, then alcohol"]},{"text":"Answer: alkene, then chloride, then alcohol."}],"trap":"Ordering by molar mass alone: the alcohol is lighter than the chloride but comes out after it, because it hydrogen-bonds."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"nonpolar gas chromatography column","note":"order by boiling point"},{"t":"reach the detector","note":"elution order"}]},{"text":"**Name each force.** C=C: London only. C–Cl: a dipole. O–H: hydrogen bonding."},{"text":"**Weakest forces leave first.**","calc":["alkene < alkyl halide < alcohol"]},{"text":"**Also:** in GC, compounds come off in order of increasing boiling point."},{"text":"**Also:** with similar sizes, an alkane boils lowest and an alcohol highest, because hydrogen bonding is the strongest force."},{"text":"**Also:** an O–H or N–H group raises the boiling point far more than added mass does, because of hydrogen bonding."},{"text":"**Also:** fractional distillation separates liquids by the same property, boiling point. An alcohol hydrogen-bonds, so it boils higher than an alkyl halide or ether of similar mass, and the lower-boiling liquid distills over first.","calc":["dipropyl ether or 1-chlorohexane vs hexan-1-ol","ether / chloride: lower bp → collects first","alcohol: H-bonding, higher bp → stays behind"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in GC, an alkene beats an alkyl halide of the same chain, and an alcohol, which hydrogen-bonds, comes out last."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-09b","s":"Stereochemistry","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:9#1","pe1-cp:9#2","pe1-cp:9#3"],"q":"(R)-2-Chlorobutane reacts by SN2 with sodium methanethiolate, CH₃SNa. What configuration does the product have?","choices":["(S) only","(R) only","Racemic mixture","Meso compound"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"SN2 inverts the center; with unchanged priorities the label must flip, so R can't survive."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Racemizing comes from SN1's planar cation; SN2 is one-step backside attack, giving a single product."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Meso needs two or more stereocenters; this product has only one."}],"rule":"SN2 gives one product with inverted configuration; a racemic mix needs SN1, and meso needs two stereocenters.","exp":"SN2 gives **inversion**. The –SCH₃ group (first atom S, 16) now ranks **first**, just as Cl did, and the order of the others (ethyl > methyl > H) is unchanged. Inverting the 3D arrangement with the same priority order flips the label: **R → S**.","how":{"ask":"Apply inversion, then check whether the priorities changed.","know":"• **SN2 → inversion** of the 3D arrangement; no carbocation, so no racemization.\n• **CIP priority** by atomic number of the first atom: S (16) and Cl (17) both outrank C (6) and H (1).\n• If the new group has the same rank as the old one, inversion switches R ↔ S.","steps":[{"text":"The reaction is SN2, so the configuration inverts.","hl":"reacts by SN2","fig":"cpt-sn2-backside"},{"text":"Priorities before and after.","calc":["before: Cl > ethyl > methyl > H","after: **SCH₃** > ethyl > methyl > H"]},{"text":"Same order, inverted geometry, so the label changes; SN2 does not racemize, and the product has one stereocenter.","strike":["(R) only","Racemic mixture","Meso compound"]},{"text":"Answer: (S) only."}],"trap":"Keeping the label R: inversion with unchanged priorities always switches the descriptor."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"(R)-2-Chlorobutane","note":"starting label"},{"t":"SN2","note":"inversion"},{"t":"CH₃SNa","note":"S nucleophile"}]},{"text":"**Flip the umbrella.** The nucleophile enters opposite Cl; the other three groups turn inside out."},{"text":"**Re-rank.** S still outranks every carbon, just as Cl did.","calc":["rank order unchanged → R becomes S"]},{"text":"**Also:** CIP priority is set by the first atom's atomic number; go farther out only to break a tie."},{"text":"**Also:** after a substitution, the product keeps the substrate's carbon chain and the new group's position; only the group's name changes."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** SN2 inverts the center; if the entering group takes the leaving group's priority rank, R and S swap."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-10a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe1-cp:10#1","pe1-cp:10#2","pe1-cp:10#3","pe3-cp:10#1","pe3-cp:10#2"],"q":"Ethanol whose oxygen is ¹⁸O is esterified with acetic acid under acid catalysis. Where does the ¹⁸O end up?","fig":["cpt-ethanol","cpt-acetic-acid"],"choices":["In the ester","In the water","In both products","In neither product"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"impossible","why":"There is only one ¹⁸O per ethanol, and one atom can't be split between two products."},{"c":3,"tag":"impossible","why":"Atoms aren't destroyed: the labeled O must end up in some product."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"The water forms from the acid's –OH plus H⁺, so it carries the acid's ordinary oxygen."}],"rule":"In acid-catalyzed esterification the acid loses its OH; the alcohol's O–C bond never breaks.","exp":"In Fischer esterification the **alcohol's oxygen is the nucleophile**: it attacks the protonated carbonyl carbon and stays bonded to it, becoming the ester's bridging –O–. The water that leaves takes an oxygen from the **acid**. So the ¹⁸O ends up **in the ester**.","how":{"ask":"Find which oxygen attacks and which one leaves, then follow the labeled one.","know":"• **Fischer esterification**: RCOOH + R′OH ⇌ RCOOR′ + H₂O (acid catalyst).\n• The **alcohol O attacks** the carbonyl carbon and is kept; **water leaves from the acid** side.\n• So the ester's C–O–R′ oxygen comes from the alcohol, and the water's O from the acid.","steps":[{"text":"The labeled O is ethanol's, the nucleophile.","hl":"oxygen is ¹⁸O","calc":["CH₃CH₂–¹⁸OH attacks the C=O carbon"]},{"text":"The ethyl–¹⁸O bond never breaks, and one ¹⁸O can't be in two places or vanish.","strike":["In both products","In neither product"]},{"text":"The water forms from the acid's –OH plus H⁺, so it carries the acid's ordinary oxygen.","strike":["In the water"]},{"text":"Answer: in the ester, as its bridging oxygen."}],"trap":"Thinking the alcohol loses its OH as water, as in an SN1 reaction: in esterification the acid loses the OH."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"esterified with acetic acid","note":"Fischer"},{"t":"oxygen is ¹⁸O","note":"the nucleophile's O"}]},{"text":"**Activate.** H⁺ protonates the acid's C=O, making the carbon more electrophilic."},{"text":"**Attack and leave.**","calc":["EtO–H attacks C → tetrahedral intermediate","acid's –OH → –OH₂⁺ → leaves as H₂O"]},{"text":"**Also:** in Fischer esterification the ester's bridging oxygen comes from the alcohol, its C=O oxygen from the acid, and the water from the acid."},{"text":"**Also:** an H on O or N exchanges rapidly with solvent water (faster with acid or base), so it cannot keep a label, so it cannot show which bond broke."},{"text":"**Also:** an H bonded to carbon, such as an aromatic ring C–H or a CH₃ hydrogen, is far too weakly acidic to swap with solvent protons, so a ³H or D placed there stays put even after hours in water. Only H on O, N or S trades places with the solvent.","calc":["O–H, N–H, S–H: swap with H₂O → label lost","C–H (aromatic or alkyl): no swap → label kept"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in Fischer esterification the alcohol keeps its oxygen as the ester's –O–, and the acid loses an oxygen as water; follow a label by asking which O attacks and which leaves."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-11a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:11#1","pe1-cp:11#2","pe1-cp:11#3","pe1-cp:45#1","pe1-cp:45#2"],"q":"A camera lens forms a sharp image of a flower 5.0 cm behind the lens when the flower is 20 cm in front. What is the focal length in metres?","choices":["0.040 m","4.0 m","0.067 m","25 m"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"units","why":"The sum done in centimetres gives 4.0 cm; labeling that metres makes it a hundred times too big."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Sign flipped: it subtracts the reciprocals; for a real image both 1/d terms add."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"Forgot to flip: 25 is 1/f in m⁻¹; the focal length is the reciprocal of that."}],"rule":"Use 1/f = 1/do + 1/di in metres, then flip; a real image means f is shorter than both distances.","exp":"Convert first: o = 0.20 m and i = 0.050 m. The **thin-lens equation** adds the reciprocals: 1/f = 5 m⁻¹ + 20 m⁻¹ = 25 m⁻¹, so **f = 0.040 m** (4.0 cm).","how":{"ask":"Use the thin-lens equation with both distances converted to metres, then invert to get f.","know":"• **Thin-lens equation: 1/f = 1/o + 1/i** (o = object distance, i = image distance, f = focal length).\n• Work in **metres**: 1 cm = 0.01 m.\n• The equation gives 1/f; flip the result to get f.","steps":[{"text":"Pick out the two distances and convert them to metres.","hl":"5.0 cm behind the lens","calc":["i = 5.0 cm = **0.050 m**","o = 20 cm = **0.20 m**"]},{"text":"Add the reciprocals.","hl":"20 cm in front","calc":["1/f = 1/o + 1/i","1/f = 1/0.20 m + 1/0.050 m","1/f = 5 m⁻¹ + 20 m⁻¹ = **25 m⁻¹**"]},{"text":"Invert to get the focal length.","calc":["f = 1 ÷ 25 m⁻¹ = **0.040 m**"]},{"text":"25 m forgets to invert 1/f. 0.067 m subtracts the reciprocals (20 − 5 = 15 m⁻¹). 4.0 m does the arithmetic in centimetres (f = 4.0 cm) and then labels it metres.","strike":["25 m","0.067 m","4.0 m"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.040 m."}],"trap":"Doing the sum in centimetres gives f = 4.0 cm; that is right in cm, but 4.0 m is a hundred times too large. Convert to metres before you start."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it measures.","marks":[{"t":"5.0 cm behind the lens","note":"image distance i"},{"t":"20 cm in front","note":"object distance o"},{"t":"in metres","note":"unit of the answer"}]},{"text":"**The thin-lens equation** links the three distances, each measured from the lens.","calc":["1/f = 1/o + 1/i"]},{"text":"**Convert to metres first,** so 1/f comes out in m⁻¹.","calc":["o = 0.20 m → 1/o = 5 m⁻¹","i = 0.050 m → 1/i = 20 m⁻¹"]},{"text":"**Add, then flip.**","calc":["1/f = 25 m⁻¹","f = 0.040 m"]},{"text":"**Also:** power in diopters = 1 ÷ (focal length in metres); converging lenses are +, diverging lenses are −."},{"text":"**Also:** in any eye problem, the image distance is the lens-to-retina distance; the object distance is lens-to-object."},{"text":"**Also:** solve 1/f = 1/o + 1/i for i, then m = −i/o: |m| scales the height and a negative m means inverted."},{"text":"**Also:** for an object beyond 2f, find i from 1/f = 1/o + 1/i and use m = −i/o: the real image is inverted and smaller, shrinking as the object moves away."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** convert o and i to metres, add their reciprocals to get 1/f, and remember to invert at the end if the question asks for f."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-12a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:12#1","pe1-cp:12#3","pe3-cp:14#1","pe3-cp:14#2"],"q":"A protease binds the tetrahedral intermediate of peptide hydrolysis much more tightly than it binds the intact peptide. How does this binding speed the reaction?","choices":["Lowers activation energy","Makes the reaction exergonic","Shifts equilibrium to products","Raises the temperature"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"An enzyme leaves the start and end energies alone, so ΔG, and whether it's exergonic, can't change."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Catalysts never move an equilibrium; they speed both directions equally."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"An enzyme doesn't heat its surroundings; it works at the ambient temperature."}],"rule":"A catalyst changes only how fast (Ea), never ΔG, K or where equilibrium lies.","exp":"The tetrahedral intermediate resembles the **transition state**. Binding it tightly **stabilizes the transition state**, so the top of the energy hill drops and the **activation energy (Ea)** falls. Reactant and product energies are untouched, so ΔG and the equilibrium stay the same, and an enzyme does not heat the solution.","how":{"ask":"Decide which part of the energy diagram the enzyme changes when it grips the transition-state-like species.","know":"• **Ea = transition-state energy − reactant energy**; a lower Ea means a faster reaction.\n• An enzyme binds the **transition state** more tightly than the substrate, lowering the peak of the curve.\n• **ΔH (or ΔG) = products − reactants**; Ea = transition state − reactants. The enzyme does not move either end, so ΔG and K are unchanged.","steps":[{"text":"The enzyme grips the species that looks like the transition state, not the starting peptide.","hl":"binds the tetrahedral intermediate of peptide hydrolysis much more tightly than it binds the intact peptide"},{"text":"Stabilizing the transition state lowers the peak; read both barriers from the curves.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Peptide hydrolysis with and without the protease","x":{"label":"Reaction progress","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Free energy (kJ/mol)","min":-40,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"No enzyme","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,0],[3,40],[5,90],[7,30],[9,-30],[10,-30]]},{"name":"With protease","marker":"square-open","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,0],[3,20],[5,50],[7,10],[9,-30],[10,-30]]}]},"calc":["Ea without enzyme = 90 − 0 = **90 kJ/mol**","Ea with protease = 50 − 0 = **50 kJ/mol**","ΔG = −30 − 0 = **−30 kJ/mol** on both curves"]},{"text":"Both curves start and end at the same heights, so ΔG and the position of equilibrium are unchanged.","strike":["Makes the reaction exergonic","Shifts equilibrium to products"]},{"text":"An enzyme works at the temperature of its surroundings; it does not heat them.","strike":["Raises the temperature"]},{"text":"Answer: it lowers the activation energy."}],"trap":"Thinking a faster reaction must also be a more favorable one: speed depends on Ea, favorability on ΔG, and an enzyme changes only Ea."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"tetrahedral intermediate","note":"looks like the transition state"},{"t":"much more tightly","note":"preferential binding"},{"t":"speed the reaction","note":"a rate question"}]},{"text":"**Read the energy diagram.** The left plateau is the reactants, the right plateau the products, the peak the transition state. The enzyme's curve has a lower peak but the same ends.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Same reaction, two paths","x":{"label":"Reaction progress","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Free energy (kJ/mol)","min":-40,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"No enzyme","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,0],[3,40],[5,90],[7,30],[9,-30],[10,-30]]},{"name":"With protease","marker":"square-open","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,0],[3,20],[5,50],[7,10],[9,-30],[10,-30]]}]}},{"text":"**Measure the hill.** Ea is peak minus start.","calc":["no enzyme: 90 − 0 = 90 kJ/mol","with protease: 50 − 0 = 50 kJ/mol","barrier is 40 kJ/mol lower → faster"]},{"text":"**Why tight binding of the transition state works:** the binding energy is released at the peak, which pulls the peak down."},{"text":"**Also:** catalysis needs the transition state stabilized more than the substrate; extra substrate binding alone raises the barrier."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** an enzyme speeds a reaction by stabilizing the transition state, which lowers Ea; it never changes the start or end energies."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-12b","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:12#2","pe1-cp:50#1","pe5-cp:58#1"],"q":"An uncatalyzed isomerization has equilibrium constant K at 30 °C. A catalyst is added and the temperature is held constant. Which property of the reaction changes?","choices":["Equilibrium constant","Standard free energy","Time to equilibrium","Equilibrium yield"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"A catalyst can't change K; only temperature does, and it's held constant."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"ΔG° = −RT ln K, so with K and T fixed, ΔG° is fixed too."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Same K means the same mixture at equilibrium, so the yield can't change."}],"rule":"A catalyst speeds both directions equally: everything about how far (K, ΔG°, yield) stays; only how fast changes.","exp":"A catalyst lowers the activation energy of the forward **and** reverse reactions by the same amount, so equilibrium is reached **sooner**. It does not change the energies of reactants or products, so **ΔG°**, **K** and the amount of product at equilibrium all stay the same.","how":{"ask":"Separate the properties set by the start and end energies from the one set by the height of the barrier.","know":"• **ΔG° = −RT ln K**: both depend only on reactant and product energies.\n• A catalyst lowers Ea for **both directions** equally, so forward and reverse rates rise by the same factor.\n• Same K → same equilibrium mixture; only the **speed of getting there** changes.","steps":[{"text":"The temperature is fixed, so K can change only if ΔG° changes.","hl":"the temperature is held constant"},{"text":"Compare the two paths: the ends sit at the same heights; only the peak is lower.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Isomerization with and without the catalyst","x":{"label":"Reaction progress","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Free energy (kJ/mol)","min":-40,"max":80,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Uncatalyzed","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,0],[3,35],[5,70],[7,20],[9,-20],[10,-20]]},{"name":"Catalyzed","marker":"square-open","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,0],[3,15],[5,40],[7,0],[9,-20],[10,-20]]}]},"calc":["ΔG° = −20 − 0 = **−20 kJ/mol** on both curves","Ea forward: 70 → 40 kJ/mol","Ea reverse: 70 − (−20) = 90 → 40 − (−20) = 60 kJ/mol","both barriers drop by **30 kJ/mol**"]},{"text":"Same ΔG° means the same K and the same mixture at equilibrium.","strike":["Equilibrium constant","Standard free energy","Equilibrium yield"]},{"text":"Answer: the time to equilibrium, which becomes shorter."}],"trap":"Thinking a catalyst pushes the reaction toward products: it speeds the reverse reaction just as much, so the final mixture is the same."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"equilibrium constant K","note":"set by ΔG°"},{"t":"A catalyst is added","note":"lowers Ea"},{"t":"held constant","note":"no temperature effect"}]},{"text":"**Two different questions.** \"How far?\" is answered by ΔG° and K. \"How fast?\" is answered by Ea."},{"text":"**Link ΔG° and K.** At a fixed temperature they move together, and both depend only on the ends of the diagram.","calc":["ΔG° = −RT ln K","same ends → same ΔG° → same K"]},{"text":"**Both directions speed up.** The catalyst lowers the forward and reverse barriers equally, so the ratio of the forward and reverse rate constants (k_f/k_r = K) is unchanged."},{"text":"**Also:** an inseparable homogeneous catalyst contaminates the product; a catalyst never shifts the equilibrium."},{"text":"**Also:** an enzyme is a catalyst too. A lipase making an ester (acid + alcohol ⇌ ester + water) only brings the mixture to equilibrium sooner; doubling the enzyme leaves K and the equilibrium amount of ester unchanged. Changing concentrations (adding acid or alcohol, taking water out) shifts the equilibrium position; only a temperature change alters K.","calc":["2× enzyme → equilibrium reached faster","K and [ester] at equilibrium: unchanged"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a catalyst changes only how fast equilibrium is reached, never ΔG°, K or the equilibrium mixture."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-13a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:13#1","pe1-cp:13#2","pe1-cp:13#3","pe1-cp:13#4"],"q":"A 0.20 M solution of formic acid is prepared. Adding which substance would lower the percent ionization of the formic acid?","choices":["Nitric acid","Pure water","Sodium hydroxide","Sodium bicarbonate"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Backwards: diluting a weak acid makes it ionize to a larger fraction, not a smaller one."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"A base removes H⁺, pulling HCOOH ⇌ H⁺ + HCOO⁻ to the right: more ionization, not less."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Bicarbonate is a base; it consumes H⁺ and pushes ionization up, not down."}],"rule":"To lower a weak acid's ionization, add one of its products (H⁺ or its conjugate base); bases and water make it ionize more.","exp":"HCOOH ⇌ H⁺ + HCOO⁻. Nitric acid is a **strong acid**, so it floods the solution with **H⁺, an ion already in the equilibrium**. By **Le Châtelier** the equilibrium shifts **left** and a smaller fraction of formic acid ionizes (the **common-ion effect**). Water dilutes the acid, and the two bases remove H⁺; all three raise ionization.","how":{"ask":"For each added substance, decide whether the formic acid equilibrium shifts left (less ionized) or right (more ionized).","know":"• **Percent ionization** = [A⁻] at equilibrium ÷ starting [HA] × 100.\n• **Le Châtelier**: adding a product pushes the equilibrium toward reactants; removing a product pulls it toward products.\n• **Common-ion effect**: a strong acid adds H⁺, a product of HA ⇌ H⁺ + A⁻, so HA ionizes less.","steps":[{"text":"Write the equilibrium for the weak acid in the stem.","hl":"0.20 M solution of formic acid","calc":["HCOOH ⇌ H⁺ + HCOO⁻   (Ka ≈ 1.8 × 10⁻⁴)","alone: x = √(Ka·C) = √(1.8 × 10⁻⁴ × 0.20) = √(3.6 × 10⁻⁵) = 6.0 × 10⁻³ M","% = 6.0 × 10⁻³ ÷ 0.20 × 100 = **3.0%**"]},{"text":"Nitric acid is a strong acid: it adds H⁺, a product, so the equilibrium shifts left. With 0.050 M HNO₃ present:","calc":["x ≈ Ka·C ÷ [H⁺] = 1.8 × 10⁻⁴ × 0.20 ÷ 0.050","x = 7.2 × 10⁻⁴ M","% = 7.2 × 10⁻⁴ ÷ 0.20 × 100 = **0.36%**  (down from 3.0%)"]},{"text":"Water dilutes the acid, and a diluted weak acid ionizes to a larger fraction.","strike":["Pure water"]},{"text":"Both bases remove H⁺ (OH⁻ + H⁺ → H₂O; HCO₃⁻ + H⁺ → H₂CO₃), so the equilibrium shifts right: more ionization.","strike":["Sodium hydroxide","Sodium bicarbonate"]},{"text":"Answer: nitric acid."}],"trap":"Choosing a base because it \"neutralizes\" the acid: removing H⁺ pulls the equilibrium to the right, so the weak acid ionizes more, not less."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"formic acid","note":"weak acid: HCOOH ⇌ H⁺ + HCOO⁻"},{"t":"lower the percent ionization","note":"shift the equilibrium left"}]},{"text":"**Percent ionization** is the fraction of the acid molecules that have given up their H⁺.","calc":["% ionization = [A⁻] ÷ [HA]₀ × 100"]},{"text":"**Le Châtelier:** add a product and the equilibrium moves back toward the reactant. H⁺ is a product, so an extra source of H⁺ makes less HA ionize.","calc":["add H⁺ → shift left → % ionization down","remove H⁺ → shift right → % ionization up"]},{"text":"**Also:** diluting a weak acid lowers [H⁺] but raises its percent ionization."},{"text":"**Also:** an electron-withdrawing substituent (such as Cl) near the acidic group makes the acid stronger, so it ionizes more."},{"text":"**Also:** a drop of indicator is too small an amount to change an acid's equilibrium or its percent ionization."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** adding a strong acid (a common ion, H⁺) lowers a weak acid's percent ionization; adding a base raises it."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-56a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:56#1","pe1-cp:14#1"],"q":"A heating element with a resistance of 250 mΩ is connected across a 4.5-V supply. Assuming constant resistance, how much current does it draw?","choices":["18 A","18 mA","56 A","0.056 A"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"units","why":"Treats 250 mΩ as 250 Ω, a resistance a thousand times too big, so the current comes out in milliamps."},{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"Divides 250 by 4.5: R ÷ V with the milli left off, so it is both upside down and a thousand off."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"That's R ÷ V, upside down; with R under 1 Ω the current must exceed 4.5 A."}],"rule":"I = V ÷ R with R in ohms (250 mΩ = 0.25 Ω); a resistance under 1 Ω draws more amps than the volts. Estimate: 4.5 × 4.","exp":"Convert first: **250 mΩ = 0.250 Ω**. Then **Ohm's law, I = V/R** = 4.5 V ÷ 0.250 Ω = **18 A**. A resistance under 1 Ω gives a current larger than the voltage number.","how":{"ask":"Put the resistance in ohms, then divide the voltage by it.","know":"• **Ohm's law: I = V/R** (A = V ÷ Ω).\n• **m (milli) = 10⁻³**: 1 mΩ = 0.001 Ω. Capital **M (mega) = 10⁶**.","steps":[{"text":"Convert milliohms to ohms.","hl":"a resistance of 250 mΩ","calc":["250 mΩ = 250 × 10⁻³ Ω = **0.250 Ω**"]},{"text":"Apply Ohm's law.","hl":"4.5-V supply","calc":["I = V ÷ R","I = 4.5 V ÷ 0.250 Ω","I = **18 A**"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Current from a 4.5-V supply versus resistance","x":{"label":"Resistance (Ω)","min":0,"max":3,"step":0.5},"y":{"label":"Current (A)","min":0,"max":20,"step":5},"series":[{"name":"I = V ÷ R","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0.25,18],[0.5,9],[0.75,6],[1,4.5],[1.5,3],[2,2.25],[3,1.5]]}]}},{"text":"18 mA divides by 250 Ω, skipping the milli conversion. 56 A divides 250 by 4.5, which is R ÷ V and unconverted. 0.056 A is R ÷ V in ohms, the ratio upside down.","strike":["18 mA","56 A","0.056 A"]},{"text":"Answer: 18 A."}],"trap":"Treating 250 mΩ as 250 Ω gives 18 mA, a thousand times too small."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the givens**.","marks":[{"t":"250 mΩ","note":"resistance R (milliohms)"},{"t":"4.5-V supply","note":"voltage V"},{"t":"how much current","note":"I in amps"}]},{"text":"**Milli means one thousandth.** Convert before dividing.","calc":["250 mΩ = 250 ÷ 1000 Ω = 0.250 Ω"]},{"text":"**Ohm's law:** current = voltage ÷ resistance.","calc":["I = V ÷ R = 4.5 ÷ 0.250 = 18 A"]},{"text":"**Also:** at constant resistance, V = IR makes voltage directly proportional to current."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** I = V/R with R in ohms; convert mΩ by dividing by 1000 first, and expect more amps than volts when R is under 1 Ω."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-15a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:15#1"],"q":"In vacuum, light travels at 300,000 km/s; inside a plastic lens it travels at 200,000 km/s. What is the plastic's index of refraction?","choices":["1.5","0.67","0.50","0.33"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"impossible","why":"Below 1: that's v/c upside down, and no material has an index under 1."},{"c":2,"tag":"impossible","why":"n < 1 would mean light moves faster than in vacuum; every real index is at least 1."},{"c":3,"tag":"impossible","why":"Below 1 again: n = c/v and v < c, so n must be greater than 1."}],"rule":"n = c ÷ v is always at least 1; any choice below 1 has the ratio flipped.","exp":"**n = c/v** = 300,000 ÷ 200,000 = **1.5**. Light is never faster in a material than in vacuum, so every real index is at least 1; the three values below 1 are impossible.","how":{"ask":"Divide the speed of light in vacuum by its speed in the plastic, then check that n ≥ 1.","know":"• **Index of refraction: n = c/v** (c = speed in vacuum, v = speed in the material).\n• Since v ≤ c, **n ≥ 1 always** (n = 1 only for vacuum).","steps":[{"text":"Pick out the two speeds.","hl":"300,000 km/s","calc":["c = 300,000 km/s","v = 200,000 km/s"]},{"text":"Apply n = c/v; the units cancel.","hl":"200,000 km/s","calc":["n = c ÷ v","n = 300,000 ÷ 200,000","n = **1.5**"]},{"text":"0.67 is v/c, the ratio upside down. 0.50 and 0.33 compare the speed difference with v or c. All are below 1, which would mean light moves faster than in vacuum.","strike":["0.67","0.50","0.33"]},{"text":"Answer: 1.5."}],"trap":"Writing v/c instead of c/v. Any index below 1 is a red flag that the ratio is flipped."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the two speeds**.","marks":[{"t":"300,000 km/s","note":"c, in vacuum"},{"t":"200,000 km/s","note":"v, in plastic"},{"t":"index of refraction","note":"n = c/v"}]},{"text":"**The index says how many times slower light is** in the material than in vacuum.","calc":["n = c ÷ v"]},{"text":"**Fast over slow.** The bigger speed goes on top, so n comes out at least 1.","calc":["300,000 ÷ 200,000 = 1.5"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** n = c/v, and n ≥ 1 for every material; a value below 1 means the ratio was flipped."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-16a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:16#1","pe5-cp:54#3"],"q":"Two lasers deliver beams of equal intensity, but laser X emits light at twice the frequency of laser Y. Compared with Y, what is doubled for X?","choices":["Energy of each photon","Photon arrival rate","Speed of the photons","Wavelength of the light"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Backwards: equal intensity with more energy per photon means fewer photons each second, not more."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Direction: λ = c/f, so doubling f halves the wavelength."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"All light in air moves at c, whatever its frequency."}],"rule":"E = hf: frequency sets each photon's energy; speed stays c, λ = c/f shrinks, and equal intensity means fewer photons.","exp":"A photon's energy is set by its frequency: **E = hf**. Doubling f **doubles the energy of each photon**. The wavelength halves (λ = c/f), the speed stays c, and since both beams have the same intensity, X needs only **only half the photons each second** to carry the same power.","how":{"ask":"Which property of a single photon depends on frequency, and what does equal intensity then force on the photon count?","know":"• **E = hf = hc/λ**: one photon's energy is proportional to its frequency.\n• **c = fλ**: higher f means shorter λ; light speed in air stays c ≈ 3.00 × 10⁸ m/s.\n• **Intensity** = (photons per second) × (energy per photon) ÷ area.","steps":[{"text":"Apply E = hf to both lasers.","hl":"twice the frequency","calc":["E_Y = hf","E_X = h(2f) = **2E_Y**","e.g. f = 5.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz: E = (6.63 × 10⁻³⁴)(5.0 × 10¹⁴) = 3.3 × 10⁻¹⁹ J","2f = 1.0 × 10¹⁵ Hz: E = 6.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Photon energy rises in proportion to frequency (E = hf)","x":{"label":"Frequency (× 10¹⁴ Hz)","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Photon energy (× 10⁻¹⁹ J)","min":0,"max":7,"step":1},"series":[{"name":"E = hf","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[2.5,1.66],[5,3.3],[7.5,4.97],[10,6.6]]}]}},{"text":"Equal intensity with twice the energy per photon means half the photons each second, so the arrival rate halves.","hl":"equal intensity","calc":["I = (photons/s) × E ÷ A","same I, 2E → photons/s × ½"],"strike":["Photon arrival rate"]},{"text":"Light speed in air is c at every frequency, and c = fλ means doubling f halves λ.","calc":["λ = c ÷ f → λ_X = λ_Y ÷ 2"],"strike":["Speed of the photons","Wavelength of the light"]},{"text":"Answer: the energy of each photon."}],"trap":"Thinking higher frequency means more photons arriving. Frequency sets each photon's energy; at the same intensity a higher frequency actually means fewer photons per second."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find what is the same and what differs**.","marks":[{"t":"equal intensity","note":"same power per area"},{"t":"twice the frequency","note":"f doubled"},{"t":"what is doubled","note":"find the factor that grew"}]},{"text":"**Light comes in photons.** Each photon's energy is set by its frequency alone.","calc":["E = hf = hc/λ","2f → 2E"]},{"text":"**Intensity belongs to the beam**: energy per photon times photons per second, per area.","calc":["I = (photons/s × E) ÷ A","fixed I and 2E → ½ the photons/s"]},{"text":"**See it:** photon energy is a straight line through zero against frequency.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"E = hf: doubling the frequency doubles the photon energy","x":{"label":"Frequency (× 10¹⁴ Hz)","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Photon energy (× 10⁻¹⁹ J)","min":0,"max":7,"step":1},"series":[{"name":"E = hf","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[2.5,1.66],[5,3.3],[7.5,4.97],[10,6.6]]}]}},{"text":"**Also:** at one fixed color, intensity is proportional to the number of photons per second; speed, wavelength and polarization don't set it."},{"text":"**Also:** one photon has an energy, a frequency and a wavelength, linked by E = hf = hc/λ. Intensity and amplitude belong to the beam (how many photons arrive each second), so a photon with twice the energy has twice the f and half the λ, but that tells you nothing about amplitude.","calc":["one photon: E, f, λ","the whole beam: photons per second → intensity, amplitude"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** E = hf, so photon energy rises in step with frequency (and λ falls); the photon rate sets intensity at a fixed color, and speed stays c."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-17a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:17#1","pe6-cp:51#1","pe6-cp:51#2"],"q":"A green LED emits light at a frequency of 580 THz. Using Planck's constant in eV·s, how much energy does each emitted photon carry?","choices":["2.4 eV","0.0024 eV","0.24 eV","2400 eV"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"units","why":"Too small by 1000: tera read as giga (10⁹); visible photons carry a few eV, and this is a microwave-range energy."},{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"One power of ten short: 580 THz is 5.8 × 10¹⁴ Hz, not 5.8 × 10¹³ Hz."},{"c":3,"tag":"units","why":"Too big by 1000: tera read as peta (10¹⁵); 2400 eV is X-ray territory, not visible light."}],"rule":"E = hf with h ≈ 4 × 10⁻¹⁵ eV·s and tera = 10¹²; any visible photon carries a few eV (about 2–3).","exp":"**E = hf** with **tera = 10¹²**: f = 5.8 × 10¹⁴ Hz, and E = (4.14 × 10⁻¹⁵ eV·s)(5.8 × 10¹⁴ s⁻¹) = 24 × 10⁻¹ eV = **2.4 eV**, a typical visible-light photon energy.","how":{"ask":"Convert THz to Hz, multiply by Planck's constant in eV·s, and combine the powers of ten.","know":"• **E = hf**: E = photon energy, h = Planck's constant, f = frequency (Hz = s⁻¹).\n• **h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s = 4.14 × 10⁻¹⁵ eV·s** (1 eV = 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ J).\n• Prefixes: giga 10⁹, **tera 10¹²**, peta 10¹⁵.\n• Visible photons carry roughly 1.7–3.1 eV.","steps":[{"text":"The frequency is given, so use E = hf.","hl":"a frequency of 580 THz","calc":["E = h × f"]},{"text":"Convert the prefix: tera = 10¹².","calc":["580 THz = 580 × 10¹² Hz","= **5.8 × 10¹⁴ Hz**"]},{"text":"Multiply the numbers and add the exponents separately.","calc":["E = (4.14 × 10⁻¹⁵ eV·s)(5.8 × 10¹⁴ s⁻¹)","4.14 × 5.8 = 24.0","10⁻¹⁵ × 10¹⁴ = 10⁻¹","E = 24.0 × 10⁻¹ eV = **2.4 eV**"]},{"text":"Check in joules: (6.63 × 10⁻³⁴)(5.8 × 10¹⁴) = 3.8 × 10⁻¹⁹ J, and 3.8 × 10⁻¹⁹ ÷ 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ ≈ 2.4 eV.","calc":["3.8 × 10⁻¹⁹ J ÷ 1.60 × 10⁻¹⁹ J/eV ≈ **2.4 eV**"]},{"text":"0.0024 eV treats tera as giga (10⁹). 2400 eV treats it as peta (10¹⁵). 0.24 eV writes 580 THz as 5.8 × 10¹³ Hz, one power of ten short.","strike":["0.0024 eV","2400 eV","0.24 eV"]},{"text":"Answer: 2.4 eV."}],"trap":"Slipping one prefix step (tera as giga) shifts the answer by a factor of 1000. A visible photon always carries a few eV, so 0.0024 eV or 2400 eV is a unit slip."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the number and its unit**.","marks":[{"t":"580 THz","note":"frequency f"},{"t":"eV·s","note":"use h in eV·s"},{"t":"each emitted photon","note":"energy of a single photon"}]},{"text":"**One photon's energy is Planck's constant times frequency.**","calc":["E = hf","h = 4.14 × 10⁻¹⁵ eV·s"]},{"text":"**Tera means 10¹².** Write the frequency in scientific notation before multiplying.","calc":["580 THz = 5.8 × 10¹⁴ Hz"]},{"text":"**Numbers and powers of ten separately.** Multiply 4.14 × 5.8, then add −15 and +14.","calc":["4.14 × 5.8 = 24.0","10⁻¹⁵ × 10¹⁴ = 10⁻¹","E = 2.4 eV"]},{"text":"**Also:** E = hf covers radio waves too; only the prefix changes. Mega = 10⁶, so an FM station at 100 MHz emits photons at 1.0 × 10⁸ Hz, each carrying millions of times less energy than a visible photon.","calc":["E = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J·s × 1.0 × 10⁸ Hz ≈ 6.6 × 10⁻²⁶ J","green light (5.8 × 10¹⁴ Hz): ≈ 3.8 × 10⁻¹⁹ J","FM-band photons (88–108 MHz): about 6–7 × 10⁻²⁶ J"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** E = hf with f in hertz (THz × 10¹²); multiply the leading numbers and add the exponents, then check that a visible photon comes out near 2–3 eV."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-18a","s":"Fluids","disc":"physics","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:18#1","pe3-cp:58#1","pe4-cp:57#1","pe6-cp:52#1"],"q":"A fluid's gauge pressure at depth h is P = ρgh, and its density is ρ = m/V. Which expression gives the depth h?","choices":["PV/(mg)","mg/(PV)","Pm/(Vg)","P/(mVg)"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Direction: higher P means deeper, so P must be on top; here it's on the bottom."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Uses ρ = V/m, the density definition upside down; a denser fluid (more m) must give a shallower depth."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Treats density as m × V; since ρ = m/V, the V must end up on top beside P."}],"rule":"Solve h = P/(ρg), then put in ρ = m/V, which flips V to the top. Check: more P means deeper, more m means shallower.","exp":"Substitute the density definition: P = (m/V)gh. Multiply both sides by V to clear the fraction: PV = mgh. Divide by mg: **h = PV/(mg)**.","how":{"ask":"Put the density definition into the pressure equation, clear the fraction, then isolate h.","know":"• **Substitution**: replace ρ with m/V.\n• **Clear a fraction** by multiplying both sides by its denominator (here V).\n• Then divide both sides by everything still multiplying h.","steps":[{"text":"Substitute ρ = m/V into P = ρgh.","hl":"ρ = m/V","calc":["P = (m/V) g h"]},{"text":"Multiply through by the denominator V.","hl":"P = ρgh","calc":["P × V = m g h","PV = mgh"]},{"text":"Divide both sides by mg.","calc":["h = **PV/(mg)**"]},{"text":"mg/(PV) is the result flipped. Pm/(Vg) comes from substituting ρ = V/m, the density definition upside down. P/(mVg) treats density as m × V instead of m ÷ V.","strike":["mg/(PV)","Pm/(Vg)","P/(mVg)"]},{"text":"Answer: PV/(mg)."}],"trap":"Putting V in the denominator: since ρ = m/V, the V sits under m, and moving it across the equals sign puts it on top with P."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the two definitions** and the unknown.","marks":[{"t":"P = ρgh","note":"pressure equation"},{"t":"ρ = m/V","note":"density definition"},{"t":"depth h","note":"isolate this"}]},{"text":"**Substitute.** Wherever ρ appears, write m/V.","calc":["P = (m/V)gh"]},{"text":"**Clear the fraction.** Multiply every term by V.","calc":["PV = mgh"]},{"text":"**Isolate h.** Divide by what multiplies it, then check units: (Pa·m³)/(kg·m/s²) = m ✓.","calc":["h = PV/(mg)"]},{"text":"**Also:** for a fluid column, h = P/(ρg): atmospheric pressure can push water up an evacuated tube about 10 m.","calc":["h = P/(ρg)","1 atm ≈ 1.0 × 10⁵ Pa; water: 1.0 × 10⁵ ÷ (1000 × 10) ≈ 10 m"]},{"text":"**Also:** when a question gives a formula, name each variable and plug the values in. In a blood pressure written systolic/diastolic, the diastolic is the lower number.","calc":["given pulse pressure = P_s − P_d","120/76 mmHg: P_s = 120, P_d = 76","pulse pressure = 120 − 76 = 44 mmHg"]},{"text":"**Also:** the same plug-in works for a formula you have never met, such as a gyromagnetic ratio γ = 2πf/B. Match each symbol to its value in the passage (f, frequency; B, field), keep the units the choices use (MHz stays MHz for an answer in MHz/T), and leave 2π standing as a factor when every choice carries it.","calc":["¹H at 400 MHz in a 9.4 T field","γ = 2π × 400 MHz ÷ 9.4 T ≈ 42.6 × 2π MHz/T"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** substitute one definition into the other, multiply through by the fraction's denominator, then divide to isolate the variable."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-19d","s":"Fluids","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:19#4","pe1-cp:19#1","pe1-cp:19#2","pe1-cp:19#3"],"q":"A patient's systolic pressure is 95 mmHg. Expressed as a percentage of atmospheric pressure, about how large is this value?","choices":["12.5%","8.0%","94%","0.13%"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"Divides 95 by 101, atmospheric pressure in kPa; mmHg over kPa mixes units, and 95 mmHg is far below an atmosphere."},{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Ratio upside down: 760 ÷ 95; a pressure smaller than an atmosphere is the numerator."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"Forgot × 100: that's the fraction written with a % sign; as a percent it's tens, not tenths."}],"rule":"Percent of an atmosphere = mmHg ÷ 760 × 100. Estimate: round 95 to 100 and 760 to 800.","exp":"Write both pressures in **the same unit**, mmHg: 95 ÷ 760 = 0.125, and 0.125 × 100 = **12.5%**. 8.0 inverts the ratio, 94% divides mmHg by kPa, and 0.13% forgets to multiply by 100.","how":{"ask":"Divide the patient's pressure by atmospheric pressure in matching units, then convert to a percentage.","know":"• **1 atm = 760 mmHg**.\n• **% = value ÷ 760 mmHg × 100** when the value is in mmHg.\n• Never divide mmHg by kPa.","steps":[{"text":"Note the value and its unit.","hl":"95 mmHg","calc":["value = 95 mmHg","1 atm = 760 mmHg"]},{"text":"Divide, then multiply by 100.","calc":["95 ÷ 760 = 0.125","0.125 × 100 = **12.5%**"]},{"text":"Eliminate the slips: 8.0 = 760 ÷ 95 (inverted); 94% ≈ 95 ÷ 101 (mmHg over kPa); 0.13% ≈ 0.125 written as a percent without the × 100.","strike":["8.0%","94%","0.13%"]},{"text":"Answer: 12.5%."}],"trap":"Dividing by 101: that is atmospheric pressure in kPa, and dividing mmHg by kPa gives a meaningless number near 94%."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the number and what to compare it with**.","marks":[{"t":"95 mmHg","note":"the value"},{"t":"atmospheric pressure","note":"760 mmHg"}]},{"text":"**Same units first.** The value is in mmHg, so use 1 atm = 760 mmHg."},{"text":"**Percentage = fraction × 100.**","calc":["% = value ÷ 760 × 100"]},{"text":"**Also:** a typical healthy adult's blood pressure is about 120/80 mmHg (systolic/diastolic)."},{"text":"**Also:** pulse pressure = systolic − diastolic."},{"text":"**Also:** 1 atm = 760 mmHg; multiply atm by 760 to get mmHg."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** percent of 1 atm = (pressure in mmHg ÷ 760) × 100."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-20b","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:20#2","pe1-cp:20#1","pe1-cp:20#3","pe4-cp:45#1","pe4-cp:45#2","pe6-cp:57#1","pe6-cp:57#2"],"q":"Strontium-90 has a half-life of 29 years. How much of a 72 mg sample of it remains after 87 years?","choices":["9 mg","18 mg","36 mg","63 mg"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"That's the amount that decayed (72 − 9); the question asks what remains."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"That's after one half-life (29 y), but 87 y is three half-lives."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"That's after two half-lives; 87 y is three, so halve once more."}],"rule":"Count half-lives (time ÷ half-life), then halve that many times; 'remains' is what's left, not what decayed.","exp":"n = 87 ÷ 29 = **3** half-lives. **Remaining = starting amount × (½)ⁿ** = 72 mg × ⅛ = **9 mg**. 63 mg is the amount that decayed, not the amount left.","how":{"ask":"Count the half-lives, then halve the starting amount that many times.","know":"• n = t ÷ t½.\n• **Remaining = N₀ × (½)ⁿ**.\n• Decayed = N₀ − remaining.","steps":[{"text":"Note the starting mass, the half-life and the time.","hl":"72 mg","calc":["N₀ = 72 mg","t½ = 29 y, t = 87 y"]},{"text":"Count half-lives.","hl":"87 years","calc":["n = 87 ÷ 29 = **3**"]},{"text":"Halve three times.","calc":["(½)³ = ⅛","72 mg × ⅛ = **9 mg**","72 → 36 → 18 → 9 mg"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Strontium-90 remaining in a 72 mg sample","x":{"label":"Time (years)","min":0,"max":87,"step":29},"y":{"label":"Mass remaining (mg)","min":0,"max":80,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Sr-90 left","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,72],[29,36],[58,18],[87,9]]}]}},{"text":"36 mg is after one half-life and 18 mg after two; 63 mg (72 − 9) is what decayed.","strike":["36 mg","18 mg","63 mg"]},{"text":"Answer: 9 mg."}],"trap":"Answering 63 mg: that is the decayed mass. The question asks what remains."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number**.","marks":[{"t":"half-life of 29 years","note":"t½"},{"t":"72 mg","note":"starting amount"},{"t":"87 years","note":"elapsed time"}]},{"text":"**Count half-lives:** n = t ÷ t½.","calc":["n = 87 ÷ 29 = 3"]},{"text":"**Halve n times**, which is the same as multiplying by (½)ⁿ.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Each half-life halves the mass","x":{"label":"Time (years)","min":0,"max":87,"step":29},"y":{"label":"Mass remaining (mg)","min":0,"max":80,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Sr-90 left","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,72],[29,36],[58,18],[87,9]]}]}},{"text":"**Also:** fraction decayed = 1 − (½)ⁿ, where n = time ÷ half-life."},{"text":"**Also:** to find the starting amount, run it backward: double once per half-life, so original = remaining × 2ⁿ.","calc":["n = 18 days ÷ 6 days = 3","original = 7 mg × 2³ = 7 × 8 = 56 mg"]},{"text":"**Also:** activity (decays per second, in Bq or Ci) is proportional to the number of nuclei left, so it falls on the same schedule: A = A₀ × (½)ⁿ, with n = elapsed time ÷ half-life.","calc":["t½ = 8.0 days, t = 24 days → n = 3","A = 600 Bq × (½)³ = 600 ÷ 8 = 75 Bq"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** amount remaining = starting amount × (½)ⁿ, with n = time ÷ half-life."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-21b","s":"Reading Data","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe1-cp:21#2","pe1-cp:21#1","pe1-cp:21#3"],"q":"A pump moves 24 L in 4.0 min. Its power curve passes through 4 W at 0 L/min, 10 W at 2 L/min, 16 W at 4 L/min and 22 W at 6 L/min. What power does the pump draw?","choices":["22 W","16 W","4 W","88 W"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"The near-zero-flow point: using the per-second rate (0.1 L/s) on a per-minute axis."},{"c":3,"tag":"units","why":"Power × time is energy (joules of work), not power; the curve already gives watts."},{"c":1,"tag":"copied","why":"Read at x = 4, the number of minutes from the stem, instead of at the computed flow rate."}],"rule":"Work out the flow rate first (volume ÷ time, in the axis's units), then read the curve at that rate.","exp":"First find the operating flow: 24 L ÷ 4.0 min = **6 L/min**. Then read the curve at that flow: **22 W**. Reading at 4 confuses the time with the flow.","how":{"ask":"Work out the flow rate in L/min, then read the power at that flow on the curve.","know":"• **Flow rate = volume ÷ time**, in the axis units (L/min).\n• To read a graph: find the x value, go up to the curve, read across to y.","steps":[{"text":"Compute the flow rate in the graph's units.","hl":"24 L in 4.0 min","calc":["Q = 24 L ÷ 4.0 min = **6 L/min**"]},{"text":"Read the curve at 6 L/min.","hl":"22 W at 6 L/min","calc":["P(6 L/min) = **22 W**"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Pump power versus flow rate","x":{"label":"Flow rate (L/min)","min":0,"max":8,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Power (W)","min":0,"max":30,"step":5},"series":[{"name":"Power","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,4],[2,10],[4,16],[6,22],[8,28]]}]}},{"text":"16 W is read at x = 4, the number of minutes. 4 W is the curve at almost zero flow, what you get by using the per-second rate (0.1 L/s) on a per-minute axis. 88 W multiplies the power by the time, which gives work, not power.","strike":["16 W","4 W","88 W"]},{"text":"Answer: 22 W."}],"trap":"Reading the graph at a number from the stem (4.0 min) instead of at the computed flow rate."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it is.","marks":[{"t":"24 L","note":"volume"},{"t":"4.0 min","note":"time"},{"t":"power curve","note":"P read from flow"}]},{"text":"**Get the x value.** The curve's x-axis is flow in L/min.","calc":["24 L ÷ 4.0 min = 6 L/min"]},{"text":"**Go up to the curve, then across.** At 6 L/min the line sits at 22 W.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Read power at the operating flow","x":{"label":"Flow rate (L/min)","min":0,"max":8,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Power (W)","min":0,"max":30,"step":5},"series":[{"name":"Power","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,4],[2,10],[4,16],[6,22],[8,28]]}]}},{"text":"**Also:** compute a rate in exactly the units of the graph's axis before reading the graph."},{"text":"**Also:** work = power × time, with power in watts and time in seconds, giving joules."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** compute the x value in the axis's units, then read the curve at that x; never read at a raw number from the question."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-22a","s":"Protein Structure","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:22#1","pe1-cp:22#2","pe1-cp:22#3","pe6-cp:30#2"],"q":"A small protein is held folded by one disulfide, one salt bridge, several hydrogen bonds and a contact between two leucine side chains. Which single interaction is weakest?","choices":["London dispersion","Disulfide cross-link","Salt bridge","Hydrogen bond"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"A disulfide is a covalent S–S bond (about 250 kJ/mol), the strongest link in the list."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A salt bridge pairs full + and − charges; full-charge attraction beats both dipole and dispersion forces."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"A hydrogen bond uses the large permanent dipole of N–H or O–H, stronger than passing induced dipoles."}],"rule":"Strength ranks covalent > ionic > hydrogen bond > induced-dipole contacts; nonpolar side chains touching is the weakest.","exp":"Rank by what holds each one. The **disulfide** shares electrons (covalent, about 250 kJ/mol). A **salt bridge** joins full charges, and a **hydrogen bond** joins a strongly polar N–H or O–H to a lone pair (each a few to about 20 kJ/mol). Two leucine side chains are nonpolar, so only **London dispersion** forces from brief induced dipoles hold them, about 1–4 kJ/mol per contact: the weakest.","how":{"ask":"Name the force behind each link, then rank them from shared electrons down to brief induced dipoles.","know":"• Strength, strongest first: **covalent** (disulfide) > **ionic** (salt bridge) > **hydrogen bond** > **London dispersion**.\n• Disulfide: shared electron pair between two Cys S atoms, about 250 kJ/mol.\n• Hydrogen bond: H on N or O attracted to a lone pair on N or O.\n• Dispersion: temporary induced dipoles, the only force between nonpolar groups such as Leu, Val, Ile, Phe.","steps":[{"text":"Classify each link by its force.","hl":"a contact between two leucine side chains","calc":["disulfide: covalent S–S","salt bridge: + to − (ionic)","hydrogen bond: N–H or O–H to a lone pair","Leu–Leu: nonpolar → dispersion only"]},{"text":"Rank them.","calc":["covalent ≈ 250 kJ/mol","ionic and H-bond: a few to ≈ 20 kJ/mol","dispersion ≈ 1–4 kJ/mol per contact → **weakest**"]},{"text":"The disulfide is covalent, so it is the strongest; the salt bridge joins full charges, stronger than anything that leans on partial or temporary charges.","strike":["Disulfide cross-link","Salt bridge"]},{"text":"A hydrogen bond uses the large permanent dipole of N–H or O–H, which is stronger than the passing induced dipoles between two hydrocarbon chains.","strike":["Hydrogen bond"]},{"text":"Answer: London dispersion."}],"trap":"Picking the hydrogen bond because H-bonds break easily on heating. They are weak next to covalent and ionic links, but stronger than the dispersion contact between nonpolar side chains."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"one disulfide","note":"covalent S–S"},{"t":"one salt bridge","note":"+ to −"},{"t":"hydrogen bonds","note":"N–H or O–H to a lone pair"},{"t":"two leucine side chains","note":"nonpolar: dispersion"}]},{"text":"**Rank the forces.** Shared electrons beat full charges, full charges beat permanent dipoles, and permanent dipoles beat brief induced ones.","calc":["covalent > ionic > hydrogen bond > London dispersion"]},{"text":"**Cysteine carries a thiol.** Oxidizing two Cys –SH groups joins the sulfurs into a disulfide, the one covalent cross-link among these.","fig":"cysteine","calc":["R–SH + HS–R → R–S–S–R"]},{"text":"**Also:** a disulfide is always covalent; it is intermolecular when its two cysteines belong to different molecules."},{"text":"**Also:** reducing agents such as β-mercaptoethanol or DTT break disulfides back into two thiols."},{"text":"**Also:** a disulfide can form only between two cysteine thiols (–SH). If a protein has no Cys, or its Cys are already paired, any oxidative cross-link in it must come from a different side chain, so look past cysteine for the residue the oxidant attacked."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** covalent (disulfide) > salt bridge > hydrogen bond > London dispersion; contacts between nonpolar side chains are the weakest, and only a disulfide is covalent."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-23c","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:23#3","pe1-cp:23#1","pe1-cp:23#2","pe5-cp:42#1","pe5-cp:42#2","pe5-cp:42#3"],"q":"A designed peptide has a cross-linking region A (Gly–Cys–Gly–Cys) and a cell-binding region B (Lys–Glu–Arg–Asp). Which residue gives cells a charged group to bind?","choices":["Arg in region B","Cys in region A","Gly in region A","Cys in region B"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Region A is the cross-linker: Cys forms disulfides, and its –SH is uncharged at pH 7."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Glycine's side chain is just H; it carries no charged group."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Read the stem: region B is Lys–Glu–Arg–Asp, with no Cys in it."}],"rule":"Charged side chains at pH 7 are Lys and Arg (+) and Asp and Glu (−); Gly and Cys carry no charge.","exp":"Match each residue to its region first. Region A holds **Gly** (no side chain) and **Cys** (a thiol for disulfide cross-links), so it offers no charge. Region B holds Lys, Glu, Arg and Asp, all **charged** at body pH; **Arg** is one of them. There is no Cys in region B.","how":{"ask":"List the residues in each region, then find a charged one in the region where it actually sits.","know":"• Charged at body pH: Asp −, Glu −, Lys +, Arg +.\n• **Cys** = thiol for **disulfide cross-links**, not a charge.\n• **Gly** = no side chain.\n• A choice must name a residue that is really in the region it names.","steps":[{"text":"Write out the two regions.","hl":"Gly–Cys–Gly–Cys","calc":["region A: Gly, Cys → cross-linking","region B: Lys, Glu, Arg, Asp → all charged"]},{"text":"Region A has no charged side chain: Gly has none, and Cys forms disulfides.","strike":["Cys in region A","Gly in region A"]},{"text":"Cys does not appear in region B, so that pairing is wrong.","hl":"Lys–Glu–Arg–Asp","strike":["Cys in region B"]},{"text":"Answer: Arg in region B."}],"trap":"Choosing cysteine because its sulfur looks reactive: it belongs to the cross-linking region and gives a covalent link between strands, not a charge for cells."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"cross-linking region A","note":"Cys for disulfides"},{"t":"cell-binding region B","note":"charged residues"},{"t":"charged group","note":"Asp, Glu, Lys, Arg"}]},{"text":"**Sort by job.** Cys makes S–S cross-links; Gly is a spacer; charged residues bind cells.","fig":["cysteine","glycine"]},{"text":"**Check the charged ones.**","calc":["Lys +, Arg +","Glu −, Asp −"],"fig":["arginine","glutamate"]},{"text":"**Check the location too.** A right residue in the wrong region is a wrong answer."},{"text":"**Also:** for an electrostatic contact, choose a side chain charged opposite to the partner: Lys or Arg for a − site, Asp or Glu for a + site."},{"text":"**Also:** glycine has no side chain (R = H), so it adds no charge or functional group to a binding site."},{"text":"**Also:** a binding pocket can name two contacts at once. A phosphate (−) makes a salt bridge only with a + side chain (Lys or Arg), and a flat aromatic ring π-stacks with an aromatic side chain (Phe, Tyr, Trp or His). The right pair satisfies both: Asp or Glu would repel the phosphate, and Ser or Ala cannot stack.","calc":["phosphate (−) → Lys⁺ or Arg⁺","aromatic ring → Phe, Tyr, Trp or His","pair: one from each list, e.g. Lys + Trp"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** match each residue to its labeled region and its job: Cys cross-links, Gly adds nothing, and Asp, Glu, Lys, Arg give charges; for a phosphate in a ring pocket, pair a + residue (Lys/Arg) with an aromatic one."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-24c","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:24#3","pe1-cp:24#1","pe1-cp:24#2","pe6-cp:10#1","pe6-cp:10#2"],"q":"A report states that a regulatory phosphate is attached to a residue whose side chain is a primary alcohol. Which residue carries the phosphate?","choices":["Serine","Asparagine","Tyrosine","Cysteine"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Asparagine ends in an amide, –C(=O)NH₂; it has no –OH at all."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Tyrosine's OH sits on a ring, which makes it a phenol, not a primary alcohol."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Cysteine has –SH, a thiol, not an alcohol."}],"rule":"Primary alcohol means –CH₂OH; Thr's OH is secondary, Tyr's is a phenol, Cys has SH.","exp":"A **primary alcohol** has its –OH on a carbon bonded to only one other carbon: **–CH₂–OH**. That is serine's side chain. Tyrosine's –OH is on a ring (a phenol), asparagine ends in an amide with no –OH, and cysteine has –SH, not –OH.","how":{"ask":"Which side chain matches the description the report gives: an –OH on a carbon with one carbon neighbor?","know":"• **Primary alcohol**: –CH₂–OH (1 C on the carbinol carbon).\n• Ser –CH₂OH (1°); Thr –CH(OH)CH₃ (2°); Tyr ring–OH (phenol); Asn –CH₂CONH₂ (amide, no OH); Cys –CH₂SH (thiol).\n• Use the description given, not the residue you expect to see phosphorylated.","steps":[{"text":"The report's clue is the class of alcohol.","hl":"side chain is a primary alcohol"},{"text":"Find the side chain that ends in –CH₂–OH.","fig":["serine-sc","t1-phosphoserine"],"calc":["Ser: –CH₂–OH → C bearing OH has 1 C neighbor → **1°** ✓"]},{"text":"Tyrosine's OH sits on a ring (a phenol), and asparagine ends in an amide with no OH.","strike":["Asparagine","Tyrosine"]},{"text":"Cysteine ends in –CH₂–SH: a thiol, not an alcohol.","strike":["Cysteine"]},{"text":"Answer: serine."}],"trap":"Picking tyrosine because it is also phosphorylated: the report names a primary alcohol, and only serine fits."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"primary alcohol","note":"–CH₂–OH"},{"t":"regulatory phosphate","note":"on an –OH"}]},{"text":"**Translate the description into a structure.** Primary means the –OH carbon has one carbon neighbor.","calc":["primary: R–CH₂–OH","secondary: R–CH(OH)–R′","phenol: ring–OH"]},{"text":"**Match it to a side chain.** Only serine ends in –CH₂–OH.","fig":"serine-sc"},{"text":"**Also:** serine is a primary alcohol, threonine a secondary alcohol, tyrosine a phenol, and alanine only a methyl group."},{"text":"**Also:** phosphorylation goes on a side-chain –OH, so look for Ser, Thr or Tyr and reject side chains with no hydroxyl."},{"text":"**Also:** in a kinase assay with [γ-³²P]ATP, the label ends up only where the enzyme can build a phosphoester, on a side-chain hydroxyl. Look at the one residue that differs between the peptides: Ser, Thr or Tyr can take the ³²P, while a residue with no hydroxyl, such as alanine, leucine or valine, leaves its peptide unlabeled.","calc":["–OH present → phosphoester → ³²P in the peptide","no –OH (Ala, Leu, Val) → no ³²P"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** turn the description into a structure first (primary alcohol → –CH₂OH → serine), then pick the residue that matches it."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-25c","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe1-cp:25#3","pe1-cp:25#1","pe1-cp:25#2","pe3-cp:43#1","pe5-cp:31#3"],"q":"A peptide's only ionizable side chains are histidines, with pKa 6.0. At pH 8.0, what is the main form of these side chains?","choices":["Mostly neutral","Mostly positive","Half positive","Mostly negative"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Backwards: at pH above the pKa the group has lost its H⁺; positive needs pH below 6.0."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Half-and-half happens only at pH = pKa (6.0), not at 8.0."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Histidine loses its proton to become neutral; its side chain has no negative form."}],"rule":"pH above pKa: the H⁺ is off; pH below pKa: it is on; pH = pKa: half and half.","exp":"pH 8.0 is **two units above** the pKa, so by Henderson–Hasselbalch the neutral (base) form outnumbers the protonated (+1) form **100 : 1**. About 99% of the histidines are neutral. They would be half positive only at pH 6.0 and mostly positive below it; a histidine side chain never turns negative.","how":{"ask":"How far is the pH from the pKa, and which side of it is the pH on?","know":"• **pH = pKa + log([base]/[acid])**.\n• Histidine: acid form = imidazolium (+1); base form = neutral imidazole (0).\n• Each pH unit away from the pKa is a 10-fold change in the ratio.","steps":[{"text":"Pick out the pKa and the pH.","hl":"pKa 6.0"},{"text":"Apply Henderson–Hasselbalch.","calc":["log([base]/[acid]) = pH − pKa","= 8.0 − 6.0 = 2.0","[base]/[acid] = 10² = **100 : 1**","fraction neutral = 100/101 ≈ **99%**"]},{"text":"See it on the titration curve: at pH 8 almost none is protonated.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Histidine side chain protonated (pKa 6.0)","x":{"label":"pH","min":3,"max":9,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Protonated, +1 (%)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Protonated","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[3,99.9],[4,99],[5,91],[5.5,76],[6,50],[6.5,24],[7,9],[8,1],[9,0.1]]}]}},{"text":"Half positive happens only at pH = pKa; mostly positive needs pH below 6.0.","strike":["Half positive","Mostly positive"]},{"text":"Losing its proton leaves histidine neutral, not negative.","strike":["Mostly negative"]},{"text":"Answer: mostly neutral."}],"trap":"Thinking of histidine as a 'basic, positive' residue regardless of pH: above its pKa of 6.0 it is mostly uncharged."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number**.","marks":[{"t":"pKa 6.0","note":"histidine side chain"},{"t":"pH 8.0","note":"two units above"}]},{"text":"**Compare pH with pKa.** pH above pKa → mostly deprotonated; below → mostly protonated.","calc":["8.0 − 6.0 = +2"]},{"text":"**Each unit is a factor of 10.** Two units above the pKa → 100 base : 1 acid.","fig":"histidine-sc","calc":["10² = 100 → ≈ 99% neutral"]},{"text":"**Also:** a side chain's charge changes only when the pH moves past its pKa; acids go 0 → −1, bases go +1 → 0 as pH rises."},{"text":"**Also:** charged side chains repel and keep peptides apart; when the pH neutralizes them, the strands can pack together."},{"text":"**Also:** at a fixed pH, lowering a group's pKa means less of it is protonated, so a structure held together by the protonated form (such as C·C⁺ pairs in an i-motif) is less stable; compare pK values to see which change lowered it.","calc":["pKa 6.5 → 6.0 at pH 6.5: 50% → 24% protonated → less stable"]},{"text":"**Also:** the same pKa-versus-pH test works for a group added to a residue. A grafted –CH₂NH₃⁺ with pKa 9.5 sits about 2 units above pH 7.4, so it stays protonated (+1) and makes the side chain basic.","calc":["9.5 − 7.4 = 2.1 units","[–CH₂NH₂] : [–CH₂NH₃⁺] = 10^(7.4 − 9.5) ≈ 1 : 126","≈ 99% carries +1"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** compare pH with each side chain's pKa; one unit past it is about 90% converted, two units about 99%."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-26a","s":"Fluids","disc":"biology","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:26#1","pe3-cp:50#1","pe3-cp:52#1","pe3-cp:52#2","pe5-cp:28#1","pe5-cp:28#2","pe3-cp:50#2","pe3-cp:50#3"],"q":"In a muscle bed, blood moves at 2.0 cm/s through arterioles with a combined cross-sectional area of 40 cm². The capillaries they feed total 800 cm². What is the capillary blood speed?","choices":["0.10 cm/s","2.0 cm/s","40 cm/s","0.0025 cm/s"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"impossible","why":"Same speed would need the same total area; flow can't be conserved if 800 cm² carries blood at the 40 cm² speed."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Direction: more total area means slower flow (Q = Av), so v must drop below 2.0, not rise."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"Divides the arteriole speed by the capillary area and skips the flow Q = A × v."}],"rule":"Q = A·v is the same in every section, so speed scales by old area ÷ new area: 20 times the area gives 20 times slower flow.","exp":"**Continuity: Q = A·v is the same at every level.** Q = 40 cm² × 2.0 cm/s = 80 cm³/s, so v = 80 ÷ 800 = **0.10 cm/s**. Blood crawls through capillaries because their **total** area is twenty times larger, not because each one is thin.","how":{"ask":"The same volume per second passes the arterioles and the capillaries. How fast must it move through twenty times the total area?","know":"• **Continuity: Q = A·v** (Q = flow rate in cm³/s, A = cross-sectional area, v = mean speed). For blood, Q is the same at every level of the circulation.\n• Vessels in parallel: use their **combined** area.\n• Larger total A → smaller v. Capillaries have the largest total area, so blood is slowest there.","steps":[{"text":"Find the flow rate in the arterioles.","hl":"2.0 cm/s through arterioles with a combined cross-sectional area of 40 cm²","calc":["Q = A × v","Q = 40 cm² × 2.0 cm/s = **80 cm³/s**"]},{"text":"The same Q passes the capillaries; divide by their total area.","hl":"total 800 cm²","calc":["v = Q ÷ A = 80 cm³/s ÷ 800 cm²","v = **0.10 cm/s**","area × 20 → speed ÷ 20"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Same flow (80 cm³/s): speed falls as total area grows","x":{"label":"Total cross-sectional area (cm²)","min":0,"max":800,"step":200},"y":{"label":"Mean speed (cm/s)","min":0,"max":2,"step":0.5},"series":[{"name":"v = Q ÷ A","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[40,2],[80,1],[160,0.5],[400,0.2],[800,0.1]]}]}},{"text":"2.0 cm/s assumes equal flow means equal speed. 40 cm/s treats the thin capillaries like a nozzle and multiplies by 20. 0.0025 cm/s divides the arteriole speed by the capillary area and skips Q.","strike":["2.0 cm/s","40 cm/s","0.0025 cm/s"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.10 cm/s."}],"trap":"Thinking narrow vessels speed blood up. One narrow tube would, but the blood is split among countless capillaries whose combined area is far larger than the arterioles'."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it is.","marks":[{"t":"2.0 cm/s","note":"arteriole speed"},{"t":"40 cm²","note":"arteriole total area"},{"t":"800 cm²","note":"capillary total area"}]},{"text":"**Continuity:** blood is not lost between levels, so the volume per second is the same everywhere.","calc":["Q = A·v = constant"]},{"text":"**Combined area decides the speed.** More total area for the same Q means slower blood.","calc":["v = Q ÷ A"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Speed versus total area at a fixed flow of 80 cm³/s","x":{"label":"Total cross-sectional area (cm²)","min":0,"max":800,"step":200},"y":{"label":"Mean speed (cm/s)","min":0,"max":2,"step":0.5},"series":[{"name":"v = Q ÷ A","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[40,2],[80,1],[160,0.5],[400,0.2],[800,0.1]]}]}},{"text":"**Also:** given n vessels each of area a, total area = n·a, so u = Av/(na).","calc":["n = 1.6 × 10⁹ capillaries, a = 5.0 × 10⁻⁷ cm² each","n·a = 1.6 × 10⁹ × 5.0 × 10⁻⁷ = 800 cm²","u = Av/(na) = (40 × 2.0) ÷ 800 = 0.10 cm/s"]},{"text":"**Also:** for one tube that narrows (no branching), A₁v₁ = A₂v₂ with A = πd²/4, so area goes as d² and v₂ = v₁(d₁/d₂)². Halving the diameter gives 4× the speed; a diameter one-third as wide gives 9×.","calc":["d: 1.2 cm → 0.6 cm, v₁ = 15 cm/s","v₂ = 15 × (1.2/0.6)² = 15 × 4 = 60 cm/s","d₁/d₂ = 3 → (3)² = 9× faster"]},{"text":"**Also:** when a passage gives a tube's diameter instead of its area, halve it to get r and use A = πr². If the speed is in mm/s and the area in μm², multiply the speed by 1000 (1 mm = 1000 μm) before Q = A·v. Using d in place of r makes A four times too big.","calc":["d = 20 μm → r = 10 μm","A = π(10 μm)² ≈ 314 μm²","v = 0.75 mm/s × 1000 = 750 μm/s","Q = 314 μm² × 750 μm/s ≈ 2.4 × 10⁵ μm³/s"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Q = A·v is the same at every level, so speed is smallest where the total cross-sectional area is largest: the capillaries."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-27a","s":"Electrochemistry & Redox","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe1-cp:27#1","pe1-cp:27#2","pe1-cp:27#3"],"q":"On a potential–pH diagram, the boundary between Cu²⁺ and copper metal stays at +0.34 V from pH 0 to 5. Which species is absent from its half-reaction?","choices":["H⁺ and OH⁻","The two electrons","The Cu²⁺ ions","The copper metal"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"A boundary at a set potential is a redox couple, so electrons must appear."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Cu²⁺ is one of the two species the line separates, so it must be in the half-reaction."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Cu metal is one of the two species the line separates, so it must be in the half-reaction."}],"rule":"On a potential–pH diagram, a flat line has electrons but no H⁺/OH⁻; a vertical line has H⁺/OH⁻ but no electrons.","exp":"A **horizontal** boundary keeps the same potential whatever the pH, so the equilibrium **does not depend on pH**: its half-reaction, Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ ⇌ Cu, contains **no H⁺ or OH⁻**. It still transfers electrons, and both copper forms appear in it.","how":{"ask":"Decide what a boundary at one fixed potential across a range of pH says about the half-reaction.","know":"• Pourbaix diagram: potential E (y) versus pH (x); each line is an equilibrium between two forms.\n• **Horizontal line**: E does not change with pH → **pH-independent** → no H⁺ or OH⁻ in the half-reaction, but electrons are transferred.","steps":[{"text":"The boundary is flat: one potential across several pH values.","hl":"stays at +0.34 V from pH 0 to 5","calc":["E constant while pH changes → pH-independent"]},{"text":"Write the half-reaction it represents.","calc":["Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ ⇌ Cu   (E° = +0.34 V)","no H⁺, no OH⁻"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Boundary lines on a simplified iron potential–pH diagram","x":{"label":"pH","min":0,"max":8,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Potential (V)","min":-0.8,"max":1.2,"step":0.4},"series":[{"name":"Fe³⁺/Fe²⁺: horizontal","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0.77],[2,0.77]]},{"name":"Fe³⁺/Fe(OH)₃: vertical","marker":"square-open","line":"straight","points":[[2,0.77],[2,1.2]]},{"name":"Fe(OH)₃/Fe²⁺","marker":"diamond","line":"straight","points":[[2,0.77],[8,-0.29]]},{"name":"Fe²⁺/Fe: horizontal","marker":"triangle","line":"straight","points":[[0,-0.44],[8,-0.44]]}]}},{"text":"A line set at a potential is a redox couple, so electrons must be in it; Cu²⁺ and Cu are the two forms the line separates.","strike":["The two electrons","The Cu²⁺ ions","The copper metal"]},{"text":"Answer: H⁺ and OH⁻."}],"trap":"Thinking a flat line means \"no electrons\": that describes a vertical line. A flat line is a redox couple with no H⁺ or OH⁻."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"potential–pH diagram","note":"E on y, pH on x"},{"t":"stays at +0.34 V","note":"horizontal line"},{"t":"from pH 0 to 5","note":"pH changes, E does not"}]},{"text":"**Read the slope of each boundary.** A flat line sits at one potential, whatever the pH.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Boundary lines on a simplified iron potential–pH diagram","x":{"label":"pH","min":0,"max":8,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Potential (V)","min":-0.8,"max":1.2,"step":0.4},"series":[{"name":"Fe³⁺/Fe²⁺: horizontal","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0.77],[2,0.77]]},{"name":"Fe³⁺/Fe(OH)₃: vertical","marker":"square-open","line":"straight","points":[[2,0.77],[2,1.2]]},{"name":"Fe(OH)₃/Fe²⁺","marker":"diamond","line":"straight","points":[[2,0.77],[8,-0.29]]},{"name":"Fe²⁺/Fe: horizontal","marker":"triangle","line":"straight","points":[[0,-0.44],[8,-0.44]]}]}},{"text":"**Flat = pH has no say.** If changing pH does not move the line, H⁺ and OH⁻ are not in the equilibrium.","calc":["horizontal → electrons yes, H⁺/OH⁻ no"]},{"text":"**Also:** a vertical Pourbaix boundary means the equilibrium depends on pH but transfers no electrons."},{"text":"**Also:** the higher a region lies on a potential–pH diagram, the more oxidized the species stable there."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a horizontal Pourbaix boundary is a redox equilibrium that does not depend on pH (no H⁺ or OH⁻ involved)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-28a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:28#1"],"q":"Phosphorus pentachloride, PCl₅, has five chlorine atoms bonded to a central phosphorus atom that carries no lone pairs. Which hybridization describes the phosphorus?","choices":["dsp³","sp³d²","sp²","sp"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"That set makes six orbitals, for six domains; PCl₅'s phosphorus has five bonds and no lone pair."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"sp² gives 3 orbitals: enough for the 3 equatorial Cl only, not the 2 axial ones."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"sp gives only 2 orbitals; phosphorus needs 5, one for each P–Cl bond."}],"rule":"Count domains on the central atom (bonds + lone pairs); the hybrid set must have exactly that many orbitals.","exp":"Five bonded atoms and no lone pairs = **5 electron domains**, so phosphorus needs **5 hybrid orbitals**: dsp³, also written **sp³d** (1 s + 3 p + 1 d = 5), pointing to the corners of a **trigonal bipyramid**. sp³d² makes 6 (octahedral), sp² makes 3 and sp makes 2.","how":{"ask":"Count phosphorus's electron domains, then pick the hybridization label that makes exactly that many orbitals.","know":"• Hybrid orbitals needed = electron domains (bonded atoms + lone pairs).\n• **2 → sp; 3 → sp²; 4 → sp³ (tetrahedral); 5 → sp³d = dsp³ (trigonal bipyramidal); 6 → sp³d² or d²sp³ (octahedral)**.\n• Count the orbitals in a label: s = 1, then read each superscript for p and d (no superscript = 1).","steps":[{"text":"Count phosphorus's domains: five bonds and no lone pairs.","hl":"five chlorine atoms bonded to a central phosphorus atom that carries no lone pairs","calc":["domains = 5 bonds + 0 lone pairs = **5**","5 domains → **5** hybrid orbitals → trigonal bipyramidal"]},{"text":"Count the orbitals each label makes.","calc":["dsp³ (= sp³d): 1 + 1 + 3 = **5** → trigonal bipyramidal","sp³d²: 1 + 3 + 2 = 6 → octahedral","sp²: 1 + 2 = 3 → trigonal planar","sp: 1 + 1 = 2 → linear"]},{"text":"sp³d² makes six orbitals, which would need a sixth domain; phosphorus's 5 valence electrons all go into the five P–Cl bonds, leaving no lone pair.","strike":["sp³d²"]},{"text":"sp² covers only the three equatorial Cl, and sp only the two axial ones; five bonds need five orbitals.","strike":["sp²","sp"]},{"text":"Answer: dsp³ (sp³d)."}],"trap":"sp³d² also uses d orbitals, so it looks right for any expanded octet, but it makes 6 orbitals: the octahedral set of SF₆, not the 5-orbital trigonal bipyramidal set of PCl₅."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"five chlorine atoms","note":"5 bonded atoms"},{"t":"no lone pairs","note":"0 extra domains"},{"t":"hybridization","note":"how many orbitals mix"}]},{"text":"**Electron domains** = atoms bonded + lone pairs. Each domain needs one hybrid orbital.","calc":["PCl₅: 5 + 0 = 5 domains → 5 orbitals"]},{"text":"**Match the count to a label.**","calc":["4 → sp³ (CH₄, tetrahedral)","5 → sp³d = dsp³ (PCl₅, trigonal bipyramidal)","6 → sp³d² or d²sp³ (SF₆, octahedral)"]},{"text":"**Also:** the order of letters doesn't change the count: dsp³ and sp³d both mix one s, three p and one d orbital; d²sp³ and sp³d² both make six, the octahedral set seen in six-coordinate metal complexes."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** 5 domains → trigonal bipyramidal → sp³d (dsp³); 6 → sp³d²; 4 → sp³."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-29b","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:29#2","pe1-cp:29#1","pe5-cp:15#1","pe5-cp:15#2"],"q":"A sealed flask holds 1.50 mol of nitrogen gas at STP. Assuming ideal behavior, what volume does the gas occupy?","choices":["33.6 L","36.7 L","14.9 L","67.2 L"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Divides 22.4 by 1.50 instead of multiplying; more than 1 mol must fill more than 22.4 L."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Uses 24.5 L/mol, the molar volume at 25 °C; STP is 0 °C."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"N₂ molecules are the gas particles; counting each N atom as a mole doubles the amount."}],"rule":"At STP one mole of ideal gas fills 22.4 L: multiply by the moles. Estimate: 1.5 × 22.","exp":"At **STP (0 °C = 273 K, 1 atm)** one mole of ideal gas fills **22.4 L**, so V = 1.50 mol × 22.4 L/mol = **33.6 L**. 36.7 L is what the sample would fill at 298 K (room temperature), not at STP; 14.9 L divides 22.4 by 1.50 instead of multiplying; 67.2 L uses 3.00 mol, counting the two N atoms in each N₂ as separate moles.","how":{"ask":"What is the molar volume at STP, and how many moles are in the flask?","know":"• **STP = 0 °C (273 K) and 1 atm**. At STP, one mole of an ideal gas takes up **22.4 L**.\n• 22.4 L/mol holds only at 0 °C; at 25 °C (298 K) a mole fills about 24.5 L.\n• Check with PV = nRT: V = nRT/P.","steps":[{"text":"Note the amount and the conditions.","hl":"1.50 mol of nitrogen gas at STP","calc":["n = 1.50 mol","STP: T = 273 K, P = 1 atm"]},{"text":"Use the molar volume at STP.","calc":["V = n × 22.4 L/mol","V = 1.50 mol × 22.4 L/mol","V = **33.6 L**"]},{"text":"Check with PV = nRT.","calc":["V = nRT ÷ P","V = (1.50 mol)(0.0821 L·atm·mol⁻¹·K⁻¹)(273 K) ÷ 1 atm","V = **33.6 L** ✓"]},{"text":"36.7 L uses 298 K (25 °C), room temperature, which is not STP.","calc":["(1.50)(0.0821)(298) ÷ 1 = 36.7 L"],"strike":["36.7 L"]},{"text":"14.9 L divides 22.4 by 1.50 instead of multiplying.","calc":["22.4 ÷ 1.50 = 14.9 L"],"strike":["14.9 L"]},{"text":"67.2 L uses 3.00 mol, counting the two N atoms in each N₂ as separate moles.","calc":["(1.50 × 2) mol × 22.4 L/mol = 67.2 L"],"strike":["67.2 L"]},{"text":"Answer: 33.6 L."}],"trap":"Using 24.5 L/mol, the molar volume at 25 °C, gives 36.7 L. The 22.4 L/mol shortcut belongs to 0 °C only."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it stands for.","marks":[{"t":"1.50 mol","note":"n"},{"t":"STP","note":"0 °C and 1 atm"},{"t":"ideal behavior","note":"use the molar volume"}]},{"text":"**STP** means standard temperature and pressure: **0 °C (273 K) and 1 atm**. There, one mole of any ideal gas fills **22.4 L**.","calc":["at STP: 1 mol ↔ 22.4 L"]},{"text":"**Temperature matters.** Volume grows with T, so at 25 °C a mole fills more.","calc":["0 °C: 22.4 L/mol","25 °C: 22.4 × 298/273 ≈ 24.5 L/mol"]},{"text":"**Also:** a gas is most ideal at high T and low P, where particle volume and intermolecular forces are both negligible."},{"text":"**Also:** run it backward to count moles: at STP, n = V ÷ 22.4 L/mol, the same answer PV/RT gives. Put the volume in litres first: 1 cm³ = 1 mL = 10⁻³ L.","calc":["112 cm³ = 112 mL = 0.112 L","n = 0.112 L ÷ 22.4 L/mol = 0.0050 mol","check: n = PV/RT = (1 atm)(0.112 L) ÷ (0.0821 × 273) = 0.0050 mol ✓"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** V = n × 22.4 L/mol only at STP (0 °C, 1 atm); at any other temperature use PV = nRT."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-30a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:30#1"],"q":"A deep-sea fish sees with a single visual pigment that absorbs most strongly at 490 nm. Which change would most directly give it color vision?","choices":["A pigment with another peak","Twice as much of its pigment","A peak shifted to 550 nm","A narrower absorption band"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"More of the same pigment boosts sensitivity, but one pigment still gives one signal."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Shifting the peak changes which light is caught best, but one pigment still gives one signal."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"A narrower band makes the one pigment more selective; it's still a single signal."}],"rule":"Color vision needs at least two pigments with different peaks, so the brain can compare their signals.","exp":"One pigment only counts absorbed photons, so a dim light at 490 nm and a brighter light elsewhere can give the **same signal**. A **second pigment with a different absorption peak** responds differently to each wavelength, and the brain **compares the two responses** to tell colors apart.","how":{"ask":"With one pigment, can the brain tell a change in color from a change in brightness? What would let it compare?","know":"• A photoreceptor reports only how many photons it absorbs, not their wavelength (**univariance**).\n• **Color vision** needs at least two pigments with **different absorption peaks**; the brain compares their responses (their ratio).\n• More pigment, a shifted peak or a narrower band still leaves one pigment, so still no comparison.","steps":[{"text":"With one pigment, 450-nm and 530-nm light are absorbed equally well, so after matching brightness they give identical signals.","hl":"a single visual pigment","calc":["one pigment → one number (photons absorbed)","color and brightness mixed together"]},{"text":"More pigment raises sensitivity; a shifted peak or a narrower band changes which light is absorbed best. Each still leaves one pigment and one signal.","strike":["Twice as much of its pigment","A peak shifted to 550 nm","A narrower absorption band"]},{"text":"Add a second pigment peaking elsewhere: the two pigments now absorb in a different ratio at each wavelength.","calc":["450 nm: first 0.7, second ≈ 0.0","530 nm: first 0.7, second 0.7","different ratios → different colors"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"The fish pigment and a second pigment with another peak","x":{"label":"Wavelength (nm)","min":400,"max":650,"step":50},"y":{"label":"Relative absorbance","min":0,"max":1,"step":0.2},"series":[{"name":"Fish pigment (490 nm)","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[400,0.1],[430,0.4],[450,0.7],[470,0.92],[490,1],[510,0.92],[530,0.7],[550,0.4],[580,0.1],[600,0.03]]},{"name":"Second pigment (560 nm)","marker":"square-open","line":"smooth","points":[[450,0.01],[480,0.1],[510,0.4],[530,0.7],[545,0.92],[560,1],[575,0.92],[590,0.7],[610,0.4],[640,0.1]]}]}},{"text":"Answer: a pigment with another peak."}],"trap":"Choosing more pigment: it makes the fish more sensitive in dim light, but one pigment, however much of it, cannot separate color from brightness."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"a single visual pigment","note":"only one kind"},{"t":"490 nm","note":"its absorption peak"},{"t":"color vision","note":"telling wavelengths apart"}]},{"text":"**One pigment sends one number:** photons absorbed. A dim light near the peak and a bright light off the peak can give the same number.","calc":["signal = brightness × absorbance"]},{"text":"**Two pigments with different peaks** absorb each wavelength in a different ratio; the brain compares them.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Two pigments, two peaks","x":{"label":"Wavelength (nm)","min":400,"max":650,"step":50},"y":{"label":"Relative absorbance","min":0,"max":1,"step":0.2},"series":[{"name":"Fish pigment (490 nm)","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[400,0.1],[430,0.4],[450,0.7],[470,0.92],[490,1],[510,0.92],[530,0.7],[550,0.4],[580,0.1],[600,0.03]]},{"name":"Second pigment (560 nm)","marker":"square-open","line":"smooth","points":[[450,0.01],[480,0.1],[510,0.4],[530,0.7],[545,0.92],[560,1],[575,0.92],[590,0.7],[610,0.4],[640,0.1]]}]}},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** color vision comes from comparing the responses of pigments with different absorption peaks; one pigment alone cannot tell wavelength from brightness."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-31a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:31#1","pe1-cp:31#2","pe4-cp:36#1","pe4-cp:36#2"],"q":"An insect odorant-binding protein holds a small hydrocarbon odorant, shown, inside a buried cavity. Which residue would most likely line that cavity?","fig":"limonene","choices":["Valine","Glutamate","Arginine","Threonine"],"choiceFigs":["valine-sc","glutamate-sc","arginine-sc","threonine-sc"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Charged: glutamate's –COO⁻ at pH 7 wants water, not a hydrocarbon pocket."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Charged: arginine's guanidinium is + at pH 7, so it faces water."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Threonine is small and uncharged, but its –OH makes it polar."}],"rule":"A pocket that holds a hydrocarbon is lined with nonpolar side chains (Leu, Ile, Val, Phe): no charge, no OH.","exp":"A hydrocarbon odorant is **nonpolar**, so it binds best in a **hydrophobic pocket** whose side chains are also hydrocarbon. Valine's isopropyl group contains only C and H and packs against the odorant (like dissolves like). Glutamate (–COO⁻) and arginine (guanidinium, +) are charged, and threonine's –OH is polar; all three would rather face water.","how":{"ask":"Is the odorant polar or nonpolar, and which side chain is itself pure hydrocarbon?","know":"• **Nonpolar side chains** (hydrophobic): Ala, **Val, Leu, Ile**, Met, **Phe**, Trp, Pro.\n• **Polar uncharged**: Ser, **Thr**, Asn, Gln, Tyr, Cys. **Charged**: Asp, **Glu** (−); Lys, **Arg**, His (+).\n• Like dissolves like: a hydrocarbon ligand is buried among hydrocarbon side chains.","steps":[{"text":"The ligand is a hydrocarbon: only C and H, so it is nonpolar.","hl":"a small hydrocarbon odorant","calc":["C–H bonds share electrons almost evenly → **nonpolar**"]},{"text":"It sits in a buried cavity, away from water, so the lining must be hydrophobic too.","hl":"inside a buried cavity"},{"text":"Valine's side chain is all C and H: it fits.","calc":["Val: –CH(CH₃)₂ → nonpolar ✓"]},{"text":"Glutamate carries –COO⁻ and arginine a positive guanidinium at pH 7: charges want water. Threonine's –OH is polar.","strike":["Glutamate","Arginine","Threonine"]},{"text":"Answer: valine."}],"trap":"Threonine looks harmless because it is small and uncharged, but its –OH makes it polar; a pocket for a pure hydrocarbon is lined with Leu, Ile, Val and Phe."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"hydrocarbon odorant","note":"only C and H → nonpolar"},{"t":"buried cavity","note":"away from water"},{"t":"line that cavity","note":"side chains facing the ligand"}]},{"text":"**Polar vs nonpolar:** O and N pull electrons and make polar or charged groups; C–H bonds are nonpolar. Hydrocarbons mix with hydrocarbons, not with water."},{"text":"**The hydrophobic four** to remember for pockets: side chains made only of carbon and hydrogen.","calc":["Leu: –CH₂CH(CH₃)₂","Ile: –CH(CH₃)CH₂CH₃","Val: –CH(CH₃)₂","Phe: –CH₂C₆H₅"]},{"text":"**Also:** charged or polar side chains don't fit a pocket for a nonpolar ligand; swapping in Asp, Glu, Lys, Arg, Ser or Thr weakens binding."},{"text":"**Also:** when a nonpolar part of a drug sits in a water-free pocket, its main interaction is hydrophobic contact (London dispersion) with nonpolar side chains such as Leu, Ile, Val and Phe, not hydrogen bonds or salt bridges."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a nonpolar ligand binds in a hydrophobic pocket lined with Leu, Ile, Val and Phe."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-32a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:32#1","pe1-cp:32#2"],"q":"ATP is synthesized with ³³P in its β-phosphate only. After hexokinase converts glucose to glucose 6-phosphate with this ATP, where is the label found?","choices":["In ADP","In glucose 6-phosphate","In free phosphate","In AMP"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Only the γ phosphate is handed to glucose; the β-labelled one doesn't move."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A kinase hands a phosphate to its substrate; it releases no free phosphate."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"AMP would need both β and γ removed; a kinase moves only one phosphate."}],"rule":"A kinase moves only ATP's terminal (γ) phosphate; the α and β stay on the leftover nucleotide.","exp":"Hexokinase, like every kinase, transfers ATP's **terminal (γ) phosphate** to its substrate. The **α and β phosphates stay** behind as ADP, so a label on β ends up in **ADP**, not in glucose 6-phosphate.","how":{"ask":"Find which of ATP's three phosphates the kinase moves, then see where the β phosphate ends up.","know":"• ATP phosphates, from the ribose outward: **α, β, γ**.\n• Kinase: ATP + R–OH → **ADP + R–O–PO₃²⁻**; only **γ** moves.\n• ADP keeps α and β; AMP would keep only α.","steps":[{"text":"The label is on β, the middle phosphate.","hl":"³³P in its β-phosphate only"},{"text":"Write the kinase reaction.","calc":["glucose + ATP(α–β*–γ) → glucose–O–P(γ) + ADP(α–β*)","label (*) stays in **ADP**"]},{"text":"Only γ reaches the glucose, and no phosphate is released free.","strike":["In glucose 6-phosphate","In free phosphate"]},{"text":"AMP would form only if β and γ left together, which a kinase does not do.","strike":["In AMP"]},{"text":"Answer: in ADP."}],"trap":"Assuming any phosphorus label in ATP is passed on: a kinase moves only the γ phosphate."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"β-phosphate","note":"the middle one"},{"t":"hexokinase","note":"a kinase"},{"t":"glucose 6-phosphate","note":"gets one phosphate"}]},{"text":"**Name ATP's phosphates** from the sugar out: α, β, γ.","fig":"atp"},{"text":"**A kinase moves the end one.** The γ phosphate goes to the substrate; α and β stay as ADP.","calc":["ATP → ADP + γ-phosphate (to substrate)"]},{"text":"**Also:** a kinase is the catalyst; the phosphate it attaches comes from ATP's γ phosphate."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** kinases transfer ATP's γ (terminal) phosphate; any label on α or β stays in ADP."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-33a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:33#1","pe1-cp:33#2","pe6-cp:13#1","pe6-cp:13#2"],"q":"In propanenitrile, CH₃CH₂C≡N, the highlighted nitrile carbon is bonded to two atoms and has no lone pairs. What are its hybridization and geometry?","fig":"cpt-fix-propanenitrile-c-hl","choices":["sp; linear","sp²; trigonal planar","sp³; tetrahedral","sp²; bent"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"sp² needs three domains, as on a C=C or C=O carbon; a triple bond leaves only two."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Four bond lines aren't four domains: the triple bond counts once, so this carbon has 2 domains, not 4."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Bent needs a lone pair on the central atom; the nitrile carbon has none."}],"rule":"Count domains: each single, double or triple bond is one, plus lone pairs; 2 domains line up, 3 are trigonal, 4 tetrahedral.","exp":"The nitrile carbon is bonded to **two atoms** (the CH₂ carbon and N) and has no lone pairs: **two domains**, because the C≡N triple bond counts once. Two domains → **sp**, **linear**, 180°. Its two unhybridized p orbitals form the two π bonds of C≡N. sp² needs three domains (trigonal planar, or bent with a lone pair); sp³ needs four (tetrahedral).","how":{"ask":"Count the electron domains on the nitrile carbon and convert them to a hybridization and shape.","know":"• Electron domains = bonded atoms + lone pairs (a double or triple bond counts once).\n• **2 → sp**, linear, 180°; **3 → sp²**, trigonal planar, 120° (with one lone pair: bent); **4 → sp³**, tetrahedral, 109.5°.\n• Each π bond uses one unhybridized p orbital: a triple bond needs two, so its carbons are sp.","steps":[{"text":"Count the domains on the nitrile carbon.","hl":"bonded to two atoms and has no lone pairs","fig":"cpt-fix-propanenitrile-c-hl","calc":["domains = 2 atoms (C, N) + 0 lone pairs = **2**","C≡N counts once, not three times"]},{"text":"Two domains → two sp hybrid orbitals, 180° apart.","calc":["2 domains → **sp**","angle = 180° → **linear**"]},{"text":"sp³ counts the four bond lines (one C–C, three C≡N) as four domains; a triple bond is one domain.","strike":["sp³; tetrahedral"]},{"text":"sp² needs three domains, as on a C=C or C=O carbon; bent would also need a lone pair on the carbon, which it lacks.","strike":["sp²; trigonal planar","sp²; bent"]},{"text":"Answer: sp; linear."}],"trap":"Thinking any multiple-bonded carbon is sp². A double bond leaves three domains (sp²), but a triple bond leaves only two (sp, linear)."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"C≡N","note":"triple bond: one domain"},{"t":"two atoms","note":"2 bonded groups"},{"t":"no lone pairs","note":"nothing extra"}]},{"text":"**Electron domains** are the regions of electrons around an atom: each bonded atom counts once (single, double or triple bond), and each lone pair counts once."},{"text":"**Domains → hybridization → shape.**","calc":["2 → sp → linear (C≡N, C≡C, O=C=O)","3 → sp² → trigonal planar (C=C, C=O)","4 → sp³ → tetrahedral"]},{"text":"**Also:** CO₂'s carbon works the same way: two O atoms, two double bonds, each counted once, no lone pair → 2 domains → sp, linear, 180°."},{"text":"**Also:** cis/trans isomers exist because a C=C cannot rotate without breaking its π bond."},{"text":"**Also:** a Schiff base is an imine, R₂C=N–R′, made when an amine condenses with an aldehyde or ketone. Count its C=N carbon like an alkene carbon: two single-bonded neighbors plus the N, with the double bond counted once, and no lone pair. So it is sp², trigonal planar, with angles near 120°.","calc":["imine C: 3 atoms + 0 lone pairs = 3 → sp², ≈120°","imine N: 2 atoms + 1 lone pair = 3 → sp² as well"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** count atoms + lone pairs, with any multiple bond once: 2 domains (a C≡N or C≡C carbon, CO₂) → sp, linear, 180°; 3 → sp², 120°; 4 → sp³."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-34b","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:34#2","pe1-cp:34#1"],"q":"Pancreatic lipase acts on the triacylglycerol drawn, freeing fatty acids from the glycerol backbone. Which kind of bond does the enzyme hydrolyze?","choices":["Ester bond","Ether bond","Glycosidic bond","Phosphodiester bond"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"An ether has no carbonyl beside its O; here the O on glycerol sits next to a C=O."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A glycosidic bond links a sugar's anomeric carbon; glycerol and fatty acids contain no sugar ring."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"A phosphodiester needs phosphate; a triacylglycerol has no P (that's a phospholipid or DNA)."}],"rule":"Look at the O's neighbours: a C=O beside it is the acyl link lipases cut; no sugar rules out glycosidic, no P rules out phosphate links.","exp":"Each fatty acid is joined to a glycerol –OH through **C(=O)–O–C**, an **ester**. Lipase adds water across that bond (ester + H₂O → carboxylic acid + alcohol), freeing the fatty acid. An ether has no carbonyl, a glycosidic bond links a sugar, and a phosphodiester needs a phosphate; none of those is in a triacylglycerol's acyl links.","how":{"ask":"Find how each fatty acid is attached to glycerol, then name that link.","know":"• A triacylglycerol = glycerol + 3 fatty acids, each attached as **R–C(=O)–O–CH₂** (an **ester**).\n• **Lipase** = esterase for fats: ester + H₂O → fatty acid + glycerol –OH.\n• Ether: C–O–C, no C=O. Glycosidic: sugar–O–R. Phosphodiester: R–O–P(=O)(O⁻)–O–R.","steps":[{"text":"Each chain is attached to glycerol through a carbonyl carbon bonded to O.","hl":"triacylglycerol","fig":"triacylglycerol","calc":["R–C(=O)–O–CH₂– → **ester**","R–C(=O)–O–CH₂– + H₂O → R–COOH + HO–CH₂–"]},{"text":"The O on glycerol sits next to a C=O, so it is not an ether, which has no carbonyl on either side.","strike":["Ether bond"]},{"text":"No sugar and no phosphorus are present, so there is nothing to make a glycosidic or phosphodiester link.","strike":["Glycosidic bond","Phosphodiester bond"]},{"text":"Answer: an ester bond."}],"trap":"Seeing the C–O–C at glycerol and calling it an ether: the carbonyl on the acyl carbon makes it an ester."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Pancreatic lipase","note":"hydrolyzes fat esters"},{"t":"freeing fatty acids","note":"each leaves as R–COOH"}]},{"text":"**Number glycerol's carbons** 1, 2, 3; each holds one acyl chain as an ester.","fig":"triacylglycerol"},{"text":"**Hydrolysis splits the ester.** Water's OH goes to the carbonyl carbon, its H to the glycerol oxygen.","calc":["R–CO–O–R′ + H₂O → R–COOH + HO–R′"]},{"text":"**Also:** pancreatic lipase cuts only the esters at positions 1 and 3, freeing two fatty acids and leaving the chain on C2: a 2-monoacylglycerol.","calc":["3 chains − 2 cut = 1 left, on C2"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** lipases hydrolyze the ester bonds of triacylglycerols, freeing fatty acids; pancreatic lipase cuts at positions 1 and 3."}],"fig":"triacylglycerol"},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-35b","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:35#2","pe1-cp:35#1"],"q":"Four residues of a soluble protein are replaced: Lys→Glu, Arg→Asp, Lys→Asp and Ala→His. What is the change in the protein's net charge at pH 7?","choices":["A decrease of 6","A decrease of 5","No change","An increase of 6"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Sign: + residues (Lys, Arg) become − ones (Glu, Asp), so the net charge must fall."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Counts His as +1, but with a pKa near 6 it is mostly neutral at pH 7."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Each Lys/Arg → Glu/Asp swap is +1 → −1, a change of −2, so the total can't be zero."}],"rule":"At pH 7 Lys and Arg are +1, Asp and Glu −1, His about 0; add up each swap's change (new minus old).","exp":"At pH 7, Lys and Arg are **+1**, Glu and Asp are **−1**, and Ala and His are **≈ 0**. Each basic-to-acidic swap goes from +1 to −1, a change of **−2**; there are three of them. Ala→His changes nothing. Total: −2 − 2 − 2 + 0 = **−6**.","how":{"ask":"For each substitution, subtract the old side-chain charge from the new one at pH 7, then add the four changes.","know":"• Charges at pH 7: Arg/Lys **+1**, Asp/Glu **−1**, His **≈ 0**, others **0**.\n• Change at each site = **new − old**.\n• Net change = sum of the site changes.","steps":[{"text":"List the swaps.","hl":"Lys→Glu, Arg→Asp, Lys→Asp and Ala→His"},{"text":"Compute new − old at each site.","fig":["lysine-sc","arginine-sc","glutamate","aspartate-sc","histidine-sc"],"calc":["Lys→Glu: (−1) − (+1) = **−2**","Arg→Asp: (−1) − (+1) = **−2**","Lys→Asp: (−1) − (+1) = **−2**","Ala→His: 0 − 0 = **0**"]},{"text":"Add them.","calc":["Δ = −2 + (−2) + (−2) + 0 = **−6**"]},{"text":"Counting His as +1 gives −2 − 2 − 2 + 1 = −5, but at pH 7 His is ≈ 0.","strike":["A decrease of 5"]},{"text":"Seeing three charged residues swapped for three charged residues as no change ignores the sign: each swap trades +1 for −1, a change of −2.","strike":["No change"]},{"text":"Positive charges are replaced by negative ones, so the net charge falls; an increase has the sign flipped.","strike":["An increase of 6"]},{"text":"Answer: a decrease of 6 (Δ = −6)."}],"trap":"Counting His as +1 at pH 7: its side-chain pKa is about 6, so at pH 7 it is mostly neutral and Ala→His adds nothing."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Four residues","note":"four sites to add"},{"t":"pH 7","note":"use pH 7 charges"},{"t":"net charge","note":"sum of changes"}]},{"text":"**Assign charges at pH 7.**","calc":["Lys, Arg: +1","Glu, Asp: −1","Ala, His: 0"]},{"text":"**Change per site = new − old.** A + → − swap is worth 2.","calc":["(−1) − (+1) = −2"]},{"text":"**Also:** at pH 7, count Arg and Lys as +1, Asp and Glu as −1, and His and everything else as 0."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for each substitution take new charge minus old charge at the stated pH, then add all the site changes."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-36a","s":"Reading Data","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe1-cp:36#1","pe1-cp:36#2"],"q":"After 15 min at 55 °C and cooling, wild-type lipase kept 30% of its activity, mutant A kept 65% and mutant B kept 25%. Which conclusion fits?","choices":["Only mutant A is more stable","Both mutants are more stable","Only mutant B is more stable","Neither mutant is more stable"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Mutant B kept 25%, below the wild type's 30%, so B is less stable, not more."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"B kept 25% vs 30% for wild type: less activity left means less stable, not more."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Mutant A kept 65% vs the wild type's 30%, clearly more activity left, so a mutant did gain stability."}],"rule":"Compare each mutant with the wild type on its own: more activity left after heating means more stable.","exp":"Retained activity is the measure of heat stability: more activity left means less damage. Mutant A kept **65% vs the wild type's 30%**, so it is more stable. Mutant B kept **25%**, slightly **less** than the wild type, so it is not more stable.","how":{"ask":"Line up each mutant's retained activity against the wild type's and decide which are higher.","know":"• **% activity kept after heating**: higher → more heat-stable.\n• Compare every variant with the **wild type**, the reference.\n• A conclusion about \"both\" fails if even one variant does not fit.","steps":[{"text":"Find the reference and each variant's value.","hl":"wild-type lipase kept 30% of its activity, mutant A kept 65% and mutant B kept 25%"},{"text":"Compare each with the wild type.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Activity kept after heating","x":{"label":"Enzyme"},"y":{"label":"Activity kept (%)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"categories":["Wild type","Mutant A","Mutant B"],"series":[{"name":"Kept","values":[30,65,25]}]},"calc":["A: 65% > 30% → **more stable**","B: 25% ≤ 30% → **not more stable**"]},{"text":"Mutant B kept less than the wild type, so it is neither more stable alone nor along with A.","strike":["Both mutants are more stable","Only mutant B is more stable"]},{"text":"Mutant A clearly kept more, so it is wrong that neither is more stable.","strike":["Neither mutant is more stable"]},{"text":"Answer: only mutant A is more stable."}],"trap":"Assuming every mutant behaves alike: check each one against the wild type separately."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it is.","marks":[{"t":"55 °C","note":"heating step"},{"t":"30%","note":"wild type, the reference"},{"t":"65%","note":"mutant A"},{"t":"25%","note":"mutant B"}]},{"text":"**Heat, cool, assay.** The activity that survives measures how well the enzyme resists heat.","calc":["% kept ↑ → heat stability ↑"]},{"text":"**Subtract the reference.**","calc":["A: 65 − 30 = +35","B: 25 − 30 = −5"]},{"text":"**Also:** keep only the claim the numbers show; reject absolute words like all, fully or every when any activity remains."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** compare each variant's retained activity with the wild type's; higher means more heat-stable, lower means less."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-37a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:37#1","pe6-cp:7#2"],"q":"A urine sample from patient A has a pH of 5.8, and a sample from patient B has a pH of 7.8. How does [H⁺] in sample B compare with sample A?","choices":["1/100 as much","100 times as much","1/2 as much","1/20 as much"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Direction: higher pH means less H⁺, and B has the higher pH."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"Multiplies 2 × 10 instead of raising 10 to the power 2; each pH unit is its own factor of ten."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"pH is a log scale; a 2-unit gap is a power of ten, not a factor of 2."}],"rule":"Each pH unit is a factor of 10 in [H⁺], and higher pH means less H⁺: the factor is 10 raised to the pH difference.","exp":"Because **pH = −log[H⁺]**, one pH unit corresponds to a **tenfold** change in [H⁺]. Sample B is 7.8 − 5.8 = 2 units higher, so its [H⁺] is 10² = 100 times lower, **1/100 as much**: about 1.6 × 10⁻⁸ M versus 1.6 × 10⁻⁶ M.","how":{"ask":"Find the pH difference, turn it into a power of ten, and decide whether a higher pH means more or less H⁺.","know":"• **pH = −log[H⁺]** → [H⁺] = 10^(−pH).\n• A difference of Δ pH units = a factor of **10^Δ** in [H⁺].\n• **Higher pH = less H⁺**.","steps":[{"text":"Find the pH change from A to B.","hl":"a pH of 5.8","calc":["ΔpH = 7.8 − 5.8 = **2** units"]},{"text":"Convert to a factor and check with real concentrations.","hl":"a pH of 7.8","calc":["factor = 10² = **100**","pH 5.8: [H⁺] = 10^−5.8 ≈ 1.6 × 10⁻⁶ M","pH 7.8: [H⁺] = 10^−7.8 ≈ 1.6 × 10⁻⁸ M","1.6 × 10⁻⁶ ÷ 1.6 × 10⁻⁸ = **100**"],"strike":["1/2 as much","1/20 as much"]},{"text":"Direction: B has the higher pH, so it has less H⁺.","strike":["100 times as much"]},{"text":"Answer: 1/100 as much."}],"trap":"\"1/2 as much\" treats pH as linear; every pH unit is a tenfold change, so 2 units is 10 × 10 = 100."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the numbers** and what you must compare.","marks":[{"t":"a pH of 5.8","note":"sample A"},{"t":"a pH of 7.8","note":"sample B"},{"t":"[H⁺]","note":"H⁺ concentration"}]},{"text":"**pH counts powers of ten:** [H⁺] = 10^(−pH).","calc":["pH 5 → 10⁻⁵ M","pH 6 → 10⁻⁶ M (10 times less)"]},{"text":"**Subtract, then raise 10 to that power.** Then set the direction: higher pH, less H⁺.","calc":["factor = 10^(ΔpH)"]},{"text":"**Also:** compare any solution with pure water (pH 7.0 at 25 °C) the same way. Each pH unit above 7 divides [H⁺] by 10, and since [H⁺][OH⁻] = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ stays fixed, [OH⁻] grows by that same factor. A fractional gap works too: 10 raised to the gap.","calc":["pH 9.0 vs water: gap 2.0 → [H⁺] 100 times lower","[OH⁻] = 10⁻¹⁴ ÷ 10⁻⁹ = 10⁻⁵ M, 100 times water's 10⁻⁷ M","gap 0.5 → 10^0.5 ≈ 3.2"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a change of Δ pH units changes [H⁺] by a factor of 10^Δ, and a lower pH means more H⁺."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-38b","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:38#2","pe1-cp:38#1","pe1-cp:38#3"],"q":"A glucopyranose has a phosphate on the ring carbon that is bonded to C5 but not to the ring oxygen. What is the compound?","choices":["Glucose 4-phosphate","Glucose 6-phosphate","Glucose 3-phosphate","Glucose 1-phosphate"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"C6 is bonded to C5 but sits outside the ring as the CH₂OH, so it is not a ring carbon."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"C3 isn't bonded to C5; it's two carbons away around the ring."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"C1 is bonded to the ring O (the anomeric carbon), which the stem rules out."}],"rule":"Number the pyranose from C1, the carbon on the ring O; C5 also touches the ring O, and C6 hangs outside the ring.","exp":"C5 is bonded to the ring O, to **C6** (the –CH₂OH outside the ring) and to **C4**. The only other **ring** carbon on C5 is C4, so the phosphate is on C4: **glucose 4-phosphate**. A sugar phosphate is named by the number of the carbon that holds the phosphate.","how":{"ask":"Which ring carbon is next to C5 on the side away from the ring O, and what number is it?","know":"• Ring order: O – C1 – C2 – C3 – C4 – C5 – O.\n• C5's neighbors: ring O, C4 (ring), C6 (outside).\n• Name = glucose **n**-phosphate, where n is the carbon carrying the phosphate.","steps":[{"text":"The clue locates the phosphate relative to C5.","hl":"bonded to C5 but not to the ring oxygen"},{"text":"List C5's neighbors.","fig":"alpha-glucopyranose","calc":["C5 → ring O, **C4**, C6","ring carbon among them: **C4**"]},{"text":"C6 is bonded to C5 but sits outside the ring, so it is not a ring carbon.","strike":["Glucose 6-phosphate"]},{"text":"C3 is two carbons from C5, and C1 is bonded to the ring O.","strike":["Glucose 3-phosphate","Glucose 1-phosphate"]},{"text":"Answer: glucose 4-phosphate."}],"trap":"Reaching for glucose 6-phosphate because it is the familiar one: C6 is bonded to C5 but is the CH₂OH outside the ring."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"ring carbon","note":"C1–C5 only"},{"t":"bonded to C5","note":"C4 or C6"},{"t":"not to the ring oxygen","note":"rules out C1"}]},{"text":"**Walk the ring.** Every carbon number follows from the ring order.","calc":["O – C1 – C2 – C3 – C4 – C5 – O","C6 hangs off C5"]},{"text":"**Name by carbon number.** The number in a sugar phosphate's name is the carbon bearing the phosphate.","fig":"alpha-glucopyranose"},{"text":"**Also:** in glucopyranose, C1 is anomeric, C2–C4 are ring carbons, C5 is bonded to the ring O, and C6 is the CH₂OH."},{"text":"**Also:** always put the phosphate where the passage says, then number the ring from C1; do not default to C6."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** number the ring from C1, find the carbon that carries the phosphate, and name it glucose n-phosphate."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-39b","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:39#2","pe1-cp:39#1","pe1-cp:43#1"],"q":"Glycogen's branching enzyme cuts a short block of glucose residues from a chain end and reattaches it to an interior residue. Which bond attaches the moved block to the interior residue?","choices":["α-1,6","α-1,4","β-1,4","β-1,6"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"α-1,4 is the linear chain bond, the one that was cut, not the branch-point link."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Glycogen contains only α links, so a β bond is out."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"β again: glycogen's glucose units are joined only by α bonds."}],"rule":"Glycogen uses only α links: α-1,4 inside chains, and a branch joins at the CH₂OH carbon of an interior residue.","exp":"The branching enzyme breaks an α-1,4 bond near the chain end and joins the released block's C1 to the **C6 –OH** of an interior glucose. That **α-1,6** bond is a new branch point. α-1,4 bonds make the linear chain, and glycogen has no β links.","how":{"ask":"When a block is moved to an interior residue to start a branch, which carbon of that residue does it bond to?","know":"• **Branching enzyme**: cuts an α-1,4 bond and forms an **α-1,6** bond (a branch point).\n• **Glycogen synthase**: forms α-1,4 bonds (chain growth).\n• β links appear in cellulose, not glycogen.","steps":[{"text":"The block is moved onto an interior residue: a new branch.","hl":"reattaches it to an interior residue"},{"text":"A branch attaches through C6.","calc":["block C1 → interior C6","→ **α-1,6** branch point"]},{"text":"α-1,4 is the bond that was cut, the linear chain bond made by glycogen synthase.","strike":["α-1,4"]},{"text":"Glycogen is built only from α links.","strike":["β-1,4","β-1,6"]},{"text":"Answer: α-1,6."}],"trap":"Choosing α-1,4 because the block is a piece of chain: inside the block the bonds are α-1,4, but the new bond to the interior residue is α-1,6."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"branching enzyme","note":"makes branch points"},{"t":"interior residue","note":"bonds through C6"}]},{"text":"**Two bonds in glycogen.** α-1,4 runs along a chain; α-1,6 starts a branch.","fig":"alpha-glucopyranose","calc":["chain: C1–O–C4","branch: C1–O–C6"]},{"text":"**The branching enzyme cuts one and makes the other.**","calc":["break α-1,4 → form α-1,6"]},{"text":"**Also:** glycogen synthase forms α-1,4 bonds; the branching enzyme forms α-1,6 bonds."},{"text":"**Also:** branch points are α-1,6 bonds, so fewer branches means fewer α-1,6 bonds."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** the branching enzyme forms α-1,6 bonds, one at each branch point."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-39c","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:39#3"],"q":"On a Haworth drawing of a D-glucose residue, its CH₂OH points up while C1's link to the next residue points down. How is this glycosidic link classified?","choices":["α, as in glycogen","β, as in glycogen","α, as in cellulose","β, as in cellulose"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Mismatch: glycogen is α-linked, so a β link can't be \"as in glycogen\"."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Mismatch: cellulose is β-linked, so an α link can't be \"as in cellulose\"."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"β would need the C1 bond on the same face as CH₂OH; here they point opposite ways."}],"rule":"C1 bond on the face opposite CH₂OH is α (glycogen, starch); on the same face is β (cellulose).","exp":"For a D-sugar in a Haworth drawing, compare the anomeric (C1) bond with the CH₂OH: **opposite faces → α**, same face → β. Here CH₂OH is up and the C1 bond is down, so the link is **α**, the type found in glycogen and starch. Cellulose uses β links.","how":{"ask":"Is the C1 bond on the same face as the CH₂OH or on the opposite face?","know":"• **D-sugar, Haworth**: C1 substituent **opposite** CH₂OH → **α**; **same** face → **β**.\n• Glycogen and starch: α-1,4 (+ α-1,6). Cellulose: β-1,4.","steps":[{"text":"Read the two directions.","hl":"its CH₂OH points up while C1's link to the next residue points down"},{"text":"Opposite faces → α.","fig":["t1-maltose","t1-cello-beta"],"calc":["CH₂OH up, C1 bond down → opposite → **α**"]},{"text":"β would need the C1 bond on the same face as CH₂OH.","strike":["β, as in glycogen","β, as in cellulose"]},{"text":"Cellulose is built from β links, so an α link is not cellulose's.","strike":["α, as in cellulose"]},{"text":"Answer: α, as in glycogen."}],"trap":"Matching α with cellulose: α links make glycogen and starch; cellulose is β-1,4."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"CH₂OH points up","note":"the reference"},{"t":"C1's link to the next residue points down","note":"the anomeric bond"}]},{"text":"**Compare C1 with CH₂OH.** Opposite faces = α; same face = β.","fig":["alpha-glucopyranose","beta-glucopyranose"]},{"text":"**Match to the polymer.** Glycogen and starch are α-linked; cellulose is β-linked.","calc":["α-1,4: glycogen, starch","β-1,4: cellulose"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** on a Haworth projection, a C1 bond opposite the CH₂OH is α and one on the same face is β."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-40a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:40#1","pe1-cp:40#2"],"q":"In lactose synthesis, UDP-galactose donates its galactose. Apart from galactose, which sugar ring is part of UDP-galactose?","choices":["Ribofuranose","Deoxyribofuranose","Glucopyranose","Fructofuranose"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"UDP has no \"d\" prefix, so its sugar keeps the 2′ –OH: it is not deoxyribose."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A nucleotide's own sugar is a five-carbon pentose; glucose is a six-carbon hexose."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Fructose is a hexose too; a nucleotide's sugar is always ribose or deoxyribose."}],"rule":"A nucleotide's own sugar is a pentose: ribose in UDP, ATP and the like; only the 'd' nucleotides (dATP, dTTP) use deoxyribose.","exp":"UDP is **uridine diphosphate**. Uridine is uracil bonded to **ribose**, a five-membered (furanose) ring with –OH groups on C2′ and C3′. UDP is an RNA-type nucleotide, so its sugar is ribose, not deoxyribose; glucose and fructose are not part of it.","how":{"ask":"What does UDP stand for, and which sugar is in that nucleoside?","know":"• **UDP** = uridine diphosphate; **uridine** = uracil + **ribose**.\n• Nucleotides without a \"d\" (ATP, UTP, UDP, CTP) carry **ribose**.\n• Deoxyribose appears in DNA nucleotides (dATP, dTTP…).","steps":[{"text":"The molecule is galactose attached to UDP.","hl":"UDP-galactose"},{"text":"Expand the name.","fig":["uracil","ribose"],"calc":["UDP = uridine diphosphate","uridine = uracil + **ribose** (furanose)"]},{"text":"UDP has no \"d\" prefix, so its sugar keeps the 2′ –OH: it is not deoxyribose.","strike":["Deoxyribofuranose"]},{"text":"Glucose and fructose are not part of a uridine nucleotide.","strike":["Glucopyranose","Fructofuranose"]},{"text":"Answer: ribofuranose."}],"trap":"Picking glucose because UDP-sugars are usually UDP-glucose: the attached sugar varies, but the nucleotide's own sugar is always ribose."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"UDP-galactose","note":"galactose + UDP"},{"t":"Apart from galactose","note":"the nucleotide's sugar"}]},{"text":"**Read UDP.** Uridine diphosphate: uracil + ribose + two phosphates.","fig":"ribose","calc":["U = uridine","D = di (2 phosphates)","P = phosphate"]},{"text":"**Ribose vs deoxyribose.** Ribose has –OH on C2′ and C3′; deoxyribose lacks the 2′ –OH.","fig":["t1-ribose-c2oh","t1-dr-c2"]},{"text":"**Also:** deoxyribose is found in DNA nucleotides such as dTTP; nucleotides without the \"d\" carry ribose."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** UDP is uridine diphosphate, and uridine contains ribose."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-41a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe1-cp:41#1","pe1-cp:41#2"],"q":"A phosphorylated enzyme carries ³²P in its phosphoserines and ³H in its leucines. It is incubated with a protein phosphatase. Which label remains on the enzyme?","choices":["³H only","³²P only","Both labels","Neither label"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Backwards: the phosphatase removes exactly the phosphate that carries ³²P, so ³²P can't be what's left."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A phosphatase strips the phosphate, so ³²P can't still be there alongside ³H."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Losing ³H would mean cutting out leucines; a phosphatase only cleaves phosphate esters, not the protein."}],"rule":"A phosphatase removes only phosphate groups (a kinase adds them); labels anywhere else on the protein stay.","exp":"A phosphatase **hydrolyzes phosphate esters**, releasing the phosphate as free Pᵢ. The ³²P rides on that phosphate, so it leaves the enzyme. The ³H is built into the leucine side chains, which the phosphatase does not touch, so **³H is the label that stays**.","how":{"ask":"Place each label on its chemical group, then ask which group the phosphatase leaves alone.","know":"• **Phosphatase**: R–O–PO₃²⁻ + H₂O → R–OH + Pᵢ (removes phosphate).\n• A radioactive label goes wherever its atom goes.\n• Kinase adds phosphate; phosphatase takes it off.","steps":[{"text":"Locate each label.","hl":"³²P in its phosphoserines and ³H in its leucines","calc":["³²P → phosphate groups","³H → leucine side chains"]},{"text":"The phosphatase cuts off the phosphate.","calc":["Ser–O–PO₃²⁻(³²P) + H₂O → Ser–OH + Pᵢ(³²P)","³²P **leaves**; ³H **stays**"]},{"text":"Removing the phosphate takes ³²P with it, so ³²P cannot remain, alone or alongside ³H.","strike":["³²P only","Both labels"]},{"text":"Leucine is untouched, so ³H is not lost.","strike":["Neither label"]},{"text":"Answer: ³H only."}],"trap":"Mixing up kinase and phosphatase: a kinase adds phosphate (³²P would stay or grow); a phosphatase removes it, leaving only labels on the rest of the protein."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"³²P in its phosphoserines","note":"label on phosphate"},{"t":"³H in its leucines","note":"label on the protein"},{"t":"protein phosphatase","note":"removes phosphate"}]},{"text":"**Each isotope tags one part.** Phosphorus is only in the phosphate; hydrogen here is in leucine.","fig":"leucine"},{"text":"**Phosphatase = phosphate off.** The phosphate leaves as free Pᵢ.","calc":["phospho-protein + H₂O → protein + Pᵢ"]},{"text":"**Also:** a phosphatase removes only phosphate, so a ¹⁴C label in the sugar backbone stays with the sugar."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a phosphatase removes phosphate groups, so a ³²P label on them leaves while labels elsewhere (³H in leucine, ¹⁴C in a sugar) stay."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-42b","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:42#2","pe1-cp:42#1","pe4-cp:49#1","pe4-cp:49#2"],"q":"Strontium-90 and iodine-131, two fission products in reactor waste, are both β⁻ emitters. Which daughter nuclides do they form?","choices":["Yttrium-90 and xenon-131","Rubidium-90 and tellurium-131","Yttrium-90 and tellurium-131","Krypton-86 and antimony-127"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Both daughters sit one place left (lower Z), which is β⁺ emission; β⁻ raises Z by one."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Strontium's daughter is fine, but tellurium is one place left of iodine; β⁻ moves one place right."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Mass numbers fell by 4, the mark of α decay; β⁻ emission leaves A unchanged."}],"rule":"β⁻ turns a neutron into a proton: the mass number stays, Z rises by one, so step one place right on the periodic table.","exp":"β⁻ decay keeps **A** and raises **Z by 1**. Sr (Z = 38) → **Y (39)**, A stays 90; I (Z = 53) → **Xe (54)**, A stays 131. Rubidium and tellurium lower Z (β⁺), and krypton-86 with antimony-127 lose 4 mass units (α).","how":{"ask":"Apply A unchanged, Z + 1 to each parent, then name the element with the new Z.","know":"• **β⁻ decay**: A unchanged, **Z + 1**.\n• Z sets the element: Kr 36, Rb 37, Sr 38, Y 39; Sb 51, Te 52, I 53, Xe 54.","steps":[{"text":"Both parents are β⁻ emitters: keep A, add 1 to Z.","hl":"both β⁻ emitters","calc":["A → A, Z → Z + 1"]},{"text":"Strontium-90 (Z = 38).","hl":"Strontium-90","calc":["⁹⁰₃₈Sr → ⁹⁰₃₉Y + ⁰₋₁e + ν̄","Z: 38 = 39 + (−1) ✓   A: 90 = 90 ✓"]},{"text":"Iodine-131 (Z = 53).","hl":"iodine-131","calc":["¹³¹₅₃I → ¹³¹₅₄Xe + ⁰₋₁e + ν̄","Z: 53 = 54 + (−1) ✓   A: 131 = 131 ✓"]},{"text":"Rubidium and tellurium have Z − 1 (positron emission); one correct daughter paired with tellurium is still wrong; krypton-86 and antimony-127 are alpha products.","strike":["Rubidium-90 and tellurium-131","Yttrium-90 and tellurium-131","Krypton-86 and antimony-127"]},{"text":"Answer: yttrium-90 and xenon-131."}],"trap":"Moving one step left on the periodic table (rubidium, tellurium): that is β⁺ decay. β⁻ moves one step right."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the parents and the decay type**.","marks":[{"t":"Strontium-90","note":"Z = 38, A = 90"},{"t":"iodine-131","note":"Z = 53, A = 131"},{"t":"β⁻ emitters","note":"A same, Z + 1"}]},{"text":"**Keep the mass number.** The emitted electron carries no nucleons.","calc":["daughters: A = 90 and A = 131"]},{"text":"**Step one element to the right.** Z + 1 is the next element in the periodic table.","calc":["Sr (38) → Y (39)","I (53) → Xe (54)"]},{"text":"**Also:** in β⁻ decay one neutron turns into a proton, emitting the electron and an antineutrino, so the daughter has one more proton and one fewer neutron.","calc":["n → p + e⁻ + antineutrino","Sr-90: 38 p, 52 n → Y-90: 39 p, 51 n"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for β⁻ decay, keep A and move one element right (Z + 1) to name the daughter."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-44c","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:44#3","pe1-cp:44#1","pe1-cp:44#2"],"q":"Arsenic acid, H₃AsO₄, is triprotic. Which of these acids dissociates to the smallest extent when dissolved in water?","choices":["HAsO₄²⁻","H₂AsO₄⁻","H₃AsO₄","HCl"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"HCl is a strong acid: it dissociates completely, the most, not the least."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"The neutral parent acid always has the largest Ka (Ka1) of a polyprotic series."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Its Ka2 is weaker than Ka1 but stronger than the next step, so it isn't the weakest."}],"rule":"In a polyprotic series each step is weaker: the more negative the species, the less it gives up H⁺; strong acids dissociate fully.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Titration of 30.0 mL of 0.050 M H₃AsO₄ with 0.10 M NaOH","x":{"label":"Volume of NaOH added (mL)","min":0,"max":45,"step":5},"y":{"label":"pH","min":0,"max":14,"step":2},"series":[{"name":"pH","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,1.8],[3,2],[7.5,2.4],[12,2.9],[14,3.4],[15,4.6],[16,5.9],[18,6.4],[22.5,7],[27,7.6],[29,8.1],[30,9.2],[31,10.3],[33,10.8],[37.5,11.3],[42,11.7],[45,11.8]]}]},"exp":"HCl is a strong acid. Arsenic acid's three Ka values fall steeply (pKa ≈ 2.2, 7.0, 11.5) because each H⁺ comes off a more negative ion. **HAsO₄²⁻** must lose H⁺ from a 2− ion, so it has the smallest Ka and dissociates least, just as HPO₄²⁻ does in the phosphate series.","how":{"ask":"Rank the four by Ka: the strong acid first, then each successive arsenic-acid step.","know":"• A strong acid (HCl) ionizes completely.\n• Triprotic acid: H₃A → H₂A⁻ → HA²⁻ → A³⁻, with Ka₁ ≫ Ka₂ ≫ Ka₃.\n• The most negative species that still has an H⁺ is the weakest acid.","steps":[{"text":"HCl is a strong acid: it dissociates completely.","strike":["HCl"]},{"text":"Write the three arsenic-acid steps.","hl":"is triprotic","calc":["H₃AsO₄ ⇌ H⁺ + H₂AsO₄⁻   pKa₁ ≈ 2.2","H₂AsO₄⁻ ⇌ H⁺ + HAsO₄²⁻   pKa₂ ≈ 7.0","HAsO₄²⁻ ⇌ H⁺ + AsO₄³⁻   pKa₃ ≈ 11.5"],"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"pH","x":7.5,"text":"buffer 1","at":"above"},{"series":"pH","x":22.5,"text":"pH 7.0 = pKa₂","at":"left"},{"series":"pH","x":37.5,"text":"buffer 3","at":"below"}]}},{"text":"Each step is about 10⁴–10⁵ times weaker than the one before, because H⁺ leaves an ever more negative ion.","calc":["Ka₁ ≈ 6 × 10⁻³ > Ka₂ ≈ 1 × 10⁻⁷ > Ka₃ ≈ 3 × 10⁻¹²"],"strike":["H₃AsO₄","H₂AsO₄⁻"]},{"text":"Answer: HAsO₄²⁻."}],"trap":"H₃AsO₄ is the weak acid named in the stem, but it is the strongest of the three arsenic species; its ions are weaker still."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"triprotic","note":"three H⁺ to lose"},{"t":"smallest extent","note":"weakest acid, smallest Ka"}]},{"text":"**Strong acids are out:** HCl ionizes completely, so it dissociates most."},{"text":"**Line up the arsenic species by charge:** neutral H₃AsO₄, then 1− H₂AsO₄⁻, then 2− HAsO₄²⁻. The more negative the ion, the more tightly it holds H⁺.","calc":["0 → 1− → 2−","Ka₁ ≫ Ka₂ ≫ Ka₃"]},{"text":"**Also:** HCl and H₂SO₄ (first H⁺) are strong acids; HF, H₃PO₄ and H₂CO₃ are weak."},{"text":"**Also:** each successive Ka of a polyprotic acid is much smaller, because H⁺ is harder to remove from a more negative ion."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in a polyprotic series, the most negative ion that still carries an H⁺ (HPO₄²⁻, HAsO₄²⁻, HCO₃⁻) is the weakest acid."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-46a","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:46#1"],"q":"Carbon-14 undergoes beta-minus (β⁻) decay to nitrogen-14. Besides an antineutrino, which particle leaves the nucleus?","choices":["An electron","A neutron","A proton","A gamma photon"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"impossible","why":"Losing a neutron would drop A by 1, but A stays at 14 from C-14 to N-14."},{"c":2,"tag":"impossible","why":"Losing a proton lowers Z, but Z rises from 6 (C) to 7 (N)."},{"c":3,"tag":"impossible","why":"A gamma photon has no charge, so it can't carry off the −1 that lets Z rise from 6 to 7."}],"rule":"Balance A and Z on both sides: β⁻ keeps A and raises Z by one, so the emitted particle has A = 0 and charge −1.","exp":"In **β⁻ decay** a neutron turns into a proton, and the nucleus throws out an **electron** (plus an antineutrino): ¹⁴₆C → ¹⁴₇N + ⁰₋₁e. A stays 14 and Z rises by 1, so the charge carried off is −1 with no mass number: an electron. Losing a neutron or proton would change A, and a gamma photon carries no charge, so it could not change the element.","how":{"ask":"Balance A and Z across the decay; the missing particle has whatever A and charge are left over.","know":"• **α** = ⁴₂He: A − 4, Z − 2.\n• **β⁻** = electron (⁰₋₁e): a neutron → proton, Z + 1, A same.\n• **β⁺** = positron (⁰₊₁e): Z − 1, A same.\n• **γ** = photon (⁰₀γ): A and Z unchanged.\n• A and Z (charge) must both balance across a nuclear equation.","steps":[{"text":"Write the decay with the unknown particle.","hl":"Carbon-14 undergoes beta-minus (β⁻) decay to nitrogen-14","calc":["¹⁴₆C → ¹⁴₇N + ᴬ_Z X"]},{"text":"Balance mass number and charge.","calc":["A: 14 = 14 + A → A = **0**","Z: 6 = 7 + Z → Z = **−1**","A = 0, charge −1 → ⁰₋₁e, an **electron**"]},{"text":"A neutron or a proton carries mass number 1, so A would fall to 13; a proton would also lower Z instead of raising it.","strike":["A neutron","A proton"]},{"text":"A gamma photon has A = 0 but no charge, so it cannot carry off the −1 that lets Z rise from 6 to 7.","strike":["A gamma photon"]},{"text":"Answer: an electron."}],"trap":"Thinking a decay that keeps A the same must emit a photon. β⁻ also keeps A, but Z changes, so a charged particle, the electron, must leave."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find what changes and what stays**.","marks":[{"t":"Carbon-14","note":"A = 14, Z = 6"},{"t":"nitrogen-14","note":"A = 14, Z = 7"},{"t":"beta-minus (β⁻)","note":"an electron leaves"}]},{"text":"**Track A and Z for each emission.**","calc":["α (⁴He): A − 4, Z − 2","β⁻ (electron): Z + 1","β⁺ (positron): Z − 1","γ (photon): no change"]},{"text":"**Inside β⁻ decay** a neutron becomes a proton; the electron created carries the charge away.","calc":["n → p⁺ + e⁻ + ν̄","¹⁴₆C → ¹⁴₇N + ⁰₋₁e + ν̄"]},{"text":"**Also:** gamma emission is the one decay that changes neither A nor Z: an excited nucleus drops to a lower state and releases a photon, which has no mass and no charge (X* → X + γ)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** β⁻ decay emits an electron (Z + 1, A same); β⁺ emits a positron (Z − 1); α emits ⁴He; γ emits a photon and changes neither A nor Z."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-47c","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:47#3","pe1-cp:47#1","pe1-cp:47#2","pe4-cp:1#1","pe4-cp:1#2","pe4-cp:1#3"],"q":"A reaction that releases a gas is spontaneous only above a certain temperature. Which signs of ΔH and ΔS fit ΔG = ΔH − TΔS?","choices":["ΔH positive, ΔS positive","ΔH negative, ΔS negative","ΔH negative, ΔS positive","ΔH positive, ΔS negative"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Sign flipped: releasing a gas raises entropy, so ΔS can't be negative."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Gas formed means ΔS is positive; this choice has the sign flipped, and with ΔH positive it would never be spontaneous."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"ΔH negative with ΔS positive gives ΔG < 0 at every temperature, so there's no threshold."}],"rule":"Gas formed means ΔS > 0. Spontaneous only at high T: ΔS helps, ΔH opposes; only at low T: the reverse; always: both help.","exp":"Spontaneous means **ΔG < 0**, with **ΔG = ΔH − TΔS**. Releasing a gas raises entropy, so **ΔS > 0** and the −TΔS term grows more negative as T rises. Being nonspontaneous at low T, where ΔG ≈ ΔH, requires **ΔH > 0**. ΔH < 0 with ΔS > 0 would be spontaneous at every temperature; ΔH > 0 with ΔS < 0 at none; both negative only at low T.","how":{"ask":"Which sign pair makes ΔG positive at low T but negative at high T?","know":"• **ΔG = ΔH − TΔS**; spontaneous when ΔG < 0.\n• At low T the ΔH term decides; at high T the −TΔS term decides.\n• A gas formed → **ΔS > 0**.\n• ΔH −, ΔS + → always; ΔH +, ΔS − → never; both + → high T only; both − → low T only.","steps":[{"text":"A gas is released, so entropy rises.","hl":"releases a gas","calc":["gas formed → ΔS > 0","−TΔS < 0, larger as T rises"]},{"text":"Not spontaneous at low T means ΔG > 0 there, where ΔG ≈ ΔH; at high T the −TΔS term wins.","hl":"spontaneous only above a certain temperature","calc":["low T: ΔG ≈ ΔH > 0 → ΔH > 0","high T: TΔS > ΔH → ΔG < 0"]},{"text":"A negative ΔH with a positive ΔS gives ΔG < 0 at every temperature, so there is no threshold.","calc":["ΔG = (−) − T(+) < 0 at all T"],"strike":["ΔH negative, ΔS positive"]},{"text":"A negative ΔS contradicts the gas release: with ΔH positive it is never spontaneous, and with ΔH negative it is spontaneous only below some temperature.","calc":["(+) − T(−) > 0 at all T","(−) − T(−) < 0 only at low T"],"strike":["ΔH positive, ΔS negative","ΔH negative, ΔS negative"]},{"text":"Answer: ΔH positive, ΔS positive."}],"trap":"Choosing ΔH negative with ΔS positive because both terms favor spontaneity: that pair is spontaneous at every temperature, with no threshold to cross."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"releases a gas","note":"ΔS > 0"},{"t":"only above a certain temperature","note":"ΔG < 0 at high T only"},{"t":"ΔG = ΔH − TΔS","note":"the sign test"}]},{"text":"**ΔG = ΔH − TΔS.** At low T the ΔH term dominates; as T rises, the −TΔS term takes over."},{"text":"**Four sign pairs.**","calc":["ΔH −, ΔS +: always spontaneous","ΔH +, ΔS −: never spontaneous","ΔH +, ΔS +: spontaneous above some T","ΔH −, ΔS −: spontaneous below some T"]},{"text":"**Also:** spontaneous means ΔG < 0, however slow the reaction."},{"text":"**Also:** a reaction that makes gas from a solid or solution has ΔS > 0."},{"text":"**Also:** for an endothermic reaction (ΔH > 0) with ΔS > 0, ΔG = ΔH − TΔS stays positive, so the reaction is non-spontaneous, as long as ΔH is greater than TΔS. It becomes spontaneous once TΔS > ΔH, i.e. above T = ΔH/ΔS = 90 ÷ 0.20 = 450 K.","calc":["ΔH = +90 kJ/mol, ΔS = +0.20 kJ/(mol·K), T = 300 K","TΔS = 300 × 0.20 = 60 kJ/mol < ΔH","ΔG = 90 − 60 = +30 kJ/mol → non-spontaneous","T > 90 ÷ 0.20 = 450 K → TΔS > ΔH → ΔG < 0"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** by ΔG = ΔH − TΔS, a reaction that is spontaneous only at high temperature has both ΔH and ΔS positive."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-48a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:48#1","pe1-cp:48#2","pe3-cp:29#1","pe3-cp:29#2","pe3-cp:29#3"],"q":"Chlorine atoms destroy ozone in two steps: Cl + O₃ → ClO + O₂, then ClO + O → Cl + O₂. What is the overall reaction?","choices":["O₃ + O → 2O₂","Cl + O₃ + O → Cl + 2O₂","O₃ + ClO → Cl + 2O₂","2O₃ → 3O₂"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"Cl appears on both sides and was never cancelled; a species on both sides drops out of a net equation."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"Keeps ClO, an intermediate (formed, then consumed), and drops the O atom; the steps weren't fully added."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Balanced, but it uses no free O atom, so it isn't the sum of these two steps."}],"rule":"Add the steps, then cancel anything on both sides (catalysts and intermediates); the net equation shows neither.","exp":"Add the two steps and cross out anything on both sides: Cl + O₃ + ClO + O → ClO + O₂ + Cl + O₂ becomes **O₃ + O → 2O₂**. Cl is used and then regenerated (a catalyst), and ClO is made and then used (an intermediate), so neither appears overall. Leaving Cl on both sides is an unfinished sum; O₃ + ClO → Cl + 2O₂ drops the O-atom reactant and leaves ClO, an intermediate, uncancelled; 2O₃ → 3O₂ is a different net reaction.","how":{"ask":"Add the steps, then cancel each species that appears on both sides.","know":"• **Overall reaction** = sum of the elementary steps (multiply a step if needed so shared species cancel).\n• Cancel species found on both sides: **catalysts** (used, then regenerated; a regenerated cofactor such as enzyme-bound FAD → FADH₂ → FAD cancels like a catalyst) and **intermediates** (made, then used).\n• What's left holds only the true reactants and products.","steps":[{"text":"Write both steps and add them.","hl":"Cl + O₃ → ClO + O₂, then ClO + O → Cl + O₂","calc":["Cl + O₃ → ClO + O₂","ClO + O → Cl + O₂","sum: Cl + O₃ + ClO + O → ClO + O₂ + Cl + O₂"]},{"text":"Cancel Cl and ClO, each found on both sides, and combine the O₂.","calc":["cancel Cl (left and right)","cancel ClO (left and right)","**O₃ + O → 2O₂**","check: O atoms 3 + 1 = 4 = 2 × 2; charge 0 = 0 ✓"],"fig":"cpt-cl-cycle"},{"text":"Leaving Cl on both sides is a sum that was never simplified.","strike":["Cl + O₃ + O → Cl + 2O₂"]},{"text":"O₃ + ClO → Cl + 2O₂ drops the O-atom reactant and leaves ClO, an intermediate, uncancelled.","strike":["O₃ + ClO → Cl + 2O₂"]},{"text":"2O₃ → 3O₂ is balanced, but it uses no free O atom, so it is not the sum of these steps.","strike":["2O₃ → 3O₂"]},{"text":"Answer: O₃ + O → 2O₂."}],"trap":"Cl + O₃ + O → Cl + 2O₂ is balanced and looks complete, but a species on both sides must be cancelled; an overall equation never shows a catalyst."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"two steps","note":"a mechanism"},{"t":"Cl + O₃ → ClO + O₂","note":"step one"},{"t":"ClO + O → Cl + O₂","note":"step two"},{"t":"overall reaction","note":"sum and cancel"}]},{"text":"**A mechanism** is the list of elementary steps a reaction actually goes through. Adding them gives the overall equation."},{"text":"**Cancel like terms**, just as in algebra: a species on both sides drops out.","calc":["A + B → C","C + D → A + E","sum: B + D → E"]},{"text":"**Also:** after summing a mechanism, confirm that atoms and total charge (coefficient × charge) balance on both sides."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** add the steps, then cancel every species on both sides (catalysts and intermediates) to get the overall reaction."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-48c","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:48#3"],"q":"A little FeSO₄ (Fe²⁺) speeds up the oxidation of iodide by persulfate: S₂O₈²⁻ + 2Fe²⁺ → 2SO₄²⁻ + 2Fe³⁺, then 2Fe³⁺ + 2I⁻ → 2Fe²⁺ + I₂. Which species is the catalyst?","choices":["Fe²⁺","Fe³⁺","I⁻","SO₄²⁻"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Fe³⁺ is made in step one and used up in step two: that's an intermediate."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"I⁻ is only used up, so it stays in the overall equation; a catalyst is given back."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"SO₄²⁻ is only formed, a product; a catalyst must be present at the start."}],"rule":"Catalyst: used in an early step and given back later. Intermediate: made first, used later. Both cancel overall.","exp":"**Fe²⁺ is used in the first step and regenerated in the second**, so it is the catalyst: present at the start and given back at the end. **Fe³⁺ is made in the first step and used up in the second**: an intermediate. I⁻ is a reactant and SO₄²⁻ a product; neither cancels. Overall: S₂O₈²⁻ + 2I⁻ → 2SO₄²⁻ + I₂.","how":{"ask":"For each iron ion, is it consumed first and returned later, or made first and consumed later?","know":"• **Catalyst**: consumed in an early step, **regenerated** later; it is present before the reaction starts.\n• **Intermediate**: **produced** in an early step, consumed later; it is never added at the start.\n• Both cancel from the overall equation; reactants and products do not.","steps":[{"text":"Track Fe²⁺: a reactant in step one, a product in step two.","hl":"S₂O₈²⁻ + 2Fe²⁺ → 2SO₄²⁻ + 2Fe³⁺","calc":["step one: 2Fe²⁺ used","step two: 2Fe²⁺ made → **regenerated** → catalyst"],"fig":"cpt-fe-cycle"},{"text":"Track Fe³⁺: made in step one, used up in step two, so it is an intermediate.","hl":"2Fe³⁺ + 2I⁻ → 2Fe²⁺ + I₂","strike":["Fe³⁺"]},{"text":"I⁻ is only consumed and SO₄²⁻ only produced: a reactant and a product, which stay in the overall equation.","calc":["overall: S₂O₈²⁻ + 2I⁻ → 2SO₄²⁻ + I₂"],"strike":["I⁻","SO₄²⁻"]},{"text":"Answer: Fe²⁺."}],"trap":"Fe³⁺ also cancels from the overall equation, but it is made first and used later: that is an intermediate. A catalyst enters first as a reactant and is given back."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"A little FeSO₄ (Fe²⁺)","note":"added at the start"},{"t":"S₂O₈²⁻ + 2Fe²⁺","note":"step one reactants"},{"t":"2Fe²⁺ + I₂","note":"step two products"}]},{"text":"**Catalyst: in, then out.** It enters an early step as a reactant and leaves a later step as a product, unchanged overall."},{"text":"**Intermediate: out, then in.** It is made in an early step and used up in a later one.","calc":["catalyst: reactant (step one) → product (step two)","intermediate: product (step one) → reactant (step two)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a catalyst is used and then regenerated; an intermediate is made and then used up. Both cancel from the overall equation."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-49b","s":"Electrochemistry & Redox","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:49#2","pe1-cp:49#1","pe1-cp:49#3"],"q":"A catalytic converter treats exhaust containing carbon monoxide and unburned propane. Taken together, which products does it form from these pollutants?","choices":["CO₂ and H₂O","CO and H₂","CH₃OH and H₂","C and H₂O"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"copied","why":"CO is one of the pollutants named in the stem; a converter removes it, so it can't be the product."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Methanol plus H₂ is only partial oxidation; with O₂ the hydrogen leaves as water."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Soot is carbon left unoxidized; the converter takes carbon all the way to +4."}],"rule":"A catalytic converter oxidizes fully: every carbon ends as carbon dioxide and every hydrogen as water.","exp":"The converter **oxidizes** with O₂: carbon monoxide goes to **CO₂** (carbon +2 → +4), and a hydrocarbon such as propane burns completely to **CO₂ and H₂O**. Soot, CO, methanol or H₂ would be incomplete or reductive products.","how":{"ask":"What do CO and a hydrocarbon become when fully oxidized by O₂ over the catalyst?","know":"• The converter's oxidation job: **CO → CO₂** and **hydrocarbons → CO₂ + H₂O**.\n• Oxidation of carbon raises its oxidation number; full oxidation of carbon ends at +4 (CO₂).","steps":[{"text":"Note the two pollutants.","hl":"carbon monoxide and unburned propane","calc":["CO and C₃H₈"]},{"text":"Oxidize each with O₂ and balance.","calc":["2CO + O₂ → 2CO₂   (C: +2 → +4)","C₃H₈ + 5O₂ → 3CO₂ + 4H₂O"]},{"text":"CO and H₂, or soot and water, are incomplete products: carbon not taken all the way to +4 and H₂ not oxidized; the converter oxidizes fully to CO₂ and H₂O.","strike":["CO and H₂","C and H₂O"]},{"text":"Methanol plus H₂ is a partial, not complete, oxidation and is not what leaves the tailpipe.","strike":["CH₃OH and H₂"]},{"text":"Answer: CO₂ and H₂O."}],"trap":"Forgetting the hydrogen in a hydrocarbon: complete oxidation gives water as well as carbon dioxide."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the pollutants**.","marks":[{"t":"carbon monoxide","note":"CO, C = +2"},{"t":"unburned propane","note":"hydrocarbon C₃H₈"}]},{"text":"**Oxidation side of the converter:** O₂ in the exhaust finishes burning anything carbon-based.","calc":["CO → CO₂","CₓHᵧ → CO₂ + H₂O"]},{"text":"**Check by oxidation number:** carbon in CO is +2 and in CO₂ is +4, so carbon loses electrons: oxidation."},{"text":"**Also:** a solid catalyst acting on gases (a catalytic converter) is a heterogeneous catalyst."},{"text":"**Also:** a catalytic converter reduces nitrogen oxides (NOₓ) to N₂ while it oxidizes CO and hydrocarbons."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a catalytic converter oxidizes CO to CO₂ and unburned hydrocarbons to CO₂ and H₂O."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-51a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe1-cp:51#1","pe1-cp:51#2","pe1-cp:51#3"],"q":"Equal masses of manganese dioxide catalyst, one as a single pellet and one as fine powder, are added to identical hydrogen peroxide solutions. Why does the powder release O₂ faster?","choices":["More exposed surface","Greater catalyst mass","Higher activation energy","Larger equilibrium constant"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Backwards: a catalyst lowers Ea, and a higher Ea would slow the reaction, not speed it."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"The masses are stated to be equal, so mass can't explain a rate difference."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"A catalyst never changes K, and K tells how far a reaction goes, not how fast."}],"rule":"A solid catalyst works at its surface: same mass in smaller pieces exposes more area and gives a faster rate; K never changes.","exp":"A solid catalyst works only at its **surface**, where H₂O₂ molecules meet it. Powder exposes far **more surface area** for the same mass, so more reaction sites act at once and the **rate rises**. The mass is equal, a catalyst lowers (not raises) Ea, and catalysts don't change K.","how":{"ask":"What differs between the pellet and the powder, given that their masses match?","know":"• A **heterogeneous catalyst reacts at its surface**.\n• Breaking a solid into small pieces greatly **raises surface area** without changing mass.\n• More surface → more active sites → **faster rate**.","steps":[{"text":"Note what is the same and what differs.","hl":"Equal masses of manganese dioxide catalyst","calc":["mass: same","form: pellet vs fine powder"]},{"text":"The masses are equal, so mass can't explain the difference.","strike":["Greater catalyst mass"]},{"text":"Powder exposes more surface where H₂O₂ meets the catalyst, so more molecules react per second.","calc":["surface area ↑ → active sites ↑ → rate ↑"]},{"text":"A catalyst lowers Ea, and a higher Ea would slow the reaction; neither form changes K.","strike":["Higher activation energy","Larger equilibrium constant"]},{"text":"Answer: more exposed surface."}],"trap":"Thinking grinding adds catalyst: the amount is unchanged; only the surface the reactants can reach grows."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find what was changed**.","marks":[{"t":"Equal masses","note":"mass held fixed"},{"t":"fine powder","note":"more surface"},{"t":"release O₂ faster","note":"rate"}]},{"text":"**Surface catalysis:** reactant molecules must touch the solid to react, so only surface atoms count."},{"text":"**Smaller pieces, more surface.** Cutting a solid exposes new faces that were inside before.","calc":["particle size ↓ → surface area ↑ → rate ↑"]},{"text":"**Also:** grinding or cutting a catalyst raises its surface area but leaves its mass unchanged."},{"text":"**Also:** cutting a cube into smaller cubes multiplies the total surface area by the ratio of the edge lengths."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** grinding a solid catalyst speeds the reaction because it exposes more surface area."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-52a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe1-cp:52#1","pe1-cp:52#2","pe1-cp:52#3"],"q":"A 9.0-V battery powers a 30-Ω heater coil. Which replacement coil would draw the largest current from the battery?","choices":["A 12-Ω coil","A 45-Ω coil","A 60-Ω coil","A 90-Ω coil"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"45 Ω is more than the present 30 Ω, so by I = V/R it lowers the current."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"60 Ω is twice the present 30 Ω, so by I = V/R it halves the current."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"I = V/R: at fixed V a bigger R gives a smaller current, and 90 Ω is the biggest R offered."}],"rule":"At fixed voltage I = V/R: more resistance means less current.","exp":"By **Ohm's law, I = V/R**, a fixed battery voltage drives more current through a smaller resistance. The 12-Ω coil draws 9.0 V ÷ 12 Ω = **0.75 A**, more than the 0.30 A now.","how":{"ask":"With the battery voltage fixed, which resistance gives the biggest I = V/R?","know":"• **Ohm's law: I = V/R**.\n• Fixed V → current is **inversely proportional** to resistance: lower R, more current.","steps":[{"text":"The battery sets V; only R changes.","hl":"A 9.0-V battery","calc":["I = V ÷ R"]},{"text":"Current now.","hl":"30-Ω heater coil","calc":["I = 9.0 V ÷ 30 Ω = **0.30 A**"]},{"text":"Compare the replacements.","calc":["12 Ω: 9.0 ÷ 12 = **0.75 A**","45 Ω: 9.0 ÷ 45 = 0.20 A","60 Ω: 9.0 ÷ 60 = 0.15 A","90 Ω: 9.0 ÷ 90 = 0.10 A"]},{"text":"The larger coils all lower the current below 0.30 A.","strike":["A 45-Ω coil","A 60-Ω coil","A 90-Ω coil"]},{"text":"Answer: the 12-Ω coil."}],"trap":"Thinking a bigger resistor \"uses more\" current. At fixed voltage, more resistance means less current."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the givens**.","marks":[{"t":"9.0-V battery","note":"V fixed"},{"t":"30-Ω heater coil","note":"R now"},{"t":"largest current","note":"goal"}]},{"text":"**Ohm's law:** the battery's push divided by the resistance gives the current.","calc":["I = V ÷ R"]},{"text":"**Smaller R, bigger I.** Halve R and the current doubles.","calc":["R ↓ → I ↑"]},{"text":"**Also:** a longer, thinner or hotter metal wire has more resistance; shorter, thicker or cooler has less."},{"text":"**Also:** lowering the ion concentrations a cell runs on lowers its voltage, and at fixed R that lowers the current."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to raise the current from a fixed voltage, lower the resistance (I = V/R)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-53a","s":"Electrochemistry & Redox","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:53#1"],"q":"Standard reduction potentials: Mg²⁺/Mg, −2.37 V; Ag⁺/Ag, +0.80 V. Strips of magnesium and silver are each dropped into 1 M HCl. Which produces H₂ gas?","choices":["Magnesium only","Silver only","Both magnesium and silver","Neither metal"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Backwards: a positive E° means silver holds its electrons more tightly than H₂ does, so it can't reduce H⁺."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Includes silver; E° = +0.80 V means Ag stays unreacted in 1 M HCl."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Mg's E° is far below 0 V, so it reduces H⁺ readily; at least one metal reacts."}],"rule":"A metal frees H₂ from acid only if its E° is below 0 V, the H⁺/H₂ value.","exp":"H⁺/H₂ is the **zero reference**. Magnesium's E° is **negative** (−2.37 V), so Mg is more easily oxidized than H₂: Mg → Mg²⁺ while **H⁺ gains electrons and becomes H₂**. Silver's E° is **positive**, so Ag holds its electrons and does not reduce H⁺; no gas forms on it.","how":{"ask":"For each metal, is it easier to oxidize than H₂ (E° below that of H⁺/H₂)?","know":"• Reference: **2H⁺ + 2e⁻ → H₂, E° = 0 V**.\n• Metal with **E° < 0** is oxidized by H⁺: M → Mⁿ⁺ and H⁺ → H₂.\n• Metal with **E° > 0** does not react with H⁺.","steps":[{"text":"Note both potentials.","hl":"Mg²⁺/Mg, −2.37 V","calc":["Mg: −2.37 V","Ag: +0.80 V","H⁺/H₂: 0 V"]},{"text":"Magnesium: E° < 0, so Mg is easier to oxidize than H₂ and reduces H⁺.","calc":["Mg + 2H⁺ → Mg²⁺ + H₂"]},{"text":"E° > 0: silver is harder to oxidize than H₂, so it cannot reduce H⁺.","strike":["Silver only","Both magnesium and silver"]},{"text":"Magnesium does react, so it is not the case that neither metal gives gas.","strike":["Neither metal"]},{"text":"Answer: magnesium only."}],"trap":"Thinking any metal dissolves in acid: only metals with E° below 0 V (more active than H₂) reduce H⁺ to H₂."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the potentials**.","marks":[{"t":"−2.37 V","note":"Mg: below 0"},{"t":"+0.80 V","note":"Ag: above 0"},{"t":"1 M HCl","note":"source of H⁺"},{"t":"H₂ gas","note":"H⁺ must be reduced"}]},{"text":"**H⁺/H₂ sits at 0 V.** A metal below it gives electrons to H⁺; a metal above it doesn't.","calc":["E°(metal) < 0 → metal oxidized, H⁺ → H₂"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a metal more easily oxidized than H₂ (E° < 0) is oxidized by acid and H⁺ is reduced to H₂ gas."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-54a","s":"Electrochemistry & Redox","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:54#1","pe1-cp:54#2"],"q":"Standard reduction potentials: Fe²⁺/Fe, −0.44 V; Ni²⁺/Ni, −0.25 V. An iron nail is placed in nickel(II) sulfate solution. Which species is oxidized?","choices":["Iron metal","Nickel metal","Iron(II) ions","Nickel(II) ions"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Fe²⁺ isn't present at the start; it is what iron becomes, the product of the oxidation, not its reactant."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Backwards: Ni²⁺ gains electrons to become nickel metal, so it is reduced, not oxidized."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Nickel metal isn't in the beaker at the start (only Ni²⁺); it can only be a product."}],"rule":"The couple with the more negative E° runs as oxidation: its solid metal loses electrons, and its ion is the product.","exp":"The half-reaction with the **lower reduction potential runs backward** as oxidation. Iron's E° (−0.44 V) is lower than nickel's (−0.25 V), so **Fe → Fe²⁺ + 2e⁻**, and Ni²⁺ is reduced to nickel metal.","how":{"ask":"Which half-reaction has the lower E°, and so runs as an oxidation?","know":"• **Higher E°** → reduced (cathode). **Lower E°** → oxidized (anode).\n• Only the reduced form of a couple (the metal) can be oxidized; the ion is already oxidized.","steps":[{"text":"Compare the two potentials.","hl":"Fe²⁺/Fe, −0.44 V","calc":["Fe²⁺/Fe: −0.44 V (lower)","Ni²⁺/Ni: −0.25 V (higher)"]},{"text":"Lower E° is oxidized: iron metal gives up electrons.","calc":["Fe → Fe²⁺ + 2e⁻"]},{"text":"Nickel(II) ions are reduced, not oxidized; nickel metal is the product they form.","calc":["Ni²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Ni"],"strike":["Nickel(II) ions","Nickel metal"]},{"text":"Fe²⁺ is the product of the oxidation, not the species being oxidized.","strike":["Iron(II) ions"]},{"text":"Answer: iron metal."}],"trap":"Picking the ion of the more negative couple: Fe²⁺ is what iron becomes, not what gets oxidized."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the potentials**.","marks":[{"t":"−0.44 V","note":"Fe: lower"},{"t":"−0.25 V","note":"Ni: higher"},{"t":"iron nail","note":"Fe metal"},{"t":"nickel(II) sulfate","note":"Ni²⁺ ions"}]},{"text":"**Higher E° wins the electrons.** Ni²⁺ has the higher E°, so it is reduced."},{"text":"**The other one is oxidized.** Iron's half-reaction runs in reverse.","calc":["Fe → Fe²⁺ + 2e⁻","Ni²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Ni"]},{"text":"**Also:** balance a redox equation by multiplying the half-reactions so the electrons lost equal the electrons gained, then check the charge."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** of two half-reactions, the metal with the lower reduction potential is oxidized."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-55a","s":"Electrochemistry & Redox","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:55#1","pe1-cp:55#2","pe1-cp:55#3","pe6-cp:41#1"],"q":"In a galvanic cell, copper is the cathode (E° = +0.34 V) and iron is the anode (E° = −0.44 V). What is E°cell?","choices":["+0.78 V","−0.10 V","+0.34 V","+0.44 V"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"impossible","why":"A galvanic cell runs on its own, so its E°cell must be positive."},{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"+0.34 V is the cathode's value copied alone; a cell potential needs both electrodes."},{"c":3,"tag":"copied","why":"+0.44 V is just the anode's value with its sign changed; the cathode was left out."}],"rule":"E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode (both reduction values), and a galvanic cell's E° is always positive.","exp":"Subtract the anode from the cathode, **E°cell = E°red(cathode) − E°red(anode)**, using both reduction potentials: +0.34 − (−0.44) = **+0.78 V**. Adding the two values gives −0.10 V, and either electrode alone (+0.34 V or +0.44 V) is only half the cell.","how":{"ask":"Subtract the anode's reduction potential from the cathode's.","know":"• **E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode** (both as reduction potentials from the table).\n• Don't flip a sign and then also subtract; do one or the other.","steps":[{"text":"Note which electrode is which.","hl":"copper is the cathode (E° = +0.34 V)","calc":["E°cathode = +0.34 V","E°anode = −0.44 V"]},{"text":"Apply the formula.","calc":["E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode","E°cell = +0.34 − (−0.44)","E°cell = +0.34 + 0.44 = **+0.78 V**"]},{"text":"Adding the two tabulated potentials skips the anode's sign change.","calc":["+0.34 + (−0.44) = −0.10 V ✗"],"strike":["−0.10 V"]},{"text":"+0.34 V is the cathode alone, and +0.44 V is only the anode's oxidation potential; the cell needs both.","calc":["oxidation of Fe: +0.44 V","reduction of Cu²⁺: +0.34 V","sum = +0.78 V"],"strike":["+0.34 V","+0.44 V"]},{"text":"Answer: +0.78 V."}],"trap":"Adding the two tabulated reduction potentials: that skips the sign change for the anode's oxidation."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the roles and values**.","marks":[{"t":"copper is the cathode","note":"reduction"},{"t":"+0.34 V","note":"E°cathode"},{"t":"iron is the anode","note":"oxidation"},{"t":"−0.44 V","note":"E°anode"}]},{"text":"**The formula** uses both reduction potentials as listed.","calc":["E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode"]},{"text":"**Watch the double negative.** Subtracting a negative number adds it.","calc":["+0.34 − (−0.44) = +0.34 + 0.44"]},{"text":"**Also:** in a galvanic cell, the cathode is the half-cell with the higher standard reduction potential."},{"text":"**Also:** a positive E°cell means spontaneous as written; a negative E°cell means not spontaneous (the reverse reaction is)."},{"text":"**Also:** when E°cell and one electrode are known, solve the same formula for the other: E°anode = E°cathode − E°cell. A strongly negative result marks a metal that gives up electrons easily.","calc":["this cell backward: +0.34 − (+0.78) = −0.44 V (iron ✓)","Cu cathode, E°cell = +1.10 V: E°anode = 0.34 − 1.10 = −0.76 V (zinc)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode, with both values taken as reduction potentials."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-57a","s":"Equilibrium","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:57#1","pe1-cp:57#2","pe1-cp:57#3","pe3-cp:56#1","pe3-cp:56#2"],"q":"Solid sodium chloride is dissolved in a saturated silver chloride solution that sits over undissolved AgCl. Immediately afterward, how does the ion product Q compare with Ksp?","choices":["Above Ksp","Below Ksp","Equal to Ksp","Exactly zero"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"impossible","why":"Ag⁺ and Cl⁻ are both still in solution, so their product can't be zero."},{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Direction: adding Cl⁻ raises one factor, so Q moves up from Ksp, not down."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Q = Ksp held only before the extra Cl⁻ arrived; raising [Cl⁻] raises Q."}],"rule":"Q = [Ag⁺][Cl⁻] right after mixing: adding a common ion raises one factor, so Q jumps and solid precipitates.","exp":"Before the addition the solution is saturated, so **Q = [Ag⁺][Cl⁻] = Ksp**. NaCl adds **Cl⁻, a common ion**, raising [Cl⁻] while [Ag⁺] is momentarily unchanged, so the product **Q now exceeds Ksp**.","how":{"ask":"What was Q before the addition, and which factor in Q just grew?","know":"• **Q = [Ag⁺][Cl⁻]** at any moment; **Ksp** is its value at saturation.\n• Saturated: Q = Ksp. Adding a **common ion** raises one factor of Q.\n• Q > Ksp → precipitate forms; Q < Ksp → more dissolves.","steps":[{"text":"Start from the saturated solution.","hl":"saturated silver chloride solution","calc":["AgCl(s) ⇌ Ag⁺ + Cl⁻","Q = [Ag⁺][Cl⁻] = Ksp = 1.8 × 10⁻¹⁰","[Ag⁺] = [Cl⁻] = √(1.8 × 10⁻¹⁰) = 1.34 × 10⁻⁵ M"]},{"text":"NaCl adds Cl⁻. Suppose [Cl⁻] becomes 0.050 M.","hl":"Solid sodium chloride","calc":["Q = (1.34 × 10⁻⁵)(0.050)","Q = 6.7 × 10⁻⁷ > 1.8 × 10⁻¹⁰"]},{"text":"Raising one factor can't leave Q equal to or below Ksp.","strike":["Below Ksp","Equal to Ksp"]},{"text":"Ag⁺ is still present, so Q is not zero.","strike":["Exactly zero"]},{"text":"Answer: Q is above Ksp."}],"trap":"Thinking Q stays at Ksp because the solution was saturated: saturation describes the moment before the common ion arrived."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"sodium chloride","note":"adds Cl⁻, a common ion"},{"t":"saturated","note":"Q = Ksp before"},{"t":"Immediately afterward","note":"before any shift"}]},{"text":"**Q has the same form as Ksp** but uses the concentrations right now.","calc":["Q = [Ag⁺][Cl⁻]"]},{"text":"**One factor up → Q up.** From Q = Ksp, any extra common ion pushes Q above Ksp.","calc":["[Cl⁻] ↑ → Q ↑ → Q > Ksp"]},{"text":"**Also (Le Châtelier):** adding a product, here the common ion, shifts the equilibrium left, so the sparingly soluble salt precipitates."},{"text":"**Also:** adding a common ion lowers a salt's solubility, but Ksp itself doesn't change unless temperature does."},{"text":"**Also:** a pure solid is left out of K and Q (its activity is 1), so adding or removing one (more undissolved AgCl, an ion-exchange resin, a precipitate filtered off) doesn't shift the equilibrium. Only changes in dissolved concentrations or gas pressures do.","calc":["AgCl(s) ⇌ Ag⁺ + Cl⁻: Q = [Ag⁺][Cl⁻], no AgCl term","more AgCl(s) → Q unchanged → no shift"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** adding a common ion to a saturated solution raises Q above Ksp."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-58a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe1-cp:58#1","pe3-cp:21#3","pe5-cp:1#1","pe5-cp:24#1","pe6-cp:35#2","pe3-cp:21#1","pe3-cp:21#2"],"q":"A recipe needs 2.22 g of calcium chloride (molar mass 111 g/mol), measured out from a 0.400 M CaCl₂ stock. What volume of stock is required?","choices":["50.0 mL","8.0 mL","20.0 mL","5.55 L"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Wrong operation: 8.0 comes from n × M (0.0200 × 0.400); volume is n ÷ M."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"Stops at the moles (0.0200) and reads them as litres; volume still needs ÷ molarity."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"Skips the grams-to-moles step: 5.55 L of 0.400 M would hold about 246 g, not 2.22 g."}],"rule":"Grams → moles (÷ molar mass), then volume = moles ÷ molarity, then L → mL. Estimate: 2 ÷ 100, then ÷ 0.4.","exp":"First grams to moles: 2.22 g ÷ 111 g/mol = **0.0200 mol**. Then **V = n ÷ M** = 0.0200 mol ÷ 0.400 mol/L = 0.0500 L = **50.0 mL**.","how":{"ask":"Convert grams to moles, then divide moles by molarity to get litres, then convert to mL.","know":"• **n = mass ÷ molar mass**.\n• **M = n ÷ V**, so **V = n ÷ M** (V in litres).\n• 1 L = 1000 mL.","steps":[{"text":"Note the mass, molar mass and molarity.","hl":"2.22 g of calcium chloride","calc":["m = 2.22 g","molar mass = 111 g/mol","M = 0.400 mol/L"]},{"text":"Grams to moles.","calc":["n = 2.22 g ÷ 111 g/mol","n = 0.0200 mol"]},{"text":"Volume from V = n ÷ M, then mL.","calc":["V = 0.0200 mol ÷ 0.400 mol/L","V = 0.0500 L","V = 0.0500 × 1000 = **50.0 mL**"]},{"text":"Multiplying n × M instead of dividing gives 8.0 mL, and stopping at the moles (0.0200 read as litres) gives 20.0 mL.","calc":["0.0200 × 0.400 = 0.0080 L = 8.0 mL ✗","0.0200 → 20.0 mL skips ÷ M ✗"],"strike":["8.0 mL","20.0 mL"]},{"text":"Skipping the grams-to-moles step divides grams by molarity.","calc":["2.22 ÷ 0.400 = 5.55 ✗"],"strike":["5.55 L"]},{"text":"Answer: 50.0 mL."}],"trap":"Dividing grams by molarity: molarity is moles per litre, so the mass must become moles first."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the givens**.","marks":[{"t":"2.22 g","note":"mass"},{"t":"111 g/mol","note":"molar mass"},{"t":"0.400 M","note":"mol per L"}]},{"text":"**Grams → moles.**","calc":["n = mass ÷ molar mass"]},{"text":"**Moles → litres → mL.** Rearrange M = n/V.","calc":["V = n ÷ M","mL = L × 1000"]},{"text":"**Also:** going the other way, mass = M × V(L) × molar mass. Turn both prefixes into base units before multiplying: μM × 10⁻⁶ = M and mL × 10⁻³ = L.","calc":["40 μM = 4.0 × 10⁻⁵ M; 750 mL = 0.750 L","n = 4.0 × 10⁻⁵ mol/L × 0.750 L = 3.0 × 10⁻⁵ mol","mass = 3.0 × 10⁻⁵ mol × 342 g/mol ≈ 1.0 × 10⁻² g ≈ 10 mg"]},{"text":"**Also:** when only the moles are wanted, n = M × V(L). A millimolar value must become molar first (mM ÷ 1000), and mL ÷ 1000 gives litres.","calc":["600 mM = 0.600 M; 400 mL = 0.400 L","n = 0.600 mol/L × 0.400 L = 0.240 mol"]},{"text":"**Also:** to make a solution from the solid, chain the units forward: mass = M × V × molar mass, with V in litres. Change mL to L first, or the mass comes out 1000 times too big.","calc":["0.050 M CaCl₂ in 300 mL = 0.300 L","mass = 0.050 mol/L × 0.300 L × 111 g/mol ≈ 1.7 g"]},{"text":"**Also:** for a concentration given in g/L, mass = concentration (g/L) × volume (L), and no molar mass enters. A microlitre is 10⁻⁶ L (a millilitre is 10⁻³ L); mixing them up makes the mass 1000 times too big.","calc":["40 g/L protein; drop = 8.0 μL = 8.0 × 10⁻⁶ L","mass = 40 g/L × 8.0 × 10⁻⁶ L = 3.2 × 10⁻⁴ g = 0.32 mg","μL read as mL: 40 × 8.0 × 10⁻³ = 0.32 g ✗ (1000× too big)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** V = n ÷ M gives litres; convert grams to moles first and litres to mL at the end; mass = M × V(L) × molar mass, or (g/L) × V(L) with μL = 10⁻⁶ L."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe1-59c","s":"Mechanics & Energy","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe1-cp:59#3","pe1-cp:59#1","pe1-cp:59#2","pe3-cp:7#1","pe3-cp:7#2"],"q":"A rehab patient pushes a weighted sled with a steady 120-N force through 15 m in 6.0 s. What average power does the patient deliver?","choices":["300 W","1800 W","48 W","720 W"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Ratio flipped: puts time on top and distance on the bottom (F × t ÷ d); power is F × d ÷ t."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"1800 is F × d, the work in joules; power still needs ÷ time."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"720 = F × t, which has units of N·s (an impulse), not watts; distance never enters."}],"rule":"Power = work ÷ time = F × d ÷ t. Estimate: round to 100 × 15 ÷ 6.","exp":"**Power = force × distance ÷ time**: W = 120 N × 15 m = 1800 J, and P = 1800 J ÷ 6.0 s = **300 W**. N·m/s = J/s = W.","how":{"ask":"Find the work as force × distance, then divide by the time.","know":"• **Work W = F·d** (N·m = J) when force and motion are in the same direction.\n• **Power P = W/t = F·d/t** (J/s = W).\n• Equivalently P = F·v.","steps":[{"text":"Work done on the sled.","hl":"a steady 120-N force through 15 m","calc":["W = F × d = 120 N × 15 m","W = **1800 J**"]},{"text":"Divide by the time.","hl":"6.0 s","calc":["P = W ÷ t = 1800 J ÷ 6.0 s","P = **300 W**","check: v = 15 m ÷ 6.0 s = 2.5 m/s; F·v = 120 × 2.5 = 300 W"]},{"text":"1800 W is the work in joules, never divided by time. 48 W uses F × t ÷ d (120 × 6.0 ÷ 15). 720 W multiplies force by time and ignores the distance.","strike":["1800 W","48 W","720 W"]},{"text":"Answer: 300 W."}],"trap":"Stopping at force × distance: 1800 J is energy. Power needs the division by time."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number**.","marks":[{"t":"120-N force","note":"F"},{"t":"15 m","note":"distance d"},{"t":"6.0 s","note":"time t"}]},{"text":"**Force × distance is work (energy).**","calc":["N·m = J","120 × 15 = 1800 J"]},{"text":"**Work ÷ time is power.**","calc":["J ÷ s = W","1800 ÷ 6.0 = 300 W"]},{"text":"**Also:** W = J/s = kg·m²·s⁻³; one more s⁻¹ than a joule means power."},{"text":"**Also:** kg·m²·s⁻² is a joule (energy), not power; power needs kg·m²·s⁻³."},{"text":"**Also:** with no change in speed there is no acceleration (ΣF = 0), so the driving force equals the resisting force; then P = F·v.","calc":["constant speed: driving force = resisting force = 120 N","P = F·v = 120 N × 2.5 m/s = 300 W"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** force × distance ÷ time is power (N·m/s = J/s = W); force × distance alone is energy."}]},
  // ── Practice Exam 3 · Chem/Phys ──
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-01a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:1#1","pe3-cp:1#2","pe3-cp:1#3"],"q":"With base and heat, 1-phenylpentane-1,4-dione, C₆H₅–CO–CH₂–CH₂–CO–CH₃, cyclizes to 3-phenylcyclopent-2-en-1-one. Which two starting carbons become the ring's C=C?","choices":["C1 and C5","C2 and C3","C3 and C4","C4 and C5"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"C2 and C3 stay CH₂ groups in the ring; neither becomes part of the C=C."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"C4 is the ketone that survives as the enone's C=O, so it can't be in the C=C."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Again C4: it stays the ring C=O, so any pair that includes it is wrong."}],"rule":"In an intramolecular aldol condensation, the surviving ketone keeps its C=O; the C=C forms where the other carbonyl lost its O.","exp":"Only the CH₃ (C5) can reach a carbonyl and close a five-membered ring: its enolate carbon bonds to **C1**, the phenyl ketone carbon. Losing water then puts the **C=C between C5 and C1**, conjugated with the C4 ketone, which stays a C=O.","how":{"ask":"Find which α-carbon attacks which carbonyl, then place the C=C made by dehydration.","know":"• **Aldol bond**: the enolate's **α-carbon** (nucleophile) bonds to the **other carbonyl carbon** (electrophile).\n• **Dehydration**: the C=C forms between the **former α-carbon** and the **former carbonyl carbon**, conjugated with the remaining C=O.\n• Number the chain, then follow each carbon by its neighbors.","steps":[{"text":"Number the chain from the phenyl end: C1 is the phenyl ketone carbon, C4 the methyl ketone carbon, C5 the CH₃.","hl":"C₆H₅–CO–CH₂–CH₂–CO–CH₃","calc":["C₆H₅–C1(=O)–C2H₂–C3H₂–C4(=O)–C5H₃"]},{"text":"Test each α-carbon. C2 or C3 attacking a carbonyl would make a three-membered ring; C5 attacking C1 makes a five-membered ring.","calc":["C5 → C1: ring = C5, C4, C3, C2, C1 = **5 atoms** ✓","C3 → C1 or C2 → C4: **3 atoms** ✗"]},{"text":"The new C5–C1 bond puts an OH on C1 (β to the C4 ketone). Dehydration removes that OH and an H from C5.","calc":["C=C: **C5 = C1**, conjugated with C4=O"]},{"text":"Check by neighbors: the phenyl is bonded to C1 at the start, and in the product it sits on ring C3, an alkene carbon.","hl":"3-phenylcyclopent-2-en-1-one"},{"text":"C2 and C3 stay CH₂ groups in the ring; C4 stays the ketone, so it is never part of the C=C.","strike":["C2 and C3","C3 and C4","C4 and C5"]},{"text":"Answer: C1 and C5."}],"trap":"Putting the surviving C=O carbon (C4) into the C=C: that carbon keeps its oxygen, and the alkene forms at C1, the carbon that lost its OH."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"1-phenylpentane-1,4-dione","note":"two ketones, 1,4 apart"},{"t":"With base and heat","note":"aldol, then dehydration"},{"t":"3-phenylcyclopent-2-en-1-one","note":"five-membered enone"}]},{"text":"**Number and label.** Write C1–C5 under the chain; mark which carbons are carbonyls (C1, C4) and which are α (C2, C3, C5)."},{"text":"**Make the aldol bond.** An α-carbon (as its enolate) bonds to the other carbonyl carbon. Pick the pair that closes a five- or six-membered ring.","calc":["C5 + C1 → 5-membered ring"]},{"text":"**Dehydrate.** The OH on the former carbonyl carbon leaves with an H from the former α-carbon, so the C=C joins those two and is conjugated with the C=O.","calc":["former α-C = former C=O carbon → α,β-unsaturated ketone"]},{"text":"**Also:** track carbons by their neighbors: a carbon bonded to a phenyl, or one next to a quaternary carbon (no H), keeps that neighbor in the product, so you can find it in the ring."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in an intramolecular aldol condensation, the α-carbon bonds to the other carbonyl carbon, and the C=C forms between those same two carbons."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-02a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:2#1"],"q":"Sulfonating naphthalene briefly at low temperature gives mainly the 1-isomer; heating for hours at high temperature, where the isomers interconvert, gives mainly the 2-isomer. Which conclusion follows?","choices":["The 2-isomer is lower in energy","The 1-isomer is lower in energy","The 2-isomer has the lower Ea","Both isomers are equally low in energy"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Backwards: the 1-isomer wins only under kinetic control (fast, cold), so it forms faster but is less stable."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Backwards: the 1-isomer forms faster in the cold, so it, not the 2-isomer, has the lower Ea."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Equal stability would give a roughly even equilibrium mixture, not a dominant 2-isomer."}],"rule":"Short, cold run = kinetic product (lower Ea); long, hot run where isomers interconvert = thermodynamic product (more stable).","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Isomer mix from sulfonating naphthalene","x":{"label":"Conditions"},"y":{"label":"Percent of product","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"categories":["Brief, cold","Hours, hot"],"series":[{"name":"1-isomer","values":[94,12]},{"name":"2-isomer","values":[6,88]}]},"exp":"Only the long, hot run lets the isomers interconvert and reach **equilibrium**, where the **more stable** (lower-energy) product dominates: the 2-isomer. The brief, cold run shows **kinetic control**: the 1-isomer forms faster, so it has the lower Ea.","how":{"ask":"Decide which run reached equilibrium, then read stability from that run only.","know":"• **Thermodynamic control** (reversible, long time, higher T): the major product is the **more stable** one.\n• **Kinetic control** (irreversible, short time, low T): the major product is the one that **forms faster** (lower Ea).","steps":[{"text":"The hot, long run is reversible, so it reflects stability.","hl":"where the isomers interconvert","calc":["equilibrium → major = more stable = **2-isomer**"],"graph":{"bars":[["Hours, hot","2-isomer"]]}},{"text":"The brief, cold run is irreversible, so it reflects rate.","hl":"briefly at low temperature","calc":["kinetic → major = faster = 1-isomer → lower Ea"],"graph":{"bars":[["Brief, cold","1-isomer"]]}},{"text":"The 1-isomer wins only under kinetic control, so it is not the more stable; and since it forms faster, the 2-isomer has the higher Ea, not the lower.","strike":["The 1-isomer is lower in energy","The 2-isomer has the lower Ea"]},{"text":"Equal stability would give a roughly even equilibrium mixture, not a dominant 2-isomer.","strike":["Both isomers are equally low in energy"]},{"text":"Answer: the 2-isomer is lower in energy."}],"trap":"Reading stability from the kinetic run: at low temperature and short times the faster-forming product wins, whatever its stability."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"briefly at low temperature","note":"kinetic conditions"},{"t":"heating for hours at high temperature","note":"thermodynamic conditions"},{"t":"interconvert","note":"equilibrium reached"}]},{"text":"**Sort the runs.** Interconversion means equilibrium; no interconversion means the first-formed product stays."},{"text":"**Equilibrium → stability.** The product favored at equilibrium has the lower free energy.","calc":["K = [2-isomer]/[1-isomer] > 1 → 2-isomer more stable"]},{"text":"**Kinetic → rate.** The product favored when nothing reverses is the one with the lower activation energy."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** under equilibrium (thermodynamic) conditions the more stable product is major; under kinetic conditions the faster-forming product is major."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-03a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:3#1","pe3-cp:3#2"],"q":"Propanal is treated with cold, dilute NaOH, and the product is isolated before any water is eliminated. Which compound is the product?","choices":["3-Hydroxy-2-methylpentanal","2-Methylpent-2-enal","2-Hydroxy-3-methylpentanal","3-Hydroxyhexanal"],"choiceFigs":["cpt-3-hydroxy-2-methylpentanal","cpt-2-methylpent-2-enal","cpt-2-hydroxy-3-methylpentanal","cpt-3-hydroxyhexanal"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"OH and methyl swapped: the OH goes on the β-carbon (C3), the α-carbon's methyl on C2."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"No OH: \"-enal\" names the dehydrated product, which the question rules out."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Straight chain: propanal's α-carbon carries a CH₃, so the aldol product must branch at C2."}],"rule":"Aldol addition: one aldehyde's α-carbon bonds to the other's C=O carbon; the OH ends on C3 and the α-substituent on C2.","exp":"The enolate carbon of one propanal (C2, which carries the CH₃) bonds to the carbonyl carbon of a second molecule. Without dehydration this **aldol addition** leaves a **β-OH**, two carbons from the aldehyde C=O: CH₃CH₂–CH(OH)–CH(CH₃)–CHO, **3-hydroxy-2-methylpentanal**. Losing water would give 2-methylpent-2-enal.","how":{"ask":"Join an α-carbon of one aldehyde to the carbonyl carbon of the other, and keep the OH.","know":"• **Aldol addition** gives a **β-hydroxy carbonyl**: the old C=O becomes C–OH, two carbons from the remaining C=O.\n• **Dehydration** (heat or more base) removes that OH and an α-H, giving the **α,β-unsaturated carbonyl**.","steps":[{"text":"No water is lost, so the product keeps every atom of both molecules: an addition, not a condensation.","hl":"isolated before any water is eliminated","calc":["2 C₃H₆O → C₆H₁₂O₂"]},{"text":"Base removes an α-H from C2 of propanal; that carbon bonds to the carbonyl carbon of a second propanal.","calc":["CH₃CH₂–CH(O⁻)–CH(CH₃)–CHO → protonate → CH₃CH₂–CH(OH)–CH(CH₃)–CHO"]},{"text":"Name it: five-carbon chain with CHO as C1, CH₃ on C2, OH on C3.","calc":["3-hydroxy-2-methylpentanal (C₆H₁₂O₂ ✓)"]},{"text":"2-Methylpent-2-enal is the dehydrated (condensation) product, which this question rules out.","strike":["2-Methylpent-2-enal"]},{"text":"The OH must be on the β-carbon and the methyl on the α-carbon, so the swapped name is wrong; a straight six-carbon chain would need the terminal CH₃ to be the nucleophile, but it is not α to the C=O.","strike":["2-Hydroxy-3-methylpentanal","3-Hydroxyhexanal"]},{"text":"Answer: 3-hydroxy-2-methylpentanal."}],"trap":"Choosing the α,β-unsaturated aldehyde out of habit: that forms only after dehydration, which the stem excludes."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"cold, dilute NaOH","note":"mild: aldol addition"},{"t":"before any water is eliminated","note":"no dehydration"},{"t":"Propanal","note":"CH₃CH₂CHO"}]},{"text":"**Find the α-carbon.** In CH₃CH₂CHO the α-carbon is the CH₂ next to C=O; its enolate is the nucleophile."},{"text":"**Join and protonate.** α-Carbon to the other C=O carbon; the O⁻ picks up H⁺, giving an OH on the β-carbon.","calc":["α-C–C(OH) next to CHO → β-hydroxy aldehyde"]},{"text":"**Also:** if the mixture is heated, the β-OH and an α-H leave as water, giving the conjugated α,β-unsaturated aldehyde.","calc":["β-hydroxy carbonyl − H₂O → α,β-unsaturated carbonyl"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** aldol addition gives a β-hydroxy carbonyl; only dehydration turns it into the α,β-unsaturated carbonyl."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-04a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:4#1","pe3-cp:4#2","pe3-cp:4#3"],"q":"In a Michael addition, cyclohexanone's enolate adds to pent-3-en-2-one, CH₃–C(=O)–CH=CH–CH₃, numbered C1–C5 from the left. Which enone carbon forms the new bond?","choices":["C2","C3","C4","C5"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"C2 is the carbonyl carbon: attack there is 1,2-addition, not a Michael (1,4) addition."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"C3 is the α-carbon; in the adduct it becomes the enolate carbon, not the electrophilic site."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"C5 is a plain methyl group with no electrophilic character."}],"rule":"Michael (1,4) addition: a stabilized enolate bonds to the β-carbon, the alkene carbon two away from the C=O carbon.","exp":"Conjugation pulls electron density from the C=C toward oxygen, leaving **C4, the β-carbon**, electron-poor. The soft enolate bonds there (1,4-addition). C2 is the carbonyl carbon (1,2-attack), C3 is the α-carbon, and C5 is just a methyl group.","how":{"ask":"Label the α- and β-carbons of the enone and add the enolate to the β-carbon.","know":"• **Michael (1,4-) addition**: an enolate bonds to the **β-carbon** of an α,β-unsaturated carbonyl.\n• **Robinson annulation**: the 1,5-diketone formed then undergoes an **intramolecular aldol + dehydration**, closing a **six-membered** enone ring.\n• Groups that do not react **ride along** on the carbon that carries them.","steps":[{"text":"Label the enone from the carbonyl: C2 is C=O, C3 is α, C4 is β.","hl":"CH₃–C(=O)–CH=CH–CH₃","calc":["C1H₃–C2(=O)–C3H=C4H–C5H₃","α = C3, β = C4"]},{"text":"Resonance puts δ+ on C4, so the enolate carbon bonds to C4.","calc":["O=C2–C3=C4 ↔ ⁻O–C2=C3–C4⁺"]},{"text":"C2 would be direct 1,2-attack, C3 is the α-carbon (it becomes the enolate carbon of the adduct), and C5 is an unactivated methyl.","strike":["C2","C3","C5"]},{"text":"Next the adduct, a 1,5-diketone, does an intramolecular aldol: the C1 methyl's enolate bonds to cyclohexanone's C=O carbon, closing a six-membered ring; dehydration gives the conjugated enone.","calc":["ring: C=O(cyclohexanone) – Cα(cyclohexanone) – C4 – C3 – C2 – C1 = **6 atoms**"]},{"text":"Where does C5 end up? It stays on C4, which in the new ring is bonded to the ring-fusion carbon and to the CH₂ next to the new ketone.","calc":["CH₃ on the ring carbon two bonds from the ketone carbon (via the CH₂), next to the ring-fusion carbon"]},{"text":"Answer: C4."}],"trap":"Attacking the carbonyl carbon (1,2-addition): a soft, stabilized enolate adds to the β-carbon."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Michael addition","note":"1,4-addition"},{"t":"pent-3-en-2-one","note":"α,β-unsaturated ketone"},{"t":"C1–C5 from the left","note":"numbering to use"}]},{"text":"**Find α and β.** α is the carbon next to C=O; β is the far carbon of the C=C."},{"text":"**Add at β.** The enolate's carbon bonds to the β-carbon; the enone's C=C becomes a single bond and its enolate is protonated."},{"text":"**Also:** the resulting 1,5-dicarbonyl closes by intramolecular aldol and loses water, building a new six-membered ring with a conjugated C=O (Robinson annulation)."},{"text":"**Also:** a substituent that takes no part (here the C5 methyl) stays on its original carbon; follow that carbon into the ring to place it."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Michael addition puts the enolate on the β-carbon; an aldol and dehydration then close the six-membered ring, carrying spectator groups along."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-05a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:5#1","pe3-cp:5#2","pe3-cp:5#3","pe3-cp:5#4"],"q":"A 25 mF capacitor is fully charged by a 12 V battery with 0.5 Ω internal resistance. What charge and energy does it store?","choices":["0.30 C; 1.8 J","0.30 C; 3.6 J","300 C; 1800 J","0.30 C; 0.15 J"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"25 mF is 0.025 F, not 25 F; reading it as farads makes the charge a thousand times too big."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"Dropped the ½ in U = ½CV², doubling the energy."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"½CV gives coulombs, not joules; energy needs V squared (½CV²)."}],"rule":"Convert mF to F first (÷ 1000), then Q = CV and U = ½CV²; a series resistor drops no voltage once charging stops.","exp":"Convert first: **25 mF = 0.025 F**. **Q = CV** = 0.025 F × 12 V = **0.30 C**. The stored energy is **U = ½CV²**, here 0.0125 × 144 = **1.8 J** (or ½QV = ½ × 0.30 × 12 = 1.8 J). Once charged, no current flows, so the 0.5 Ω internal resistance drops no voltage and changes neither value.","how":{"ask":"Put the capacitance in farads, find the voltage across the capacitor, then apply Q = CV and U = ½CV².","know":"• **Q = CV** (coulombs = farads × volts).\n• **U = ½CV² = ½QV** (joules).\n• **m (milli) = 10⁻³**: 1 mF = 0.001 F.\n• A **fully charged** capacitor passes no current, so a resistor in series with it drops I·r = 0 V.","steps":[{"text":"Convert milli-farads to farads.","hl":"25 mF","calc":["25 mF = 25 × 10⁻³ F = **0.025 F**"]},{"text":"Fully charged means the current has stopped. With I = 0, the internal resistance drops no voltage, so the capacitor holds the full battery voltage.","hl":"0.5 Ω internal resistance","calc":["V across r = I·r = 0 × 0.5 Ω = 0 V","V across C = **12 V**"]},{"text":"Charge from Q = CV. 300 C comes from leaving 25 mF as 25 F.","hl":"12 V battery","calc":["Q = CV","Q = 0.025 F × 12 V","Q = **0.30 C**"],"strike":["300 C; 1800 J"]},{"text":"Energy from U = ½CV², checked with ½QV. 3.6 J drops the ½, and 0.15 J forgets to square V (½CV).","calc":["U = ½CV²","U = ½ × 0.025 F × (12 V)²","U = ½ × 0.025 × 144 = **1.8 J**","check: U = ½QV = ½ × 0.30 C × 12 V = **1.8 J**"],"strike":["0.30 C; 3.6 J","0.30 C; 0.15 J"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.30 C; 1.8 J."}],"trap":"Reading mF as farads (300 C) or dropping the ½ in U = ½CV² (3.6 J). The internal resistance only slows the charging; it does not change the final charge."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number**.","marks":[{"t":"25 mF","note":"capacitance, in millifarads"},{"t":"fully charged","note":"no current now"},{"t":"12 V battery","note":"voltage across C"},{"t":"0.5 Ω internal resistance","note":"drops 0 V once I = 0"}]},{"text":"**Milli means one thousandth.** Convert before multiplying.","calc":["25 mF = 25 ÷ 1000 F = 0.025 F"]},{"text":"**Charge:** Q = CV.","calc":["Q = 0.025 F × 12 V = 0.30 C"]},{"text":"**Energy:** U = ½CV², or the same thing written ½QV.","calc":["½CV² = ½ × 0.025 × 12² = 1.8 J","½QV = ½ × 0.30 × 12 = 1.8 J"]},{"text":"**Also:** once a capacitor is fully charged, no current flows, so a series or internal resistance carries no voltage (V = IR = 0). It sets how fast the capacitor charges, never the final Q or U."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Q = CV and U = ½CV² = ½QV, with C in farads (1 mF = 10⁻³ F) and V the full source voltage once charging has stopped."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-06a","s":"Waves & Sound","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:6#1"],"q":"A jogger runs at 7 m/s past a parked ambulance's 700 Hz siren. Compared with 700 Hz, what does she hear approaching, then receding?","choices":["Higher, then lower","Lower, then higher","Higher, then higher","Equal, then equal"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Order flipped: closing in on a source raises the pitch you hear and moving away lowers it, not the reverse."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Approaching and receding shift the pitch opposite ways, so it can't stay high both times."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"She moves straight toward, then away from, the siren, so the Doppler shift can't be zero."}],"rule":"Closing in on a sound source raises the pitch you hear; moving apart lowers it, whether the source or the listener moves.","exp":"A listener moving **toward** a still source meets more crests each second, so the heard **frequency is higher**; moving **away**, she meets fewer, so it is **lower**. Taking sound at 350 m/s: 700 × 357/350 = 714 Hz approaching and 700 × 343/350 = 686 Hz receding.","how":{"ask":"Decide whether the listener is closing on or leaving the source in each half of the run, and how that changes the crests she meets per second.","know":"• Moving listener, still source: **f′ = f(v ± v_L)/v**, with v the speed of sound.\n• Use **+** when the listener moves **toward** the source (higher f′), **−** when moving **away** (lower f′).\n• Only motion along the line joining them shifts the frequency.","steps":[{"text":"The source is parked; only the listener moves. While approaching she runs into the crests, so she meets more per second. Take sound at 350 m/s.","hl":"runs at 7 m/s","calc":["f′ = f × (v + v_L) ÷ v","f′ = 700 Hz × (350 + 7) ÷ 350","f′ = 700 × 1.02 = **714 Hz** > 700 Hz"]},{"text":"After passing she runs away from the crests, so fewer reach her each second. The pitch drops below 700 Hz, so neither the reversed order nor staying high fits.","calc":["f′ = f × (v − v_L) ÷ v","f′ = 700 Hz × (350 − 7) ÷ 350","f′ = 700 × 0.98 = **686 Hz** < 700 Hz"],"strike":["Lower, then higher","Higher, then higher"]},{"text":"She hears exactly 700 Hz only when she has no velocity toward or away from the siren; while running along the road she hears a shift.","strike":["Equal, then equal"]},{"text":"Answer: higher, then lower."}],"trap":"Thinking only a moving source causes a Doppler shift. A moving listener shifts the heard frequency too: up while closing in, down while leaving."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find who moves**.","marks":[{"t":"runs at 7 m/s","note":"the listener moves"},{"t":"parked ambulance's 700 Hz siren","note":"the source is still"},{"t":"approaching, then receding","note":"two directions"}]},{"text":"**Count crests.** Running toward the siren, she meets the crests sooner than if she stood still, so more arrive each second: higher pitch."},{"text":"**Put numbers on it** (sound at 350 m/s).","calc":["toward: 700 × 357/350 = 714 Hz","away: 700 × 343/350 = 686 Hz"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** moving toward a source raises the frequency you hear, and moving away lowers it, whether the source or the listener is the one moving."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-08a","s":"Mechanics & Energy","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe3-cp:8#1","pe3-cp:8#2"],"q":"A trolley enters a magnetic brake zone at 12 m/s, and the braking force is proportional to its speed. Which describes its speed-versus-time graph?","choices":["Steep, then flattening","Straight downward line","Gentle, then steepening","Horizontal line"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"impossible","why":"A nonzero braking force must change the speed, so the graph can't be flat."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Steepening would need the force to grow as the trolley slows; here the force shrinks with speed."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"A straight line means a constant slope, so a constant force; here the force shrinks with v."}],"rule":"The slope of a v–t graph is the acceleration; a force that shrinks with speed gives a slope that shrinks as the object slows.","exp":"With **F ∝ v**, the deceleration a = F/m is largest at entry and shrinks as the trolley slows, so the speed drops steeply at first and then levels off: an **exponential** decay toward zero. A **constant** force would give a constant slope, a straight line.","how":{"ask":"Link the slope of a speed–time graph to the force, then ask how the force changes as the speed falls.","know":"• Slope of a v–t graph = acceleration = F/m.\n• **F ∝ v** (F = −bv): the slope is steep when v is large and gentle when v is small, giving exponential decay.\n• **Constant F**: constant slope, a straight line.","steps":[{"text":"The force, and so the deceleration, scales with speed.","hl":"the braking force is proportional to its speed","calc":["F = −bv","a = F ÷ m = −(b/m)v","large v → large deceleration; small v → small deceleration"]},{"text":"So the slope is steepest at entry and fades: the speed halves in equal times, dropping by less each interval. A graph that starts gentle and steepens would need the force to grow as the trolley slows.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Trolley speed in the brake zone","x":{"label":"Time (s)","min":0,"max":8,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Speed (m/s)","min":0,"max":12,"step":3},"series":[{"name":"F ∝ v (magnetic brake)","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,12],[2,6],[4,3],[6,1.5],[8,0.75]]},{"name":"Constant force","marker":"square-open","line":"straight","points":[[0,12],[2,9],[4,6],[6,3],[8,0]]}]},"calc":["12 → 6 → 3 → 1.5 m/s every 2 s","drops: 6, 3, 1.5 m/s: the slope shrinks"],"strike":["Gentle, then steepening"]},{"text":"A straight line needs a constant slope, which means a constant force; here the force fades with the speed.","strike":["Straight downward line"]},{"text":"A horizontal line means no change in speed, but the force is nonzero while the trolley moves.","strike":["Horizontal line"]},{"text":"Answer: steep, then flattening."}],"trap":"Picking the straight line out of habit: that is the graph for a constant force, like friction, not for a force that weakens as the object slows."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"proportional to its speed","note":"F ∝ v"},{"t":"12 m/s","note":"starting speed"},{"t":"speed-versus-time graph","note":"slope = acceleration"}]},{"text":"**Slope is acceleration.** On a speed–time graph, a steeper drop means a bigger braking force.","calc":["a = F/m","F ∝ v → a ∝ v"]},{"text":"**Compare the two forces.** A speed-dependent brake gives a curve that flattens; a constant force gives a straight line.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Two kinds of braking from 12 m/s","x":{"label":"Time (s)","min":0,"max":8,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Speed (m/s)","min":0,"max":12,"step":3},"series":[{"name":"F ∝ v","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,12],[2,6],[4,3],[6,1.5],[8,0.75]]},{"name":"Constant F","marker":"square-open","line":"straight","points":[[0,12],[2,9],[4,6],[6,3],[8,0]]}]}},{"text":"**Also:** a constant braking force gives a constant deceleration, so the speed falls by equal amounts in equal times: a straight line."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a force proportional to speed gives exponential slowing, steep at first and then flattening; a constant force gives a straight-line drop."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-09a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe3-cp:9#1","pe3-cp:9#2"],"q":"Four C₄H₈ isomers, W–Z, each burn completely to 4 CO₂ + 4 H₂O. Their heats of combustion are W 2717, X 2710, Y 2707 and Z 2701 kJ/mol. Which isomer is most stable?","choices":["W","X","Y","Z"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"W releases the most heat, so it starts highest in energy: the least stable, not the most."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"X releases more heat than two other isomers, so it starts above them in energy."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Y still releases more heat than one other isomer, so it isn't the lowest in energy."}],"rule":"Same products, so less heat released means a lower starting energy: the smallest heat of combustion is the most stable.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Heat released on burning one mole of each C₄H₈ isomer","x":{"label":"Isomer"},"y":{"label":"Heat released (kJ/mol)","min":2690,"max":2720,"step":10},"categories":["W","X","Y","Z"],"series":[{"name":"Heat released","values":[2717,2710,2707,2701]}]},"exp":"All four isomers end at the **same products**, so the difference in heat released is the difference in their starting enthalpies. Z releases the **least heat**, so it began lowest in energy and is the most stable; W, releasing the most, is the least stable.","how":{"ask":"Compare the heats of combustion; the smallest one marks the most stable isomer.","know":"• Isomers that burn to the **same products** can be ranked by heat of combustion: **less heat released = lower starting enthalpy = more stable**.\n• Boiling points, UV spectra and mass spectra do **not** measure thermodynamic stability.","steps":[{"text":"Same formula, same products, so all four reactions end at the same enthalpy.","hl":"each burn completely to 4 CO₂ + 4 H₂O"},{"text":"Rank by heat released; the gap from the products is the starting enthalpy.","hl":"W 2717, X 2710, Y 2707 and Z 2701 kJ/mol","calc":["Z 2701 < Y 2707 < X 2710 < W 2717 kJ/mol","most stable: **Z**; Z is 2717 − 2701 = 16 kJ/mol below W"],"graph":{"bars":[["Z","Heat released"]]}},{"text":"W releases the most heat, so it starts highest and is least stable; X and Y lie in between.","strike":["W","X","Y"]},{"text":"Answer: Z."}],"trap":"Thinking more heat released means a 'stronger' or more stable molecule: more heat out means it started higher in energy."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"C₄H₈ isomers","note":"same formula"},{"t":"4 CO₂ + 4 H₂O","note":"same products"},{"t":"W 2717","note":"heat released, kJ/mol"},{"t":"Z 2701 kJ/mol","note":"smallest heat"}]},{"text":"**Common endpoint.** Products are identical, so each heat of combustion measures how far its isomer sits above the same floor.","calc":["ΔH_comb = H(products) − H(isomer)"]},{"text":"**Smaller drop, lower start.** The isomer giving off the smallest amount of heat starts lowest in enthalpy: most stable.","calc":["2701 kJ/mol → lowest start → Z"]},{"text":"**Also:** boiling points reflect intermolecular forces, UV absorption reflects conjugation, and mass-spec patterns reflect mass and fragmentation; none of them measures stability."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** among isomers, the smallest heat of combustion marks the most stable one."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-11a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:11#1","pe3-cp:11#2","pe3-cp:11#3"],"q":"A 0.82 L flask holds an ideal gas at 1.5 atm and 27 °C. Using R = 0.082 L·atm/(mol·K), about how many gas molecules does it contain?","choices":["3.0 × 10²²","3.3 × 10²³","0.050","1.2 × 10²⁵"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"units","why":"Used 27 (°C) for T instead of about 300 K; gas laws need kelvin, so the count comes out about 11 times too big."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Gas law inverted (RT/PV): under 1 L at 1.5 atm is far less than 1 mol, not about 20 mol."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"0.050 is a mole count; a number of molecules needs × N_A (≈ 10²³)."}],"rule":"n = PV/RT with T in kelvin, then × 6 × 10²³. Round: 1 × 1.5 ÷ (0.08 × 300) is well under 0.1 mol.","exp":"**n = PV/RT** with T in kelvin (27 + 273 = 300 K): n = (1.5)(0.82) ÷ (0.082 × 300) = 0.050 mol. Molecules = **n × N_A** = 0.050 × 6.0 × 10²³ = 3.0 × 10²².","how":{"ask":"Find moles with the ideal gas law, then convert moles to molecules.","know":"• **PV = nRT**, so **n = PV/RT**.\n• **T must be in kelvin**: K = °C + 273.\n• Particles = **n × N_A** (N_A = 6.0 × 10²³ /mol).","steps":[{"text":"Convert the temperature to kelvin.","hl":"27 °C","calc":["T = 27 + 273 = **300 K**"]},{"text":"Solve for moles.","calc":["n = PV / RT","n = (1.5 atm)(0.82 L) ÷ (0.082 × 300)","n = 1.23 ÷ 24.6 = **0.050 mol**"]},{"text":"Convert moles to molecules.","calc":["N = 0.050 × 6.0 × 10²³ = **3.0 × 10²²**"]},{"text":"3.3 × 10²³ uses 27 instead of 300 K; 0.050 stops at moles; 1.2 × 10²⁵ inverts the gas law (RT/PV).","strike":["3.3 × 10²³","0.050","1.2 × 10²⁵"]},{"text":"Answer: 3.0 × 10²²."}],"trap":"Plugging in degrees Celsius: the gas law needs absolute temperature, so 27 °C is 300 K."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"0.82 L","note":"V"},{"t":"1.5 atm","note":"P"},{"t":"27 °C","note":"T, convert to K"},{"t":"molecules","note":"multiply by N_A"}]},{"text":"**Kelvin first.**","calc":["T = 27 + 273 = 300 K"]},{"text":"**Moles from PV = nRT.**","calc":["n = PV/RT = (1.5 × 0.82) ÷ (0.082 × 300) = 0.050 mol"]},{"text":"**Also:** moles × Avogadro's number gives particles.","calc":["0.050 × 6.0 × 10²³ = 3.0 × 10²²"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** number of gas particles = (PV/RT) × N_A, with T in kelvin."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-12a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe3-cp:12#1","pe3-cp:12#2"],"q":"Sulfurous acid, H₂SO₃, has Ka1 = 1.4 × 10⁻² and Ka2 = 6.3 × 10⁻⁸. What is the Kb of HSO₃⁻, and is a solution of NaHSO₃ acidic or basic?","choices":["7.1 × 10⁻¹³; acidic","7.1 × 10⁻¹³; basic","6.3 × 10⁻⁸; basic","1.4 × 10⁻¹⁶; acidic"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Behavior flipped: the ion's own Ka2 is far larger than its Kb, so it gives up H⁺ more than it takes it."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Kb = Kw ÷ Ka1; 1.4 × 10⁻¹⁶ multiplies Kw by Ka1 instead of dividing."},{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"6.3 × 10⁻⁸ is HSO₃⁻'s own Ka (Ka2) copied from the stem, not its Kb."}],"rule":"Kb of an amphiprotic ion = 10⁻¹⁴ ÷ the Ka that formed it (Ka1); if its own Ka2 beats that Kb, the solution is acidic.","exp":"HSO₃⁻ can lose H⁺ (its Ka is Ka2 = 6.3 × 10⁻⁸) or gain H⁺ to become H₂SO₃. **Kb = Kw/Ka of the conjugate acid** = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ ÷ 1.4 × 10⁻² ≈ 7.1 × 10⁻¹³. **Ka > Kb**, so HSO₃⁻ acts mainly as an acid (sodium hydrogen sulfite solutions are acidic).","how":{"ask":"Find the ion's Kb from its conjugate acid, compare it with the ion's own Ka, and let the larger one decide.","know":"• An **amphoteric** ion can donate or accept H⁺.\n• Its **Ka** is the Ka for losing its own H⁺.\n• Its **Kb = Kw / Ka(conjugate acid)**, the acid it becomes when it gains H⁺.\n• **Larger constant wins**: Ka > Kb → acid; Kb > Ka → base.","steps":[{"text":"HSO₃⁻ losing H⁺ is the second ionization, so its Ka is Ka2.","hl":"Ka2 = 6.3 × 10⁻⁸","calc":["Ka(HSO₃⁻) = **6.3 × 10⁻⁸**"]},{"text":"Gaining H⁺ turns HSO₃⁻ into H₂SO₃, whose Ka is Ka1. Use Kb = Kw/Ka(conjugate acid).","hl":"Ka1 = 1.4 × 10⁻²","calc":["Kb = Kw / Ka1","Kb = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ ÷ 1.4 × 10⁻²","Kb ≈ **7.1 × 10⁻¹³**"]},{"text":"Compare: 6.3 × 10⁻⁸ is about 10⁵ times larger than 7.1 × 10⁻¹³, so the acid reaction dominates.","calc":["Ka ≫ Kb → acid"]},{"text":"The right Kb with the wrong behavior ignores the comparison; 6.3 × 10⁻⁸ is the ion's Ka, not its Kb; 1.4 × 10⁻¹⁶ multiplies Kw by Ka1 instead of dividing.","strike":["7.1 × 10⁻¹³; basic","6.3 × 10⁻⁸; basic","1.4 × 10⁻¹⁶; acidic"]},{"text":"Answer: 7.1 × 10⁻¹³; acidic."}],"trap":"Using Kw/Ka2 for Kb: that is the Kb of SO₃²⁻. The Kb of HSO₃⁻ comes from the acid it forms, H₂SO₃ (Ka1)."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"Ka1 = 1.4 × 10⁻²","note":"H₂SO₃ → HSO₃⁻: the conjugate acid's Ka"},{"t":"Ka2 = 6.3 × 10⁻⁸","note":"HSO₃⁻ → SO₃²⁻: the ion's own Ka"},{"t":"HSO₃⁻","note":"can gain or lose H⁺"}]},{"text":"**Get Kb from the conjugate acid.** The ion's Kb is Kw divided by the Ka of the species it becomes after taking H⁺.","calc":["Kb = Kw / Ka1 = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ ÷ 1.4 × 10⁻² ≈ 7.1 × 10⁻¹³"]},{"text":"**Compare the two constants.** Whichever is larger names the main behavior.","calc":["Ka = 6.3 × 10⁻⁸ > Kb = 7.1 × 10⁻¹³ → acid"]},{"text":"**Also:** the same test on HCO₃⁻ (Ka = 4.7 × 10⁻¹¹; Kb = Kw/4.3 × 10⁻⁷ ≈ 2.3 × 10⁻⁸) gives Kb > Ka, so bicarbonate acts as a base."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for an amphoteric ion, Kb = Kw/Ka(its conjugate acid); compare it with the ion's own Ka, and the larger constant wins."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-13a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:13#1","pe3-cp:13#2","pe3-cp:13#3"],"q":"For 2 Al + 3 Cl₂ → 2 AlCl₃, a chemist mixes 5.4 g of Al (27 g/mol) and 14.2 g of Cl₂ (71 g/mol). Which reactant limits, and what remains?","choices":["Cl₂; 0.067 mol Al","Al; 0.067 mol Cl₂","Cl₂; 0.13 mol Al","Al; 0.10 mol Cl₂"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Equal moles (0.20 each), but Cl₂ is used 3 per 2 Al, so Cl₂ runs out first; Al can't limit."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Al limiting is out: 0.20 mol Al would need 0.30 mol Cl₂, more than the 0.20 mol present."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"0.13 mol is the Al consumed; the start amount was never subtracted to get what remains."}],"rule":"Divide each reactant's moles by its coefficient; the smaller result limits. Leftover = start − amount used.","exp":"Moles: Al = 5.4 ÷ 27 = 0.20 mol; Cl₂ = 14.2 ÷ 71 = 0.20 mol. Dividing by coefficients gives Al 0.10 and Cl₂ 0.067, so **Cl₂ limits**. It uses 0.20 × 2/3 ≈ 0.133 mol Al, leaving **0.067 mol Al** in excess.","how":{"ask":"Turn each mass into moles, divide by its coefficient, and the smallest result names the limiting reagent.","know":"• **Moles = mass ÷ molar mass.**\n• **Divide each reactant's moles by its coefficient**; the smallest quotient is the **limiting reagent**.\n• Whatever is **left over** when the reaction stops was **in excess**.","steps":[{"text":"Find the masses, molar masses and coefficients.","hl":"2 Al + 3 Cl₂ → 2 AlCl₃"},{"text":"Convert each reactant to moles.","calc":["Al: 5.4 g ÷ 27 g/mol = **0.20 mol**","Cl₂: 14.2 g ÷ 71 g/mol = **0.20 mol**"]},{"text":"Divide by each coefficient. The smaller value limits.","calc":["Al: 0.20 ÷ 2 = 0.10","Cl₂: 0.20 ÷ 3 = **0.067** → Cl₂ limits"]},{"text":"Equal moles do not mean neither limits: Cl₂ needs 3 per 2 Al. Choices that make Al limiting fail.","strike":["Al; 0.067 mol Cl₂","Al; 0.10 mol Cl₂"]},{"text":"Find the Al used, then subtract. 0.13 mol is the Al consumed, not what remains.","calc":["Al used = 0.20 mol Cl₂ × 2/3 = 0.133 mol","Al left = 0.20 − 0.133 = **0.067 mol**"],"strike":["Cl₂; 0.13 mol Al"]},{"text":"**Also:** Al left over at the end confirms it was the reactant in excess."},{"text":"Answer: Cl₂; 0.067 mol Al."}],"trap":"Comparing raw moles or masses: the moles must first be divided by the coefficients in the balanced equation."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"5.4 g of Al (27 g/mol)","note":"mass and molar mass of Al"},{"t":"14.2 g of Cl₂ (71 g/mol)","note":"mass and molar mass of Cl₂"},{"t":"2 Al + 3 Cl₂","note":"the coefficients"}]},{"text":"**Grams to moles.** Reactions count particles, not grams.","calc":["Al: 5.4 ÷ 27 = 0.20 mol","Cl₂: 14.2 ÷ 71 = 0.20 mol"]},{"text":"**Divide by the coefficient.** The smallest quotient runs out first.","calc":["Al: 0.20/2 = 0.10","Cl₂: 0.20/3 = 0.067 → limiting"]},{"text":"**Leftover = start − used.**","calc":["Al used = 0.20 × 2/3 = 0.133 mol","Al left = 0.067 mol"]},{"text":"**Also:** a reactant recovered unreacted at the end was in excess; the other one was limiting."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** moles ÷ coefficient for each reactant; the smallest is limiting, and the leftover reactant was in excess."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-15a","s":"Atoms & Periodic Trends","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:15#1","pe3-cp:15#2","pe3-cp:15#3"],"q":"Cadmium, Z = 48, lies directly below zinc and forms colorless Cd²⁺ salts. Which configuration describes the ground state of the Cd²⁺ ion?","choices":["[Kr]4d¹⁰","[Kr]4d⁸5s²","[Kr]4d⁹5s¹","[Kr]4d¹⁰5s²"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Order flipped: transition metals lose 5s before 4d, so a 2+ ion can't keep 5s²."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"Count: [Kr]4d¹⁰5s² has 48 electrons, the neutral atom; no electrons were removed for the 2+ ion."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Both electrons leave the 5s; taking one from each subshell is not how a group 12 cation forms."}],"rule":"Transition metals lose their outer s electrons before d electrons: remove from 5s first, then check the electron count.","exp":"Neutral Cd is [Kr]4d¹⁰5s² (36 + 10 + 2 = 48). Transition metals lose their **outer s electrons before d electrons**, so Cd²⁺ (46 electrons) is **[Kr]4d¹⁰**, a filled d subshell like Zn²⁺'s [Ar]3d¹⁰.","how":{"ask":"Write the neutral atom, then remove electrons from the highest-n subshell (5s) first.","know":"• Zn is **[Ar]3d¹⁰4s²**: a filled 3d and a filled 4s (18 + 10 + 2 = 30). Cd, below it, is [Kr]4d¹⁰5s².\n• Cations of transition metals **lose ns electrons before (n−1)d**: Zn²⁺ = [Ar]3d¹⁰.\n• **Half-filled** means d⁵ or s¹; filled means d¹⁰ or s².","steps":[{"text":"Start from the neutral atom.","hl":"Z = 48","calc":["Kr core: 36 electrons","36 + 10 (4d) + 2 (5s) = 48 → [Kr]4d¹⁰5s²"]},{"text":"Remove two electrons from 5s, the highest-n subshell.","calc":["Cd²⁺: 46 electrons → **[Kr]4d¹⁰**"]},{"text":"[Kr]4d¹⁰5s² is the neutral atom; no electrons were removed.","strike":["[Kr]4d¹⁰5s²"]},{"text":"[Kr]4d⁸5s² takes the electrons from 4d first, the wrong order.","strike":["[Kr]4d⁸5s²"]},{"text":"[Kr]4d⁹5s¹ takes one from each subshell; a half-filled s¹ is not what forms.","strike":["[Kr]4d⁹5s¹"]},{"text":"**Also, zinc:** Zn = [Ar]3d¹⁰4s² (filled 3d, filled 4s), and Zn²⁺ loses both 4s electrons first → [Ar]3d¹⁰."},{"text":"Answer: [Kr]4d¹⁰."}],"trap":"Removing d electrons first because d is filled last: on ionization, the ns electrons leave first."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key facts**.","marks":[{"t":"Z = 48","note":"electron count of the neutral atom"},{"t":"directly below zinc","note":"same pattern as Zn, one row down"},{"t":"Cd²⁺","note":"two electrons removed"}]},{"text":"**Fill the neutral atom.** Zn: [Ar]3d¹⁰4s², filled 3d and filled 4s. Cd, one row down: [Kr]4d¹⁰5s².","calc":["Zn: 18 + 10 + 2 = 30","Cd: 36 + 10 + 2 = 48"]},{"text":"**Remove ns first.** Zn²⁺ = [Ar]3d¹⁰; Cd²⁺ = [Kr]4d¹⁰. A filled d¹⁰ means all electrons are paired (diamagnetic, colorless salts)."},{"text":"**Also:** \"half-filled\" means d⁵ (e.g. Mn²⁺, [Ar]3d⁵) or s¹ (e.g. Cu, [Ar]3d¹⁰4s¹). Zn and its ion have only filled subshells, d¹⁰ and s²."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** fill the neutral atom, then ionize from the highest-n s subshell first; d¹⁰ and s² are filled, d⁵ and s¹ are half-filled."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-16a","s":"Electrochemistry & Redox","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:16#1","pe3-cp:32#1","pe3-cp:32#2","pe3-cp:32#3"],"q":"In aqueous AgNO₃, silver crystals grow on a copper wire as the solution turns blue. Which species in this reaction is reduced?","choices":["Ag⁺","Cu","NO₃⁻","H₂O"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Copper goes from 0 to +2, losing electrons: it is oxidized, the reducing agent, not the species reduced."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Nitrate is a spectator ion: N stays +5 and O stays −2 before and after, so it gains no electrons."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Water is only the solvent: no H₂ or O₂ forms, so H stays +1 and O stays −2."}],"rule":"Reduced = gains electrons = oxidation number falls. The metal that dissolves and colors the solution is oxidized.","exp":"Cu(s) + 2 Ag⁺ → Cu²⁺ + 2 Ag(s). Silver's oxidation state falls from +1 to 0, so **Ag⁺ gains an electron and is reduced**; it is the oxidizing agent. Copper rises from 0 to +2 and is oxidized; it is the reducing agent. Nitrate and water keep their oxidation states.","how":{"ask":"Assign oxidation states before and after, and find the species whose oxidation state falls.","know":"• **Oxidation** = loss of electrons (state rises); **reduction** = gain of electrons (state falls).\n• The species reduced is the **oxidizing agent**; the species oxidized is the **reducing agent**.\n• Elements (Cu, Ag, O₂) are 0; in NO₃⁻, N is +5 and O is −2; in H₂O, H is +1 and O is −2 (O is −1 in peroxides such as H₂O₂).\n• **Any change in oxidation state makes a reaction redox.**","steps":[{"text":"Write the change: silver metal forms and blue Cu²⁺ appears.","hl":"silver crystals grow on a copper wire","calc":["Cu(s) + 2 Ag⁺(aq) → Cu²⁺(aq) + 2 Ag(s)"]},{"text":"Assign oxidation states. Silver's falls, so Ag⁺ gains electrons and is reduced. Copper's rises, so Cu is oxidized; calling it reduced mixes up the reducing agent with the species reduced.","calc":["Ag: +1 → 0 (gains 1 e⁻, **reduced**)","Cu: 0 → +2 (loses 2 e⁻, oxidized)"],"strike":["Cu"]},{"text":"Nitrate and water keep their oxidation states: NO₃⁻ is a spectator ion and water is the solvent.","calc":["NO₃⁻: N + 3(−2) = −1 → N = +5, before and after","H₂O: H +1, O −2, before and after"],"strike":["NO₃⁻","H₂O"]},{"text":"**Also, oxygen:** O₂ → H₂O₂ lowers O from 0 to −1; O₂ → H₂O lowers it to −2. O gains electrons, so O₂ is reduced.","calc":["O₂ (0) → H₂O₂ (−1) → H₂O (−2)"]},{"text":"**Also, cofactors:** a cofactor that is reduced and then reoxidized (e.g. FAD → FADH₂ → FAD) ends where it started, so it is not net changed; ask which species is reduced overall."},{"text":"Answer: Ag⁺."}],"trap":"Picking Cu because it is the reducing agent: the reducing agent is itself oxidized. The species reduced is the one whose oxidation state falls."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key observations**.","marks":[{"t":"copper wire","note":"Cu metal, oxidation state 0"},{"t":"AgNO₃","note":"Ag⁺, oxidation state +1"},{"t":"turns blue","note":"Cu²⁺ forming"}]},{"text":"**Track oxidation states.** Cu 0 → +2 and Ag +1 → 0: states change, so the reaction is redox, even though a solid appears."},{"text":"**Falls = gains electrons = reduced.** Ag⁺ is reduced (the oxidizing agent); Cu is oxidized (the reducing agent)."},{"text":"**Also:** O₂ to H₂O₂ or H₂O lowers oxygen from 0 to −1 or −2, so O₂ is reduced (it is the oxidizing agent)."},{"text":"**Also:** in a multistep pathway, a cofactor that is reduced and then regenerated is not net changed; the overall reduced species is the one whose oxidation state ends lower."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** assign oxidation states; the species whose state falls gains electrons and is reduced (the oxidizing agent), and any change at all makes the reaction redox."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-17a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:17#1","pe3-cp:17#2"],"q":"A feverish patient's temperature reads 122 °F. Expressed on the absolute scale used in gas-law calculations, this temperature is about:","choices":["323 K","50 K","395 K","341 K"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"impossible","why":"Sense check: 50 K is colder than liquid nitrogen; a body is about 310 K, so 50 is a °C value."},{"c":2,"tag":"impossible","why":"395 K is about 122 °C, above boiling, no living patient: it adds 273 to °F without converting to °C first."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"Skipped subtracting 32 before multiplying by 5/9, so the value comes out too hot."}],"rule":"°C = (°F − 32) × 5/9, then add 273. A living body is near 310 K, so anything far from that is out.","exp":"**°F → °C: subtract 32, then × 5/9**: (122 − 32) × 5/9 = 90 × 5/9 = 50 °C. **K = °C + 273** = 323 K.","how":{"ask":"Convert °F to °C, then °C to kelvin.","know":"• **°C = (°F − 32) × 5/9**.\n• **K = °C + 273**.\n• Never add 273 to a Fahrenheit value.","steps":[{"text":"Start from the Fahrenheit reading.","hl":"122 °F","calc":["°C = (122 − 32) × 5/9","= 90 × 5/9","= **50 °C**"]},{"text":"Add 273 to reach kelvin.","calc":["K = 50 + 273 = **323 K**"]},{"text":"50 K stops at Celsius; 395 K adds 273 to the Fahrenheit value; 341 K skips subtracting 32 (122 × 5/9 + 273).","strike":["50 K","395 K","341 K"]},{"text":"Answer: 323 K."}],"trap":"Skipping the −32 (122 × 5/9 + 273 ≈ 341 K): subtract 32 first, then scale by 5/9."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"122 °F","note":"Fahrenheit: convert to °C first"},{"t":"absolute scale","note":"kelvin"}]},{"text":"**°F → °C.** Subtract 32, then multiply by 5/9.","calc":["(122 − 32) × 5/9 = 50 °C"]},{"text":"**Also:** °C → K: add 273.","calc":["50 + 273 = 323 K"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** °C = (°F − 32) × 5/9 and K = °C + 273; gas laws always use kelvin."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-18a","s":"Reading Data","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe3-cp:18#1","pe3-cp:18#2","pe4-cp:15#1","pe4-cp:15#2"],"q":"A stimulator outputs 0.30 ms pulses separated by 7.7 ms of zero output, repeating steadily. What is the pulse frequency?","choices":["125 Hz","130 Hz","3333 Hz","135 Hz"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"135 Hz subtracts the times (7.7 − 0.30 ms); one period is the pulse plus the gap, so add them."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"Too big: 3333 Hz inverts the 0.30 ms pulse alone, but the pattern repeats only every ~8 ms."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"130 Hz inverts the 7.7 ms gap alone; the period is longer than the gap, so f must be lower."}],"rule":"Period = on time + off time; f = 1 ÷ period. Round: the period is about 8 ms, so f is just over 100 Hz.","exp":"**One period is one full repeat: on time plus off time** = 0.30 ms + 7.7 ms = 8.0 ms. Inverting that period in seconds (**f = 1/T**) gives 1 ÷ 0.0080 s = **125 Hz**.","how":{"ask":"Find the time for one complete on-plus-off cycle, then invert it.","know":"• **Period T** = time for one full repeat of the pattern = on time + off time.\n• **f = 1/T**, with T in seconds for f in hertz.\n• 1 ms = 10⁻³ s.","steps":[{"text":"One repeat is a pulse plus the gap after it.","hl":"0.30 ms pulses","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Stimulator output","x":{"label":"Time (ms)","min":0,"max":16,"step":4},"y":{"label":"Output (on = 1)","min":0,"max":1.5,"step":0.5},"series":[{"name":"Output","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,1],[0.3,1],[0.3,0],[8,0],[8,1],[8.3,1],[8.3,0],[16,0]]}]},"calc":["T = t_on + t_off","T = 0.30 ms + 7.7 ms = **8.0 ms**"]},{"text":"Invert the period, in seconds.","calc":["8.0 ms = 8.0 × 10⁻³ s","f = 1/T = 1 ÷ 0.0080 s","f = **125 Hz**"]},{"text":"3333 Hz inverts the pulse alone (1 ÷ 0.30 ms) and 130 Hz the gap alone (1 ÷ 7.7 ms). 135 Hz subtracts the times (7.7 − 0.30 = 7.4 ms) instead of adding them.","strike":["3333 Hz","130 Hz","135 Hz"]},{"text":"Answer: 125 Hz."}],"trap":"Using only the pulse width as the period: the pattern does not repeat until the off time has passed too."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number**.","marks":[{"t":"0.30 ms pulses","note":"on time"},{"t":"7.7 ms of zero output","note":"off time"},{"t":"repeating steadily","note":"periodic"}]},{"text":"**One period is one full repeat.** Start at the rise of one pulse and stop at the rise of the next.","calc":["T = 0.30 + 7.7 = 8.0 ms"]},{"text":"**Also:** frequency is the number of repeats per second, f = 1/T.","calc":["f = 1 ÷ 0.0080 s = 125 Hz"]},{"text":"**Also:** T runs from one pulse's start to the next pulse's start, however short each pulse is, and ms means 10⁻³ s, so convert before taking 1/T.","calc":["starts 40 ms apart: T = 40 × 10⁻³ s = 0.040 s","f = 1 ÷ 0.040 s = 25 Hz"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for a pulsed signal, T = on time + off time, and f = 1/T with T in seconds."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-19a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:19#1","pe3-cp:19#2","pe3-cp:19#3","pe4-cp:6#1","pe4-cp:6#2","pe4-cp:6#3","pe5-cp:56#1"],"q":"Taking hc = 1.98 × 10⁻²⁵ J·m, what is the greatest photon energy from a lamp emitting wavelengths between 330 nm and 550 nm?","choices":["6.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J","3.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J","6.0 × 10⁻²⁸ J","6.5 × 10⁻³² J"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"Off by about a billion: that divides by 330 as if in meters; convert nm to m (× 10⁻⁹) first."},{"c":3,"tag":"units","why":"Units: hc × λ gives J·m², not J; E = hc/λ divides by λ."},{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"That's the 550 nm photon; longer wavelength means less energy, so it's the weakest, not the greatest."}],"rule":"E = hc/λ, so the shortest wavelength gives the most energy. Round: 2 × 10⁻²⁵ ÷ (3 × 10⁻⁷) is a bit under 10⁻¹⁸ J.","exp":"**E = hc/λ** puts λ in the denominator, so the most energetic photon has the **shortest** wavelength, 330 nm = 3.30 × 10⁻⁷ m. E = 1.98 × 10⁻²⁵ J·m ÷ 3.30 × 10⁻⁷ m = **6.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J**. The 550 nm photons carry only 3.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J.","how":{"ask":"Pick the wavelength that gives the most energy, convert it to metres, and divide hc by it.","know":"• **E = hc/λ**: a shorter wavelength means a more energetic photon (E ∝ 1/λ).\n• **n (nano) = 10⁻⁹**: 1 nm = 10⁻⁹ m.\n• With hc in J·m, λ must be in metres to get joules.","steps":[{"text":"E = hc/λ, so the smallest λ gives the largest E. Use 330 nm, not 550 nm.","hl":"between 330 nm and 550 nm","calc":["E ∝ 1/λ","330 nm < 550 nm → 330 nm gives the most energy"]},{"text":"Convert nanometres to metres.","calc":["330 nm = 330 × 10⁻⁹ m = **3.30 × 10⁻⁷ m**"]},{"text":"Divide hc by the wavelength.","hl":"hc = 1.98 × 10⁻²⁵ J·m","calc":["E = hc ÷ λ","E = 1.98 × 10⁻²⁵ J·m ÷ 3.30 × 10⁻⁷ m","E = **6.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J**"]},{"text":"3.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J is the 550 nm photon, the least energetic (1.98 × 10⁻²⁵ ÷ 5.50 × 10⁻⁷). 6.0 × 10⁻²⁸ J divides by 330 without converting nm to m. 6.5 × 10⁻³² J multiplies hc by λ instead of dividing.","strike":["3.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J","6.0 × 10⁻²⁸ J","6.5 × 10⁻³² J"]},{"text":"Answer: 6.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J."}],"trap":"Using the longest wavelength because it is the bigger number: energy goes as 1/λ, so the shortest wavelength wins."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number**.","marks":[{"t":"hc = 1.98 × 10⁻²⁵ J·m","note":"constant, in J·m"},{"t":"greatest photon energy","note":"want the maximum E"},{"t":"330 nm","note":"shortest λ"},{"t":"550 nm","note":"longest λ"}]},{"text":"**Photon energy: E = hc/λ.** Halve the wavelength and the energy doubles, so energy climbs toward the short-wavelength end.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Photon energy across the lamp's range","x":{"label":"Wavelength (nm)","min":300,"max":600,"step":50},"y":{"label":"Energy (× 10⁻¹⁹ J)","min":0,"max":7,"step":1},"series":[{"name":"E = hc/λ","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[330,6],[400,4.95],[450,4.4],[500,3.96],[550,3.6]]}]}},{"text":"**Also:** nm means 10⁻⁹ m. Convert before dividing.","calc":["330 nm = 3.30 × 10⁻⁷ m"]},{"text":"**Compute.**","calc":["E = 1.98 × 10⁻²⁵ ÷ 3.30 × 10⁻⁷ = 6.0 × 10⁻¹⁹ J"]},{"text":"**Also:** to get electronvolts, divide the energy in joules by 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J/eV; the shortcut E(eV) ≈ 1240/λ(nm) skips both conversions.","calc":["450 nm: E = 1.98 × 10⁻²⁵ ÷ 4.5 × 10⁻⁷ = 4.4 × 10⁻¹⁹ J","4.4 × 10⁻¹⁹ J ÷ 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J/eV ≈ 2.8 eV","shortcut: 1240 ÷ 450 ≈ 2.8 eV ✓"]},{"text":"**Also:** run the shortcut backward to get a wavelength from an energy: λ(nm) ≈ 1240 ÷ E(eV).","calc":["E = 3.1 eV: λ ≈ 1240 ÷ 3.1 = 400 nm (violet)","E = 2.0 eV: λ ≈ 1240 ÷ 2.0 = 620 nm (orange-red)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** E = hc/λ with λ in metres (1 nm = 10⁻⁹ m); the shortest wavelength in a range gives the most energetic photon."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-20a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:20#1","pe3-cp:20#2","pe4-cp:14#1"],"q":"Two large parallel plates are held at 12 V, the left one positive. Which describes the field lines between them, away from the edges?","choices":["Evenly spaced; left to right","Evenly spaced; right to left","Denser near plates; left to right","Curved outward; right to left"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Field lines leave the positive plate and end on the negative one, so they can't point toward the positive plate."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Wrong way and wrong shape: lines run from + to −, and they curve only at the plate edges."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Large plates, away from the edges: the field is uniform, so lines can't crowd near a plate."}],"rule":"Between large parallel plates the field is uniform (straight, equally spaced lines) and points from the + plate to the − plate.","exp":"Between large parallel plates the field has the **same strength and direction at every point**, so its lines are straight and **evenly spaced**. Field lines start on positive charge and end on negative charge, so they run **from the + plate to the − plate**: left to right.","how":{"ask":"Decide what a uniform field looks like, then which way its lines point.","know":"• **Line spacing shows strength**: closer lines mean a stronger field.\n• Between large parallel plates the field is **uniform**: E = V/d at every point, so lines are straight, parallel and evenly spaced.\n• Field lines point **from + to −**, the way a positive test charge would be pushed.","steps":[{"text":"Large plates, away from the edges: the field is uniform. If the gap were 4.0 mm, E would be the same everywhere, so there is no crowding near a plate and no curving (lines bulge only at the edges).","hl":"Two large parallel plates","calc":["E = V ÷ d","E = 12 V ÷ 0.0040 m = **3000 V/m** at every point"],"strike":["Denser near plates; left to right","Curved outward; right to left"]},{"text":"A positive test charge is pushed away from the positive plate and toward the negative one, so the lines run from left to right.","hl":"the left one positive","strike":["Evenly spaced; right to left"]},{"text":"Answer: evenly spaced; left to right."}],"trap":"Drawing the lines from − to +: field lines leave positive charge and end on negative charge."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"large parallel plates","note":"uniform field"},{"t":"the left one positive","note":"sets the direction"},{"t":"away from the edges","note":"no fringing"}]},{"text":"**Uniform means the same everywhere.** Equal strength means equal line spacing; equal direction means straight, parallel lines.","calc":["E = V/d, the same at every point between the plates"]},{"text":"**Also:** field lines point from + to −: they start on the positive plate and end on the negative plate."},{"text":"**Also:** the field's size is E = V/d, with d in metres and 1 kV = 10³ V.","calc":["12 kV across 5.0 mm","E = 12 × 10³ V ÷ 5.0 × 10⁻³ m = 2.4 × 10⁶ V/m"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** between parallel plates the field lines are straight, parallel and evenly spaced, pointing from the positive plate to the negative plate."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-22a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:22#1","pe3-cp:22#2","pe3-cp:40#1","pe3-cp:40#2"],"q":"Cholesterol oxidase converts cholesterol to the ketone shown, handing the two removed hydrogens to O₂. Which enzyme belongs to the same class?","fig":["cholesterol","cpt-cholestenone"],"choices":["Lactate dehydrogenase","Hexokinase","Triose phosphate isomerase","Pyruvate carboxylase"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Hexokinase only moves a phosphoryl group onto glucose: a transferase, no electrons change hands."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"The name says isomerase: it rearranges one molecule, a different class from an oxidase."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"A carboxylase adds CO₂ using ATP (a ligase); no H is removed."}],"rule":"Removing H atoms or electrons makes an enzyme an oxidoreductase: look for -oxidase, -reductase or -dehydrogenase in the name.","exp":"Removing hydrogens (electrons) from a substrate is an **oxidation**, so any oxidase is an **oxidoreductase**, like glucose oxidase. Lactate dehydrogenase also moves H between lactate and NAD⁺: an oxidoreductase. Hexokinase is a transferase (moves a phosphoryl), triose phosphate isomerase an isomerase, and pyruvate carboxylase a ligase (uses ATP to join CO₂).","how":{"ask":"Classify the oxidase's reaction, then classify each listed enzyme by what it does.","know":"The six classes:\n• **Oxidoreductase**: moves electrons/H (oxidases, dehydrogenases, reductases).\n• **Transferase**: moves a group (phosphoryl, methyl, amino) between molecules (kinases, methyltransferases).\n• **Hydrolase**: breaks a bond by adding water (proteases, lipases).\n• **Lyase**: breaks or makes a bond without water or redox, often leaving a C=C (decarboxylases, aldolase).\n• **Isomerase**: rearranges one molecule into an isomer.\n• **Ligase**: joins two molecules using ATP (synthetases, carboxylases).","steps":[{"text":"Removing two H from a substrate and passing them to O₂ is an oxidation: an oxidoreductase.","hl":"handing the two removed hydrogens to O₂","calc":["cholesterol − 2 H → ketone (oxidized)","O₂ + 2 H → H₂O₂ (reduced)"]},{"text":"Classify the other enzymes by their reactions.","calc":["lactate dehydrogenase: lactate + NAD⁺ ⇌ pyruvate + NADH → **oxidoreductase**","hexokinase: ATP's phosphoryl → glucose → transferase","triose phosphate isomerase: DHAP ⇌ G3P → isomerase","pyruvate carboxylase: pyruvate + CO₂ + ATP → ligase"]},{"text":"Hexokinase only moves a phosphoryl group; no electrons change hands.","strike":["Hexokinase"]},{"text":"Triose phosphate isomerase rearranges one molecule; pyruvate carboxylase joins two using ATP.","strike":["Triose phosphate isomerase","Pyruvate carboxylase"]},{"text":"Answer: lactate dehydrogenase."}],"trap":"Thinking only enzymes named “oxidase” are oxidoreductases: dehydrogenases and reductases are in the same class."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"oxidase","note":"oxidation"},{"t":"two removed hydrogens","note":"electrons leave the substrate"},{"t":"same class","note":"match the reaction type"}]},{"text":"**Ask what moves.** Electrons or H → oxidoreductase; a group between molecules → transferase; water splits a bond → hydrolase; a bond breaks without water or redox → lyase; one molecule rearranges → isomerase; ATP joins two molecules → ligase."},{"text":"**Also:** glucose oxidase oxidizes glucose (removes 2 H, which go to O₂), so it is an oxidoreductase.","calc":["glucose − 2 H → gluconolactone","O₂ + 2 H → H₂O₂"]},{"text":"**Also:** an enzyme that moves a methyl group from a donor (such as SAM) onto a molecule is a transferase (a methyltransferase).","calc":["donor–CH₃ + R–H → donor–H + R–CH₃"]},{"text":"**Also:** turning a –CH₃ into –CH₂OH with O₂ adds a C–O bond, an oxidation, so that enzyme is an oxidoreductase.","calc":["R–CH₃ + [O] → R–CH₂OH (C gains a bond to O)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** name the class by what the enzyme moves: electrons → oxidoreductase, a group → transferase, water → hydrolase, no water → lyase, within one molecule → isomerase, ATP-driven joining → ligase."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-23a","s":"Electrochemistry & Redox","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:23#1","pe3-cp:23#2","pe3-cp:23#3"],"q":"A fuel-cell anode oxidizes hydrazine: N₂H₄ → N₂ + 4H⁺ + 4e⁻. If 193 C of charge flows, what mass of hydrazine (32 g/mol) was consumed? (F = 96,500 C/mol)","choices":["16 mg","32 mg","64 mg","256 mg"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Direction: more electrons per molecule means fewer molecules per coulomb, so × 4 goes the wrong way."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"64 mg treats each N₂H₄ as giving 1 e⁻; the half-reaction shows 4 e⁻, so a ÷ 4 is missing."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"32 mg divides by 2 electrons; the half-reaction releases 4 e⁻ per N₂H₄."}],"rule":"mol e⁻ = charge ÷ F, then ÷ electrons per molecule, then × molar mass. Round: 193 C is 2 × 10⁻³ mol e⁻.","exp":"**mol e⁻ = Q/F** = 193 ÷ 96,500 = 2.00 × 10⁻³ mol. Each N₂H₄ gives 4 e⁻, so **mol N₂H₄ = mol e⁻ ÷ 4** = 5.0 × 10⁻⁴ mol. **× molar mass**: 5.0 × 10⁻⁴ × 32 g/mol = 0.016 g = 16 mg.","how":{"ask":"Charge → moles of electrons → moles of fuel → grams.","know":"• **mol e⁻ = Q / F** (F = 96,500 C/mol).\n• **mol substance = mol e⁻ ÷ electrons per molecule** (from the half-reaction).\n• **mass = mol × molar mass**.","steps":[{"text":"Convert charge to moles of electrons.","hl":"193 C","calc":["mol e⁻ = Q / F","= 193 ÷ 96,500","= **2.00 × 10⁻³ mol**"]},{"text":"The half-reaction releases 4 e⁻ per N₂H₄.","hl":"4e⁻","calc":["mol N₂H₄ = 2.00 × 10⁻³ ÷ 4 = **5.0 × 10⁻⁴ mol**"]},{"text":"Convert to mass.","calc":["m = 5.0 × 10⁻⁴ mol × 32 g/mol","= 0.016 g = **16 mg**"]},{"text":"32 mg divides by 2 instead of 4; 64 mg skips the ÷ 4; 256 mg multiplies by 4.","strike":["32 mg","64 mg","256 mg"]},{"text":"Answer: 16 mg."}],"trap":"Treating moles of electrons as moles of substance: divide by the electrons each molecule transfers."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"193 C","note":"charge Q"},{"t":"96,500 C/mol","note":"Faraday's constant"},{"t":"4e⁻","note":"electrons per N₂H₄"},{"t":"32 g/mol","note":"molar mass"}]},{"text":"**Charge to electrons.**","calc":["mol e⁻ = Q/F = 193 ÷ 96,500 = 2.00 × 10⁻³ mol"]},{"text":"**Electrons to molecules.** Divide by the electrons each molecule gives up.","calc":["2.00 × 10⁻³ ÷ 4 = 5.0 × 10⁻⁴ mol N₂H₄"]},{"text":"**Also:** moles × molar mass gives the mass.","calc":["5.0 × 10⁻⁴ × 32 = 0.016 g = 16 mg"]},{"text":"**Also:** a sensor that oxidizes glucose collects 2 e⁻ per glucose, so there mol glucose = mol e⁻ ÷ 2.","calc":["2.00 × 10⁻³ mol e⁻ ÷ 2 = 1.00 × 10⁻³ mol glucose","1.00 × 10⁻³ mol × 180 g/mol = 0.180 g"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** mol e⁻ = Q/F; divide by electrons per molecule; multiply by molar mass."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-24a","s":"Metabolism & Bioenergetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:24#1","pe3-cp:24#2"],"q":"Succinate dehydrogenase strips two H atoms from succinate, making fumarate. In what form is its covalently bound flavin cofactor at the end of this step?","choices":["FADH₂","FAD","NADH","NADP⁺"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"FAD is the oxidized form that enters; after taking two H atoms the flavin leaves reduced."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Not a flavin: NADH is a nicotinamide carrier, and the question names a flavin."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"NADP⁺ is a nicotinamide carrier too, not a flavin."}],"rule":"A carrier that accepts H atoms leaves in its reduced form; also match the carrier type the stem names (flavin vs nicotinamide).","exp":"The flavin starts as oxidized **FAD**, accepts the **2 H (2 H⁺ + 2 e⁻)** taken from succinate, and leaves as reduced **FADH₂**. NADH and NADP⁺ are nicotinamide carriers, not flavins, and FAD is the form before the step.","how":{"ask":"Identify the flavin cofactor, then decide whether accepting two H leaves it oxidized or reduced.","know":"• **Flavin cofactor**: FAD (or FMN), often **tightly bound** to its enzyme.\n• **FAD + 2 H⁺ + 2 e⁻ → FADH₂**: accepting 2 H reduces it.\n• An acceptor must start oxidized (FAD, NAD⁺); it ends reduced (FADH₂, NADH).","steps":[{"text":"Two H atoms leave the substrate and go to the bound flavin.","hl":"strips two H atoms from succinate","calc":["succinate → fumarate + 2 H"]},{"text":"The flavin is FAD; adding 2 H gives FADH₂.","calc":["FAD + 2 H⁺ + 2 e⁻ → **FADH₂**"]},{"text":"FAD is the form that enters, not the one that leaves.","strike":["FAD"]},{"text":"NADH and NADP⁺ are nicotinamide cofactors, not flavins.","strike":["NADH","NADP⁺"]},{"text":"Answer: FADH₂."}],"trap":"Choosing FAD because it is the flavin's usual name: after accepting the two H it is FADH₂."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"strips two H atoms","note":"oxidation of succinate"},{"t":"covalently bound flavin cofactor","note":"FAD"},{"t":"at the end of this step","note":"after accepting H"}]},{"text":"**Two H in, reduced out.** The bound flavin accepts both hydrogens.","calc":["FAD (oxidized) + 2 H → FADH₂ (reduced)"]},{"text":"**Also:** glucose oxidase works the same way with tightly bound FAD: glucose gives 2 H to FAD, and the FADH₂ then hands them to O₂, regenerating FAD.","calc":["glucose + E–FAD → gluconolactone + E–FADH₂","E–FADH₂ + O₂ → E–FAD + H₂O₂"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a bound flavin enzyme uses FAD, which accepts 2 H (2 H⁺ + 2 e⁻) and becomes FADH₂."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-25a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:25#1","pe3-cp:25#2","pe5-cp:21#1"],"q":"A hormone enters its target cell and binds an intracellular steroid receptor. Which molecule has the ring skeleton of a steroid?","choices":["Estradiol","Epinephrine","Thyroxine","Prostaglandin E₂"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Epinephrine is an amine (catecholamine) hormone with one ring; one ring can't be a four-ring core."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Thyroxine has two benzene rings linked through an O, not four fused rings."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Prostaglandins come from a 20-C fatty acid; one five-membered ring, not four fused rings."}],"rule":"Steroid core: 6-6-6-5 fused ring system; sex hormones and cortisol are steroids, amines and eicosanoids are not.","exp":"A steroid is defined by its **6-6-6-5 fused ring core** (rings A, B and C of six atoms, ring D of five). Estradiol has it. Epinephrine has one benzene ring, thyroxine has two separate rings joined by an ether O, and prostaglandin E₂ has a single five-membered ring with two chains.","how":{"ask":"Look for four fused rings, three of six atoms and one of five, in each molecule.","know":"• **Steroid skeleton**: four **fused** rings, **6-6-6-5** (rings A, B, C, D), 17 ring carbons, derived from cholesterol.\n• Fused = neighboring rings share a bond.\n• Rings joined by a chain or an O are not fused, and a single ring is never a steroid.","steps":[{"text":"A steroid receptor binds molecules built on the steroid core.","hl":"steroid receptor"},{"text":"Draw the core to search for: three six-membered rings and one five-membered ring, all fused.","fig":["t3-steroid-abcd","cholesterol"],"calc":["rings: 6 + 6 + 6 + 5","fused: each pair shares one bond"]},{"text":"Estradiol has rings A–D (ring A aromatic): the 6-6-6-5 core."},{"text":"Epinephrine is a catecholamine with one benzene ring; thyroxine has two benzene rings linked through an O, not fused.","strike":["Epinephrine","Thyroxine"]},{"text":"Prostaglandin E₂ has a single five-membered ring with two side chains, a fatty-acid derivative.","strike":["Prostaglandin E₂"]},{"text":"Answer: estradiol."}],"trap":"Choosing any lipid-soluble or ring-containing hormone: only the fused 6-6-6-5 skeleton makes a steroid."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"steroid receptor","note":"needs a steroid"},{"t":"ring skeleton","note":"count and fuse the rings"}]},{"text":"**Know the core.** Four rings fused edge to edge: three six-membered (A, B, C) and one five-membered (D).","fig":"t3-steroid-abcd","calc":["6 + 6 + 6 + 5 = four rings, 17 ring C"]},{"text":"**Spot it in a drawing.** Find the five-membered ring, then check that it is fused to three six-membered rings in a row; extra groups (OH, C=O, a side chain) do not matter.","fig":"cholesterol"},{"text":"**Rule out look-alikes.** One ring, rings joined by a linker, or no rings at all: not a steroid."},{"text":"**Also:** every steroid hormone (estradiol, cortisol, progesterone) is built from cholesterol, which already has the 6-6-6-5 core; the body shortens its 8-carbon side chain (to 2 carbons in progesterone and cortisol, none in estradiol) and keeps rings A–D. Asked for a steroid's precursor, choose the lipid drawn with those four rings, not a fatty acid or another open-chain lipid.","fig":"cholesterol"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a steroid has four fused rings, three of six atoms and one of five; look for that 6-6-6-5 skeleton and ignore the groups on it."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-26a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe3-cp:26#1","pe3-cp:26#2","pe3-cp:26#3","pe4-cp:40#1","pe6-cp:8#1"],"q":"A carbonate buffer (HCO₃⁻/CO₃²⁻, pKa 10.3) reads pH 10.8. Which compound, when added, moves it toward its greatest buffer capacity?","choices":["NaHCO₃","Na₂CO₃","NaOH","NaCl"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"pH 10.8 is already above pKa 10.3; adding more of the base form pushes the pH even higher."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Sign: pH 10.8 is above pKa 10.3, so the pH must fall; a strong base only pushes it higher."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"NaCl is neither buffer form, so it can't change the base:acid ratio at all."}],"rule":"Buffer capacity peaks at pH = pKa. If the pH is above pKa, add the acid form; if below, add the base form.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Titration of 30.0 mL of 0.040 M NaHCO₃ with 0.080 M NaOH","x":{"label":"Volume of NaOH added (mL)","min":0,"max":15,"step":3},"y":{"label":"pH","min":9,"max":12,"step":0.5},"series":[{"name":"pH","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[1.5,9.35],[3,9.7],[6,10.12],[7.5,10.3],[9,10.48],[11.4,10.8],[13.5,11.25]]}]},"exp":"**pH = pKa + log([base]/[acid])**. At pH 10.8 the ratio [CO₃²⁻]/[HCO₃⁻] = 10^0.5 ≈ 3.2. Capacity is greatest at **pH = pKa**, where the ratio is 1, so the pH must fall: add more of the **acid form**, NaHCO₃.","how":{"ask":"Decide whether the pH must rise or fall to reach pKa, then add the form that moves it that way.","know":"• **Henderson–Hasselbalch**: pH = pKa + log([base]/[acid]).\n• A buffer works best at **pH = pKa** ([base] = [acid]).\n• To **lower** pH, raise the share of the **acid** form; to raise pH, add the base form.","steps":[{"text":"Read the buffer pair and its pKa.","hl":"HCO₃⁻/CO₃²⁻, pKa 10.3","calc":["acid = HCO₃⁻, base = CO₃²⁻"]},{"text":"Find the current base:acid ratio.","hl":"pH 10.8","calc":["10.8 = 10.3 + log(ratio)","log(ratio) = 0.5","ratio = 10^0.5 ≈ **3.2**"],"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"pH","x":11.4,"text":"now: pH 10.8","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"Best capacity is at pH = pKa = 10.3, ratio 1. The ratio must fall, so the pH must drop by 0.5: add the acid form.","calc":["pH 10.8 → 10.3: add HCO₃⁻ (as NaHCO₃)"],"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"pH","x":7.5,"text":"pH = pKa 10.3","at":"left"}]}},{"text":"Na₂CO₃ and NaOH raise the base share and push the pH higher; NaCl adds neither buffer form.","strike":["Na₂CO₃","NaOH","NaCl"]},{"text":"Answer: NaHCO₃."}],"trap":"Adding the base form because the pH must \"reach\" pKa: here the buffer is above its pKa, so it needs more acid form."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"pKa 10.3","note":"best buffering pH"},{"t":"pH 10.8","note":"current pH, above pKa"},{"t":"greatest buffer capacity","note":"pH = pKa"}]},{"text":"**Write Henderson–Hasselbalch.** It links pH to the base:acid ratio.","calc":["pH = pKa + log([base]/[acid])","10.8 − 10.3 = 0.5 → ratio ≈ 3.2"]},{"text":"**Aim for pH = pKa.** There the ratio is 1, and the buffer resists added acid and base equally well."},{"text":"**Lower pH = more acid form.** The pH is too high, so raise [HCO₃⁻]."},{"text":"**Also:** for a phosphate buffer, the acid form is H₂PO₄⁻ (the monosodium salt, NaH₂PO₄); adding it lowers the pH, while Na₂HPO₄ raises it."},{"text":"**Also:** a buffer works only within pKa ± 1, where the base:acid ratio stays between one-tenth and ten; check each pH against that window.","calc":["pKa 10.3 → useful from pH 9.3 to 11.3"]},{"text":"**Also:** write Henderson–Hasselbalch with the two forms named before plugging in: the conjugate base (A⁻, the salt form) goes on top and the weak acid (HA) on the bottom. Then undo the log to get the ratio.","calc":["pH = pKa + log([A⁻]/[HA])","phosphate, pKa 7.2, target pH 6.9: log ratio = −0.3","[A⁻]/[HA] = 10^−0.3 ≈ 0.5, i.e. 1 : 2"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** pH = pKa + log([base]/[acid]); to lower the pH add the acid form, to raise it add the base form, and capacity peaks at pH = pKa."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-27a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:27#1","pe3-cp:27#2"],"q":"Glycine (75 Da) and leucine (131 Da) are coupled into the dipeptide shown by a single peptide bond. What is the dipeptide's mass?","fig":"cpt-gly-leu-sites","choices":["170 Da","188 Da","206 Da","224 Da"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Direction: a condensation releases water, so the dipeptide must weigh less than 75 + 131 = 206 Da, never more."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"206 Da is the plain sum; every peptide bond formed gives off one H₂O, so the mass must drop."},{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"170 Da removes two waters, but a single peptide bond releases only one H₂O."}],"rule":"Each peptide bond loses one water (18 Da): add the amino acid masses, then subtract 18 per bond.","exp":"Forming a peptide bond is a **condensation**: one H₂O (18 Da) leaves. Sum the amino acids, 75 + 131 = 206 Da, then subtract one water: 206 − 18 = **188 Da**.","how":{"ask":"Add the two amino-acid masses, then subtract one water for the one peptide bond.","know":"• Peptide bond formation releases **one H₂O, 18 amu**, per bond (condensation).\n• **Peptide mass = sum of amino-acid masses − 18 × (number of peptide bonds)**.","steps":[{"text":"Find the masses and the number of bonds formed.","hl":"Glycine (75 Da) and leucine (131 Da)"},{"text":"Add the free amino acids.","calc":["75 + 131 = 206 Da"]},{"text":"One peptide bond releases one water.","calc":["206 − 18 = **188 Da**"]},{"text":"206 Da forgets the lost water, 170 Da removes two waters, and 224 Da adds a water instead of removing it.","strike":["206 Da","170 Da","224 Da"]},{"text":"Answer: 188 Da."}],"trap":"Reporting the simple sum (206 Da): the condensation removes H₂O, so the dipeptide is 18 Da lighter."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"75 Da","note":"glycine"},{"t":"131 Da","note":"leucine"},{"t":"a single peptide bond","note":"one water lost"}]},{"text":"**Name the byproduct.** The carboxyl OH of one amino acid and an H from the other's amino group leave together as H₂O.","calc":["H₂O = 2(1) + 16 = 18 amu"]},{"text":"**Sum, then subtract.**","calc":["75 + 131 − 18 = 188 Da"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** each peptide bond releases one H₂O (18 amu), so a dipeptide's mass is the sum of its amino acids minus 18."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-28a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe3-cp:28#1","pe3-cp:28#2","pe3-cp:28#3","pe3-cp:28#4"],"q":"A student is planning Michaelis–Menten measurements on a purified enzyme. Which condition is NOT required for valid data?","choices":["Initial rates measured","Constant buffered pH","Far less enzyme than substrate","Reaction run to equilibrium"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Initial rates (v₀) are the very data a Michaelis–Menten plot uses, so they are required."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Enzyme rates shift with pH, so a fixed, buffered pH is required for comparable rates."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"[E] ≪ [S] keeps free [S] at its set value, an assumption the Michaelis–Menten equation needs."}],"rule":"Michaelis–Menten needs initial rates under fixed conditions with [E] ≪ [S]; the odd one out is the condition that is not part of that setup.","exp":"Michaelis–Menten analysis needs **initial rates (v₀)** under **steady state**, a **constant pH**, and **[E] ≪ [S]** so [S] stays at its set value. Letting the reaction reach **equilibrium** is not wanted: by then [S] has fallen, product has built up and the net rate is zero.","how":{"ask":"Check each condition against the assumptions behind v₀ = Vmax[S]/(Km + [S]).","know":"• **v₀ = Vmax[S]/(Km + [S])** assumes **initial rates** (little product, [S] ≈ starting value) and a **steady state** of ES.\n• **Constant pH** (a buffer) keeps the enzyme's charges and activity fixed.\n• **[E] ≪ [S]** so binding barely depletes free substrate.\n• At **equilibrium** the net rate is zero, so it gives no v₀.","steps":[{"text":"This is a NOT question: find the condition the analysis does not want.","hl":"NOT required"},{"text":"Initial rates are the data Michaelis–Menten uses: the slope at the start of the product-versus-time curve.","strike":["Initial rates measured"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Product formed over time in one enzyme run","x":{"label":"Time (min)","min":0,"max":30,"step":5},"y":{"label":"Product (μM)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Product","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[2,22],[5,46],[10,68],[15,80],[20,86],[25,88],[30,89]]}]}},{"text":"A buffered pH holds the enzyme's ionizable groups steady, and [E] ≪ [S] keeps free [S] at its set value.","strike":["Constant buffered pH","Far less enzyme than substrate"]},{"text":"At equilibrium the curve is flat: net rate zero, [S] no longer the starting value. That is not wanted."},{"text":"Answer: reaction run to equilibrium."}],"trap":"Thinking a reaction must be “complete” to be measured: Michaelis–Menten uses only the first, straight part of the curve."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Michaelis–Menten","note":"uses v₀"},{"t":"NOT required","note":"find the odd one out"}]},{"text":"**Initial rates, steady state.** Measure v₀ while [ES] is steady and little product has formed; later the curve bends over.","calc":["v₀ = slope at t ≈ 0"]},{"text":"**Also:** keep the pH constant with a buffer; a changing pH changes the enzyme's activity during the run."},{"text":"**Also:** keep [E] much less than [S], so the enzyme does not tie up a significant share of the substrate.","calc":["[E] ≪ [S] → free [S] ≈ total [S]"]},{"text":"**Also:** letting the reaction reach equilibrium is not wanted: the net rate there is zero and [S] has changed, so rates must be initial."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** valid Michaelis–Menten data use initial rates at steady state, constant pH and [E] ≪ [S]; never wait for equilibrium."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-30a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:30#1"],"q":"Many metal-chelating drugs, such as vorinostat, contain a hydroxamic acid group. Which compound is a hydroxamic acid (Ph = phenyl)?","choices":["PhC(=O)NH–OH","PhC(=O)O–NH₂","PhCH=N–OH","PhC(=O)NH₂"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Here the acyl group is bonded to O, with NH₂ on that O; a hydroxamic acid has the acyl on N."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"No C=O: a hydroxamic acid is an acyl (amide-type) group, and C=N–OH is an oxime."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"No OH at all: 'hydroxamic' needs an OH on the nitrogen."}],"rule":"A hydroxamic acid is an amide whose nitrogen carries an OH: C=O bonded to N, and that N bonded to OH.","exp":"A hydroxamic acid is an **amide whose nitrogen carries an OH**: R–C(=O)–N(OH)–R′, here with R = phenyl and R′ = H. Moving the acyl group to oxygen gives an O-acyl hydroxylamine, C=N–OH is an oxime, and an amide without the N–OH is a plain amide.","how":{"ask":"Look for a C=O bonded directly to an N that also bears an OH.","know":"• **Hydroxamic acid**: **R–C(=O)–N(OH)–R′**, an amide whose N carries an OH (R′ may be H).\n• Contrast: oxime R–CH=N–OH; O-acyl hydroxylamine R–C(=O)–O–NH₂; amide R–C(=O)–NH₂.","steps":[{"text":"Write the target pattern.","calc":["R–C(=O)–N(OH)–R′"]},{"text":"PhC(=O)NH–OH has C=O on N, and N bears OH: R = Ph, R′ = H.","hl":"PhC(=O)NH–OH"},{"text":"PhC(=O)O–NH₂ bonds the acyl group to O, not N; PhCH=N–OH has no C=O (an oxime); PhC(=O)NH₂ lacks the N–OH.","strike":["PhC(=O)O–NH₂","PhCH=N–OH","PhC(=O)NH₂"]},{"text":"Answer: PhC(=O)NH–OH."}],"trap":"Picking the O-acyl isomer: both contain C=O, N and O, but in a hydroxamic acid the carbonyl carbon is bonded to nitrogen."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"hydroxamic acid","note":"amide + N–OH"},{"t":"metal-chelating","note":"the C=O oxygen and the N–O oxygen bind the metal together"}]},{"text":"**Start from an amide.** R–C(=O)–NH–R′ is an amide."},{"text":"**Put OH on the nitrogen.** Replace the N–H with N–OH.","calc":["R–C(=O)–NH–R′ → R–C(=O)–N(OH)–R′"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a hydroxamic acid is R–C(=O)–N(OH)–R′: an amide whose N carries an OH."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-31a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:31#1"],"q":"At pH 7.0, one enzyme oxidizes substrate X with a rate constant of 5.6 s⁻¹ and substrate Y with one of 1.6 s⁻¹. If both substrates are equally available, X is oxidized how many times as fast as Y?","choices":["0.29","3.5","4.0","7.2"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"Direction: X has the larger k, so X is faster and the ratio must be above 1; 0.29 is upside down."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"'Times as fast' asks for a ratio; 4.0 is the difference 5.6 − 1.6."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"'Times as fast' asks for a ratio; 7.2 is the sum 5.6 + 1.6."}],"rule":"'Times as fast' = larger rate constant ÷ smaller, so it is above 1. Round: 6 ÷ 2 gives the ballpark.","exp":"With the same availability, everything but k cancels: rate X ÷ rate Y = k_X ÷ k_Y = 5.6 ÷ 1.6 = **3.5**. 0.29 is the ratio upside down, 4.0 the difference, and 7.2 the sum.","how":{"ask":"Write each rate as k × availability, then divide the two rates.","know":"• **rate = k × [substrate]** (first order in each).\n• With **equal availability**, the concentrations cancel: **rate₁/rate₂ = k₁/k₂**.\n• “How many times faster” is a ratio, not a difference.","steps":[{"text":"Pick out the two rate constants.","hl":"5.6 s⁻¹","calc":["k_X = 5.6 s⁻¹","k_Y = 1.6 s⁻¹"]},{"text":"Write the ratio of rates; equal availability cancels.","calc":["rate X ÷ rate Y = (k_X·[S]) ÷ (k_Y·[S])","= k_X ÷ k_Y"]},{"text":"Divide.","calc":["5.6 ÷ 1.6 = **3.5**"]},{"text":"0.29 is 1.6 ÷ 5.6 (upside down), 4.0 is 5.6 − 1.6, and 7.2 is 5.6 + 1.6.","strike":["0.29","4.0","7.2"]},{"text":"Answer: 3.5."}],"trap":"Subtracting the rate constants (4.0): “times faster” asks for a ratio."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"5.6 s⁻¹","note":"k for X"},{"t":"1.6 s⁻¹","note":"k for Y"},{"t":"equally available","note":"concentrations cancel"}]},{"text":"**Rate = k × amount.** With the same amount of each substrate, only k differs.","calc":["rate X/rate Y = k_X/k_Y"]},{"text":"**Divide, larger over smaller** for “how many times faster”.","calc":["5.6 ÷ 1.6 = 3.5"]},{"text":"**Also:** rate constants change with pH; from a table, take both k values at the stated pH before dividing."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** with equal availability, the ratio of two rates equals the ratio of their rate constants."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-33a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:33#1","pe3-cp:33#2","pe3-cp:33#3","pe3-cp:33#4"],"q":"A biochemist needs two residues whose side chains are basic and hydrophilic to line a DNA-binding groove. Which pair should she choose?","choices":["Lysine and histidine","Aspartate and glutamate","Leucine and valine","Phenylalanine and isoleucine"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Aspartate and glutamate carry side-chain carboxylates: acidic, the opposite of basic."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Leu and Val side chains are only C and H: nonpolar, not hydrophilic."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Phe and Ile side chains are pure hydrocarbon: nonpolar, not hydrophilic."}],"rule":"Basic side chains carry extra N (Lys, Arg, His); acidic ones carry COO⁻ (Asp, Glu); side chains of only C and H are nonpolar.","exp":"**Lysine, arginine and histidine** have nitrogen-containing side chains that accept protons: they are **basic and hydrophilic**. Aspartate and glutamate carry carboxylates (acidic); leucine, valine, phenylalanine and isoleucine have hydrocarbon side chains (nonpolar).","how":{"ask":"Sort each amino acid by its side chain: basic, acidic or nonpolar.","know":"• **Basic, hydrophilic**: Lys, Arg, His (N-containing side chains that take H⁺).\n• **Acidic**: Asp, Glu (side-chain COOH/COO⁻).\n• **Nonpolar**: Leu, Ile, Val, Phe (hydrocarbon side chains).\n• Arg's guanidino side chain (pKa ≈ 12.5) is protonated at pH 7.4, so free Arg is **+1** overall.","steps":[{"text":"The groove needs basic, water-loving side chains.","hl":"basic and hydrophilic"},{"text":"Lysine's side chain ends in –NH₃⁺ and histidine's in an imidazole: both basic.","hl":"Lysine and histidine","fig":"lysine"},{"text":"Aspartate and glutamate have side-chain carboxylates, so they are acidic.","strike":["Aspartate and glutamate"]},{"text":"Leucine, valine, phenylalanine and isoleucine have only C and H in their side chains, so they are nonpolar.","strike":["Leucine and valine","Phenylalanine and isoleucine"]},{"text":"Also check arginine's charge at pH 7.4: compare the pH with each pKa.","calc":["α-COOH (≈2): pH above → COO⁻, −1","α-NH₂ (≈9): pH below → NH₃⁺, +1","guanidino (≈12.5): pH below → protonated, +1","net = −1 + 1 + 1 = **+1**"]},{"text":"Answer: lysine and histidine."}],"trap":"Calling histidine nonpolar because of its ring: its imidazole nitrogens take H⁺, so it is grouped with the basic amino acids."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"basic and hydrophilic","note":"N-containing side chains"},{"t":"DNA-binding groove","note":"DNA is negatively charged"}]},{"text":"**Basic three.** Lys, Arg and His have side chains that accept protons; they are basic and hydrophilic."},{"text":"**Acidic two.** Asp and Glu have side-chain carboxyl groups; they are acidic and negatively charged at pH 7.4."},{"text":"**Nonpolar group.** Leu, Ile, Val and Phe have hydrocarbon side chains; they are hydrophobic."},{"text":"**Also:** at pH 7.4 arginine's side chain is fully protonated (pKa far above 7.4), so with COO⁻ and NH₃⁺ the molecule carries a net +1 charge."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Lys, Arg and His are basic and hydrophilic; Asp and Glu are acidic; Leu, Ile, Val and Phe are nonpolar."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-34a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:34#1"],"q":"A diluted household ammonia cleaner measures pH 11.3 at room temperature. What is the hydroxide ion concentration, [OH⁻], in the cleaner?","choices":["2.0 × 10⁻³ M","5.0 × 10⁻¹² M","2.7 × 10⁻³ M","5.0 × 10⁻³ M"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"impossible","why":"Basic solution (pH > 7) needs [OH⁻] > 10⁻⁷ M; 5.0 × 10⁻¹² M is the [H⁺]."},{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"Digits trap: pOH 2.7 means 10^−2.7, not 2.7 × 10⁻³."},{"c":3,"tag":"copied","why":"The 5.0 digits belong to [H⁺]; pasting them onto the pOH's power of ten mixes two different ions."}],"rule":"pOH = 14 − pH, then [OH⁻] = 10^−pOH. A pOH between 2 and 3 gives [OH⁻] between 10⁻³ and 10⁻² M.","exp":"At room temperature **pOH = 14 − pH** = 14 − 11.3 = 2.7, and **[OH⁻] = 10^−pOH** = 10^−2.7 ≈ 2.0 × 10⁻³ M. The value 5.0 × 10⁻¹² M is [H⁺], not [OH⁻].","how":{"ask":"Convert pH to pOH, then pOH to [OH⁻].","know":"• At 25 °C, **pH + pOH = 14** (Kw = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴).\n• **[OH⁻] = 10^−pOH**; [H⁺] = 10^−pH.\n• 10^−2.7 = 10^0.3 × 10⁻³ ≈ 2 × 10⁻³.","steps":[{"text":"Start from the pH.","hl":"pH 11.3","calc":["pOH = 14 − 11.3 = **2.7**"]},{"text":"Undo the log.","calc":["[OH⁻] = 10^−2.7","= 10^0.3 × 10⁻³","≈ **2.0 × 10⁻³ M**"]},{"text":"5.0 × 10⁻¹² M is 10^−11.3, the [H⁺]; 2.7 × 10⁻³ M puts the pOH in as the digits; 5.0 × 10⁻³ M mixes the [H⁺] digits with the pOH exponent.","strike":["5.0 × 10⁻¹² M","2.7 × 10⁻³ M","5.0 × 10⁻³ M"]},{"text":"Answer: 2.0 × 10⁻³ M."}],"trap":"Computing 10^−pH and calling it [OH⁻]: that is [H⁺]. A basic solution has [OH⁻] larger than [H⁺]."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"pH 11.3","note":"basic, so [OH⁻] > [H⁺]"},{"t":"room temperature","note":"pH + pOH = 14"},{"t":"[OH⁻]","note":"use pOH, not pH"}]},{"text":"**Switch to pOH.**","calc":["pOH = 14 − pH = 14 − 11.3 = 2.7"]},{"text":"**Undo the log.** Split the exponent into a whole number and a decimal.","calc":["10^−2.7 = 10^0.3 × 10⁻³ ≈ 2.0 × 10⁻³ M"]},{"text":"**Check.** [H⁺][OH⁻] should be about 10⁻¹⁴.","calc":["(5.0 × 10⁻¹²)(2.0 × 10⁻³) = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ ✓"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** pOH = 14 − pH, then [OH⁻] = 10^−pOH."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-35a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:35#1","pe3-cp:36#1","pe3-cp:36#2","pe3-cp:36#3"],"q":"A peroxidase pulls one proton and one electron off the O–H group of 4-methylphenol. Which species results?","choices":["4-Methylphenoxyl radical","4-Methylphenoxide anion","4-Methylphenol radical cation","4-Methylbenzyl cation"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"Losing only H⁺ gives the anion; the electron was removed too, so H⁺ + e⁻ (a neutral H•) leaves no charge."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"Losing only e⁻ gives a radical cation; the proton left too, so the charge cancels."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Nothing leaves the CH₃ group, so no benzyl cation forms; the change is at the O–H."}],"rule":"H⁺ + e⁻ = H•: removing a hydrogen atom leaves a neutral radical; removing only H⁺ leaves an anion, only e⁻ a radical cation.","exp":"A proton plus an electron is one **H• atom**. Once H• is gone from Ar–OH, the O keeps a **single, unpaired electron**: the **phenoxyl radical Ar–O•**, neutral. Losing only H⁺ would give the phenoxide anion; losing only an electron gives a radical cation; a benzyl cation would need loss from the CH₃ group.","how":{"ask":"Add up what leaves (H⁺ + e⁻) and see what the oxygen keeps.","know":"• **H⁺ + e⁻ = H•** (one hydrogen atom).\n• **Ar–OH − H• → Ar–O•**, a **phenoxyl radical** (neutral, unpaired electron on O).\n• Ar–OH − H⁺ → Ar–O⁻ (anion); Ar–OH − e⁻ → Ar–OH•⁺ (radical cation).\n• **Peroxidases** oxidize **phenols** this way.","steps":[{"text":"Both a proton and an electron leave from the O–H.","hl":"one proton and one electron","calc":["H⁺ + e⁻ = H•"]},{"text":"Remove H• from the phenol: O keeps one electron of the old O–H bond.","fig":"phenol","calc":["Ar–OH → Ar–O• + (H⁺ + e⁻)","charge: 0 → 0, one unpaired electron"]},{"text":"Losing only H⁺ gives the anion; losing only e⁻ gives a radical cation.","strike":["4-Methylphenoxide anion","4-Methylphenol radical cation"]},{"text":"Nothing leaves the CH₃ group, so no benzyl cation forms.","strike":["4-Methylbenzyl cation"]},{"text":"Answer: the 4-methylphenoxyl radical."}],"trap":"Stopping at deprotonation (phenoxide): the electron leaves too, so the oxygen ends neutral with one unpaired electron."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"peroxidase","note":"oxidizes phenols"},{"t":"one proton and one electron","note":"= one H atom"},{"t":"4-methylphenol","note":"a phenol, like Tyr's side chain"}]},{"text":"**Count what leaves.** H⁺ + e⁻ = H•, so the O–H loses a whole hydrogen atom.","calc":["Ar–O–H → Ar–O• + H•"]},{"text":"**Also:** peroxidases act on phenols: they use H₂O₂ to pull H• from an aromatic O–H."},{"text":"**Also:** tyrosine is the amino acid with a phenol side chain, so it is the residue a peroxidase can oxidize (to a tyrosyl radical).","fig":"tyrosine-sc"},{"text":"**Also:** the look-alikes are not phenols: lysine's side chain ends in an amine, leucine's is an alkyl group, and glutamine's ends in an amide.","fig":["lysine-sc","leucine-sc","glutamine-sc"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** removing H• (H⁺ + e⁻) from a phenol gives a phenoxyl radical, Ar–O•; peroxidases do this to phenols such as Tyr's side chain."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-37a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:37#1","pe6-cp:42#1"],"q":"Four residues line a Zn²⁺-binding site in an enzyme at pH 7.4. Which side-chain nitrogen is best placed to bind the zinc ion directly?","choices":["His imidazole N","Lys –NH₃⁺ nitrogen","Arg guanidinium N","Trp indole N–H"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"At pH 7.4 Lys's –NH₃⁺ is protonated: it is positive and its lone pair is used in an N–H bond, so it repels Zn²⁺."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Arg's guanidinium (pKa ≈ 12.5) is protonated at pH 7.4: positive, with no free lone pair, so it repels Zn²⁺."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Trp's ring N–H lone pair is part of the aromatic sextet, so it is not free to donate to a metal ion."}],"rule":"A metal ion needs a free lone pair: at pH 7.4 Lys and Arg are protonated, and Trp's N lone pair sits in its aromatic ring.","exp":"Zn²⁺ needs a **neutral nitrogen with a free lone pair**. At pH 7.4, His's imidazole (pKa ≈ 6) is mostly neutral, and its ring **N without an H** has an unshared lone pair in the ring plane, so it coordinates Zn²⁺ (carbonic anhydrase holds its zinc with three His). Lys –NH₃⁺ and the Arg guanidinium are protonated and positive; Trp's ring N–H donates its electron pair to the aromatic sextet.","how":{"ask":"Find the nitrogen that is neutral at pH 7.4 and still has a lone pair free to give to the metal ion.","know":"• A metal ion binds an atom that **donates a lone pair**; a lone pair already used in an N–H bond or in an aromatic ring is not available.\n• **pKa vs pH 7.4**: His imidazole ≈ 6 (mostly neutral); Lys ≈ 10.5 and Arg ≈ 12.5 (protonated, positive).\n• In His's imidazole, the N bearing H gives its lone pair to the aromatic ring; the **other ring N** keeps a free lone pair.\n• Trp's indole N–H lone pair is part of the aromatic sextet.\n• Common Zn²⁺ ligands: **His N**, **Cys S⁻**, and the carboxylate O of Asp and Glu.","steps":[{"text":"The atom must give a lone pair to a 2+ cation, so it must be electron-rich and not positive.","hl":"bind the zinc ion directly"},{"text":"His's imidazole has pKa ≈ 6, so at pH 7.4 it is mostly neutral, and its ring N without an H keeps a lone pair free.","hl":"pH 7.4","fig":"histidine-sc","calc":["[neutral]/[protonated] = 10^(7.4 − 6) ≈ 25 → about 96% neutral","ring N (no H): free lone pair → binds Zn²⁺"]},{"text":"Lys's –NH₃⁺ (pKa ≈ 10.5) and Arg's guanidinium (pKa ≈ 12.5) are protonated at pH 7.4: positive, with no free lone pair, so they repel Zn²⁺.","fig":["lysine-sc","arginine-sc"],"strike":["Lys –NH₃⁺ nitrogen","Arg guanidinium N"]},{"text":"Trp's indole N carries an H, and its lone pair is part of the aromatic sextet, so it cannot donate.","fig":"tryptophan-sc","strike":["Trp indole N–H"]},{"text":"Answer: the His imidazole N."}],"trap":"Picking any nitrogen because N has a lone pair: in Lys and Arg the N is protonated and positive, and in Trp the lone pair belongs to the aromatic ring."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Zn²⁺-binding site","note":"a positive metal ion"},{"t":"pH 7.4","note":"sets which groups are protonated"},{"t":"side-chain nitrogen","note":"which N can donate"}]},{"text":"**A free lone pair and no positive charge.** Compare each nitrogen at pH 7.4.","calc":["His ring N (pKa ≈ 6): neutral, free lone pair → binds","Lys –NH₃⁺ (pKa ≈ 10.5): + → repels","Arg guanidinium (pKa ≈ 12.5): + → repels","Trp N–H: lone pair in the aromatic ring → no bond"]},{"text":"**Also:** zinc sites usually mix His N with Cys S⁻ or the carboxylate O of Asp and Glu."},{"text":"**Also:** Ca²⁺ prefers oxygen: the negatively charged carboxylate O of Asp and Glu (drawn) and the δ− carbonyl O of the backbone, Asn and Gln. A positive N or a carbon never binds a metal ion.","fig":"aspartate-sc"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a metal ion binds an atom with a free lone pair and no positive charge: His's ring N (neutral at pH 7.4), Cys S⁻, or carboxylate and carbonyl O; protonated Lys and Arg and the Trp ring N do not."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-38a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:38#1","pe3-cp:38#2","pe3-cp:38#3","pe5-cp:14#2","pe5-cp:14#3"],"q":"Heme's porphyrin ring is built by joining four aromatic rings of the same type through one-carbon bridges. Which ring is that building block?","choices":["Five-membered, one N","Five-membered, two N","Six-membered, one N","Six-membered, two N"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Five-membered with two N is imidazole, histidine's ring; it binds the heme iron but isn't part of the porphyrin."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Six-membered with one N is pyridine; a porphyrin is made of pyrroles, which are five-membered."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Six-membered with two N is a pyrimidine, a nucleobase ring, not a porphyrin unit."}],"rule":"A porphyrin is four pyrrole rings joined by CH bridges. Know the rings: pyrrole, imidazole, pyridine, pyrimidine.","exp":"A porphyrin is four **pyrrole** rings linked by =CH– bridges, and a pyrrole is **five-membered with one N**. The five-membered ring with two N is imidazole (histidine's side chain, which binds the heme iron from below); six-membered with one N is pyridine, and with two N a pyrimidine.","how":{"ask":"Name the unit heme is made of, then describe that ring by its size and its count of nitrogens.","know":"• **Heme** = a porphyrin ring (four **pyrrole** units joined by =CH– bridges) with Fe²⁺ held by the four inward-facing N.\n• **Pyrrole**: 5 ring atoms, **1 N** (an N–H).\n• **Imidazole**: 5 ring atoms, **2 N**; the side chain of **His**, whose free N binds the heme Fe.\n• **Pyridine**: 6 ring atoms, 1 N. **Pyrimidine**: 6 ring atoms, 2 N.","steps":[{"text":"The question asks for the repeating unit of the porphyrin ring.","hl":"four aromatic rings of the same type"},{"text":"That unit is pyrrole. Count its ring atoms and its nitrogens.","fig":"pyrrole","calc":["ring atoms: 4 C + 1 N = **5**","nitrogens: **1** (N–H)"]},{"text":"Five-membered with two N is imidazole, histidine's ring. It is not part of the porphyrin; it is the residue whose free N lone pair binds the heme iron from below.","fig":["imidazole","t3-his-ring"],"strike":["Five-membered, two N"]},{"text":"Six-membered rings are pyridine (one N) and pyrimidine (two N); neither builds a porphyrin.","fig":"pyridine","strike":["Six-membered, one N","Six-membered, two N"]},{"text":"Answer: five-membered, one N (pyrrole)."}],"trap":"Picking imidazole because histidine is drawn next to heme: His only binds the iron; the ring itself is made of pyrroles."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"porphyrin ring","note":"the ring of heme"},{"t":"four aromatic rings of the same type","note":"the repeating unit"}]},{"text":"**Heme = porphyrin + Fe.** The porphyrin is four pyrrole rings joined by one-carbon bridges, with their four N pointing at the central iron."},{"text":"**Tell the rings apart by size and N count.**","fig":["pyrrole","imidazole","pyridine"],"calc":["pyrrole: 5 atoms, 1 N","imidazole: 5 atoms, 2 N","pyridine: 6 atoms, 1 N"]},{"text":"**Also:** imidazole (two N) is the ring on histidine's R group; in heme proteins, His binds the heme iron through its N that carries a free lone pair.","fig":"t3-his-ring"},{"text":"**Also:** to name any ring, count its atoms first (5 or 6), then count the nitrogens (1 or 2)."},{"text":"**Also:** to write a ring's formula, give every ring atom its full set of bonds: each C makes four (fill with H), each N three. Pyridine (6 atoms, 1 N with no H) is C₅H₅N; pyrrole (5 atoms, 1 N–H) is C₄H₅N. Imidazole works the same way: 5 ring atoms, two of them N (one N–H, one with only a lone pair); count its C and H as above.","calc":["pyridine: 5 C–H, N has only a lone pair → C₅H₅N","pyridine mass: 5(12) + 5(1) + 14 = 79 g/mol","pyrrole: 4 C–H + 1 N–H → C₄H₅N"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** heme's porphyrin is four pyrroles (5 atoms, 1 N); imidazole (5 atoms, 2 N) is His, which binds the iron; pyridine has 6 atoms and 1 N."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-39a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:39#1"],"q":"At 37 °C, muscle has a conductivity of 2.5 × 10⁻³ (Ω·cm)⁻¹ and fat has 4.0 × 10⁻⁴ (Ω·cm)⁻¹. Which value is muscle's resistivity?","choices":["400 Ω·cm","2500 Ω·cm","40 Ω·cm","2.5 × 10⁻³ Ω·cm"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"Decimal slipped by 10: that is 1 ÷ 0.025, not 1 ÷ 0.0025."},{"c":3,"tag":"copied","why":"ρ = 1/σ turns a small σ into a big ρ; keeping σ's number and swapping only the unit is wrong."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Wrong tissue: 2500 Ω·cm is 1 ÷ (4.0 × 10⁻⁴), fat's value; the question asks about muscle."}],"rule":"Resistivity = 1 ÷ conductivity, for the tissue asked about. Round: 1 ÷ (2.5 × 10⁻³) is a few hundred Ω·cm.","exp":"**Resistivity is the reciprocal of conductivity: ρ = 1/σ.** For muscle, ρ = 1 ÷ (2.5 × 10⁻³ (Ω·cm)⁻¹) = **400 Ω·cm**; taking 1/σ also flips the (Ω·cm)⁻¹ unit to Ω·cm. 2500 Ω·cm belongs to fat.","how":{"ask":"Pick the muscle value and take its reciprocal, unit included.","know":"• **ρ = 1/σ**: resistivity and conductivity are reciprocals.\n• Units flip too: (Ω·cm)⁻¹ ↔ Ω·cm.\n• A good conductor (large σ) has a small ρ.","steps":[{"text":"Use the muscle value, not the fat value. 2500 Ω·cm is fat's resistivity (1 ÷ 4.0 × 10⁻⁴).","hl":"2.5 × 10⁻³ (Ω·cm)⁻¹","strike":["2500 Ω·cm"]},{"text":"Take the reciprocal.","calc":["ρ = 1 ÷ σ","ρ = 1 ÷ (2.5 × 10⁻³ (Ω·cm)⁻¹)","ρ = 0.4 × 10³ = **400 Ω·cm**"]},{"text":"2.5 × 10⁻³ Ω·cm keeps σ's number and only swaps the unit. 40 Ω·cm misplaces the decimal (1 ÷ 0.025).","strike":["2.5 × 10⁻³ Ω·cm","40 Ω·cm"]},{"text":"Answer: 400 Ω·cm."}],"trap":"Copying the conductivity value and writing Ω·cm after it: an inverted unit, (Ω·cm)⁻¹, means you must take the reciprocal of the number too."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the right number**.","marks":[{"t":"muscle has a conductivity of 2.5 × 10⁻³ (Ω·cm)⁻¹","note":"σ for muscle"},{"t":"fat has 4.0 × 10⁻⁴ (Ω·cm)⁻¹","note":"not needed"},{"t":"muscle's resistivity","note":"want ρ"}]},{"text":"**Reciprocals.** Conductivity measures how easily current flows; resistivity measures how hard it is. One is 1 over the other.","calc":["ρ = 1/σ","unit: 1 ÷ (Ω·cm)⁻¹ = Ω·cm"]},{"text":"**Compute.**","calc":["1 ÷ 0.0025 = 400","ρ = 400 Ω·cm"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** ρ = 1/σ, and the unit flips from (Ω·cm)⁻¹ to Ω·cm; read the value for the material asked about."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-42a","s":"Reading Data","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe3-cp:42#1","pe3-cp:42#2"],"q":"Variant R of a DNA fold unfolds between pH 6.3 and 6.7, with pK 6.5; variant S unfolds between pH 5.8 and 8.6, with pK 7.2. Which is more cooperative, and what is its pK?","choices":["R; pK 6.5","S; pK 7.2","R; pK 7.2","S; pK 6.5"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Narrow range = steep = cooperative: R spans 0.4 pH units, S spans 2.8, so not S."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"S has the broad 2.8-unit transition, so it is the less cooperative one."},{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"7.2 is S's pK pasted onto R; each variant keeps its own midpoint."}],"rule":"Cooperative = sharp: the narrower the pH range of the transition, the more cooperative. The pK is that curve's own midpoint.","exp":"Cooperativity is read from **steepness**: R goes from folded to unfolded within 0.4 pH units, S over 2.8 units. The narrow, steep transition (R) is the more cooperative, all-or-none one, and its midpoint, its pK, is 6.5. The pK of S (7.2) says where S unfolds, not how sharply.","how":{"ask":"Compare the width of each transition, then read the pK of the narrower one.","know":"• A **cooperative** transition is all-or-none: the structure unfolds over a **narrow** range, so the curve is **steep**.\n• A broad, shallow curve means a gradual, less cooperative change.\n• The **midpoint** (half folded) gives the pK (or Tm); it tells **where**, not **how sharply**.","steps":[{"text":"Read each variant's range.","hl":"between pH 6.3 and 6.7","calc":["R: 6.7 − 6.3 = **0.4** pH units","S: 8.6 − 5.8 = **2.8** pH units"]},{"text":"Plot both: R drops like a cliff, S slopes gently.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Fraction folded of two DNA variants versus pH","x":{"label":"pH","min":5,"max":9,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Fraction folded","min":0,"max":1,"step":0.2},"series":[{"name":"Variant R","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[5,1],[6,0.99],[6.3,0.95],[6.5,0.5],[6.7,0.05],[7,0.01],[9,0]]},{"name":"Variant S","marker":"square-open","line":"smooth","points":[[5,0.99],[5.8,0.95],[6.5,0.78],[7.2,0.5],[7.9,0.22],[8.6,0.05],[9,0.02]]}]}},{"text":"The narrower, steeper transition is the more cooperative one: R, not S.","strike":["S; pK 7.2","S; pK 6.5"]},{"text":"Match the curve to its own pK: R's midpoint is 6.5; 7.2 belongs to S.","strike":["R; pK 7.2"]},{"text":"Answer: R; pK 6.5."}],"trap":"Judging cooperativity by the midpoint (a higher pK or Tm): the midpoint gives position; only the steepness gives cooperativity."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"between pH 6.3 and 6.7","note":"R's range"},{"t":"pK 6.5","note":"R's midpoint"},{"t":"between pH 5.8 and 8.6","note":"S's range"},{"t":"pK 7.2","note":"S's midpoint"}]},{"text":"**Width = cooperativity.** Subtract the ends of each range; the smaller width is the steeper, more cooperative curve.","calc":["R: 0.4 units","S: 2.8 units"]},{"text":"**Midpoint = pK.** On a curve, the pK is the pH where the fraction folded is one half.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Steep (cooperative) versus shallow transition","x":{"label":"pH","min":5,"max":9,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Fraction folded","min":0,"max":1,"step":0.2},"series":[{"name":"Variant R","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[5,1],[6,0.99],[6.3,0.95],[6.5,0.5],[6.7,0.05],[7,0.01],[9,0]]},{"name":"Variant S","marker":"square-open","line":"smooth","points":[[5,0.99],[5.8,0.95],[6.5,0.78],[7.2,0.5],[7.9,0.22],[8.6,0.05],[9,0.02]]}]}},{"text":"**Also:** read each curve separately: its steepness and its own pK belong together."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a steeper, narrower unfolding transition means more cooperative unfolding; the midpoint (pK or Tm) tells only where it happens."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-44a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biology","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:44#1","pe3-cp:44#2"],"q":"A single DNA strand is 28% A, 35% G and 37% C, with no T. Which nucleoside is absent from its complementary strand?","choices":["Deoxyadenosine","Deoxythymidine","Deoxyguanosine","Deoxycytidine"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"The template is 28% A, and every A pairs with T, so T is in the complement."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"The template is 37% C, and C pairs with G, so G is in the complement."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"The template is 35% G, and G pairs with C, so C is in the complement."}],"rule":"The complement holds the partner of every base present (A–T, G–C). A base missing from the template means its partner is missing from the complement.","exp":"Pair each base: **A↔T, G↔C**. The template's A (28%) gives T in the complement, its G (35%) gives C, and its C (37%) gives G. With no T in the template, nothing calls for an A, so **deoxyadenosine** is absent from the new strand.","how":{"ask":"Write the partner of every base in the template, then see which partner never appears.","know":"• **Watson–Crick pairs**: A↔T (2 H-bonds), G↔C (3 H-bonds).\n• The complement's share of each base equals the template's share of its partner.\n• A base missing from the template means **its partner** is missing from the complement.","steps":[{"text":"List the template's bases.","hl":"28% A, 35% G and 37% C, with no T"},{"text":"Convert each to its partner in the complementary strand.","calc":["A 28% → **T 28%**","G 35% → **C 35%**","C 37% → **G 37%**","T 0% → **A 0%**"]},{"text":"The complement contains thymidine, cytidine and guanosine.","strike":["Deoxythymidine","Deoxycytidine","Deoxyguanosine"]},{"text":"Answer: deoxyadenosine."}],"trap":"Naming the base missing from the template (deoxythymidine): the complement lacks that base's partner, A."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"28% A","note":"pairs with T"},{"t":"35% G","note":"pairs with C"},{"t":"37% C","note":"pairs with G"},{"t":"no T","note":"its partner is missing"}]},{"text":"**Pair each base.** A↔T and G↔C, so each template percentage becomes its partner's percentage in the new strand.","calc":["A → T, T → A","G → C, C → G"]},{"text":"**Missing in, missing out.** A base absent from the template leaves its partner absent from the complement.","calc":["no T in template → no A in complement"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** pair A↔T and G↔C; whatever base the template lacks, the complementary strand lacks its partner."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-45a","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe3-cp:45#1","pe3-cp:45#2","pe3-cp:45#3","pe5-cp:35#1","pe5-cp:35#2","pe6-cp:29#1"],"q":"Proteins with pI values of 4.6, 5.9, 7.3, 8.2 and 9.4 are loaded at pH 6.8 onto a cation-exchange column with negatively charged beads. How many bind?","choices":["0","2","3","5"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"Sign rule reversed: a protein in a buffer below its pI is positive and sticks to negative beads, and several pIs exceed 6.8."},{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Two is the count of pIs below 6.8; those proteins are negative, so a cation exchanger lets them pass."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"The pIs fall on both sides of 6.8, so the proteins carry opposite charges; they can't all stick to the same beads."}],"rule":"Buffer pH below a protein's pI makes it positive, so it binds a cation exchanger (negative beads). Count the pIs above the buffer pH.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Net charge versus pH for the five proteins","x":{"label":"pH","min":3,"max":11,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Net charge","min":-10,"max":10,"step":5},"series":[{"name":"Protein A (pI 4.6)","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[3,6.4],[4,2.9],[4.6,0],[5,-2],[6,-5.9],[6.8,-7.6],[7,-7.8],[8,-8.6],[9,-8.9],[10,-9],[11,-9]]},{"name":"Protein B (pI 5.9)","marker":"square","line":"smooth","points":[[3,5.5],[4,4.7],[5,2.8],[5.9,0],[6,-0.3],[6.8,-2.8],[7,-3.3],[8,-4.9],[9,-5.6],[10,-5.9],[11,-6]]},{"name":"Protein C (pI 7.3)","marker":"triangle","line":"smooth","points":[[3,8],[4,7.9],[5,7.6],[6,6.2],[6.8,3],[7,1.9],[7.3,0],[8,-4.1],[9,-7],[10,-7.8],[11,-8]]},{"name":"Protein D (pI 8.2)","marker":"circle-open","line":"smooth","points":[[3,6],[4,6],[5,5.9],[6,5.7],[6.8,4.8],[7,4.5],[8,1],[8.2,0],[9,-3.4],[10,-5.4],[11,-5.9]]},{"name":"Protein E (pI 9.4)","marker":"diamond","line":"smooth","points":[[3,7],[4,7],[5,6.9],[6,6.7],[6.8,6.3],[7,6.1],[8,4.6],[9,1.5],[9.4,0],[10,-2.3],[11,-5]]}]},"exp":"A protein is **positive below its pI** and **negative above it**. At pH 6.8 the proteins with pI 7.3, 8.2 and 9.4 are still below their pI, so they are positive and bind the negative beads: **3**. The pI 4.6 and 5.9 proteins are negative; they would bind an anion exchanger instead.","how":{"ask":"Compare the buffer pH with each pI to get each protein's charge, then count the ones the beads attract.","know":"• **pH < pI → net positive**; **pH > pI → net negative**; pH = pI → no net charge.\n• **Cation exchanger** = negative beads → binds **positive** proteins.\n• **Anion exchanger** = positive beads → binds **negative** proteins.","steps":[{"text":"Read the pIs and the buffer pH.","hl":"4.6, 5.9, 7.3, 8.2 and 9.4"},{"text":"Compare each pI with pH 6.8.","calc":["pI 4.6 < 6.8 → negative","pI 5.9 < 6.8 → negative","pI 7.3 > 6.8 → **positive**","pI 8.2 > 6.8 → **positive**","pI 9.4 > 6.8 → **positive**"],"graph":{"read":[{"series":"Protein A (pI 4.6)","x":6.8},{"series":"Protein B (pI 5.9)","x":6.8},{"series":"Protein C (pI 7.3)","x":6.8},{"series":"Protein D (pI 8.2)","x":6.8},{"series":"Protein E (pI 9.4)","x":6.8}]}},{"text":"Negative beads bind positive proteins: three.","calc":["positive proteins = **3**"]},{"text":"2 counts the negative proteins, which only an anion exchanger binds; 5 ignores charge; 0 reverses the sign rule and misses all three pI values above the buffer pH.","strike":["2","5","0"]},{"text":"Answer: 3."}],"trap":"Reversing the sign rule: a protein is positive when the pH is BELOW its pI, so only pI values above 6.8 bind a cation exchanger."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"4.6, 5.9, 7.3, 8.2 and 9.4","note":"the pIs"},{"t":"pH 6.8","note":"buffer"},{"t":"cation-exchange column","note":"negative beads"}]},{"text":"**Charge from pI.** Below its pI a protein holds extra H⁺ and is positive; above its pI it is negative.","calc":["pH < pI → +","pH > pI → −"]},{"text":"**Also:** a cation exchanger has negative beads, so it binds the positive proteins: here pI 7.3, 8.2 and 9.4, three of them."},{"text":"**Also:** an anion exchanger has positive beads and binds the negative proteins: here pI 4.6 and 5.9, two of them."},{"text":"**Also:** two forms of one protein with almost the same mass but different net charge (say −3 and −5 at the buffer pH) cannot be split by size, so use ion exchange. Both are negative, so an anion exchanger holds both, and the −5 form clings tighter: it stays on the column longer and comes off only at a higher salt concentration.","calc":["same size → size exclusion fails","−3 form: released first, at lower salt","−5 form: released later, at higher salt"]},{"text":"**Also:** in electrophoresis the same sign rule sets the direction. When the buffer pH equals the pI, the charges cancel and the protein stays in its well; below its pI it is positive and drifts to the negative electrode (cathode); above its pI it is negative and drifts to the positive electrode (anode).","calc":["pH 6.8, pI 4.6: negative → toward the anode (+)","pH 6.8, pI 9.4: positive → toward the cathode (−)","a protein with pI 6.8 would not migrate"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** proteins are positive below their pI and negative above it; a cation exchanger binds the positive ones, an anion exchanger the negative ones."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-47a","s":"Atoms & Periodic Trends","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:47#1"],"q":"Of four nonmetals in groups 16 and 17 of periods 3 and 4, which needs the most energy to lose its first electron?","choices":["sulfur","chlorine","selenium","bromine"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Across a period, IE rises to group 17, so group 16 sulfur is below its period-3 neighbor."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"IE falls down a group: selenium sits in period 4, below sulfur, and in group 16."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"IE falls down a group: bromine sits in period 4, below its period-3 group-mate."}],"rule":"First ionization energy rises up a group and to the right across a period: pick the element highest and farthest right.","exp":"First ionization energy **rises across a period** (more protons, same shell) and **rises up a group** (fewer shells, so the outer electrons sit nearer the protons). The group 17, period 3 atom, chlorine, is the smallest of the four and holds its electrons most tightly.","how":{"ask":"Place the four atoms on the table and apply both ionization-energy trends.","know":"• First ionization energy **increases left → right** across a period and **increases bottom → top** in a group.\n• Smaller atom → outer electrons nearer the protons → harder to remove.\n• Among halogens, **F** (smallest) is the hardest to ionize.","steps":[{"text":"Place the atoms: period 3 holds S (16) and Cl (17); period 4 holds Se (16) and Br (17).","hl":"groups 16 and 17 of periods 3 and 4"},{"text":"Across a period, group 17 beats group 16: S and Se lose.","strike":["sulfur","selenium"]},{"text":"Up a group, period 3 beats period 4: Br, lower in group 17, loses.","strike":["bromine"]},{"text":"**Also:** extend the trend up group 17: F > Cl > Br > I, so fluorine, the smallest halogen, is the hardest of them all to ionize."},{"text":"Answer: chlorine."}],"trap":"Thinking a heavier atom with more protons holds its electrons more tightly: extra shells put them farther away and shield them."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"lose its first electron","note":"first ionization energy"},{"t":"groups 16 and 17","note":"across a period"},{"t":"periods 3 and 4","note":"up and down a group"}]},{"text":"**Across a period:** more protons pull on the same shell, so ionization energy rises toward group 17 (and 18)."},{"text":"**Up a group:** fewer shells, a smaller atom, less shielding, so ionization energy rises toward the top."},{"text":"**Also:** of the halogens, fluorine is the hardest atom to ionize, because it is the smallest."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** first ionization energy rises up a group and across a period; the smallest atom, top right, holds its electrons tightest."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-48a","s":"Mechanics & Energy","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe3-cp:48#1","pe3-cp:48#2"],"q":"A sled starts from rest and slides down an icy slope with constant acceleration of 3 m/s². Which describes its velocity-versus-time graph?","choices":["Upward parabola (v ∝ t²)","Straight line (v ∝ t)","Horizontal line (v fixed)","Leveling curve (v ∝ √t)"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"A t² parabola is the shape of the position graph (d = ½at²), not the velocity graph."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A flat v–t line means constant velocity and zero acceleration, but this sled gains 3 m/s every second."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"A leveling v–t curve means the acceleration is shrinking, but the stem says it is constant."}],"rule":"The slope of a v–t graph is the acceleration: a constant acceleration gives a constant slope; the t² curve belongs to position.","exp":"From rest with constant a, **v = v₀ + at = 3t**: the sled gains 3 m/s each second (3, 6, 9, 12 m/s at 1–4 s), so the v–t graph is a **straight line through the origin** whose slope is the acceleration. It is the distance, d = ½at² = 1.5t², that curves as a parabola.","how":{"ask":"Write velocity as a function of time for a start from rest with constant acceleration, and see how v scales with t.","know":"• **v = v₀ + at**; from rest, **v = at**, so **v ∝ t**.\n• Slope of a v–t graph = acceleration; a constant a gives a constant slope.\n• **d = ½at²** from rest: d ∝ t², so the d–t graph is the parabola.\n• Slope of a d–t graph = velocity.","steps":[{"text":"From rest, the v₀ term is zero.","hl":"starts from rest","calc":["v₀ = 0","v = v₀ + at = at"]},{"text":"Put in the acceleration and test a few times: equal steps in time add equal steps in speed.","hl":"3 m/s²","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Velocity of the sled versus time","x":{"label":"Time (s)","min":0,"max":4,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Velocity (m/s)","min":0,"max":15,"step":3},"series":[{"name":"Sled, a = 3 m/s²","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[1,3],[2,6],[3,9],[4,12]]}]},"calc":["v = 3 m/s² × t","t = 1 s → 3 m/s; t = 2 s → 6 m/s","t = 3 s → 9 m/s; t = 4 s → 12 m/s","+3 m/s every second: **v ∝ t**, a straight line"]},{"text":"A parabola is the shape of the d–t graph, not v–t: d = ½at² grows as t², while v grows as t.","calc":["d = 1.5t²: 1.5, 6, 13.5, 24 m at 1–4 s (parabola)"],"strike":["Upward parabola (v ∝ t²)"]},{"text":"A flat line means no acceleration, and a leveling curve means the acceleration is fading; this sled's acceleration is a steady 3 m/s².","strike":["Horizontal line (v fixed)","Leveling curve (v ∝ √t)"]},{"text":"Answer: straight line (v ∝ t)."}],"trap":"Picking the parabola because the sled speeds up: the t² belongs to distance. With constant acceleration, velocity grows linearly."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"starts from rest","note":"v₀ = 0"},{"t":"constant acceleration of 3 m/s²","note":"slope of v–t fixed"},{"t":"velocity-versus-time graph","note":"shape of v(t)"}]},{"text":"**From rest:** v = at.","calc":["v = 3t","1 s → 3 m/s, 2 s → 6 m/s: doubling t doubles v"]},{"text":"**Compare with distance.** The same sled's distance curves upward, because d = ½at² grows as t².","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Distance of the same sled versus time","x":{"label":"Time (s)","min":0,"max":4,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Distance (m)","min":0,"max":25,"step":5},"series":[{"name":"d = 1.5t²","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,1.5],[2,6],[3,13.5],[4,24]]}]}},{"text":"**Also:** when t doubles, v doubles but d quadruples (1.5 m → 6 m), because d ∝ t². A constant velocity gives a flat v–t line and a straight d–t line."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** from rest with constant acceleration, v = at is a straight line whose slope is a, while d = ½at² is a parabola; a constant velocity gives a flat v–t line."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-49a","s":"Waves & Sound","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:49#1","pe3-cp:51#1","pe5-cp:53#1"],"q":"A 4.0 MHz ultrasound beam passes from fat (sound speed 1440 m/s) into liver (1560 m/s). Besides its speed, which property of the beam changes?","choices":["Wavelength","Frequency","Period","Angular frequency"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"The source sets the frequency: every crest sent crosses into the liver, so f stays 4.0 MHz."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Period is T = 1/f; with f fixed by the source, T stays 0.25 μs in both tissues."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Angular frequency is ω = 2πf, just f times a constant, so a fixed f means a fixed ω."}],"rule":"Crossing into a new medium, frequency (and so period and ω) is fixed by the source; only speed and v/f can change.","exp":"The **source sets the frequency**, so the beam stays at 4.0 MHz in liver, and its period (1/f) and angular frequency (2πf) stay fixed with it. The speed depends on the tissue, so **λ = v/f** changes: 0.36 mm in fat, 0.39 mm in liver.","how":{"ask":"Sort the properties into those set by the source and those set by the medium; one set by the medium is the one that changes.","know":"• The **source** fixes how many crests leave per second (f); a boundary cannot add or remove crests.\n• **T = 1/f** and **ω = 2πf** are fixed whenever f is.\n• The **medium** sets the speed v.\n• **λ = v/f**, so if v changes at fixed f, λ changes in step.\n• **MHz = 10⁶ Hz**.","steps":[{"text":"The transducer sends a fixed number of crests per second, and all of them cross into the liver, so f stays the same.","hl":"4.0 MHz","calc":["f in fat = f in liver = **4.0 MHz**"],"strike":["Frequency"]},{"text":"Period and angular frequency are both set by f, so they stay the same too.","calc":["T = 1/f = 1 ÷ (4.0 × 10⁶ Hz) = 2.5 × 10⁻⁷ s = 0.25 μs","ω = 2πf = 2π × 4.0 × 10⁶ ≈ 2.5 × 10⁷ rad/s"],"strike":["Period","Angular frequency"]},{"text":"The speed changes with the tissue, so λ = v/f changes: faster liver, same f, longer wavelength.","hl":"1560 m/s","calc":["λ = v ÷ f","fat: 1440 ÷ (4.0 × 10⁶) = 3.6 × 10⁻⁴ m = **0.36 mm**","liver: 1560 ÷ (4.0 × 10⁶) = 3.9 × 10⁻⁴ m = **0.39 mm**"]},{"text":"Answer: wavelength."}],"trap":"Thinking the frequency adjusts to the new speed. The source locks f (and with it T and ω); λ = v/f takes up the whole change."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"4.0 MHz","note":"set by the source"},{"t":"passes from fat","note":"new medium"},{"t":"1560 m/s","note":"speed changes"},{"t":"which property of the beam changes","note":"find the one tied to the medium"}]},{"text":"**Frequency belongs to the source.** Each crest that reaches the boundary leaves it, so f, T = 1/f and ω = 2πf all stay fixed."},{"text":"**Wavelength follows the speed:** λ = v/f, with MHz = 10⁶ Hz.","calc":["λ(fat) = 1440 m/s ÷ 4.0 × 10⁶ Hz = 3.6 × 10⁻⁴ m = 0.36 mm","λ(liver) = 1560 m/s ÷ 4.0 × 10⁶ Hz = 3.9 × 10⁻⁴ m = 0.39 mm"]},{"text":"**Also:** light in vacuum has no medium to change: its speed is always c, so c = fλ and f = c/λ. Doubling the wavelength halves the frequency, and the photon energy (E = hf) halves with it.","calc":["λ = 500 nm: f = 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s ÷ 5.0 × 10⁻⁷ m = 6.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz","λ = 1000 nm: f = 3.0 × 10¹⁴ Hz (half)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** at a boundary the source keeps the frequency (and period) fixed; the speed changes, so the wavelength λ = v/f changes with it."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-53a","s":"Equilibrium","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe3-cp:53#1","pe3-cp:53#2","pe3-cp:53#3","pe4-cp:2#1","pe4-cp:2#2"],"q":"Calcium phosphate, Ca₃(PO₄)₂, dissolves very slightly in water, giving Ca²⁺ and PO₄³⁻ ions. Which expression gives its solubility product, Ksp?","choices":["[Ca²⁺]³[PO₄³⁻]²","[Ca²⁺][PO₄³⁻]","[Ca²⁺]²[PO₄³⁻]³","[Ca²⁺]³[PO₄³⁻]²/[Ca₃(PO₄)₂]"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Exponents swapped: each formula unit releases three Ca²⁺ and two phosphates, so calcium gets the larger power."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"Exponents missing: 3 Ca²⁺ and 2 phosphate ions per formula unit must appear as powers."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Pure solids never appear in an equilibrium expression, so nothing goes in the denominator."}],"rule":"Ksp = product of the ion concentrations, each raised to its coefficient in the dissolving equation; leave the solid out.","exp":"Ca₃(PO₄)₂(s) ⇌ 3Ca²⁺(aq) + 2PO₄³⁻(aq). **The solid does not appear** in Ksp, and **each ion's concentration is raised to its coefficient**: Ksp = [Ca²⁺]³[PO₄³⁻]².","how":{"ask":"Write the dissolution equation, drop the solid, and use the coefficients as exponents.","know":"• Ksp = product of the **ion** concentrations at saturation.\n• **Solids (and pure liquids) are left out**: their activity is 1.\n• Each concentration is raised to the power of its **coefficient** in the balanced equation.","steps":[{"text":"Balance the dissolution: three Ca²⁺ and two PO₄³⁻ per formula unit.","hl":"Ca₃(PO₄)₂","calc":["Ca₃(PO₄)₂(s) ⇌ **3**Ca²⁺(aq) + **2**PO₄³⁻(aq)"]},{"text":"Leave out the solid; raise each ion to its coefficient.","calc":["Ksp = [Ca²⁺]³[PO₄³⁻]²"]},{"text":"The solid never appears; omitting the exponents or swapping them misreads the coefficients.","strike":["[Ca²⁺]³[PO₄³⁻]²/[Ca₃(PO₄)₂]","[Ca²⁺][PO₄³⁻]","[Ca²⁺]²[PO₄³⁻]³"]},{"text":"Answer: [Ca²⁺]³[PO₄³⁻]²."}],"trap":"Writing the solid in the denominator, as in an ordinary K expression: a pure solid's activity is 1, so it is left out."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Ca₃(PO₄)₂","note":"3 Ca, 2 PO₄ per unit"},{"t":"solubility product","note":"ions only"}]},{"text":"**Write the dissolution.** One formula unit releases 3 Ca²⁺ and 2 PO₄³⁻.","calc":["Ca₃(PO₄)₂(s) ⇌ 3Ca²⁺ + 2PO₄³⁻"]},{"text":"**Drop the solid.** A pure solid has activity 1, so it never appears in Ksp."},{"text":"**Coefficients become exponents.**","calc":["Ksp = [Ca²⁺]³[PO₄³⁻]²"]},{"text":"**Also:** for a 1:1 salt such as CaCO₃, every coefficient is 1, so Ksp = [Ca²⁺][CO₃²⁻]."},{"text":"**Also:** every K leaves out pure solids and liquids, so for a solid that gives off CO₂ only the gas remains: K = [CO₂] (or K_p = P_CO₂). Adding more of a solid changes nothing in K, so the equilibrium does not shift.","calc":["MgCO₃(s) ⇌ MgO(s) + CO₂(g)","K = [CO₂]; extra MgCO₃(s) or MgO(s) → no shift"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Ksp is the product of the ion concentrations, each raised to its coefficient, with the solid left out."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-54a","s":"Atoms & Periodic Trends","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:54#1","pe3-cp:54#2","pe4-cp:20#1","pe4-cp:20#2","pe4-cp:20#3","pe4-cp:52#1"],"q":"A metal's first three ionization energies are 480, 4400 and 6800 kJ/mol. To which family of the periodic table does the metal belong?","choices":["alkali metals","alkaline earth metals","inner transition metals","halogens"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Group 2 metals rise only modestly from IE₁ to IE₂ and jump after the 2nd electron; here the jump comes after the 1st."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Inner transition metals usually form 3+ ions, so their jump comes after the 3rd electron; here 4400 → 6800 is under a 2× rise."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"The stem says it is a metal; halogens are nonmetals."}],"rule":"Find the big jump between successive ionization energies: the electrons removed before it are the valence electrons, which sets the group.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Successive ionization energies of the metal","x":{"label":"Electron removed"},"y":{"label":"Ionization energy (kJ/mol)","min":0,"max":8000,"step":1000},"categories":["IE₁","IE₂","IE₃"],"series":[{"name":"Ionization energy","values":[480,4400,6800]}]},"exp":"Ionization energy leaps **right after the first electron** (480 → 4400 kJ/mol, about 9×), so the atom has one valence electron and forms **1+ ions**: group 1, the **alkali metals**.","how":{"ask":"Find where the big jump in ionization energy falls; that counts the valence electrons and names the group.","know":"• **Group names:** alkali metals = group 1; alkaline earth metals = group 2; halogens = group 17; noble gases = group 18; metalloids = the staircase elements (B, Si, Ge, As, Sb, Te).\n• A huge jump in successive ionization energies comes once the valence shell is empty.\n• Group 1 loses 1 electron → **1+ ions**, so its jump comes right after the 1st electron; group 2 jumps after the 2nd.","steps":[{"text":"Compare each step with the one before it.","hl":"480, 4400 and 6800 kJ/mol","calc":["4400 ÷ 480 ≈ 9.2 (**big jump**)","6800 ÷ 4400 ≈ 1.5 (modest)"],"graph":{"bars":[["IE₁","Ionization energy"],["IE₂","Ionization energy"]]}},{"text":"The jump comes after electron 1: one valence electron, group 1, 1+ ions."},{"text":"Alkaline earth metals rise only modestly from IE₁ to IE₂ and jump after the 2nd electron; inner transition metals typically form 3+ ions, so their jump comes after the 3rd; halogens are nonmetals that gain one electron.","strike":["alkaline earth metals","inner transition metals","halogens"]},{"text":"**Also:** group 17 = halogens, group 18 = noble gases, and the metalloids sit on the staircase between metals and nonmetals."},{"text":"Answer: alkali metals."}],"trap":"Confusing alkali (group 1, 1+) with alkaline earth (group 2, 2+): count the electrons removed before the jump."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number**.","marks":[{"t":"480","note":"1st electron"},{"t":"4400","note":"2nd electron: from the core"},{"t":"6800","note":"3rd electron"}]},{"text":"**Find the jump.** Divide each ionization energy by the one before it.","calc":["4400/480 ≈ 9.2 → jump after the 1st electron","6800/4400 ≈ 1.5"]},{"text":"**Count valence electrons.** One before the jump → group 1 → forms 1+ ions (Li⁺, Na⁺, K⁺)."},{"text":"**Also:** a group 2 metal shows its jump after the 2nd electron and forms 2+ ions; calcium is one, so its compounds, such as CaO, contain Ca²⁺. The transition metals fill the d block in the middle of the table."},{"text":"**Also:** sodium (group 1) forms only 1+ ions, while Mg and the transition metals Zn and Fe can form 2+, so Na cannot stand in for a divalent metal ion."},{"text":"**Also:** the group names to know: alkali metals (1), alkaline earth metals (2), halogens (17), noble gases (18), and metalloids along the staircase."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** the big ionization-energy jump comes right after the last valence electron; after the 1st means group 1, the alkali metals (1+ ions), and after the 2nd means group 2 (2+ ions)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-55a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:55#1","pe3-cp:41#1","pe3-cp:41#2","pe3-cp:41#3"],"q":"At 360 K, a reaction has ΔG° = −6.0 kJ/mol and RT = 3.0 kJ/mol. What is its equilibrium constant K? (e² ≈ 7.4)","choices":["0.14","0.50","2.0","7.4"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"Sign: ΔG° < 0 means K > 1, so 0.14 (the sign of ΔG° flipped, e⁻²) is out."},{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Sign: ΔG° < 0 means K > 1, so 0.50 is out."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"2.0 is ln K, not K: the log was never undone with e^x."}],"rule":"ln K = −ΔG°/RT, so a negative ΔG° means K > 1. Find the exponent first, then undo the log.","exp":"**ΔG° = −RT ln K**, so ln K = −ΔG° ÷ RT = 6.0 ÷ 3.0 = 2.0, and K = e^2.0 ≈ **7.4**. A negative ΔG° always gives K > 1: products are favored once equilibrium is reached.","how":{"ask":"Rearrange ΔG° = −RT ln K for K, then undo the natural log.","know":"• **ΔG° = −RT ln K** (natural log). Rearranged: **K = e^(−ΔG°/RT)**.\n• **ΔG° < 0 ⇔ K > 1** (products favored); ΔG° > 0 ⇔ K < 1; ΔG° = 0 ⇔ K = 1.\n• K is always products over reactants as the reaction is written.","steps":[{"text":"Pick out ΔG° and RT. Both are in kJ/mol, so the units cancel.","hl":"ΔG° = −6.0 kJ/mol"},{"text":"Solve ΔG° = −RT ln K for ln K.","calc":["ln K = −ΔG° ÷ RT","ln K = −(−6.0 kJ/mol) ÷ 3.0 kJ/mol","ln K = **2.0**"]},{"text":"Undo the natural log: raise e to that power.","calc":["K = e^2.0 ≈ **7.4**"]},{"text":"Check the sign: a negative ΔG° means K exceeds 1. That rules out 0.14 (sign of ΔG° flipped, e^−2) and 0.50 (RT ÷ ΔG°, with no log at all).","strike":["0.14","0.50"]},{"text":"2.0 is ln K, not K: the log was never undone.","strike":["2.0"]},{"text":"**Also, for unfolding:** for native ⇌ unfolded, products over reactants gives **K = [U]/[N]**, and **ΔG°′ = −RT ln K** with the natural log, never e. With 10% unfolded: K = 10/90 ≈ 0.11, ln K < 0, so ΔG°′ > 0 (unfolding unfavorable).","calc":["K = [U]/[N] = 10 ÷ 90 ≈ 0.11","ΔG°′ = −RT ln(0.11) > 0"]},{"text":"Answer: 7.4."}],"trap":"Stopping at ln K = 2.0, or writing ΔG° = −RT e^K: the formula uses ln K, so K itself is e raised to −ΔG°/RT."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"ΔG° = −6.0 kJ/mol","note":"negative: products favored"},{"t":"RT = 3.0 kJ/mol","note":"same units as ΔG°"},{"t":"e² ≈ 7.4","note":"the antilog you will need"}]},{"text":"**Link ΔG° and K.** The standard free energy and the equilibrium constant are tied by one formula with a natural log.","calc":["ΔG° = −RT ln K"]},{"text":"**Solve for K.**","calc":["ln K = −ΔG°/RT = 6.0/3.0 = 2.0","K = e^2.0 ≈ 7.4"]},{"text":"**Sign check.** ΔG° < 0 ⇔ ln K > 0 ⇔ K > 1. A positive ΔG° gives K < 1; ΔG° = 0 gives K = 1."},{"text":"**Also:** for a native → unfolded change, K = [U]/[N] (product over reactant), so ΔG°′ = −RT ln([U]/[N]). If fewer than half the molecules are unfolded, K < 1 and ΔG°′ is positive."},{"text":"**Also:** the log is ln, not e: ΔG°′ = −RT ln K, while K = e^(−ΔG°′/RT) is the same law solved the other way."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** ΔG° = −RT ln K with K = products/reactants; ΔG° < 0 means K > 1."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-57a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:57#1","pe3-cp:57#2","pe3-cp:57#3","pe3-cp:57#4","pe3-cp:57#5"],"q":"In each species below, the central atom is bonded only to halogen atoms. Which one has an octahedral molecular geometry?","fig":"cpt-halide-centers","choices":["TeF₆","ICl₄⁻","ClF₃","SiCl₄"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"ICl₄⁻ has only four bonded atoms (two lone pairs on opposite corners): square planar, not octahedral."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Octahedral molecular geometry needs six bonded atoms; ClF₃ has only three."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Octahedral needs six bonded atoms; SiCl₄ has four and no lone pairs: tetrahedral."}],"rule":"Molecular geometry counts bonded atoms only: octahedral needs six atoms around the center with no lone pairs (AX₆).","exp":"Count electron domains on the central atom. **TeF₆**: 6 bonds, 0 lone pairs → **octahedral**. ICl₄⁻: 4 bonds + 2 lone pairs → square planar. ClF₃: 3 bonds + 2 lone pairs → T-shaped. SiCl₄: 4 bonds → tetrahedral.","how":{"ask":"For each central atom, count bonds and lone pairs, then name the shape.","know":"• **Lone pairs** = (valence e⁻ − bonding e⁻) ÷ 2, after adding 1 e⁻ per negative charge and subtracting 1 per positive charge; each single bond uses 1 e⁻ of the central atom.\n• 6 bonds, 0 LP → **octahedral**.\n• 4 bonds + 2 LP → **square planar**.\n• 3 bonds + 2 LP → **T-shaped**.\n• 4 bonds, 0 LP → **tetrahedral**.","steps":[{"text":"Count the central atom's electron domains in each species.","hl":"octahedral molecular geometry","calc":["TeF₆: Te 6 e⁻, 6 bonds → 0 LP → 6 domains","ICl₄⁻: I 7 + 1 = 8 e⁻, 4 bonds → 2 LP → 6 domains","ClF₃: Cl 7 e⁻, 3 bonds → 2 LP → 5 domains","SiCl₄: Si 4 e⁻, 4 bonds → 0 LP → 4 domains"]},{"text":"Only TeF₆ has all six domains as bonds, so atoms sit at all six octahedral corners.","calc":["TeF₆ → **octahedral**"]},{"text":"ICl₄⁻ is square planar (two lone pairs take opposite corners), ClF₃ is T-shaped, and SiCl₄ is tetrahedral.","strike":["ICl₄⁻","ClF₃","SiCl₄"]},{"text":"Answer: TeF₆."}],"trap":"Picking ICl₄⁻ because it has six electron domains: its electron geometry is octahedral, but with two lone pairs the molecular shape is square planar."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"central atom","note":"count its domains"},{"t":"octahedral molecular geometry","note":"6 bonds, no lone pairs"}]},{"text":"**Count electron domains.** Bonds plus lone pairs on the central atom set the electron geometry; only the bonded atoms set the molecular shape."},{"text":"**6 bonds, 0 lone pairs = octahedral.** TeF₆: Te's six valence electrons all go into six Te–F bonds."},{"text":"**Also:** 4 bonds + 2 lone pairs is square planar: in ICl₄⁻ the lone pairs sit opposite each other."},{"text":"**Also:** 3 bonds + 2 lone pairs is T-shaped: ClF₃'s lone pairs take two equatorial positions of a trigonal bipyramid."},{"text":"**Also:** 4 bonds and no lone pairs is tetrahedral, as in SiCl₄ (109.5° angles)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** count domains on the central atom; 6 bonds/0 LP octahedral, 4 bonds/2 LP square planar, 3 bonds/2 LP T-shaped, 4 bonds/0 LP tetrahedral."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe3-59a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe3-cp:59#1","pe3-cp:59#2","pe3-cp:59#3"],"q":"A colorless crystal is extremely hard, does not conduct electricity, and has an extremely high melting point (above 3500 °C). Which substance is it?","choices":["diamond","solid CO₂","solid I₂","white phosphorus"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"CO₂ is a gas at room temperature, so its solid can't melt above 3500 °C."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"I₂ is a molecular solid held by London forces; it melts near 114 °C."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"White phosphorus is P₄ molecules held by weak London forces, so it melts low (about 44 °C)."}],"rule":"Very hard, nonconducting and very high melting = covalent network solid; small molecules held by London forces melt low.","exp":"Extreme hardness and a very high melting point mean **covalent bonds must break**, the mark of a **covalent network solid**. Diamond is carbon with each atom bonded to four others. CO₂, I₂ and P₄ are molecular solids held by weak forces.","how":{"ask":"Decide which kind of solid the properties fit, then find the substance of that kind.","know":"• **Covalent network solid**: every atom bonded covalently to several neighbors in one extended lattice → very hard, very high melting point. Period-2 example: **carbon** (diamond, graphite).\n• **Molecular solids** (N₂, O₂, CO₂, I₂, P₄): separate molecules held by weak forces → low melting points.\n• **P and I are not period 2** (P is period 3, I is period 5).","steps":[{"text":"Very hard, nonconducting and stable to thousands of degrees: covalent bonds hold the whole crystal.","hl":"extremely high melting point (above 3500 °C)"},{"text":"Solid CO₂ (dry ice) sublimes near −78 °C: separate molecules.","strike":["solid CO₂"]},{"text":"I₂ and P₄ are molecular solids with low melting points; neither element is in period 2 anyway.","strike":["solid I₂","white phosphorus"]},{"text":"**Also:** of the period-2 nonmetals C, N and O, only carbon forms a network; N₂ and O₂ are small diatomic molecules that are gases at room temperature."},{"text":"Answer: diamond."}],"trap":"Thinking strong bonds inside a molecule (C=O, N≡N) make a high melting point: melting a molecular solid breaks only the weak forces between molecules."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key properties**.","marks":[{"t":"extremely hard","note":"strong bonds throughout"},{"t":"does not conduct electricity","note":"not a metal"},{"t":"above 3500 °C","note":"covalent bonds must break"}]},{"text":"**Name the solid type.** Hard + nonconducting + very high melting point = covalent network solid."},{"text":"**Period 2 example:** carbon. In diamond each C bonds to 4 others; in graphite, to 3 in sheets."},{"text":"**Also:** N₂ and O₂ are discrete molecules (gases at room temperature), and P and I are not in period 2."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** an atom covalently bonded to several neighbors in an endless lattice makes a network solid; of the period-2 nonmetals C, N and O, only carbon forms one."}]},
  // ── Practice Exam 4 · Chem/Phys ──
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-03a","s":"Electrochemistry & Redox","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:3#1","pe4-cp:3#2"],"q":"Dihydrogen phosphate gives up a proton to water: H₂PO₄⁻ + H₂O → HPO₄²⁻ + H₃O⁺. What happens to phosphorus's oxidation state, and what type of reaction is this?","choices":["Stays +5; acid–base","Stays +5; redox","Rises to +6; redox","Rises to +7; acid–base"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"impossible","why":"P is in group 15, with five valence electrons, so +5 is its highest oxidation state; +6 is impossible."},{"c":3,"tag":"impossible","why":"P can't exceed +5 (group 15), so +7 is impossible."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Nothing changes oxidation state; only a proton is transferred, so the reaction is not redox."}],"rule":"If only H⁺ moves between species, no oxidation state changes, so it isn't redox. A group 15 element tops out at +5.","exp":"In H₂PO₄⁻: 2(+1) + P + 4(−2) = −1, so P = +5. In HPO₄²⁻: (+1) + P + 4(−2) = −2, so P = +5. **Only an H⁺ moves**, with its oxidation state of +1 unchanged, so no element changes oxidation state: it is **acid–base, not redox**.","how":{"ask":"Assign P's oxidation state before and after, then decide whether anything was oxidized or reduced.","know":"• Oxidation states: H = +1, O = −2; they add up to the species' charge.\n• **Redox** needs an element whose oxidation state changes. Moving H⁺ (still +1) changes no oxidation state: that is **acid–base**.","steps":[{"text":"Before: phosphorus in the acid.","hl":"H₂PO₄⁻","calc":["2(+1) + P + 4(−2) = −1","P = −1 − 2 + 8 = **+5**"]},{"text":"After: phosphorus in the conjugate base.","hl":"HPO₄²⁻","calc":["(+1) + P + 4(−2) = −2","P = −2 − 1 + 8 = **+5**"]},{"text":"P stays +5, so the rising values are wrong.","strike":["Rises to +6; redox","Rises to +7; acid–base"]},{"text":"Nothing changes oxidation state; only a proton is transferred, so the reaction is not redox.","strike":["Stays +5; redox"]},{"text":"Answer: Stays +5; acid–base."}],"trap":"Thinking that losing a bond to H oxidizes the central atom: the H leaves as H⁺, taking no electrons, so the central atom's oxidation state is unchanged."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"gives up a proton","note":"H⁺ transfer"},{"t":"H₂PO₄⁻","note":"charge −1"},{"t":"HPO₄²⁻","note":"charge −2"}]},{"text":"**Assign oxidation states.** H = +1, O = −2, and the sum equals the charge.","calc":["H₂PO₄⁻: P = +5","HPO₄²⁻: P = +5"]},{"text":"**Also:** the same holds for nitrogen: in NH₃, N + 3(+1) = 0 → N = −3; in NH₄⁺, N + 4(+1) = +1 → N = −3. Gaining H⁺ leaves N at −3.","calc":["NH₃: N = −3","NH₄⁺: N = −3"]},{"text":"**Classify.** No oxidation state changes and only H⁺ moves: acid–base, not redox."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a reaction is redox only if some element's oxidation state changes; a pure H⁺ transfer is acid–base."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-04a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:4#1","pe4-cp:4#2","pe6-cp:26#2"],"q":"Propane burns completely: C₃H₈ + 5 O₂ → 4 H₂O + 3 CO₂. If 8.8 g of propane (44 g/mol) burns, what volume of CO₂ is released at STP?","choices":["4.5 L","13.4 L","14.7 L","17.9 L"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"skipped","why":"Too small: 4.5 L is 0.20 × 22.4, the propane moles; the × 3 for 3 CO₂ per propane was skipped."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"14.7 L uses 24.5 L/mol, the molar volume at 25 °C; at STP a mole of gas is 22.4 L."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"17.9 L is 0.80 × 22.4, the 4 H₂O coefficient, not the 3 CO₂."}],"rule":"Moles of fuel × the CO₂ coefficient × 22.4 L at STP. Round: 8.8 ÷ 44 is 0.2 mol, × 3, × about 22.","exp":"8.8 g ÷ 44 g/mol = 0.20 mol propane. The equation gives 3 CO₂ per propane: 0.60 mol CO₂. **At STP one mole of ideal gas occupies 22.4 L**: 0.60 × 22.4 = 13.44 ≈ **13.4 L**.","how":{"ask":"Convert grams to moles, use the coefficients to get moles of CO₂, then multiply by the STP molar volume.","know":"• moles = mass ÷ molar mass.\n• The coefficients give the **mole ratio** of product to reactant.\n• **V = n × 22.4 L/mol**, true only at STP (0 °C, 1 atm); at 25 °C the molar volume is about 24.5 L/mol.","steps":[{"text":"Moles of propane.","hl":"8.8 g of propane (44 g/mol)","calc":["n = 8.8 g ÷ 44 g/mol = **0.20 mol**"]},{"text":"Use the ratio from the equation.","hl":"3 CO₂","calc":["n(CO₂) = 0.20 mol × 3 = **0.60 mol**"]},{"text":"Convert to volume at STP.","hl":"at STP","calc":["V = 0.60 mol × 22.4 L/mol = 13.44 L ≈ **13.4 L**"]},{"text":"4.5 L skips the 3 : 1 ratio (0.20 × 22.4); 17.9 L uses the water coefficient (0.80 × 22.4); 14.7 L uses 24.5 L/mol, the molar volume at 25 °C, not at STP.","strike":["4.5 L","17.9 L","14.7 L"]},{"text":"Answer: 13.4 L."}],"trap":"Using 22.4 L/mol without the mole ratio, or at a temperature other than STP."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"8.8 g","note":"mass burned"},{"t":"44 g/mol","note":"molar mass"},{"t":"3 CO₂","note":"3 mol CO₂ per mol propane"},{"t":"at STP","note":"22.4 L/mol applies"}]},{"text":"**Grams to moles.**","calc":["8.8 ÷ 44 = 0.20 mol"]},{"text":"**Moles of reactant to moles of product** with the coefficients.","calc":["0.20 × 3 = 0.60 mol CO₂"]},{"text":"**Moles to liters at STP.** One mole of any ideal gas fills 22.4 L at 0 °C and 1 atm; at other conditions use PV = nRT instead.","calc":["0.60 × 22.4 = 13.4 L"]},{"text":"**Also:** the same coefficients give the oxygen used up. Moles of O₂ = moles of fuel × (O₂ coefficient ÷ fuel coefficient), then × 22.4 L/mol at STP.","calc":["0.20 mol C₃H₈ × 5/1 = 1.00 mol O₂","V(O₂) = 1.00 mol × 22.4 L/mol = 22.4 L at STP"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** V(gas, STP) = (moles of reactant × product/reactant coefficient ratio) × 22.4 L/mol, and 22.4 L/mol holds at STP only."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-07a","s":"Spectroscopy","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe4-cp:7#1","pe4-cp:7#2","pe4-cp:8#1","pe4-cp:8#2"],"q":"Glucose standards of 0.8, 1.6 and 2.4 mM read A = 0.16, 0.32 and 0.48. A serum sample read 0.84, so it was diluted 10-fold and reread at 0.28. What was its original concentration?","choices":["1.4 mM","4.2 mM","14 mM","42 mM"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"skipped","why":"1.4 mM is the diluted sample; the 10-fold dilution must be multiplied back."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"4.2 mM comes from 0.84, which lies above the top standard (0.48), off the calibrated line."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"42 mM converts the out-of-range 0.84 reading and then multiplies by 10; only the in-range reread is valid."}],"rule":"Use only a reading inside the standards' range (here A = 0.20 per mM); convert it, then multiply by the dilution factor.","exp":"The standards rise 0.20 absorbance unit per mM (**A ∝ c**). The first reading, 0.84, lies above the top standard, so it cannot be trusted; the diluted reading, 0.28, lies inside the range and gives 0.28 ÷ 0.20 = 1.4 mM. Undo the 10-fold dilution: 1.4 × 10 = **14 mM**.","how":{"ask":"Read the in-range reading off the standard line, then multiply by the dilution factor.","know":"• **Beer's law**: A = εbc, so at a fixed path length **A ∝ c**; the standards give the slope (absorbance per unit concentration).\n• Only **interpolate**: a reading above the highest standard must be diluted into the standard range, never read off an extended line.\n• Original concentration = **diluted concentration × dilution factor**.","steps":[{"text":"The standards set the line; find its slope.","hl":"0.8, 1.6 and 2.4 mM read A = 0.16, 0.32 and 0.48","calc":["slope = 0.16 ÷ 0.8 = **0.20 per mM**","check: 0.48 ÷ 2.4 = 0.20 per mM"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Glucose standards: absorbance rises in step with concentration","x":{"label":"Glucose (mM)","min":0,"max":3,"step":0.5},"y":{"label":"Absorbance","min":0,"max":0.6,"step":0.1},"series":[{"name":"Standards (with blank)","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[0.8,0.16],[1.6,0.32],[2.4,0.48]]}]}},{"text":"The first reading lies above the top standard (0.48), outside the proven straight range, so it is not used.","hl":"A serum sample read 0.84"},{"text":"Use the diluted reading, which falls inside the range.","hl":"reread at 0.28","calc":["c(diluted) = A ÷ slope","c(diluted) = 0.28 ÷ 0.20 per mM = **1.4 mM**"]},{"text":"Undo the dilution.","hl":"diluted 10-fold","calc":["c(original) = 1.4 mM × 10 = **14 mM**"]},{"text":"1.4 mM forgets to multiply back by the dilution factor. 4.2 mM extrapolates the line to the out-of-range 0.84 (0.84 ÷ 0.20) and 42 mM then multiplies that by 10 as well.","strike":["1.4 mM","4.2 mM","42 mM"]},{"text":"Answer: 14 mM."}],"trap":"Reporting the concentration in the cuvette (1.4 mM): the sample was diluted, so multiply by 10."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"0.8, 1.6 and 2.4 mM","note":"standard concentrations"},{"t":"0.16, 0.32 and 0.48","note":"their absorbances"},{"t":"read 0.84","note":"above the top standard"},{"t":"diluted 10-fold","note":"dilution factor"},{"t":"reread at 0.28","note":"the in-range reading"}]},{"text":"**The standards give the slope.** Absorbance is proportional to concentration, so divide any standard's A by its c.","calc":["0.32 ÷ 1.6 = 0.20 per mM"]},{"text":"**Interpolate, don't extrapolate.** The standards only show the line is straight up to A = 0.48. A reading above that is diluted until it lands between the standards."},{"text":"**Read, then multiply back.** The line gives the concentration in the diluted sample; the dilution factor returns it to the original.","calc":["0.28 ÷ 0.20 = 1.4 mM","1.4 mM × 10 = 14 mM"]},{"text":"**Also:** with no reading above range, the same two steps apply: a sample diluted 5-fold that reads A = 0.24 contains 0.24 ÷ 0.20 = 1.2 mM in the cuvette and 1.2 × 5 = 6.0 mM originally."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** c(original) = (A ÷ slope of the standards) × dilution factor, using only a reading that falls inside the range of the standards."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-10a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:10#1","pe4-cp:10#2","pe4-cp:10#3"],"q":"Four alcohols are each heated with chromic acid, H₂CrO₄, a strong oxidizing agent. Which alcohol is NOT oxidized?","choices":["Pentan-1-ol","Pentan-2-ol","3-Methylbutan-2-ol","2-Methylbutan-2-ol"],"choiceFigs":["cpt-pentan-1-ol","cpt-pentan-2-ol","cpt-3-methylbutan-2-ol","cpt-2-methylbutan-2-ol"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"'-1-ol' puts the OH on an end CH₂: a primary alcohol, readily oxidized."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Unbranched pentan-2-ol has its OH on a CH between two carbons: secondary, so it becomes a ketone."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"In 3-methylbutan-2-ol the OH carbon is a CH bonded to two carbons: secondary, so it oxidizes to a ketone."}],"rule":"Chromic acid needs an H on the carbon holding the OH: primary and secondary alcohols oxidize; tertiary alcohols do not.","exp":"Oxidizing an alcohol removes **the H on the carbon bearing the OH** and turns C–OH into C=O. In **2-methylbutan-2-ol** that carbon carries three carbon groups and no H, so it is a tertiary alcohol and resists oxidation. Pentan-1-ol (primary) goes to pentanal and then pentanoic acid; the two secondary alcohols become ketones.","how":{"ask":"Classify each alcohol as 1°, 2° or 3° by counting the carbons on the carbinol carbon, then find the one with no H there.","know":"• **Carbinol carbon** = the carbon holding the OH. Oxidation needs an **H on that carbon**.\n• **1° alcohol → aldehyde → carboxylic acid** (a strong oxidant like H₂CrO₄ goes all the way to the acid).\n• **2° alcohol → ketone** (stops there).\n• **3° alcohol → no reaction** (no H on the carbinol carbon).","steps":[{"text":"The reagent is a strong oxidant, so any alcohol that can be oxidized will be.","hl":"chromic acid, H₂CrO₄"},{"text":"Pentan-1-ol: the OH carbon is CH₂, bonded to one carbon, so it is primary. It is oxidized to an aldehyde and then on to the acid.","calc":["CH₃(CH₂)₃CH₂OH → CH₃(CH₂)₃CHO → CH₃(CH₂)₃COOH"],"strike":["Pentan-1-ol"]},{"text":"Pentan-2-ol and 3-methylbutan-2-ol: the OH carbon is CH, bonded to two carbons, so both are secondary and become ketones.","calc":["CH₃CH(OH)CH₂CH₂CH₃ → pentan-2-one","CH₃CH(OH)CH(CH₃)₂ → 3-methylbutan-2-one"],"strike":["Pentan-2-ol","3-Methylbutan-2-ol"]},{"text":"2-Methylbutan-2-ol: the OH carbon holds two CH₃ groups and an ethyl, three carbons and no H. It is tertiary and cannot form a C=O without breaking a C–C bond.","calc":["(CH₃)₂C(OH)CH₂CH₃: carbinol C has 0 H → no oxidation"]},{"text":"Answer: 2-Methylbutan-2-ol."}],"trap":"Picking a branched secondary alcohol because it looks crowded: branching elsewhere does not matter; only the H on the carbinol carbon does."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"chromic acid","note":"strong oxidant"},{"t":"NOT oxidized","note":"look for the alcohol that cannot lose an H"}]},{"text":"**Find the carbinol carbon** in each name: the number before \"-ol\" tells you which carbon holds the OH. Count how many carbons are bonded to it: one = 1°, two = 2°, three = 3°."},{"text":"**Primary alcohols** have two H's on the carbinol carbon: they go to an **aldehyde**, and a strong oxidant carries them on to the **carboxylic acid**.","calc":["RCH₂OH → RCHO → RCOOH"]},{"text":"**Secondary alcohols** have one H there: they become a **ketone** and stop.","calc":["R₂CHOH → R₂C=O"]},{"text":"**Tertiary alcohols** have no H there: nothing to remove, so **no oxidation**.","calc":["R₃COH → no reaction"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** an alcohol is oxidized only if its carbinol carbon carries an H: 1° gives aldehyde then acid, 2° gives ketone, 3° does not react."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-11a","s":"Stereochemistry","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:11#1","pe4-cp:11#2","pe4-cp:11#3","pe4-cp:11#4"],"q":"In CH₃–CH=C(Br)–CH₂CH₃, shown, the bromine and the terminal CH₃ bonded to the CH carbon are cis to each other. What is the compound's name?","fig":"cpt-z-3-bromopent-2-ene","choices":["(Z)-3-bromopent-2-ene","(E)-3-bromopent-2-ene","(Z)-3-bromopent-3-ene","(E)-3-bromopent-3-ene"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"The higher-priority groups, Br and the C1 CH₃, are on the same side; E would need them opposite."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Both wrong: Br and the C1 CH₃ are cis, not E, and 'pent-3-ene' renumbers to a lower locant from the other end."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Locant rule: number from the end nearer the C=C; in a 5-carbon chain a C=C at 3 is at 2 counted from the other end."}],"rule":"Number from the end nearest the C=C, then compare the higher-priority group on each alkene carbon: same side = Z, opposite = E.","exp":"The five-carbon chain holds the C=C, and numbering from the CH₃–CH= end puts it at **C2**, with Br on **C3**: 3-bromopent-2-ene. On C2, **CH₃ beats H**; on C3, **Br (atomic number 35) beats the ethyl carbon (6)**. Those two winners are on the same side, so the alkene is **Z**, even though the methyl and the ethyl are on opposite sides.","how":{"ask":"Number the chain to give the C=C the lowest locant, name the substituent, then compare the higher-priority group on each alkene carbon.","know":"• **Parent chain**: the longest chain that contains the C=C, numbered from the end **nearer the C=C**.\n• **Substituents** get the locant of the carbon they sit on.\n• **CIP priority**: on each alkene carbon, the atom of higher atomic number wins.\n• **Z** = the two winners on the **same side**; **E** = opposite sides.","steps":[{"text":"The longest chain through the C=C has five carbons. From the left, the C=C starts at C2; from the right, at C3. Number from the left.","hl":"CH₃–CH=C(Br)–CH₂CH₃","calc":["left: C1H₃–C2H=C3(Br)–C4H₂–C5H₃ → C=C at 2 ✓","right: C=C at 3 ✗"]},{"text":"Numbering from the ethyl end gives the C=C a locant of 3, which is higher than needed.","strike":["(Z)-3-bromopent-3-ene","(E)-3-bromopent-3-ene"]},{"text":"The only substituent is Br, on C3.","calc":["3-bromopent-2-ene"]},{"text":"Rank the groups on each alkene carbon by the atomic number of the first atom.","calc":["C2: CH₃ (C, 6) > H (1) → CH₃ wins","C3: Br (35) > CH₂CH₃ (C, 6) → Br wins"]},{"text":"The winners, Br and the C1 CH₃, are on the same side, so the alkene is Z. Calling it E comes from comparing the two alkyl groups instead of Br.","hl":"are cis to each other","strike":["(E)-3-bromopent-2-ene"]},{"text":"Answer: (Z)-3-bromopent-2-ene."}],"trap":"Reading E/Z as cis/trans of the carbon chain: the ethyl and the methyl are on opposite sides, but Br outranks the ethyl, so the winners are cis and the name is Z."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"CH₃–CH=C(Br)–CH₂CH₃","note":"five carbons, one Br on an alkene carbon"},{"t":"the bromine and the terminal CH₃","note":"the groups whose positions are given"},{"t":"cis to each other","note":"same side; compare with the priorities"}]},{"text":"**Pick and number the chain.** Take the longest chain that includes both alkene carbons, and number from the end that reaches the C=C first.","calc":["C=C at 2 from the left vs 3 from the right → left"]},{"text":"**Name the substituents** with their locants and put them in front of the parent, in alphabetical order if there are several.","calc":["Br on C3 → 3-bromo + pent-2-ene"]},{"text":"**Rank by CIP.** On each alkene carbon, compare the two attached atoms; higher atomic number wins. If they tie, move out to the next atoms.","calc":["Br (35) > C (6) > H (1)"]},{"text":"**Assign Z or E.** Winners on the same side = Z (zusammen); on opposite sides = E (entgegen)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** number the longest chain containing the C=C from its nearer end, name the substituents, then put Z when the higher-priority groups on the two alkene carbons share a side and E when they do not."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-12a","s":"Mechanics & Energy","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:12#1"],"q":"A hiker walks 11 km in a straight line, turns through any angle, then walks 7 km more. Which distance from the start is impossible?","choices":["4 km","10 km","18 km","19 km"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"4 km = 11 − 7, the turn-around case, so it can happen."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"10 km lies between 11 − 7 and 11 + 7, so some turn angle gives it."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"18 km = 11 + 7, walking straight on, so it can happen."}],"rule":"After two straight legs, the distance from the start lies between the difference and the sum of the legs; anything outside cannot happen.","exp":"Two displacements add as vectors: the resultant lies between **|11 − 7| = 4 km** (turning back) and **11 + 7 = 18 km** (going straight on). **19 km** is outside that range, so it can't happen.","how":{"ask":"Find the smallest and largest possible magnitudes of the vector sum.","know":"• For vectors of magnitudes A and B: **|A − B| ≤ |A + B| ≤ A + B**.\n• Minimum when they point opposite (180°), maximum when parallel (0°); every value between is reachable at some angle.","steps":[{"text":"The two legs are vectors of 11 km and 7 km at an adjustable angle.","hl":"turns through any angle"},{"text":"Compute the two extremes.","calc":["min = |11 − 7| = **4 km** (walks back)","max = 11 + 7 = **18 km** (keeps straight)","at 90°: √(11² + 7²) = √170 ≈ 13 km"]},{"text":"4 km and 18 km are the ends of the range, and 10 km lies inside it.","strike":["4 km","18 km","10 km"]},{"text":"Answer: 19 km."}],"trap":"Thinking the endpoints are impossible: turning fully back or not at all gives exactly 4 km or 18 km."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words and numbers**.","marks":[{"t":"11 km","note":"vector A"},{"t":"7 km","note":"vector B"},{"t":"any angle","note":"the sum can vary"},{"t":"impossible","note":"look outside the range"}]},{"text":"**Smallest sum:** the vectors point opposite, so they subtract.","calc":["|A − B| = |11 − 7| = 4 km"]},{"text":"**Largest sum:** the vectors point the same way, so they add.","calc":["A + B = 11 + 7 = 18 km"]},{"text":"**Everything between is possible** at some angle; anything outside is not.","calc":["4 km ≤ R ≤ 18 km","19 km > 18 km → impossible"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** |A − B| ≤ |A + B| ≤ A + B; a magnitude outside that range is impossible."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-13a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe4-cp:13#1","pe4-cp:13#2"],"q":"Overnight contact lenses flatten the center of the cornea of a myopic eye so distant objects focus on the retina. What does flattening change?","choices":["Larger radius; lower power","Larger radius; higher power","Smaller radius; lower power","Smaller radius; higher power"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Higher power means a shorter focal length, which would pull the focus further forward and worsen myopia."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Flatter means less curved, part of a bigger sphere: the radius grows, not shrinks."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"A smaller radius means more curvature, the opposite of flattening."}],"rule":"A flatter curve belongs to a bigger circle, and a gentler curve bends light less: lens power falls as the radius grows.","exp":"A **flatter** surface is part of a bigger sphere: its **radius of curvature increases**. A less curved surface bends light less, so the **focal length gets longer** (lower power, P = 1/f) and the focus moves back from in front of the retina onto it.","how":{"ask":"Link flatter shape → radius of curvature → focal length → where the image lands.","know":"• **Flatter = larger radius of curvature** (a flat surface has an infinite radius).\n• For a refracting surface or lens, **f grows with R** (lensmaker: 1/f ∝ 1/R); power P = 1/f falls.\n• **Myopia**: the eye is too converging, so the image forms in front of the retina; weakening it moves the focus back.","steps":[{"text":"Flattening means less curvature, so the radius of curvature grows.","hl":"flatten the center of the cornea of a myopic eye","calc":["flatter → R larger"]},{"text":"A larger radius bends light less: the cornea is less converging.","calc":["1/f ∝ 1/R → R up → f up","power P = 1/f → down"]},{"text":"A smaller radius means more curvature, the opposite of flattening.","strike":["Smaller radius; lower power","Smaller radius; higher power"]},{"text":"Higher power means a shorter focal length, which would pull the focus further forward and worsen myopia.","strike":["Larger radius; higher power"]},{"text":"Answer: larger radius; lower power (longer focal length)."}],"trap":"Pairing a larger radius with higher power (shorter focal length): a flatter surface is weaker, not stronger."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"myopic","note":"focus falls in front of the retina"},{"t":"flatten","note":"less curved surface"},{"t":"focus on the retina","note":"the goal"}]},{"text":"**Flatter means a bigger circle.** A gently curved surface belongs to a sphere with a large radius.","calc":["more curved → small R","flatter → large R"]},{"text":"**Bigger radius, weaker focusing.** Focal length rises with R, so the cornea converges light less.","calc":["R up → f up → power down"]},{"text":"**Also:** a myopic eye focuses too strongly, in front of the retina; a longer focal length moves the image back onto the retina.","calc":["too strong → focus in front","weaker cornea → focus moves back onto retina"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** flattening the cornea raises its radius of curvature, which lengthens the focal length and pushes a nearsighted eye's focus back onto the retina."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-16a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"physics","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:16#1"],"q":"A laser can fire continuously or in 4-ms pulses every 20 ms at equal peak power. Why is pulsing gentler on nearby tissue?","choices":["Gaps let the heat dissipate","Its mean output is larger","Its wavelength becomes shorter","More light is reflected"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"The pulsed beam is dark most of the time, so its mean output is smaller, not larger."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Switching a beam on and off changes its timing, not its wavelength."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Pulsing changes only when the light arrives, not how much the surface reflects."}],"rule":"Pulsing at the same peak power lowers the average power and adds off-time between pulses; it doesn't change the light's wavelength or reflection.","exp":"The pulsed laser is on only 4 of every 20 ms, so it is **off 16 of every 20 ms**. During each 16-ms off-time, **deposited heat diffuses away**, so the surrounding tissue stays at a lower average temperature than under a beam that never stops.","how":{"ask":"What happens to the heat in the tissue while the pulsed laser is off?","know":"Heat flows from a warm spot into cooler surroundings over time, so any off-time lets the tissue cool before the next burst. The pulsed beam is on 4 ms and **off 16 ms** of every 20-ms cycle.","steps":[{"text":"Find the timing: same strength while on, but the pulsed beam is on only part of each cycle.","hl":"4-ms pulses every 20 ms at equal peak power"},{"text":"Find the off-time in each cycle.","calc":["off-time = 20 − 4 = **16 ms** per cycle"]},{"text":"The pulsed beam is dark most of the time, so it can't deliver more energy.","strike":["Its mean output is larger"]},{"text":"Pulsing changes only when the light arrives, not its wavelength or how much the surface reflects.","strike":["Its wavelength becomes shorter","More light is reflected"]},{"text":"During each 16-ms gap, heat diffuses out of the treated spot, so the nearby tissue's average temperature stays lower.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Nearby tissue temperature (sketch)","x":{"label":"Time (ms)","min":0,"max":60,"step":10},"y":{"label":"Temperature (°C)","min":35,"max":55,"step":5},"series":[{"name":"Continuous","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,37],[20,42],[40,47],[60,52]]},{"name":"Pulsed","marker":"square","line":"straight","points":[[0,37],[4,38],[20,37.3],[24,38.3],[40,37.6],[44,38.6],[60,37.9]]}]}},{"text":"Answer: gaps let the heat dissipate."}],"trap":"Equal peak power does not mean equal energy per second. The pulsed beam is off 16 of every 20 ms, so heat has time to leave between pulses."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"continuously","note":"always on"},{"t":"4-ms pulses","note":"on-time"},{"t":"every 20 ms","note":"one full cycle"},{"t":"equal peak power","note":"same strength while on"}]},{"text":"**Heat spreads** from a hot spot into cooler surroundings over time. Each off-time lets it leave before the next pulse arrives.","calc":["off-time = 20 − 4 = 16 ms","continuous beam: off-time = 0 → heat piles up"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** pulsed light at the same peak power heats tissue less, because the gaps let heat dissipate and the average temperature stays lower."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-17a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:17#1","pe4-cp:17#2","pe4-cp:17#3","pe4-cp:17#4"],"q":"Which intermolecular forces act between molecules in a sample of pure liquid methanol, CH₃OH?","fig":"cpt-methanol","choices":["London forces only","Hydrogen bonds only","Dipole–dipole and London forces","Ion–dipole forces and London forces"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Methanol is polar: the O pulls electron density and the bent C–O–H gives a net dipole, so London forces aren't alone."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Every molecule has London forces, so a list without them is incomplete."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Pure methanol has no ions, so there is no ion–dipole force."}],"rule":"Every molecule has London forces; a polar molecule adds dipole forces (H-bonding is the strongest kind); ion forces need ions.","exp":"**Every** molecule has London dispersion forces. Methanol is **polar** (bent C–O–H with a strongly electronegative O), so it also has dipole–dipole attractions, and because its H sits directly on oxygen those attractions are **hydrogen bonds**, the strongest kind of dipole–dipole force. Methanol has no ions, so there is no ion–dipole force.","how":{"ask":"List the forces in order: London (always), then dipole–dipole if the molecule is polar, then check whether that dipole force is hydrogen bonding.","know":"• **London dispersion**: present between **all** molecules.\n• **Dipole–dipole**: added when the molecule is **polar**.\n• **Hydrogen bonding**: an especially strong **dipole–dipole** force, when H is bonded to **N, O or F**.\n• **Ion–dipole**: needs an **ion** in the mixture.","steps":[{"text":"Every molecule has London forces, so the answer must include them. A list without them, or with only one polar force, is incomplete.","strike":["Hydrogen bonds only"]},{"text":"Methanol is polar: the O pulls electron density from C and H, and the bent C–O–H gives a net dipole. So dipole–dipole forces are present too.","hl":"methanol, CH₃OH","strike":["London forces only"]},{"text":"The H on O makes those dipole–dipole forces hydrogen bonds, which are a strong kind of dipole–dipole force, not a separate force outside it.","calc":["O–H present → H-bonding ⊂ dipole–dipole"]},{"text":"Ion–dipole forces need ions, and pure methanol is a molecular liquid.","hl":"pure liquid","strike":["Ion–dipole forces and London forces"]},{"text":"**Also:** ammonia works the same way: NH₃ is polar and has H on N, so its molecules attract by dipole–dipole forces (hydrogen bonds) plus London forces."},{"text":"Answer: Dipole–dipole and London forces."}],"trap":"Choosing hydrogen bonds alone: hydrogen bonding is a kind of dipole–dipole force, and London forces are always there as well."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"intermolecular forces","note":"between molecules, not bonds inside one"},{"t":"pure liquid methanol","note":"no ions present"},{"t":"CH₃OH","note":"H on O"}]},{"text":"**Step 1: London forces.** Every molecule has a temporary dipole from moving electrons, so London dispersion forces are always on the list."},{"text":"**Step 2: polar?** If the molecule has polar bonds that do not cancel, add dipole–dipole forces.","calc":["CH₃OH: bent C–O–H → polar → dipole–dipole ✓"]},{"text":"**Step 3: hydrogen bonding?** If H is bonded to N, O or F, the dipole–dipole force is a hydrogen bond, the strongest dipole–dipole type.","calc":["O–H → hydrogen bonds"]},{"text":"**Also:** for NH₃: London ✓, polar (pyramidal) ✓, H on N ✓ → dipole–dipole forces (hydrogen bonds) and London forces."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** start with London forces (always), add dipole–dipole for a polar molecule, and call that dipole–dipole force hydrogen bonding when H sits on N, O or F."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-18a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:18#1","pe4-cp:18#2"],"q":"Hydroxylamine, NH₂OH, is a weak base with Kb = 9.0 × 10⁻⁹. Approximately what is [OH⁻] in a 0.40 M hydroxylamine solution at equilibrium?","choices":["3.6 × 10⁻⁹ M","9.5 × 10⁻⁵ M","6.0 × 10⁻⁵ M","1.9 × 10⁻⁴ M"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"skipped","why":"No square root: [OH⁻] = √(Kb·C), and 3.6 × 10⁻⁹ is just Kb·C."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"9.5 × 10⁻⁵ M is √Kb alone; the 0.40 M concentration was left out."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"Too big: with C = 0.40 M (< 1 M), √(Kb·C) must be below √Kb ≈ 9.5 × 10⁻⁵ M; the exponent wasn't made even first."}],"rule":"Weak base: [OH⁻] ≈ √(Kb × C). Rewrite Kb × C with an even power of ten before taking the root.","exp":"For a weak base, **Kb = x²/C**, so **x = √(Kb·C)** = √(9.0 × 10⁻⁹ × 0.40) = √(3.6 × 10⁻⁹). Rewrite with an even power: √(36 × 10⁻¹⁰) = 6.0 × 10⁻⁵ M. So little base reacts that C barely changes.","how":{"ask":"Set up Kb with x for the ions formed, solve x = √(Kb·C), and take the root of the power of ten carefully.","know":"• B + H₂O ⇌ BH⁺ + OH⁻, so Kb = [BH⁺][OH⁻]/[B] = **x²/C** when x is small next to C.\n• **x = √(Kb·C)**; x is both [OH⁻] and [BH⁺].\n• To take a square root of 10ⁿ, first make n even (move a factor of 10 into the coefficient), then halve it.","steps":[{"text":"Collect the base's Kb and its starting concentration.","hl":"Kb = 9.0 × 10⁻⁹","calc":["C = 0.40 M"]},{"text":"Write the small-x expression and solve for x.","calc":["Kb = x² / C","x = √(Kb · C)","Kb · C = 9.0 × 10⁻⁹ × 0.40 = 3.6 × 10⁻⁹"]},{"text":"Make the exponent even before taking the root.","calc":["3.6 × 10⁻⁹ = 36 × 10⁻¹⁰","√36 = 6.0; √10⁻¹⁰ = 10⁻⁵","x = **6.0 × 10⁻⁵ M**"]},{"text":"Check the approximation: x is far below C.","calc":["6.0 × 10⁻⁵ ÷ 0.40 ≈ 0.015 %"]},{"text":"3.6 × 10⁻⁹ M is Kb·C with no square root; 9.5 × 10⁻⁵ M is √Kb, which leaves out C; 1.9 × 10⁻⁴ M takes √3.6 without first making the exponent even.","strike":["3.6 × 10⁻⁹ M","9.5 × 10⁻⁵ M","1.9 × 10⁻⁴ M"]},{"text":"Answer: 6.0 × 10⁻⁵ M."}],"trap":"Halving an odd exponent as if it were even: rewrite 3.6 × 10⁻⁹ as 36 × 10⁻¹⁰ first."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words and numbers**.","marks":[{"t":"weak base","note":"only a little reacts"},{"t":"Kb = 9.0 × 10⁻⁹","note":"base constant"},{"t":"0.40 M","note":"starting concentration C"},{"t":"[OH⁻]","note":"x, the amount that reacts"}]},{"text":"**Write Kb with x.** Each base that reacts makes one BH⁺ and one OH⁻, and the base left is still about C.","calc":["Kb = x·x / (C − x) ≈ x² / C"]},{"text":"**Solve for x.**","calc":["x = √(Kb · C) = √(3.6 × 10⁻⁹)"]},{"text":"**Estimate the root of the power of ten.** Shift one factor of 10 so the exponent is even, then halve it; the coefficient's root is a nearby perfect square.","calc":["3.6 × 10⁻⁹ = 36 × 10⁻¹⁰","√36 × 10⁻⁵ = 6.0 × 10⁻⁵ M"]},{"text":"**Also:** e.g. Kb = 2.5 × 10⁻⁵, C = 0.10 M: x = √(2.5 × 10⁻⁶) ≈ 1.6 × 10⁻³ M (√2.5 ≈ 1.6, √10⁻⁶ = 10⁻³)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for a weak base, [OH⁻] = [BH⁺] ≈ √(Kb·C); make the power of ten even, then take half of it."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-19a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:19#1","pe4-cp:19#2","pe4-cp:47#1","pe5-cp:3#1","pe5-cp:3#2"],"q":"Four sealed flasks each hold a different gas above a small volume of water. After shaking, in which flask would the gas pressure fall the least?","choices":["Hydrogen chloride","Sulfur dioxide","Methylamine","Ethane"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"HCl is a strong acid that ionizes in water, so it dissolves heavily and its pressure drops a lot."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"SO₂ is a polar gas that dissolves readily, giving sulfurous acid, so its pressure drops."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Methylamine is a base that H-bonds to water: very soluble, big pressure drop."}],"rule":"Polar, acidic, basic or H-bonding gases dissolve in water and lose pressure; a nonpolar hydrocarbon barely dissolves.","exp":"**Like dissolves like.** HCl, SO₂ and CH₃NH₂ are polar (and HCl and CH₃NH₂ also react with or hydrogen-bond to water), so they dissolve readily and leave the gas phase, lowering the pressure. Ethane is nonpolar, barely dissolves, and its pressure stays nearly the same.","how":{"ask":"Find the gas that remains a gas above the water: the one least soluble in it.","know":"• **Like dissolves like**: polar or hydrogen-bonding solutes dissolve in water; nonpolar ones barely do.\n• Gas pressure in a sealed flask falls only if gas molecules leave the gas phase, for example by dissolving.\n• Hydrocarbons (C–H and C–C bonds only) are nonpolar.","steps":[{"text":"The pressure falls only when gas dissolves, so find the least water-soluble gas.","hl":"gas pressure fall the least"},{"text":"Classify each gas by polarity.","calc":["HCl: polar, ionizes in water","SO₂: bent, polar","CH₃NH₂: polar, N–H hydrogen bonds","C₂H₆: only C–H and C–C bonds, nonpolar"]},{"text":"The polar gases dissolve readily, so their pressures drop sharply.","strike":["Hydrogen chloride","Sulfur dioxide","Methylamine"]},{"text":"Answer: Ethane."}],"trap":"Thinking only gases that ionize dissolve well: a polar gas like SO₂ dissolves readily, while a nonpolar gas barely dissolves."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"sealed flasks","note":"gas can only leave by dissolving"},{"t":"small volume of water","note":"a polar solvent"},{"t":"fall the least","note":"find the least soluble gas"}]},{"text":"**Like dissolves like.** Water is polar and hydrogen-bonds, so it holds polar and hydrogen-bonding molecules and excludes nonpolar ones."},{"text":"**Link solubility to pressure.** Dissolved gas no longer pushes on the walls; a nonpolar gas remains above the water, so its pressure (and the container) is unchanged."},{"text":"**Also:** for saturated fatty acids, each has one polar –COOH head and a nonpolar tail. The **shortest chain** is the least hydrophobic, so it dissolves best of the three.","calc":["butanoic acid (4 C) > octanoic acid (8 C) > stearic acid (18 C)"]},{"text":"**Also:** the same rule ranks analogs of a drug. Replacing a ring C–H with N (benzene ring → pyridine ring), or adding an O or N that water can H-bond to, raises solubility. Adding CH₃ groups, or trading a polar group for C–H or C–Cl, lowers it.","fig":"pyridine","calc":["ring CH → N: a lone pair for water to H-bond → more soluble","–OH → –OCH₃, ring H → –CH₃ or –Cl: more nonpolar surface → less soluble"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** like dissolves like: the more nonpolar the molecule (a nonpolar gas, a longer hydrocarbon chain), the less it dissolves in water; a ring CH → N or an added O/N raises water solubility, added CH₃ lowers it."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-21a","s":"Reading Data","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe4-cp:21#1","pe4-cp:21#2","pe4-cp:21#3","pe4-cp:23#1","pe4-cp:23#2","pe4-cp:24#1","pe4-cp:24#2"],"q":"Drug Q's lethal dose is 60 μM alone and 6 μM with compound R in leukemia cells; in healthy cells it is 50 μM alone and 48 μM with R. Which conclusion do the data best support?","choices":["R sensitizes only the leukemia cells.","R sensitizes healthy and leukemia cells alike.","R is toxic to leukemia cells on its own.","Q has no effect unless R is present."],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"In healthy cells the lethal dose barely moves with R (50 vs 48 μM), so R does not act on both alike."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Untested: no condition gives R alone, so the data can't show R's own toxicity."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Q alone kills leukemia cells at 60 μM, so Q works without R."}],"rule":"Compare each cell type with and without the add-in: a big drop in lethal dose is sensitization. Reject claims about conditions never tested.","exp":"Compare each cell type with and without R. Leukemia cells need **10-fold less** Q once R is added (60 ÷ 6), while healthy cells barely shift (50 ÷ 48 ≈ 1.04). A dose that falls for one cell type only means R is a **selective** sensitizer. R was never tested by itself, and Q kills leukemia cells alone at 60 μM.","how":{"ask":"For each cell type, how much does the dose of Q drop when R is added, and does it drop for both types or one?","know":"• A **sensitizer** lowers the dose of a drug needed for the same effect.\n• Compare **with vs without** the sensitizer within each cell type, then compare the cell types.\n• **Selective** = the dose falls for the target (cancer) cells but not for normal cells.\n• **Enhancement = dose alone ÷ dose with the sensitizer** (a fold change, not a difference).\n• Reject any claim about a condition the data never tested.","steps":[{"text":"Lay out the four doses as a small table: two cell types, each without and with R.","hl":"60 μM alone and 6 μM with compound R in leukemia cells","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Lethal dose of drug Q, without and with compound R","x":{"label":"Cell type"},"y":{"label":"Lethal dose of Q (μM)","min":0,"max":70,"step":10},"categories":["Leukemia","Healthy"],"series":[{"name":"Q alone","values":[60,50]},{"name":"Q + R","values":[6,48]}]}},{"text":"Find the fold change for each cell type: dose alone ÷ dose with R.","calc":["leukemia: 60 μM ÷ 6 μM = **10-fold**","healthy: 50 μM ÷ 48 μM ≈ **1.04-fold** (essentially no change)"]},{"text":"R helps Q only in the leukemia cells, so it does not act on both types equally.","strike":["R sensitizes healthy and leukemia cells alike."]},{"text":"No row gives R by itself, so its own toxicity is untested; and Q already kills leukemia cells at 60 μM without R.","strike":["R is toxic to leukemia cells on its own.","Q has no effect unless R is present."]},{"text":"Answer: R sensitizes only the leukemia cells."}],"trap":"Reading a claim the data never tested (R alone) as supported, or calling R a general sensitizer because some number moved slightly."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"60 μM alone","note":"leukemia, no R"},{"t":"6 μM with compound R","note":"leukemia, with R"},{"t":"50 μM alone","note":"healthy, no R"},{"t":"48 μM with R","note":"healthy, with R"}]},{"text":"**Compare within each cell type first.** Does adding R lower the dose?","calc":["leukemia: 60 → 6 μM (large drop)","healthy: 50 → 48 μM (tiny drop)"]},{"text":"**Selective means one type only.** The dose falls for the cancer cells and stays put for the normal cells, so the effect is selective."},{"text":"**Test only what was measured.** No condition gives R alone, so a claim about R killing cells by itself is unsupported either way."},{"text":"**Also:** enhancement is a **ratio**, dose alone ÷ dose with the sensitizer, never the difference. Compute it for every line and pick the largest.","calc":["line X: 90 → 30 μM: 90 ÷ 30 = 3-fold (drop 60 μM)","line Y: 8 → 0.5 μM: 8 ÷ 0.5 = 16-fold (drop 7.5 μM)","line Y is enhanced most, despite the smaller drop"]},{"text":"**Also:** order matters when a sensitizer blocks a pathway. Give the sensitizer **first**, long enough for it to act, then give the drug that works best once that pathway is blocked; giving the drug first or both together lets the pathway run while the drug is present."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** compare dose alone vs with the sensitizer in each cell type as a fold change (alone ÷ with); selective means only the cancer cells' dose falls, and claims about untested conditions are rejected."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-22a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:22#1","pe4-cp:22#2","pe4-cp:42#1","pe4-cp:42#2","pe5-cp:1#2","pe5-cp:1#3"],"q":"A blood test reports a peptide hormone at 640 nM, but the lab's software accepts only micromolar units. Which value should be entered?","choices":["0.064 μM","0.64 μM","64 μM","6.4 × 10⁵ μM"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"units","why":"Divided by 10,000; nano to micro is only a factor of 1000."},{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"Sanity: 64 μM would be 64,000 nM, 100 times the 640 nM given."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Direction: converting to a larger unit (μM) must give a smaller number than 640."}],"rule":"1 μM = 1000 nM, so nM → μM means divide by 1000: a bigger unit always gives a smaller number.","exp":"**nM = 10⁻⁹ M and μM = 10⁻⁶ M**, so 1 μM = 1000 nM. Going to the larger unit gives a smaller number: 640 nM ÷ 1000 = **0.64 μM**.","how":{"ask":"Write each prefix as a power of ten, then convert from nM to μM.","know":"• **Prefixes**: m = 10⁻³, μ = 10⁻⁶, n = 10⁻⁹, p = 10⁻¹².\n• 1 μM = 1000 nM, so **nM → μM: divide by 1000**.\n• **[H₃O⁺] = 10^−pH M**; **M → μM: × 10⁶**.","steps":[{"text":"Start from the given value and unit.","hl":"640 nM"},{"text":"Convert through molar.","calc":["640 nM = 640 × 10⁻⁹ M = 6.4 × 10⁻⁷ M","6.4 × 10⁻⁷ M ÷ 10⁻⁶ M/μM = **0.64 μM**"]},{"text":"Shortcut: nM → μM is ÷ 1000.","calc":["640 ÷ 1000 = 0.64"]},{"text":"6.4 × 10⁵ μM multiplies by 1000 instead of dividing; 64 μM divides by only 10; 0.064 μM divides by 10⁴.","strike":["6.4 × 10⁵ μM","64 μM","0.064 μM"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.64 μM."}],"trap":"Multiplying by 1000: a micromole is larger than a nanomole, so the number must get smaller."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words and numbers**.","marks":[{"t":"640 nM","note":"value in nanomolar"},{"t":"micromolar","note":"target unit, μM"}]},{"text":"**Write the prefixes as powers of ten.** n = 10⁻⁹ and μ = 10⁻⁶: they differ by 10³."},{"text":"**Bigger unit, smaller number.** nM → μM: divide by 1000.","calc":["640 nM ÷ 1000 = 0.64 μM"]},{"text":"**Also:** from pH to μM: [H₃O⁺] = 10^−pH M, then × 10⁶ for μM. At pH 6.3, [H₃O⁺] = 10⁻⁶·³ M ≈ 5 × 10⁻⁷ M = 0.5 μM.","calc":["[H₃O⁺] = 10^−pH M","5 × 10⁻⁷ M × 10⁶ μM/M = 0.5 μM"]},{"text":"**Also:** in a weighing problem, chain the prefixes. Going in, μM → M (× 10⁻⁶) and mL → L (× 10⁻³); coming out, grams → milligrams (× 1000).","calc":["25 μM, 40 mL, solute 300 g/mol","n = 25 × 10⁻⁶ mol/L × 40 × 10⁻³ L = 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ mol","mass = 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ mol × 300 g/mol = 3.0 × 10⁻⁴ g","3.0 × 10⁻⁴ g × 1000 mg/g = 0.30 mg"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** convert through powers of ten: nM → μM ÷ 1000, M → μM × 10⁶, and [H₃O⁺] = 10^−pH M."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-25a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:25#1","pe4-cp:25#2","pe4-cp:25#3","pe4-cp:37#1","pe4-cp:37#2","pe4-cp:37#3","pe4-cp:37#4"],"q":"The molecule shown contains one benzene ring, one ketone C=O, one C=C outside the ring, and two C≡N groups. How many π bonds does it contain?","fig":"cpt-benzoyl-methylene-malononitrile","choices":["5","7","9","11"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"skipped","why":"Too few: the ring's 3 π plus the C=O and C=C already make 5 before any C≡N is counted."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"7 counts each C≡N as one π bond; a triple bond holds 2 π bonds."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Too many: a triple bond is 1 σ + 2 π, so 3 π per C≡N overcounts."}],"rule":"Count π bonds: each double bond 1, each triple bond 2, a benzene ring 3; then add them.","exp":"Any pair of bonded atoms shares exactly one **σ bond**; every extra bond joining that pair is a **π bond**. So the C=O gives 1 π, the C=C 1 π, each C≡N 2 π, and the benzene ring (three C=C) 3 π: **3 + 1 + 1 + 2 + 2 = 9**.","how":{"ask":"Give each multiple bond its π count, then add group by group.","know":"• **Single bond** = 1 σ, 0 π.\n• **Double bond** (C=O, C=C) = 1 σ + **1 π**.\n• **Triple bond** (C≡N, C≡C) = 1 σ + **2 π**.\n• **Benzene ring** = three C=C = **3 π**.\n• More shared pairs pull atoms closer: a **double bond is shorter than a single bond**.","steps":[{"text":"List the groups with multiple bonds.","hl":"one benzene ring, one ketone C=O, one C=C outside the ring, and two C≡N groups"},{"text":"The ketone C=O is a double bond: one σ bond holds the atoms, and the second bond is π.","calc":["C=O = 1 σ + 1 π → 1 π"]},{"text":"Each C≡N is a triple bond: one σ and two π.","calc":["2 × C≡N = 2 × 2 π = 4 π"]},{"text":"The ring has three C=C (3 π), and the extra C=C has 1 π. Add everything.","calc":["3 (ring) + 1 (C=O) + 1 (C=C) + 4 (C≡N) = 9 π"]},{"text":"Counting each C≡N as only 1 π gives 7; also counting the ring as a single π gives 5; counting all three lines of each triple bond as π gives 11.","calc":["3 + 1 + 1 + 2 = 7","1 + 1 + 1 + 2 = 5","3 + 1 + 1 + 6 = 11"],"strike":["7","5","11"]},{"text":"**Also:** the π bond adds a second shared pair, so C=O (about 120 pm) is shorter than a C–O single bond (about 140 pm)."},{"text":"Answer: 9."}],"trap":"Counting a triple bond as one π bond: only its first bond is σ, so the other two are both π."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"one benzene ring","note":"three C=C"},{"t":"one ketone C=O","note":"a double bond"},{"t":"two C≡N groups","note":"two triple bonds"},{"t":"π bonds","note":"count only the extra bonds"}]},{"text":"**σ first, then π.** Between two atoms the first bond is always σ (head-on overlap); every extra bond is π (side-by-side p overlap).","calc":["double = 1 σ + 1 π","triple = 1 σ + 2 π"]},{"text":"**A carbonyl** C=O is one σ plus one π, not two σ bonds and not a lone π bond."},{"text":"**Count by group.** Benzene ring 3 π; each C=O or C=C 1 π; each C≡N or C≡C 2 π. Then add.","calc":["ring 3 + C=O 1 + C=C 1 + C≡N 2 + C≡N 2 = 9"]},{"text":"**Also:** more bonds between the same two atoms means a shorter, stronger link: triple < double < single in length."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** count one π per double bond and two per triple bond; a benzene ring has three; add them group by group, and remember a double bond is shorter than a single one."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-26a","s":"Atoms & Periodic Trends","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:26#1","pe4-cp:26#2"],"q":"Four neutral atoms have the ground-state configurations 1s²2s²2p⁶, [Ne]3s²3p⁵, [Ne]3s² and [Ar]4s¹. Which element releases the most energy on accepting an electron?","choices":["Neon","Chlorine","Magnesium","Potassium"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Full shell: an extra electron must start a new, higher shell, so neon releases essentially nothing."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Mg is a metal with a filled 3s²; metals tend to lose electrons, and a filled subshell resists another."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"K is an alkali metal that readily loses its 4s¹; it releases little energy on gaining one."}],"rule":"Electron affinity is largest for atoms one electron short of a full p subshell (group 17); noble gases and metals release little.","exp":"Chlorine **needs just one more electron to fill its p subshell** (3p⁵ → 3p⁶), so the added electron completes a noble-gas configuration: halogens have the most negative electron affinities. Neon's shell is full, and the metals magnesium and potassium tend to lose electrons, not gain them.","how":{"ask":"Find the atom whose configuration is completed, not disrupted, by one added electron.","know":"• **Electron affinity**: the energy change when a gaseous atom gains an electron; more negative = gained more readily.\n• **Halogens** (ns²np⁵) need one electron to fill the p subshell, so they have the most favorable electron affinities.\n• **Noble gases** must start a new shell; **metals** (few valence electrons) tend to lose electrons.","steps":[{"text":"Look at each valence configuration.","hl":"[Ne]3s²3p⁵","calc":["Cl: 3p⁵ + e⁻ → 3p⁶, filled"]},{"text":"Neon is already full: an added electron would go into a new, higher shell.","hl":"1s²2s²2p⁶","strike":["Neon"]},{"text":"Magnesium (3s², filled s) and potassium (4s¹) are metals that lose valence electrons to reach a noble-gas core.","strike":["Magnesium","Potassium"]},{"text":"Answer: Chlorine."}],"trap":"Choosing potassium because its 4s orbital has room for one more: metals give up electrons far more readily than they take them."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"3p⁵","note":"one short of a filled p subshell"},{"t":"1s²2s²2p⁶","note":"already full"},{"t":"releases the most energy","note":"most negative electron affinity"}]},{"text":"**Ask what the new electron completes.** An electron that fills a subshell (np⁵ → np⁶) is strongly held, so energy is released."},{"text":"**Rule out the full and the metallic.** A noble gas has no room in its valence shell; Group 1 and 2 metals have small electron affinities and form cations."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** halogens (np⁵) gain an electron most readily; noble gases and metals do not."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-27a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"physics","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe4-cp:27#1","pe4-cp:27#2","pe4-cp:27#3","pe4-cp:27#4","pe6-cp:54#1"],"q":"Compound Z's triple point is 0.40 atm and 15 °C. Its vapor, held at 0.10 atm, is cooled until frost forms at −10 °C. Which process makes the frost?","choices":["Deposition","Freezing","Condensation","Sublimation"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Sublimation is solid → gas, made on warming; this vapor is cooling into a solid, the reverse crossing."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Freezing starts from a liquid, and no liquid exists below the triple-point pressure (0.10 < 0.40 atm)."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Condensation makes a liquid, which cannot exist below the triple-point pressure of 0.40 atm."}],"rule":"Below the triple-point pressure there is no liquid, so gas and solid convert directly: gas to solid on cooling, solid to gas on warming.","exp":"At 0.10 atm, below the triple-point pressure, Z has no liquid phase, so cooling the vapor reaches only the **solid–gas line** (at −10 °C). There the gas turns straight into solid: **deposition**, the reverse of sublimation.","how":{"ask":"Which boundary does the cooling vapor cross below the triple-point pressure, and in which direction does it cross it?","know":"• A **phase diagram** plots pressure (up) against temperature (across). Solid sits at low T and high P, gas at high T and low P, liquid in between.\n• Each line is where two phases coexist, crossed one way or the other: **solid–gas = sublimation (solid → gas) / deposition (gas → solid)**, **solid–liquid = melting / freezing**, **liquid–gas = vaporization / condensation**.\n• The **triple point** is where all three lines meet; the **critical point** ends the liquid–gas line.","steps":[{"text":"Compare the path with the triple point.","hl":"held at 0.10 atm","calc":["0.10 atm < 0.40 atm (below the triple-point pressure)","−10 °C < 15 °C (colder than the triple point)"]},{"text":"Below the triple-point pressure, liquid cannot exist, so the only border on this path is the solid–gas line, which the vapor reaches at −10 °C.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Phase diagram of compound Z (sketch)","x":{"label":"Temperature (°C)","min":-40,"max":100,"step":20},"y":{"label":"Pressure (atm)","min":0,"max":3.5,"step":0.5},"series":[{"name":"Solid–gas","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[-40,0.02],[-10,0.1],[15,0.4]]},{"name":"Solid–liquid","marker":"square","line":"straight","points":[[15,0.4],[18,1.8],[21,3.2]]},{"name":"Liquid–gas","marker":"triangle","line":"smooth","points":[[15,0.4],[50,1.2],[80,2.4],[100,3.2]]}]}},{"text":"Freezing and condensation both involve a liquid, which has no region below 0.40 atm.","strike":["Freezing","Condensation"]},{"text":"The vapor is cooling, so it crosses the solid–gas line from the gas side to the solid side. Sublimation (solid → gas) is the reverse crossing, made on warming.","hl":"frost forms at −10 °C","strike":["Sublimation"]},{"text":"Answer: deposition (gas → solid)."}],"trap":"Naming the right line but the wrong direction: sublimation and deposition share the solid–gas line, and vapor cooling into frost is deposition."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"triple point","note":"where all three lines meet"},{"t":"0.40 atm and 15 °C","note":"triple-point P and T"},{"t":"held at 0.10 atm","note":"the path's pressure"},{"t":"frost forms at −10 °C","note":"solid appears from the gas"}]},{"text":"**Read the map:** up = more pressure, right = hotter. Cold and squeezed = solid; hot and low pressure = gas; liquid is the wedge in between.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Phase diagram of compound Z (sketch)","x":{"label":"Temperature (°C)","min":-40,"max":100,"step":20},"y":{"label":"Pressure (atm)","min":0,"max":3.5,"step":0.5},"series":[{"name":"Solid–gas","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[-40,0.02],[-10,0.1],[15,0.4]]},{"name":"Solid–liquid","marker":"square","line":"straight","points":[[15,0.4],[18,1.8],[21,3.2]]},{"name":"Liquid–gas","marker":"triangle","line":"smooth","points":[[15,0.4],[50,1.2],[80,2.4],[100,3.2]]}]}},{"text":"**Place the path:** cooling at a fixed 0.10 atm moves left along a horizontal line from the gas region into the solid region, crossing only the solid–gas line.","calc":["0.10 < 0.40 atm → no liquid region on this path","gas → solid at −10 °C: deposition"]},{"text":"**Also:** warming the solid back along the same line reverses the crossing: solid → gas with no liquid stage, which is **sublimation**."},{"text":"**Also:** a point on the solid–liquid line (nearly vertical, above the triple point) is **melting** or freezing; a point on the liquid–gas line is **boiling** (vaporization) or condensation, and at 1 atm it gives the normal boiling point."},{"text":"**Also:** two points are special. The **triple point** is the one T and P where solid, liquid and gas all coexist. The **critical point** ends the liquid–gas line; beyond it liquid and gas merge into one supercritical fluid."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** find which line the path crosses and in which direction: solid–gas = sublimation (warming) or deposition (cooling), solid–liquid = melting or freezing, liquid–gas = vaporization or condensation."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-28a","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe4-cp:28#1"],"q":"Electron levels: E₁ = −12.0 eV, E₂ = −7.0 eV, E₃ = −5.5 eV, E₄ = −5.0 eV. Which downward jump emits the longest-wavelength photon?","choices":["E₄ → E₃","E₄ → E₁","E₂ → E₁","E₃ → E₂"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Largest gap (7.0 eV) means the shortest λ, the opposite of what's asked."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Drops all the way to E₁ have big gaps (5.0 eV here): high energy, short λ."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"E₃ → E₂ spans 1.5 eV; another listed jump has a smaller gap, so its photon is longer than this one."}],"rule":"Longest wavelength = smallest energy gap (λ = hc/ΔE): find the two closest levels.","exp":"**A photon carries the energy gap, and λ = hc/ΔE**, so the smallest gap gives the longest wavelength. The gaps are 0.5 eV (E₄ → E₃), 1.5 eV (E₃ → E₂), 5.0 eV (E₂ → E₁) and 7.0 eV (E₄ → E₁); the 0.5 eV drop emits light near 1240 ÷ 0.5 ≈ 2500 nm, the longest.","how":{"ask":"Find the energy gap of each transition and pick the smallest one.","know":"• An emitted photon's energy equals the gap: **ΔE = hc/λ**, so **λ = hc/ΔE**.\n• Wavelength is inversely proportional to the gap: **smallest ΔE → longest λ**.\n• Shortcut: λ(nm) ≈ 1240/ΔE(eV).","steps":[{"text":"List the levels.","hl":"E₁ = −12.0 eV, E₂ = −7.0 eV, E₃ = −5.5 eV, E₄ = −5.0 eV"},{"text":"Compute each choice's gap.","calc":["E₄ → E₃: −5.0 − (−5.5) = **0.5 eV**","E₃ → E₂: −5.5 − (−7.0) = 1.5 eV","E₂ → E₁: −7.0 − (−12.0) = 5.0 eV","E₄ → E₁: −5.0 − (−12.0) = 7.0 eV"]},{"text":"λ = hc/ΔE, so convert the smallest and largest gaps to wavelengths.","calc":["0.5 eV: λ ≈ 1240 ÷ 0.5 ≈ 2500 nm","7.0 eV: λ ≈ 1240 ÷ 7.0 ≈ 177 nm"]},{"text":"The three larger gaps all give shorter wavelengths; E₄ → E₁, the largest gap, gives the shortest.","strike":["E₄ → E₁","E₂ → E₁","E₃ → E₂"]},{"text":"Answer: E₄ → E₃."}],"trap":"Picking the biggest drop: a large gap means a high-energy photon, which has the shortest wavelength, not the longest."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"E₁ = −12.0 eV","note":"lowest level"},{"t":"E₄ = −5.0 eV","note":"highest level"},{"t":"longest-wavelength photon","note":"smallest gap"}]},{"text":"**The photon carries the gap.** A falling electron emits one photon with ΔE = E(upper) − E(lower).","calc":["E₄ → E₃: −5.0 − (−5.5) = 0.5 eV"]},{"text":"**Flip to wavelength:** λ = hc/ΔE, so wavelength and gap are inversely related.","calc":["λ(nm) ≈ 1240 ÷ ΔE(eV)","1.5 eV → ≈ 830 nm; 0.5 eV → ≈ 2500 nm"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** λ = hc/ΔE, so the transition with the smallest energy gap emits the longest wavelength, and the largest gap the shortest."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-29a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:29#1","pe4-cp:29#2","pe4-cp:29#3"],"q":"In CH₃CH₂–NH–CH₂CH₂–C(=O)–OCH₃, the carbonyl carbon and the two oxygens bonded to it are highlighted. Which functional group do these atoms form?","fig":"cpt-fix-amino-ester-co-hl","choices":["Ketone","Ester","Amide","Carboxylic acid"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"A ketone's C=O carbon has two carbon neighbors; this one also carries an O."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"An amide needs N bonded to the C=O carbon; here the N is two carbons away."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"An acid needs O–H on the C=O carbon; this O carries a CH₃, not an H."}],"rule":"Classify by what the C=O carbon is bonded to: two C = ketone, N = amide, O–H = carboxylic acid, O–C = another group.","exp":"Classify a carbonyl by the atoms on the C=O carbon. Here it carries a carbon (the chain CH₂) and an **–O–CH₃**, an O bonded to another carbon: that is an **ester**. The nitrogen is two carbons away, so it is not an amide; an acid would need –OH, and a ketone would need two carbons.","how":{"ask":"List the atoms bonded to the C=O carbon, then name the group they make.","know":"• Classify a **carbonyl** by its two neighbors: C and C → **ketone**; H → **aldehyde**; OH → **carboxylic acid**; OR → **ester**; N → **amide**.\n• Classify an **N** by its direct neighbors: only C and H → **amine** (1°/2°/3° by its carbons); a C=O carbon on N → amide.\n• Only atoms bonded directly to the atom you are classifying count.","steps":[{"text":"Find the C=O carbon and its neighbors.","hl":"C(=O)–OCH₃","calc":["C=O carbon neighbors: CH₂ (chain), =O, –O–CH₃"]},{"text":"The N is not bonded to the C=O carbon, so this is not an amide; the C=O carbon has an O neighbor, so it is not a ketone.","strike":["Amide","Ketone"]},{"text":"That O carries a CH₃, not an H: an ester, not a carboxylic acid.","calc":["–O–CH₃ → ester; –O–H → acid"],"strike":["Carboxylic acid"]},{"text":"Answer: Ester."}],"trap":"Calling it an amide because the molecule has an N: the N must be bonded to the C=O carbon itself."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"C(=O)–OCH₃","note":"the carbonyl and its O neighbor"},{"t":"–NH–","note":"an N, but two carbons away"},{"t":"Which functional group","note":"classify by neighbors"}]},{"text":"**Carbonyl groups by neighbor.** C,C ketone; H aldehyde; OH acid; OR ester; N amide."},{"text":"**Also:** classify the nitrogen the same way, by its direct neighbors: two carbons and one H, none a C=O carbon, make it a 2° amine (CH₃–NH–CH₃ is another). One carbon: 1°; three: 3°."},{"text":"**Also:** a carbamate needs N–C(=O)–O on one carbon, as in CH₃NHC(=O)OCH₃; an imine has C=N."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** name a group by the atoms bonded directly to its key atom: for C=O, its two neighbors (C, H, OH, OR, N); for N, whether a C=O carbon is on it and how many carbons."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-30a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:30#1","pe4-cp:30#2"],"q":"Methyl 4-oxopentanoate, CH₃CO–CH₂CH₂–CO₂CH₃, shown, is treated with NaBH₄ and then warmed with a trace of acid. Which organic product is isolated?","fig":"cpt-methyl-4-oxopentanoate","choices":["γ-Valerolactone","δ-Valerolactone","Pentane-1,4-diol","Methyl 4-hydroxypentanoate"],"choiceFigs":["cpt-gamma-valerolactone","cpt-delta-valerolactone","cpt-pentane-14-diol","cpt-methyl-4-hydroxypentanoate"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"Warm acid lets a γ-OH close onto the ester, so the open hydroxy ester doesn't survive."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"A δ-lactone would need the OH one carbon farther out, on C5; here the new OH is on C4."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"NaBH₄ reduces ketones but not esters; a diol would need LiAlH₄."}],"rule":"NaBH₄ reduces ketones, not esters; a hydroxy ester with the OH γ or δ to the C=O then closes to a ring on warming with acid.","exp":"NaBH₄ is a mild hydride donor: it reduces the **ketone** at C4 to a secondary alcohol but leaves the **ester** alone. The new OH sits on the γ-carbon of the ester, so it attacks the ester carbonyl in the same molecule, expels **methanol** and closes a ring of C1–C4 plus the O: five atoms, **γ-valerolactone**.","how":{"ask":"Decide which carbonyl NaBH₄ reduces, then count the ring formed when the new OH attacks the ester.","know":"• **NaBH₄** reduces aldehydes and ketones to alcohols; it does **not** reduce esters (that needs LiAlH₄).\n• A **γ-hydroxy ester** (OH on the γ-carbon, C4, two carbons past the α-carbon) cyclizes to a **five-membered lactone**: count the ring as the carbons from C1 to the OH carbon, plus the O. The ester's alcohol part (here methanol) leaves.","steps":[{"text":"Number from the ester carbon: C1 is the CO₂CH₃ carbon, C4 the ketone carbon.","hl":"CH₃CO–CH₂CH₂–CO₂CH₃","calc":["C5H₃–C4(=O)–C3H₂–C2H₂–C1(=O)OCH₃"]},{"text":"NaBH₄ reduces only the ketone, so the ester survives. A diol would need the ester reduced too, which takes LiAlH₄.","hl":"NaBH₄","calc":["C4=O → C4H–OH"],"strike":["Pentane-1,4-diol"]},{"text":"The C4 OH is γ to the ester carbonyl. With acid and warmth it attacks C1 and methanol leaves, so the open hydroxy ester does not survive.","hl":"warmed with a trace of acid","strike":["Methyl 4-hydroxypentanoate"]},{"text":"Count the ring atoms: C1, C2, C3, C4 and the O.","calc":["ring = 4 C + 1 O = 5 atoms → γ-lactone"]},{"text":"A δ-lactone would need the OH one carbon farther out, on C5.","strike":["δ-Valerolactone"]},{"text":"Answer: γ-Valerolactone."}],"trap":"Letting NaBH₄ reduce the ester as well: it is too mild, so the ester stays to be attacked by the new OH."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"4-oxopentanoate","note":"ketone at C4 of an ester"},{"t":"NaBH₄","note":"reduces the ketone only"},{"t":"warmed with a trace of acid","note":"ring closure"}]},{"text":"**Step 1: what does the reducing agent touch?** NaBH₄ turns a C=O of an aldehyde or ketone into C–OH and leaves esters, acids and amides unchanged.","calc":["R₂C=O → R₂CH–OH; RCO₂R′ unchanged"]},{"text":"**Step 2: find the hydroxy ester.** Locate the new OH relative to the ester carbon: on the γ-carbon (C4) or the δ-carbon (C5)."},{"text":"**Step 3: cyclize.** The OH attacks the ester C=O inside the molecule, the OR′ group leaves as R′OH, and a lactone forms. Ring size = carbons from C1 to the OH carbon, plus the O.","calc":["γ: 4 C + O = 5-membered"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** NaBH₄ reduces a ketone to an alcohol but leaves an ester; a γ- or δ-hydroxy ester then closes to a five- or six-membered lactone and releases the ester's alcohol."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-31a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:31#1"],"q":"A compound has two ethyl ester groups and one six-membered lactone ring. After heating with excess aqueous KOH, how many carboxylate groups does the product carry?","choices":["0","1","2","3"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"The two ethyl esters alone already give 2 carboxylates, so 1 is too few."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"Two counts only the ethyl esters; the lactone is a cyclic ester and opens to a carboxylate too."},{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Hot excess KOH saponifies esters into carboxylates, so the count can't be zero."}],"rule":"Hot excess hydroxide opens every ester, cyclic (lactone) or not: count each ester group as one carboxylate.","exp":"Hot excess hydroxide **saponifies** every ester. Each ethyl ester becomes a carboxylate and releases ethanol, and the lactone, being a cyclic ester, opens to a carboxylate with an alcohol (–OH) on the other end. That makes **2 + 1 = 3** carboxylate groups.","how":{"ask":"Count every ester linkage, the lactone included, because each one becomes a carboxylate in excess base.","know":"• **Saponification**: RCO₂R′ + OH⁻ → RCO₂⁻ + R′OH, irreversible because the carboxylate is deprotonated.\n• A **lactone is a cyclic ester**: hydroxide opens it to a carboxylate plus an alcohol on the same chain.\n• **Excess** base means every ester group reacts.","steps":[{"text":"Excess hydroxide with heat hydrolyzes every ester group present, so the answer is not zero.","hl":"heating with excess aqueous KOH","strike":["0"]},{"text":"Each ethyl ester gives a carboxylate and ethanol.","calc":["2 × (RCO₂Et + OH⁻ → RCO₂⁻ + EtOH) = 2 carboxylates"]},{"text":"The lactone is also an ester, just in a ring: it opens to one carboxylate and one alcohol on the same chain.","hl":"one six-membered lactone ring","calc":["lactone + OH⁻ → HO–(CH₂)…–CO₂⁻ = 1 carboxylate"]},{"text":"Add them. Stopping at 1 treats only the ring as reactive; stopping at 2 forgets that the lactone is an ester.","calc":["2 + 1 = 3"],"strike":["1","2"]},{"text":"Answer: 3."}],"trap":"Leaving the lactone intact: it looks like a ring, not an ester, but it is an ester and hydroxide opens it just as easily."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"two ethyl ester groups","note":"two esters"},{"t":"lactone","note":"a cyclic ester"},{"t":"excess aqueous KOH","note":"saponification of all"}]},{"text":"**Saponification**: hydroxide adds to the ester C=O, the alkoxide leaves, and the acid is deprotonated to a carboxylate, so the reaction does not reverse.","calc":["RCO₂R′ + OH⁻ → RCO₂⁻ + R′OH"]},{"text":"**Lactones count.** A lactone is an ester whose alcohol part is on the same chain; opening it gives a hydroxy carboxylate (one carboxylate per lactone)."},{"text":"**Count.** With excess base, carboxylates = number of ester groups + number of lactone rings.","calc":["2 + 1 = 3"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** excess hydroxide hydrolyzes every ester and every lactone, each one ending as a carboxylate plus its alcohol."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-32a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:32#1","pe4-cp:32#2"],"q":"Piperidine hydrochloride is stirred with aqueous NaOH before benzyl bromide is added for an SN2 reaction. Why is the base needed?","choices":["It removes H⁺ from the ammonium ion","It makes benzyl bromide more electrophilic","It turns piperidine into an amide anion","It converts bromide into a nucleophile"],"choiceFigs":["cpt-piperidine","cpt-benzyl-bromide","cpt-piperidide","cpt-bromide"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Roles flipped: bromide is the leaving group in this SN2, not the nucleophile."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"A base removes protons; it does nothing to make the benzylic carbon more δ+."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"An amide anion needs a base far stronger than hydroxide, since a neutral N–H has a pKa near 36."}],"rule":"An amine salt (R₂NH₂⁺) has no free lone pair to attack; hydroxide frees the neutral amine but can't make an N⁻ anion.","exp":"In the hydrochloride salt the nitrogen is a **piperidinium ion**: its lone pair already holds a proton, leaving nothing to attack carbon with. NaOH takes that H⁺ off, giving **neutral piperidine**, whose free lone pair attacks the benzylic carbon and displaces bromide. Hydroxide is far too weak to remove a second H from N.","how":{"ask":"Ask which form of the amine has a lone pair free to attack the C–Br carbon.","know":"• An **ammonium salt** (R₂NH₂⁺ Cl⁻) has **no lone pair** on N, so it is **not a nucleophile**.\n• A base stronger than the amine removes H⁺ and frees the **neutral amine**, whose lone pair is nucleophilic.\n• Removing H⁺ from a neutral amine (pKa about 36) needs a far stronger base than hydroxide.","steps":[{"text":"The starting material is a salt: the N carries two H's and a positive charge, with no lone pair.","hl":"Piperidine hydrochloride","calc":["R₂NH₂⁺ Cl⁻: 4 bonds on N, 0 lone pairs"]},{"text":"Hydroxide removes one H⁺, freeing the amine.","hl":"aqueous NaOH","calc":["R₂NH₂⁺ + OH⁻ → R₂NH + H₂O"]},{"text":"Only the neutral amine has a lone pair, so only it can attack the benzylic carbon and push out bromide.","calc":["R₂NH + PhCH₂Br → R₂N⁺H–CH₂Ph Br⁻"]},{"text":"The base does not touch the electrophile, and bromide is the leaving group, not the nucleophile.","strike":["It makes benzyl bromide more electrophilic","It converts bromide into a nucleophile"]},{"text":"An amide anion would need a base far stronger than hydroxide, since a neutral N–H has a pKa near 36.","strike":["It turns piperidine into an amide anion"]},{"text":"Answer: It removes H⁺ from the ammonium ion."}],"trap":"Thinking the salt is already a nucleophile because it contains nitrogen: until the H⁺ is removed, the nitrogen has no lone pair to donate."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Piperidine hydrochloride","note":"an ammonium salt"},{"t":"aqueous NaOH","note":"base"},{"t":"SN2 reaction","note":"needs a nucleophile with a lone pair"}]},{"text":"**Salt vs free amine.** An amine \"hydrochloride\" is R₂NH₂⁺ Cl⁻: the lone pair on N has been used to bond H⁺.","calc":["R₂NH + HCl → R₂NH₂⁺ Cl⁻"]},{"text":"**The base frees it.** A base whose conjugate acid is weaker than the ammonium ion takes the H⁺ back.","calc":["R₂NH₂⁺ + OH⁻ → R₂NH + H₂O"]},{"text":"**Only the free amine is a nucleophile.** Its lone pair attacks the carbon bearing the leaving group in the SN2 step."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a base removes H⁺ from an ammonium salt to free the amine, and only that free amine, with its lone pair, can act as the nucleophile."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-33a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:33#1","pe4-cp:33#2"],"q":"An amine protected as an ethyl carbamate, R–NH–CO₂Et, goes through four steps: (I) NaBH₄, EtOH, 0 °C; (II) TsCl, pyridine; (III) NaCN, DMSO; (IV) KOH, H₂O, reflux. In which step is the carbamate cleaved?","choices":["Step I","Step II","Step III","Step IV"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"NaBH₄ at 0 °C reduces aldehydes and ketones only; it can't touch a carbamate."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"TsCl in pyridine just turns an OH into a tosylate; no water, so no hydrolysis."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"NaCN in dry DMSO only displaces the tosylate by SN2; with no water present, nothing is hydrolyzed."}],"rule":"A carbamate is cleaved by hydrolysis: look for water plus strong base or acid and heat. Mild hydride, TsCl and SN2 steps leave it alone.","exp":"A carbamate is a stable amide-like ester: breaking it means **hydrolysis**, which needs **strong aqueous base or acid and heat**. Only the KOH/water/reflux step supplies all three, so that is where the carbamate is cut. The other steps are a mild reduction, a tosylation and an SN2 substitution.","how":{"ask":"Find the step that has water, a strong acid or base, and heat: that is what carbamate hydrolysis needs.","know":"• A **carbamate** (R₂N–CO–OR′) is cleaved by **hydrolysis**: strong base (NaOH, KOH) or strong acid (HCl) in **water**, with **heat** (reflux).\n• Products: amine + CO₂ + R′OH.\n• Mild reagents (NaBH₄), dry conditions or room temperature leave it intact.","steps":[{"text":"Look for the hydrolysis conditions in each step.","hl":"(IV) KOH, H₂O, reflux"},{"text":"Step I: NaBH₄ is a mild hydride donor at 0 °C; it does not reduce carbamates, esters or amides.","hl":"(I) NaBH₄, EtOH, 0 °C","strike":["Step I"]},{"text":"Step II: TsCl in pyridine converts an OH into a tosylate. There is no water and no strong base, so nothing is hydrolyzed.","strike":["Step II"]},{"text":"Step III: cyanide displaces the tosylate by SN2 in dry DMSO; with no water there is nothing to hydrolyze it.","strike":["Step III"]},{"text":"Step IV has strong base, water and heat: the carbamate is hydrolyzed.","calc":["R–NH–CO₂Et + KOH/H₂O, Δ → R–NH₂ + CO₃²⁻ + EtOH"]},{"text":"Answer: Step IV."}],"trap":"Choosing the reduction step because NaBH₄ attacks C=O groups: it reduces a ketone or aldehyde C=O but leaves a carbamate untouched."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"ethyl carbamate","note":"amide + ester on one C=O"},{"t":"KOH, H₂O, reflux","note":"strong base, water, heat"},{"t":"cleaved","note":"hydrolysis"}]},{"text":"**What breaks a carbamate?** Like an amide, it is resonance-stabilized and slow to react, so it needs **hydrolysis under forcing conditions**: strong acid or base, water and heat.","calc":["R₂N–CO₂R′ + H₂O (acid or base, Δ) → R₂NH + CO₂ + R′OH"]},{"text":"**Scan each step for all three**: water present? strong acid or base? heat? A step missing any one leaves the carbamate in place."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a carbamate is cleaved only by hydrolysis with strong base or acid and heat, so pick the step that supplies those conditions."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-34a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:34#1","pe4-cp:34#2","pe4-cp:34#3","pe4-cp:34#4","pe4-cp:34#5","pe6-cp:15#1","pe6-cp:15#2","pe6-cp:15#3","pe6-cp:15#4"],"q":"With an inhibitor added, an enzyme's Lineweaver–Burk plot keeps its y-intercept at 0.25 s/μM, but its x-intercept moves from −0.50 to −0.125 μM⁻¹. How did the inhibitor change KM and Vmax?","choices":["KM raised; Vmax same","Vmax lowered; KM same","KM lowered; Vmax lowered","Vmax lowered; KM shifted"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"The x-intercept (−1/KM) moved toward zero, so KM grew; lowering it reads the shift backwards."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Same y-intercept means same Vmax (1/Vmax did not move), so a lowered Vmax is ruled out."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"A lowered Vmax would raise the y-intercept above 0.25, and it stayed put."}],"rule":"Lineweaver–Burk: y-intercept = 1/Vmax, x-intercept = −1/KM. An intercept that moves toward zero means that value got larger.","exp":"On a double-reciprocal plot the **y-intercept is 1/Vmax** and the **x-intercept is −1/KM**. The y-intercept stays at 0.25, so **Vmax stays 4 μM/s**. The x-intercept moves toward zero, so **KM rises from 2 to 8 μM**. A larger apparent KM at the same Vmax is the signature of a competitive inhibitor: the lines cross on the y-axis.","how":{"ask":"Translate each intercept into Vmax or KM, then see which one changed and in which direction.","know":"• Lineweaver–Burk: 1/v = (KM/Vmax)(1/[S]) + 1/Vmax.\n• **y-intercept = 1/Vmax**; **x-intercept = −1/KM**; slope = KM/Vmax.\n• Competitive: Vmax same, KM up (lines cross on the y-axis).\n• Noncompetitive: Vmax down, KM same (lines cross on the x-axis).\n• Mixed: Vmax down and KM changed (lines cross off both axes).\n• Uncompetitive: Vmax and KM both fall by the same factor (parallel lines).","steps":[{"text":"The y-intercept gives Vmax; it did not move.","hl":"keeps its y-intercept at 0.25 s/μM","calc":["Vmax = 1 ÷ 0.25 s/μM = **4 μM/s** (with and without inhibitor)"]},{"text":"The x-intercept gives KM; it moved toward zero.","hl":"from −0.50 to −0.125 μM⁻¹","calc":["−1/KM = −0.50 μM⁻¹ → KM = **2 μM**","−1/KM = −0.125 μM⁻¹ → KM = **8 μM**"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Lineweaver–Burk plot with and without the inhibitor","x":{"label":"1/[S] (μM⁻¹)","min":-0.5,"max":1,"step":0.25},"y":{"label":"1/v (s/μM)","min":0,"max":2.5,"step":0.5},"series":[{"name":"No inhibitor","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[-0.5,0],[0,0.25],[0.5,0.5],[1,0.75]]},{"name":"+ Inhibitor","marker":"square","line":"straight","points":[[-0.125,0],[0,0.25],[0.5,1.25],[1,2.25]]}]}},{"text":"Vmax did not change, which rules out the noncompetitive and mixed patterns (both would raise the y-intercept).","strike":["Vmax lowered; KM same","Vmax lowered; KM shifted"]},{"text":"KM rose from 2 to 8 μM rather than fell; lowering KM and Vmax together (uncompetitive) would also lift the y-intercept, as a parallel line.","strike":["KM lowered; Vmax lowered"]},{"text":"Answer: KM raised, Vmax the same, the pattern of a competitive inhibitor."}],"trap":"Mixing up the axes: the y-intercept is 1/Vmax and the x-intercept is −1/KM, so an x-intercept closer to zero means a larger KM, not a smaller one."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and which intercept it belongs to.","marks":[{"t":"y-intercept at 0.25 s/μM","note":"1/Vmax"},{"t":"x-intercept","note":"−1/KM"},{"t":"−0.50 to −0.125","note":"moves toward zero"}]},{"text":"**Read the intercepts.** y-intercept = 1/Vmax; x-intercept = −1/KM.","calc":["Vmax = 1/0.25 = 4 μM/s, both lines","KM: 1/0.50 = 2 μM → 1/0.125 = 8 μM"]},{"text":"**Competitive:** the inhibitor competes for the active site, so enough substrate wins it back: Vmax is unchanged and KM rises. The lines meet on the y-axis."},{"text":"**Also:** noncompetitive inhibition lowers Vmax with KM unchanged: the y-intercept rises and the lines meet on the x-axis."},{"text":"**Also:** mixed inhibition lowers Vmax and changes KM: the lines meet away from both axes."},{"text":"**Also:** uncompetitive inhibition lowers both Vmax and KM by the same factor: the lines are parallel (same slope, higher intercepts)."},{"text":"**Also:** a table of KM and Vmax, with and without the inhibitor, names the type with no plot at all. Compare each column with the control: KM up and Vmax the same → competitive; Vmax down and KM the same → noncompetitive; both down by the same factor → uncompetitive; Vmax down and KM moved either way → mixed.","calc":["control: KM 3 mM, Vmax 60 μM/min","+ inhibitor: KM 3 mM, Vmax 20 μM/min","Vmax ÷ 3, KM unchanged → noncompetitive"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** on a Lineweaver–Burk plot, y-intercept = 1/Vmax and x-intercept = −1/KM; competitive keeps Vmax and raises KM, noncompetitive lowers Vmax only, mixed lowers Vmax and shifts KM, uncompetitive lowers both."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-35a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:35#1","pe4-cp:35#2","pe4-cp:35#3"],"q":"Lactate dehydrogenase uses NADH and pyruvate as its two substrates. Which finding would show that it follows an ordered sequential mechanism?","choices":["Pyruvate binds only to the enzyme–NADH complex","Either substrate can bind the free enzyme first","A product is released prior to pyruvate binding","Pyruvate binds the free enzyme tightly"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Either substrate binding first is the definition of random order, not ordered."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A product leaving before the second substrate binds is ping-pong, not sequential."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Pyruvate binding the free enzyme shows no required order; it fits random binding as well."}],"rule":"Ordered = the second substrate binds only after the first; random = either binds first; ping-pong = a product leaves before the second binds.","exp":"**Ordered sequential**: one substrate must bind first, and both are on the enzyme before any product forms. If pyruvate can bind only the enzyme–NADH complex, NADH is the obligatory first substrate. Either-first binding is **random**; a product leaving before the second substrate arrives is **ping-pong**; pyruvate binding the free enzyme doesn't show a required order; it is consistent with random binding or with pyruvate binding first.","how":{"ask":"What does ordered mean for which substrate can bind the free enzyme?","know":"• **Sequential** mechanisms: both substrates bind (a ternary complex) before any product leaves.\n• **Ordered**: one substrate must bind first; the second binds only to the enzyme–first-substrate complex.\n• **Random**: either substrate can bind first.\n• **Ping-pong**: the first substrate binds and a product leaves (leaving a modified enzyme) before the second substrate binds.","steps":[{"text":"Ordered means a required binding order: the second substrate cannot bind free enzyme.","hl":"ordered sequential mechanism"},{"text":"If pyruvate binds only once NADH is on, NADH must come first: that is the ordered pattern.","hl":"ordered sequential mechanism"},{"text":"Either substrate binding first describes a random mechanism.","strike":["Either substrate can bind the free enzyme first"]},{"text":"A product leaving before the second substrate binds describes ping-pong, not sequential.","strike":["A product is released prior to pyruvate binding"]},{"text":"Pyruvate binding the free enzyme doesn't show a required order; it is consistent with random binding or with pyruvate binding first.","strike":["Pyruvate binds the free enzyme tightly"]},{"text":"Answer: pyruvate binds only to the enzyme–NADH complex."}],"trap":"Thinking any two-step binding is ping-pong: ping-pong needs a product to leave between the two substrates."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"two substrates","note":"a bisubstrate enzyme"},{"t":"ordered sequential","note":"fixed binding order, no early product"}]},{"text":"**Ordered:** substrate 1 binds free enzyme; substrate 2 binds only the E·S1 complex; products leave after both are bound.","calc":["E + NADH → E·NADH","E·NADH + pyruvate → E·NADH·pyruvate → products"]},{"text":"**Also:** random sequential means either substrate can bind the free enzyme first; both still bind before any product leaves."},{"text":"**Also:** ping-pong (double displacement) means substrate 1 binds, a first product leaves and the enzyme is left modified, and only then does substrate 2 bind.","calc":["E + S1 → E′ + P1","E′ + S2 → E + P2"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** ordered = one substrate must bind first; random = either can bind first; ping-pong = a product leaves before the second substrate binds."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-38a","s":"Spectroscopy","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:38#1","pe4-cp:38#2","pe4-cp:39#1","pe4-cp:5#1","pe4-cp:5#2"],"q":"A dye solution in a 0.40 cm cell absorbs A = 0.63 at 430 nm. Its molar absorptivity is 2.1 × 10⁴ M⁻¹cm⁻¹ for 430 nm and 7.0 × 10³ M⁻¹cm⁻¹ for 550 nm. What is its concentration?","choices":["12 μM","30 μM","75 μM","225 μM"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"That multiplies by the path length instead of dividing by it; a cell under 1 cm must make c bigger than A/ε, not smaller."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"Forgot b: that is A/ε alone, as if the cell were 1 cm; divide by the 0.40 cm path too."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Uses the 550 nm ε; ε depends on wavelength, so always use the ε at the wavelength you measured."}],"rule":"c = A ÷ (εb), with ε at the measured wavelength; a cell shorter than 1 cm raises c. Round: 0.6 ÷ (2 × 10⁴ × 0.4), then × 10⁶ for μM.","exp":"**Beer's law, A = εbc, so c = A/(εb).** Use the ε at the measured wavelength (430 nm): c = 0.63 ÷ (2.1 × 10⁴ × 0.40) = 0.63 ÷ 8400 = 7.5 × 10⁻⁵ M = **75 μM**.","how":{"ask":"Rearrange Beer's law for c, using the ε that matches the wavelength of the reading.","know":"• **Beer's law: A = εbc** (ε in M⁻¹cm⁻¹, b = path length in cm, c in M), so **c = A/(εb)**.\n• ε depends on wavelength: use ε for the wavelength at which you measured A.\n• A blank subtracts what the beam passes through only if the reference and sample cells are identical except for the analyte.","steps":[{"text":"The reading was taken at 430 nm, so use ε at 430 nm and ignore the 550 nm value.","hl":"2.1 × 10⁴ M⁻¹cm⁻¹ for 430 nm"},{"text":"Rearrange Beer's law and substitute, keeping the path length.","hl":"0.40 cm cell","calc":["A = εbc → c = A ÷ (εb)","εb = 2.1 × 10⁴ M⁻¹cm⁻¹ × 0.40 cm = 8.4 × 10³ M⁻¹","c = 0.63 ÷ 8.4 × 10³ M⁻¹ = **7.5 × 10⁻⁵ M**","7.5 × 10⁻⁵ M × 10⁶ μM/M = **75 μM**"]},{"text":"225 μM uses the 550 nm absorptivity (0.63 ÷ 2800). 30 μM drops the path length (0.63 ÷ 2.1 × 10⁴). 12 μM multiplies by b instead of dividing.","strike":["225 μM","30 μM","12 μM"]},{"text":"If A were too high for the detector, dilute the sample or use a shorter cell: A = εbc falls in proportion to c or b.","calc":["halve c → A halves","halve b → A halves"]},{"text":"Answer: 75 μM."}],"trap":"Grabbing the other wavelength's ε, or forgetting to divide by a path length that is not 1 cm."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"0.40 cm cell","note":"path length b"},{"t":"A = 0.63 at 430 nm","note":"absorbance, read at 430 nm"},{"t":"2.1 × 10⁴ M⁻¹cm⁻¹ for 430 nm","note":"the ε to use"},{"t":"7.0 × 10³ M⁻¹cm⁻¹ for 550 nm","note":"wrong wavelength: a distractor"}]},{"text":"**Beer's law:** absorbance grows with ε, path and concentration. Solve for c.","calc":["A = εbc","c = A ÷ (εb)"]},{"text":"**Match the wavelength.** ε is a property of the compound at one wavelength; the reading was at 430 nm.","calc":["c = 0.63 ÷ (2.1 × 10⁴ × 0.40)","c = 0.63 ÷ 8400 = 7.5 × 10⁻⁵ M = 75 μM"]},{"text":"**Also:** a reading above the detector's linear range is fixed by lowering A: dilute the sample (smaller c) or use a shorter path (smaller b). A longer cell makes A larger, not smaller.","calc":["A = εbc: dilute 3-fold → A ÷ 3","1.0 cm → 0.20 cm cell → A ÷ 5"]},{"text":"**Also:** the reference (blank) and sample cells must differ only in the analyte, so the two readings can be compared. A cell's own absorbance (a scratch, a different glass) would add to the sample reading.","calc":["A(read) = A(analyte) + A(cell difference)","cell difference 0.10 → reading too high by 0.10"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** c = A/(εb) with ε at the measured wavelength; lower a too-high A by diluting or shortening the path, and blank with an identical cell."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-41a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:41#1","pe4-cp:41#2"],"q":"An esterase cleaves ethyl acetate, shown, through a covalent acyl-enzyme intermediate formed by an active-site serine. Which substrate atom does the serine oxygen attack?","fig":"ethyl-acetate","choices":["Carbonyl carbon","Carbonyl oxygen","Bridging ester oxygen","Ethyl CH₂ carbon"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"The carbonyl O is δ− and electron-rich, so a nucleophile won't attack it; it takes the electrons as the C=O opens."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"The bridging O is electron-rich too; it is the leaving group, departing with the ethyl group as ethanol."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Attack at the ethyl CH₂ would put ethyl, not acetyl, on the enzyme; an acyl-enzyme needs the new bond at the acyl group."}],"rule":"A nucleophile bonds to the most δ+ atom, the one pulled on by two oxygens; oxygens are electron-rich and never the target.","exp":"The serine oxygen is a **nucleophile**, so it bonds to the most **electrophilic** atom: the **carbonyl carbon**, made δ+ by two oxygens. Attack gives a tetrahedral intermediate; the C–O bond to the ethoxy group then breaks, ethanol leaves, and the acetyl group sits on the enzyme as an **acyl-enzyme** (Ser–O–C(=O)CH₃), later hydrolyzed by water.","how":{"ask":"The attacking oxygen has lone pairs; which atom of the ester is electron-poor enough to accept them, and which bond must break to leave acetyl on the enzyme?","know":"• A nucleophile (Ser O, Cys S, His N, Asp O) attacks an **electrophilic** atom.\n• In an ester R–C(=O)–O–R′, the **carbonyl carbon** is bonded to two O atoms and is strongly δ+.\n• Oxygens are electron-rich: a nucleophile does not attack them.\n• Attack at the carbonyl C gives a tetrahedral intermediate; the alkoxy group then leaves and the **acyl group stays on the enzyme**.\n• In a phosphate ester the same role falls to **P**, bonded to four O atoms.","steps":[{"text":"The serine oxygen is the nucleophile in this step.","hl":"serine oxygen attack","fig":"serine-sc"},{"text":"Find the electron-poor atom: the carbonyl carbon, pulled on by two oxygens.","calc":["C=O carbon bonded to 2 O → δ+ → the electrophile","Ser–OH + CH₃C(=O)OCH₂CH₃ → Ser–O–C(=O)CH₃ + HOCH₂CH₃"]},{"text":"Oxygen atoms are electron-rich, so the nucleophile attacks neither the carbonyl nor the bridging oxygen; the bridging O leaves with the ethyl group.","strike":["Carbonyl oxygen","Bridging ester oxygen"]},{"text":"Attack at the ethyl CH₂ would break the CH₂–O bond and put ethyl, not acetyl, on the enzyme; an acyl-enzyme needs the new bond at the acyl carbon.","strike":["Ethyl CH₂ carbon"]},{"text":"Answer: the carbonyl carbon."}],"trap":"Picking the bridging oxygen because the bond that breaks is the acyl C–O bond: the nucleophile forms its new bond to the carbonyl C, and the bridging O leaves with the ethyl group."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"acyl-enzyme intermediate","note":"acyl group bonded to the enzyme"},{"t":"serine","note":"hydroxyl O nucleophile"},{"t":"ethyl acetate","note":"an ester"}]},{"text":"**Nucleophile meets electrophile.** Lone pairs go to the δ+ atom; in an ester, that is the carbonyl carbon."},{"text":"**Follow the acyl group.** The Ser O–C bond forms as the C–O(ethyl) bond breaks, so the acetyl group is transferred to the enzyme.","calc":["E–Ser–OH + CH₃CO–OEt → E–Ser–O–COCH₃ + EtOH","E–Ser–O–COCH₃ + H₂O → E–Ser–OH + CH₃COOH"]},{"text":"**Also:** a phosphatase does the same at phosphorus: an Asp O or His N attacks the δ+ P of a phosphate ester, the alcohol leaves, and a phospho-enzyme forms (phospho-Asp or phosphohistidine).","fig":"histidine-sc"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** the enzyme nucleophile bonds to the δ+ center, never to an oxygen: the carbonyl carbon of an ester (giving an acyl-enzyme) or the phosphorus of a phosphate ester (giving a phospho-enzyme)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-43a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe4-cp:43#1","pe4-cp:43#2","pe4-cp:43#3"],"q":"An enzyme (5.0 nM) gives: pH 6, kcat 300 s⁻¹, KM 50 μM; pH 8, kcat 600 s⁻¹, KM 200 μM. As pH rises from 6 to 8, how do kcat/KM and Vmax change?","choices":["kcat/KM halves; Vmax doubles","kcat/KM doubles; Vmax doubles","kcat/KM halves; Vmax is unchanged","kcat/KM quadruples; Vmax halves"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Direction: kcat doubled but KM quadrupled, so kcat/KM falls; it can't double."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Both parts flipped: efficiency falls because KM grew more than kcat, and Vmax = kcat[E] rises with kcat."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Vmax = kcat[E]; with [E] fixed and kcat doubled, Vmax can't stay the same."}],"rule":"Divide each pH's kcat by its KM (300/50 vs 600/200) and compare. Vmax = kcat × [E], so at fixed [E] it moves with kcat.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Initial rate vs [S] at 5.0 nM enzyme, from each pH's kcat and KM","x":{"label":"[S] (μM)","min":0,"max":600,"step":100},"y":{"label":"v₀ (μM/s)","min":0,"max":3,"step":0.5},"series":[{"name":"pH 6","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[10,0.25],[25,0.5],[50,0.75],[100,1],[200,1.2],[300,1.286],[400,1.333],[600,1.385]]},{"name":"pH 8","marker":"square","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[10,0.143],[25,0.333],[50,0.6],[100,1],[200,1.5],[300,1.8],[400,2],[600,2.25]]}]},"exp":"**Catalytic efficiency = kcat/KM**: 300/50 = 6 μM⁻¹s⁻¹ at pH 6 and 600/200 = 3 μM⁻¹s⁻¹ at pH 8, so it **halves**. **Vmax = kcat[E]total** with the same enzyme amount: 1.5 μM/s rises to 3.0 μM/s, so it **doubles**. A faster kcat does not mean higher efficiency when KM grows even more.","how":{"ask":"Compute kcat/KM at each pH, then Vmax = kcat × [E]total at each pH.","know":"• **Catalytic efficiency = kcat/KM** (units μM⁻¹s⁻¹ or M⁻¹s⁻¹); higher means better at low [S].\n• **Vmax = kcat[E]total**: with the same enzyme concentration, Vmax tracks kcat.\n• KM is the [S] at half Vmax; a larger KM means weaker apparent binding.\n• Read a table row by row: compute each quantity at each condition, then compare.","steps":[{"text":"Pull the values for each pH.","hl":"pH 6, kcat 300 s⁻¹, KM 50 μM; pH 8, kcat 600 s⁻¹, KM 200 μM","calc":["[E]total = 5.0 nM = 0.0050 μM"]},{"text":"Catalytic efficiency at each pH.","calc":["pH 6: 300 s⁻¹ ÷ 50 μM = **6 μM⁻¹s⁻¹**","pH 8: 600 s⁻¹ ÷ 200 μM = **3 μM⁻¹s⁻¹**","6 → 3: **halves**"],"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"pH 6","x":25,"text":"pH 6 faster at low [S]","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"Vmax at each pH.","calc":["pH 6: 300 s⁻¹ × 0.0050 μM = **1.5 μM/s**","pH 8: 600 s⁻¹ × 0.0050 μM = **3.0 μM/s**","1.5 → 3.0: **doubles**"],"graph":{"labels":[{"series":"pH 8","x":600,"text":"pH 8: still rising toward 3.0","at":"below"}]}},{"text":"Efficiency falls, so it does not double or quadruple; Vmax follows kcat, so it neither stays the same nor halves.","strike":["kcat/KM doubles; Vmax doubles","kcat/KM halves; Vmax is unchanged","kcat/KM quadruples; Vmax halves"]},{"text":"Answer: kcat/KM halves; Vmax doubles."}],"trap":"Judging efficiency from kcat alone: kcat doubled, but KM quadrupled, so kcat/KM fell."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"5.0 nM","note":"[E]total, same at both pH"},{"t":"kcat 300 s⁻¹","note":"pH 6 turnover"},{"t":"KM 50 μM","note":"pH 6"},{"t":"kcat 600 s⁻¹","note":"pH 8 turnover"},{"t":"KM 200 μM","note":"pH 8"}]},{"text":"**Efficiency is a ratio.** kcat/KM, so look at how both numbers change.","calc":["kcat × 2, KM × 4 → kcat/KM × 2/4 = × ½"]},{"text":"**Also:** Vmax = kcat[E]total, so at a fixed enzyme concentration Vmax changes by the same factor as kcat.","calc":["kcat × 2 → Vmax × 2"]},{"text":"**Also:** for a pH table, compute kcat/KM and Vmax in each row and state the direction of change as the pH rises; the two can move in opposite directions."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** catalytic efficiency = kcat/KM and Vmax = kcat[E]total; compute each at every condition in the table before comparing."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-44a","s":"Fluids","disc":"physics","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:44#1"],"q":"In a clean glass capillary tube, water rises 3.0 cm above the surrounding surface. To what height does it rise in a tube of half the inner radius?","choices":["6.0 cm","1.5 cm","12 cm","3.0 cm"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Direction: a narrower tube lifts water higher (h ∝ 1/r), so halving r cannot lower the column."},{"c":3,"tag":"copied","why":"That is the starting height copied over; height does depend on radius, since the column's weight scales with the tube's area."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Squares the ratio: capillary rise goes as 1/r, not 1/r², so halving r doesn't quadruple the height."}],"rule":"Capillary height h ∝ 1/r: halve the radius and the column doubles; double the radius and it halves.","exp":"Surface tension pulls up along the rim of the meniscus with a force **γ·2πr·cosθ**, which grows with the circumference, while the lifted column weighs **ρg·πr²h**. Setting them equal gives **h = 2γcosθ/(ρgr)**, so **h ∝ 1/r**: half the radius, twice the height, 2 × 3.0 cm = **6.0 cm**.","how":{"ask":"Balance the surface-tension pull on the rim against the weight of the column, and see how the height scales with the radius.","know":"• **Capillary rise**: adhesion to glass plus **surface tension** pulls water up a narrow tube.\n• Upward pull = γ × rim length × cosθ = **γ·2πr·cosθ** (∝ r).\n• Column weight = ρ·(πr²h)·g (∝ r²h).\n• Setting them equal: **h = 2γcosθ/(ρgr)**, so **h ∝ 1/r**.","steps":[{"text":"Write the balance: rim pull up, column weight down.","hl":"clean glass capillary tube","calc":["γ·2πr·cosθ = ρ·πr²h·g","h = 2γcosθ / (ρgr)"]},{"text":"Only r changes, so h scales as 1/r.","hl":"half the inner radius","calc":["r → r/2","h → 2h = 2 × 3.0 cm = **6.0 cm**"]},{"text":"1.5 cm has the direction backward (it treats h as growing with r), and 3.0 cm ignores the r in the formula.","strike":["1.5 cm","3.0 cm"]},{"text":"12 cm uses h ∝ 1/r², as if the pull did not grow with the rim; the pull's one power of r cancels one of the two in the weight.","calc":["wrong: (1/2)² → ×4 → 4 × 3.0 = 12 cm","right: r/r² = 1/r → ×2 → 6.0 cm"],"strike":["12 cm"]},{"text":"Answer: 6.0 cm."}],"trap":"Using 1/r² because the column's area goes as r²: the surface-tension pull also grows with r (the rim), so only one power of r is left, h ∝ 1/r."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"clean glass capillary tube","note":"water wets glass: it rises"},{"t":"rises 3.0 cm","note":"starting height"},{"t":"half the inner radius","note":"r → r/2"}]},{"text":"**Pull versus weight.** The meniscus edge pulls up with γ along the whole rim; the column's weight pulls down.","calc":["up: γ·2πr·cosθ (∝ r)","down: ρg·πr²h (∝ r²h)","h = 2γcosθ/(ρgr) ∝ 1/r"]},{"text":"**Scale it:** half the radius → twice the height.","calc":["3.0 cm × 2 = 6.0 cm"]},{"text":"**Also:** the same surface tension, which hydrogen-bond cohesion at the surface creates, acts as a skin that holds up a water strider or a gently placed clip, objects denser than water that buoyancy alone could not float."},{"text":"**Also:** viscosity is resistance to flow; it slows how fast the water climbs but does not set the final height."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** capillary height h = 2γcosθ/(ρgr), so h ∝ 1/r: halve the radius and the water climbs twice as high."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-46a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:46#1"],"q":"Viewed from the side in white light, a 7 mm pencil standing in a glass of water (n = 1.33) looks broken at the water line, with no colored fringes. What causes this?","choices":["Refraction","Diffraction","Dispersion","Polarization"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Diffraction shows only for objects near the light's wavelength; a 7 mm pencil is about 10⁴ times bigger than visible λ."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Dispersion spreads white light into colors, but the stem says the image shows no colored fringes."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Polarization concerns the orientation of the light's field; it does not bend or shift an image."}],"rule":"An image bent at a surface between two media comes from light changing speed; colored fringes mean dispersion, wavelength-sized gaps mean diffraction.","exp":"Light from the underwater part of the pencil **changes speed as it crosses the water–air surface** (v = c/n, slower in water) and bends away from the normal as it exits, so that part appears shifted. Bending at a boundary between two media is **refraction**, described by Snell's law, n₁sinθ₁ = n₂sinθ₂.","how":{"ask":"Is the light crossing into a new medium, or bending around an edge close to its wavelength in size?","know":"• **Refraction**: light changes speed at a boundary between media (v = c/n) and bends: n₁sinθ₁ = n₂sinθ₂.\n• **Diffraction**: bending around an edge or through a gap, strong only when its size is near the wavelength (visible λ ≈ 0.4–0.7 μm).\n• **Dispersion**: n varies with λ, so white light spreads into colors.\n• **Interference**: overlapping waves add (bright) or cancel (dark), making fringes.","steps":[{"text":"The light leaving the underwater pencil crosses from water into air, a change of medium, so its speed and direction change.","hl":"looks broken at the water line","calc":["v in water = c/n = 3.0 × 10⁸ ÷ 1.33 ≈ 2.3 × 10⁸ m/s","faster in air → bends away from the normal at the surface"]},{"text":"Compare the pencil with the wavelength: diffraction is noticeable only for objects near λ in size.","hl":"7 mm pencil","calc":["visible λ ≈ 0.5 μm","7 mm ÷ 0.5 μm = 14,000 wavelengths → diffraction negligible"],"strike":["Diffraction"]},{"text":"Dispersion would fringe the image with colors, which the stem rules out; polarization changes the field's orientation, not the light's direction.","hl":"no colored fringes","strike":["Dispersion","Polarization"]},{"text":"Answer: refraction."}],"trap":"Choosing diffraction because the light bends: bending around an edge needs an object near the wavelength, while bending at a boundary between media is refraction."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the clues**.","marks":[{"t":"glass of water (n = 1.33)","note":"a second medium"},{"t":"7 mm pencil","note":"far larger than λ"},{"t":"broken at the water line","note":"image shifted at the boundary"},{"t":"no colored fringes","note":"not dispersion"}]},{"text":"**New medium → speed change → bending.** That is refraction, with Snell's law n₁sinθ₁ = n₂sinθ₂."},{"text":"**Size versus wavelength.** Diffraction matters only when an object or gap is near λ: bright and dark rings around 1 μm specks in green laser light are diffraction, not refraction.","calc":["7 mm ≫ 0.5 μm → refraction dominates","1 μm ≈ 2 × 0.532 μm → diffraction rings"]},{"text":"**Also:** bright and dark fringes form by interference: waves in step add (bright), and waves half a wavelength out of step cancel (dark)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** bending where light enters a new medium is refraction (Snell's law); bending around an obstacle near λ in size is diffraction; colors split because n varies with λ is dispersion."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-48a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:48#1","pe4-cp:48#2","pe4-cp:51#1","pe4-cp:51#2","pe4-cp:51#3"],"q":"At physiological pH, which tautomer of guanine predominates, and how many hydrogen bonds does it form with cytosine in a Watson–Crick pair?","choices":["Keto; 3","Enol; 3","Keto; 2","Enol; 2"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"The enol is the rare tautomer; at pH 7 guanine sits in the keto form."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Enol again: bases are keto at physiological pH, so any enol option falls."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Two H-bonds is the A–T count; a G–C pair has one more, which is why G–C-rich DNA melts at higher T."}],"rule":"Nucleobases sit in their keto (and amino) forms at pH 7; G–C pairs by three H-bonds, A–T by two.","exp":"Under physiological conditions guanine, like thymine and uracil, is almost entirely in the **keto** form (C6=O with N1–H). Its pairing face is then acceptor, donor, donor (O6, N1–H, N2–H), matching cytosine's donor, acceptor, acceptor (N4–H, N3, O2): **three hydrogen bonds**. A·T pairs make only two.","how":{"ask":"Which tautomer has the C=O and ring N–H, and how many donor–acceptor matches line up across G and C?","know":"• **Tautomers** differ in where an H and a double bond sit: keto C=O / N–H versus enol C–OH / C=N.\n• At physiological pH, **G, T and U are mainly keto**; rare enol forms change the pairing face and cause mispairs.\n• A **hydrogen bond** forms where an H on N or O (donor) faces a lone-pair N or O (acceptor).\n• Normal pairs: **G·C has 3 H-bonds; A·T has 2.**","steps":[{"text":"The common form of guanine is the keto tautomer: C6=O and N1–H.","hl":"which tautomer of guanine predominates","fig":"guanine"},{"text":"Count donor–acceptor matches across the Watson–Crick face.","fig":"cytosine","calc":["G O6 (acceptor) ··· H–N4 of C (donor): 1","G N1–H (donor) ··· N3 of C (acceptor): 2","G N2–H (donor) ··· O2 of C (acceptor): 3"]},{"text":"The enol is the rare form, so both enol options fall.","strike":["Enol; 3","Enol; 2"]},{"text":"Two hydrogen bonds is the A·T count, not G·C.","strike":["Keto; 2"]},{"text":"Answer: keto; 3."}],"trap":"Thinking the enol is favored because it has an O–H: in water at pH 7, the keto (lactam) form dominates for G, T and U."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"physiological pH","note":"keto forms dominate"},{"t":"tautomer of guanine","note":"keto vs enol"},{"t":"Watson–Crick pair","note":"G·C"}]},{"text":"**Keto wins.** G, T and U sit mainly as keto (C=O, ring N–H) under physiological conditions; the enol forms are rare."},{"text":"**Count the H-bonds pair by pair.** An H on N or O facing a lone-pair N or O is one hydrogen bond.","calc":["G·C: O6···H–N4, N1–H···N3, N2–H···O2 = 3","A·T: N6–H···O4, N1···H–N3 = 2"]},{"text":"**Also:** a rare enol tautomer mispairs. Enol guanine has O6–H (donor) and N1 (acceptor) in place of C6=O and N1–H, so its face reads donor, acceptor, donor and pairs with thymine instead of cytosine; against cytosine only one hydrogen bond lines up.","calc":["enol G O6–H ··· O4 of T","enol G N1 ··· H–N3 of T","enol G N2–H ··· O2 of T","O6–H faces H–N4: donor meets donor, no bond","N1 faces N3: acceptor meets acceptor, no bond","N2–H···O2: 1 bond, so 3 → 1"]},{"text":"**Also:** swapping a donor for an acceptor changes the count in a drawn pair: redo the count for the tautomer actually drawn, matching each donor H to an acceptor across the pair."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** G, T and U are mainly keto at physiological pH, and rare enols mispair; G·C has 3 H-bonds and A·T 2, counted as donor H facing acceptor N or O."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-50a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:50#1","pe4-cp:50#2"],"q":"An esterase's active-site aspartate uses its negatively charged side chain to hold a protonated histidine in place. Which mutation most likely preserves activity?","choices":["Asp → Glu","Asp → Ala","Asp → Lys","Asp → Phe"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Alanine's side chain is an uncharged methyl, so the ion pair with the protonated His is lost."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Lysine is positive at pH 7; it would repel the protonated His, not hold it."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Phe is nonpolar and uncharged, so the ion pair is lost."}],"rule":"To keep an ion pair, keep the charge: replace an acidic residue with another acidic one, never a neutral or basic one.","exp":"The job depends on the **negative carboxylate** pairing with the positive histidine. Glutamate keeps a –COO⁻, one CH₂ longer, so it is a **conservative substitution**. Alanine and phenylalanine are nonpolar and carry no charge, and lysine is positive, so it would repel the histidine.","how":{"ask":"What chemical feature of the aspartate side chain does the job, and which replacement keeps that feature?","know":"• **Conservative substitution**: replace a residue with one of similar chemistry (charge, polarity, size), usually keeping function.\n• At pH 7: Asp and Glu are −1 (carboxylates); Lys and Arg are +1; Ala and Phe are nonpolar and neutral.\n• Asp and Glu differ only by one CH₂ in the side chain.","steps":[{"text":"The key feature is the negative charge.","hl":"negatively charged side chain","fig":"aspartate"},{"text":"Glutamate also ends in a carboxylate, so the ion pair survives.","hl":"negatively charged side chain","fig":"glutamate"},{"text":"Alanine and phenylalanine have no charge, so the ion pair is lost.","strike":["Asp → Ala","Asp → Phe"]},{"text":"Lysine is positive at pH 7 and would repel the protonated histidine.","strike":["Asp → Lys"]},{"text":"Answer: Asp → Glu."}],"trap":"Choosing by size alone: the carboxylate charge, not the side-chain length, carries the function."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"negatively charged side chain","note":"the carboxylate"},{"t":"protonated histidine","note":"positive partner"},{"t":"preserves activity","note":"conservative swap"}]},{"text":"**Name the job.** An Asp that pairs with a positive group needs a –COO⁻."},{"text":"**Keep the chemistry.** Glu is the only standard residue besides Asp with a side-chain carboxylate.","calc":["Asp: –CH₂–COO⁻","Glu: –CH₂–CH₂–COO⁻"]},{"text":"**Also:** swapping Asp for Ala, Lys or Phe loses the negative charge (Lys even reverses it), so activity is lost."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to keep an Asp's function, swap it for Glu, since both carry a carboxylate; Ala, Lys or Phe lose the negative charge."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-53a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:53#1","pe4-cp:53#2","pe4-cp:53#3"],"q":"An 8.0 μF capacitor holds 7.0 kV. How high must a 20 kg crate rise to match its energy (g = 9.8 m/s²)?","choices":["1.0 m","2.0 m","1.0 × 10⁶ m","1.0 × 10⁻⁶ m"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"Reads μF as F: a small capacitor can't lift a crate 1000 km; C must be in farads (× 10⁻⁶)."},{"c":3,"tag":"units","why":"Reads kV as V: using 7.0 V instead of 7000 V shrinks V² by 10⁶."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"Dropped the ½: CV² alone is twice the stored energy, so the height comes out doubled."}],"rule":"U = ½CV² with C in F and V in V, then h = U ÷ (mg). Round: ½ × 8×10⁻⁶ × 5×10⁷ ≈ 200 J, over mg ≈ 200 N.","exp":"**Set the capacitor's energy equal to mgh.** U = ½CV² = ½ × 8.0 × 10⁻⁶ F × (7.0 × 10³ V)² = 196 J. Then h = U/(mg) = 196 J ÷ (20 kg × 9.8 m/s²) = 196 ÷ 196 = **1.0 m**.","how":{"ask":"Find the capacitor's stored energy in joules, then find the height that gives the crate the same potential energy.","know":"• **Capacitor energy: U = ½CV²** (C in farads, V in volts).\n• **Gravitational potential energy: U = mgh**, so h = U/(mg).\n• Prefixes: **μF = 10⁻⁶ F**, **kV = 10³ V**.","steps":[{"text":"Convert both prefixed values to SI units first.","hl":"8.0 μF capacitor holds 7.0 kV","calc":["C = 8.0 μF = 8.0 × 10⁻⁶ F","V = 7.0 kV = 7.0 × 10³ V"]},{"text":"Compute the stored energy with U = ½CV².","calc":["V² = (7.0 × 10³)² = 4.9 × 10⁷ V²","U = ½ × 8.0 × 10⁻⁶ × 4.9 × 10⁷ = **196 J**"]},{"text":"Set U = mgh and solve for h.","hl":"20 kg crate","calc":["mg = 20 kg × 9.8 m/s² = 196 N","h = U/(mg) = 196 J ÷ 196 N = **1.0 m**"]},{"text":"2.0 m drops the ½ (CV² = 392 J). 1.0 × 10⁶ m leaves C in μF as if it were farads. 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ m leaves V in kV as if it were volts.","strike":["2.0 m","1.0 × 10⁶ m","1.0 × 10⁻⁶ m"]},{"text":"Answer: 1.0 m."}],"trap":"Skipping a prefix: a missed 10⁻⁶ or a missed (10³)² moves the answer by a factor of a million."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"8.0 μF","note":"capacitance C"},{"t":"7.0 kV","note":"voltage V"},{"t":"20 kg","note":"mass m"},{"t":"g = 9.8 m/s²","note":"gravity"}]},{"text":"**Convert the prefixes.** Micro is 10⁻⁶ and kilo is 10³.","calc":["8.0 μF = 8.0 × 10⁻⁶ F","7.0 kV = 7.0 × 10³ V"]},{"text":"**Capacitor energy:** U = ½CV². Square the voltage, exponent included.","calc":["U = ½ × 8.0 × 10⁻⁶ × 4.9 × 10⁷ = 196 J"]},{"text":"**Equal energies:** set ½CV² = mgh and solve for h.","calc":["h = ½CV²/(mg)","h = 196 ÷ (20 × 9.8) = 1.0 m"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to match a capacitor's energy with a lifted mass, convert to F and V, compute U = ½CV², then h = U/(mg)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-54a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:54#1","pe4-cp:54#2","pe4-cp:54#3","pe6-cp:40#1","pe6-cp:40#2"],"q":"A 3.7 V drone battery is rated at 5.0 A·h. What total charge flows through it from full to empty?","choices":["5.0 C","300 C","1.8 × 10⁴ C","6.7 × 10⁴ C"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"units","why":"5.0 is in A·h, not C; an hour is thousands of seconds, so Q must be thousands of coulombs."},{"c":1,"tag":"units","why":"Uses 60 s per hour; an hour is 3600 s, sixty times more."},{"c":3,"tag":"units","why":"A·h × V gives an energy (W·h, or J), not a charge; the battery voltage isn't needed."}],"rule":"Q = I × t with t in seconds: 1 A·h = 3600 C. Round: 5 × 3600 ≈ 20,000 C; ignore the voltage.","exp":"Amp-hours measure **charge, since Q = I·t**. 1 A·h = 1 A × 3600 s = 3600 C, so 5.0 A·h × 3600 C/(A·h) = **1.8 × 10⁴ C**. The 3.7 V is not needed; multiplying by it gives energy in joules.","how":{"ask":"Convert the amp-hour rating to coulombs.","know":"• **Q = I·t** (C = A × s).\n• **1 A·h = 1 A × 3600 s = 3600 C**.\n• Voltage × charge = energy (J), not charge.","steps":[{"text":"Amp-hours multiply current by time, which gives charge.","hl":"rated at 5.0 A·h","calc":["Q = I·t → A·h is a charge unit"]},{"text":"Convert hours to seconds and multiply.","calc":["1 h = 3600 s","Q = 5.0 A × 3600 s","Q = 18 000 C = **1.8 × 10⁴ C**"]},{"text":"The voltage plays no part in the charge.","hl":"3.7 V","calc":["3.7 V × 1.8 × 10⁴ C ≈ 6.7 × 10⁴ J: energy, not charge"]},{"text":"6.7 × 10⁴ C multiplies by the voltage, 300 C uses 60 s per hour, and 5.0 C skips the time conversion.","strike":["6.7 × 10⁴ C","300 C","5.0 C"]},{"text":"Answer: 1.8 × 10⁴ C."}],"trap":"Multiplying by the voltage: that gives energy in joules, not charge in coulombs."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the numbers** and what they are.","marks":[{"t":"3.7 V","note":"voltage: not needed"},{"t":"5.0 A·h","note":"charge rating"},{"t":"What total charge","note":"answer in coulombs"}]},{"text":"**Charge = current × time.** An amp-hour already is a charge.","calc":["Q = I·t"]},{"text":"**Also:** convert with 1 A·h = 3600 C.","calc":["1 A·h = 1 A × 3600 s = 3600 C","5.0 A·h × 3600 C = 1.8 × 10⁴ C"]},{"text":"**Also:** the voltage doesn't matter for charge; it only enters if you want energy (E = QV)."},{"text":"**Also:** when an electrode material is rated per gram, multiply by its mass in grams (mg × 10⁻³) to get the capacity, then convert to coulombs: 1 mAh = 10⁻³ A × 3600 s = 3.6 C.","calc":["150 mAh/g × 20 mg = 150 × 0.020 g = 3.0 mAh","3.0 mAh × 3.6 C/mAh ≈ 11 C"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Q = I·t, and 1 A·h = 3600 C; ignore the voltage when the question asks for charge."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-55a","s":"Mechanics & Energy","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:55#1","pe4-cp:55#2"],"q":"A 75 g toy cart speeds up from 15 cm/s to 95 cm/s in 0.40 s. Find the mean net force on the cart:","choices":["0.15 N","0.18 N","15 N","150 N"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"units","why":"Leaves the mass in grams; 75 g is 0.075 kg, a factor of 1000 smaller."},{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"Leaves the speeds in cm/s; convert to m/s (÷ 100) first."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"Uses the final speed instead of the change; subtract the starting speed from the final one first."}],"rule":"F = m·Δv ÷ Δt with m in kg and v in m/s. Round: 0.08 kg × 0.8 m/s ÷ 0.4 s.","exp":"**F = mΔv/Δt** in SI units: m = 0.075 kg, Δv = 0.95 − 0.15 = 0.80 m/s. F = 0.075 kg × 0.80 m/s ÷ 0.40 s = **0.15 N**.","how":{"ask":"Convert to kg and m/s, then divide the momentum change by the time.","know":"• **F = ma = mΔv/Δt** (N = kg·m/s²).\n• **1 g = 10⁻³ kg; 1 cm/s = 10⁻² m/s**: convert before multiplying.","steps":[{"text":"Convert the mass and speeds to SI.","hl":"75 g","calc":["m = 75 g = **0.075 kg**","15 cm/s = 0.15 m/s; 95 cm/s = 0.95 m/s"]},{"text":"Find the change in velocity.","hl":"from 15 cm/s to 95 cm/s","calc":["Δv = 0.95 − 0.15 = **0.80 m/s**"]},{"text":"Apply F = mΔv/Δt.","hl":"0.40 s","calc":["F = 0.075 kg × 0.80 m/s ÷ 0.40 s","F = 0.060 ÷ 0.40 = **0.15 N**"]},{"text":"0.18 N uses the final speed instead of the change; 15 N leaves speeds in cm/s; 150 N leaves mass in grams.","strike":["0.18 N","15 N","150 N"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.15 N."}],"trap":"Skipping a unit conversion: grams or cm/s each make the force off by a power of ten."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and its unit.","marks":[{"t":"75 g","note":"mass in grams"},{"t":"15 cm/s","note":"initial speed"},{"t":"95 cm/s","note":"final speed"},{"t":"0.40 s","note":"time interval"}]},{"text":"**Average force = mass × velocity change ÷ time.**","calc":["F = mΔv/Δt"]},{"text":"**Also:** convert to SI first: g → kg (÷ 1000) and cm/s → m/s (÷ 100).","calc":["75 g = 0.075 kg","Δv = 80 cm/s = 0.80 m/s"]},{"text":"**Compute.**","calc":["F = 0.075 × 0.80 ÷ 0.40 = 0.15 N"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** F = mΔv/Δt with mass in kg and speeds in m/s."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-56a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:56#1"],"q":"A solid aluminum block sits in a uniform field of 3.0 × 10³ N/C. Once its charges stop moving, the field inside it is zero because:","choices":["mobile electrons rearrange.","the metal absorbs it.","its electrons are all bound.","the block has no net charge."],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Metals don't absorb static fields; the field inside is cancelled, not soaked up."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Aluminum is a metal, so it has free electrons; they are not all bound."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"A neutral block still has free charges that separate; zero net charge doesn't cancel an outside field by itself."}],"rule":"Inside a conductor at equilibrium E = 0: free charges shift to its surfaces until their own field cancels the applied one.","exp":"A metal's **free electrons** drift in the outside field and pile up on one face, leaving the other face positive. They keep moving until **their field exactly cancels** the outside one inside the metal: **electrostatic equilibrium**, E = 0 inside.","how":{"ask":"Explain what the free electrons do until they stop moving.","know":"• A conductor has **free electrons** that move whenever a field acts on them.\n• They pile up on the **surface**, and the field they make opposes the applied one.\n• **Electrostatic equilibrium**: charges stopped, so E inside = 0 and any excess charge sits on the surface.","steps":[{"text":"The outside field pushes the block's free electrons toward one face.","hl":"uniform field of 3.0 × 10³ N/C","calc":["electrons → one face (−)","other face left (+)"]},{"text":"Those surface charges make a field opposite to the applied one; motion stops only when the two cancel inside.","hl":"Once its charges stop moving","calc":["E(inside) = E(applied) − E(surface charges)","stop only when E(inside) = 0"]},{"text":"Metals don't absorb fields, aluminum has free electrons, and a neutral block still has free charges that separate.","strike":["the metal absorbs it.","its electrons are all bound.","the block has no net charge."]},{"text":"Answer: mobile electrons rearrange, and their surface charge cancels the field inside."}],"trap":"Saying the metal blocks or absorbs the field: the zero is the result of induced surface charges cancelling it."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"solid aluminum block","note":"a conductor: free electrons"},{"t":"Once its charges stop moving","note":"electrostatic equilibrium"},{"t":"field inside it is zero","note":"explain why"}]},{"text":"**Free electrons move in a field.** They drift until they reach the surface and pile up there."},{"text":"**Their field opposes the outside field.** If any field were left inside, electrons would keep moving.","calc":["E inside ≠ 0 → electrons move","electrons stop → E inside = 0"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in a conductor at electrostatic equilibrium, free electrons move to the surface until their field cancels any field inside, so E = 0 there."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-58a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"biology","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe4-cp:58#1","pe4-cp:58#2"],"q":"A motor neuron receives excitatory synaptic input. In the action potential that follows, which of these events occurs first?","choices":["Na⁺ influx","K⁺ efflux","Ca²⁺ influx","Hyperpolarization"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"K⁺ leaves only after the Na⁺ upstroke, during repolarization."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Ca²⁺ enters at the axon terminal, only after the spike arrives there."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Hyperpolarization is the last phase of a spike, after repolarization."}],"rule":"Spike order: Na⁺ channels open to depolarize, K⁺ channels open to repolarize, then the undershoot; Ca²⁺ enters only at the terminal.","exp":"Graded potentials first bring the **axon hillock to threshold**. Then the **Na⁺ channels, opened by the voltage change, let Na⁺ rush in** (depolarization), K⁺ channels open and K⁺ leaves (repolarization), the membrane briefly hyperpolarizes, and only when the spike reaches the axon terminal does Ca²⁺ enter.","how":{"ask":"Put the four events in order, starting from the moment the hillock reaches threshold.","know":"Order of an action potential: (1) **graded potentials reach threshold at the axon hillock**; (2) **Na⁺ enters through voltage-opened channels** (depolarization); (3) **K⁺ leaves through voltage-opened channels** (repolarization); (4) **hyperpolarization** (K⁺ channels slow to close). Later, at the **axon terminal**, voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels open, Ca²⁺ enters, and neurotransmitter is released.","steps":[{"text":"The input is excitatory, so graded potentials spread to the axon hillock and bring it to threshold. That starts the spike.","hl":"excitatory synaptic input"},{"text":"Plot the spike at the hillock: rise, fall, dip, recover.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Membrane potential at the axon hillock (sketch)","x":{"label":"Time (ms)","min":0,"max":6,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Membrane potential (mV)","min":-100,"max":40,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Vm","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,-70],[1,-70],[1.6,-55],[2.1,35],[2.7,-40],[3.2,-82],[4.5,-73],[6,-70]]}]},"calc":["−70 → −55 mV: graded potentials reach threshold","−55 → +35 mV: Na⁺ in (step 2)","+35 → −70 mV: K⁺ out (step 3)","below −70 mV: hyperpolarization (step 4)"]},{"text":"K⁺ leaves only after the Na⁺ upstroke, and the dip below rest comes after that.","strike":["K⁺ efflux","Hyperpolarization"]},{"text":"Ca²⁺ enters at the axon terminal, after the spike has traveled down the axon, to trigger neurotransmitter release.","strike":["Ca²⁺ influx"]},{"text":"Answer: Na⁺ influx."}],"trap":"Ca²⁺ influx sounds like an early trigger, but it happens last and far away, at the axon terminal, where it releases neurotransmitter."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words.**","marks":[{"t":"excitatory synaptic input","note":"makes graded potentials"},{"t":"action potential","note":"the all-or-none spike"},{"t":"occurs first","note":"put the events in order"}]},{"text":"**Step 1, threshold:** small graded potentials add up at the axon hillock. At about −55 mV the spike fires.","calc":["rest ≈ −70 mV → threshold ≈ −55 mV"]},{"text":"**Step 2, depolarization:** the voltage change opens Na⁺ channels; Na⁺ rushes in (influx = moving in). **Step 3, repolarization:** slower voltage-opened K⁺ channels let K⁺ leave (efflux = moving out). **Step 4:** K⁺ channels close slowly, so the membrane dips below rest (hyperpolarization).","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Membrane potential at the axon hillock (sketch)","x":{"label":"Time (ms)","min":0,"max":6,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Membrane potential (mV)","min":-100,"max":40,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Vm","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,-70],[1,-70],[1.6,-55],[2.1,35],[2.7,-40],[3.2,-82],[4.5,-73],[6,-70]]}]}},{"text":"**Also:** Ca²⁺ influx and neurotransmitter release come later, at the axon terminal: the spike arrives, voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels open, and Ca²⁺ triggers vesicle fusion."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** threshold at the hillock → Na⁺ in → K⁺ out → hyperpolarization; Ca²⁺ entry and transmitter release happen last, at the terminal."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe4-59a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe4-cp:59#1","pe4-cp:59#2"],"q":"A 300 W heater warms 0.50 kg of ethanol (c = 2.4 kJ/(kg·°C)) from 18 °C to 68 °C. If no heat is lost, how long does this take?","choices":["0.20 s","72 s","200 s","272 s"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"units","why":"Divides kJ by W without converting to J; warming half a kilogram in a fraction of a second is absurd."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"Uses 68 °C as ΔT; subtract the starting temperature to get the change."},{"c":1,"tag":"copied","why":"Uses the starting reading, 18 °C, as if it were ΔT; neither reading alone is the change."}],"rule":"t = m·c·ΔT ÷ P, with c in J (kJ × 1000) and ΔT = final − start. Round: 0.5 × 2400 × 50 ÷ 300.","exp":"**q = mcΔT** = 0.50 kg × 2.4 kJ/(kg·°C) × 50 °C = 60 kJ = 6.0 × 10⁴ J. **t = q/P** = 6.0 × 10⁴ J ÷ 300 J/s = **200 s**.","how":{"ask":"Find the heat the ethanol absorbs, convert it to joules, then divide by the heater's power.","know":"**q = mcΔT**, with m in the unit c uses (kJ/(kg·°C) takes kg; J/(g·°C) takes g) and **ΔT = T_final − T_initial**. **P = q/t**, so **t = q/P**; 1 W = 1 J/s and 1 kJ = 1000 J.","steps":[{"text":"Note the values; c is per kilogram, so the mass stays in kg.","hl":"0.50 kg of ethanol (c = 2.4 kJ/(kg·°C)) from 18 °C to 68 °C"},{"text":"Use the temperature change, not either reading: ΔT = 18 gives 72 s and ΔT = 68 gives 272 s.","calc":["ΔT = 68 − 18 = **50 °C**"],"strike":["72 s","272 s"]},{"text":"Compute the heat.","calc":["q = m c ΔT","q = 0.50 × 2.4 × 50","q = **60 kJ** = 6.0 × 10⁴ J"]},{"text":"Divide by the power, in joules per second.","hl":"300 W","calc":["t = q ÷ P","t = 60 000 J ÷ 300 J/s","t = **200 s**"]},{"text":"Dividing 60 kJ by 300 W without changing kJ to J gives a time 1000 times too short.","strike":["0.20 s"]},{"text":"Answer: 200 s."}],"trap":"Mixing kJ with W (J/s): 60 ÷ 300 = 0.20 s. Convert kJ to J first. Using 18 or 68 as ΔT gives 72 s or 272 s."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"300 W","note":"power: 300 J each second"},{"t":"0.50 kg","note":"mass"},{"t":"c = 2.4 kJ/(kg·°C)","note":"per kg, so keep kg"},{"t":"from 18 °C to 68 °C","note":"start → end"}]},{"text":"**Match the units:** c is in kJ per kg per °C, so the mass goes in as 0.50 kg; no gram conversion.","calc":["ΔT = 68 − 18 = 50 °C"]},{"text":"**Heat needed: q = mcΔT.**","calc":["q = 0.50 × 2.4 × 50","0.50 × 2.4 = 1.2","1.2 × 50 = **60 kJ**"]},{"text":"**Time from power: t = q/P.** Power is in W = J/s, so change kJ to J first.","calc":["60 kJ = 60 000 J","t = 60 000 ÷ 300 = **200 s**"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** q = mcΔT with the mass in the unit c uses, then t = q ÷ P with q in joules (1 W = 1 J/s)."}]},
  // ── Practice Exam 5 · Chem/Phys ──
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-02a","s":"Equilibrium","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:2#1","pe5-cp:2#2","pe5-cp:58#2"],"q":"A red Fe³⁺/SCN⁻ solution sits at equilibrium with FeSCN²⁺. Adding Ag⁺ precipitates SCN⁻ as solid AgSCN. How does the color change?","choices":["Deeper red, as more FeSCN²⁺ forms","Paler red, as FeSCN²⁺ breaks apart","No change, since K stays fixed","No change, since Ag⁺ is not in the K expression"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"Direction: removing a reactant shifts the equilibrium left; more product would need added reactant, not removed reactant."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"K stays fixed, but Q doesn't: losing SCN⁻ raises Q above K, so the mixture must shift until Q = K again."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Ag⁺ need not appear in K to matter; it pulls SCN⁻, which is in K, out of solution as a solid."}],"rule":"Removing a reactant (for example as a precipitate) shifts the equilibrium toward reactants; then follow the colored species.","exp":"In Fe³⁺ + SCN⁻ ⇌ FeSCN²⁺, Ag⁺ takes SCN⁻ out of solution as AgSCN. **Removing a reactant shifts the equilibrium toward reactants**, so red FeSCN²⁺ dissociates to restore some SCN⁻ and the **red fades**, while K stays the same.","how":{"ask":"Which species does Ag⁺ remove, which side of the equation is it on, and which way must the equilibrium shift to make some of it back?","know":"• **Le Châtelier**: a system at equilibrium shifts to offset a stress.\n• **Removing a reactant** (or adding a product) raises Q above K → shift toward reactants.\n• **Adding a reactant** (or removing a product) lowers Q below K → shift toward products.\n• A precipitating ion (Ag⁺ + SCN⁻ → AgSCN(s)) acts by lowering the free concentration of the ion it grabs.\n• K changes only with temperature.","steps":[{"text":"Write the equilibrium and find what Ag⁺ removes.","hl":"precipitates SCN⁻ as solid AgSCN","calc":["Fe³⁺ + SCN⁻ ⇌ FeSCN²⁺ (red)","Ag⁺ + SCN⁻ → AgSCN(s): free [SCN⁻] drops"]},{"text":"A reactant was removed, so Q rises above K and the reverse reaction runs.","calc":["Q = [FeSCN²⁺]/([Fe³⁺][SCN⁻])","[SCN⁻] ↓ → Q > K → shift left"]},{"text":"Shifting left uses up FeSCN²⁺, the red species; more product would need added reactant, not removed reactant.","strike":["Deeper red, as more FeSCN²⁺ forms"]},{"text":"K is fixed at constant temperature, yet the mixture still shifts; and Ag⁺ acts through SCN⁻, which is in the K expression.","strike":["No change, since K stays fixed","No change, since Ag⁺ is not in the K expression"]},{"text":"Answer: paler red, as FeSCN²⁺ breaks apart."}],"trap":"Reading any addition as a push toward products: Ag⁺ is added, but what it does is remove SCN⁻, a reactant."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"sits at equilibrium","note":"Q = K before the stress"},{"t":"Adding Ag⁺","note":"the stress"},{"t":"precipitates SCN⁻","note":"a reactant is removed"}]},{"text":"**Stress and response.** Removing a reactant makes Q > K, so the reverse reaction runs until Q = K again: the shift remakes some of what was removed.","calc":["SCN⁻ removed → Q > K → shift left","FeSCN²⁺ (red) ↓ → color fades"]},{"text":"**Also:** the opposite stress gives the opposite color change. Adding extra Fe³⁺ or SCN⁻ makes Q < K, the system shifts right, and the red deepens."},{"text":"**Also:** removing a product shifts right. In an esterification, RCOOH + R′OH ⇌ RCOOR′ + H₂O, pulling water out (a drying agent or distillation) makes Q < K and **raises the ester yield**.","calc":["remove H₂O → Q falls below K","→ shift right → more ester"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** removing a reactant (even indirectly, by precipitating it) shifts an equilibrium toward reactants, and adding one shifts it toward products (Le Châtelier's principle); K changes only with temperature."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-04a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe5-cp:4#1","pe5-cp:4#2"],"q":"Separate assays at fixed T and pH give Ki = 5.0 μM for the inhibitor and an intrinsic kcat/KM of 4.0 × 10⁶ M⁻¹s⁻¹ for the enzyme. If the assay [I] is raised from 15 μM to 45 μM, what are Ki and kcat/KM?","choices":["5.0 μM; 4.0 × 10⁶ M⁻¹s⁻¹","15 μM; 4.0 × 10⁶ M⁻¹s⁻¹","5.0 μM; 1.3 × 10⁶ M⁻¹s⁻¹","1.7 μM; 4.0 × 10⁶ M⁻¹s⁻¹"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"copied","why":"15 μM is the stem's starting [I], not Ki; a binding constant doesn't take on the assay concentration."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Ki doesn't scale down with [I] either; 1.7 μM is just 5.0 ÷ 3."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Intrinsic kcat/KM belongs to the enzyme at fixed T and pH; more inhibitor slows the observed rate, not this constant."}],"rule":"Ki and intrinsic kcat/KM are constants at fixed T and pH; changing [I] changes the observed rate, never the constants.","exp":"**Ki** (the EI dissociation constant) and **kcat/KM** (the enzyme's catalytic efficiency) are **constants** of the enzyme–inhibitor pair at a fixed temperature and pH. Tripling [I] inhibits more enzyme and slows the observed rate, but both values stay **5.0 μM and 4.0 × 10⁶ M⁻¹s⁻¹**, read straight from the data.","how":{"ask":"Which quantities change when [I] changes, and which are fixed by the enzyme, the inhibitor, T and pH?","know":"• **Ki** = [E][I]/[EI], an equilibrium constant: set by the enzyme, inhibitor, T and pH, not by how much inhibitor is added.\n• **KM, kcat and kcat/KM** are properties of the enzyme with its substrate under fixed conditions.\n• Raising [I] changes the **observed** rate (and the apparent KM or Vmax), not these constants.\n• The apparent kcat/KM measured with a competitive inhibitor present falls as [I] rises; the intrinsic constant does not.","steps":[{"text":"Temperature and pH stay fixed; only the inhibitor concentration changes.","hl":"at fixed T and pH","calc":["[I]: 15 μM → 45 μM (× 3)"]},{"text":"As a dissociation constant, Ki does not scale with [I]: tripling it or dividing it by 3 confuses the constant with the concentration.","strike":["15 μM; 4.0 × 10⁶ M⁻¹s⁻¹","1.7 μM; 4.0 × 10⁶ M⁻¹s⁻¹"]},{"text":"kcat/KM belongs to the enzyme itself; dividing it by 3 treats the slower observed rate as a new constant.","strike":["5.0 μM; 1.3 × 10⁶ M⁻¹s⁻¹"]},{"text":"Read both constants straight from the given data: Ki for the inhibitor and the intrinsic kcat/KM for the enzyme.","hl":"Ki = 5.0 μM for the inhibitor"},{"text":"Answer: 5.0 μM; 4.0 × 10⁶ M⁻¹s⁻¹."}],"trap":"Scaling a constant with [I] (Ki × 3 = 15 μM): more inhibitor means more inhibition, not a new Ki."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"fixed T and pH","note":"held fixed"},{"t":"Ki = 5.0 μM","note":"a constant"},{"t":"intrinsic kcat/KM","note":"the enzyme's own constant"},{"t":"raised from 15 μM to 45 μM","note":"3× more inhibitor"}]},{"text":"**Constants versus conditions.** A constant is fixed by the identity of the molecules and by T and pH; a concentration is something the experimenter sets.","calc":["Ki = [E][I]/[EI]: more I → more EI, same ratio"]},{"text":"**Read, don't recompute.** When a question asks for a constant under a new [I], copy the value from the data table unchanged."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Ki, KM, kcat and kcat/KM are constants of the enzyme–inhibitor pair at fixed T and pH, so raising [I] changes the observed rate but none of these values."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-05a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:5#1"],"q":"An alcohol dehydrogenase reduces 2-butanone, shown, transferring a hydride from NADH and a proton from water to its C=O. Which product forms?","fig":"cpt-pe5-2-butanone","choices":["2-Butanol","1-Butanol","Butane","Butanoic acid"],"choiceFigs":["cpt-pe5-2-butanol","1-butanol","cpt-pe5-butane","cpt-pe5-butanoic-acid"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Direction: NADH reduces, but an acid is an oxidation product."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"1-Butanol has its OH on carbon 1, which never held the oxygen; the H adds to the existing C=O carbon."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Removing the O entirely takes 4 e⁻; one hydride plus one H⁺ adds only 2 e⁻."}],"rule":"Hydride + H⁺ on a ketone's C=O gives an alcohol on that same carbon; the carbon chain and the O position don't move.","exp":"The hydride and the proton add **H₂ across the C=O**, so C2 becomes CH(OH) with the same CH₃ and CH₂CH₃ on either side: **2-butanol, a secondary alcohol**. 1-Butanol moves the O to C1, butane would need 4 e⁻, and butanoic acid is an oxidation.","how":{"ask":"A hydride plus a proton add H₂ across the C=O. Which product keeps the oxygen, now as OH, on the carbon that held it?","know":"• **Reduction** of a carbonyl adds 2 e⁻ + 2 H⁺ (here H⁻ from NADH and H⁺ from water) across C=O; no C–C bond is made or broken.\n• A **ketone** carbon has two C neighbors, so it becomes a **secondary** alcohol; an **aldehyde** carbon has one, so it becomes a **primary** alcohol.\n• Removing the O altogether (to an alkane) takes 2 more electrons; a carboxylic acid is an oxidation.","steps":[{"text":"The C=O is on carbon 2, between a CH₃ and a CH₂CH₃: a ketone.","hl":"2-butanone"},{"text":"Put the H⁻ on the carbon and the H⁺ on the oxygen; C2 now holds OH, H, CH₃ and CH₂CH₃.","hl":"a hydride from NADH and a proton from water","calc":["CH₃–CO–CH₂CH₃ + H⁻ + H⁺ → CH₃–CH(OH)–CH₂CH₃","C2 oxidation state: **+2 → 0**, a gain of 2 e⁻"]},{"text":"1-Butanol has its OH on carbon 1, which never held the oxygen.","strike":["1-Butanol"]},{"text":"Butane has lost the O (4 e⁻ gained, not 2). Butanoic acid carries more bonds to O, on C1: an oxidation, not a reduction.","strike":["Butane","Butanoic acid"]},{"text":"Answer: 2-butanol, a secondary alcohol."}],"trap":"Choosing 1-butanol because it is also an alcohol; adding H atoms never moves the O to a different carbon."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"reduces","note":"gain of electrons"},{"t":"2-butanone","note":"ketone at C2"},{"t":"a hydride from NADH and a proton from water","note":"2 e⁻ + 2 H⁺ in all"}]},{"text":"**Count what is added.** A hydride is H with 2 electrons; a proton is H⁺ with none. Together they make H₂: 2 e⁻ + 2 H⁺."},{"text":"**Open one bond of the C=O.** The H⁻ bonds to the carbon and the H⁺ to the oxygen, leaving C–OH on the same carbon."},{"text":"**Classify the alcohol.** The C–OH carbon has two carbon neighbors, so it is secondary. An aldehyde (CHO at a chain end) would give a primary alcohol instead."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** 2 e⁻ + 2 H⁺ on a C=O gives H–C–OH on the same carbon: a ketone becomes a secondary alcohol, an aldehyde a primary alcohol."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-06a","s":"Metabolism & Bioenergetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:6#1","pe5-cp:6#2","pe5-cp:6#3"],"q":"In the FAD structure drawn here, atoms W–Z mark four regions. Which labeled atom lies in the ring system that riboflavin, FMN and FAD all contain?","fig":"cpt-pe5-fad-parts","choices":["W","X","Y","Z"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"X is in adenine, part of the AMP half that only FAD has."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Y is the ribose ring O of the AMP half, another FAD-only piece."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Z is a phosphorus outside any ring, and riboflavin has no phosphate at all."}],"rule":"Riboflavin, FMN and FAD share only the flavin ring (three fused six-rings, four N); phosphates and the AMP part are added on.","exp":"All three flavin cofactors are built on the **isoalloxazine ring**: three fused six-membered rings with four N, where W sits. FMN adds a phosphate (Z) to riboflavin, and only FAD adds the AMP part, with its ribose (Y) and **adenine (X)**.","how":{"ask":"Which part of FAD is the flavin, the piece common to all three cofactors, and which labeled atom sits in it?","know":"• **Riboflavin** (vitamin B₂) = isoalloxazine ring + ribityl chain. **FMN** = riboflavin + one phosphate. **FAD** = FMN + AMP (a second phosphate, ribose and adenine).\n• **Isoalloxazine**: three fused six-membered rings in a row holding four N and two C=O; it carries the electrons.\n• Adenine is a purine (a six- fused to a five-membered ring); among the three cofactors only FAD has it.","steps":[{"text":"Find the three fused rings with four ring N and two C=O: the isoalloxazine. W, highlighted, is one of its nitrogens.","hl":"riboflavin, FMN and FAD all contain","fig":"cpt-pe5-fad-flavin"},{"text":"Build the three cofactors by adding pieces.","calc":["riboflavin = isoalloxazine + ribityl chain","FMN = riboflavin + phosphate","FAD = FMN + AMP (phosphate + ribose + adenine)"]},{"text":"X is in adenine and Y is the ring O of ribose: both belong to the AMP half, which only FAD has.","strike":["X","Y"]},{"text":"Z is a phosphorus outside any ring, and riboflavin has no phosphate at all.","strike":["Z"]},{"text":"Answer: W, on the isoalloxazine ring."}],"trap":"Choosing the adenine because it is also a fused, nitrogen-rich ring system; it has only two rings and comes with FAD's AMP part alone."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"FAD","note":"flavin adenine dinucleotide"},{"t":"atoms W–Z mark four regions","note":"one label per part"},{"t":"riboflavin, FMN and FAD all contain","note":"the shared core"}]},{"text":"**Recognize the flavin.** Three six-membered rings fused in a row, four ring N and two C=O: the isoalloxazine ring, the redox part of every flavin cofactor."},{"text":"**Also:** read the name, flavin **adenine** dinucleotide. Adenine (X) and its ribose (Y) arrive only with the AMP that FAD adds to FMN; riboflavin and FMN have no adenine."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** riboflavin, FMN and FAD share the isoalloxazine ring (three fused rings, four N); FMN adds a phosphate, and only FAD adds AMP with its adenine."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-06b","s":"Metabolism & Bioenergetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:6#4"],"q":"Four cofactor fragments are shown. Which one is the lipid-soluble carrier that shuttles electrons within the inner mitochondrial membrane?","choices":["Flavin ring","Nicotinamide ring","Adenine","Ubiquinone"],"choiceFigs":["cpt-pe5-lumiflavin","cpt-pe5-nicotinamide-ring","adenine","cpt-pe5-ubiquinone"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"The flavin ring carries electrons but is polar with no tail; it works bound to enzymes as FAD or FMN."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Nicotinamide (NAD⁺) is polar and water-soluble, so it works in the matrix, not the membrane."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Adenine carries no electrons in these cofactors; it is a binding handle."}],"rule":"A carrier that moves inside a membrane must be lipid-soluble: look for a redox ring with a long hydrocarbon tail.","exp":"**Ubiquinone** (coenzyme Q) has a quinone ring, two C=O across from each other, that takes 2 e⁻ + 2 H⁺, and a long **isoprenoid tail** that dissolves it in the membrane. The flavin and nicotinamide rings are polar and work bound to enzymes or in solution; adenine carries no electrons.","how":{"ask":"Which drawing has a six-membered ring with two C=O across from each other and a long hydrocarbon tail?","know":"• **Ubiquinone (CoQ)**: a quinone ring (six-membered, two C=O para to each other, plus two OCH₃) carrying a long isoprenoid tail (10 units in humans; shortened in the drawing).\n• The tail makes it lipid-soluble, so it diffuses within the membrane, carrying electrons from complexes I and II to complex III.\n• The flavin (three fused rings, four N), nicotinamide (a pyridinium ring with CONH₂) and adenine (two fused rings, five N) are polar.","steps":[{"text":"A membrane carrier must dissolve in the bilayer, so look for a redox ring on a long nonpolar tail.","hl":"lipid-soluble carrier"},{"text":"The ubiquinone drawing has a ring with two C=O para to each other and a branched C–H tail built from repeating isoprene units."},{"text":"The flavin and nicotinamide rings carry electrons too, but they are polar and have no tail; they work bound to enzymes (FAD, FMN) or dissolved in the matrix (NAD⁺).","strike":["Flavin ring","Nicotinamide ring"]},{"text":"Adenine is a nucleobase handle that carries no electrons, and it has no tail.","strike":["Adenine"]},{"text":"Answer: ubiquinone."}],"trap":"Choosing the flavin because it also takes 2 e⁻ + 2 H⁺; it stays bound to its protein and cannot roam through the membrane."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Four cofactor fragments","note":"four rings to compare"},{"t":"lipid-soluble","note":"needs a long oily tail"},{"t":"inner mitochondrial membrane","note":"electron transport chain"}]},{"text":"**Spot a quinone.** A six-membered ring with two C=O directly across from each other (1,4)."},{"text":"**Spot the tail.** A zigzag of C and H with a methyl branch every four carbons (isoprene units) is oily; it anchors the molecule in the middle of the bilayer."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a quinone ring plus a long isoprenoid tail is ubiquinone (CoQ), the lipid-soluble electron carrier of the membrane."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-07a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:7#1"],"q":"A binding assay contains 18 mM MgCl₂, 20 mM HEPES buffer and 4.0 μM receptor protein. About how many Cl⁻ ions are present per receptor molecule?","choices":["9000","4500","9","4.5"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"units","why":"Way too small: skips the 1000-fold mM → μM factor and also counts one Cl⁻ per formula."},{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"Divides mM by μM without converting; 1 mM = 1000 μM."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"Counts one Cl⁻ per MgCl₂; each formula unit releases two, so multiply by 2."}],"rule":"Ions per molecule = (salt conc × ions per formula) ÷ protein conc, in the same units. Round: 2 × 18 mM ÷ 4 μM, with 1 mM = 1000 μM.","exp":"Only MgCl₂ supplies Cl⁻, two per formula unit: [Cl⁻] = 2 × 18 mM = 36 mM = 36,000 μM. **Divide in the same units:** 36,000 μM ÷ 4.0 μM = **9000** Cl⁻ per receptor.","how":{"ask":"Find the chloride concentration, put both in the same unit, then divide.","know":"• In one solution, a **particle ratio = concentration ratio**, because the volume cancels.\n• Count only the salt that supplies the ion, times the ions per formula unit (MgCl₂ → 2 Cl⁻).\n• Put both in one unit first: 1 mM = 1000 μM.","steps":[{"text":"Only the magnesium salt contains chloride; the buffer is a distractor.","hl":"18 mM MgCl₂","calc":["[Cl⁻] = 2 × 18 mM = 36 mM"]},{"text":"Convert to the protein's unit.","calc":["36 mM × 1000 μM/mM = 36,000 μM"]},{"text":"Divide.","calc":["36,000 μM ÷ 4.0 μM = **9000**"]},{"text":"4500 counts one Cl⁻ per MgCl₂, 9 divides mM by μM without converting, and 4.5 makes both mistakes.","strike":["4500","9","4.5"]},{"text":"Answer: about 9000 Cl⁻ ions per receptor."}],"trap":"Dividing 36 by 4.0 directly: the two numbers are in different units (mM and μM)."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and its unit.","marks":[{"t":"18 mM MgCl₂","note":"2 Cl⁻ each"},{"t":"20 mM HEPES","note":"no Cl⁻"},{"t":"4.0 μM","note":"different unit"}]},{"text":"**Count the ion.** Each MgCl₂ gives two Cl⁻.","calc":["2 × 18 mM = 36 mM Cl⁻"]},{"text":"**Match the units, then divide.**","calc":["36 mM = 36,000 μM","36,000 ÷ 4.0 = 9000 per receptor"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a ratio of particles in one solution is the ratio of their concentrations in the same unit, counting ions per formula unit."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-08a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:8#1"],"q":"In a marine bacterium, a membrane enzyme passes electrons from NADH to a quinone, an exergonic step that releases little heat. Where does the energy go?","choices":["Pumping Na⁺ outward","Re-forming NADH","Speeding the electrons","Warming the cytoplasm"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Direction: re-forming NADH reverses an exergonic step; it costs energy rather than storing it."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Electrons hop between carriers; their speed isn't where redox energy is stored."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"The step releases little heat, so the energy can't be warming the cytoplasm."}],"rule":"Energy from a membrane redox step that isn't lost as heat is stored, usually as an ion gradient pumped across the membrane.","exp":"ΔG for the transfer is fixed by its start and end states, so the energy cannot simply vanish. The enzyme **couples** the electron transfer to **pumping Na⁺ out against its gradient**, storing the free energy as an ion gradient instead of releasing it as heat.","how":{"ask":"Decide what work an exergonic membrane redox step can do, so that its energy does not come out as heat.","know":"• An exergonic step (ΔG < 0) can drive an uphill one if one enzyme **couples** them.\n• Membrane redox complexes couple electron flow to **moving ions (H⁺ or Na⁺) up their gradient**, storing energy as an electrochemical gradient.\n• Energy that does work is not released as heat.","steps":[{"text":"The step is exergonic, and it happens inside a membrane enzyme.","hl":"a membrane enzyme passes electrons from NADH to a quinone"},{"text":"Little heat means the free energy did work instead; the work a membrane enzyme can do is moving ions across the membrane.","hl":"releases little heat"},{"text":"In many marine bacteria the ion is Na⁺: pumping it out builds a Na⁺ gradient that later drives ATP synthesis or the flagellum."},{"text":"Re-forming NADH would run the step backward and cost energy; warming the cytoplasm is exactly the heat that is missing.","strike":["Re-forming NADH","Warming the cytoplasm"]},{"text":"The electrons end up bound to the quinone; their speed is not a store of energy.","strike":["Speeding the electrons"]},{"text":"Answer: pumping Na⁺ outward."}],"trap":"Looking for an enzyme trick that makes the reaction release less energy; catalysts never change ΔG, so the energy must go into work."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"membrane enzyme","note":"can move ions across"},{"t":"exergonic step","note":"ΔG < 0: energy available"},{"t":"releases little heat","note":"energy went somewhere else"}]},{"text":"**Energy coupling:** an exergonic reaction can drive an uphill process when one enzyme links them; the energy is then stored, not lost as heat."},{"text":"**In membranes, the uphill process is ion pumping.** Mitochondria pump H⁺; many marine bacteria pump Na⁺. Either way the gradient stores the energy for later work."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** when a membrane redox step releases far less heat than its ΔG, the energy was coupled to pumping ions (H⁺ or Na⁺) against their gradient."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-09a","s":"Fluids","disc":"physics","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:9#1"],"q":"An open tank holds oil (ρ = 800 kg/m³) to a depth of 5.0 m. With P_atm = 101 kPa and g = 9.8 m/s², what is the absolute pressure at the bottom?","choices":["140 kPa","39 kPa","101 kPa","62 kPa"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Sign: going deeper adds pressure, so the bottom can't be below 101 kPa."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"That is the gauge pressure from the oil alone; absolute pressure also adds the air pressing on the open surface."},{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"101 kPa is the given surface value; 5 m of oil must add something."}],"rule":"Absolute P = P_atm + ρgh, always above P_atm in an open tank. Round ρgh: 800 × 10 × 5 = 40 kPa, then add the air.","exp":"**P = P_atm + ρgh.** The oil adds ρgh = 800 × 9.8 × 5.0 = 39,200 Pa ≈ 39 kPa on top of the air's 101 kPa, so P ≈ **140 kPa**. The 39 kPa alone is the gauge pressure.","how":{"ask":"Add the pressure of the oil column to the atmospheric pressure on the open surface.","know":"• **Absolute pressure: P = P_atm + ρgh**; **gauge pressure = ρgh** alone.\n• SI units: ρ in kg/m³, g in m/s² and h in m give Pa.\n• 1 kPa = 1000 Pa.","steps":[{"text":"Collect ρ, h and g in SI units.","hl":"to a depth of 5.0 m","calc":["ρ = 800 kg/m³, h = 5.0 m, g = 9.8 m/s²"]},{"text":"Find the pressure of the oil column.","calc":["ρgh = 800 × 9.8 × 5.0","ρgh = 39,200 Pa ≈ **39 kPa**"]},{"text":"Add the atmosphere pressing on the open surface.","hl":"absolute pressure","calc":["P = P_atm + ρgh","P = 101 kPa + 39 kPa","P ≈ **140 kPa**"]},{"text":"39 kPa is the gauge pressure (the oil alone); 101 kPa is the surface value, ignoring the oil.","strike":["39 kPa","101 kPa"]},{"text":"62 kPa subtracts the oil's pressure from the air's; going deeper always adds pressure.","strike":["62 kPa"]},{"text":"Answer: 140 kPa."}],"trap":"Stopping at ρgh: that is the gauge pressure; absolute pressure also counts the atmosphere on the open surface."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the givens**.","marks":[{"t":"open tank","note":"air pushes on the surface"},{"t":"800 kg/m³","note":"density ρ"},{"t":"5.0 m","note":"depth h"},{"t":"absolute pressure","note":"include P_atm"}]},{"text":"**Pressure grows with depth** because each layer carries the weight of the fluid above it, which adds ρgh."},{"text":"**Compute in SI units**, then add the air.","calc":["800 × 9.8 × 5.0 = 39,200 Pa","39.2 kPa + 101 kPa ≈ 140 kPa"]},{"text":"**Gauge versus absolute:** a gauge reads only the excess over the air, ρgh; absolute pressure adds P_atm back."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** absolute pressure at depth h is P = P_atm + ρgh; gauge pressure is ρgh alone; use kg/m³, m/s² and m to get Pa."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-09b","s":"Fluids","disc":"physics","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:9#2"],"q":"A wide basin, a thin tube and an upward-flaring cone, all open, hold ethanol. How do the pressures 30 cm below each surface compare?","choices":["Equal in all three","Highest in the basin","Highest in the tube","Highest in the cone"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"P = ρgh has no area or volume term; the basin's extra weight rests on extra floor area."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Narrowness isn't in ρgh either; a thin tube doesn't concentrate pressure."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"The cone's flare isn't in ρgh; only the depth below the surface and the liquid's density matter."}],"rule":"Pressure in a liquid depends only on its density, g and depth (P = ρgh), never on the container's shape or width.","exp":"**P = P_atm + ρgh** contains only the liquid's density, g and the depth below an open surface. Same ethanol, same 30 cm, same air above: the pressure is **equal in all three**, whatever their width, shape or volume.","how":{"ask":"Decide which quantities in the depth-pressure formula differ between the vessels.","know":"• **P = P_atm + ρgh**: only ρ, g, h and the surface pressure appear.\n• Width, shape and volume are not in the formula (the hydrostatic paradox).\n• Same liquid + same depth + same surface pressure → same pressure.","steps":[{"text":"List what the vessels share.","hl":"hold ethanol","calc":["ρ: same (ethanol)","h: same (30 cm)","surface: open to the air in all three"]},{"text":"Compute the pressure once; it serves all three.","calc":["ρgh = 789 kg/m³ × 9.8 m/s² × 0.30 m ≈ 2.3 kPa","P ≈ 101 kPa + 2.3 kPa ≈ 103 kPa in each vessel"]},{"text":"The basin holds more liquid, but its extra weight rests on extra floor area.","strike":["Highest in the basin"]},{"text":"A thin tube does not concentrate pressure, and in the cone the slanted walls carry the liquid that overhangs the bottom.","strike":["Highest in the tube","Highest in the cone"]},{"text":"Answer: equal in all three."}],"trap":"Thinking more liquid means more pressure; pressure depends on depth, not on how much liquid lies off to the side."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"all open","note":"same P_atm on each surface"},{"t":"hold ethanol","note":"same ρ"},{"t":"30 cm below each surface","note":"same depth h"}]},{"text":"**Pressure at depth: P = P_atm + ρgh.** Check which letters change between the vessels: none do."},{"text":"**Why shape drops out:** in a wide vessel the extra liquid presses on extra floor; in a flared one the slanted walls hold up the overhanging liquid."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** at equal depth in the same liquid under the same surface pressure, the pressure is equal, whatever the container's width, shape or volume."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-10a","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:10#1","pe5-cp:10#2"],"q":"A nucleus has a mass number of 40 and contains 22 neutrons. Which element is it?","choices":["Argon","Calcium","Potassium","Titanium"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"copied","why":"22 is the neutron count; titanium copies N into Z."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Calcium is Z = 20, so calcium-40 has 20 neutrons, not 22."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Potassium is Z = 19, so potassium-40 has 21 neutrons; it only shares the mass number."}],"rule":"Z = A − N, and Z alone names the element; never read the mass number or the neutron count as the atomic number.","exp":"**Z = A − N** = 40 − 22 = **18 protons**, and Z = 18 is **argon**. Calcium-40 (Z = 20) and potassium-40 (Z = 19) share the mass number but not the proton count; titanium is Z = 22, the neutron count.","how":{"ask":"Get the proton number from the mass number and neutrons, then name the element with that Z.","know":"• **Mass number A = protons (Z) + neutrons (N)**, so Z = A − N.\n• **Z alone identifies the element**; different elements can share a mass number (isobars such as ⁴⁰Ar, ⁴⁰K, ⁴⁰Ca).","steps":[{"text":"Pull out A and N.","hl":"mass number of 40 and contains 22 neutrons"},{"text":"Subtract.","calc":["Z = A − N = 40 − 22 = **18**"]},{"text":"Z = 18 is argon. Calcium (20) and potassium (19) only share A = 40; titanium (22) uses the neutron count as Z.","strike":["Calcium","Potassium","Titanium"]},{"text":"Answer: argon."}],"trap":"Picking calcium because ⁴⁰Ca is the familiar mass-40 nucleus: mass number never names the element."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"mass number of 40","note":"A = Z + N"},{"t":"22 neutrons","note":"N"}]},{"text":"**Split the mass number.** A counts protons plus neutrons.","calc":["Z = 40 − 22 = 18"]},{"text":"**Name by Z.** The periodic table orders elements by proton number: Z = 18 is argon."},{"text":"**Also:** ⁴⁰Ar, ⁴⁰K and ⁴⁰Ca all have A = 40 but Z = 18, 19 and 20, so they are different elements."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Z = A − N, and only Z names the element."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-11a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:11#1","pe5-cp:11#2","pe5-cp:11#3"],"q":"Each answer choice shows a molecule's bonds, without the lone pairs. Which molecule is polar?","choices":["Beryllium chloride","Sulfur trioxide","Carbon tetrafluoride","Nitrogen trichloride"],"choiceFigs":["cpt-pe5-becl2","cpt-pe5-so3","cpt-pe5-cf4","cpt-pe5-ncl3"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"BeCl₂ is linear with two identical bonds, so the dipoles cancel."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"SO₃ is trigonal planar with three equivalent S–O bonds and no lone pair on S, so its dipoles cancel."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"CF₄ is a symmetric tetrahedron with four identical bonds: nonpolar."}],"rule":"Symmetric shapes with identical bonds (linear, trigonal planar, tetrahedral) are nonpolar; a lone pair on the center (pyramidal, bent) makes it polar.","exp":"In **NCl₃**, nitrogen holds three N–Cl bonds plus **a lone pair** (4 domains), which makes a pyramid whose bond dipoles cannot cancel. BeCl₂ is linear, SO₃ trigonal planar and CF₄ tetrahedral, each with identical bonds and no lone pair on the middle atom, so their dipoles cancel.","how":{"ask":"Find each shape by VSEPR, then ask whether the bond dipoles cancel.","know":"• **VSEPR**: count bonding domains and lone pairs around the middle atom.\n• Identical bonds in a **symmetric shape** (linear, trigonal planar, tetrahedral) cancel: nonpolar.\n• A **central-atom lone pair** (trigonal pyramidal, bent) leaves a net dipole: polar.","steps":[{"text":"Assign each shape.","hl":"Which molecule is polar?","calc":["BeCl₂: 2 domains, 0 lone pairs → linear","SO₃: 3 domains, 0 lone pairs → trigonal planar","CF₄: 4 domains, 0 lone pairs → tetrahedral","NCl₃: 3 bonds + 1 lone pair → trigonal pyramidal"]},{"text":"The three symmetric shapes with identical bonds cancel their dipoles.","strike":["Beryllium chloride","Sulfur trioxide","Carbon tetrafluoride"]},{"text":"Answer: nitrogen trichloride."}],"trap":"Judging from the drawing alone: the drawings omit lone pairs, so count N's five valence electrons to find the hidden pair."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"without the lone pairs","note":"count them yourself"},{"t":"polar","note":"net dipole ≠ 0"}]},{"text":"**VSEPR first:** count the bonding domains plus lone pairs around the middle atom; that sets the shape."},{"text":"**Count electrons on the central atom.** N has 5 valence electrons: 3 go to bonds, 2 stay as a lone pair.","calc":["N: 5 − 3 = 2 → one lone pair"]},{"text":"**Shape decides cancellation.** A lone pair pushes the three N–Cl bonds into a pyramid, so their dipoles add up instead of canceling."},{"text":"**Also:** symmetric shapes with identical bonds, such as linear BeCl₂, trigonal planar SO₃ and tetrahedral CF₄, are nonpolar even though each bond is polar."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** symmetric shapes with identical bonds cancel their dipoles; a central-atom lone pair leaves a net dipole."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-12a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:12#1","pe5-cp:12#2","pe5-cp:12#3","pe6-cp:53#1"],"q":"A gas under a free piston is at 50 °C, 2.4 atm and 0.80 mol. All else constant, which change would NOT double its volume?","choices":["Heating it to 100 °C","Doubling the moles","Dropping P to 1.2 atm","Heating it to 646 K"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"V ∝ n, so doubling the moles of gas doubles V."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"V ∝ 1/P, and 2.4 → 1.2 atm halves P, so V doubles."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"50 °C is 323 K, and 646 K is twice that: T doubles in kelvin, so V doubles."}],"rule":"Gas laws use kelvin: add 273 before comparing temperatures. V ∝ nT/P, so only a doubled n, doubled K or halved P doubles V.","exp":"From PV = nRT, **V ∝ nT/P** with T in **kelvin**. 50 °C is 323 K and 100 °C is 373 K, so V grows only by 373/323 ≈ 1.15. Doubling n (0.80 → 1.60 mol), halving P (2.4 → 1.2 atm) or doubling the kelvin temperature (323 → 646 K) each doubles V.","how":{"ask":"Turn each change into a factor on n, T (in kelvin) or P, and find the one that does not give ×2.","know":"• **Ideal gas: V = nRT/P**, so **V ∝ nT/P**.\n• T must be in **kelvin**: T(K) = T(°C) + 273.\n• ×2 n or ×2 T(K) doubles V; ×2 P halves V; ×½ P doubles V.","steps":[{"text":"Convert the starting temperature to kelvin.","hl":"50 °C","calc":["T = 50 + 273 = **323 K**"]},{"text":"Heating to 100 °C: find the kelvin factor.","hl":"Heating it to 100 °C","calc":["100 °C = 373 K","factor = 373 ÷ 323 ≈ **1.15**","V rises about 15%, not ×2"]},{"text":"Heating to 646 K is 2 × 323 K: T doubles in kelvin, so V doubles.","calc":["646 ÷ 323 = 2"],"strike":["Heating it to 646 K"]},{"text":"Doubling the amount takes n from 0.80 to 1.60 mol, and V ∝ n.","strike":["Doubling the moles"]},{"text":"Dropping P to 1.2 atm halves P; V ∝ 1/P, so V doubles. (Raising P to 4.8 atm would halve V instead.)","strike":["Dropping P to 1.2 atm"]},{"text":"Answer: heating it to 100 °C."}],"trap":"Doubling the Celsius number: 50 → 100 °C is only 323 → 373 K, a 15% change."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"free piston","note":"V is free to change"},{"t":"50 °C","note":"convert to K"},{"t":"NOT double","note":"find the odd one out"}]},{"text":"**Write V in terms of the others:** V = nRT/P, so V ∝ nT/P.","calc":["V₂/V₁ = (n₂/n₁)(T₂/T₁)(P₁/P₂)"]},{"text":"**Also: pressure sits in the denominator.** Doubling P halves V; halving P doubles V.","calc":["P ×2 → V ×½","P ×½ → V ×2"]},{"text":"**Also: always use kelvin.** Doubling a Celsius temperature does not double the kelvin temperature.","calc":["50 °C → 323 K","100 °C → 373 K","373/323 ≈ 1.15"]},{"text":"**Also:** with P and n both fixed, V ∝ T in kelvin (Charles's law). Cooling shrinks a gas sample even when none is lost: the same moles simply fill less space.","calc":["60 °C → 0 °C: 333 K → 273 K","V₂ = V₁ × 273/333 ≈ 0.82 V₁, about 18% smaller","the °C ratio (0/60) would wrongly give zero"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** V ∝ nT/P with T in kelvin: ×2 n or ×2 T(K) doubles V, ×2 P halves it, and doubling °C does not double V."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-13a","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:13#1","pe6-cp:20#1"],"q":"Silver has an average atomic mass of 107.87 u and two stable isotopes, ¹⁰⁷Ag and ¹⁰⁹Ag. How many neutrons does a ¹⁰⁹Ag nucleus contain?","choices":["62","60","61","47"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"copied","why":"Uses the other isotope's mass number, 107; take A from this nucleus's own superscript."},{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"Uses the rounded average atomic mass, 108; one nucleus has its own whole-number A."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"47 is Z, the proton count, not the neutron count."}],"rule":"N = A − Z, with A from the isotope's own superscript and Z from the periodic table; the average atomic mass is never a nucleus's A.","exp":"Silver is Z = 47 on the periodic table. **N = A − Z** = 109 − 47 = **62**. The ¹⁰⁷Ag isotope has 60, two fewer, matching its mass number being 2 lower; the average mass 107.87 u describes the mixture, not one nucleus.","how":{"ask":"Read Z from the periodic table, then subtract it from the isotope's mass number.","know":"• **N = A − Z**, with A the isotope's mass number (the superscript) and Z from the periodic table.\n• Isotopes of one element share Z, so their neutron counts differ by exactly their difference in A.\n• The **average atomic mass** is a weighted mean of the mixture, never a mass number.","steps":[{"text":"Use the isotope's own mass number.","hl":"¹⁰⁹Ag nucleus","calc":["A = 109; Z(Ag) = 47"]},{"text":"Subtract.","calc":["N = 109 − 47 = **62**"]},{"text":"60 belongs to ¹⁰⁷Ag (107 − 47), 61 uses the rounded average mass (108 − 47), and 47 is the proton count.","strike":["60","61","47"]},{"text":"Answer: 62 neutrons."}],"trap":"Using the average atomic mass as if it were a mass number: 107.87 u describes the natural mix of isotopes."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"107.87 u","note":"average, not A"},{"t":"¹⁰⁹Ag","note":"A = 109"}]},{"text":"**Get Z.** Silver is element 47, so every Ag nucleus has 47 protons."},{"text":"**Subtract.**","calc":["N = A − Z = 109 − 47 = 62"]},{"text":"**Check with the other isotope.** Same Z, so A differs only by neutrons.","calc":["¹⁰⁷Ag: 107 − 47 = 60","109 − 107 = 2 = 62 − 60 ✓"]},{"text":"**Also:** two isotopes of one element share Z, so a mass number one lower means exactly one neutron fewer, and a lower neutron-to-proton ratio. Carbon-11, a PET tracer, against stable carbon-12:","calc":["Z(C) = 6 for both","¹¹C: N = 11 − 6 = 5; ¹²C: N = 12 − 6 = 6","N/Z: 5/6 ≈ 0.83 versus 6/6 = 1.00"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** neutrons = mass number − atomic number, with Z from the periodic table; the average atomic mass is never a mass number."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-14a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:14#1","pe5-cp:14#4"],"q":"4-Methylimidazole, shown, is a small model of histidine's aromatic side chain. What is its molecular formula?","fig":"cpt-pe5-4-methylimidazole","choices":["C₄H₅N₂","C₄H₆N₂","C₄H₇N₂","C₄H₁₀N₂"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"impossible","why":"Odd H with two N: a neutral molecule of C, H and an even number of N must have an even H count; this one forgets the N–H."},{"c":2,"tag":"impossible","why":"Odd H again, impossible for a neutral C, H, N₂ molecule; it adds an H to the N that already has three bonds."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Too many H: that is the saturated ring, but this aromatic ring keeps its two double bonds."}],"rule":"Count H atom by atom (C makes 4 bonds, N makes 3). With an even number of N, a neutral molecule has an even number of H.","exp":"The ring has five atoms, **3 C and 2 N**, plus a CH₃ carbon: **4 C**. Two ring carbons each hide **one H**, the ring carbon holding the methyl has none, the CH₃ has **3 H**, and the N drawn with H adds **one more**: 2 + 3 + 1 = 6, so **C₄H₆N₂**. Missing the N–H gives C₄H₅N₂, an H on both N gives C₄H₇N₂, and saturating the ring gives C₄H₁₀N₂.","how":{"ask":"How many carbons and nitrogens are there, and how many hidden H does each atom need to reach its full set of bonds?","know":"• Unlabeled corners and line ends are C; each C makes **4 bonds** and each neutral N makes **3**; missing bonds are hidden H.\n• An aromatic ring keeps its double bonds, so count them when filling each atom.\n• An H drawn on a heteroatom (N–H) counts too; an N with a single and a double ring bond needs no H.\n• With an even number of N, a neutral molecule has an even number of H.","steps":[{"text":"The five-membered ring has three C corners and two N (only one drawn with H); the line off the ring is a CH₃.","hl":"4-Methylimidazole, shown","calc":["C: 3 ring + 1 methyl = **4 C**","N: **2**"]},{"text":"Fill each atom to its full set of bonds.","calc":["2 ring C (2 ring bonds + 1 double-bond extra = 3) → **1 H each** = 2 H","ring C with CH₃: 4 bonds already → **0 H**","CH₃ → **3 H**","N–H → **1 H**; other N (single + double) → **0 H**","H total: 2 + 3 + 1 = **6** → **C₄H₆N₂**"]},{"text":"C₄H₅N₂ forgets the N–H, and C₄H₇N₂ adds an H to the N that already has three bonds.","strike":["C₄H₅N₂","C₄H₇N₂"]},{"text":"C₄H₁₀N₂ fills the ring as if both double bonds were gone (the saturated ring).","strike":["C₄H₁₀N₂"]},{"text":"Answer: C₄H₆N₂."}],"trap":"Giving both nitrogens an H: the N with a double bond already has three bonds, so only the drawn N–H counts."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"4-Methylimidazole","note":"5-membered ring, two N, one CH₃"},{"t":"aromatic","note":"double bonds stay"},{"t":"molecular formula","note":"count C, H, N"}]},{"text":"**Worked example: pyridine.** Its six-membered ring has 5 C corners and one N. Each C shows one single and one double ring bond (3 bonds), so it hides 1 H; the N has a single and a double ring bond (3 bonds), so it has none.","fig":"pyridine","calc":["5 C × 1 H = 5 H","N: 0 H","→ **C₅H₅N**"]},{"text":"**Same count here.** Two ring C hide 1 H each, the ring C bearing the methyl hides none, the CH₃ has 3, one N carries its drawn H, and the other N, like pyridine's, has three bonds and no H.","calc":["2 + 0 + 3 + 1 = 6 H","→ **C₄H₆N₂**"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** count every C and N, then give every C four bonds and every N three; the missing bonds are H, and only an N drawn with H carries one. A substituted ring carbon loses the H it would otherwise hide."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-16a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe5-cp:16#1","pe5-cp:16#2"],"q":"A protein label fluoresces green at 520 nm when it is excited at 470 nm. Which light could also make it give off the 520 nm emission?","choices":["Dim 430 nm light","Bright 560 nm light","Bright 610 nm light","Pulsed 680 nm light"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"560 nm is longer than the 520 nm emission, so each photon has less energy than the one the label must emit."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"610 nm photons carry less energy than 520 nm ones; brightness adds photons, not energy per photon."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"680 nm is longer still; pulsing changes timing, not the energy each photon carries."}],"rule":"Excitation must be at a shorter wavelength (more energy per photon) than the emission; brightness or pulsing never makes up for it.","exp":"In fluorescence part of the absorbed energy turns into heat first, so the **absorbed photon must carry more energy** than the 520 nm photon: a **shorter wavelength**. Only 430 nm qualifies; 560, 610 and 680 nm photons each carry too little energy, and brightness only adds more such photons.","how":{"ask":"Find the excitation light whose single photons carry more energy than one emitted 520 nm photon.","know":"• **E = hc/λ**: shorter λ means more energy per photon (E in eV ≈ 1240 ÷ λ in nm).\n• **Fluorescence** emits less energy than it absorbs; the difference is lost as heat (vibrational relaxation).\n• So the exciting λ must be **shorter** than the emitted λ (the shorter wavelength must also fall in the label's absorption band). Intensity only changes how many photons arrive, not the energy of each.","steps":[{"text":"The emitted photon sets the minimum energy each absorbed photon needs.","hl":"fluoresces green at 520 nm","calc":["E(520 nm) ≈ 1240 ÷ 520 ≈ **2.38 eV**"]},{"text":"Compare each candidate photon with that minimum.","calc":["430 nm: 1240 ÷ 430 ≈ 2.88 eV > 2.38 eV ✓","560 nm: ≈ 2.21 eV < 2.38 eV ✗","610 nm: ≈ 2.03 eV ✗; 680 nm: ≈ 1.82 eV ✗"]},{"text":"Longer wavelengths than the emission can never supply enough energy per photon, however bright or pulsed the beam.","strike":["Bright 560 nm light","Bright 610 nm light","Pulsed 680 nm light"]},{"text":"Answer: dim 430 nm light."}],"trap":"Thinking a brighter beam can make up the gap: brightness adds photons, but each one still has too little energy."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"520 nm","note":"emitted photon"},{"t":"470 nm","note":"known excitation, shorter"},{"t":"also make it","note":"same energy rule"}]},{"text":"**Energy runs downhill in fluorescence.** The molecule absorbs a photon, loses a little energy as heat, then emits a photon of lower energy.","calc":["E(absorbed) > E(emitted)"]},{"text":"**Turn energy into wavelength.** Since E = hc/λ, more energy means a shorter λ.","calc":["E(absorbed) > E(emitted) → λ(absorbed) < λ(emitted) = 520 nm"]},{"text":"**Also:** intensity counts photons per second; it cannot raise the energy of any single photon."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** fluorescence needs excitation at a shorter wavelength than the emission; longer-wavelength light never works, however intense."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-17a","s":"Stereochemistry","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:17#1"],"q":"Circular dichroism of proteins needs chiral centers. L-threonine, shown with atoms labeled W–Z, has two stereocenters: the unlabeled α-carbon and one labeled atom. Which one?","fig":"cpt-pe5-threonine-sites","choices":["W","X","Y","Z"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"The carboxyl carbon is trigonal planar with only three groups, so it can't be a stereocenter."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A CH₃ carbon has three identical H, so it can't be a stereocenter."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"The amine N carries two identical H and inverts freely, so it isn't a stereocenter."}],"rule":"A stereocenter is an sp³ atom with four different groups; any atom with two identical groups (H, H) or a double bond is out.","exp":"Besides the α-carbon, only the **β-carbon (X)** holds four different groups: H, OH, CH₃ and the α-carbon. The carboxyl carbon (W) is planar with three groups, the methyl carbon (Y) has three identical H, and the amine N (Z) has two identical H. Threonine's two stereocenters both make it chiral, which is what a CD signal needs.","how":{"ask":"Apart from the α-carbon, which labeled atom carries four different groups?","know":"• A **stereocenter** (chiral carbon) bears four different groups, so the molecule is not superimposable on its mirror image.\n• **CD** works because a chiral molecule absorbs the two circular polarizations of light by different amounts; in proteins the chiral α-carbons start it.\n• Every standard amino acid except **glycine** has a chiral α-carbon; **threonine** and **isoleucine** also have a chiral β-carbon.","steps":[{"text":"List the groups on each labeled atom; X is highlighted.","fig":"cpt-pe5-threonine-beta","calc":["W, carboxyl C: =O, OH, Cα → **3 groups, planar**","X, Cβ: H, OH, CH₃, Cα → **4 different**","Y, CH₃: three identical H","Z, N: H, H, Cα → **not a stereocenter**"]},{"text":"The methyl carbon carries three identical H, and the amine N carries two identical H and inverts freely.","strike":["Y","Z"]},{"text":"The carboxyl carbon is trigonal planar with only three groups.","strike":["W"]},{"text":"Answer: X, the β-carbon."}],"trap":"Picking the carboxyl carbon because its neighbors look varied; a planar carbon with only three groups cannot be a stereocenter."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Circular dichroism","note":"needs chirality"},{"t":"L-threonine","note":"Cα and Cβ both chiral"},{"t":"unlabeled α-carbon","note":"already counted"},{"t":"two stereocenters","note":"find the second"}]},{"text":"**Test each atom.** Count its groups, hidden H included; all four must differ."},{"text":"**Also:** the α-carbon (H, NH₂, COOH, side chain) is the stereocenter in every amino acid but glycine, whose α-carbon carries two H. Isoleucine, like threonine, adds a second one at Cβ."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a stereocenter is an sp³ carbon with four different groups; besides the α-carbon of every amino acid but glycine, threonine's β-carbon (H, OH, CH₃, Cα) is one too."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-18a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:18#1","pe5-cp:18#2"],"q":"A mutation changes an alanine in a protein's surface loop to serine at pH 7.0. Both are drawn. Which side-chain property changes?","fig":["alanine","serine"],"choices":["Charge at pH 7","Side-chain carbon count","Hydrogen bonding","Aromatic character"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Neither a methyl nor a CH₂OH ionizes at pH 7, so both side chains stay uncharged."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Both side chains have exactly one carbon (CH₃ vs CH₂OH)."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Neither Ala nor Ser has a ring, so aromatic character can't change."}],"rule":"Compare the two side chains piece by piece: carbons, rings, groups that ionize at pH 7, and O–H or N–H groups.","exp":"Serine's CH₂OH differs from alanine's CH₃ by one **O–H**, which can donate and accept **hydrogen bonds** and makes the side chain polar. Both side chains have one carbon and no ring, and the O–H (pKa ≈ 13) stays protonated at pH 7.0, so neither side chain is charged.","how":{"ask":"What does turning alanine's CH₃ into CH₂OH add, and which listed property does that new group alter?","know":"• Compare only the atoms that differ: serine's side chain is CH₂OH, alanine's is CH₃.\n• An O–H adds polarity and the ability to give and take hydrogen bonds; a CH₃ can do neither.\n• A group changes charge only by gaining or losing H⁺, and it keeps its H while pH < pKa. Serine's alcohol has pKa ≈ 13.","steps":[{"text":"The only difference is one O on the side chain: CH₃ becomes CH₂OH.","hl":"an alanine"},{"text":"The new O–H can donate an H bond (its H) and accept one (its O lone pairs); CH₃ can do neither. That property changes.","hl":"Hydrogen bonding"},{"text":"Count the side-chain carbons and look for a ring: one C in each, and neither is aromatic.","calc":["Ala side chain: –CH₃ → **1** C","Ser side chain: –CH₂OH → **1** C","no ring in either"],"strike":["Side-chain carbon count","Aromatic character"]},{"text":"Ask whether the new O–H ionizes at this pH.","hl":"pH 7.0","calc":["pH 7.0 ≪ pKa ≈ 13","[RO⁻]/[ROH] = 10^(7.0 − 13) = 10⁻⁶","→ O–H stays **neutral**; alanine's CH₃ is neutral too"],"strike":["Charge at pH 7"]},{"text":"Answer: hydrogen bonding, which the O–H adds."}],"trap":"Assuming any O–H brings a charge; an alcohol with pKa ≈ 13 stays protonated and neutral at pH 7.0, so the charge does not change, but its H-bonding does."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"an alanine","note":"side chain CH₃"},{"t":"serine","note":"side chain CH₂OH"},{"t":"pH 7.0","note":"compare with pKa"},{"t":"changes","note":"the one that differs"}]},{"text":"**One property at a time.** For carbon count, ring, H bonding and charge, ask whether one added O–H changes it."},{"text":"**Also:** when both side chains are uncharged at the stated pH, the substitution leaves the net charge the same, however much else differs."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** compare only the atoms that differ, one property at a time; an added O–H brings polarity and H bonding, and the charge changes only if a new group gains or loses H⁺ at that pH."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-19a","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe5-cp:19#1"],"q":"Denatured proteins of 12, 34, 78 and 145 kDa are boiled in SDS and loaded in one SDS-PAGE lane. Which band travels farthest from the well?","choices":["145 kDa","78 kDa","34 kDa","12 kDa"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"The heaviest chain is held back most by the gel, so 145 kDa stays nearest the well."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"78 kDa is the second heaviest; it can't outrun the smaller chains."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"34 kDa is still heavier than another chain in the lane, so it can't travel farthest."}],"rule":"SDS gives every chain the same charge per mass, so the gel sorts by size: the lighter the chain, the farther it runs.","exp":"SDS unfolds every chain and gives each about the same charge per mass, so the gel sorts by **mass alone**. The smallest chain, **12 kDa**, threads through the mesh fastest and runs farthest; the 145-kDa chain stays nearest the well.","how":{"ask":"In SDS-PAGE, what single property sets how far a band moves, and which way do small chains go?","know":"• **SDS** unfolds each chain and coats it with negative charge in proportion to its length, so every chain has about the same charge per mass.\n• The polyacrylamide gel is a sieve: **small chains slip through faster and run farther** from the well; large ones stay near the top.\n• Distance falls roughly with log(mass), so here only the order of the masses matters.","steps":[{"text":"SDS has removed differences in shape and charge density; chain mass is the only thing left to sort the bands.","hl":"boiled in SDS"},{"text":"Rank the masses; the lightest chain moves fastest through the mesh.","hl":"12, 34, 78 and 145 kDa","calc":["12 < 34 < 78 < 145 kDa","lightest → farthest; heaviest → nearest the well"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Distance run vs chain mass (illustrative)","x":{"label":"Chain mass (kDa)","min":0,"max":150,"step":30},"y":{"label":"Distance from well (cm)","min":0,"max":8,"step":2},"series":[{"name":"Band position","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[12,6.8],[34,4.6],[78,2.7],[145,1.2]]}]}},{"text":"The 145-kDa chain is the heaviest, so the sieve holds it back most: it stays closest to the well.","strike":["145 kDa"]},{"text":"The 78- and 34-kDa chains are in between, so their bands sit between the two extremes.","strike":["78 kDa","34 kDa"]},{"text":"Answer: 12 kDa, the lightest chain."}],"trap":"Picking the heaviest protein because it binds the most SDS; its charge grows with its length, so the pull per unit mass is the same and the sieve slows it most."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"12, 34, 78 and 145 kDa","note":"four chain masses"},{"t":"boiled in SDS","note":"unfolded, equal charge per mass"},{"t":"farthest from the well","note":"the fastest band"}]},{"text":"**What SDS does.** The detergent wraps each unfolded chain (roughly one SDS per two residues), so every chain carries the same charge-to-mass ratio and feels the same pull per gram."},{"text":"**What the gel does.** The mesh is a sieve: short chains thread through it quickly, long chains get tangled and lag behind."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** SDS-PAGE sorts denatured chains by mass alone; the smaller the chain, the farther it runs from the well."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-19b","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe5-cp:19#2"],"q":"A protease cuts a 90-kDa enzyme once, into 54- and 36-kDa pieces. Samples taken at 4, 12 and 40 min run on SDS-PAGE. How do later lanes differ?","choices":["90 kDa fainter; 54 and 36 kDa darker","90 kDa darker; 54 and 36 kDa fainter","90 kDa fainter; 144 kDa darker","90 kDa unchanged; 54 and 36 kDa unchanged"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"impossible","why":"A protease only shortens chains; no band heavier than the intact parent (like 144 kDa, parent plus a piece) can appear."},{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Direction flipped: the intact chain is used up, never made, so its band fades over time."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Each lane had a different digestion time, so the lanes can't be identical."}],"rule":"During a digest the parent band fades and the cut pieces grow; no band can be heavier than the parent.","exp":"Each sample was digested longer than the one before, so less **90-kDa** chain remains and more **54- and 36-kDa** pieces build up (54 + 36 = 90). A protease only shortens chains, so nothing heavier than the parent can appear.","how":{"ask":"As the protease works longer, what happens to the amount of intact chain and to the amount of each piece?","know":"• A protease hydrolyzes peptide bonds; one cut turns a chain into two shorter ones whose masses add up to the parent's.\n• A later sample has had more digestion time, so the intact band weakens and the piece bands strengthen from lane to lane.\n• A band's darkness tracks how much protein it holds; cutting never makes a chain heavier, so no band can appear above the parent.","steps":[{"text":"One cut splits each intact chain into two pieces whose masses sum to the parent.","hl":"into 54- and 36-kDa pieces","calc":["54 + 36 = **90 kDa** ✓"]},{"text":"Each later sample had more time with the protease: less intact chain is left and more pieces have formed.","hl":"4, 12 and 40 min","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Band strength in each lane (illustrative)","x":{"label":"Sampling time (min)","min":0,"max":40,"step":10},"y":{"label":"Share of total protein (%)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"90-kDa band","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,100],[4,82],[12,54],[40,15]]},{"name":"54- + 36-kDa bands","marker":"triangle-open","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[4,18],[12,46],[40,85]]}]}},{"text":"The intact chain is used up, never made, so its band cannot darken; and a cut cannot produce a band heavier than 90 kDa, such as 144 kDa (parent plus a piece).","strike":["90 kDa darker; 54 and 36 kDa fainter","90 kDa fainter; 144 kDa darker"]},{"text":"Identical lanes would mean no cutting between samples, but each sample was stopped at a different time. Answer: 90 kDa fainter; 54 and 36 kDa darker.","strike":["90 kDa unchanged; 54 and 36 kDa unchanged"]}],"trap":"Expecting identical lanes because the protease is stopped in every sample; each was stopped at a different time, so each holds a different mix."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"90-kDa enzyme","note":"the intact chain"},{"t":"54- and 36-kDa pieces","note":"products of one cut"},{"t":"4, 12 and 40 min","note":"one lane per time"}]},{"text":"**Check the masses.** One cut makes two pieces that add back to the whole.","calc":["54 + 36 = 90 kDa"]},{"text":"**Track amounts over time.** Intact chains get used up while pieces pile up, so the top band fades as the two lower bands darken."},{"text":"**Place the bands.** On the gel the 36-kDa piece runs lowest, the 54-kDa piece above it, and the intact chain highest."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in a digestion time course the parent band fades lane by lane while lower bands, whose masses add to the parent's, appear and darken."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-20a","s":"Metabolism & Bioenergetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:20#1","pe5-cp:20#2"],"q":"Hexokinase transfers a phosphoryl group from ATP (507 g/mol) to glucose (180 g/mol), giving ADP (427 g/mol) and glucose 6-phosphate. What molar mass does the glucose 6-phosphate have?","choices":["80 g/mol","260 g/mol","275 g/mol","687 g/mol"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"impossible","why":"The product is glucose plus a group, so it can't weigh less than glucose's 180 g/mol; 80 is only the moved group."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"Adds the whole ATP to glucose; first subtract ADP to find the small group that actually moves."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Adds a free phosphate ion (95 g/mol), but the group transferred is ATP minus ADP, not a whole ion."}],"rule":"Mass moved = ATP − ADP; add it to the acceptor. Round: 507 − 427, then + 180.","exp":"A transfer reaction only moves atoms, so **mass is conserved**: glucose + ATP = glucose 6-phosphate + ADP. The group that moves weighs **507 − 427 = 80 g/mol**, so the product is **180 + 80 = 260 g/mol**, and the combined mass on each side is 687 g/mol (a net change of zero).","how":{"ask":"Use mass conservation: what does the donor lose, and where does it go?","know":"• In a **group transfer** (phosphoryl, methyl, acetyl), atoms move from donor to acceptor; none are created or destroyed.\n• **substrate + donor = product + spent donor**, so **product = substrate + (donor − spent donor)**.\n• Total mass before equals total mass after: the reaction's net mass change is **zero**.","steps":[{"text":"Find the mass the donor loses.","hl":"ATP (507 g/mol)","calc":["ATP − ADP = 507 − 427 = **80 g/mol** transferred"]},{"text":"Add it to the acceptor.","hl":"glucose (180 g/mol)","calc":["glucose 6-phosphate = 180 + 80 = **260 g/mol**"]},{"text":"Check conservation on both sides.","calc":["left: 180 + 507 = 687 g/mol","right: 260 + 427 = 687 g/mol","net change = **zero**"]},{"text":"80 g/mol is only the moved group, 687 g/mol adds the whole ATP to glucose, and 275 g/mol adds a free phosphate ion (95 g/mol) instead of the mass actually transferred.","strike":["80 g/mol","687 g/mol","275 g/mol"]},{"text":"Answer: 260 g/mol."}],"trap":"Adding a whole phosphate ion's mass: the acceptor gains exactly what the donor loses, ATP − ADP."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"ATP (507 g/mol)","note":"donor before"},{"t":"glucose (180 g/mol)","note":"acceptor"},{"t":"ADP (427 g/mol)","note":"donor after"}]},{"text":"**Mass is conserved; the group just moves.** Whatever the donor loses, the acceptor gains.","calc":["product = substrate + (donor − spent donor)","= 180 + (507 − 427) = 260 g/mol"]},{"text":"**Also:** add up each side. Substrate + cofactor before equals product + used cofactor after, so the net mass change of the reaction is zero.","calc":["180 + 507 = 687","260 + 427 = 687"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in a group transfer, substrate + donor = product + spent donor, so the product's mass = substrate + (donor − spent donor) and the net mass change is zero."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-22a","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:22#1","pe5-cp:22#2"],"q":"A protein fused to glutathione S-transferase binds beads carrying glutathione. After a wash, which added solution releases it from the beads still folded and active?","choices":["Excess free glutathione","High-salt buffer, 1 M NaCl","pH 2.0 glycine buffer","Boiling 2% SDS buffer"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"The grip is a ligand in its binding site, not a charge pair, so salt leaves the protein on the beads."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"pH 2 acid unfolds most proteins, so whatever it releases isn't folded and active."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Boiling in SDS denatures proteins, so it can't release one still folded."}],"rule":"Elute an affinity tag gently by competition with its own ligand; heat, detergent and extreme pH release only unfolded protein.","exp":"GST binds glutathione **specifically** (affinity chromatography), so the fusion protein comes off only when something takes the beads' place in its binding site: **excess free glutathione competes** for the GST site and releases it intact. Salt does not break a specific ligand fit, and pH 2 or boiling SDS release it only by denaturing it.","how":{"ask":"What holds the fusion protein on the beads, and what can take the beads' place without harming the protein?","know":"• **Affinity chromatography**: the resin carries a partner that binds one target specifically; a free competitor for the same site elutes it gently.\n• High salt breaks ionic attraction (ion exchange), not a specific ligand fit.\n• Strong acid, SDS and heat unfold proteins: they free a bound protein only by destroying its fold.","steps":[{"text":"The GST part of the fusion binds its own substrate, glutathione, fixed on the beads: one specific partner.","hl":"binds beads carrying glutathione"},{"text":"Free glutathione fills the same GST site, so the protein trades the bead-bound ligand for a free one and floats off, unharmed.","hl":"Excess free glutathione"},{"text":"The grip is a ligand in its binding site, not a charge pair, so salt leaves the protein on the beads.","strike":["High-salt buffer, 1 M NaCl"]},{"text":"pH 2 and boiling SDS do pull the protein off, but only by unfolding it: not folded and active.","hl":"still folded and active","strike":["pH 2.0 glycine buffer","Boiling 2% SDS buffer"]},{"text":"Answer: excess free glutathione, a competitor."}],"trap":"Choosing high salt because it elutes ion-exchange columns; a specific ligand fit is not ionic, and the gentle release from an affinity resin is by a competitor for the binding site."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"glutathione S-transferase","note":"the tag (GST)"},{"t":"beads carrying glutathione","note":"partner on the resin: affinity"},{"t":"still folded and active","note":"no denaturing"}]},{"text":"**Affinity binding, competitive elution.** A resin that grabs one tag through its ligand is affinity chromatography; to release the protein gently, flood the column with the free ligand (or a mimic) so it competes for the same site.","calc":["specific partner on resin → affinity","free ligand in excess → competes → elutes intact","salt → elutes ion exchange, not affinity","acid, SDS, heat → elute only by unfolding"]},{"text":"**Also:** a His-tagged protein binds a Ni²⁺ resin through its histidine rings; to elute it, a small molecule that mimics the tag competes for the metal. Same logic: affinity capture, competitive release."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a resin that grabs one tag or ligand is affinity chromatography, and it releases the protein intact when an excess of the free ligand, or a small mimic of the tag, competes for the binding site."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-23a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe5-cp:23#1"],"q":"To pull a membrane transporter out of the bilayer and keep it folded and soluble in buffer, which compound shown should be added?","choices":["Dimethyl sulfoxide","Hexadecane","Glycerol","Dodecyl maltoside"],"choiceFigs":["cpt-pe5-dmso","cpt-pe5-hexadecane","p1-glycerol","cpt-pe5-dodecyl-maltoside"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"No oily tail: DMSO is a small polar solvent, not a detergent, so it can't coat a membrane protein."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"No polar head: a pure alkane won't dissolve in buffer, so it can't carry a protein into water."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Glycerol is all polar head with no oily tail; it mixes with water but can't grip the protein's buried surface."}],"rule":"To solubilize a membrane protein, use a mild detergent: one molecule with both a polar head and a long oily tail.","exp":"A detergent must be **amphipathic**. Dodecyl maltoside has a **12-carbon tail** that coats the transporter's oily surface and a **maltose head** that faces water, gently enough to keep the fold. DMSO and glycerol have no tail; hexadecane has no head.","how":{"ask":"Which drawing has, on one molecule, a long C–H chain and a polar head with many O–H groups?","know":"• A **detergent** is amphipathic: a long hydrophobic tail plus a polar head. Tails coat the protein's lipid-facing surface; heads face water, forming a micelle around it.\n• Nonionic detergents with sugar heads are mild: they replace the lipids without unfolding the protein.\n• A molecule that is only polar or only nonpolar cannot bridge the two environments.","steps":[{"text":"Two jobs: cover the transporter's oily belt and stay in water. That takes a tail and a head on one molecule.","hl":"keep it folded and soluble in buffer"},{"text":"Sort each drawing into tail and head.","calc":["dodecyl maltoside: tail **C₁₂**, head **two sugar rings, 7 O–H**","DMSO: tail **none**, small polar S=O","hexadecane: tail **C₁₆**, head **none**","glycerol: tail **none**, **3 O–H**"]},{"text":"DMSO and glycerol are all head: they mix with water but have nothing oily to grip the protein.","strike":["Dimethyl sulfoxide","Glycerol"]},{"text":"Hexadecane is all tail: it stays in the oily phase and cannot carry the protein into water.","strike":["Hexadecane"]},{"text":"Answer: dodecyl maltoside, a 12-carbon tail on a maltose head."}],"trap":"Choosing glycerol because its O–H groups make it very water-soluble; with no tail it cannot cover the transporter's hydrophobic surface."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"membrane transporter","note":"has an oily belt"},{"t":"out of the bilayer","note":"must reach lipid chains"},{"t":"soluble in buffer","note":"must face water"}]},{"text":"**Read each drawing.** A chain of only C and H is oily (the tail); O–H groups or charges are polar (the head)."},{"text":"**A detergent has both on one molecule.** Its tails cover the protein where it once met the lipid chains, and its heads keep the whole complex in water."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to keep a membrane protein in water, use an amphipathic detergent: a long hydrophobic tail plus a polar head on one molecule."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-25a","s":"Protein Structure","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe5-cp:25#1","pe5-cp:25#2"],"q":"A recombinant kinase is purified through three columns. Mass spectrometry gives the mass predicted from its gene, its GST tag is retained, and its kcat equals that of kinase isolated from liver. Which result best shows native folding?","choices":["Its kcat matches the liver enzyme","Its weight fits the gene sequence","Its GST tag survived purification","It went through three column steps"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Mass spectrometry weighs the chain, and an unfolded kinase has exactly the same mass."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A tag stays attached whether the chain is folded or not, so it says nothing about the fold."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Columns show purity, not structure; unfolded proteins pass through columns too."}],"rule":"Only a functional test, matching the native protein's activity, proves the fold; an unfolded chain keeps its mass, its tag and its purity.","exp":"Turnover needs a properly built **active site**, so a kcat equal to the liver kinase's shows a **native-like fold**. Mass, the retained tag and the column steps would look the same for an unfolded chain.","how":{"ask":"Which of these results would come out differently if the kinase were unfolded?","know":"• The **native fold** (tertiary structure) builds the active site; an unfolded chain keeps the same sequence, mass and tags.\n• **kcat** (turnover number) counts catalytic events per active site per second, so it needs a correctly formed site.\n• Mass, a retained tag and the number of purification steps report identity and purity, not structure.","steps":[{"text":"Look for the one measurement that depends on 3-D shape.","hl":"best shows native folding"},{"text":"Mass spectrometry weighs the chain, and an unfolded kinase has exactly the same mass.","hl":"Mass spectrometry gives the mass predicted from its gene","strike":["Its weight fits the gene sequence"]},{"text":"A tag stays attached, and a protein passes through columns, whether or not it is folded.","strike":["Its GST tag survived purification","It went through three column steps"]},{"text":"Catalysis needs a correctly built active site, so matching the natural enzyme's kcat shows a native-like fold. Answer: its kcat matches the liver enzyme."}],"trap":"Trusting the exact mass: it proves the right chain was made, but a denatured chain weighs the same."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"mass predicted from its gene","note":"identity check"},{"t":"GST tag is retained","note":"tag check"},{"t":"kcat equals that of kinase isolated from liver","note":"function check"},{"t":"native folding","note":"correct 3-D shape"}]},{"text":"**Ask of each result: would an unfolded protein give the same answer?** If yes, it says nothing about the fold."},{"text":"**Also:** mass, a retained tag and the number of purification steps are identical for folded and unfolded protein, so none of them can show the fold."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** only a native-like function (the same kcat, the same ligand Kd) shows a purified protein is folded; mass, tags and purity do not."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-26a","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe5-cp:26#1"],"q":"Fusion happens only when two nuclei collide fast enough to get within reach of the strong force. Which pair of nuclei needs the hottest plasma to fuse?","choices":["¹H + ¹H","²H + ³H","³He + ³He","¹²C + ¹²C"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Lowest charge: the barrier grows with Z₁Z₂, and hydrogen's product is 1, the smallest possible."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Still hydrogen: Z₁Z₂ = 1. Extra neutrons add mass, not charge, so this barrier is also the lowest."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Helium gives Z₁Z₂ = 4, higher than hydrogen but far below a pair of heavier nuclei."}],"rule":"The Coulomb barrier grows with Z₁ × Z₂: multiply the two atomic numbers, and the largest product needs the hottest plasma.","exp":"Every nucleus is positive, so two nuclei repel with a force ∝ **Z₁Z₂**. The product is 1 for any pair of hydrogen nuclei, 2 × 2 = 4 for helium and **6 × 6 = 36** for carbon. The largest charge product gives the largest Coulomb barrier, so **¹²C + ¹²C** needs the fastest nuclei, which means the hottest plasma.","how":{"ask":"What keeps two nuclei apart, and which pair has the most charge pushing back?","know":"• Every nucleus is **positive** with charge Z (its proton count): H 1, He 2, C 6. Two nuclei repel with F = kZ₁Z₂e²/r².\n• The energy barrier to get close scales with **Z₁Z₂**; nuclear size grows only slowly with mass, so the charge product dominates.\n• **Temperature ∝ average kinetic energy**: a bigger barrier needs a hotter plasma.\n• The mass number (the superscript) changes the mass, not the charge.","steps":[{"text":"Read each nucleus's charge Z from its element, not from its mass number.","calc":["H: Z = 1 (¹H, ²H, ³H alike)","He: Z = 2","C: Z = 6"]},{"text":"Multiply the two charges in each pair.","calc":["¹H + ¹H: 1 × 1 = **1**","²H + ³H: 1 × 1 = **1**","³He + ³He: 2 × 2 = **4**","¹²C + ¹²C: 6 × 6 = **36**"]},{"text":"Hydrogen pairs have the smallest charge product, so they need the coolest plasma; the heavier isotopes add mass, not charge.","strike":["¹H + ¹H","²H + ³H"]},{"text":"Helium's product of 4 beats hydrogen's but is far below carbon's 36.","strike":["³He + ³He"]},{"text":"Answer: ¹²C + ¹²C, the largest Z₁Z₂ and so the largest Coulomb barrier."}],"trap":"Ranking by mass number: the superscript counts nucleons, but only the protons repel, so compare Z₁Z₂, not A."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"collide fast enough","note":"kinetic energy vs repulsion"},{"t":"strong force","note":"acts only at short range"},{"t":"hottest plasma","note":"largest barrier"}]},{"text":"**Charge sets the barrier.** Two positive nuclei push apart more strongly the closer they get, in proportion to Z₁Z₂.","calc":["barrier ∝ Z₁Z₂"]},{"text":"**Temperature buys speed.** Average kinetic energy rises with T, so the larger the barrier, the hotter the plasma must be before nuclei get close enough.","calc":["KE(avg) ∝ T"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to rank how hot a fusion plasma must be, multiply the two nuclei's proton counts; the larger Z₁Z₂, the larger the Coulomb barrier and the higher the temperature."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-26b","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe5-cp:26#2"],"q":"The strong nuclear force binds protons inside a nucleus, yet two free protons 10 nm apart only repel each other. Which property of the strong force explains this?","choices":["Its short range","Its weak strength","Its repulsive sign","Its charge dependence"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"False premise: the strong force is the strongest of the four forces, so weakness can't be it."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Wrong sign: between nucleons it attracts; that is why nuclei hold together."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"The strong force acts the same on protons and neutrons; it doesn't depend on charge."}],"rule":"The strong force is the strongest and attractive, but it reaches only about a nucleus's width (~1 fm); farther apart, only Coulomb repulsion acts.","exp":"The strong force attracts nucleons and overpowers Coulomb repulsion, but it acts **only over about a nucleon's width, a few femtometres (~10⁻¹⁵ m)**. At 10 nm, ten million times farther, it is effectively zero, so only the electric repulsion remains.","how":{"ask":"Decide why a force that wins inside a nucleus does nothing at a larger distance.","know":"• The **strong force** attracts protons and neutrons alike and is stronger than the electric force at nuclear distances.\n• It is **short-ranged**: it falls to nearly zero beyond a few × 10⁻¹⁵ m.\n• The electric force falls only as 1/r², so it dominates at larger distances.","steps":[{"text":"Compare the separation with the strong force's reach.","hl":"10 nm apart","calc":["10 nm = 10⁻⁸ m","10⁻⁸ m ÷ 10⁻¹⁵ m = 10⁷ times too far"]},{"text":"The strong force is the strongest force, is attractive, and acts the same on protons and neutrons.","strike":["Its weak strength","Its repulsive sign","Its charge dependence"]},{"text":"Answer: its short range."}],"trap":"Calling the strong force weak because it vanishes at distance: it is the strongest force, but only within about one nucleus."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"binds protons inside a nucleus","note":"wins at ~10⁻¹⁵ m"},{"t":"10 nm apart","note":"far beyond its reach"}]},{"text":"**Two forces, two ranges.** Coulomb repulsion reaches far; the strong attraction reaches only about a nucleon's width."},{"text":"**Where each wins.** Inside a nucleus the strong force wins; beyond about 10⁻¹⁵ m, repulsion wins.","calc":["r ≲ 10⁻¹⁵ m → strong force dominates","r ≫ 10⁻¹⁵ m → electric repulsion only"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** the strong force beats Coulomb repulsion only within about 10⁻¹⁵ m; beyond that range, like charges simply repel."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-27a","s":"Stereochemistry","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:27#1","pe5-cp:27#2"],"q":"(R)-Carvone, shown with its mirror image (S)-carvone, smells of spearmint, while the S form smells of caraway. Which property differs between them?","fig":["cpt-pe5-r-carvone","cpt-pe5-s-carvone"],"choices":["Sign of rotation","Boiling temperature","Infrared spectrum","Solubility in ethanol"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Mirror-image molecules have the same intermolecular forces, so they boil at the same temperature."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"IR sees bonds and functional groups, and mirror images have identical bonds."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Ethanol is achiral, so it interacts with both mirror images identically; their solubility is the same."}],"rule":"Enantiomers match in every achiral property (bp, IR, solubility in achiral solvents); they differ only toward polarized light or other chiral things.","exp":"The two forms are **enantiomers** (mirror images at one stereocenter). In an achiral setting they share boiling point, density, solubility and IR spectrum; they differ only in the **sign of optical rotation** and in how they bind chiral partners, such as the protein odor receptors that tell them apart.","how":{"ask":"Decide which properties can tell two mirror-image molecules apart.","know":"• **Enantiomers** have identical physical properties in an achiral environment: bp, mp, density, solubility in achiral solvents, IR and NMR.\n• They rotate plane-polarized light by **equal amounts in opposite directions**.\n• They interact differently with **chiral** things: enzymes, receptors, chiral columns.","steps":[{"text":"R and S at the same single stereocenter, with the same connectivity, make the two forms enantiomers.","hl":"shown with its mirror image (S)-carvone"},{"text":"Boiling point, IR and solubility in an achiral solvent such as ethanol are the same for both.","strike":["Boiling temperature","Infrared spectrum","Solubility in ethanol"]},{"text":"Answer: the sign of optical rotation."}],"trap":"Concluding from the different smells that the molecules differ in ordinary physical properties: the odor difference comes from chiral receptors."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"mirror image","note":"enantiomers"},{"t":"smells of spearmint","note":"chiral receptor response"}]},{"text":"**Classify the pair.** Same connectivity, opposite configuration at the one stereocenter: enantiomers."},{"text":"**Achiral tests can't tell them apart.** Heat, solvents like ethanol and IR light treat both the same."},{"text":"**Also:** enantiomers smell or act differently because odor receptors and enzymes are made of L-amino acids and are therefore chiral, binding each form differently."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** enantiomers share bp, density, solubility and IR; they differ only in the sign of optical rotation and in interactions with chiral partners."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-29a","s":"Atoms & Periodic Trends","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:29#1"],"q":"Which of the following ions has the same ground-state electron configuration as a noble gas?","choices":["Se²⁻","Fe³⁺","Cu⁺","Sn²⁺"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Odd count: 26 − 3 = 23 electrons, and every noble-gas count (2, 10, 18, 36, 54) is even."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"29 − 1 = 28 electrons falls between Ar (18) and Kr (36), not on a noble-gas count."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"50 − 2 = 48 electrons ([Kr]4d¹⁰5s²) sits between Kr (36) and Xe (54)."}],"rule":"Count electrons as Z − charge (add for anions) and compare with the noble-gas counts 2, 10, 18, 36, 54, 86.","exp":"**Electrons = Z − charge.** Se²⁻: 34 + 2 = **36**, the count of krypton. Fe³⁺ has 23, Cu⁺ 28 and Sn²⁺ 48, none a noble-gas count (2, 10, 18, 36, 54); a half-filled or full d subshell is not a noble-gas configuration.","how":{"ask":"Count each ion's electrons and compare with the noble-gas counts.","know":"• **Electrons = Z − charge** (a 2− ion has Z + 2).\n• Noble-gas electron counts: **2 (He), 10 (Ne), 18 (Ar), 36 (Kr), 54 (Xe)**.\n• Transition-metal ions lose s electrons first but keep their d electrons, so they rarely match a noble gas.","steps":[{"text":"Count electrons in each ion.","hl":"same ground-state electron configuration as a noble gas","calc":["Se²⁻: 34 + 2 = **36** = Kr ✓","Fe³⁺: 26 − 3 = 23","Cu⁺: 29 − 1 = 28","Sn²⁺: 50 − 2 = 48"]},{"text":"23, 28 and 48 are not noble-gas counts: Fe³⁺ is [Ar]3d⁵, Cu⁺ is [Ar]3d¹⁰, and Sn²⁺ is [Kr]4d¹⁰5s².","strike":["Fe³⁺","Cu⁺","Sn²⁺"]},{"text":"Answer: Se²⁻."}],"trap":"Choosing an ion with a half-filled or full d subshell: those are stable, but not noble-gas configurations."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"noble gas","note":"2, 10, 18, 36, 54 electrons"},{"t":"ground-state","note":"count electrons"}]},{"text":"**Count electrons.** Subtract a positive charge from Z; add a negative charge to it.","calc":["Se: Z = 34, charge 2− → 36"]},{"text":"**Match a noble gas.** 36 electrons is krypton's count, [Ar]3d¹⁰4s²4p⁶."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** electrons = Z − charge, and an ion is noble-gas-like only if that count is 2, 10, 18, 36 or 54."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-30a","s":"Reading Data","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe5-cp:30#1"],"q":"A reagent tags tyrosines. Buffer A: Y21 70%, Y64 25%, Y118 none. Buffer B: Y21 none, Y64 50%, Y118 none. Which tyrosine can be tagged selectively?","choices":["Y21, in buffer A","Y64, in buffer A","Y64, in buffer B","Y118, in buffer B"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"In buffer A another tyrosine is tagged too (25%), so Y21 is never tagged alone there."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"In buffer A that residue is tagged alongside Y21 (70%), so it isn't tagged alone there."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Y118 reads none in both buffers; a residue never tagged can't be tagged selectively."}],"rule":"Selective means one residue tagged while every other reads none: scan each buffer for a column with a single nonzero value.","exp":"**Selective** means one residue reacts while every other residue reads zero. In buffer B only **Y64** is tagged (50%); in buffer A, Y21 and Y64 are tagged together, and Y118 is never tagged.","how":{"ask":"Under which buffer does exactly one tyrosine react while the others stay at zero?","know":"• **Selective modification**: under one condition, the target is labeled and **every other residue reads 0%**.\n• Read the data **one condition at a time**; a high percentage alone is not enough if another residue is also labeled.","steps":[{"text":"Read buffer A as one row: two tyrosines are tagged.","hl":"Y21 70%, Y64 25%, Y118 none","calc":["buffer A: Y21 and Y64 both > 0 → **not selective**"]},{"text":"So nothing tagged in buffer A is tagged alone: Y21 brings Y64 with it, and Y64 brings Y21.","strike":["Y21, in buffer A","Y64, in buffer A"]},{"text":"Read buffer B as one row: only one tyrosine reacts.","hl":"Y21 none, Y64 50%, Y118 none","calc":["buffer B: Y64 > 0, Y21 = 0, Y118 = 0 → **Y64 alone**"]},{"text":"Y118 reads none in both buffers, so it is never tagged at all, selectively or otherwise.","strike":["Y118, in buffer B"]},{"text":"Answer: Y64, in buffer B."}],"trap":"Picking the largest percentage (Y21 at 70%): it is tagged alongside Y64, so it is not selective."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find what each number means**.","marks":[{"t":"Buffer A","note":"first condition"},{"t":"Y21 70%, Y64 25%, Y118 none","note":"two residues react"},{"t":"Buffer B","note":"second condition"},{"t":"selectively","note":"only one reacts"}]},{"text":"**Define the target.** A selective condition labels the residue of interest while all the others read zero. Size of the percentage doesn't matter; exclusivity does."},{"text":"**Scan each condition across.** Count the nonzero entries in each row: one nonzero entry is a selective condition for that residue.","calc":["buffer A: 2 nonzero → none selective","buffer B: 1 nonzero (Y64) → Y64 selective"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to find a selective condition, read the table one condition at a time and pick the condition where only the target residue reacts and every other residue reads 0%."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-31a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:31#1","pe5-cp:31#2"],"q":"A phenylalanine analog carries an added ring –CH₂NH₂ (pKa of its NH₃⁺ form ≈ 9.5), as shown. At pH 7.0, which best describes its side chain?","fig":"cpt-pe5-aminomethyl-phe","choices":["Negatively charged","Positively charged","Uncharged and nonpolar","Uncharged but polar"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"An amine can only gain H⁺ and turn positive; a negative side chain needs an acid group, like COOH, that loses H⁺."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"pH 7.0 is below pKa 9.5, so the group is a charged NH₃⁺, and a charged side chain isn't nonpolar."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Below its pKa the amine keeps its H⁺ as NH₃⁺, so the side chain carries a charge; it isn't uncharged."}],"rule":"pH below pKa: the group keeps its H⁺. An amine with its H⁺ is a cation (NH₃⁺); an acid with its H⁺ is neutral (COOH).","exp":"pH 7.0 lies **2.5 units below** the pKa of 9.5, so the added amine is almost fully protonated: [NH₃⁺]/[NH₂] = 10^2.5 ≈ 300. Like lysine's amine, it gives the side chain a **net +1 charge**, so it is **positively charged** (basic).","how":{"ask":"Compare the added group's pKa with the pH: which form dominates, and what charge does it give the side chain?","know":"• **pH = pKa + log([base]/[acid])**: when pH < pKa the protonated form dominates; when pH > pKa the deprotonated form does.\n• An amine (pKa ≈ 9–10) is **NH₃⁺ (+1)** at pH 7 → positively charged, basic side chain, like Lys.\n• A carboxyl (pKa ≈ 4) is **COO⁻ (−1)** at pH 7 → negatively charged, acidic side chain, like Asp and Glu.","steps":[{"text":"Pick out the added group's pKa and the pH.","hl":"pKa of its NH₃⁺ form ≈ 9.5","calc":["pKa − pH = 9.5 − 7.0 = **2.5**"]},{"text":"Apply Henderson–Hasselbalch.","calc":["log([NH₂]/[NH₃⁺]) = 7.0 − 9.5 = −2.5","[NH₃⁺]/[NH₂] = 10^2.5 ≈ **300 : 1**","side-chain charge ≈ **+1**"]},{"text":"A charged side chain is not uncharged, whether nonpolar or polar; the benzene ring no longer decides the class.","strike":["Uncharged and nonpolar","Uncharged but polar"]},{"text":"A negative charge needs a group that loses H⁺, such as a carboxyl; an amine only gains H⁺.","strike":["Negatively charged"]},{"text":"Answer: positively charged."}],"trap":"Calling it uncharged because phenylalanine is nonpolar, or because an NH₂ is drawn: at pH 7.0 the amine is mostly NH₃⁺."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words and numbers**.","marks":[{"t":"–CH₂NH₂","note":"the added group"},{"t":"pKa of its NH₃⁺ form ≈ 9.5","note":"amine pKa"},{"t":"pH 7.0","note":"well below the pKa"}]},{"text":"**Compare pKa with pH.** Below the pKa a group is mostly protonated; above it, mostly deprotonated. Each pH unit is a 10-fold change.","calc":["9.5 − 7.0 = 2.5 units below","ratio NH₃⁺ : NH₂ ≈ 10^2.5 ≈ 300 : 1"],"fig":"cpt-pe5-aminomethyl-phe"},{"text":"**Charge sets the class.** A +1 side chain at physiological pH is basic (Lys, Arg, His when protonated); a −1 side chain is acidic (Asp, Glu)."},{"text":"**Also:** an added carboxyl works the other way. For –CH₂COOH (pKa ≈ 4.3), pH 7.0 is 2.7 units above the pKa, so the group is COO⁻ (−1) and the side chain is **negatively charged** (acidic).","calc":["7.0 − 4.3 = 2.7","[COO⁻]/[COOH] ≈ 10^2.7 ≈ 500 : 1 → −1"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** compare an added group's pKa with the pH: an amine (pKa ≈ 9–10) is protonated to NH₃⁺ at pH 7, making the side chain positively charged; a carboxyl (pKa ≈ 4) is ionized to COO⁻, making it negatively charged."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-32a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:32#3"],"q":"An enzyme's activity falls as the pH rises, as plotted. Its catalytic residue must be protonated to act. Which residue is most likely the catalytic acid?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Activity of the enzyme versus pH","x":{"label":"pH","min":5,"max":11,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Relative activity (%)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Activity","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[5,99],[6,98],[6.5,97],[7,95],[7.5,86],[8.3,50],[8.8,22],[9.3,8],[9.8,3],[10.3,1],[11,0]]}]},"choices":["Cysteine","Histidine","Aspartate","Lysine"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Histidine (pKa ≈ 6.0) would lose its proton, and its activity, near pH 6, lower than where this curve drops."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Aspartate (pKa ≈ 3.9) loses its H⁺ near pH 4, far below where this activity drops."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Lysine (pKa ≈ 10.5) would stay protonated, and active, until about pH 10.5."}],"rule":"Activity that needs a protonated residue drops near that residue's pKa: read the pH of the drop, then match it (Asp ≈ 4, His ≈ 6, Lys ≈ 10.5).","exp":"Activity tracks how much of the residue is still protonated, so the **pH at 50% activity equals its pKa**. On this plot that is **pH ≈ 8.3**, which matches cysteine's thiol (–SH, pKa ≈ 8.3). Asp (≈ 3.9), His (≈ 6.0) and Lys (≈ 10.5) would put the midpoint elsewhere.","how":{"ask":"Read the pH at half activity; that pH is the pKa of the residue. Which side chain has that pKa?","know":"• When activity depends on one ionizable group, the **pH at 50% activity = that group's pKa** (Henderson–Hasselbalch: half protonated at pH = pKa).\n• Side-chain pKa values: **Asp ≈ 3.9, Glu ≈ 4.1, His ≈ 6.0, Cys ≈ 8.3, Tyr ≈ 10.1, Lys ≈ 10.5, Arg ≈ 12.5**.\n• A falling curve: the group must be **protonated** (an acid); a rising curve: it must be deprotonated (a base).","steps":[{"text":"The curve falls with pH because the residue loses its proton as pH rises.","hl":"must be protonated to act"},{"text":"Read the midpoint: drop from 50% activity to the x-axis.","calc":["50% activity at **pH ≈ 8.3**","pKa ≈ 8.3"]},{"text":"Aspartate (pKa ≈ 3.9) and histidine (pKa ≈ 6.0) would lose their protons at much lower pH; their midpoints would sit near 4 and 6.","strike":["Aspartate","Histidine"]},{"text":"Lysine (≈ 10.5) stays protonated past pH 9, so its activity would not drop until about pH 10.5.","strike":["Lysine"]},{"text":"Answer: cysteine."}],"trap":"Thinking a residue that must be protonated has to be basic, like lysine: the midpoint, not the direction, identifies the residue."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"falls as the pH rises","note":"loses a proton"},{"t":"must be protonated","note":"acts as an acid"},{"t":"catalytic acid","note":"donates H⁺"}]},{"text":"**Midpoint = pKa.** At pH = pKa a group is half protonated, so an enzyme that needs it protonated runs at half speed there.","calc":["pH = pKa + log([A⁻]/[HA])","at pH = pKa: [A⁻] = [HA] → 50%"]},{"text":"**Match the pKa.** A midpoint near 8.3 fits cysteine's –SH; Asp/Glu sit near 4, His near 6, Lys near 10.5."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** the pH at half activity is the catalytic group's pKa; match it to a side chain: Asp/Glu ≈ 4, His ≈ 6, Cys ≈ 8.3, Lys ≈ 10.5."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-32b","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:32#1","pe5-cp:32#2"],"q":"Tryptophan and proline each carry a ring in their side chains. Which pair names those two rings, listed in that order?","choices":["Indole; pyrrolidine","Imidazole; pyrrolidine","Indole; pyrimidine","Imidazole; pyrimidine"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Imidazole is histidine's ring (two N atoms in a five-membered ring), not tryptophan's."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Pyrimidine is a nucleobase ring (C, T, U); no amino-acid side chain carries one."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Wrong twice: imidazole is histidine's ring, and pyrimidines belong to nucleobases."}],"rule":"Trp: a benzene fused to a five-ring with one N. Pro: a saturated five-ring with one N. His: a five-ring with two N (imidazole).","exp":"Tryptophan's side chain is an **indole** (a benzene ring fused to a five-membered N–H ring). Proline's ring is a **pyrrolidine**, a saturated five-membered ring that includes the backbone nitrogen. Imidazole belongs to histidine, and pyrimidines are nucleobases (C, T, U), never side chains.","how":{"ask":"Name the ring on tryptophan, then the ring on proline.","know":"• **His → imidazole** (5-membered, 2 N, aromatic).\n• **Trp → indole** (benzene fused to a 5-membered N–H ring; 9 ring atoms).\n• **Pro → pyrrolidine** (5-membered, saturated, the backbone N is in the ring).\n• **Phe, Tyr → benzene** (phenyl).\n• **Pyrimidines** (cytosine, thymine, uracil) and **purines** (A, G) are nucleobases, not amino-acid side chains.","steps":[{"text":"Tryptophan: a fused two-ring system with one N–H, the indole.","hl":"Tryptophan","fig":"tryptophan"},{"text":"Imidazole is histidine's ring (two N atoms in a five-membered ring), not tryptophan's.","strike":["Imidazole; pyrrolidine","Imidazole; pyrimidine"]},{"text":"Proline: its side chain loops back onto its own α-amino N, closing a saturated five-membered ring, the pyrrolidine.","hl":"proline","fig":"proline"},{"text":"A pyrimidine is a six-membered ring with two N atoms found in nucleic-acid bases; no amino acid carries one.","strike":["Indole; pyrimidine"]},{"text":"Answer: indole; pyrrolidine."}],"trap":"Picking pyrimidine because it sounds like pyrrolidine: pyrimidines are DNA/RNA bases, never side chains."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Tryptophan and proline","note":"two ring residues"},{"t":"in that order","note":"Trp first, then Pro"}]},{"text":"**Ring residues and their rings.** Count the ring atoms and nitrogens to tell them apart.","chart":{"type":"bar","caption":"Side-chain rings of the ring-bearing amino acids","x":{"label":"Residue (ring)"},"y":{"label":"Count","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"categories":["His (imidazole)","Trp (indole)","Pro (pyrrolidine)","Phe (benzene)"],"series":[{"name":"Ring atoms","values":[5,9,5,6]},{"name":"Ring nitrogens","values":[2,1,1,0]}]}},{"text":"**Also:** pyrimidines (cytosine, thymine, uracil) and purines (adenine, guanine) are nucleobases in DNA and RNA. A choice that names one as a side-chain ring is always wrong."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** His = imidazole, Trp = indole, Pro = pyrrolidine, Phe/Tyr = benzene; pyrimidines and purines are nucleobases, never amino-acid side chains."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-33a","s":"Protein Structure","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:33#1","pe6-cp:31#1"],"q":"Heating an enzyme to 70 °C, with no reducing agent present, exposes a tryptophan buried in its core, yet its mass is unchanged. Which interaction survived the heating?","choices":["Disulfide bonds","Hydrogen bonds","Salt bridges","Hydrophobic contacts"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Hydrogen bonds are noncovalent and weak, so 70 °C heating breaks them as the protein unfolds."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Noncovalent: an ionic pair is weak enough for 70 °C heating to break."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"The buried Trp got exposed, so the hydrophobic core came apart; those contacts didn't survive."}],"rule":"Heat breaks noncovalent forces (H-bonds, salt bridges, hydrophobic contacts); only covalent cross-links survive, and a reducing agent is what cuts S–S.","exp":"Heat **denatures** a protein by breaking its **noncovalent** contacts (hydrogen bonds, salt bridges, hydrophobic interactions), so the chain unfolds and buried side chains reach the solvent. **Disulfide bonds** are covalent S–S bonds; without a reducing agent they survive, as does the peptide backbone, so the mass does not change.","how":{"ask":"Which of these interactions are covalent, and which does heat break?","know":"• **Denaturation** by heat breaks **noncovalent** interactions (H-bonds, salt bridges, hydrophobic and van der Waals contacts); the fold is lost and buried residues are exposed.\n• **Primary structure** (peptide bonds) and **disulfides** are covalent and survive heating.\n• Reducing an S–S bond adds 2 H (+2 Da) and needs a reducing agent.","steps":[{"text":"The buried residue became exposed, so the fold opened up: the weak contacts that held it broke.","hl":"exposes a tryptophan buried in its core"},{"text":"Hydrogen bonds, salt bridges and hydrophobic contacts are all noncovalent, so heating breaks them.","strike":["Hydrogen bonds","Salt bridges","Hydrophobic contacts"]},{"text":"An S–S bond is covalent, and breaking it by reduction would need a reducing agent and add mass.","hl":"no reducing agent present","calc":["S–S + 2 H → 2 SH: **+2 Da**","observed: **0**","→ disulfides intact"]},{"text":"Answer: disulfide bonds, which are covalent."}],"trap":"Assuming the disulfides broke because the protein unfolded: S–S bonds need a reducing agent, not just heat."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Heating","note":"adds thermal energy"},{"t":"no reducing agent","note":"S–S stays"},{"t":"mass is unchanged","note":"no covalent change"},{"t":"survived","note":"the one left intact"}]},{"text":"**Heat targets the weak bonds.** The fold is held by many weak interactions; heat breaks them and the chain unfolds (denatures)."},{"text":"**Mass is a covalent-bond detector.** Any covalent cut adds or removes atoms, so an unchanged mass means only noncovalent contacts broke.","calc":["hydrolysis of one peptide bond: +18 Da","no change → no covalent bond broken"]},{"text":"**Also:** when data give the percent denatured for each treatment, rank them: the more of the protein is unfolded, the more of its buried nonpolar core faces the water. A buffer-only control at 0% leaves the core buried.","calc":["SDS 90% > acid 55% > cold 5% > buffer 0%","→ the core is most exposed after SDS"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** heating denatures a protein by breaking noncovalent interactions, while every covalent bond (the backbone and disulfides, unless a reducing agent is added) survives."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-34a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:34#1","pe6-cp:50#1"],"q":"Methylamine, CH₃NH₂, is neutralized with formic acid, HCOOH, to make a volatile buffer salt. Which salt forms?","choices":["Methylammonium formate","Methylammonium acetate","Dimethylammonium formate","Methyl formate"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Formic acid gives formate (HCOO⁻); acetate would need acetic acid."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Dimethylammonium needs a base with two methyls, (CH₃)₂NH; methylamine has only one."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"An ester, not a salt: an acid plus an amine swap a proton and make ions, not an ester."}],"rule":"Acid + amine = proton transfer: name the protonated amine (-ammonium) plus the acid's anion (-ate), each keeping its own carbons.","exp":"The base takes H⁺ from the acid: **CH₃NH₂ + HCOOH → CH₃NH₃⁺ + HCOO⁻**. The salt is named cation first: **methylammonium formate**.","how":{"ask":"Move one H⁺ from the acid to the base, then name the cation and the anion.","know":"• **Acid + base → salt** of the base's conjugate acid (cation) and the acid's conjugate base (anion).\n• An amine RNH₂ becomes RNH₃⁺ (ammonium-type cation); HCOOH becomes HCOO⁻ (formate).\n• Salts are named **cation, then anion**.","steps":[{"text":"Transfer the proton.","hl":"CH₃NH₂, is neutralized with formic acid, HCOOH","calc":["CH₃NH₂ + HCOOH → CH₃NH₃⁺ + HCOO⁻"]},{"text":"Name the ions: CH₃NH₃⁺ is methylammonium; HCOO⁻ is formate."},{"text":"Acetate needs acetic acid, dimethylammonium needs a base with two methyls, (CH₃)₂NH, and methyl formate is an ester, not a salt.","strike":["Methylammonium acetate","Dimethylammonium formate","Methyl formate"]},{"text":"Answer: methylammonium formate."}],"trap":"Picking the ester: neutralization moves a proton and makes ions; an ester needs an alcohol and a condensation."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Methylamine","note":"the base"},{"t":"formic acid","note":"the acid"},{"t":"neutralized","note":"proton transfer"}]},{"text":"**Move one proton.** The acid gives H⁺; the base accepts it.","calc":["CH₃NH₂ + H⁺ → CH₃NH₃⁺","HCOOH − H⁺ → HCOO⁻"]},{"text":"**Name cation, then anion.** methylammonium + formate."},{"text":"**Also:** a weak base plus a weak acid gives the salt of their conjugates: NH₃ + HCN → NH₄⁺ + CN⁻; name the cation first, then the anion: ammonium cyanide."},{"text":"**Also:** an ionic compound is named cation, then anion, with no Greek prefixes (those belong to molecular compounds such as N₂O₄, dinitrogen tetroxide). NH₄⁺ is ammonium, never \"ammonia\" (ammonia is the neutral base NH₃), and a one-atom anion ends in -ide.","calc":["NH₃ + HBr → NH₄⁺ + Br⁻: ammonium bromide","NH₄⁺ with NO₃⁻: ammonium nitrate"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** acid + base → a salt of the base's conjugate acid and the acid's conjugate base, named cation then anion."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-34b","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:34#2"],"q":"A blood-gas buffer contains sodium bicarbonate dissolved in water. Which is the formula of the anion it supplies?","choices":["HCO₃⁻","HCOO⁻","CO₃²⁻","CH₃COO⁻"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"HCOO⁻ has its C bonded to an H and two O: that's formate, not bicarbonate."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"No H: that's carbonate; the bi- (hydrogen) carbonate ion still carries one H."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"That's acetate; bicarbonate has one carbon and no C–H bonds."}],"rule":"Bicarbonate = hydrogen carbonate: carbonate (CO₃²⁻) plus one H on an O, which leaves a 1− charge and no C–H bond.","exp":"**Bicarbonate is HCO₃⁻** (hydrogen carbonate). HCOO⁻ is formate, CO₃²⁻ is carbonate (no H, 2− charge), and CH₃COO⁻ is acetate.","how":{"ask":"Match the anion's name to its formula.","know":"• **Formate** HCOO⁻ (from formic acid, 1 C).\n• **Acetate** CH₃COO⁻ (from acetic acid, 2 C).\n• **Carbonate** CO₃²⁻; **bicarbonate** (hydrogen carbonate) HCO₃⁻: carbonate plus one H⁺.","steps":[{"text":"The salt names the anion.","hl":"sodium bicarbonate","calc":["NaHCO₃ → Na⁺ + **HCO₃⁻**"]},{"text":"HCOO⁻ looks similar but has C bonded to H and two O (formate); CO₃²⁻ has lost the H; CH₃COO⁻ is acetate.","strike":["HCOO⁻","CO₃²⁻","CH₃COO⁻"]},{"text":"Answer: HCO₃⁻."}],"trap":"Confusing HCO₃⁻ with HCOO⁻: count the oxygens (3 in bicarbonate, 2 in formate)."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"sodium bicarbonate","note":"NaHCO₃"},{"t":"anion","note":"the negative ion"}]},{"text":"**Build from carbonate.** CO₃²⁻ plus one H⁺ gives HCO₃⁻, bicarbonate.","calc":["CO₃²⁻ + H⁺ → HCO₃⁻"]},{"text":"**Count O to separate look-alikes.** HCO₃⁻ has 3 O; formate HCOO⁻ and acetate CH₃COO⁻ have 2."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** formate HCOO⁻, acetate CH₃COO⁻, carbonate CO₃²⁻, bicarbonate HCO₃⁻."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-36a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:36#1","pe5-cp:36#2","pe5-cp:36#3","pe5-cp:36#4"],"q":"Each of the four compounds drawn has a C=O group, but with different atoms bonded to the carbonyl carbon. Which compound is an ester?","choices":["Compound W","Compound X","Compound Y","Compound Z"],"choiceFigs":["butanal","cpt-pe5-2-butanone","cpt-pe5-butanoic-acid","cpt-pe5-methyl-propanoate"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"An ester needs an O on the C=O carbon; W has an H there, so it's an aldehyde."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"X has carbon on both sides of the C=O and no O neighbor, so it's a ketone."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Y's C=O carbon carries an –OH, not an –OR, so it's a carboxylic acid."}],"rule":"Name a carbonyl by its two neighbors: C,C ketone; C,H aldehyde; C,OH acid; C,OR ester; C,N amide.","exp":"Classify a carbonyl by its **two neighbors**. In Z (methyl propanoate) the C=O carbon is bonded to a carbon and to an **O that carries a CH₃** (an –OR group): an ester. W (butanal) has an H on it, an aldehyde; X (2-butanone) has carbon on both sides, a ketone; Y (butanoic acid) has an –OH, a carboxylic acid.","how":{"ask":"For each structure, look at the two atoms on the carbonyl carbon besides the O.","know":"• **C and C** → ketone (R–CO–R′).\n• **C and H** → aldehyde (R–CHO), at a chain end.\n• **C and OH** → carboxylic acid (R–COOH).\n• **C and OR** → ester (R–COOR′).","steps":[{"text":"Compound W: the C=O sits at the chain end with an H on it, so it is an aldehyde (butanal).","strike":["Compound W"]},{"text":"Compound X: the C=O is in the middle of the chain, flanked by a methyl and an ethyl: carbon on both sides, a ketone (2-butanone).","strike":["Compound X"]},{"text":"Compound Y: one neighbor is an –OH, so it is a carboxylic acid (butanoic acid).","strike":["Compound Y"]},{"text":"Compound Z: one neighbor is an O that carries a CH₃, an –OR group, so it is an ester (methyl propanoate).","hl":"Compound Z"},{"text":"Answer: Compound Z."}],"trap":"Calling the ester a ketone because its carbonyl is not at the end of a carbon chain, or an acid because it has two O atoms: check whether the O neighbor carries an H (acid) or a carbon (ester)."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"C=O group","note":"the carbonyl"},{"t":"atoms bonded to the carbonyl carbon","note":"what decides the class"},{"t":"ester","note":"C and OR"}]},{"text":"**Look past the O.** The carbonyl carbon has two other bonds; name the group from those two neighbors. Here, a carbon and an –OCH₃: an ester, methyl propanoate (CH₃CH₂COOCH₃).","fig":"cpt-pe5-methyl-propanoate"},{"text":"**Also:** carbon on both sides of the carbonyl carbon makes a **ketone**, as in 2-butanone (CH₃COCH₂CH₃).","fig":"cpt-pe5-2-butanone"},{"text":"**Also:** an H on the carbonyl carbon makes an **aldehyde**, as in butanal (CH₃CH₂CH₂CHO).","fig":"butanal"},{"text":"**Also:** an –OH on the carbonyl carbon makes a **carboxylic acid**, as in butanoic acid (CH₃CH₂CH₂COOH).","fig":"cpt-pe5-butanoic-acid"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** classify a carbonyl by its two neighbors: C and C = ketone, H = aldehyde, OH = carboxylic acid, OR = ester."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-37a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:37#1","pe5-cp:37#2"],"q":"A rigid flask at 298 K holds NH₃ and O₂ in a 4:5 mole ratio at 2.7 atm. Once 4 NH₃(g) + 5 O₂(g) → 4 NO(g) + 6 H₂O(l) is complete, what pressure remains?","choices":["1.2 atm","3.0 atm","2.7 atm","6.1 atm"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Ratio flipped: gas moles drop from 9 to 4, so the final pressure must be below 2.7 atm, not above it."},{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"That's the starting pressure copied; gas moles change (9 → 4), so in a rigid flask at fixed T it can't stay the same."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Counts the liquid water as gas; a liquid's volume is tiny, so only NO is left pushing on the walls."}],"rule":"Only gases count: P_final = P_start × (gas moles after ÷ gas moles before). Round: 2.7 × 4/9 ≈ 3 × 0.4.","exp":"In a rigid flask at fixed T, **P ∝ moles of gas**. Gas moles go from 4 + 5 = 9 to 4 (the water is liquid), so P = 2.7 atm × 4/9 = **1.2 atm**.","how":{"ask":"Count gas moles before and after from the coefficients, then scale the pressure by that ratio.","know":"• At constant V and T, PV = nRT gives **P ∝ n(gas)**: P_final = P_initial × n_after/n_before.\n• Take the moles from the **coefficients** when the reactants are in the stoichiometric ratio and react completely.\n• Count **only gases**; (l) and (s) species add no pressure (water's small vapor pressure is ignored).","steps":[{"text":"The flask is rigid and the temperature is fixed, so only the gas moles change.","hl":"A rigid flask at 298 K"},{"text":"Gas moles before: both reactants are gases.","hl":"in a 4:5 mole ratio","calc":["n_before = 4 + 5 = **9**"]},{"text":"Gas moles after: the mix is exactly stoichiometric, so nothing is left over; NO is a gas, the water is liquid.","calc":["n_after = 4 (NO) + 0 (H₂O, liquid) = **4**"]},{"text":"Scale the pressure.","calc":["P_final = P_initial × n_after/n_before","P_final = 2.7 atm × 4/9","P_final = **1.2 atm**"]},{"text":"3.0 atm counts the liquid water as gas (2.7 × 10/9); 6.1 atm flips the ratio (2.7 × 9/4); 2.7 atm assumes the pressure does not change.","strike":["3.0 atm","6.1 atm","2.7 atm"]},{"text":"Answer: 1.2 atm."}],"trap":"Counting every product coefficient: a liquid or solid product takes up almost no volume and adds no pressure."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"rigid flask","note":"V constant"},{"t":"298 K","note":"T constant"},{"t":"4:5 mole ratio","note":"exactly stoichiometric"},{"t":"H₂O(l)","note":"liquid: not counted"}]},{"text":"**P tracks gas moles.** With V and T fixed, PV = nRT makes P directly proportional to n.","calc":["P₂/P₁ = n₂/n₁"]},{"text":"**Also: read the gas moles from the coefficients.** Add the coefficients of the (g) species on each side.","calc":["left: 4 + 5 = 9 mol gas","right: 4 mol gas (the 6 mol of water is liquid)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** at constant V and T, P_final = P_initial × (gas moles after ÷ gas moles before), counting only (g) species from the balanced equation."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-38a","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:38#1","pe5-cp:38#2"],"q":"Naphthalene and sodium benzoate are shaken together with water and dichloromethane. Which layers hold naphthalene and the benzoate ion, respectively?","choices":["Organic; aqueous","Aqueous; organic","Organic; organic","Aqueous; aqueous"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Both swapped: the neutral hydrocarbon goes to the organic layer and the ion to the water, not the other way."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Benzoate is an ion, and ions stay in water, not in nonpolar CH₂Cl₂."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Naphthalene is a neutral hydrocarbon; it won't go into water."}],"rule":"Like dissolves like: neutral, nonpolar compounds go to the organic layer; ions (salts, charged acids or bases) stay in water.","exp":"**Like dissolves like.** Naphthalene is a neutral hydrocarbon, so it moves into **dichloromethane**; benzoate is an **anion**, held in **water** by ion–dipole attraction. Layer density decides only which phase sits on top.","how":{"ask":"Which compound is neutral and nonpolar, which is an ion, and which solvent does each resemble?","know":"• **Like dissolves like**: neutral, nonpolar molecules go to the organic layer; ions are surrounded by water dipoles and stay in the aqueous layer.\n• Dichloromethane is an organic solvent that does not mix with water.\n• Density only decides which layer is on top (dichloromethane sinks), not where a compound goes.","steps":[{"text":"Naphthalene is two fused benzene rings of C and H only: neutral and nonpolar, so it dissolves in the organic solvent.","hl":"Naphthalene"},{"text":"Sodium benzoate is a salt: in water it is Na⁺ plus the benzoate anion, and water's dipoles surround that charge.","hl":"sodium benzoate"},{"text":"Aqueous; organic swaps both; aqueous; aqueous puts a hydrocarbon in water; organic; organic puts an ion in a nonpolar solvent.","strike":["Aqueous; organic","Aqueous; aqueous","Organic; organic"]},{"text":"Answer: organic; aqueous."}],"trap":"Placing compounds by layer position: dichloromethane is denser and lies under the water, but which layer is on top says nothing about where a compound dissolves."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Naphthalene","note":"C and H only"},{"t":"sodium benzoate","note":"a salt: ions"},{"t":"water and dichloromethane","note":"polar vs nonpolar layer"}]},{"text":"**Sort by charge first.** An ion, cation or anion, stays in water: it cannot leave its shell of water dipoles for a nonpolar solvent."},{"text":"**Then by polarity.** A neutral molecule with no polar groups, such as a hydrocarbon, moves into the organic layer."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in an extraction, like dissolves like: a neutral nonpolar compound goes to the organic layer, and an ionized species stays in water."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-39a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe5-cp:39#1","pe5-cp:39#2"],"q":"Enzyme activity is plotted against log₁₀ of the inhibitor concentration in molar units, as shown. The inhibitor concentration giving half-maximal activity is closest to:","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Enzyme activity versus inhibitor concentration","x":{"label":"log₁₀([I] / M)","min":-8,"max":-3,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Activity (% of uninhibited)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Activity","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[-8,99],[-7.5,97],[-7,91],[-6.5,76],[-6,50],[-5.5,24],[-5,9],[-4.5,3],[-4,1],[-3,0]]}]},"choices":["0.1 μM","1 μM","6 μM","50 μM"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"units","why":"One decade off: that is ten times too little inhibitor, where activity is still about 90%."},{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"6 is the x-axis log value; [I] = 10^(log value) M, never the log number itself."},{"c":3,"tag":"copied","why":"50 is the activity percentage on the y-axis, not a concentration; read the x-axis."}],"rule":"Find 50% activity on the y-axis, read its x value (log [I]), then [I] = 10^x M; each x unit is a factor of 10.","exp":"Read across from **50% activity** to the curve and down: **log[I] = −6**. The x-axis is the log of the molar concentration, so [I] = 10⁻⁶ M, and 10⁻⁶ M × (10⁶ μM per M) = **1 μM**.","how":{"ask":"Find 50% activity, read the x-value below it, then undo the log and convert M to μM.","know":"• On a log-scale plot, the x-value is **log₁₀[I]**: [I] = 10^x in the axis unit.\n• **Check the unit** on the axis label (here M) before converting: 1 μM = 10⁻⁶ M.\n• The half-maximal point (IC₅₀) is read straight down from 50% activity.","steps":[{"text":"Find half-maximal activity on the y-axis and drop to the x-axis.","hl":"half-maximal activity","calc":["50% activity → log₁₀([I]/M) = **−6**"]},{"text":"Undo the logarithm in the axis unit, then convert.","calc":["[I] = 10⁻⁶ M","10⁻⁶ M × 10⁶ μM/M = **1 μM**"]},{"text":"0.1 μM is 10⁻⁷ M, one decade off: it is the point where activity is still about 90%.","strike":["0.1 μM"]},{"text":"6 μM reads the log value as if it were a concentration, and 50 μM reads the percentage instead of the x-axis.","strike":["6 μM","50 μM"]},{"text":"Answer: 1 μM."}],"trap":"Reporting the axis number itself (6) as a concentration: the x-axis is a log, so the concentration is 10 raised to it."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"log₁₀ of the inhibitor concentration","note":"x is a log"},{"t":"molar units","note":"axis unit is M"},{"t":"half-maximal activity","note":"read at 50%"}]},{"text":"**Read across, then down.** Go to 50% on the y-axis, across to the curve, then straight down to the x-axis."},{"text":"**Undo the log and mind the units.** The axis gives log₁₀([I]/M); raise 10 to it to get molar, then convert.","calc":["x = −6 → 10⁻⁶ M","10⁻⁶ M = 1 μM","x = −5 would be 10⁻⁵ M = 10 μM"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** on an activity-versus-log[I] curve, find 50% activity, drop to the x-axis, raise 10 to that value in the axis unit, then convert to the asked unit (10⁻⁶ M = 1 μM)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-40a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:40#1","pe5-cp:40#2","pe5-cp:40#3"],"q":"A dimeric enzyme binds its substrate with a Hill coefficient of 0.6. What does this value indicate about its binding?","choices":["Positive cooperativity","Negative cooperativity","No cooperativity","Exactly two binding sites"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Positive cooperativity needs n > 1; 0.6 is below 1."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"No cooperativity means n = 1 exactly, and 0.6 isn't 1."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"The Hill coefficient isn't a site count; it measures how binding at one site shifts the others."}],"rule":"Hill n above 1: one binding helps the next. n = 1: independent sites. n below 1: one binding hinders the next. n isn't a site count.","exp":"A Hill coefficient **n < 1** means **negative cooperativity**: binding at one site lowers the affinity of the others, so the curve rises quickly and then flattens out early. n > 1 would be positive cooperativity (sigmoidal), and n = 1 independent sites (hyperbolic). n is not a count of sites; it can only reach the number of sites in the limit of perfect positive cooperativity.","how":{"ask":"Compare the Hill coefficient with 1.","know":"• **n < 1**: negative cooperativity (one binding makes the next harder).\n• **n > 1**: positive cooperativity (one binding makes the next easier; sigmoidal curve).\n• **n = 1**: no cooperativity (independent sites; hyperbolic curve).\n• n measures interaction between sites; it is at most the number of sites, never a direct count.","steps":[{"text":"Compare n with 1.","hl":"Hill coefficient of 0.6","calc":["n = 0.6 < 1 → **negative cooperativity**"]},{"text":"Positive cooperativity needs n > 1, and no cooperativity needs n = 1 exactly.","strike":["Positive cooperativity","No cooperativity"]},{"text":"n is not the number of sites: a dimer has two sites, but n = 0.6 only says each binding hinders the next, and a tetramer could show n = 0.6 too.","strike":["Exactly two binding sites"]},{"text":"Answer: negative cooperativity."}],"trap":"Reading n as the number of binding sites, or calling any n other than 1 positive: n below 1 means the sites hinder each other."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words and numbers**.","marks":[{"t":"dimeric","note":"two subunits"},{"t":"Hill coefficient of 0.6","note":"compare with 1"}]},{"text":"**n < 1: negative cooperativity.** Binding at one site makes binding at the next harder, so the curve rises fast and then flattens out early (the n = 0.6 curve).","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Fractional saturation for three Hill coefficients (same half-saturation, 4 μM)","x":{"label":"[S] (μM)","min":0,"max":20,"step":4},"y":{"label":"Fractional saturation","min":0,"max":1,"step":0.2},"series":[{"name":"n = 2.5","marker":"square","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,0.03],[2,0.15],[3,0.33],[4,0.5],[6,0.73],[8,0.85],[12,0.94],[16,0.97],[20,0.98]]},{"name":"n = 1","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,0.2],[2,0.33],[3,0.43],[4,0.5],[6,0.6],[8,0.67],[12,0.75],[16,0.8],[20,0.83]]},{"name":"n = 0.6","marker":"triangle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,0.3],[2,0.4],[3,0.46],[4,0.5],[6,0.56],[8,0.6],[12,0.66],[16,0.7],[20,0.72]]}]}},{"text":"**n > 1: positive cooperativity.** Binding at one site makes binding at the next easier, so the curve is sigmoidal (the n = 2.5 curve)."},{"text":"**n = 1: no cooperativity.** The sites bind independently, and the curve is a plain hyperbola, like a monomer's."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** read the Hill coefficient against 1: n > 1 is positive cooperativity, n < 1 negative cooperativity, n = 1 none (independent sites)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-40b","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:40#4"],"q":"Binding curves are plotted for two oxygen carriers, X and Y. Which carrier binds with no cooperativity, and what Hill coefficient does its curve imply?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Fractional saturation versus ligand concentration","x":{"label":"[L] (μM)","min":0,"max":20,"step":4},"y":{"label":"Fractional saturation","min":0,"max":1,"step":0.2},"series":[{"name":"X","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,0.33],[2,0.5],[3,0.6],[4,0.67],[5,0.71],[6,0.75],[8,0.8],[10,0.83],[15,0.88],[20,0.91]]},{"name":"Y","marker":"square","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,0.01],[2,0.07],[3,0.19],[4,0.35],[5,0.5],[6,0.62],[8,0.79],[10,0.87],[15,0.96],[20,0.98]]}]},"choices":["Y; equal to 1","X; greater than 1","X; equal to 1","Y; greater than 1"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Y's curve is S-shaped, the mark of cooperativity, so it isn't the noncooperative carrier."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"No cooperativity means n = 1 exactly; n > 1 is positive cooperativity, so this pairing contradicts itself."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Y's S-curve does mean n > 1, but that is cooperative, which the question rules out."}],"rule":"Hyperbolic curve (steep from the start, no lag): no cooperativity, n = 1. S-shaped curve: positive cooperativity, n > 1.","exp":"X traces a **hyperbola**: it rises steeply from the start and bends over smoothly toward full saturation, with no lag. That shape means every site binds independently, **no cooperativity, n = 1**. Y's curve is **S-shaped (sigmoidal)**, the mark of positive cooperativity (n > 1), even though Y reaches half saturation later (about 5 μM vs about 2 μM for X).","how":{"ask":"Look at the shape of each curve, not where it reaches half saturation.","know":"• **Sigmoidal** (S-shaped) binding curve → positive cooperativity, **n > 1**, typical of multi-subunit proteins such as hemoglobin.\n• **Hyperbolic** curve → no cooperativity, **n = 1** (like myoglobin).\n• The half-saturation point measures **affinity**, not cooperativity: a carrier can bind tightly and still be noncooperative.","steps":[{"text":"X rises steeply from the start and bends over, with no flat lag: a hyperbola.","calc":["X: 0.33 at 1 μM → 0.50 at 2 μM → 0.83 at 10 μM"]},{"text":"A hyperbola means independent sites, so n = 1 exactly, not n > 1.","strike":["X; greater than 1"]},{"text":"Y starts flat, then rises steeply: an S shape, so Y is the cooperative one (n > 1) and is not the carrier asked about.","calc":["Y: 0.07 at 2 μM → 0.50 at 5 μM → 0.87 at 10 μM"],"strike":["Y; equal to 1","Y; greater than 1"]},{"text":"Answer: X; equal to 1."}],"trap":"Picking Y because it is half-saturated at a higher [L], as if weaker binding meant no cooperativity: half saturation is affinity; only the shape shows cooperativity."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"no cooperativity","note":"look at the shape"},{"t":"Hill coefficient","note":"n vs 1"}]},{"text":"**Shape, not position.** A sigmoid means binding gets easier as sites fill; a hyperbola means every site binds the same way."},{"text":"**Half saturation is affinity.** X reaches 0.5 at about 2 μM and Y at about 5 μM, so X binds more tightly, but tight binding says nothing about cooperativity: X is noncooperative and Y cooperative."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a sigmoidal binding curve means positive cooperativity (n > 1) and a hyperbolic curve means none (n = 1), whatever their half-saturation points."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-41a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:41#1"],"q":"In the pathway W → X → Y → Z, enzymes K, L and M catalyze the steps in order. Which knockout builds up pigment Y but stops toxic Z?","choices":["Enzyme K","Enzyme L","Enzyme M","Enzymes K and M"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Knocking out K stops W → X, upstream of everything, so no Y pigment builds up."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"L makes Y (X → Y); without it, Y can't build up at all."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Adding the K knockout shuts the pathway off upstream of Y, so no pigment is made."}],"rule":"A knockout piles up the substrate of the blocked step: to build up an intermediate, block the step right after it.","exp":"To keep an intermediate but block what it becomes, **inhibit the enzyme that consumes it**. Enzyme M converts Y → Z, so knocking it out stops Z while Y piles up. Knocking out K or L stops the pathway before Y is made, so no pigment forms.","how":{"ask":"Which enzyme uses up Y?","know":"• In a linear pathway, an intermediate **accumulates** when the enzyme that **consumes** it is blocked.\n• Blocking any step **before** the intermediate stops its own production, so it is lost as well.","steps":[{"text":"Map each enzyme to its step.","hl":"W → X → Y → Z","calc":["K: W → X","L: X → Y","M: Y → Z"]},{"text":"Y is made by L and used up by M; block M and Y can only accumulate, while Z can no longer form.","hl":"stops toxic Z"},{"text":"Knocking out K or L shuts the pathway off upstream of Y, so no pigment is made; adding K to the M knockout has the same flaw.","strike":["Enzyme K","Enzyme L","Enzymes K and M"]},{"text":"Answer: Enzyme M."}],"trap":"Blocking the enzyme that makes the desired product (L): that removes Y instead of saving it."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"W → X → Y → Z","note":"linear pathway"},{"t":"K, L and M","note":"one enzyme per step"},{"t":"builds up pigment Y but stops toxic Z","note":"the goal"}]},{"text":"**Block the exit, not the entrance.** An intermediate piles up when its consuming step is blocked and production continues."},{"text":"**Upstream blocks lose everything downstream.** If K or L is missing, Y is never made, and neither is Z."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to accumulate an intermediate of a linear pathway, inhibit the enzyme that consumes it (the step right after it); blocking an earlier step loses the intermediate too."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-43a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:43#1","pe5-cp:43#3","pe5-cp:43#4"],"q":"Cysteine desulfhydrase breaks down cysteine: HSCH₂–CH(NH₃⁺)–COO⁻ + H₂O → CH₃–CO–COO⁻ + NH₄⁺ + ___. What is the missing product?","choices":["H₂S","HS⁻","H₂S + H⁺","SO₂"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Charge check: the sides are already neutral, and HS⁻ adds a − charge and is one H short."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Charge check: both sides are already neutral, so adding H⁺ leaves the right side +1."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Atom check: the 3 O on the left all end up in pyruvate, so no O atoms remain for SO₂."}],"rule":"Balance atoms, then charge: count each element and the net charge on both sides before picking the missing species.","exp":"Count atoms and charge. Left: C₃H₉NO₃S, charge 0. Right so far: pyruvate C₃H₃O₃⁻ plus NH₄⁺ = C₃H₇NO₃, charge 0. The leftover is **S + 2 H with no charge = H₂S**.","how":{"ask":"Subtract the known products' atoms and charge from the reactants'; whatever is left is the missing product.","know":"• Atoms and charge are both conserved.\n• **Missing product = reactant atoms − known-product atoms**, with the leftover charge.\n• Then name the leftover (S + 2 H, neutral = H₂S; N + 4 H, +1 = NH₄⁺) and recheck the total charge.","steps":[{"text":"Total the reactants.","hl":"HSCH₂–CH(NH₃⁺)–COO⁻ + H₂O","calc":["cysteine C₃H₇NO₂S (charge 0) + H₂O","= **C₃H₉NO₃S**, charge 0"]},{"text":"Total the known products.","calc":["pyruvate C₃H₃O₃⁻ + NH₄⁺","= **C₃H₇NO₃**, charge 0"]},{"text":"Subtract.","calc":["C₃H₉NO₃S − C₃H₇NO₃ = **H₂S**, charge 0 − 0 = 0"]},{"text":"HS⁻ has one H too few and a − charge the sides do not need; adding H⁺ leaves a + charge unbalanced; SO₂ adds O atoms that are not available.","strike":["HS⁻","H₂S + H⁺","SO₂"]},{"text":"Answer: H₂S."}],"trap":"Balancing only the atoms: a choice can carry the right sulfur yet break the charge balance."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"HSCH₂–CH(NH₃⁺)–COO⁻","note":"zwitterion, net 0"},{"t":"NH₄⁺","note":"known product, +1"},{"t":"missing product","note":"atom bookkeeping"}]},{"text":"**Count the left side.** Cysteine C₃H₇NO₂S plus water.","calc":["C₃H₉NO₃S, charge 0"]},{"text":"**Count the right side.** Pyruvate (−1) and ammonium (+1).","calc":["C₃H₇NO₃, charge 0"]},{"text":"**Name the leftover.** S and 2 H, no charge: H₂S."},{"text":"**Also:** the same subtraction names an ammonium ion: a leftover of one N, four H and a +1 charge is NH₄⁺, as when an amino acid loses its amino group."},{"text":"**Also:** finish by checking charge: here 0 on the left and −1 + 1 + 0 = 0 on the right."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** missing product = reactant atoms − known-product atoms; name what is left, then confirm the total charge matches on both sides."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-43b","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:43#2"],"q":"Urease hydrolyzes urea in water: (NH₂)₂CO + ___ + H⁺ → 2 NH₄⁺ + HCO₃⁻. Which reactant completes the balanced equation?","choices":["2 H₂O","3 H₂O","1 O₂","2 OH⁻"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Atom check: 3 H₂O brings one O and two H more than the right side holds."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"No H: the right side has 9 H vs 5 on the left, and O₂ adds none."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Charge check: the right side is +1 (2+ and 1−), and 2 OH⁻ would make the left −1."}],"rule":"Balance O first, then H, then check charge: both sides must match atom for atom and in total charge.","exp":"The right side has 3 O and 9 H; urea and H⁺ supply only 1 O and 5 H. **Water supplies the missing O and H**: 2 H₂O adds 2 O and 4 H, giving CH₉N₂O₃ with charge +1 on both sides.","how":{"ask":"Find which side is short of O and H, and add water to fix both.","know":"• In aqueous (biochemical) reactions, **H₂O supplies missing O and H** (hydrolysis).\n• Add water to the side short of O, then recheck H and charge.","steps":[{"text":"Count the products.","hl":"2 NH₄⁺ + HCO₃⁻","calc":["2 NH₄⁺: N₂H₈ (+2); HCO₃⁻: HCO₃ (−1)","total C H₉ N₂ O₃, charge +1"]},{"text":"Count the known reactants.","calc":["(NH₂)₂CO: CH₄N₂O; H⁺: H (+1)","total C H₅ N₂ O, charge +1"]},{"text":"The left is short 2 O and 4 H, which is exactly 2 H₂O.","calc":["missing: H₄O₂ = 2 H₂O"]},{"text":"3 H₂O adds too many atoms, O₂ brings no H, and 2 OH⁻ brings only 2 H and a 2− charge.","strike":["3 H₂O","1 O₂","2 OH⁻"]},{"text":"Answer: 2 H₂O."}],"trap":"Adding O₂ to fix the oxygen count: in a hydrolysis the extra O arrives with H, as water."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"hydrolyzes","note":"water is a reactant"},{"t":"2 NH₄⁺ + HCO₃⁻","note":"3 O, 9 H"}]},{"text":"**Find the shortfall.** Compare O and H on both sides.","calc":["O: 3 right vs 1 left → need 2","H: 9 right vs 5 left → need 4"]},{"text":"**Fill it with water.** 2 O and 4 H together are 2 H₂O; the charge stays +1 on each side."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in an aqueous reaction, add H₂O to the side short of O, then recheck H and charge."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-44a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:44#1","pe5-cp:44#2","pe5-cp:44#3","pe6-cp:55#1","pe6-cp:55#2","pe6-cp:55#3"],"q":"Reactions with barriers under 35 kJ/mol run within seconds. Based on the free-energy profile shown, the forward reaction is:","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Free energy along the reaction coordinate","x":{"label":"Reaction progress","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Free energy (kJ/mol)","min":0,"max":90,"step":10},"series":[{"name":"Reaction","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,50],[1,50],[2,53],[3,61],[4,71],[5,75],[6,68],[7,48],[8,30],[9,21],[10,20]]}]},"choices":["fast and spontaneous","slow and spontaneous","fast and nonspontaneous","slow and nonspontaneous"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Sign: products (20) end below reactants (50), so ΔG < 0 and the reaction is spontaneous."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Products sit below reactants: downhill means spontaneous, so nonspontaneous fails here too."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"The barrier is peak minus reactants (75 − 50), under the 35 kJ/mol cutoff, so it isn't slow."}],"rule":"Speed from the barrier (peak − reactants vs the cutoff); spontaneity from the ends (products − reactants < 0). Read each separately.","exp":"Read three heights: reactants **50**, peak **75**, products **20 kJ/mol**. **Ea = 75 − 50 = 25 kJ/mol**, under 35, so it is fast. **ΔG = 20 − 50 = −30 kJ/mol**, below zero, so it is spontaneous.","how":{"ask":"Read Ea (peak − reactants) for the speed and ΔG (products − reactants) for spontaneity, separately.","know":"• **Ea = transition state − reactants**: a low Ea means a fast reaction.\n• **ΔG = products − reactants**: ΔG < 0 means spontaneous.\n• Speed (kinetics) and spontaneity (thermodynamics) are independent: read each from its own pair of heights.","steps":[{"text":"Read the three heights from the plot.","calc":["reactants = **50 kJ/mol**","peak = **75 kJ/mol**","products = **20 kJ/mol**"]},{"text":"Speed from the barrier.","hl":"barriers under 35 kJ/mol","calc":["Ea = 75 − 50 = **25 kJ/mol** < 35 → **fast**"]},{"text":"A low barrier rules out both slow choices.","strike":["slow and spontaneous","slow and nonspontaneous"]},{"text":"Spontaneity from the ends.","calc":["ΔG = 20 − 50 = **−30 kJ/mol** < 0 → **spontaneous**"],"strike":["fast and nonspontaneous"]},{"text":"Answer: fast and spontaneous."}],"trap":"Measuring the barrier from the products (75 − 20 = 55 kJ/mol): Ea for the forward reaction starts at the reactants."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words and numbers**.","marks":[{"t":"free-energy profile","note":"reactants left, products right"},{"t":"barriers under 35 kJ/mol","note":"cutoff for fast"}]},{"text":"**Fast means low Ea.** The barrier height above the reactants sets the rate.","calc":["Ea = peak − reactants"]},{"text":"**Spontaneous means ΔG < 0.** Products lower than reactants is downhill.","calc":["ΔG = products − reactants"]},{"text":"**Also:** if no profile is given, infer the same two facts from words: \"occurs within seconds\" or \"rapid\" means a low Ea; \"favored\", \"releases free energy\" or \"proceeds to completion\" means ΔG < 0."},{"text":"**Also:** a solid that sits unchanged until a spark or shock sets it off is two things at once. Thermodynamically it is favorable: reacting releases heat, so products lie below reactants (exothermic). Kinetically it is stuck behind a tall barrier (a large Ea), so nothing happens until a trigger supplies that energy. Read any energy diagram with the same two subtractions: end heights give ΔH on an enthalpy diagram (ΔG on a free-energy diagram), peak minus reactants for Ea.","calc":["explosive: reactants 30, peak 200, products −250 kJ/mol","ΔH = −250 − 30 = −280 kJ/mol → exothermic","Ea = 200 − 30 = 170 kJ/mol → high: needs a trigger"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** on an energy profile, the rate is set by Ea (peak − reactants; low = fast) and spontaneity by ΔG (products − reactants; < 0 = spontaneous); read each separately."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-45a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:45#1","pe5-cp:45#2"],"q":"Glutamic acid, shown fully protonated with its acidic groups labeled A–C, is titrated with NaOH from a strongly acidic solution. Which group loses its proton second?","fig":"cpt-pe5-glu-sites","choices":["Group B","Group A","Group C","All at once"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"A is the α-NH₃⁺ (pKa ≈ 9.7), the weakest acid, so it gives up its H⁺ last."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"C is the α-COOH (pKa ≈ 2), the strongest acid, so it loses its H⁺ first, not second."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Separate pKa values (≈ 2, 4, 10) mean the groups lose H⁺ one at a time, not together."}],"rule":"Protons leave in pKa order, lowest first: α-COOH (≈ 2), side-chain COOH (≈ 4), then α-NH₃⁺ (≈ 10).","exp":"Groups lose H⁺ in order of **increasing pKa**: the α-COOH (C, ≈ 2.2) first, then the **side-chain COOH (B, ≈ 4.3)**, then the α-NH₃⁺ (A, ≈ 9.7). The pKa values are far apart, so the groups titrate one after another.","how":{"ask":"Rank the labeled groups by pKa; the second-lowest pKa loses its proton second.","know":"• As base is added, groups deprotonate in order of **increasing pKa**.\n• Typical pKa: α-COOH ≈ 2, side-chain COOH ≈ 4, α-NH₃⁺ ≈ 9–10, basic side chains (Lys ε-NH₃⁺ ≈ 10.5, Arg ≈ 12.5) last.","steps":[{"text":"Identify each label.","hl":"labeled A–C","calc":["A = α-NH₃⁺ (≈ 9.7)","B = side-chain COOH (≈ 4.3)","C = α-COOH (≈ 2.2)"]},{"text":"Order by pKa.","calc":["C (2.2) < B (4.3) < A (9.7)","first C, **second B**, third A"]},{"text":"C goes first, A goes last, and the well-separated pKa values rule out a single step.","strike":["Group A","Group C","All at once"]},{"text":"Answer: group B, the side-chain carboxyl."}],"trap":"Picking the side-chain group first because it is farther out: the α-COOH is the most acidic, pulled by the nearby NH₃⁺."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"fully protonated","note":"every group holds its H⁺"},{"t":"titrated with NaOH","note":"rising pH"},{"t":"second","note":"second-lowest pKa"}]},{"text":"**Lowest pKa goes first.** The α-COOH (≈ 2) is made extra acidic by the neighboring α-NH₃⁺."},{"text":"**Then the side-chain acid.** Glutamate's γ-COOH has pKa ≈ 4.3.","calc":["order: α-COOH → side-chain COOH → α-NH₃⁺"]},{"text":"**Also:** in a basic amino acid the order ends with the side chain: lysine goes α-COOH (≈ 2.2), α-NH₃⁺ (≈ 9.0), then ε-NH₃⁺ (≈ 10.5)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a fully protonated amino acid loses H⁺ in order of increasing pKa: α-COOH, acidic side chain, α-NH₃⁺, basic side chain."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-46a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe5-cp:46#1","pe5-cp:46#2"],"q":"The enthalpy profile shown compares a reaction uncatalyzed and with an enzyme. What are the catalyzed forward activation energy and ΔH?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Enthalpy along the reaction coordinate, uncatalyzed and catalyzed","x":{"label":"Reaction progress","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Enthalpy (kJ/mol)","min":0,"max":160,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Uncatalyzed","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,30],[1,30],[2,40],[3,72],[4,118],[5,140],[6,128],[7,100],[8,75],[9,62],[10,60]]},{"name":"Catalyzed","marker":"square","line":"smooth","points":[[0,30],[1,30],[2,36],[3,55],[4,80],[5,90],[6,85],[7,74],[8,65],[9,61],[10,60]]}]},"choices":["60 kJ/mol; +30 kJ/mol","110 kJ/mol; +30 kJ/mol","50 kJ/mol; −30 kJ/mol","90 kJ/mol; +30 kJ/mol"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Sign: the products sit above the reactants on the profile, so ΔH is positive, not negative."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"That's the catalyzed peak's height above zero; subtract the reactants' energy to get the barrier."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"That barrier uses the uncatalyzed peak (140); the enzyme's path is the lower peak."}],"rule":"Ea = peak − reactants on the curve asked about; ΔH = products − reactants, and a catalyst lowers the peak but never changes ΔH.","exp":"Catalyzed **Ea = 90 − 30 = 60 kJ/mol**: the climb from the reactant level to the enzyme's lower peak. **ΔH = 60 − 30 = +30 kJ/mol** (endothermic) on both paths: an enzyme lowers the barrier but leaves the reactant and product levels where they were.","how":{"ask":"Measure Ea from the reactants to the catalyzed peak; measure ΔH from the reactants to the products.","know":"• **Ea = transition-state energy − reactant energy**, using the peak of the path asked about.\n• **ΔH = products − reactants**; a catalyst lowers the peak but leaves both ends, so ΔH is the same on either path.","steps":[{"text":"Read the levels.","calc":["reactants = **30**","uncatalyzed peak = **140**","catalyzed peak = **90**","products = **60** kJ/mol"]},{"text":"Catalyzed activation energy.","hl":"catalyzed forward activation energy","calc":["Ea(cat) = 90 − 30 = **60 kJ/mol**"]},{"text":"110 kJ/mol uses the uncatalyzed peak (140 − 30), and 90 kJ/mol is the peak's height above zero, not above the reactants.","strike":["110 kJ/mol; +30 kJ/mol","90 kJ/mol; +30 kJ/mol"]},{"text":"Enthalpy change, the same on both paths.","calc":["ΔH = 60 − 30 = **+30 kJ/mol**"]},{"text":"50 kJ/mol is only how far the enzyme lowers the peak (140 − 90), and −30 flips the sign of ΔH by subtracting products from reactants.","strike":["50 kJ/mol; −30 kJ/mol"]},{"text":"Answer: 60 kJ/mol; +30 kJ/mol."}],"trap":"Reading the peak's height straight off the axis (90): Ea is measured from the reactant level, not from zero."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"uncatalyzed and with an enzyme","note":"two paths"},{"t":"forward activation energy","note":"from reactants up"},{"t":"ΔH","note":"products − reactants"}]},{"text":"**Ea: reactants to the peak of the path asked.** The enzyme's curve has the lower peak.","calc":["Ea = TS − reactants = 90 − 30 = 60 kJ/mol","uncatalyzed: 140 − 30 = 110 kJ/mol"]},{"text":"**Also:** ΔH = products − reactants, and both curves start and end at the same levels, so ΔH is the same with or without a catalyst.","calc":["ΔH = 60 − 30 = +30 kJ/mol (endothermic)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** Ea = transition-state energy − reactant energy (using the catalyzed peak for the catalyzed path) and ΔH = products − reactants, which a catalyst never changes."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-47a","s":"Mechanics & Energy","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:47#1","pe5-cp:47#2"],"q":"Crates of 3.0 kg and 7.0 kg hang on a rope over an ideal pulley. Taking g = 10 m/s², what is their acceleration?","choices":["4.0 m/s²","5.7 m/s²","13 m/s²","10 m/s²"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"impossible","why":"Faster than free fall is impossible for a hanging pair; a can't exceed g = 10 m/s²."},{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"Divides the net force by the heavier crate alone; one force moves both masses, so divide by the total."},{"c":3,"tag":"copied","why":"That's g copied from the stem, free fall; the lighter crate holds the pair back, so a must be less than g."}],"rule":"a = (net weight) ÷ (total mass), always below g. Round: (7 − 3) × 10 ÷ (7 + 3).","exp":"Only the **weight difference** drives the pair, and it must accelerate **both** crates: a = (m₂ − m₁)g/(m₁ + m₂) = (7.0 − 3.0)(10) ÷ 10 = **4.0 m/s²**. That is less than g, as it must be for a hanging pair.","how":{"ask":"Find the net force on the two-crate system, then divide by all the mass it moves.","know":"• **Newton's second law for the system:** a = F_net ÷ m_total.\n• Atwood machine: F_net = m₂g − m₁g (heavier weight minus lighter weight); the rope tension is internal and cancels.\n• So **a = (m₂ − m₁)g/(m₁ + m₂)**, always less than g.","steps":[{"text":"Read the masses and g.","hl":"Crates of 3.0 kg and 7.0 kg","calc":["m₁ = 3.0 kg, m₂ = 7.0 kg, g = 10 m/s²"]},{"text":"Net force: the heavier crate's weight minus the lighter crate's weight.","calc":["F_net = m₂g − m₁g","F_net = (7.0 − 3.0) kg × 10 m/s²","F_net = **40 N**"]},{"text":"Divide by the total mass, because the rope makes both crates move together.","calc":["a = F_net ÷ (m₁ + m₂)","a = 40 N ÷ 10 kg","a = **4.0 m/s²**"]},{"text":"5.7 m/s² divides the 40 N by the heavier crate alone; 13 m/s² divides it by the lighter crate alone. Both forget that one force moves both masses.","strike":["5.7 m/s²","13 m/s²"]},{"text":"10 m/s² is free fall; the lighter crate's weight holds the pair back, so a must be less than g.","strike":["10 m/s²"]},{"text":"Answer: 4.0 m/s²."}],"trap":"Dividing the net force by only one of the masses; the rope links them, so the 40 N accelerates all 10 kg."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the givens**.","marks":[{"t":"3.0 kg","note":"lighter mass m₁"},{"t":"7.0 kg","note":"heavier mass m₂"},{"t":"g = 10 m/s²","note":"use this g"}]},{"text":"**Treat both crates as one system.** The rope pulls up on each crate and cancels inside the system; only the two weights act from outside, in opposite senses."},{"text":"**Also: net force ÷ total mass.** The driving force is the weight difference, and it must accelerate everything that moves.","calc":["F_net = (7.0 − 3.0)(10) = 40 N","m_total = 3.0 + 7.0 = 10 kg","a = 40 ÷ 10 = 4.0 m/s²"]},{"text":"**Check the limits.** Equal masses give a = 0; a very light partner gives a close to g. Any answer at or above g is wrong."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for two masses over a pulley, a = (m₂ − m₁)g/(m₁ + m₂): the weight difference divided by the total mass."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-48a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:48#1","pe5-cp:48#2"],"q":"A heating curve follows 2.0 g of ice warmed steadily from −20 °C to steam at 110 °C. Which step absorbs the least heat?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Heating curve for 2.0 g of water","x":{"label":"Heat added (kJ)","min":0,"max":7,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Temperature (°C)","min":-20,"max":120,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Water","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,-20],[0.08,0],[0.75,0],[1.59,100],[6.11,100],[6.15,110]]}]},"choices":["Warming the ice","Melting the ice","Boiling the water","Warming the steam"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Ice warms through 20 °C, twice the steam's 10 °C span, with a similar specific heat, so it takes more heat."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"A phase change: melting takes q = mL with L = 334 J/g, far more than either warming step."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Phase changes take the most heat; boiling's plateau is the widest on the curve."}],"rule":"Phase changes (q = mL) dwarf warming steps; between warming steps compare c × ΔT, the temperature span times the specific heat.","exp":"With heat on the x-axis, a segment's heat is its **width**. Warming the steam spans only 10 °C: **2.0 g × 2.01 J/(g·°C) × 10 °C ≈ 40 J**, less than warming the ice 20 °C (2.0 × 2.09 × 20 = 84 J), melting (2.0 × 334 = 668 J) or boiling (2.0 × 2260 = 4520 J).","how":{"ask":"Compute q for each listed step (m·L for plateaus, m·c·ΔT for slopes) and find the smallest.","know":"• Plateau (phase change): **q = m·L**. Water: L_fus = 334 J/g, L_vap = 2260 J/g.\n• Sloped segment (one phase warming): **q = m·c·ΔT**. c(ice) ≈ 2.09, c(liquid) = 4.18, c(steam) ≈ 2.01 J/(g·°C).\n• On a heating curve with heat on the x-axis, each step's heat is its **width**.","steps":[{"text":"Find the mass and the range.","hl":"2.0 g of ice","calc":["m = 2.0 g; −20 °C → 110 °C"]},{"text":"Phase changes, q = m·L: both plateaus are wide.","calc":["melt: 2.0 × 334 = **668 J**","boil: 2.0 × 2260 = **4520 J**"],"strike":["Melting the ice","Boiling the water"]},{"text":"The two short warming steps, q = m·c·ΔT: similar specific heats, but the ice warms through 20 °C and the steam through only 10 °C.","calc":["ice: 2.0 × 2.09 × 20 = **84 J**","steam: 2.0 × 2.01 × 10 = **40 J**","40 J < 84 J"],"strike":["Warming the ice"]},{"text":"Answer: warming the steam, about 40 J."}],"trap":"Choosing warming the ice because both short slopes look equally thin on the graph: compute m·c·ΔT; the ice spans 20 °C and the steam only 10 °C, with nearly equal c."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"2.0 g of ice","note":"the mass"},{"t":"−20 °C","note":"starts below melting"},{"t":"steam at 110 °C","note":"ends above boiling"}]},{"text":"**Compare the three kinds of step.** Plateaus use latent heat; slopes use specific heat.","calc":["q = m·L_fus, q = m·c·ΔT, q = m·L_vap"]},{"text":"**Small slopes need numbers.** When two segments look equally thin, compare m·c·ΔT: ice and steam have nearly the same c (≈ 2 J/(g·°C)), so the smaller ΔT takes less heat.","calc":["ice: ΔT = 20 °C → 84 J","steam: ΔT = 10 °C → 40 J"]},{"text":"**Also:** vaporization is by far the largest step for water: per gram it costs 2260 J, versus 334 J to melt and 418 J to warm the liquid all the way from 0 to 100 °C.","calc":["2260 ÷ 418 ≈ 5.4","2260 ÷ 334 ≈ 6.8"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** compare q = m·L_fus, m·c·ΔT and m·L_vap for each heating step; for water, vaporization (2260 J/g) is by far the largest, and a short warming step with a small ΔT is the smallest."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-49a","s":"Fluids","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:49#1","pe5-cp:49#2","pe5-cp:49#3"],"q":"A robot's square foot pad, 4.0 cm on a side, presses straight down on the floor with 72 N. What pressure does it exert?","choices":["45 kPa","450 kPa","4.5 kPa","450 Pa"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"units","why":"Too big: that's 72 N on just 1.6 cm², ten times too little area; the pad is 16 cm²."},{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"That's 72 ÷ 16, a value in N/cm² mislabeled as kPa; convert cm² to m² first."},{"c":3,"tag":"units","why":"Area slip: that uses 1 cm² = 10⁻² m²; one cm² is really 10⁻⁴ m²."}],"rule":"P = F ÷ A with A in m², and 1 cm² = 10⁻⁴ m². Round: 70 N ÷ 16 cm² ≈ 4 N/cm², then × 10⁴ for Pa.","exp":"Pressure is force over area in square metres, **P = F/A**. The pad is 0.040 m × 0.040 m = 1.6 × 10⁻³ m², so P = 72 N ÷ 0.0016 m² = 45,000 Pa = **45 kPa**. One cm² is 10⁻⁴ m², not 10⁻² m².","how":{"ask":"Take the force from the sentence, find the pad's area in square metres, then divide.","know":"• **Pressure: P = F/A**, in Pa = N/m².\n• **1 cm = 10⁻² m, so 1 cm² = 10⁻⁴ m²**: multiply the side lengths in cm, then × 10⁻⁴.\n• 1 kPa = 10³ Pa.","steps":[{"text":"Pull the force out of the sentence: it is the push straight down on the floor.","hl":"presses straight down on the floor with 72 N","calc":["F = **72 N** (perpendicular to the floor)"]},{"text":"Area in cm², then in m².","hl":"4.0 cm on a side","calc":["A = 4.0 cm × 4.0 cm = 16 cm²","A = 16 × 10⁻⁴ m² = **1.6 × 10⁻³ m²**"]},{"text":"Divide the force by the area.","calc":["P = F ÷ A","P = 72 N ÷ (1.6 × 10⁻³ m²)","P = 4.5 × 10⁴ Pa = **45 kPa**"]},{"text":"450 Pa converts the area with 10⁻² instead of 10⁻⁴; 4.5 kPa is 72 ÷ 16, a value in N/cm² mislabeled as kPa; 450 kPa slips one extra power of ten into the area.","strike":["450 Pa","4.5 kPa","450 kPa"]},{"text":"Answer: 45 kPa."}],"trap":"Squaring the length but not the conversion: 1 cm² is (10⁻² m)² = 10⁻⁴ m²."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the givens**.","marks":[{"t":"square foot pad","note":"area = side × side"},{"t":"4.0 cm on a side","note":"convert to m"},{"t":"72 N","note":"force F"},{"t":"What pressure","note":"P in Pa"}]},{"text":"**P = F/A.** Pressure is the force spread over each square metre of contact."},{"text":"**Also: convert cm² to m².** Multiply the sides in cm, then × 10⁻⁴.","calc":["4.0 × 4.0 = 16 cm²","16 cm² × 10⁻⁴ = 0.0016 m²","72 ÷ 0.0016 = 45,000 Pa = 45 kPa"]},{"text":"**Also: take F from the passage.** A passage states the force in a sentence; pick the number in newtons that describes the push on the surface, here the 72 N pressing straight down."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** P = F/A with A in m²; for sides in cm, multiply them and then × 10⁻⁴ before dividing."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-50a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:50#1","pe5-cp:50#2"],"q":"A 30 μF capacitor charged to 800 V holds 9.6 J. It empties fully through a heating coil in 3.0 ms. What average current flows?","choices":["8.0 A","8.0 mA","3.2 kA","24 mA"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"units","why":"The time was left in milliseconds; 3.0 ms is 0.0030 s, so the current is 1000 times off."},{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"J ÷ s gives watts, a power, not amperes: that divides the stored energy, not the charge, by the time."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"That's the charge, Q = 0.024 C, relabeled; a current needs Q ÷ t."}],"rule":"Current is charge per second: Q = CV, then divide by t in seconds; the joules are a distractor. Estimate 30 μ × 800 ≈ 0.024 C.","exp":"**Q = CV** = 30 × 10⁻⁶ F × 800 V = 0.024 C. **I = Q/t** = 0.024 C ÷ (3.0 × 10⁻³ s) = **8.0 A**. The 9.6 J of stored energy is not needed.","how":{"ask":"Find the charge that leaves the plates, then divide it by the discharge time in seconds.","know":"• **Q = CV** (C in farads, V in volts, Q in coulombs).\n• **Average current: I = Q/t** (A = C/s).\n• **μ = 10⁻⁶, m = 10⁻³**: convert μF and ms first.","steps":[{"text":"Convert the capacitance to farads.","hl":"30 μF","calc":["C = 30 μF = 30 × 10⁻⁶ F"]},{"text":"Find the charge on the plates.","hl":"charged to 800 V","calc":["Q = CV","Q = 30 × 10⁻⁶ F × 800 V","Q = **0.024 C**"]},{"text":"All of that charge flows out in 3.0 ms; convert the time and divide.","hl":"in 3.0 ms","calc":["t = 3.0 ms = 3.0 × 10⁻³ s","I = Q ÷ t","I = 0.024 C ÷ (3.0 × 10⁻³ s)","I = **8.0 A**"]},{"text":"8.0 mA divides by 3.0 with the time left in milliseconds; 24 mA is the charge, 0.024 C, read as a current without dividing by t.","strike":["8.0 mA","24 mA"]},{"text":"3.2 kA divides the energy by the time, 9.6 J ÷ 0.0030 s, which is a power in watts, not a current.","strike":["3.2 kA"]},{"text":"Answer: 8.0 A."}],"trap":"Reaching for the stored energy: current is charge per time, so use Q = CV and skip the joules."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the givens**.","marks":[{"t":"30 μF","note":"C: convert μ"},{"t":"800 V","note":"V"},{"t":"9.6 J","note":"energy: not needed"},{"t":"3.0 ms","note":"t: convert m"},{"t":"average current","note":"I = Q/t"}]},{"text":"**Q = CV.** The stored charge is capacitance × voltage, in farads × volts.","calc":["Q = 30 × 10⁻⁶ × 800 = 0.024 C"]},{"text":"**Also: average current = charge ÷ time**, with μF → 10⁻⁶ F and ms → 10⁻³ s.","calc":["3.0 ms = 0.0030 s","I = 0.024 ÷ 0.0030 = 8.0 A"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for a capacitor discharge, I_avg = Q/t = CV/t, with μF → 10⁻⁶ F and ms → 10⁻³ s; the stored energy is a distractor."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-51a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:51#1","pe5-cp:51#2"],"q":"A parallel-plate capacitor stays wired to a 12 V battery. What change raises the amount of charge its plates hold?","choices":["Inserting a glass sheet","Moving the plates apart","Cutting the plates smaller","Swapping to a 6 V battery"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"C = κε₀A/d: a bigger d lowers C, so Q = CV falls."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Smaller plates shrink A, which lowers C, so the charge drops instead of rising."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Q = CV: halving V halves Q, so a smaller battery can't raise the charge."}],"rule":"With the battery attached, Q = CV: only a change that raises C (bigger A, smaller d, a dielectric) or raises V adds charge.","exp":"The battery holds V at 12 V, so the charge **Q = CV** can grow only through a larger C. Since **C = κε₀A/d**, a glass sheet (κ ≈ 5) raises C about fivefold, and the battery moves about five times the charge onto the plates.","how":{"ask":"Hold V fixed and decide which change raises C = κε₀A/d.","know":"• **Q = CV**; with the battery connected, **V is fixed**.\n• **C = κε₀A/d**: C rises with larger area A, smaller separation d, or a dielectric (κ > 1).\n• At fixed C, a higher V also raises Q.","steps":[{"text":"The battery stays connected, so V is fixed and Q follows C.","hl":"stays wired to a 12 V battery","calc":["Q = C × 12 V"]},{"text":"A glass sheet is a dielectric (κ ≈ 5): C rises about fivefold, and so does Q.","hl":"Inserting a glass sheet","calc":["C' = κC ≈ 5C","Q' = 5C × 12 V = 5Q"]},{"text":"Moving the plates apart raises d, and cutting the plates shrinks A; both lower C and so lower Q.","strike":["Moving the plates apart","Cutting the plates smaller"]},{"text":"A 6 V battery halves V, which halves Q at the same C.","strike":["Swapping to a 6 V battery"]},{"text":"Answer: inserting a glass sheet."}],"trap":"Thinking a dielectric lowers the charge because it weakens the field: that happens only in an isolated capacitor; with the battery connected, V and E stay fixed and the plates take on κ times the charge."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"parallel-plate capacitor","note":"C = κε₀A/d"},{"t":"stays wired","note":"V fixed"},{"t":"12 V battery","note":"V"},{"t":"amount of charge","note":"Q = CV"}]},{"text":"**Q = CV and C = κε₀A/d.** At fixed V, anything that raises C raises Q."},{"text":"**Also: the four ways up.** More plate area, a smaller gap, a dielectric, or a higher voltage each increase Q.","calc":["A ×2 → Q ×2","d ×½ → Q ×2","κ = 5 → Q ×5","V ×2 → Q ×2"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** with Q = CV and C = κε₀A/d, charge rises with larger area, smaller separation, an inserted dielectric, or a higher voltage."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-52a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:52#1"],"q":"A 15 μF capacitor charged to 800 V is swapped for a 40 μF capacitor. What voltage would give the new one the same charge?","choices":["300 V","2100 V","800 V","490 V"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Ratio upside-down: a bigger capacitor needs less voltage for the same Q, so V must drop below 800 V."},{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"That keeps the old voltage; the same V on a bigger C puts more charge on it (Q = CV)."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"That matches the stored energy (½CV²), a square-root ratio; equal charge needs Q = CV."}],"rule":"Same charge means C₁V₁ = C₂V₂: a bigger capacitor needs a smaller voltage. Estimate 800 × 15/40, about 800 × 0.4.","exp":"**Same Q: C₁V₁ = C₂V₂.** Q = 15 μF × 800 V = 0.012 C, so V₂ = 0.012 C ÷ 40 μF = **300 V**. A larger capacitor needs proportionally less voltage to hold the same charge.","how":{"ask":"Write the charge on the first capacitor and solve for the voltage that puts that charge on the second.","know":"• **Q = CV**; equal charge means **C₁V₁ = C₂V₂**.\n• So **V₂ = C₁V₁/C₂**: a bigger C needs less V.\n• The μF units cancel in the ratio C₁/C₂.","steps":[{"text":"Find the charge on the first capacitor.","hl":"A 15 μF capacitor charged to 800 V","calc":["Q = C₁V₁ = 15 × 10⁻⁶ F × 800 V","Q = **0.012 C**"]},{"text":"Solve for V₂.","hl":"a 40 μF capacitor","calc":["V₂ = C₁V₁ ÷ C₂","V₂ = (15 μF × 800 V) ÷ 40 μF","V₂ = **300 V**"]},{"text":"2100 V multiplies by C₂/C₁ instead of C₁/C₂; 800 V keeps the old voltage, which would put more charge on the larger capacitor.","strike":["2100 V","800 V"]},{"text":"490 V matches the stored energy (½CV²) instead of the charge.","calc":["800 V × √(15/40) ≈ 490 V"],"strike":["490 V"]},{"text":"Answer: 300 V."}],"trap":"Flipping the ratio: a larger capacitor holds the same charge at a lower voltage, so V₂ must come out below 800 V."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the givens**.","marks":[{"t":"15 μF","note":"C₁"},{"t":"800 V","note":"V₁"},{"t":"40 μF","note":"C₂"},{"t":"same charge","note":"Q₁ = Q₂"}]},{"text":"**Equal charge: C₁V₁ = C₂V₂.** Since Q = CV, set the two charges equal."},{"text":"**Solve as a ratio.** The μF cancel.","calc":["V₂ = 800 V × 15/40","V₂ = 800 V × 0.375 = 300 V"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for equal charge on two capacitors, V₂ = C₁V₁/C₂; the larger capacitor needs proportionally less voltage."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-54a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe5-cp:54#1","pe5-cp:54#2"],"q":"An ultraviolet photon carries three times the energy of an infrared photon. Compared with the infrared photon, the ultraviolet photon has:","choices":["3× the frequency; ⅓ the wavelength.","3× the frequency; 3× the wavelength.","⅓ the frequency; 3× the wavelength.","9× the frequency; ⅓ the wavelength."],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"λ = c/f, so the wavelength must fall when the frequency rises; it can't triple too."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"E = hf: more energy means more frequency, never ⅓ of it."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"E = hf is linear, with nothing squared: tripling the energy triples the frequency, it doesn't make it 9×."}],"rule":"E = hf and λ = c/f: energy and frequency change by the same factor, and wavelength changes by the inverse factor.","exp":"**E = hf**: energy is proportional to frequency, so 3× the energy means **3× the frequency**. **E = hc/λ**: energy is inversely proportional to wavelength, so 3× the energy means **⅓ the wavelength**.","how":{"ask":"Scale frequency and wavelength by the energy factor.","know":"• **E = hf** (h is a constant): f ∝ E.\n• **E = hc/λ** (c fixed): λ ∝ 1/E.\n• A photon with k times the energy has k times the frequency and 1/k the wavelength.","steps":[{"text":"The energy factor is k = 3.","hl":"three times the energy"},{"text":"Frequency scales directly with energy.","calc":["f = E/h → f(UV) = 3 × f(IR)"]},{"text":"Wavelength scales inversely with energy.","calc":["λ = hc/E → λ(UV) = λ(IR) ÷ 3"]},{"text":"Wavelength cannot rise with energy, frequency cannot fall, and nothing squares here.","strike":["3× the frequency; 3× the wavelength.","⅓ the frequency; 3× the wavelength.","9× the frequency; ⅓ the wavelength."]},{"text":"Answer: 3× the frequency and ⅓ the wavelength."}],"trap":"Scaling wavelength the same way as frequency: since c = fλ is fixed, they always move in opposite directions."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"three times the energy","note":"k = 3"},{"t":"ultraviolet photon","note":"higher-energy one"}]},{"text":"**Frequency follows energy.** E = hf, so f is proportional to E.","calc":["E × 3 → f × 3"]},{"text":"**Wavelength opposes energy.** E = hc/λ, so λ is inversely proportional to E.","calc":["E × 3 → λ ÷ 3"]},{"text":"**Also:** a photon with k times the energy has 1/k the wavelength, because f and λ multiply to the fixed speed c."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** E = hf = hc/λ, so k times the energy means k times the frequency and 1/k the wavelength."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-55a","s":"Light & Optics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:55#1","pe5-cp:55#2"],"q":"A diode emits blue-green light with a wavelength of 480 nm in air. Approximately what is the frequency of this light?","choices":["6.3 × 10¹⁴ Hz","6.3 × 10⁵ Hz","6.3 × 10¹⁷ Hz","1.6 × 10⁻¹⁵ Hz"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"units","why":"Skips the nm → m step (10⁻⁹); 10⁵ Hz is radio, far below visible light."},{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"Uses picometres instead of nanometres, 1000 times too high: 10¹⁷ Hz is X-ray, not visible light."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Flipped: that's λ ÷ c, a time in seconds; frequency is c ÷ λ."}],"rule":"f = c/λ with λ in metres (1 nm = 10⁻⁹ m). Estimate 3 × 10⁸ ÷ 5 × 10⁻⁷; visible light is always hundreds of terahertz.","exp":"**f = c/λ** with λ in metres: 480 nm = 4.8 × 10⁻⁷ m, so f = (3 × 10⁸ m/s) ÷ (4.8 × 10⁻⁷ m) ≈ **6.3 × 10¹⁴ Hz**, inside the visible band of about 4–7.5 × 10¹⁴ Hz.","how":{"ask":"Convert the wavelength to metres, then divide the speed of light by it.","know":"• **c = fλ**, so **f = c/λ**, with c ≈ 3 × 10⁸ m/s and λ in **metres**.\n• 1 nm = 10⁻⁹ m.\n• Visible light (about 400–750 nm) has f ≈ 4–7.5 × 10¹⁴ Hz, so any visible answer must land near 10¹⁴–10¹⁵ Hz.","steps":[{"text":"Convert the wavelength to metres.","hl":"480 nm","calc":["480 nm × 10⁻⁹ m/nm = 4.8 × 10⁻⁷ m"]},{"text":"Divide c by λ.","calc":["f = (3 × 10⁸ m/s) ÷ (4.8 × 10⁻⁷ m)","= (3 ÷ 4.8) × 10¹⁵ Hz","= 0.625 × 10¹⁵ ≈ **6.3 × 10¹⁴ Hz**"]},{"text":"Check the band: blue-green light must sit between 4 and 7.5 × 10¹⁴ Hz. 10⁵ Hz skips the nm conversion (radio range), 10¹⁷ Hz uses picometres (X-ray range), and 10⁻¹⁵ divides λ by c.","strike":["6.3 × 10⁵ Hz","6.3 × 10¹⁷ Hz","1.6 × 10⁻¹⁵ Hz"]},{"text":"Answer: 6.3 × 10¹⁴ Hz."}],"trap":"Dividing by 480 instead of 4.8 × 10⁻⁷ m: keep λ in metres, and use the visible band as a sanity check."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and its unit.","marks":[{"t":"480 nm","note":"convert to m"},{"t":"blue-green light","note":"visible: expect ~10¹⁴ Hz"}]},{"text":"**Fix the unit.** Speed of light is in m/s, so λ must be in m.","calc":["480 × 10⁻⁹ m = 4.8 × 10⁻⁷ m"]},{"text":"**Divide.**","calc":["f = c/λ = 3 × 10⁸ ÷ 4.8 × 10⁻⁷ ≈ 6.3 × 10¹⁴ Hz"]},{"text":"**Also:** visible light spans about 400–750 nm, which is about 7.5 × 10¹⁴ down to 4 × 10¹⁴ Hz; a visible-light frequency far outside 10¹⁴–10¹⁵ Hz signals a unit error."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** f = c/λ with λ in metres (nm × 10⁻⁹), then check that visible light lands near 4–7.5 × 10¹⁴ Hz."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-57a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe5-cp:57#1","pe5-cp:57#2","pe5-cp:57#3"],"q":"Hypochlorous acid, HOCl, has a pKa of 7.5 in water. What is Kb of its conjugate base, the hypochlorite ion?","choices":["3.2 × 10⁻⁷","3.2 × 10⁻⁸","3.2 × 10⁻²²","6.5"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Kb = Kw/Ka; multiplying Ka by Kw instead gives 10⁻²², far too small."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"6.5 is pKb (14 − 7.5); the last step, Kb = 10^−pKb, was skipped. Kb for a weak base is a small power of ten."},{"c":1,"tag":"copied","why":"That's Ka of the acid itself, 10^−7.5, taken straight from the given pKa; the base's Kb is different."}],"rule":"Conjugate pairs: pKa + pKb = 14 (Ka·Kb = 10⁻¹⁴), so the weaker the acid, the larger its conjugate base's Kb.","exp":"**pKa + pKb = 14**, so pKb = 14 − 7.5 = 6.5, and **Kb = 10^−pKb** = 10⁻⁶·⁵ ≈ **3.2 × 10⁻⁷**. Check: Ka·Kb = (3.2 × 10⁻⁸)(3.2 × 10⁻⁷) ≈ 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ = Kw.","how":{"ask":"Get pKb from the pKa, then turn pKb into Kb.","know":"• For a conjugate pair: **pKa + pKb = 14** and **Ka·Kb = Kw = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴** (at 25 °C).\n• **Kb = 10^−pKb**.\n• A weaker acid (higher pKa) has a stronger conjugate base (larger Kb).","steps":[{"text":"Start from the acid's pKa.","hl":"pKa of 7.5","calc":["pKb = 14 − 7.5 = **6.5**"]},{"text":"Convert pKb to Kb.","calc":["Kb = 10⁻⁶·⁵ = 10⁰·⁵ × 10⁻⁷ ≈ 3.16 × 10⁻⁷ ≈ **3.2 × 10⁻⁷**"]},{"text":"Check with Ka·Kb = Kw.","calc":["Ka = 10⁻⁷·⁵ ≈ 3.2 × 10⁻⁸","Kb = Kw/Ka = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴ ÷ 3.2 × 10⁻⁸ ≈ 3.2 × 10⁻⁷ ✓"]},{"text":"3.2 × 10⁻⁸ is Ka itself, 3.2 × 10⁻²² multiplies Ka by Kw instead of dividing, and 6.5 is pKb, not Kb.","strike":["3.2 × 10⁻⁸","3.2 × 10⁻²²","6.5"]},{"text":"Answer: 3.2 × 10⁻⁷."}],"trap":"Stopping at pKb = 6.5: the question asks for Kb, so finish with Kb = 10^−pKb."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"pKa of 7.5","note":"the acid's strength"},{"t":"Kb of its conjugate base","note":"base strength"}]},{"text":"**Use the pair rule.**","calc":["pKa + pKb = 14 → pKb = 6.5"]},{"text":"**Undo the log.**","calc":["Kb = 10⁻⁶·⁵ ≈ 3.2 × 10⁻⁷"]},{"text":"**Also:** Kb = 10^−pKb; a half-unit pKb gives 10⁰·⁵ ≈ 3.16 in front of the power of ten."},{"text":"**Also:** Ka·Kb = Kw, so Kb = Kw/Ka; the weaker the acid (smaller Ka), the stronger its conjugate base."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** pKb = 14 − pKa and Kb = 10^−pKb (equivalently Kb = Kw/Ka)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe5-59a","s":"Mechanics & Energy","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe5-cp:59#1","pe5-cp:59#2"],"q":"A robot drags a sled 12 m on a level track. The graph shows its parallel pull and the friction on the sled. How much work does the pull do?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Forces on the sled versus position","x":{"label":"Position (m)","min":0,"max":12,"step":2},"y":{"label":"Force (N)","min":0,"max":50,"step":10},"series":[{"name":"Pull","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[2,20],[4,40],[6,40],[8,40],[10,40],[11,20],[12,0]]},{"name":"Friction","marker":"square","line":"straight","points":[[0,5],[12,5]]}]},"choices":["360 J","480 J","300 J","240 J"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"skipped","why":"40 N × 12 m treats the pull as 40 N all the way, but the graph ramps up and down."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"6 m × 40 N counts only the flat top and drops both ramp triangles."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"That subtracts friction's 5 N × 12 m: it is the net work, but the question asks for the pull's work alone."}],"rule":"Work by one force = area under that force's line on a force-vs-distance graph; count ramps as triangles, ½ × base × height.","exp":"Work done by a changing force is the **area under its F–x line**. Split the pull into a triangle, a rectangle and a triangle: ½(4)(40) + (6)(40) + ½(2)(40) = 80 + 240 + 40 = **360 J**. Friction is not part of the pull's work.","how":{"ask":"Find the area under the pull line only, from 0 to 12 m.","know":"• **W = area under the F–x graph** (N × m = J) for a force along the motion.\n• Split the area into **rectangles (base × height)** and **triangles (½ × base × height)**.\n• Use only the force the question names.","steps":[{"text":"Read the corners of the pull line off the graph.","hl":"How much work does the pull do","calc":["0 → 4 m: rises 0 → 40 N","4 → 10 m: flat at 40 N","10 → 12 m: falls 40 → 0 N"],"graph":{"focus":["Pull"]}},{"text":"Find the area of each piece.","calc":["ramp up: ½ × 4 m × 40 N = 80 J","plateau: 6 m × 40 N = 240 J","ramp down: ½ × 2 m × 40 N = 40 J"],"graph":{"focus":["Pull"],"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":12,"text":"area = work by the pull"}]}},{"text":"Add the pieces.","calc":["W = 80 J + 240 J + 40 J = **360 J**"]},{"text":"480 J is 40 N × 12 m, treating the pull as 40 N the whole way; 240 J keeps only the flat part.","strike":["480 J","240 J"]},{"text":"300 J subtracts friction's 5 N × 12 m = 60 J, which gives the net work, not the pull's work.","strike":["300 J"]},{"text":"Answer: 360 J."}],"trap":"Multiplying the peak force by the whole distance; the ramps are triangles, worth only half of their rectangles."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"12 m","note":"total distance"},{"t":"parallel pull","note":"all of the pull does work"},{"t":"work does the pull do","note":"pull only, not friction"}]},{"text":"**Work is force × distance**, but this pull changes with position, so no single force works. Instead, the **work equals the area under the pull line**.","graph":{"focus":["Pull"],"shade":[{"x1":0,"x2":12,"text":"area = work"}]}},{"text":"**Also: split the shape into rectangles and triangles.** Here: a rising ramp, a flat top and a falling ramp.","calc":["triangle: ½ × base × height","rectangle: base × height"]},{"text":"**Ignore the friction line.** It shares the axes, but the question asks only about the pull.","graph":{"labels":[{"series":"Friction","x":6,"text":"friction: not asked","at":"above"}]}},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** the work by a varying force is the area under the line of that force on an F–x plot; cut it into rectangles and triangles, add the pieces, and use only the force asked about."}]},
  // ── Practice Exam 6 · Chem/Phys ──
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-01a","s":"Spectroscopy","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:1#1"],"q":"An unknown six-carbon liquid gives the IR spectrum shown. Which compound matches the spectrum?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"IR spectrum of the unknown liquid","x":{"label":"Wavenumber (cm⁻¹)","min":500,"max":4000,"step":500},"y":{"label":"Transmittance (%)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Unknown","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[500,88],[600,80],[800,72],[1000,45],[1070,30],[1150,70],[1250,78],[1350,62],[1450,55],[1550,88],[1650,92],[1700,92],[1800,92],[2000,94],[2500,92],[2800,85],[2860,45],[2930,30],[3000,65],[3100,50],[3200,30],[3350,15],[3450,22],[3550,45],[3650,82],[3800,92],[4000,94]]}]},"choices":["Cyclohexanol","Cyclohexanone","Cyclohexene","Hexanoic acid"],"choiceFigs":["cyclohexanol","cyclohexanone","cpt-pe6-cyclohexene","cpt-pe6-hexanoic-acid"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"A ketone's C=O makes a deep, sharp dip near 1700 cm⁻¹; this trace stays above 90% there."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"An alkene has no O–H, so it can't make the broad, deep dip centered near 3350 cm⁻¹."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"A carboxylic acid has a C=O too, so it would dip hard near 1700 cm⁻¹; the trace stays flat there."}],"rule":"Check two IR windows: a broad deep dip near 3300 cm⁻¹ means O–H; a sharp strong dip near 1700 means C=O. Keep the compound matching both.","exp":"The trace has a **broad, deep dip centered near 3350 cm⁻¹ (O–H)** and **nothing near 1700 cm⁻¹ (no C=O)**, plus C–H dips just under 3000 and a C–O dip near 1070. An O–H with no C=O is an alcohol: cyclohexanol. A carboxylic acid would also show a strong C=O near 1710.","how":{"ask":"Is there a broad dip between 3200 and 3600 cm⁻¹, and is there a sharp dip near 1700 cm⁻¹?","know":"• IR windows: **O–H** broad, 3200–3600 cm⁻¹; **N–H** 3300–3500, narrower; **C–H** 2850–3100; **C=O** sharp and strong, ~1700; **C=C** weaker, ~1650.\n• Carboxylic acid = C=O plus a very broad O–H that spreads down toward 2500.\n• Alcohol = O–H with no C=O; ketone = C=O with no O–H.","steps":[{"text":"Check the O–H window first: a broad, deep dip centered near 3350 cm⁻¹.","hl":"IR spectrum shown","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Unknown","x":3350}],"shade":[{"x1":3200,"x2":3600,"text":"O–H window"}]},"calc":["3350 cm⁻¹: about **15 %** transmittance → strong, broad O–H"]},{"text":"Now the C=O window: the trace stays near the top at 1700 cm⁻¹, so there is no carbonyl. That removes the ketone and the acid.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Unknown","x":1700}],"shade":[{"x1":1650,"x2":1780,"text":"C=O window"}]},"calc":["1700 cm⁻¹: about **92 %** → no C=O"],"strike":["Cyclohexanone","Hexanoic acid"]},{"text":"An alkene has no O–H; its weak C=C band would sit near 1650 and could not make the deep dip at 3350.","strike":["Cyclohexene"]},{"text":"Also: the other windows. N–H (3300–3500) needs nitrogen, which no choice has; the C–H dips at 2850–2930 appear for all four compounds, so they never decide."},{"text":"Answer: cyclohexanol.","fig":"cyclohexanol"}],"trap":"Seeing a broad O–H and picking the acid: an acid always shows a strong C=O near 1700 too, and this spectrum has none."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"six-carbon liquid","note":"all choices are C6"},{"t":"IR spectrum","note":"which bonds are present"}]},{"text":"**Read dips, not peaks.** Low transmittance means a bond absorbed that light."},{"text":"**Know five windows.**","calc":["O–H: 3200–3600, broad","N–H: 3300–3500","C–H: 2850–3100","C=O: ~1700, sharp","C=C: ~1650, weak"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** check 3200–3600 cm⁻¹ for a broad O–H and ~1700 for a sharp C=O: O–H alone = alcohol, C=O alone = ketone or aldehyde, both (with a very broad O–H) = carboxylic acid."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-01b","s":"Spectroscopy","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:1#2"],"q":"Cyclohexanol is heated with H₃PO₄ to make cyclohexene, and IR spectra are recorded before and after, as plotted. Which spectral change confirms that the alcohol was consumed?","fig":["cyclohexanol","cpt-pe6-cyclohexene"],"chart":{"type":"line","caption":"IR spectra before and after the dehydration","x":{"label":"Wavenumber (cm⁻¹)","min":500,"max":4000,"step":500},"y":{"label":"Transmittance (%)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Before","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[500,86],[600,82],[800,78],[1000,60],[1100,30],[1200,70],[1380,58],[1460,55],[1600,90],[1720,90],[1800,92],[2000,94],[2500,92],[2800,85],[2880,45],[2970,30],[3050,60],[3150,45],[3250,25],[3350,15],[3450,22],[3550,48],[3650,85],[4000,94]]},{"name":"After","marker":"square","line":"smooth","points":[[500,88],[600,85],[800,80],[1000,82],[1170,75],[1300,80],[1370,60],[1460,58],[1600,90],[1650,68],[1720,90],[1780,88],[2000,94],[2500,93],[2800,88],[2880,50],[2980,35],[3025,62],[3150,90],[3300,93],[3600,94],[4000,95]]}]},"choices":["Broad dip near 3350 cm⁻¹ disappears","New sharp dip near 1720 cm⁻¹","C–H dips near 2950 cm⁻¹ all disappear","Broad dip near 3350 cm⁻¹ deepens"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Direction flipped: the alcohol is used up, so its O–H band should weaken, not grow."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"1720 is the C=O window; losing water makes a C=C, not a carbonyl, and neither trace dips there."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Cyclohexene still has many C–H bonds, so dips near 2950 stay in both traces; they cannot vanish."}],"rule":"Confirm a reaction by the band of the group it removes or adds; C–H bands stay in any hydrocarbon product.","exp":"Heating with acid removes water: **C–OH plus a neighbor H becomes C=C**. So the **broad O–H dip near 3350 cm⁻¹ (Before) disappears** After, and at most a weak C=C dip appears near 1650. The C–H dips near 2950 stay in both traces, and no C=O dip appears at 1720.","how":{"ask":"Which band is present in the Before trace but missing from the After trace?","know":"• Follow a reaction by IR through the band that appears or disappears.\n• Alcohol → alkene: lose the broad O–H (3200–3600 cm⁻¹); a weak C=C (~1650 cm⁻¹) may appear.\n• Bands present in both spectra (C–H near 2950) say nothing about the change.","steps":[{"text":"Hot H₃PO₄ removes H₂O: the –OH and an H on the next carbon leave, and a C=C forms.","hl":"heated with H₃PO₄","fig":["cyclohexanol","cpt-pe6-cyclohexene"]},{"text":"Compare at 3350 cm⁻¹: a broad deep dip Before, flat After. The O–H is gone.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Before","x":3350},{"series":"After","x":3350}],"shade":[{"x1":3200,"x2":3600,"text":"O–H window"}]},"calc":["Before at 3350: ~15 % → **O–H**","After at 3350: ~93 % → no O–H"],"strike":["Broad dip near 3350 cm⁻¹ deepens"]},{"text":"Compare at 1720 cm⁻¹: flat in both traces. No C=O forms in a dehydration.","graph":{"read":[{"series":"Before","x":1720},{"series":"After","x":1720}]},"calc":["Before at 1720: ~90 %","After at 1720: ~90 % → no C=O"],"strike":["New sharp dip near 1720 cm⁻¹"]},{"text":"C–H dips sit near 2950 in both traces: the alkene still has C–H bonds.","strike":["C–H dips near 2950 cm⁻¹ all disappear"]},{"text":"Answer: the broad dip near 3350 cm⁻¹ disappears."}],"trap":"Looking for a new band only: here the proof is a band that vanishes, since the product lost the –OH and gained only a weak C=C."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Cyclohexanol","note":"alcohol: O–H"},{"t":"heated with H₃PO₄","note":"dehydration: loses H₂O"},{"t":"before and after","note":"compare the traces"}]},{"text":"**What changes in the molecule?** C–OH and a neighbor C–H become C=C: the O–H bond is lost."},{"text":"**Map each change to a window.**","calc":["lost O–H → broad dip 3200–3600 disappears","gained C=C → weak dip ~1650 may appear"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to follow a reaction by IR, watch the band that appears or vanishes; the broad 3200–3600 cm⁻¹ dip tracks –OH: it appears when –OH is gained and vanishes when it is lost."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-02a","s":"Spectroscopy","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe6-cp:2#1","pe6-cp:2#2"],"q":"A mixture of two isomers is analyzed by ¹H NMR. Isomer A's unique 9H singlet integrates to 27 units, and isomer B's unique 2H quartet integrates to 4. What is the A : B mole ratio?","choices":["3 : 2","2 : 3","27 : 4","9 : 2"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Ratio upside-down: the question puts A first, so this is B : A."},{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"27 : 4 compares raw areas; A's peak has 9 H per molecule and B's only 2, so each area must be divided by its H count."},{"c":3,"tag":"copied","why":"9 : 2 is the H count of each peak, fixed by structure, not by how much of each isomer is present."}],"rule":"Mole ratio from NMR: divide each peak's area by the H it stands for, then compare in the order the question names.","exp":"An integral scales with how many H make the peak, so divide each by its H count: A = 27 ÷ 9 = 3, B = 4 ÷ 2 = 2. The mole ratio is **A : B = 3 : 2**. IR could not do this: isomers with the same functional groups give nearly the same bands.","how":{"ask":"How many molecules' worth of signal does each integral stand for once you divide by the H count of its peak?","know":"• ¹H NMR integral ∝ number of H nuclei making the peak.\n• Relative moles of a component = (integral of its unique peak) ÷ (H in that peak).\n• IR spots functional groups only; isomers that share groups give near-identical IR, so IR can't give a ratio.","steps":[{"text":"Take each isomer's own peak and its H count.","hl":"unique 9H singlet integrates to 27 units"},{"text":"Divide each area by its H count.","calc":["A: 27 ÷ 9 H = **3**","B: 4 ÷ 2 H = **2**","A : B = **3 : 2**"]},{"text":"Raw areas (27 : 4) overweight A's 9-H peak, and the H counts (9 : 2) ignore the areas entirely.","strike":["27 : 4","9 : 2"]},{"text":"The question puts A first, so the flipped 2 : 3 is B : A.","strike":["2 : 3"]},{"text":"Also: IR would be useless here. Both isomers carry identical functional groups, and their IR bands overlap and give identity, not amounts."},{"text":"Answer: 3 : 2."}],"trap":"Reading the integrals as the ratio directly: a big peak can just mean many equivalent H in each molecule."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"two isomers","note":"one unique peak each"},{"t":"9H singlet integrates to 27","note":"A: 27 for 9 H"},{"t":"2H quartet integrates to 4","note":"B: 4 for 2 H"},{"t":"mole ratio","note":"molecules, not H"}]},{"text":"**Area counts H.** Every H gives the same area, so area ÷ H per molecule = relative number of molecules."},{"text":"**Also:** IR identifies functional groups, which constitutional isomers often share, so it cannot measure how much of each is present."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for a mixture ratio, divide each unique ¹H NMR integral by its number of H, then compare; IR gives identity, not amounts."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-03a","s":"Reaction Mechanisms","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:3#1","pe6-cp:3#2","pe6-cp:3#3"],"q":"With BF₃, 2,2-dimethyloxirane, shown, rearranges to the aldehyde 2-methylpropanal. Which carbocation forms when the epoxide ring opens?","fig":"cpt-pe6-dimethyloxirane","choices":["Tertiary cation on C2","Primary cation on C1","Secondary cation on C1","Carbanion on C2"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Charge flipped: a Lewis acid pulls electron density toward O, so the carbon left behind is electron-poor, never a carbanion."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"A primary cation is the least stable; the ring opens at the carbon that holds + charge best, not at the CH₂."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"C1 is a CH₂ bonded to only one other carbon, so a cation there would be primary, not secondary."}],"rule":"Lewis-acid ring opening breaks the C–O bond that leaves the most stable carbocation: tertiary > secondary > primary.","exp":"BF₃ binds the epoxide O, and the C–O bond that breaks is the one leaving the **more stable cation**: the **tertiary** one on C2. A **1,2-hydride shift** from C1 then moves the + onto C1, where the O lone pair forms **C=O**: (CH₃)₂CH–CHO, 2-methylpropanal.","how":{"ask":"Which C–O bond breaks so that the cation left behind is as stable as possible, and does that cation lead to the aldehyde?","know":"• A Lewis acid (BF₃) takes a lone pair of the epoxide O, weakening both C–O bonds.\n• The ring opens toward the **more stable carbocation**: 3° > 2° > 1°, and benzylic or allylic beats plain alkyl.\n• A **1,2-hydride (or alkyl) shift** from the C–O carbon to the cation then lets O form C=O on that carbon: if it still carries an H after the shift the product is an aldehyde; if it carries only carbons, a ketone.","steps":[{"text":"BF₃ binds the ring oxygen. C1 is the CH₂; C2 holds both methyls.","hl":"2,2-dimethyloxirane","fig":"cpt-pe6-dimethyloxirane"},{"text":"Lewis-acid activation makes the carbon electron-poor, so the opened carbon is a cation.","hl":"BF₃","strike":["Carbanion on C2"]},{"text":"Compare the two possible cations. C1 has only one carbon neighbor, so it can only be primary.","calc":["break C2–O → C2⁺ with 3 carbon neighbors → **3°**","break C1–O → C1⁺ with 1 carbon neighbor → 1°"],"strike":["Primary cation on C1","Secondary cation on C1"],"fig":"cpt-pe6-oxirane-cation"},{"text":"Check it gives the product: an H shifts from C1 to C2⁺, and the O on C1 forms C=O.","hl":"2-methylpropanal","calc":["(CH₃)₂C⁺–CH₂–O–BF₃⁻","1,2-H shift → (CH₃)₂CH–CH⁺–O–BF₃⁻","lose BF₃ → (CH₃)₂CH–CH=O ✓"]},{"text":"Answer: the tertiary cation on C2."}],"trap":"Opening at the less hindered CH₂ as in a basic, SN2-type epoxide opening; with a Lewis acid the bond breaks toward the carbon that best holds a + charge."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"BF₃","note":"Lewis acid"},{"t":"2,2-dimethyloxirane","note":"C2 has two CH₃"},{"t":"aldehyde","note":"C=O at a chain end"},{"t":"ring opens","note":"one C–O bond breaks"}]},{"text":"**Lewis acid on O.** BF₃ accepts a lone pair from the epoxide O, turning the oxygen into a good leaving group.","fig":"cpt-pe6-dimethyloxirane"},{"text":"**Open toward the stable cation.**","calc":["C2: tertiary, three alkyl donors","C1: primary → much higher energy"],"fig":"cpt-pe6-oxirane-cation"},{"text":"**Also:** a hydride (H⁻) or alkyl group then shifts from the C–O carbon to the cation, and the O lone pair pushes in to form C=O. What that carbon keeps decides the product: an H left on it → aldehyde; only carbons → ketone. Here C1 is a CH₂: one H shifts, one H stays → aldehyde."},{"text":"**Also:** to choose the intermediate, work backward from the product: 2-methylpropanal has its C=O on the former CH₂ (C1), so the + must first sit on C2, and an H must move from C1 to C2."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** with a Lewis acid, an epoxide opens to its most stable carbocation, then a 1,2-shift from the C–O carbon builds the C=O (an H left on the C=O carbon → aldehyde; only carbons → ketone)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-04a","s":"Stereochemistry","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:4#1","pe6-cp:4#2"],"q":"The structure shows 3-methylhexan-1-ol with four carbons marked W, X, Y and Z. Which marked carbon is a chirality center?","fig":"cpt-pe6-methylhexanol-sites","choices":["W","X","Y","Z"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"The CH₂OH carbon has two H atoms: two identical groups rule it out."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A CH₃ carbon carries three identical hydrogens, so it can never be a chirality center."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Z is a CH₂ in the chain; its two hydrogens are identical groups, so it fails the four-groups test."}],"rule":"A chirality center is an sp³ carbon with four different groups: any CH₂ or CH₃ (repeated H) is out at a glance.","exp":"To be a **chirality center**, a tetrahedral (sp³) carbon must hold **four different groups**. **X (C3)** carries H, CH₃, CH₂CH₂OH and CH₂CH₂CH₃. W and Z are CH₂ carbons (two H each) and Y is a CH₃. With exactly one such center and no mirror plane, the molecule is **chiral**.","how":{"ask":"Check each marked carbon for four different attached groups.","know":"• **Chirality center**: sp³ carbon with **four different groups**.\n• Instant rejects: **CH₃, CH₂** (repeated H), and any **C=C, C=O or aromatic** carbon.\n• One chirality center and no internal mirror plane → the **molecule is chiral**.","steps":[{"text":"Look at the drawn structure and list the groups on each marked carbon.","hl":"3-methylhexan-1-ol","fig":"cpt-pe6-methylhexanol-sites"},{"text":"Reject carbons with repeated hydrogens.","calc":["W: CH₂OH → H, H ✗","Y: CH₃ → H, H, H ✗","Z: CH₂ → H, H ✗"]},{"text":"W, Y and Z each carry at least two hydrogens.","strike":["W","Y","Z"]},{"text":"Check the remaining carbon.","calc":["X: H, CH₃, CH₂CH₂OH, CH₂CH₂CH₃ → all different ✓"]},{"text":"Answer: X."}],"trap":"Picking the carbon that holds the OH: being next to a heteroatom doesn't help if it still has two H atoms."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"3-methylhexan-1-ol","note":"six-carbon chain, CH₃ on C3"},{"t":"chirality center","note":"four different groups"}]},{"text":"**The four-groups test.** An sp³ carbon is a chirality center only if all four substituents differ; two hydrogens on one carbon fail at once.","calc":["CH₃, CH₂: never","CH with three different other groups: yes"]},{"text":"**Compare the chains, not just the first atom.** On C3, both CH₂ neighbors start alike, but one leads to OH and the other to a CH₃ end, so they differ.","fig":"cpt-pe6-methylhexanol-sites"},{"text":"**Also:** a molecule with exactly one chirality center has no internal mirror plane, so it is chiral: its mirror image cannot be superimposed and the two are enantiomers."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** only a tetrahedral carbon holding four different groups can be a chirality center (skip every CH₂, CH₃ and double-bonded carbon); one such center and no mirror plane makes the molecule chiral."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-05a","s":"Mechanics & Energy","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:5#1","pe6-cp:5#2"],"q":"The graph tracks a cart's position along a level track over time. During which interval does the cart feel a net force directed opposite to its velocity?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Cart position over time","x":{"label":"Time (s)","min":0,"max":8,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Position (m)","min":0,"max":15,"step":3},"series":[{"name":"Cart","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,3],[2,6],[3,9],[3.5,10.4],[4,11.5],[4.5,12.4],[5,13],[5.5,13.4],[6,13.5],[7,13.5],[8,13.5]]}]},"choices":["1 to 3 s","3 to 6 s","6 to 8 s","3 to 8 s"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"From 1 to 3 s the line is straight, 3 m every second: constant velocity, so a = 0 and the net force is zero."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"After 6 s the line is flat: the cart sits still and stays still, so its acceleration and net force are zero."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"That range includes the flat stretch after 6 s, where the cart is at rest and feels no net force."}],"rule":"On a position-time graph, a straight line (or flat line) means zero net force; a curve that flattens out means slowing, force against v.","exp":"A force opposite the velocity **slows** the cart. On a position–time graph the slope is the velocity, so slowing shows as a curve that flattens: from 3 to 6 s the cart gains 2.5 m, then 1.5 m, then 0.5 m each second. Before 3 s the line is straight (a steady 3 m/s, no net force), and after 6 s it is flat (at rest), so no force acts after 6 s.","how":{"ask":"In which stretch does the slope of the position graph shrink while the cart is still moving forward?","know":"• Slope of an x–t graph = **velocity**; slope of a v–t graph = **acceleration**.\n• Straight x–t line → constant v → a = 0 → F_net = 0. A flattening curve → v falling → a and F_net point against the motion. A flat line → at rest.\n• Area under a v–t graph = **displacement**.","steps":[{"text":"A force opposite the velocity means the cart is slowing: look for the stretch where the slope gets smaller.","hl":"opposite to its velocity"},{"text":"Before 3 s the cart gains the same distance every second: a straight line.","calc":["slope = (9 − 0) m ÷ (3 − 0) s = **3 m/s**, constant","a = 0 → F_net = 0"],"strike":["1 to 3 s"]},{"text":"From 3 to 6 s the gains shrink, so the velocity (the slope) falls.","calc":["3 → 4 s: 9 → 11.5 m = +2.5 m","4 → 5 s: 11.5 → 13 m = +1.5 m","5 → 6 s: 13 → 13.5 m = +0.5 m","v: 3 m/s → 0 in 3 s → a = −3 ÷ 3 = **−1 m/s²**"]},{"text":"After 6 s the line is flat: v = 0 and stays 0, so there is no acceleration and no net force. So 3 to 8 s fails too: the force acts only while the cart slows, not while it sits still.","strike":["6 to 8 s","3 to 8 s"]},{"text":"Also: the area under a v–t graph is the displacement. This cart's v–t graph is a 3 m/s plateau for 3 s, then a straight drop to 0 over 3 s; its slope there is the −1 m/s² found above.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"The same cart: velocity versus time","x":{"label":"Time (s)","min":0,"max":8,"step":1},"y":{"label":"Velocity (m/s)","min":0,"max":4,"step":1},"series":[{"name":"Cart","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,3],[3,3],[6,0],[8,0]]}]},"calc":["0–3 s: rectangle 3 m/s × 3 s = **9 m**","3–6 s: triangle ½ × 3 s × 3 m/s = **4.5 m**","total 9 + 4.5 = **13.5 m**, the final position on the x–t graph"]},{"text":"Answer: 3 to 6 s."}],"trap":"Thinking the force is largest where the cart moves fastest (before 3 s): a steady velocity, however large, needs no net force; only a change in velocity does."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"position","note":"y axis: meters along the track"},{"t":"over time","note":"x axis: seconds"},{"t":"opposite to its velocity","note":"force against motion = slowing"}]},{"text":"**Slope of x–t = velocity.** Steep = fast, flat = stopped, a straight line = steady speed."},{"text":"**Slope of v–t = acceleration.** By F_net = ma, the net force points the way the velocity is changing.","calc":["speeding up: a along v","slowing down: a opposite v","steady v: a = 0, F_net = 0"]},{"text":"**Also:** the area under a v–t graph is the displacement. A cart holding 2 m/s for 4 s covers a rectangle of 2 × 4 = 8 m.","calc":["displacement = area under v–t"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** on x–t, slope = velocity (straight = constant v and zero net force; flattening = slowing, force against the motion); on v–t, slope = acceleration and area = displacement."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-06a","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:6#1","pe6-cp:6#2"],"q":"A DNA fragment and a positively charged dye are loaded into central wells of a gel. Positive positions on the plot point toward the + electrode. Which band is the DNA, and where is it heading?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Band positions during the run","x":{"label":"Time (min)","min":0,"max":60,"step":10},"y":{"label":"Position from the wells (cm)","min":-3,"max":5,"step":1},"series":[{"name":"Band X","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[10,0.7],[20,1.4],[30,2.1],[40,2.8],[50,3.5],[60,4.2]]},{"name":"Band Y","marker":"square","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[10,-0.4],[20,-0.8],[30,-1.2],[40,-1.6],[50,-2],[60,-2.4]]}]},"choices":["Band X; toward the anode","Band X; toward the cathode","Band Y; toward the anode","Band Y; toward the cathode"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Anode vs cathode flipped: the + electrode of a gel rig is the anode, and the plot's + side is where band X heads."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Band Y's position goes negative, toward the − electrode (the cathode), so it cannot be heading to the anode."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Band Y does head to the − electrode, so it carries + charge: that is the dye, not DNA."}],"rule":"Phosphates make DNA carry negative charge, so it heads for the + electrode (the anode); a cationic dye heads the other way.","exp":"Each nucleotide carries one negative phosphate, so DNA has a large **net negative charge** and is pulled toward the **positive electrode, the anode**. Band X climbs steadily on the + side (0.7 cm every 10 min), so X is the DNA; Band Y drifts toward the − electrode (the cathode), as a cation like the dye should.","how":{"ask":"Which band moves toward +, and what is the + electrode called in electrophoresis?","know":"• DNA's backbone has one negatively charged phosphate per nucleotide, so DNA is an anion at any working pH.\n• Opposite charges attract: anions move toward the + electrode, cations toward −.\n• In electrophoresis (an electrolytic setup) the + electrode is the **anode** and the − electrode the **cathode**.","steps":[{"text":"The dye is a cation, so it heads to −; DNA, an anion, heads to +.","hl":"positively charged dye"},{"text":"Read the plot: X rises on the + side, Y falls on the − side.","calc":["X at 60 min: **+4.2 cm** (toward +)","Y at 60 min: **−2.4 cm** (toward −)"]},{"text":"Y moves toward −, so it is the positive dye; whichever electrode it is paired with, it is not the DNA.","strike":["Band Y; toward the anode","Band Y; toward the cathode"]},{"text":"The + electrode of a gel rig is the anode, so X is heading to the anode, not the cathode.","strike":["Band X; toward the cathode"]},{"text":"Answer: Band X; toward the anode."}],"trap":"Recalling that the anode is − in a battery: in electrophoresis an outside supply drives the cell, and the anode is the + electrode."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"DNA fragment","note":"phosphates: negative"},{"t":"positively charged dye","note":"a cation"},{"t":"toward the + electrode","note":"up on the plot"}]},{"text":"**Why DNA moves:** one − charge per phosphate, so the field pulls the whole strand toward +."},{"text":"**Name the electrodes.** In electrophoresis the + end is the anode (oxidation happens there) and the − end is the cathode."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** DNA's phosphate backbone is negative, so in a gel it runs away from the − end toward the + electrode, the anode."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-06b","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:6#3"],"q":"The figure plots migration distance against fragment size for a DNA ladder. An unknown fragment on the same gel traveled 4.6 cm. Its length is closest to:","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"DNA ladder: distance traveled versus size","x":{"label":"Fragment size (bp)","min":0,"max":5000,"step":1000},"y":{"label":"Distance from well (cm)","min":0,"max":7,"step":1},"series":[{"name":"Ladder","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[150,6.2],[300,5.1],[600,4],[1200,2.9],[2400,1.8],[4800,0.7]]}]},"choices":["About 200 bp","About 400 bp","About 900 bp","About 4600 bp"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Read backwards: farther means smaller, and 4.6 cm is past the 600 bp band (4.0 cm), so the fragment is under 600."},{"c":3,"tag":"copied","why":"That copies the 4.6 cm distance as a size; a fragment that long would stop near 0.7 cm."},{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"A 200 bp fragment would run past the 300 bp band, beyond 5.1 cm, but this one stopped short of it."}],"rule":"Small fragments run farther: find the two ladder bands that bracket the distance and pick a size between their sizes.","exp":"Shorter DNA slips through the gel faster, so it travels farther. 4.6 cm lies **between the 300 bp band (5.1 cm) and the 600 bp band (4.0 cm)**, so the fragment is between 300 and 600 bp; on the curve it reads **about 400 bp**.","how":{"ask":"Which two ladder bands bracket 4.6 cm, and where between them does the unknown fall?","know":"• In a gel, shorter fragments move farther; the ladder gives known sizes at known distances.\n• Find the two ladder bands whose distances bracket the unknown; its size lies between theirs.\n• Distance falls roughly with log(size): on this gel each doubling of size costs about 1.1 cm.","steps":[{"text":"Find 4.6 cm on the distance axis.","hl":"traveled 4.6 cm"},{"text":"Bracket it with ladder bands.","calc":["300 bp → 5.1 cm (ran farther)","600 bp → 4.0 cm (ran less far)","4.6 cm lies between → **300–600 bp**"]},{"text":"Place it: 4.6 is 0.5 cm short of 5.1, a bit under half of the 1.1 cm doubling step.","calc":["(5.1 − 4.6) ÷ 1.1 ≈ 0.45 of a doubling","300 × 2^0.45 ≈ 300 × 1.37 ≈ **410 bp**"]},{"text":"A 200 bp fragment would run past the 300 bp band (beyond 5.1 cm), and a 900 bp one would stop short of the 600 bp band (before 4.0 cm).","strike":["About 200 bp","About 900 bp"]},{"text":"4600 bp copies the distance number as a size; a band that long would sit near 0.7 cm.","strike":["About 4600 bp"]},{"text":"Answer: about 400 bp."}],"trap":"Thinking longer DNA runs farther, which flips the bracket: the gel mesh holds long fragments back."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"migration distance","note":"y axis: cm"},{"t":"DNA ladder","note":"known sizes"},{"t":"traveled 4.6 cm","note":"read across"}]},{"text":"**Short runs far.** The gel is a mesh: small fragments slip through, large ones are held back."},{"text":"**Bracket, then place.** Find the ladder bands just above and below the unknown's distance; the size lies between theirs."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** shorter DNA migrates farther; size an unknown by the two ladder bands whose distances bracket it, reading the curve, not a straight line."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-07a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:7#1"],"q":"A cell culture medium kept at room temperature has a pH of 6.7. Compared with pure water at that temperature, the medium is:","choices":["weakly basic; [OH⁻] ≈ 2 × 10⁻⁷ M.","weakly acidic; [H⁺] ≈ 1.3 × 10⁻⁷ M.","neutral; [H⁺] = 1 × 10⁻⁷ M.","weakly acidic; [H⁺] ≈ 2 × 10⁻⁷ M."],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"pH below 7 means more H⁺ than water, not less: a lower pH is always more acidic."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"pH is a log scale: 0.3 units below 7 multiplies [H⁺] by 10^0.3, much more than the 1.3 a linear reading gives."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Only pH 7.00 exactly is neutral at 25 °C; any pH below it has [H⁺] > [OH⁻]."}],"rule":"At 25 °C, pH below 7 is acidic. [H⁺] = 10^−pH: each 0.3 pH units is a factor of about 2, each whole unit a factor of 10.","exp":"Pure water at 25 °C has **[H⁺] = [OH⁻] = 10⁻⁷ M** (pH 7). At **pH 6.7** the solution is **weakly acidic**: [H⁺] = 10⁻⁶·⁷ ≈ 2.0 × 10⁻⁷ M, about **twice** the value in water. The ion product Kw = 10⁻¹⁴ then gives [OH⁻] ≈ 5 × 10⁻⁸ M.","how":{"ask":"Place pH 6.7 relative to neutral 7, then convert the 0.3-unit gap into a factor.","know":"• At 25 °C, pure water: **[H⁺] = [OH⁻] = 1 × 10⁻⁷ M, pH 7**.\n• **pH just below 7 → weakly acidic** ([H⁺] above 10⁻⁷, [OH⁻] below); just above 7 → weakly basic.\n• A gap of Δ pH units is a factor of **10^Δ**; [H⁺][OH⁻] = 10⁻¹⁴ moves them oppositely.","steps":[{"text":"Compare with neutral.","hl":"pH of 6.7","calc":["6.7 < 7.0 → weakly acidic"]},{"text":"Turn the gap into a factor.","calc":["Δ = 7.0 − 6.7 = 0.3","10^0.3 ≈ 2"]},{"text":"Apply it to each ion.","calc":["[H⁺] = 10⁻⁷ × 2 ≈ 2 × 10⁻⁷ M","[OH⁻] = 10⁻¹⁴ ÷ (2 × 10⁻⁷) = 5 × 10⁻⁸ M"]},{"text":"Basic flips the direction, neutral ignores the gap, and 1.3 × 10⁻⁷ M treats the log scale as linear.","strike":["weakly basic; [OH⁻] ≈ 2 × 10⁻⁷ M.","neutral; [H⁺] = 1 × 10⁻⁷ M.","weakly acidic; [H⁺] ≈ 1.3 × 10⁻⁷ M."]},{"text":"Answer: weakly acidic, with [H⁺] about twice that of water."}],"trap":"Reading a 0.3 pH change as 30%: every pH unit is a factor of 10, so 0.3 units is a factor of about 2."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"6.7","note":"just below neutral"},{"t":"room temperature","note":"25 °C: neutral pH is 7"},{"t":"pure water","note":"10⁻⁷ M of each ion"}]},{"text":"**Neutral is the reference.** Below 7, [H⁺] exceeds [OH⁻]; the closer to 7, the weaker the effect.","calc":["pH 6.7: weakly acidic"]},{"text":"**Log factors to remember.** 10^0.3 ≈ 2, 10^0.5 ≈ 3, 10^0.7 ≈ 5, 10^1 = 10.","calc":["0.3 units → ×2"]},{"text":"**Also:** the mirror case: a pH just above 7 is weakly basic ([OH⁻] above 10⁻⁷ M)."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a pH just off 7 is weakly acidic (below) or weakly basic (above); the gap gives the factor 10^Δ."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-08a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:8#2"],"q":"The graph shows 30 mL of a weak acid HA titrated with NaOH of equal concentration. For a buffer of pH 3.4 made from HA and A⁻, what [A⁻]/[HA] ratio is needed?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Titration of 30 mL of weak acid HA with NaOH","x":{"label":"Volume of NaOH added (mL)","min":0,"max":40,"step":10},"y":{"label":"pH","min":0,"max":14,"step":2},"series":[{"name":"HA + NaOH","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,2.26],[3,2.75],[6,3.1],[9,3.33],[12,3.52],[15,3.7],[18,3.88],[21,4.07],[24,4.3],[27,4.65],[29,5.16],[30,8.29],[31,11.39],[33,11.85],[36,12.13],[40,12.33]]}]},"choices":["2.0","0.30","1.0","0.50"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"pH 3.4 is below the pKa, so acid must outnumber base: the ratio has to be under 1; 2.0 flips it."},{"c":1,"tag":"copied","why":"0.30 is the pKa − pH gap used as the ratio itself; the gap is a log, so undo it with 10^x."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A 1:1 mix sits exactly at the pKa, the half-equivalence pH (3.7 at 15 mL), not at 3.4."}],"rule":"pH = pKa + log([A⁻]/[HA]); read the pKa at half-equivalence. pH below pKa → ratio under 1; 0.3 log units is a factor of 2.","exp":"Equivalence is at **30 mL** (the steep jump), so half-equivalence is **15 mL**, where pH = **pKa ≈ 3.7**. Henderson–Hasselbalch: 3.4 − 3.7 = −0.3 is the log of the base:acid ratio, so **[A⁻]/[HA] = 10^(−0.3) ≈ 0.50**.","how":{"ask":"Read the pKa at half-equivalence, then raise 10 to (pH − pKa).","know":"• **pH = pKa + log([A⁻]/[HA])**, so **[A⁻]/[HA] = 10^(pH − pKa)**.\n• At the **half-equivalence volume**, [A⁻] = [HA] and pH = pKa.\n• Equivalence = the midpoint of the steep jump.","steps":[{"text":"Find the equivalence volume from the steep jump, then halve it.","hl":"30 mL of a weak acid HA titrated with NaOH of equal concentration","calc":["equivalence ≈ 30 mL → half-equivalence = 15 mL"]},{"text":"Read the pH there: that is the pKa.","calc":["pH at 15 mL ≈ 3.7 = pKa"]},{"text":"Apply the ratio form of Henderson–Hasselbalch.","hl":"pH 3.4","calc":["pH − pKa = 3.4 − 3.7 = −0.3","ratio = 10^(−0.3) ≈ **0.50**"]},{"text":"2.0 inverts the ratio, 1.0 is the buffer at its pKa, and 0.30 uses the exponent as the ratio.","strike":["2.0","1.0","0.30"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.50."}],"trap":"Reading the pKa at the equivalence point (pH ≈ 8.3) instead of at half the equivalence volume."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"30 mL","note":"acid volume; equal molarity → equivalence at 30 mL"},{"t":"pH 3.4","note":"target buffer pH"},{"t":"[A⁻]/[HA]","note":"base over acid"}]},{"text":"**pKa from the curve.** Halfway to equivalence, half the acid has become A⁻, so the log term is zero and pH = pKa.","calc":["V(½ eq) = 15 mL → pKa ≈ 3.7"]},{"text":"**Ratio form.** Solve Henderson–Hasselbalch for the ratio.","calc":["[A⁻]/[HA] = 10^(pH − pKa) = 10^(−0.3) ≈ 0.50"]},{"text":"**Also:** a pH exactly 1 unit below the pKa gives a base:acid ratio of 0.1, and a pH equal to the pKa gives 1; each unit is a factor of 10."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** base:acid = 10^(pH − pKa), with the pKa read at the half-equivalence volume."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-09a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:9#1","pe6-cp:9#2"],"q":"Supercooled water at −10 °C freezes spontaneously once seeded, and its surroundings warm. Which set of signs describes this freezing?","choices":["ΔH negative, ΔS negative, ΔG negative","ΔH negative, ΔS positive, ΔG negative","ΔH positive, ΔS negative, ΔG negative","ΔH negative, ΔS negative, ΔG positive"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Sign flipped: liquid → crystal is more ordered, so ΔS is negative, not positive."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"ΔH > 0 means heat is absorbed; here the surroundings warm, so the water releases heat and ΔH is negative."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"A process that happens on its own has ΔG < 0 by definition; a negative ΔS does not forbid it."}],"rule":"Read each sign from the story: heat given off → ΔH < 0, heat taken in → ΔH > 0; more order → ΔS < 0, less → ΔS > 0; happens on its own → ΔG < 0.","exp":"Spontaneous means **ΔG < 0**, the only requirement. The surroundings warm, so heat leaves the water: **ΔH < 0**. Freezing orders the molecules: **ΔS < 0**. ΔG = ΔH − TΔS stays negative because |ΔH| > T|ΔS| at this low temperature: an enthalpy-driven process.","how":{"ask":"Read each sign from an observation: spontaneity, heat flow and order.","know":"• **ΔG = ΔH − TΔS**; spontaneous ⇔ ΔG < 0, the only sign that is required.\n• Surroundings warm → the system releases heat → ΔH < 0; surroundings cool → ΔH > 0.\n• More order (gas → liquid → solid, fewer particles) → ΔS < 0.","steps":[{"text":"Spontaneous: ΔG is negative.","hl":"freezes spontaneously","strike":["ΔH negative, ΔS negative, ΔG positive"]},{"text":"The surroundings warm, so heat flows out of the water: exothermic.","hl":"its surroundings warm","strike":["ΔH positive, ΔS negative, ΔG negative"]},{"text":"Liquid → crystal is more ordered, so ΔS is negative.","strike":["ΔH negative, ΔS positive, ΔG negative"]},{"text":"Check: ΔG = ΔH − TΔS is negative only if the enthalpy term wins.","calc":["ΔH < 0, and −TΔS > 0 (it opposes)","ΔG < 0 → |ΔH| > T|ΔS| (enthalpy-driven)"]},{"text":"Answer: ΔH negative, ΔS negative, ΔG negative."}],"trap":"Believing entropy must rise for anything spontaneous; only the total entropy (system + surroundings) must rise, and the released heat raises the surroundings' entropy."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"freezes spontaneously","note":"ΔG < 0"},{"t":"its surroundings warm","note":"heat released: ΔH < 0"},{"t":"Supercooled water","note":"liquid → solid: ΔS < 0"}]},{"text":"**Only ΔG is required.** Spontaneous ⇔ ΔG < 0; nothing forces ΔH or ΔS to a particular sign."},{"text":"**Also: ΔH and ΔS can each have either sign**, as long as ΔG = ΔH − TΔS comes out negative:","calc":["ΔH −, ΔS + → spontaneous at every T","ΔH −, ΔS − → spontaneous at low T (enthalpy-driven)","ΔH +, ΔS + → spontaneous at high T (entropy-driven)","ΔH +, ΔS − → never spontaneous"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** spontaneity needs only ΔG < 0; read ΔH from heat flow and ΔS from order, then check that ΔH − TΔS is negative."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-11a","s":"Reading Data","disc":"physics","aamc":"4A","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe6-cp:11#1","pe6-cp:11#2"],"q":"The resistance R of a copper wire was measured for several lengths L, as plotted. Which relationship between R and L do the data support?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Resistance of a copper wire versus its length","x":{"label":"Length L (m)","min":0,"max":2.5,"step":0.5},"y":{"label":"Resistance R (Ω)","min":0,"max":7,"step":1},"series":[{"name":"Wire","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[0,0],[0.6,1.5],[1.2,3],[1.8,4.5],[2.4,6]]}]},"choices":["R ∝ L","R ∝ L squared","R ∝ square root of L","R ∝ inverse of L"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"An inverse law means a longer wire reads lower R; here every longer wire reads higher."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"With R ∝ L², doubling L (0.6 → 1.2 m) would make R 4 times larger; it only doubles (1.5 → 3.0 Ω)."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A square-root law would raise R only about 1.4× when L doubles; the data double it."}],"rule":"Doubling test: double x and see what y does: ×2 linear, ×4 square, ×1.4 square root, ×½ inverse.","exp":"Doubling L from 0.6 to 1.2 m doubles R from 1.5 to 3.0 Ω, and quadrupling L (to 2.4 m) quadruples R (to 6.0 Ω). The ratio R/L stays **2.5 Ω/m**, and the points fall on a straight line through the origin: **R ∝ L**.","how":{"ask":"Divide each R by its L: is that ratio constant, or is R/L² constant instead?","know":"• y ∝ x: y/x is constant and the graph is a straight line through the origin; doubling x doubles y.\n• y ∝ x²: y/x² is constant; doubling x multiplies y by 4.\n• y ∝ √x: doubling x multiplies y by about 1.4. y ∝ 1/x: y × x is constant, and y falls as x grows.","steps":[{"text":"Test the ratios instead of eyeballing the line.","hl":"Which relationship between R and L","calc":["R/L: 1.5 ÷ 0.6 = **2.5**; 3.0 ÷ 1.2 = **2.5**","4.5 ÷ 1.8 = **2.5**; 6.0 ÷ 2.4 = **2.5** Ω/m"]},{"text":"R/L² changes from point to point, so the square law fails.","calc":["1.5 ÷ 0.6² = 1.5 ÷ 0.36 ≈ 4.2","6.0 ÷ 2.4² = 6.0 ÷ 5.76 ≈ 1.0"],"strike":["R ∝ L squared"]},{"text":"Doubling test: L × 2 (0.6 → 1.2 m) gives R × 2 (1.5 → 3.0 Ω), which is linear. A square-root law would give only × 1.4, and an inverse law would halve R.","calc":["√L: 1.5 × 1.41 ≈ 2.1 Ω (measured 3.0)","1/L: 1.5 ÷ 2 = 0.75 Ω (measured 3.0)"],"strike":["R ∝ square root of L","R ∝ inverse of L"]},{"text":"Answer: R ∝ L."}],"trap":"Calling any rising graph \"proportional\": a square law also rises, so test a ratio. Here only R/L stays fixed."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"resistance R","note":"y axis: ohms"},{"t":"several lengths L","note":"x axis: meters"},{"t":"relationship between R and L","note":"which power law?"}]},{"text":"**Two ratio tests.** Divide y by x and by x²; whichever ratio stays fixed names the law.","calc":["y/x fixed → y ∝ x","y/x² fixed → y ∝ x²"]},{"text":"**Doubling shortcut.** Double x and watch y: × 2 → linear, × 4 → square, × 1.4 → square root, × ½ → inverse."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** test y/x and y/x² across the data (or double x): a constant y/x, or y doubling with x, means y ∝ x; a constant y/x², or y × 4, means y ∝ x²."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-12a","s":"Stereochemistry","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:12#1","pe6-cp:12#2","pe6-cp:12#3"],"q":"A chemist draws trans-1-tert-butyl-3-methylcyclohexane, shown, in its lower-energy chair. Which substituent occupies an axial position in that chair?","fig":"cpt-pe6-trans-tbu-3-me-chx","choices":["Only the methyl","Only the tert-butyl","Both substituents","Neither substituent"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"impossible","why":"Trans at C1 and C3 puts the groups on opposite faces, which on a chair is always one axial plus one equatorial; both axial can't happen."},{"c":3,"tag":"impossible","why":"Trans at C1 and C3 means opposite faces, and on a chair that is always one axial plus one equatorial bond; no diequatorial chair exists."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"tert-Butyl is the bulkiest common group: axial, it hits the two axial H's on its face (1,3-diaxial strain), so the favored chair keeps it equatorial."}],"rule":"Cis/trans fixes how many groups must be axial; when one must be, the favored chair puts the bulkier group equatorial.","exp":"In a trans-1,3 ring the groups sit on opposite faces, and a chair then gives C1 and C3 **one axial and one equatorial** group in either conformation. The ring flips so the bulky **tert-butyl is equatorial**, which leaves the **methyl axial**.","how":{"ask":"Can this substitution pattern put both groups equatorial? If not, which group takes the axial spot?","know":"• Around a chair the axial bonds alternate up, down, up, so C1 and C3 point their axial bonds to the same face.\n• **Diequatorial is possible only for cis-1,3, trans-1,2 and trans-1,4**; cis-1,2, cis-1,4 and trans-1,3 always keep one group axial.\n• When one group must be axial, the favored chair gives that spot to the **smaller** group (tert-butyl ≫ isopropyl > ethyl ≈ methyl).","steps":[{"text":"Read the pattern: trans means opposite faces, and the groups sit on C1 and C3.","hl":"trans-1-tert-butyl-3-methylcyclohexane","fig":"cpt-pe6-trans-tbu-3-me-chx"},{"text":"At C1 and C3 same-face bonds are the same type, so opposite faces force one axial and one equatorial group in both chairs. Both axial and both equatorial are impossible.","calc":["trans-1,3 → one axial + one equatorial","both axial ✗; both equatorial ✗"],"strike":["Both substituents","Neither substituent"]},{"text":"Compare the two chairs by the cost of the axial group: tert-butyl axial is far worse than methyl axial.","calc":["CH₃ axial: ≈ 7 kJ/mol of strain","tert-butyl axial: > 20 kJ/mol","favored chair: tert-butyl equatorial"],"strike":["Only the tert-butyl"]},{"text":"Answer: only the methyl is axial."}],"trap":"Assuming the favored chair always has every group equatorial; that is true only for cis-1,3, trans-1,2 and trans-1,4 patterns."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"trans-1-tert-butyl-3-methylcyclohexane","note":"opposite faces, C1 and C3"},{"t":"lower-energy chair","note":"best placement of groups"},{"t":"axial position","note":"straight up or down"}]},{"text":"**Big groups go equatorial.** An axial group bumps the two axial H atoms on its own face; the bigger the group, the bigger the penalty, so tert-butyl is effectively locked equatorial.","fig":"cpt-pe6-trans-tbu-3-me-chx"},{"text":"**Check the pattern.** Axial bonds alternate up and down around the ring, so C1 and C3 share a direction.","calc":["same face at C1, C3 → same type (cis-1,3)","opposite faces at C1, C3 → one of each (trans-1,3)"]},{"text":"**Also:** the patterns that can be diequatorial are cis-1,3, trans-1,2 and trans-1,4; for those, the favored chair puts both groups equatorial."},{"text":"**Also:** in every other pattern (cis-1,2, cis-1,4, trans-1,3) one group must be axial, and the favored chair hands that spot to the smaller group."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** place the biggest group equatorial first, then ask whether the pattern lets the second group be equatorial too; if not, the smaller group is axial."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-14a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe6-cp:14#1","pe6-cp:14#2","pe6-cp:14#3"],"q":"A wild-type enzyme reaches half its Vmax of 30 μmol/min at [S] = 2.5 mM. A mutant has Vmax = 90 μmol/min and the same KM. Its rate at 2.5 mM is:","choices":["15 μmol/min","22.5 μmol/min","45 μmol/min","90 μmol/min"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"copied","why":"That's the mutant's Vmax copied from the stem: a plateau reached only at saturating [S]; at [S] = KM the rate is half of it."},{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"15 is half the wild-type's Vmax; the mutant's own plateau is 90, so its half-plateau rate is higher."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"22.5 assumes KM tripled along with Vmax; the stem says KM is unchanged."}],"rule":"At [S] = KM, any enzyme runs at half of its own Vmax; first check whether KM changed.","exp":"KM is the [S] that gives **half of Vmax**, so the wild-type data show KM = 2.5 mM. The mutant has the **same KM**, so at 2.5 mM it also runs at half of **its own** plateau: 90 ÷ 2 = **45 μmol/min**.","how":{"ask":"What fraction of its own Vmax does an enzyme reach when [S] equals KM?","know":"• **Michaelis–Menten:** v = Vmax[S]/(KM + [S]).\n• **Vmax** = the height of the plateau; **KM** = the [S] where v = Vmax/2.\n• Same KM → every curve reaches half of its own plateau at the same [S], however high the plateaus are.","steps":[{"text":"The wild-type data give KM directly.","hl":"half its Vmax of 30 μmol/min at [S] = 2.5 mM","calc":["v = Vmax/2 at [S] = KM → KM = 2.5 mM"]},{"text":"The mutant keeps that KM, so 2.5 mM is still its half-plateau point.","calc":["v = 90 × 2.5/(2.5 + 2.5)","v = 90 × 0.5 = 45 μmol/min"]},{"text":"15 μmol/min is the wild-type's half; 90 μmol/min is a plateau reached only at saturating [S].","strike":["15 μmol/min","90 μmol/min"]},{"text":"22.5 μmol/min assumes KM tripled along with Vmax; the stem says KM is unchanged.","calc":["(wrong) 90 × 2.5/(7.5 + 2.5) = 22.5"],"strike":["22.5 μmol/min"]},{"text":"Answer: 45 μmol/min."}],"trap":"Copying the wild-type's half-Vmax rate; each curve's halfway point is half of its own Vmax."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"half its Vmax","note":"that [S] is KM"},{"t":"the same KM","note":"halfway at the same [S]"},{"t":"Vmax = 90 μmol/min","note":"the mutant's plateau"}]},{"text":"**Plateau = Vmax.** On a rate-vs-[S] curve the flat top is Vmax; the mutant's sits three times higher.","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Rate vs [S], same KM, different Vmax (sketch)","x":{"label":"[S] (mM)","min":0,"max":15,"step":2.5},"y":{"label":"Rate (μmol/min)","min":0,"max":100,"step":20},"series":[{"name":"Wild-type","marker":"circle","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,8.6],[2.5,15],[5,20],[10,24],[15,25.7]]},{"name":"Mutant","marker":"square","line":"smooth","points":[[0,0],[1,25.7],[2.5,45],[5,60],[10,72],[15,77.1]]}]}},{"text":"**KM = [S] at half the plateau.** Draw a line at Vmax/2 and drop straight down to the [S] axis.","calc":["wild-type: 30/2 = 15 at 2.5 mM","mutant: 90/2 = 45 at 2.5 mM"]},{"text":"**Also:** same KM means both curves pass their own halfway marks at the same [S]; only the heights differ. A changed KM would shift that point left or right."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** plateau height = Vmax, the [S] at half the plateau = KM; equal KM puts every curve's halfway point at the same [S]."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-16a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe6-cp:16#1"],"q":"Carbamoyl phosphate synthetase I uses HCO₃⁻ and NH₃ and is activated allosterically by N-acetylglutamate (NAG). With NAG saturating but HCO₃⁻ far below KM, which change most raises the rate?","choices":["Adding more HCO₃⁻","Adding more NAG","Adding carbamoyl phosphate","Removing some NH₃"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"NH₃ is a substrate; removing it can only lower the rate."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"NAG is already saturating: every activator site is filled, so more NAG adds nothing."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Carbamoyl phosphate is the product; adding product does not push the forward reaction faster."}],"rule":"Raise a rate by adding whatever is limiting: a substrate far below KM, not an activator that is already saturating.","exp":"Two things set the rate of an allosterically activated enzyme: the **activator** and the **substrate**. The activator is maxed out, so the limit now is **substrate**: HCO₃⁻ is far below KM, so adding it raises the rate most. Product (carbamoyl phosphate) does not speed the forward reaction, and removing NH₃ slows it.","how":{"ask":"Which factor still limits the rate once the activator is saturating?","know":"• An **allosteric activator** binds away from the active site and shifts the enzyme toward its active form.\n• **Substrate** below KM limits the rate roughly in proportion to [S].\n• The top rate needs **both** a saturating activator and plenty of substrate.","steps":[{"text":"Sort the species: NAG is the activator; HCO₃⁻ and NH₃ are substrates; carbamoyl phosphate is the product.","hl":"activated allosterically by N-acetylglutamate (NAG)"},{"text":"The activator is saturated, so more of it changes nothing.","hl":"NAG saturating","strike":["Adding more NAG"]},{"text":"Product does not drive the forward reaction, and less NH₃ means less substrate.","strike":["Adding carbamoyl phosphate","Removing some NH₃"]},{"text":"HCO₃⁻ far below KM is the bottleneck.","calc":["[S] ≪ KM → v ≈ (Vmax/KM)[S]","double [HCO₃⁻] → roughly double v"]},{"text":"Answer: adding more HCO₃⁻."}],"trap":"Reaching for the activator because allosteric control sounds powerful; a saturated activator site cannot be filled any further."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"activated allosterically","note":"activator switches the enzyme on"},{"t":"NAG saturating","note":"activator maxed out"},{"t":"far below KM","note":"substrate is limiting"}]},{"text":"**Two dials.** The activator turns the enzyme on; substrate feeds it. The rate peaks only when both dials are high."},{"text":"**Find the low dial.** Saturating activator + scarce substrate → substrate caps the rate, so raising substrate pays off most."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for an allosterically activated enzyme, the top rate needs high activator and high substrate together; raise whichever one is still low."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-17a","s":"Electricity & Magnetism","disc":"physics","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:17#1","pe6-cp:17#2"],"q":"A copper wire carries a steady current from terminal P to terminal Q. In which direction do the wire's charge carriers move?","choices":["From Q to P","From P to Q","Both directions, as ions","No net direction"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Electrons are negative, so they drift against the conventional current, not along it."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Ions moving both ways is electrolytic conduction in a solution or melt; in solid copper the ions are locked in the lattice."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Electrons do jiggle randomly, but a current is a net drift; with no net motion there would be no current."}],"rule":"In a metal wire the carriers are electrons, and they drift opposite to the conventional current arrow.","exp":"In a metal the carriers are **free electrons**, while the copper ion cores stay fixed in the lattice. Conventional current is defined as the flow of positive charge, so the negative electrons **drift opposite to it, from Q toward P**. Ions moving both ways happens only in electrolytic conduction (solutions or molten salts).","how":{"ask":"What carries charge in a solid metal, and how does its motion relate to the direction of conventional current?","know":"• **Metallic conduction:** free (delocalized) electrons drift through a fixed lattice of metal cations.\n• **Electrolytic conduction:** in a solution or molten salt, cations and anions move in opposite directions.\n• Conventional current = the direction + charge would move; electrons (−) move the opposite way.","steps":[{"text":"Copper is a metal: its mobile charges are free electrons, not ions.","hl":"copper wire"},{"text":"Ions moving in both directions describes a salt solution, not a solid wire.","strike":["Both directions, as ions"]},{"text":"A steady current needs a net drift of carriers, even though each electron also jiggles at random.","strike":["No net direction"]},{"text":"Electrons are negative, so they drift against the conventional current.","calc":["current: P → Q","electrons (−): **Q → P**"],"strike":["From P to Q"]},{"text":"Also: in a salt solution the carriers are ions. In saline, Na⁺ drifts with the current and Cl⁻ against it; that is electrolytic conduction."},{"text":"Answer: from Q to P."}],"trap":"Assuming carriers move the way the current arrow points: the arrow follows positive charge, and copper's carriers are negative."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"copper wire","note":"a metal"},{"t":"from terminal P to terminal Q","note":"conventional current"},{"t":"charge carriers","note":"what actually moves"}]},{"text":"**Metals:** a lattice of fixed copper ion cores in a sea of free electrons; only the electrons move (metallic conduction)."},{"text":"**Also:** in a solution or a molten salt, ions carry the current (electrolytic conduction): cations toward −, anions toward +."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** name the carrier by the material: metal → free electrons, drifting opposite the conventional current; solution or melt → ions."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-18a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"physics","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:18#1","pe6-cp:18#2"],"q":"How much heat melts 150 g of gold already at its melting point? (L_fusion = 6.4 × 10⁴ J/kg; c = 129 J/kg·K)","choices":["9.6 × 10³ J","6.4 × 10⁴ J","4.3 × 10⁵ J","9.6 × 10⁶ J"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"units","why":"Using 150 g in place of 0.150 kg with L in J/kg inflates the answer 1000-fold."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"That divides L by the mass instead of multiplying: heat for melting grows with mass, Q = mL."},{"c":1,"tag":"copied","why":"That is L for a whole kilogram, copied from the stem; the sample is only 0.150 kg, so the heat must be smaller."}],"rule":"Melting at the melting point: Q = mL with m in kg; no temperature change, so c is a distractor. Estimate 0.15 × 6 × 10⁴.","exp":"The gold is already at its melting point, so there is **no temperature change** and the specific heat is not needed. **q = mL** = 0.150 kg × 6.4 × 10⁴ J/kg = **9.6 × 10³ J**.","how":{"ask":"Which heat formula applies when a solid changes phase at constant temperature?","know":"• **Phase change:** q = m·L (no ΔT); L_fusion for melting, L_vap for boiling.\n• **Temperature change:** q = m·c·ΔT, only when T changes.\n• Match units: L in J/kg needs m in kg (g ÷ 1000).","steps":[{"text":"Already at the melting point: no ΔT, so q = mL only; the specific heat is a distractor.","hl":"already at its melting point"},{"text":"Convert the mass to kilograms.","hl":"150 g","calc":["150 g ÷ 1000 = 0.150 kg"],"strike":["9.6 × 10⁶ J"]},{"text":"Multiply mass by the heat of fusion.","calc":["q = 0.150 kg × 6.4 × 10⁴ J/kg","q = 0.96 × 10⁴ J = 9.6 × 10³ J"],"strike":["6.4 × 10⁴ J","4.3 × 10⁵ J"]},{"text":"Answer: 9.6 × 10³ J."}],"trap":"Adding an m·c·ΔT term; the solid is already at its melting point, so there is no warming step and c goes unused."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"already at its melting point","note":"no ΔT"},{"t":"150 g","note":"→ 0.150 kg"},{"t":"6.4 × 10⁴ J/kg","note":"L_fusion"},{"t":"129 J/kg·K","note":"distractor"}]},{"text":"**Pick the formula.** Melting at a fixed temperature uses q = mL; m·c·ΔT is for warming or cooling."},{"text":"**Also: units first.** L is per kilogram, so grams must become kilograms before multiplying.","calc":["150 g = 0.150 kg"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** heat for a phase change at constant T is q = m·L with m in kg; use m·c·ΔT only when the temperature moves."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-19a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:19#1","pe6-cp:19#2"],"q":"A chlorinated sugar analog is glucose (C₆H₁₂O₆, 180 g/mol) with one hydroxyl group replaced by a chlorine atom (35.5 g/mol). What is its approximate molar mass?","choices":["163 g/mol","197.5 g/mol","198.5 g/mol","215.5 g/mol"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"skipped","why":"That removes the OH (17) and stops; the chlorine atom that takes its place still adds 35.5."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"That adds the Cl but never takes the OH away: a swap is one subtraction plus one addition, not just an addition."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"That subtracts 18, as if a whole water left; OH weighs 17 (16 + 1), one H less."}],"rule":"A swap is minus the old group, plus the new atom: OH = 17, Cl = 35.5. Estimate 180 + about 18.","exp":"Start from glucose, **180 g/mol**. Take off one **OH (16 + 1 = 17)** and put on one **Cl (35.5)**: 180 − 17 + 35.5 = **198.5 g/mol** (formula C₆H₁₁ClO₅: 72 + 11 + 35.5 + 80 = 198.5).","how":{"ask":"Adjust the parent sugar's molar mass for the group that leaves and the atom that enters.","know":"• A substitution changes the molar mass by **(mass added) − (mass removed)**.\n• OH = 16 + 1 = **17 g/mol** (not 18: that is a whole water).\n• Cl ≈ **35.5 g/mol**.\n• Net change for OH → Cl: **+18.5 g/mol**.","steps":[{"text":"Start from the parent molecule's molar mass.","hl":"C₆H₁₂O₆, 180 g/mol","calc":["parent = 180 g/mol"]},{"text":"Subtract the hydroxyl group that leaves.","hl":"one hydroxyl group replaced","calc":["OH = 16 + 1 = 17","180 − 17 = 163 g/mol"]},{"text":"Add the chlorine atom that takes its place.","hl":"chlorine atom (35.5 g/mol)","calc":["163 + 35.5 = **198.5 g/mol**"]},{"text":"163 stops after the subtraction, and 215.5 adds the chlorine without removing the OH.","strike":["163 g/mol","215.5 g/mol"]},{"text":"197.5 subtracts 18, as if a whole water left, but only the OH (17) is replaced.","strike":["197.5 g/mol"]},{"text":"Answer: 198.5 g/mol."}],"trap":"Subtracting 18 for the leaving group (197.5 g/mol): in a direct swap only OH (17) leaves, not H₂O."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"180 g/mol","note":"parent sugar"},{"t":"one hydroxyl group","note":"leaves: −17"},{"t":"chlorine atom (35.5 g/mol)","note":"enters: +35.5"}]},{"text":"**Start from the parent.** The new molecule is glucose with one change, so its molar mass is glucose's plus a correction.","calc":["M(new) = M(parent) − M(removed) + M(added)"]},{"text":"**Weigh the swapped pieces.** OH is one O (16) and one H (1); Cl is 35.5.","calc":["−17 + 35.5 = +18.5","180 + 18.5 = 198.5 g/mol"]},{"text":"**Also:** the same method works for any swap. Glucose with one OH replaced by ¹⁸F (mass 18) changes by −17 + 18 = +1."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** for a substituted molecule, molar mass = parent − group removed + group added; OH (17) → Cl (35.5) is a net +18.5."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-20a","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:20#2","pe6-cp:32#1"],"q":"Carbon-11 (6 protons), a tracer used in brain scans, decays by positron emission. Which nucleus does it become?","choices":["Boron-11","Nitrogen-11","Boron-10","Beryllium-7"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Z rises only in β⁻ decay (a neutron becomes a proton); positron emission runs the other way, so Z must fall."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"A positron has almost no mass, so the mass number can't change; that drops a nucleon that never left."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Losing 4 mass units and 2 protons is alpha decay, not positron emission."}],"rule":"Positron emission: a proton becomes a neutron, so Z drops by 1 and A stays the same. β⁻ raises Z; alpha drops A by 4.","exp":"In **β⁺ (positron) emission** a **proton turns into a neutron**: p → n + e⁺ + ν. Z falls from 6 to 5 (boron) and A stays 11, so ¹¹C → **¹¹B** + e⁺. The daughter has 5 protons and 6 neutrons, one more neutron than the parent.","how":{"ask":"Apply the β⁺ rule: A unchanged, Z down by one; then name the element from Z.","know":"• **Positron emission (β⁺)**: p → n + e⁺ + ν. **A stays the same, Z drops by 1.**\n• **β⁻ emission** is the reverse: n → p + e⁻, Z rises by 1.\n• **Alpha emission** loses ⁴He: A − 4, Z − 2.","steps":[{"text":"Read the parent's mass number and proton count.","hl":"Carbon-11 (6 protons)","calc":["A = 11, Z = 6, N = 11 − 6 = 5"]},{"text":"A proton becomes a neutron; the positron carries off charge but essentially no mass.","hl":"positron emission","calc":["Z: 6 − 1 = 5 → boron","A: 11 (unchanged)","N: 5 + 1 = 6"]},{"text":"Nitrogen-11 raises Z (β⁻ thinking), boron-10 drops a nucleon that never left, and beryllium-7 is alpha decay.","strike":["Nitrogen-11","Boron-10","Beryllium-7"]},{"text":"Answer: boron-11."}],"trap":"Mixing up the two beta decays: β⁻ adds a proton, β⁺ removes one."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"6 protons","note":"Z = 6"},{"t":"Carbon-11","note":"A = 11"},{"t":"positron emission","note":"β⁺: p → n"}]},{"text":"**What the positron does.** A positron is a positive electron. To emit one, one proton in the nucleus changes into a neutron, so the charge drops by one.","calc":["p → n + e⁺ + ν"]},{"text":"**Balance the numbers.** Mass numbers and charges must match on both sides.","calc":["¹¹₆C → ¹¹₅B + ⁰₊₁e"]},{"text":"**Also:** β⁺ emitters are proton-rich (too few neutrons for their Z); each decay raises the neutron count by one and lowers the proton count by one, moving the nucleus toward stability."},{"text":"**Also:** a nucleus left in an excited state (marked m, as in ⁸¹ᵐKr) relaxes without changing A or Z: only energy leaves, as a photon."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** positron emission turns a proton into a neutron: A stays, Z goes down by 1."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-21a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:21#1","pe6-cp:21#2","pe6-cp:21#3"],"q":"A salt bridge in a folded protein pairs the side chains –(CH₂)₄–NH₃⁺ and –(CH₂)₂–COO⁻. Which two amino acids form it?","choices":["Lysine and glutamate","Arginine and glutamate","Lysine and aspartate","Histidine and glutamine"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Arginine ends in a guanidinium, –NH–C(=NH₂⁺)–NH₂, after three CH₂; a plain –NH₃⁺ after four CH₂ is a different residue."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Aspartate's side chain is –CH₂–COO⁻, one CH₂; the stem's carboxylate sits after two."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Histidine's side chain is an imidazole ring and glutamine ends in an amide (–CONH₂); neither ends in NH₃⁺ or COO⁻."}],"rule":"Count the CH₂ groups and read the end group: Lys –(CH₂)₄NH₃⁺, Arg guanidinium, Asp –CH₂COO⁻, Glu –(CH₂)₂COO⁻.","exp":"**Four CH₂ then –NH₃⁺** is lysine (arginine ends in a guanidinium after three CH₂). **Two CH₂ then –COO⁻** is glutamate (aspartate has one CH₂; glutamine ends in –CONH₂). Lys⁺ and Glu⁻ form the salt bridge.","how":{"ask":"Name each side chain by its end group, then count its CH₂ groups.","know":"• **Lys**: –(CH₂)₄–NH₃⁺. **Arg**: –(CH₂)₃–NH–C(=NH₂⁺)–NH₂ (guanidinium).\n• **Asp**: –CH₂–COO⁻. **Glu**: –(CH₂)₂–COO⁻.\n• **Asn**: –CH₂–CONH₂. **Gln**: –(CH₂)₂–CONH₂.","steps":[{"text":"First side chain: four CH₂ and a plain ammonium end. Arginine would end in a guanidinium; histidine is a ring.","hl":"–(CH₂)₄–NH₃⁺","calc":["(CH₂)₄ + NH₃⁺ → **lysine**","arginine: (CH₂)₃ + guanidinium","histidine: imidazole ring"],"strike":["Arginine and glutamate","Histidine and glutamine"]},{"text":"Second side chain: two CH₂ and a carboxylate. Aspartate has only one CH₂.","hl":"–(CH₂)₂–COO⁻","calc":["(CH₂)₂ + COO⁻ → **glutamate**","aspartate: one CH₂","glutamine: –CONH₂, no charge"],"strike":["Lysine and aspartate"]},{"text":"Answer: lysine and glutamate."}],"trap":"Aspartate and glutamate share the carboxylate end; only the CH₂ count tells them apart."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"–(CH₂)₄–NH₃⁺","note":"side chain 1"},{"t":"–(CH₂)₂–COO⁻","note":"side chain 2"},{"t":"salt bridge","note":"+ meets −"}]},{"text":"**Name the end group first.** A lone –NH₃⁺ is lysine's; a carbon bonded to three N with a + charge is arginine's guanidinium.","fig":["lysine-sc","arginine-sc"],"calc":["–(CH₂)₄–NH₃⁺ → Lys","–(CH₂)₃–NH–C(=NH₂⁺)–NH₂ → Arg"]},{"text":"**Then count the CH₂ groups** to tell close relatives apart.","calc":["–CH₂–COO⁻ → Asp","–(CH₂)₂–COO⁻ → Glu"]},{"text":"**Also:** side-chain amides: –CH₂–C(=O)NH₂ is asparagine, and one more CH₂ makes glutamine. Neither is charged."},{"text":"**Also:** side-chain carboxylates: aspartate (one CH₂) and glutamate (two CH₂) are the negative residues at pH 7."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** read the end group (ammonium, guanidinium, amide, carboxylate), then count CH₂ groups: Lys 4, Arg 3, Asp and Asn 1, Glu and Gln 2."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-22a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:22#1","pe6-cp:22#2","pe6-cp:22#3"],"q":"In a proposed mechanism, the cysteine shown first donates its side-chain S–H proton to a substrate oxygen; its thiolate then attacks a substrate carbonyl carbon. Its roles are:","fig":"cysteine","choices":["General acid; nucleophile","General base; nucleophile","Nucleophile; general acid","General acid; electrophile"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Cys loses its proton here; a general base removes a proton, so Cys is not acting as a base in step one."},{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"Order flipped: the proton hand-off comes first and the attack second, so the roles are listed in reverse."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"An electrophile accepts electrons; the thiolate gives its lone pair to the carbonyl carbon, which is the electrophile."}],"rule":"Name each role by what moves: gives H⁺ = acid, takes H⁺ = base; gives a lone pair = nucleophile, takes one = electrophile. Keep step order.","exp":"Name each role by what the group does in that step. Giving away H⁺ to the substrate oxygen makes cysteine a **general acid**. The thiolate then **donates a lone pair to the carbonyl carbon**: a **nucleophile**. One group can play different roles in different steps.","how":{"ask":"What does the cysteine do with H⁺ in step one, and with its lone pair in step two?","know":"• **Nucleophile:** gives a lone pair to an electron-poor atom (a C=O carbon, a P).\n• **General base:** removes a proton from another group.\n• **General acid:** donates a proton to another group.\n• Label each step separately; the same residue can switch roles.","steps":[{"text":"Step one: Cys hands its S–H proton to the substrate oxygen, so it donates H⁺.","hl":"donates its side-chain S–H proton","strike":["General base; nucleophile"]},{"text":"The order matters: the acid step comes first, the attack second.","strike":["Nucleophile; general acid"]},{"text":"Step two: the S⁻ lone pair attacks the carbonyl carbon, the electrophile.","hl":"attacks a substrate carbonyl carbon","strike":["General acid; electrophile"]},{"text":"Answer: general acid; nucleophile."}],"trap":"Calling the attacking group the electrophile; the attacker brings the electrons, and the carbonyl carbon that receives them is the electrophile."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"donates its side-chain S–H proton","note":"gives H⁺: general acid"},{"t":"attacks a substrate carbonyl carbon","note":"lone pair: nucleophile"}]},{"text":"**Step by step.** Name each role by the action in that step alone, never by the residue's usual job."},{"text":"**Also: removing a proton = general base.** Whatever takes the H⁺ acts as the base; in step one that is the substrate oxygen, so the cysteine is not the base.","calc":["Cys–SH + O (substrate) → Cys–S⁻ + H–O (substrate)"]},{"text":"**Also: giving a proton = general acid.** Losing H⁺ leaves the cysteine as a thiolate, S⁻, whose lone pair is then ready to attack."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** lone pair to an electrophile = nucleophile; takes H⁺ = general base; gives H⁺ = general acid; one residue can do each in turn."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-24a","s":"Enzymes & Kinetics","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:24#1"],"q":"Near pH 7, one active-site residue must take a proton from a substrate –OH and later hand that same proton to a leaving group. Which side chain is best suited?","choices":["Histidine (pKa ≈ 6.5)","Lysine (pKa ≈ 10.5)","Aspartate (pKa ≈ 3.9)","Arginine (pKa ≈ 12.5)"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Lys (pKa ≈ 10.5) stays protonated near pH 7, so it has no free lone pair to take a proton from the –OH."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Asp (pKa ≈ 3.9) stays deprotonated near pH 7, so it has no proton to hand to the leaving group."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Arg (pKa ≈ 12.5) stays protonated near pH 7, so it has no free lone pair to take a proton from the –OH."}],"rule":"A residue that both takes and gives a proton at pH 7 needs a pKa near 7; side chains far above or below are stuck in one form.","exp":"To take H⁺ and later give it back, a residue must act as a **base, then an acid**, so both of its forms must be present. Near pH 7 **His** (pKa close to 7) has both forms present, so it can do both. **Lys and Arg** stay protonated (no free lone pair), and **Asp** stays deprotonated (no H⁺ to give).","how":{"ask":"Which side chain has both its protonated and neutral forms present near pH 7?","know":"• Takes H⁺ from another group = **general base**; gives H⁺ = **general acid**.\n• pH below the pKa → mostly protonated; pH above → mostly deprotonated; each unit away shifts the ratio tenfold.\n• His (side-chain pKa ≈ 6–7): near pH 7 both its protonated and neutral forms are present.","steps":[{"text":"The residue must first act as a base (take H⁺), then as an acid (give it), so it needs both forms near pH 7.","hl":"take a proton from a substrate –OH","calc":["step 1: base (needs the neutral form)","step 2: acid (needs the protonated form)"]},{"text":"His: its pKa sits close to 7, so both forms are present.","calc":["7 − 6.5 = 0.5 → neutral : protonated ≈ 10^0.5 ≈ 3 : 1"]},{"text":"Lys and Arg: their pKa values are far above 7, so they stay protonated and cannot take a proton.","calc":["Lys: 7 − 10.5 = −3.5 → about 1 in 3000 neutral","Arg: 7 − 12.5 = −5.5 → about 1 in 300,000 neutral"],"strike":["Lysine (pKa ≈ 10.5)","Arginine (pKa ≈ 12.5)"]},{"text":"Asp: its pKa is far below 7, so it stays deprotonated and has no proton to give.","hl":"hand that same proton to a leaving group","calc":["7 − 3.9 = 3.1 → about 1 in 1300 protonated"],"strike":["Aspartate (pKa ≈ 3.9)"]},{"text":"Answer: histidine."}],"trap":"Choosing lysine because it is basic; at pH 7 it is almost all –NH₃⁺, so it has no free lone pair to take a proton."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"take a proton","note":"base step"},{"t":"hand that same proton","note":"acid step"},{"t":"Near pH 7","note":"compare each pKa with 7"}]},{"text":"**Name each step.** Taking H⁺ = general base; giving H⁺ = general acid. One residue doing both needs both forms on hand."},{"text":"**Also:** at pH = pKa the two forms are 1 : 1; each pH unit away shifts the ratio tenfold, so a pKa 3 or more units from 7 leaves almost only one form."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** follow the H⁺: takes it = base, gives it = acid; a residue that must do both near pH 7 needs a side-chain pKa near 7, which points to His."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-25a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:25#1","pe6-cp:25#2","pe6-cp:25#3"],"q":"Complete hydrolysis of a tripeptide gives twice as much Phe as Ser. Partial hydrolysis gives the dipeptides Phe–Phe and Phe–Ser. What is the tripeptide's sequence?","choices":["Phe–Phe–Ser","Ser–Phe–Phe","Phe–Ser–Phe","Phe–Ser–Ser"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Dipeptides read N → C: this chain holds Ser–Phe, the reverse of the observed Phe–Ser, and no Phe–Ser at all."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Two Ser and one Phe reverses the count: complete hydrolysis showed twice as much Phe as Ser."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Its neighbor pairs are Phe–Ser and Ser–Phe; with no Phe next to Phe, it cannot release a Phe–Phe dipeptide."}],"rule":"Get the composition first, then overlap the fragments; dipeptides are read N → C, so each must appear in that order in the chain.","exp":"Counts: **2 Phe + 1 Ser**. Each dipeptide is a pair of neighbors written N → C. Overlap them on the shared Phe: Phe–**Phe** + **Phe**–Ser → **Phe–Phe–Ser**, which releases exactly Phe–Phe and Phe–Ser.","how":{"ask":"Which order of two Phe and one Ser contains both Phe–Phe and Phe–Ser as neighbor pairs?","know":"• Complete hydrolysis breaks every peptide bond: it gives the **composition**.\n• Partial hydrolysis leaves short pieces that **keep their order** (N → C).\n• Overlap the pieces on a shared residue to build the chain, then check the counts.","steps":[{"text":"Composition: twice as much Phe as Ser in three residues means two Phe and one Ser, which rules out two Ser.","hl":"twice as much Phe as Ser","calc":["2 Phe + 1 Ser"],"strike":["Phe–Ser–Ser"]},{"text":"List the neighbor pairs each remaining order would release.","calc":["Phe–Phe–Ser → **Phe–Phe**, **Phe–Ser**","Ser–Phe–Phe → Ser–Phe, Phe–Phe","Phe–Ser–Phe → Phe–Ser, Ser–Phe"]},{"text":"Only one order gives both observed pieces: the others give Ser–Phe or lack Phe–Phe.","hl":"Phe–Phe and Phe–Ser","strike":["Ser–Phe–Phe","Phe–Ser–Phe"]},{"text":"Answer: Phe–Phe–Ser."}],"trap":"Ser–Phe is not the same piece as Phe–Ser; the order is fixed N → C, so a reversed pair is a different dipeptide."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Complete hydrolysis","note":"all bonds cut: counts"},{"t":"twice as much Phe as Ser","note":"2 Phe, 1 Ser"},{"t":"Partial hydrolysis","note":"some bonds cut: neighbors"},{"t":"Phe–Phe and Phe–Ser","note":"two neighbor pairs"}]},{"text":"**Each dipeptide shows two neighbors**, in N → C order.","calc":["Phe–Phe: a Phe right before a Phe","Phe–Ser: a Phe right before Ser"]},{"text":"**Assemble one chain that contains both**: overlap them on the shared Phe.","calc":["Phe–Phe","     Phe–Ser","→ Phe–Phe–Ser"]},{"text":"**Check against the counts.**","calc":["Phe–Phe–Ser: 2 Phe, 1 Ser ✓"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** get the composition from complete hydrolysis, overlap the partial pieces on shared residues, and keep only the order that explains every piece and the counts."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-26a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:26#1"],"q":"Butane (C₄H₁₀) burns completely to CO₂ and H₂O. When the equation is balanced with the smallest whole numbers, what is the coefficient of O₂?","choices":["8","9","13","26"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"skipped","why":"8 is the CO₂ coefficient of the doubled equation; the O₂ must also supply the oxygen in the water."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"26 counts O atoms in the doubled equation (8 × 2 + 10); each O₂ molecule carries two of them, so halve it."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"That adds the CO₂ and H₂O counts (4 + 5) as if each product needed one O₂; a water needs only half an O₂."}],"rule":"Balance C, then H, then O last: count O atoms on the right, halve for O₂, and double everything if a half is left.","exp":"Balance C, then H, then O: C₄H₁₀ + 6.5 O₂ → 4 CO₂ + 5 H₂O, because the products hold 4 × 2 + 5 × 1 = 13 O atoms = 6.5 O₂. Doubling to clear the half gives **2 C₄H₁₀ + 13 O₂ → 8 CO₂ + 10 H₂O**.","how":{"ask":"After C and H are balanced, how many O atoms sit on the product side, and how many O₂ molecules supply them?","know":"• Balance a hydrocarbon combustion in the order C, H, O: CₓHᵧ + O₂ → x CO₂ + (y/2) H₂O.\n• O atoms needed = 2x + y/2, so O₂ = x + y/4 per fuel molecule.\n• If O₂ comes out as a half, double every coefficient.","steps":[{"text":"Carbon first: 4 C → 4 CO₂. Hydrogen next: 10 H → 5 H₂O.","hl":"Butane (C₄H₁₀)","calc":["C₄H₁₀ + ? O₂ → 4 CO₂ + 5 H₂O"]},{"text":"Oxygen last: count the O atoms on the right.","calc":["4 CO₂: 4 × 2 = 8 O","5 H₂O: 5 × 1 = 5 O","total 13 O → 13 ÷ 2 = **6.5 O₂**"]},{"text":"8 is the doubled CO₂ coefficient, and 9 adds the CO₂ and H₂O counts (4 + 5) as if each product needed one O₂.","strike":["8","9"]},{"text":"Clear the half by doubling everything. 26 is the number of O atoms, not O₂ molecules.","calc":["2 C₄H₁₀ + **13** O₂ → 8 CO₂ + 10 H₂O","check O: 13 × 2 = 26 = 8 × 2 + 10 ✓"],"strike":["26"]},{"text":"Answer: 13."}],"trap":"Stopping at 6.5, or counting O atoms (26) instead of O₂ molecules: a coefficient counts molecules, and each O₂ brings two atoms."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Butane (C₄H₁₀)","note":"x = 4, y = 10"},{"t":"burns completely","note":"only CO₂ + H₂O"},{"t":"smallest whole numbers","note":"clear any ½"}]},{"text":"**C, then H, then O last.** Oxygen appears in both products, so it can be fixed only after C and H.","calc":["x = 4 → 4 CO₂","y = 10 → 5 H₂O"]},{"text":"**Shortcut:** O₂ per fuel molecule = x + y/4.","calc":["butane: 4 + 10/4 = 6.5 O₂","ethane check: 2 + 6/4 = 3.5 → 2 C₂H₆ + 7 O₂"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** balance a hydrocarbon combustion C → H → O: x CO₂, y/2 H₂O, then O₂ = x + y/4; double everything if O₂ ends in a half."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-27a","s":"Spectroscopy","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:27#2","pe6-cp:27#1","pe6-cp:27#3"],"q":"Four small oxygen-containing compounds are shown. Which one gives a ¹H NMR signal that is a doublet integrating to 6H?","choices":["2-Methyl-1-propanol","1-Propanol","Ethanol","tert-Butyl methyl ether"],"choiceFigs":["cpt-pe6-isobutanol","1-propanol","cpt-ethanol","cpt-pe6-mtbe"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"1-Propanol's CH₃ sits next to a CH₂ (2 H), so it is a 3H triplet, not a 6H doublet."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Ethanol's CH₃ sits next to a CH₂ (2 H), so it is a 3H triplet, not a 6H doublet."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Its (CH₃)₃C– carbon bears no H, so those 9 H are a singlet, and the OCH₃ is a singlet too: no doublet at all."}],"rule":"A 6H doublet means two equal CH₃ groups on a carbon with one H: look for an isopropyl-type (CH₃)₂CH– group.","exp":"**n + 1 rule:** a signal is split into n + 1 peaks by the n H on adjacent carbons. A doublet means **one neighbor H**. In 2-methyl-1-propanol, (CH₃)₂CH–CH₂OH, the two equivalent CH₃ groups (6 H) border a single CH, so they appear as a **6H doublet**. 1-Propanol's and ethanol's CH₃ are triplets (next to CH₂), and the ether gives only singlets.","how":{"ask":"Which compound has six equivalent H on carbons that border a carbon carrying exactly one H?","know":"• n + 1 rule: n H on neighboring carbons split a signal into n + 1 peaks.\n• Doublet → 1 neighbor H; triplet → 2; quartet → 3; singlet → 0.\n• A 6H signal = two equivalent CH₃ groups. A (CH₃)₃C– group sits on a carbon with no H, so its 9 H give a singlet.","steps":[{"text":"6 H means two equivalent CH₃ groups; a doublet means exactly one H next door.","hl":"doublet integrating to 6H"},{"text":"2-Methyl-1-propanol is (CH₃)₂CH–CH₂OH: each CH₃ borders the CH, which carries 1 H.","calc":["n = 1 → 1 + 1 = **2 peaks** (doublet)","2 × CH₃ = **6 H**"]},{"text":"1-Propanol's CH₃ borders a CH₂, and so does ethanol's.","calc":["n = 2 → 2 + 1 = 3 peaks (triplet), only 3 H"],"strike":["1-Propanol","Ethanol"]},{"text":"Also: in the ether the central carbon of (CH₃)₃C– has no H, so its 9 H have n = 0 and give a singlet; the OCH₃ is a singlet as well.","calc":["n = 0 → 0 + 1 = 1 peak (singlet)"],"strike":["tert-Butyl methyl ether"]},{"text":"Answer: 2-methyl-1-propanol.","fig":"cpt-pe6-isobutanol"}],"trap":"Counting the H in the group itself instead of its neighbors: a CH₃ is split by the H next door, not by its own three."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"doublet","note":"1 neighbor H"},{"t":"6H","note":"two equivalent CH₃"}]},{"text":"**Also: the n + 1 rule.** Count the H on the adjacent carbons (not the O–H) and add 1.","calc":["0 neighbors → singlet","1 → doublet","2 → triplet","3 → quartet"]},{"text":"**Also:** a group whose neighbor carbon has no H is never split: the 9 H of a (CH₃)₃C– group show as one singlet."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** splitting = n + 1 from the H on neighboring carbons, so a 6H doublet points to (CH₃)₂CH– and an unsplit 9H signal to (CH₃)₃C–."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-27b","s":"Spectroscopy","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"4D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:27#4"],"q":"Ethanol, shown, gives a routine ¹H NMR spectrum. What splitting patterns appear for its CH₂ signal and its OH signal?","fig":"cpt-ethanol","choices":["Quartet; singlet","Quintet; triplet","Quartet; triplet","Triplet; singlet"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"Triplet uses n, not n + 1: the CH₂ has 3 neighbor H on the CH₃, so 3 + 1 = 4 peaks."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"This counts the OH proton as a neighbor of CH₂ and lets it split; fast exchange of the OH proton erases that coupling."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"An OH proton hops between molecules faster than coupling can register, so it can't show as a triplet."}],"rule":"n + 1 rule: count H on neighboring carbons only; an exchanging O–H proton shows one broad peak and splits nothing.","exp":"The CH₂ is split only by the 3 H of the neighboring CH₃: 3 + 1 = **quartet**. The **O–H proton exchanges rapidly** with traces of water or acid, so it appears as a broad **singlet**: it is not split by the CH₂ and does not split it. (Shaking with D₂O makes the OH peak vanish.)","how":{"ask":"Which neighbors actually couple to the CH₂, and does the fast-exchanging OH proton couple to anything?","know":"• n + 1 rule: count H on neighboring carbons.\n• An O–H (or N–H) proton hops between molecules quickly, so its coupling averages to zero: a broad singlet that neither splits nor is split.\n• Shaking with D₂O swaps OH → OD and the OH peak disappears.","steps":[{"text":"Two signals to predict.","hl":"its CH₂ signal and its OH signal"},{"text":"CH₂: its neighbors are the CH₃ (3 H) and the OH, which exchanges and is ignored.","calc":["n = 3 → 3 + 1 = **quartet**"],"strike":["Triplet; singlet"]},{"text":"OH: its proton hops between molecules faster than coupling can register, so it shows one broad peak.","calc":["OH → **singlet** (not split)"],"strike":["Quartet; triplet"]},{"text":"Counting the OH as a fourth neighbor of CH₂ would give a quintet, and would make the OH a triplet; exchange cancels both couplings.","strike":["Quintet; triplet"]},{"text":"Answer: quartet; singlet."}],"trap":"Treating the OH proton like a C–H neighbor: in routine spectra it exchanges too fast to split or be split."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"CH₂ signal","note":"neighbors: CH₃"},{"t":"OH signal","note":"exchanging H"},{"t":"routine","note":"traces of water present"}]},{"text":"**Count carbon neighbors.** CH₂ sees the 3 H of CH₃: quartet. CH₃ sees the 2 H of CH₂: triplet."},{"text":"**OH is special.** It trades places with H from traces of water many times per second, so its spin coupling averages out."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** an O–H proton exchanges fast, so it shows as a broad singlet that is not split and does not split its neighbors; apply n + 1 to carbon-bound H only."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-28a","s":"Fluids","disc":"physics","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe6-cp:28#1"],"q":"A plastic block of density 0.72 g/cm³ floats in a liquid of density 0.90 g/cm³. What percentage of the block's volume is below the surface?","choices":["65%","72%","80%","90%"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"copied","why":"72% is the block's density read as a percentage, as if the liquid were water; this liquid is lighter, so the block sinks deeper."},{"c":3,"tag":"copied","why":"90% is the liquid's density read as a percentage, not the ratio."},{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"65% multiplies the densities (0.72 × 0.90 ≈ 0.65); the fraction is a ratio, object density over fluid density."}],"rule":"Floating: fraction under = object density ÷ fluid density. Estimate 0.7 ÷ 0.9, about 7/9.","exp":"A floating object displaces its own weight of fluid, so **submerged fraction = ρ_object ÷ ρ_fluid** = 0.72 ÷ 0.90 = **0.80, or 80%**. In water the same block would sit 72% under; a less dense liquid gives less buoyancy per cm³, so the block rides lower.","how":{"ask":"What fraction of the block must be under so that the displaced liquid weighs as much as the block?","know":"• Floating: buoyant force = weight, so ρ_fluid × V_sub × g = ρ_obj × V × g.\n• So V_sub/V = ρ_obj/ρ_fluid.\n• Same object, denser fluid → smaller fraction under (it floats higher); less dense fluid → larger fraction under.","steps":[{"text":"Take the two densities: object and liquid.","hl":"block of density 0.72 g/cm³"},{"text":"Set buoyancy equal to weight and solve for the submerged fraction.","calc":["ρ_fluid × V_sub × g = ρ_obj × V × g","V_sub/V = 0.72 ÷ 0.90 = **0.80** = 80%"]},{"text":"Multiplying the densities gives 65%, and dividing by water's 1.00 gives 72%: both are wrong setups.","strike":["65%","72%"]},{"text":"90% is the liquid's density read as a percentage, not the ratio.","strike":["90%"]},{"text":"Answer: 80%."}],"trap":"Using water's density out of habit: the block is in a lighter liquid, so it sits deeper than the 72% it would show in water."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"floats","note":"buoyancy = weight"},{"t":"block of density 0.72 g/cm³","note":"object"},{"t":"liquid of density 0.90 g/cm³","note":"fluid"}]},{"text":"**Archimedes:** the buoyant force equals the weight of the fluid pushed aside.","calc":["F_b = ρ_fluid × V_sub × g"]},{"text":"**Floating balance:** weight = buoyancy; V and g cancel, leaving a density ratio.","calc":["fraction under = ρ_obj ÷ ρ_fluid"]},{"text":"**Compare fluids:** the same block is 72% under in water (1.00 g/cm³) but only 60% under in brine of 1.20 g/cm³. Denser fluid, higher float.","calc":["0.72 ÷ 1.20 = 0.60"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** submerged fraction = ρ_object ÷ ρ_fluid; the same object floats higher in a denser fluid."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-28b","s":"Fluids","disc":"physics","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe6-cp:28#2"],"q":"A hydrometer has an average density of 0.90 g/cm³. Its scale is marked from fully submerged down to 75% submerged. What is the highest liquid density it can read?","choices":["0.68 g/cm³","0.83 g/cm³","0.90 g/cm³","1.20 g/cm³"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"0.68 multiplies 0.90 × 0.75; floating gives ρ_fluid = ρ_object ÷ fraction, so a fraction below 1 must raise the density."},{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Ratio upside-down (0.75 ÷ 0.90); riding higher needs a denser liquid, so the answer must sit above 0.90."},{"c":2,"tag":"copied","why":"0.90 g/cm³ is the hydrometer's own density, the fully submerged reading: the lowest on the scale, not the highest."}],"rule":"Fluid density = object density ÷ fraction submerged: the less it sinks, the denser the liquid, so the top reading uses the smallest fraction.","exp":"From fraction = ρ_object ÷ ρ_fluid, **ρ_fluid = ρ_object ÷ fraction**. Fully submerged (fraction 1) gives the **lowest** reading, 0.90 g/cm³. The smallest marked fraction gives the **highest**: 0.90 ÷ 0.75 = **1.20 g/cm³**.","how":{"ask":"At which mark does the hydrometer float highest, and what liquid density puts it there?","know":"• Floating: submerged fraction = ρ_object ÷ ρ_fluid, so ρ_fluid = ρ_object ÷ fraction.\n• Denser liquid → floats higher → smaller fraction under.\n• Range: fraction 1 → lowest density (= ρ_object); smallest marked fraction → highest density.","steps":[{"text":"The highest density is where the hydrometer floats highest: the smallest marked fraction.","hl":"75% submerged"},{"text":"Solve the floating rule for the liquid's density.","calc":["ρ_fluid = ρ_object ÷ fraction","= 0.90 ÷ 0.75 = **1.20 g/cm³**"]},{"text":"0.83 flips the ratio (0.75 ÷ 0.90) and 0.68 multiplies; both land below 0.90, yet riding higher needs a denser liquid.","strike":["0.83 g/cm³","0.68 g/cm³"]},{"text":"0.90 g/cm³ is the fully submerged reading: the bottom of the range.","strike":["0.90 g/cm³"]},{"text":"Answer: 1.20 g/cm³."}],"trap":"Thinking the fully submerged mark is the top of the scale: a hydrometer sinks deepest in the thinnest liquid, so full submersion is its lowest reading."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"average density of 0.90 g/cm³","note":"ρ_object"},{"t":"fully submerged","note":"fraction 1"},{"t":"75% submerged","note":"smallest fraction"},{"t":"highest liquid density","note":"floats highest"}]},{"text":"**Flip the floating rule.**","calc":["fraction = ρ_obj ÷ ρ_fluid → ρ_fluid = ρ_obj ÷ fraction"]},{"text":"**Both ends of the scale.**","calc":["fraction 1.00 → 0.90 ÷ 1.00 = 0.90 g/cm³ (lowest)","fraction 0.75 → 0.90 ÷ 0.75 = 1.20 g/cm³ (highest)"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a hydrometer reads ρ_fluid = ρ_object ÷ fraction submerged: fully under is its lowest reading, the smallest marked fraction its highest."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-30a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:30#1"],"q":"A cysteine-free protein forms covalent dimers when exposed to hydroxyl radicals. Which single mutation at an exposed site would most reduce this cross-linking?","choices":["Tyr → Phe","Phe → Tyr","Lys → Arg","Ser → Ala"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Phe → Tyr adds a phenol O–H, so it creates a new cross-linking site instead of removing one."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Lys and Arg have no phenol ring, so neither one makes the ring-to-ring link; swapping them changes nothing."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Ser's –OH is an alcohol, not a phenol: its radical has no ring to spread over, and Ser forms no ring-to-ring link."}],"rule":"Radical cross-links join tyrosine phenol rings (dityrosine); a mutation helps only if it removes a phenol O–H.","exp":"Radicals readily take H• from **tyrosine's phenol O–H** (the tyrosyl radical is resonance-stabilized), and two tyrosyl radicals join **ring to ring** (dityrosine). Phe has the ring but no OH, so **Tyr → Phe removes the site**; Phe → Tyr adds one. With no Cys there are no disulfides, and Lys, Arg and Ser form no such link.","how":{"ask":"Which residue starts the radical cross-link, and which swap takes it away?","know":"• Covalent cross-links: **disulfide** (Cys–S–S–Cys, needs two thiols) and **dityrosine** (Tyr ring–C–C–Tyr ring, made by radical oxidation).\n• Radicals readily take H• from tyrosine's phenol O–H: the tyrosyl radical is resonance-stabilized.\n• Phe has the same ring but no OH, so it forms no tyrosyl radical.","steps":[{"text":"No cysteine means no thiols, so the link cannot be a disulfide.","hl":"cysteine-free","calc":["no Cys → no –SH → no S–S"]},{"text":"Radicals oxidize Tyr: the phenol loses H•, and two tyrosyl radicals bond ring to ring.","hl":"hydroxyl radicals","fig":"tyrosine-sc","calc":["Tyr–OH + •OH → Tyr–O• + H₂O","2 Tyr–O• → dityrosine (C–C between rings)"]},{"text":"Tyr → Phe keeps the ring but drops the OH, so no tyrosyl radical can form there.","calc":["Tyr (ring + OH) → Phe (ring only) → no H• to give → no link"]},{"text":"Phe → Tyr adds a phenol, a new site; Lys → Arg and Ser → Ala swap residues that make no such link.","strike":["Phe → Tyr","Lys → Arg","Ser → Ala"]},{"text":"Answer: Tyr → Phe, which removes the dityrosine site.","fig":"cpt-pe6-dityrosine"}],"trap":"Choosing Phe → Tyr because it involves the aromatic ring; it adds a phenol O–H, so it creates a site instead of removing one."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"cysteine-free","note":"no S–S possible"},{"t":"covalent dimers","note":"a real bond, not a contact"},{"t":"hydroxyl radicals","note":"steal H atoms"}]},{"text":"**Which side chain gives up H• most easily?** Tyrosine's phenol O–H; the unpaired electron then spreads over the ring.","fig":"tyrosine-sc"},{"text":"**Two radicals pair up.** Two tyrosyl rings join by a C–C bond beside each OH: dityrosine.","fig":"cpt-pe6-dityrosine"},{"text":"**Sort the links.**","calc":["covalent: disulfide (needs Cys), dityrosine (needs Tyr + radicals)","noncovalent: salt bridge, H-bond, hydrophobic contact"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to stop a cross-link, remove the group that starts it: without Cys, radicals start at Tyr's phenol O–H, so Tyr → Phe (ring kept, OH gone) blocks dityrosine."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-33a","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe6-cp:33#1","pe6-cp:33#2"],"q":"After a dye-labeling reaction, the Coomassie-stained sample lane shows two bands: one level with untreated protein, and a shifted band, one-third as dark, that fluoresces. Roughly what percent of the protein stayed unlabeled?","choices":["About 25%","About 75%","About 100%","About 33%"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"backwards","why":"25% is the labeled share: the faint glowing band is the dye-bearing protein. Assigning it to unlabeled protein flips the answer."},{"c":3,"tag":"copied","why":"33% copies the stem's 'one-third' as is; that is a band-to-band ratio, not a share of all the protein."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"The shifted band takes the protein stain AND fluoresces, so some protein carries dye; not all of it can be unlabeled."}],"rule":"Turn a darkness ratio into shares of the total (a : b means a ÷ (a + b)), and decide which band is which first.","exp":"Coomassie shows all protein; fluorescence shows dye. The band level with untreated protein **stains but does not glow**: unlabeled. The shifted band that fluoresces is **labeled** protein. Darkness 3 : 1, so unlabeled = 3 ÷ (3 + 1) ≈ **75%**.","how":{"ask":"Which stained band is the unlabeled protein, and what share of the total stain does it hold?","know":"• **Protein stain** (Coomassie) shows every protein band; **fluorescence** shows only dye.\n• A lane of untreated protein marks where unlabeled protein runs.\n• Band darkness is proportional to amount, so a fraction = that band ÷ the sum of all protein bands.","steps":[{"text":"Assign each band by the two tests.","hl":"one level with untreated protein","calc":["level with untreated: stain ✓, glow ✗ → **unlabeled**","shifted band: stain ✓, glow ✓ → **labeled**"]},{"text":"The labeled band both stains and glows, so some of the protein carries dye.","hl":"that fluoresces","strike":["About 100%"]},{"text":"Turn darkness into a share of the total. 25% is the labeled share, and 33% is the 1 : 3 ratio, not a share of all the protein.","hl":"one-third as dark","calc":["unlabeled : labeled = 3 : 1","unlabeled = 3 ÷ (3 + 1) = **0.75 = 75%**","labeled = 1 ÷ 4 = 25%"],"strike":["About 25%","About 33%"]},{"text":"Answer: about 75% stayed unlabeled."}],"trap":"Reading 1 : 3 as one-third; a fraction divides by the total of both bands (4 parts), not by the other band."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Coomassie-stained","note":"shows all protein"},{"t":"level with untreated protein","note":"where unlabeled runs"},{"t":"fluoresces","note":"carries dye"},{"t":"one-third as dark","note":"1 part vs 3 parts"}]},{"text":"**Compare the lanes.** The untreated-protein lane is the ruler: a sample band at the same height is protein with no dye on it."},{"text":"**Two tests per band.**","calc":["stain ✓ glow ✓ → labeled protein","stain ✓ glow ✗ → unlabeled protein","stain ✗ glow ✓ → free dye"]},{"text":"**Turn darkness into a share.**","calc":["unlabeled 3 parts + labeled 1 part = 4 parts","unlabeled = 3/4 = 75%"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** find the unlabeled band by its match to the untreated lane (stains, no glow), then divide its darkness by the total of all protein bands."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-33b","s":"Separations & Lab Techniques","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5C","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe6-cp:33#3"],"q":"A dye-labeled enzyme is run on SDS-PAGE. A band at the very bottom of the gel glows under UV light but stays blank after protein staining. What is this band?","choices":["Unreacted free dye","Unlabeled enzyme","Labeled enzyme fragments","Dye-labeled enzyme dimer"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Unlabeled enzyme carries no dye, so it cannot glow; and being protein, it would take up the stain."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Enzyme fragments are still protein, so they would take up the protein stain."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"A dimer is twice the enzyme's size, so it runs near the top, not the bottom; it would also stain for protein."}],"rule":"Glows but doesn't stain means dye with no protein attached; on SDS-PAGE the smallest species runs farthest down.","exp":"Glow = dye present; no stain = **no protein**. Small, unreacted dye molecules run farthest, to the dye front at the bottom. Free dye is not protein, so it is left out when counting labeled and unlabeled protein.","how":{"ask":"What glows but contains no protein, and why does it run the farthest?","know":"• Fluorescence reports **dye**; a protein stain reports **protein**.\n• In SDS-PAGE, small species run farthest; a free dye of about 1 kDa or less runs with the dye front.\n• Free dye is not protein: leave it out when you count labeled and unlabeled protein.","steps":[{"text":"Two tests on this band: glow yes, stain no.","hl":"glows under UV light but stays blank after protein staining","calc":["glow ✓ → dye present","stain ✗ → no protein"]},{"text":"Anything made of protein would stain: the enzyme, its fragments or a dimer. Unlabeled enzyme also has no dye to glow, and a dimer would run near the top.","strike":["Unlabeled enzyme","Labeled enzyme fragments","Dye-labeled enzyme dimer"]},{"text":"It sits at the bottom, where the smallest species run.","hl":"very bottom of the gel"},{"text":"Answer: unreacted free dye."}],"trap":"Calling every fluorescent band labeled protein; a glow only proves dye, and a band with no protein stain holds no protein."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"SDS-PAGE","note":"smaller runs farther"},{"t":"very bottom of the gel","note":"smallest species"},{"t":"glows under UV light","note":"dye is there"},{"t":"stays blank after protein staining","note":"no protein"}]},{"text":"**Two stains, two questions.** UV glow asks “is dye here?”; the protein stain asks “is protein here?”","calc":["glow ✓ stain ✓ → labeled protein","glow ✗ stain ✓ → unlabeled protein","glow ✓ stain ✗ → free dye"]},{"text":"**Size sets the position.** A dye of under a kilodalton outruns any protein and collects at the dye front."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a glowing band that a protein stain leaves blank is free label: small, near the dye front, and never counted as protein."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-34a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:34#1","pe6-cp:34#3"],"q":"An electrophilic dye is mixed with a protein in neutral buffer. Of the side chains shown, which is the strongest nucleophile toward the dye?","choices":["Lys","Thr","Gln","Glu"],"choiceFigs":["lysine-sc","threonine-sc","glutamine-sc","glutamate-sc"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"An alcohol O–H is a weak nucleophile in water; that is why amine- and thiol-reactive dyes leave Ser and Thr alone."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"An amide N's lone pair is delocalized into the C=O, so Gln (like Asn) barely reacts as a nucleophile."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"A carboxylate spreads its charge over two O atoms by resonance, so Glu is a weak nucleophile."}],"rule":"In protein labeling the strong nucleophiles are a free amine or thiol lone pair; O–H, amides and carboxylates are weak.","exp":"Lysine's ε-amine (pKa ≈ 10.5) is mostly –NH₃⁺ at pH 7, but its small **neutral –NH₂ share** has a free lone pair and reacts fast. Thr's –OH, Gln's amide and Glu's resonance-stabilized carboxylate are **poor nucleophiles**. Cysteine's –SH/–S⁻ is the other strong nucleophile at pH 7.","how":{"ask":"Which side chain has a lone pair that is free (not delocalized, not tightly held) at pH 7?","know":"• Good nucleophiles at pH 7: **Lys –NH₂** (the unprotonated share) and **Cys –SH/–S⁻**.\n• Poor: alcohol O (Ser, Thr), amide N (Asn, Gln), carboxylate O (Asp, Glu).\n• Amide and carboxylate lone pairs are delocalized by resonance.","steps":[{"text":"The dye needs an electron-pair donor at pH 7.","hl":"electrophilic dye","fig":"lysine-sc"},{"text":"Rule out the weak donors: Thr holds its O lone pairs tightly, and Gln and Glu spread theirs by resonance.","calc":["Thr: R–OH, weak O nucleophile","Gln: R–C(=O)NH₂, N lone pair in resonance","Glu: R–COO⁻, charge spread over two O"],"strike":["Thr","Gln","Glu"]},{"text":"Lys: only a tiny share is neutral –NH₂ at pH 7, but that free amine reacts, and more deprotonates as it is used up.","calc":["share as –NH₂ = 1 ÷ (1 + 10^(10.5 − 7))","= 1 ÷ (1 + 3162) ≈ 0.03%"]},{"text":"Answer: Lys."}],"trap":"Picking Glu because it carries a full negative charge; charge alone doesn't make a good nucleophile when resonance spreads it over two oxygens."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"electrophilic dye","note":"needs a lone-pair donor"},{"t":"neutral buffer","note":"pH ≈ 7: check protonation"},{"t":"strongest nucleophile","note":"most available lone pair"}]},{"text":"**Two strong nucleophiles at pH 7:** lysine's –NH₂ (amine) and cysteine's –SH (thiol, partly S⁻).","fig":["lysine-sc","cysteine-sc"]},{"text":"**Also:** Asp, Glu, Ser, Thr, Asn and Gln are poor nucleophiles at pH 7: alcohol oxygens hold their lone pairs tightly, and amides and carboxylates delocalize theirs."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** to find a labeling site, look for an available lone pair: the Lys amine and the Cys thiol react; O–H, amide and carboxylate side chains do not."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-34b","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:34#2"],"q":"A protein has several exposed lysines but no free cysteine. Which class of reactive dye should be used to label it?","choices":["NHS ester","Maleimide","Iodoacetamide","Acyl hydrazide"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Maleimides add to thiols (Cys –SH); with no free cysteine there is nothing for them to label."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Iodoacetamides alkylate thiols (Cys –SH); with no free cysteine there is nothing for them to label."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Hydrazides react with aldehydes and ketones (such as oxidized sugars), not with amine side chains."}],"rule":"Match the dye to a group the protein has: maleimides and iodoacetamides need Cys thiols; hydrazides need carbonyls; amines take acylating esters.","exp":"Match the dye to its target. **NHS esters** (and isothiocyanates) are **amine-reactive**: they acylate Lys –NH₂ and the N-terminus. **Maleimides and iodoacetamides** are **thiol-reactive** (Cys). Hydrazides target carbonyls. With lysines and no cysteine, use an NHS ester.","how":{"ask":"Which side-chain group is available, and which dye chemistry reacts with it?","know":"• **Amine-reactive**: NHS esters, isothiocyanates → Lys and the N-terminal NH₂.\n• **Thiol-reactive**: maleimides, iodoacetamides → Cys.\n• **Carbonyl-reactive**: hydrazides → aldehydes and ketones.","steps":[{"text":"The available nucleophile is the lysine amine; there is no thiol.","hl":"exposed lysines but no free cysteine","fig":"lysine-sc"},{"text":"Thiol-reactive dyes need a cysteine –SH, which this protein lacks.","strike":["Maleimide","Iodoacetamide"],"fig":"cysteine-sc"},{"text":"Hydrazides need a carbonyl, which ordinary side chains don't offer.","strike":["Acyl hydrazide"]},{"text":"Answer: an NHS ester (amine-reactive), which forms an amide with the Lys –NH₂.","calc":["R–CO–NHS + Lys–NH₂ → R–CO–NH–Lys + NHS"]}],"trap":"Iodoacetamide sounds like an amine reagent; its iodo carbon is an alkylating group aimed at thiols."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"exposed lysines","note":"–NH₂ targets"},{"t":"no free cysteine","note":"no –SH target"},{"t":"reactive dye","note":"match chemistry to group"}]},{"text":"**Pair each dye class with its group.**","calc":["NHS ester, isothiocyanate → amine (Lys)","maleimide, iodoacetamide → thiol (Cys)","hydrazide → C=O"]},{"text":"**How the NHS ester works.** The Lys amine attacks the ester C=O, NHS leaves, and an amide bond holds the dye.","fig":"lysine-sc"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** amine-reactive dyes (NHS esters, isothiocyanates) label Lys and the N-terminus; thiol-reactive dyes (maleimides, iodoacetamides) label Cys."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-35a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:35#1"],"q":"Hypertonic saline used to treat brain swelling is 3.0% (w/v) NaCl (Na 23 g/mol, Cl 35.5 g/mol). What is its approximate molarity?","choices":["0.051 M","0.51 M","1.0 M","1.3 M"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"units","why":"That skips the × 10 to a litre: 3.0% w/v is 3.0 g per 100 mL, so 30 g per litre."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"That divides by 23, sodium alone; the formula unit NaCl weighs 58.5 g/mol."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"That doubles for Na⁺ and Cl⁻: it is the total ion (osmolar) concentration, not the molarity of NaCl."}],"rule":"w/v %: grams per 100 mL, so × 10 gives grams per litre, then ÷ molar mass. Estimate 30 ÷ 60.","exp":"**% (w/v) = grams per 100 mL**, so 3.0% = 3.0 g/100 mL = **30 g/L**. Molarity = 30 g/L ÷ 58.5 g/mol ≈ **0.51 M**. For comparison, normal saline (0.9%) is 9 g/L ÷ 58.5 ≈ 0.15 M.","how":{"ask":"Turn the percent into grams per litre, then divide by the molar mass.","know":"• **% (w/v) = g solute per 100 mL**; × 10 gives g/L.\n• **M = (g/L) ÷ (g/mol)**.\n• Normal saline is **0.9% NaCl = 9 g/L ≈ 0.15 M**, isotonic with blood (≈ 0.3 osmol/L of ions).","steps":[{"text":"Grams per litre from the percent.","hl":"3.0% (w/v) NaCl","calc":["3.0 g per 100 mL × 10 = 30 g/L"]},{"text":"Find the formula mass and divide.","hl":"Na 23 g/mol, Cl 35.5 g/mol","calc":["23 + 35.5 = 58.5 g/mol","30 ÷ 58.5 ≈ **0.51 M**"]},{"text":"0.051 M skips the × 10 to a litre, 1.0 M counts both ions, and 1.3 M uses sodium's mass alone.","strike":["0.051 M","1.0 M","1.3 M"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.51 M."}],"trap":"Treating 3.0 g per 100 mL as 3.0 g per litre: multiply by 10 before dividing."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"3.0% (w/v)","note":"3.0 g per 100 mL"},{"t":"Cl 35.5 g/mol","note":"add Na: 58.5 g/mol"},{"t":"Hypertonic","note":"stronger than normal saline"}]},{"text":"**Percent to molarity in two moves.** × 10 converts g/100 mL to g/L; ÷ molar mass converts g to mol.","calc":["M = (% × 10) ÷ M_r"]},{"text":"**Also:** normal saline is 0.9% NaCl: 0.9 × 10 = 9 g/L, and 9 ÷ 58.5 ≈ 0.15 M. The 3.0% solution is about 3.3 times stronger, hence hypertonic."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** % (w/v) × 10 = g/L; divide by molar mass for molarity. Normal saline, 0.9%, is about 0.15 M NaCl."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-36a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:36#1","pe6-cp:36#2"],"q":"A student pipettes 1.5 mL of an antibody fragment solution at 16 mg/mL into a tube. The fragment is 80 kDa. How many moles were transferred?","choices":["0.3 nmol","3 μmol","0.3 mmol","0.3 μmol"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"units","why":"That divides by 1000 one time too many; 24 mg is 0.024 g, one factor of 1000 only."},{"c":1,"tag":"units","why":"That uses 8000 g/mol; 80 kDa is 80 000 g/mol, so the result is 10 times too big."},{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"That divides grams by 80 g/mol: 1 kDa = 1000 g/mol, so the molar mass is 80 000 g/mol, not 80."}],"rule":"Mass = volume × concentration (1.5 mL × 16 mg/mL); 1 kDa = 1000 g/mol. Estimate 24 mg ÷ 80 000 g/mol.","exp":"**Mass = concentration × volume**: 16 mg/mL × 1.5 mL = 24 mg = 0.024 g. **80 kDa = 80 000 g/mol.** Moles = 0.024 g ÷ 80 000 g/mol = 3 × 10⁻⁷ mol = **0.3 μmol**.","how":{"ask":"Get the mass in grams, convert kDa to g/mol, then divide.","know":"• **mass = (mg/mL) × mL**, then mg ÷ 1000 = g.\n• **1 kDa = 1000 Da**, and Da per molecule = g/mol, so **kDa × 1000 = g/mol**.\n• **mol = g ÷ (g/mol)**; 10⁻⁶ mol = 1 μmol.","steps":[{"text":"Mass delivered = concentration × volume.","hl":"1.5 mL of an antibody fragment solution at 16 mg/mL","calc":["16 mg/mL × 1.5 mL = 24 mg","24 mg ÷ 1000 = 0.024 g"]},{"text":"Convert the size to a molar mass.","hl":"80 kDa","calc":["80 kDa × 1000 = 80 000 g/mol"]},{"text":"Divide grams by g/mol.","calc":["0.024 ÷ 80 000 = 3 × 10⁻⁷ mol","3 × 10⁻⁷ mol = **0.3 μmol**"]},{"text":"0.3 mmol skips the kDa conversion, 3 μmol uses 8000 g/mol, and 0.3 nmol divides by 1000 one time too many.","strike":["0.3 mmol","3 μmol","0.3 nmol"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.3 μmol."}],"trap":"Dividing by 80 instead of 80 000: kDa must become g/mol before you divide."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"1.5 mL","note":"volume"},{"t":"16 mg/mL","note":"mass per volume"},{"t":"80 kDa","note":"molar mass, ×1000"}]},{"text":"**Mass from concentration.** A mg/mL concentration times a volume in mL gives mg directly.","calc":["16 × 1.5 = 24 mg = 2.4 × 10⁻² g"]},{"text":"**Moles from mass.** Divide by the molar mass in g/mol, after turning kDa into g/mol.","calc":["2.4 × 10⁻² g ÷ 8.0 × 10⁴ g/mol = 3.0 × 10⁻⁷ mol"]},{"text":"**Also:** writing every quantity in powers of ten keeps the zeros honest: 10⁻² ÷ 10⁴ = 10⁻⁶, the micro range, as expected for milligrams of a large protein."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** mol of protein = (mg/mL × mL ÷ 1000) ÷ (kDa × 1000)."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-37a","s":"Protein Structure","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe6-cp:37#1"],"q":"An amine-reactive dye labels a single lysine of a kinase. The labeled kinase stays folded but loses all activity. Where is that lysine most likely?","choices":["In the substrate cleft","On a distant surface loop","Inside the nonpolar core","Anywhere on the protein"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"A tag on a loop far from the catalytic pocket leaves activity intact, as most surface labels do."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Lysine is charged and faces water; a dye in buffer can't reach the buried core while the kinase stays folded."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Most surface labels leave activity untouched, so a total loss points to one special spot, not a random one."}],"rule":"One small label that kills activity but not folding must sit where the protein does its work; buried sites are out of reach.","exp":"A dye in water reaches exposed residues. A tag on an exposed patch **far from the catalytic pocket** leaves activity unchanged. A tag that **abolishes activity while the fold is intact** must sit where substrate binds, physically blocking it.","how":{"ask":"Activity is lost but the fold is intact: which spot must a bulky dye be blocking?","know":"• A water-soluble reagent labels **exposed** residues; buried ones are out of reach while the protein is folded.\n• Exposed sites far from the catalytic pocket: label tolerated, activity unchanged.\n• Label in or at the mouth of the catalytic pocket: substrate blocked, activity lost.","steps":[{"text":"Two clues: still folded, all activity gone.","hl":"stays folded but loses all activity"},{"text":"Folded means the core is closed to the dye, and a charged lysine sits at the surface anyway.","hl":"amine-reactive dye","strike":["Inside the nonpolar core"]},{"text":"A tag far from the catalytic pocket would leave activity intact, and most surface sites are like that.","strike":["On a distant surface loop","Anywhere on the protein"]},{"text":"Answer: in the substrate cleft, where the dye blocks substrate binding."}],"trap":"Assuming any label disturbs an enzyme; most surface labels are harmless, so a total loss of activity pinpoints the catalytic pocket."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"amine-reactive dye","note":"tags exposed Lys"},{"t":"stays folded","note":"core still closed"},{"t":"loses all activity","note":"catalytic pocket hit"}]},{"text":"**Where can a dye in water reach?** Only exposed residues; a folded protein keeps its core shut."},{"text":"**Activity lost + fold intact** → the dye sits where substrate binds."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a water-borne label reaches only exposed residues; unchanged activity puts it far from the catalytic pocket, and lost activity with an intact fold puts it in the pocket."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-38a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:38#1"],"q":"A technician draws 5.0 mL of a 3.0 mM calcium chloride (CaCl₂) solution. How many moles of chloride ions does this volume hold?","choices":["15 μmol","30 μmol","30 mmol","30 nmol"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"units","why":"mM × mL gives μmol, not mmol: (10⁻³ mol/L)(10⁻³ L) = 10⁻⁶ mol."},{"c":3,"tag":"units","why":"That applies one 10⁻³ too many; only the mM and the mL each carry a factor of 10⁻³."},{"c":0,"tag":"skipped","why":"15 μmol is the CaCl₂ itself; each formula unit releases two Cl⁻, so the ×2 was skipped."}],"rule":"mM × mL = μmol; then multiply by the ions per formula unit (CaCl₂ gives 2 Cl⁻).","exp":"**mM × mL = μmol**: 3.0 mM × 5.0 mL = 15 μmol of CaCl₂. Each **CaCl₂ gives 2 Cl⁻**, so n(Cl⁻) = 2 × 15 = **30 μmol** (3.0 × 10⁻⁵ mol).","how":{"ask":"Multiply concentration by volume with matched prefixes, then count chlorides per formula unit.","know":"• **n = M × V.** Prefix pairs: **M × L = mol**, **M × mL = mmol**, **mM × mL = μmol**.\n• Ions per formula unit come from the subscripts: CaCl₂ → 1 Ca²⁺ + 2 Cl⁻.","steps":[{"text":"Multiply, keeping the prefixes.","hl":"5.0 mL of a 3.0 mM","calc":["3.0 mM × 5.0 mL = 15 μmol CaCl₂","check: 3.0 × 10⁻³ mol/L × 5.0 × 10⁻³ L = 1.5 × 10⁻⁵ mol"]},{"text":"Count chloride ions.","hl":"CaCl₂","calc":["2 × 15 μmol = **30 μmol Cl⁻**"]},{"text":"15 μmol is the salt, not the chloride; 30 mmol and 30 nmol misplace a factor of 1000.","strike":["15 μmol","30 mmol","30 nmol"]},{"text":"Answer: 30 μmol."}],"trap":"Writing mM × mL as mmol: two milli factors make micro."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"5.0 mL","note":"volume, 10⁻³ L"},{"t":"3.0 mM","note":"10⁻³ mol/L"},{"t":"chloride ions","note":"two per CaCl₂"}]},{"text":"**Milli × milli = micro.** 10⁻³ × 10⁻³ = 10⁻⁶, so the product of mM and mL comes out in μmol.","calc":["3.0 × 5.0 = 15 → 15 μmol"]},{"text":"**Ions per unit.** Dissolving one CaCl₂ gives one Ca²⁺ and two Cl⁻.","calc":["n(Cl⁻) = 2 × 15 μmol = 30 μmol"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** n = M × V with matched prefixes (mM × mL = μmol, M × mL = mmol), then multiply by the ions per formula unit."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-39a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:39#1","pe6-cp:39#2"],"q":"A 12.0 g rock sample is 60% magnetite, Fe₃O₄ (Fe 56 g/mol, O 16 g/mol), by mass. Approximately how much iron does the sample contain?","choices":["1.7 g","5.2 g","7.2 g","8.7 g"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"skipped","why":"That counts one Fe per formula unit; Fe₃O₄ holds three iron atoms, so the fraction is 3 × 56, not 56."},{"c":2,"tag":"skipped","why":"7.2 g is the mass of magnetite in the rock; part of that oxide is oxygen, so the iron must weigh less."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"That skips the 60%: it treats the whole 12.0 g rock as magnetite."}],"rule":"Chain the fractions: rock mass × % mineral × (Fe mass in formula ÷ formula mass). Estimate 12 × 0.6 × 0.7.","exp":"Magnetite in the rock: 0.60 × 12.0 g = 7.2 g. **Fe₃O₄ = 3(56) + 4(16) = 232 g/mol**, and iron's mass fraction is **168 ÷ 232 ≈ 0.72**. Iron = 0.72 × 7.2 g ≈ **5.2 g**.","how":{"ask":"Find the oxide's mass, then the iron fraction of the oxide, and multiply.","know":"• **Mass fraction of an element = (atoms of it × atomic mass) ÷ formula mass.**\n• Mass of the element = fraction × mass of the compound.\n• In a mixture, first find the compound's own mass: % × total.","steps":[{"text":"Mass of the compound in the mixture.","hl":"12.0 g rock sample is 60% magnetite","calc":["0.60 × 12.0 g = 7.2 g Fe₃O₄"]},{"text":"Formula mass and iron's share.","hl":"Fe₃O₄","calc":["3 × 56 = 168; 4 × 16 = 64","168 + 64 = 232 g/mol","168 ÷ 232 ≈ 0.72"]},{"text":"Multiply the fraction by the oxide's mass.","calc":["0.72 × 7.2 g ≈ **5.2 g Fe**"]},{"text":"7.2 g stops at the oxide, 8.7 g skips the 60%, and 1.7 g counts only one iron atom.","strike":["7.2 g","8.7 g","1.7 g"]},{"text":"Answer: 5.2 g."}],"trap":"Counting one Fe atom per formula unit: the subscript 3 multiplies the iron's mass."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"12.0 g","note":"whole rock"},{"t":"60%","note":"share that is oxide"},{"t":"Fe₃O₄","note":"three Fe per unit"}]},{"text":"**Peel the layers from outside in.** Rock → oxide (60%) → iron (its mass fraction in the oxide).","calc":["mass Fe = total × %oxide × (n·M_Fe ÷ M_oxide)"]},{"text":"**Mass fraction of the metal.** Count the metal atoms, multiply by the atomic mass, and divide by the formula mass.","calc":["(3 × 56) ÷ 232 = 168 ÷ 232 ≈ 0.72"]},{"text":"**Also:** multiply that fraction by the mass of the compound to get the element's mass: 0.72 × 7.2 g ≈ 5.2 g."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** element mass = (atoms × atomic mass ÷ formula mass) × compound mass, after taking the compound's % of any mixture."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-42a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"organic_chem","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:42#2"],"q":"Aspartate side chains that hold Ca²⁺ at neutral pH release it in strong acid. Which form of free aspartate, shown, dominates at pH 1.2 (pKa: α-COOH 1.9, side chain 3.7, α-NH₃⁺ 9.6)?","choices":["Cation (net +1)","Zwitterion (net 0)","Anion (net −1)","Dianion (net −2)"],"choiceFigs":["cpt-pe6-asp-plus1","cpt-pe6-asp-net0","cpt-pe6-asp-minus1","cpt-pe6-asp-minus2"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Net 0 needs the α-COOH ionized, at pH above 1.9; at pH 1.2 it still holds its H⁺."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"−1 has both carboxyls ionized, the pH 7 form that grips Ca²⁺; pH 1.2 is below both carboxyl pKa values."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"−2 needs the amine deprotonated, at pH above 9.6; at pH 1.2 the amine is NH₃⁺."}],"rule":"Below a group's pKa it keeps its H⁺: compare the pH with each pKa, then add up the charges.","exp":"pH 1.2 is **below every pKa**, so every group keeps its H⁺: α-COOH (0), side-chain COOH (0), α-NH₃⁺ (+1) → **net +1**. With its side chain as neutral –COOH instead of –COO⁻, aspartate can no longer hold Ca²⁺, so the ion is released.","how":{"ask":"For each group, is pH 1.2 above or below its pKa? Then add up the charges.","know":"• **pH < pKa → protonated** (COOH neutral, NH₃⁺ +1); pH > pKa → deprotonated (COO⁻ −1, NH₂ neutral).\n• Metal ions are held by negative **–COO⁻**; protonating it to –COOH releases the metal.","steps":[{"text":"Compare 1.2 with each pKa.","hl":"pKa: α-COOH 1.9, side chain 3.7, α-NH₃⁺ 9.6","calc":["α-COOH: 1.2 < 1.9 → COOH (0)","side-chain COOH: 1.2 < 3.7 → COOH (0)","α-NH₃⁺: 1.2 < 9.6 → NH₃⁺ (+1)","net = 0 + 0 + 1 = **+1**"]},{"text":"The matching drawing has two COOH groups and NH₃⁺.","fig":"cpt-pe6-asp-plus1"},{"text":"Ionized carboxyls need pH above their pKa values (1.9 and 3.7), and a neutral amine needs pH above 9.6.","strike":["Zwitterion (net 0)","Anion (net −1)","Dianion (net −2)"]},{"text":"Answer: the cation, net charge +1; the side chain is neutral –COOH and lets go of Ca²⁺."}],"trap":"Stopping after the side chain: check the α-COOH too. pH 1.2 is even below its pKa of 1.9, so the zwitterion is not the main form."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"Ca²⁺","note":"held by –COO⁻"},{"t":"release it in strong acid","note":"–COO⁻ picks up H⁺"},{"t":"pH 1.2","note":"very acidic"},{"t":"pKa: α-COOH 1.9, side chain 3.7, α-NH₃⁺ 9.6","note":"compare each"}]},{"text":"**Carboxylates bind metal ions.** At pH 7 the side chain is –COO⁻, and its negative oxygens grip Ca²⁺.","fig":"cpt-pe6-asp-minus1"},{"text":"**Lower the pH below the side chain's pKa** and –COO⁻ picks up H⁺; the neutral –COOH cannot hold the metal, so the ion is released."},{"text":"**Then check every group** against its own pKa and add the charges.","calc":["below pKa → H⁺ on","above pKa → H⁺ off"],"fig":"cpt-pe6-asp-plus1"},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** metal-binding carboxylates release the metal once pH falls below their pKa; to find the dominant form, compare pH with each group's pKa and add the charges."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-43a","s":"Electrochemistry & Redox","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4C","skill":"SIRS 3","from":["pe6-cp:43#1","pe6-cp:43#2"],"q":"In a sodium-ion cell, NaClO₄ dissolved in a dry solvent fills the gap between the electrodes. Which change would stop the cell's current, even with the external wire connected?","choices":["Removing the dissolved salt","Using a longer wire","Adding extra dissolved salt","Using a thicker wire"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"More salt means more ions in the gap, which carries charge better, not worse."},{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"A thicker wire lowers the resistance, so the current would rise slightly, not stop."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"A longer wire adds a little resistance, but the loop stays closed, so current still flows."}],"rule":"A cell needs a closed loop: electrons in the wire and ions in the electrolyte. Only breaking one path stops the current.","exp":"The dissolved NaClO₄ is the **electrolyte**: its Na⁺ and ClO₄⁻ ions carry charge across the gap and keep each side neutral. Without the salt the pure solvent has almost no ions, so charge piles up at the electrodes and **the current stops**. Electrons still travel only through the external wire; its length or thickness just tweaks the resistance.","how":{"ask":"What carries charge across the gap between the electrodes, and what carries it through the wire?","know":"• A battery's loop has two halves: **electrons** travel through the external wire; **ions** travel through the electrolyte.\n• The electrolyte is a dissolved (or molten) salt; its ions balance the charge each electrode gains or loses.\n• The electrolyte never carries electrons; if it did, the cell would short itself.","steps":[{"text":"The dissolved salt supplies Na⁺ and ClO₄⁻: it is the electrolyte.","hl":"NaClO₄ dissolved in a dry solvent"},{"text":"Electrons leave the anode through the wire; Na⁺ moves through the electrolyte toward the cathode to balance the charge.","calc":["wire: e⁻ anode → cathode","electrolyte: Na⁺ anode side → cathode side"]},{"text":"Wire length and thickness change the resistance a little; neither breaks the loop.","strike":["Using a longer wire","Using a thicker wire"]},{"text":"More salt means more ions, a better conductor across the gap.","strike":["Adding extra dissolved salt"]},{"text":"Answer: removing the dissolved salt. With no ions, charge builds up at the electrodes and the current stops."}],"trap":"Thinking the wire alone completes the circuit: the loop also runs through the cell, where only ions can carry the charge."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"NaClO₄ dissolved","note":"ions: the electrolyte"},{"t":"gap between the electrodes","note":"ions cross here"},{"t":"external wire","note":"electrons go here"}]},{"text":"**Two kinds of carriers.** Electrons can't swim through a solution, and ions can't enter copper, so the loop uses each where it can move."},{"text":"**Also:** electrons go only through the external wire, from anode to cathode; the electrolyte carries ions, never electrons."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** in any battery the electrolyte carries ions between the electrodes and the external wire carries the electrons; no ions, no current."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-44a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:44#1","pe6-cp:44#2"],"q":"The four lipids shown were all extracted from human skin. Which one is NOT a structural lipid of cell membranes?","choices":["Sphingomyelin","Galactosyl ceramide","Cetyl palmitate","Phosphatidylinositol"],"choiceFigs":["cpt-pe6-sphingomyelin","cpt-pe6-galactocerebroside","cpt-pe6-cetyl-palmitate","cpt-pe6-phosphatidylinositol"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Sphingomyelin is a sphingolipid with a phosphocholine head: a classic membrane lipid, rich in the outer leaflet."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Galactosyl ceramide is sphingosine, one acyl chain and a sugar head: a glycosphingolipid of membranes."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Phosphatidylinositol is glycerol + two acyl chains + a phosphate–inositol head: a glycerophospholipid, so it sits in membranes."}],"rule":"Membrane lipids are amphipathic, with a polar head (phosphate or sugar) on a glycerol or sphingosine backbone; a wax ester has no head.","exp":"Membrane lipids carry a polar head: **glycerophospholipids** (phosphatidylinositol), **sphingolipids** (sphingomyelin, galactosyl ceramide) and **sterols** (cholesterol). **Cetyl palmitate** is a wax: a fatty acid esterified to a long alcohol, with no polar head. Like storage fat, it is not built into membranes; it waterproofs the skin surface.","how":{"ask":"Which lipid lacks the polar head that every membrane lipid needs?","know":"• **Membrane lipids:** glycerophospholipids (PE, PS, PI), sphingolipids (sphingomyelin, cerebrosides, gangliosides) and sterols (cholesterol).\n• **Not membrane lipids:** neutral storage fats, free fatty acids (fuel), waxes (protective coatings).\n• Quick test: no polar head group → not a membrane lipid.","steps":[{"text":"Sort each lipid by its backbone and its head group.","hl":"NOT a structural lipid"},{"text":"Phosphatidylinositol: glycerol, two acyl chains and a phosphate–inositol head, so a glycerophospholipid.","strike":["Phosphatidylinositol"]},{"text":"Sphingomyelin and galactosyl ceramide: sphingosine, one acyl chain and a polar head (phosphocholine or galactose), so sphingolipids.","strike":["Sphingomyelin","Galactosyl ceramide"]},{"text":"Cetyl palmitate: palmitic acid esterified to a C16 alcohol. Two oily chains and no head: a wax.","calc":["C₁₅H₃₁–CO–O–C₁₆H₃₃: no phosphate, no sugar, no free OH"]},{"text":"Answer: cetyl palmitate."}],"trap":"Rejecting a sphingolipid because it has no glycerol; membranes are built from glycerol-based and sphingosine-based lipids alike."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"NOT a structural lipid","note":"find the odd one out"},{"t":"cell membranes","note":"bilayer lipids have polar heads"}]},{"text":"**Glycerophospholipids:** glycerol, two fatty acyl chains, a phosphate and a head group (ethanolamine, serine, inositol…). The bulk of every membrane."},{"text":"**Sphingolipids:** built on sphingosine instead of glycerol. Sphingomyelin carries phosphocholine; a cerebroside carries one sugar. Both are membrane lipids."},{"text":"**Sterols:** cholesterol's four fused rings and single OH tuck between phospholipids and tune fluidity, so it is a membrane lipid too."},{"text":"**Also:** test every choice for a polar head: a lipid made only of acyl chains on a backbone, with no phosphate, sugar or OH head (a neutral storage fat or a wax), is not a membrane lipid."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a membrane lipid has a polar head on an oily body: phospholipid, sphingolipid or sterol. Head-less lipids (fat, wax) store energy or waterproof."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-44b","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:44#3"],"q":"Which lipid shown, mixed alone with water, forms micelles rather than a bilayer?","choices":["SDS","Trimyristin","Phosphatidylserine","Ergosterol"],"choiceFigs":["sodium-dodecyl-sulfate","cpt-pe6-trimyristin","cpt-pe6-phosphatidylserine","cpt-pe6-ergosterol"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Trimyristin's three acyl chains carry no polar head, so in water it just separates as oil droplets."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Phosphatidylserine has two tails under one head: a cylinder that packs into flat bilayers, not micelles."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Ergosterol is fused rings with one small OH: too little head to assemble on its own."}],"rule":"Shape decides: one tail under a big head (a cone) → micelle; two tails (a cylinder) → bilayer; no head → oil.","exp":"Assembly follows shape. **SDS** (sodium dodecyl sulfate) has **one tail under a big charged sulfate head**: a **cone**, and cones pack into spheres, so it forms **micelles**. Phosphatidylserine (two tails + head) is a cylinder and forms bilayers; trimyristin has no head and separates as droplets; ergosterol's tiny OH cannot organize it alone.","how":{"ask":"Which molecule has one tail under a big polar head, the cone shape that packs into a sphere?","know":"• **Shape decides the assembly:** one tail + big head = cone → **micelle**; two tails + head = cylinder → **bilayer**; no head → oil droplet.\n• Detergents and fatty acids form micelles; phospholipids and sphingolipids form bilayers.\n• Sterols and triacylglycerols cannot assemble alone.","steps":[{"text":"For each molecule, look for a polar head and count the oily tails.","hl":"forms micelles rather than a bilayer"},{"text":"Phosphatidylserine: two acyl tails under a charged head, a cylinder, so it forms a bilayer, not micelles.","calc":["head width ≈ 2 tails → flat sheet → bilayer"],"strike":["Phosphatidylserine"]},{"text":"Trimyristin: three tails, no head, so an oil droplet. Ergosterol: fused rings with one small OH, too little head to assemble alone.","strike":["Trimyristin","Ergosterol"]},{"text":"SDS: one C12 tail on a big sulfate head, a cone.","calc":["wide head + 1 tail → cone → packs into a sphere: micelle"]},{"text":"Answer: SDS (sodium dodecyl sulfate)."}],"trap":"Choosing phosphatidylserine because it is the most familiar membrane lipid; its two tails make a cylinder, which builds bilayers, not micelles."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"micelles","note":"one-tailed cones"},{"t":"rather than a bilayer","note":"two tails would give a sheet"},{"t":"alone with water","note":"it must work alone"}]},{"text":"**Amphipathic first.** A lipid needs a water-loving head and oily tails to organize in water at all; trimyristin has no head."},{"text":"**Then count tails.** One tail (fatty acid, SDS) = cone → micelle. Two-tailed glycerophospholipids such as phosphatidylserine (and sphingolipids) are cylinders → bilayer."},{"text":"**Sterols need partners.** Ergosterol and cholesterol slip between phospholipids but cannot assemble on their own."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** count tails under a polar head: one tail (cone) → micelle; two tails (cylinder) → bilayer; no head → oil."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-45a","s":"Carbohydrates, Lipids & Nucleic Acids","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:45#4","pe6-cp:45#1","pe6-cp:45#2","pe6-cp:45#3"],"q":"The deoxynucleotide dCTP is shown. How many carbon atoms does one molecule of dCTP contain?","fig":"cpt-pe6-dctp","choices":["8","9","11","12"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Deoxyribose keeps all 5 carbons; only the 2′ OH is gone, so no carbon is lost from the sugar."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Cytosine's ring has 6 atoms, but 2 of them are N; counting every ring atom as carbon gives 5 + 6 = 11."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Phosphates hold P and O but no carbon; adding one C per phosphate is the trap."}],"rule":"Count by part: sugar (deoxyribose, 5 C) + only the carbons of the base ring (not N) + phosphates (no C).","exp":"Count by parts: **deoxyribose 5 C** (deoxy = one O missing, not a C) + **cytosine 4 C** + **three phosphates 0 C** = **9 C**.","how":{"ask":"Add the carbons of the sugar, the base and the phosphates.","know":"• **Sugar:** ribose and deoxyribose are both 5-carbon pentoses (C1′–C5′); 'deoxy' means one O fewer at C2′.\n• **Bases:** adenine, guanine and thymine have 5 C each; cytosine and uracil have 4 C.\n• **Phosphate:** PO₄ has no carbon, so one, two or three of them add 0 C.","steps":[{"text":"Split dCTP into its three parts: sugar, base, phosphates.","hl":"dCTP is shown"},{"text":"Sugar: deoxyribose keeps 5 carbons; only the 2′ OH is gone.","calc":["deoxyribose: 5 C"],"strike":["8"]},{"text":"Base: cytosine's ring holds 4 C and 2 N, with an NH₂ and a C=O attached.","calc":["cytosine: 4 C, 3 N"],"strike":["11"]},{"text":"Phosphates: three PO₄ groups, no carbon.","calc":["3 × 0 = 0 C","5 + 4 + 0 = 9 C"],"strike":["12"]},{"text":"Answer: 9 carbon atoms."}],"trap":"Counting deoxyribose as 4 carbons because 'deoxy' sounds like something was removed; it lost an oxygen, not a carbon."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"deoxynucleotide","note":"sugar + base + phosphate(s)"},{"t":"carbon atoms","note":"count C only"}]},{"text":"**Also: the sugar.** Deoxyribose has 5 C, the same as ribose; deoxy only means no O on C2′.","calc":["deoxyribose C₅H₁₀O₄: 5 C"]},{"text":"**Also: the bases.** Adenine and guanine each have 5 C (a six-ring fused to a five-ring that share 2 C); thymine has 5 C (4 ring C + a methyl); cytosine has 4 C."},{"text":"**Also: the phosphates** have no carbon, so dCMP, dCDP and dCTP all have the same 9 C."},{"text":"**Also: purines vs pyrimidines.** Adenine and guanine bring 5 C, one more than cytosine, so a purine nucleotide has one carbon more than the matching cytosine nucleotide."},{"text":"**Also:** count N the same way, by parts: sugar and phosphate have none, so every N sits in the base; dCTP has cytosine's 3 N."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** atoms in a nucleotide = sugar + base + phosphate; sugar 5 C, adenine, guanine and thymine 5 C, cytosine and uracil 4 C, phosphate 0 C."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-46a","s":"Fluids","disc":"biology","aamc":"4B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:46#1"],"q":"Chronic lung disease raises pulmonary arterial pressure from about 25 to 50 mmHg. How would the right ventricle most likely adapt over time?","choices":["Its wall thickens","Its wall thins","Its stroke volume doubles","Its chamber stays unchanged"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"A thinner wall makes less force; a higher pressure to beat calls for more muscle, as the systemic-side ventricle shows."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"The two ventricles are in series, so they must eject the same volume per beat; the right side can't keep pumping twice the left's."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Doubling the pressure load is lasting extra work, and muscle under chronic load remodels; it does not stay the same."}],"rule":"Chronic pressure overload makes heart muscle grow (as in the left ventricle with hypertension); the two sides must pump equal volumes.","exp":"Muscle mass follows the pressure a chamber must generate. Normally the left ventricle pushes into the systemic circuit (~120 mmHg) and the right only into the lungs (~25 mmHg), so the left wall is thicker. **Doubling the pulmonary pressure doubles the right ventricle's pressure load, so its wall thickens** (right ventricular hypertrophy); both sides still eject the same stroke volume.","how":{"ask":"Which job sets a ventricle's wall thickness: the volume it moves or the pressure it must reach?","know":"• The two ventricles are in series and eject the same stroke volume (~70 mL).\n• Left: systemic circuit, high resistance, ~120 mmHg. Right: pulmonary circuit, low resistance, ~25 mmHg.\n• Heart muscle thickens when its pressure load stays high (hypertrophy).","steps":[{"text":"The right ventricle must now reach twice its usual pressure on every beat.","hl":"from about 25 to 50 mmHg"},{"text":"Normal comparison: the left wall is thicker because it pushes against the high-pressure systemic circuit, not because it moves more blood.","calc":["left: ~120 mmHg","right: ~25 mmHg → now ~50 mmHg"]},{"text":"Stroke volume can't double: the two sides are in series and must match.","strike":["Its stroke volume doubles"]},{"text":"A higher pressure load is more work for the muscle, so it neither thins nor stays the same.","strike":["Its wall thins","Its chamber stays unchanged"]},{"text":"Answer: its wall thickens."}],"trap":"Linking a thick wall to a bigger output: each ventricle ejects the same volume per beat; wall thickness tracks pressure."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"pulmonary arterial pressure","note":"the right ventricle's load"},{"t":"25 to 50 mmHg","note":"doubled"},{"t":"adapt over time","note":"remodeling"}]},{"text":"**Two pumps in series.** Blood leaving the right ventricle returns to the left, so both eject the same volume each beat."},{"text":"**Wall follows pressure.** The left wall is thicker because the systemic circuit needs ~120 mmHg; the lungs need only ~25 mmHg."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** ventricular wall thickness tracks the pressure the chamber must generate, not the volume it pumps; a raised pressure load thickens that wall."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-47a","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:47#1","pe6-cp:47#2"],"q":"A beam of ground-state nitrogen atoms (1s² 2s² 2p³) passes through a nonuniform magnetic field. What is each atom's total electron spin, and how does it behave?","choices":["7/2; paramagnetic","½; paramagnetic","3/2; diamagnetic","3/2; paramagnetic"],"ans":3,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"impossible","why":"7/2 needs all seven spins parallel, but Pauli forces the two electrons in each of 1s and 2s to pair with opposite spins."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"½ pairs two of the 2p electrons; Hund's rule puts one electron in each 2p orbital before any pairing."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Diamagnetic means every electron is paired; any unpaired electron makes an atom paramagnetic."}],"rule":"Count only unpaired electrons, ½ spin each; Hund's rule fills p orbitals one electron each first. Any unpaired electron → paramagnetic.","exp":"By the **Pauli principle** the 1s and 2s pairs have **opposite spins** and cancel. **Hund's rule** spreads the three 2p electrons over the three 2p orbitals, all spin-up, so the total spin is **3 × ½ = 3/2**. Unpaired electrons make nitrogen **paramagnetic**: drawn toward the stronger field.","how":{"ask":"Pair the electrons orbital by orbital, add the leftover spins, then classify the magnetism.","know":"• **Pauli exclusion**: two electrons in one orbital must have opposite spins (+½ and −½), so a full orbital contributes **0**.\n• **Hund's rule**: in a partly filled subshell, electrons occupy separate orbitals with parallel spins first.\n• **Unpaired electrons → paramagnetic** (attracted); **all paired → diamagnetic** (weakly repelled).","steps":[{"text":"Read the configuration.","hl":"1s² 2s² 2p³","calc":["1s: 2 electrons, 2s: 2 electrons","2p: 3 electrons in 3 orbitals"]},{"text":"Pair the full orbitals, then add the 2p spins.","calc":["1s: +½ − ½ = 0; 2s: +½ − ½ = 0","2p: +½ +½ +½ = 3/2","total S = **3/2**"]},{"text":"Unpaired electrons mean the atom is paramagnetic.","strike":["3/2; diamagnetic"]},{"text":"7/2 ignores Pauli pairing; ½ pairs two 2p electrons against Hund's rule.","strike":["7/2; paramagnetic","½; paramagnetic"]},{"text":"Answer: 3/2; paramagnetic."}],"trap":"Adding ½ for every electron: paired electrons in the same orbital always cancel."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"ground-state nitrogen atoms","note":"lowest-energy filling"},{"t":"1s² 2s² 2p³","note":"two full orbitals, a half-full p set"},{"t":"nonuniform magnetic field","note":"tests para- vs diamagnetism"}]},{"text":"**Pauli in one line.** No two electrons in an atom share all four quantum numbers, so two electrons in the same orbital must have opposite spins.","calc":["full orbital: +½ + (−½) = 0"]},{"text":"**Also:** an atom whose subshells are all filled, such as Be (1s² 2s²) or Ne, has total spin 0, so it is diamagnetic and is weakly pushed out of a strong field."},{"text":"**Also:** any unpaired electron (H, Li, N) leaves net spin, so the atom is paramagnetic and is drawn into the field."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** paired electrons cancel (Pauli); count only unpaired ones for the spin, and any unpaired electron means paramagnetic."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-48a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:48#1"],"q":"In a nucleotide pocket, a histidine ring stacks on adenine and donates an N–H hydrogen bond to it. Which side chain shown would best keep both contacts?","choices":["Tyrosine","Asparagine","Valine","Proline"],"choiceFigs":["tyrosine-sc","asparagine-sc","valine-sc","proline-sc"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Asparagine's amide N–H can donate, but its side chain has no ring to stack on adenine."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Valine's branched alkyl side chain offers no flat aromatic ring for stacking and no N–H or O–H donor."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Proline's ring is saturated, neither flat nor aromatic, and its N sits in the backbone with no H to give."}],"rule":"To both stack and H-bond, a side chain needs a flat aromatic ring plus an N–H or O–H donor.","exp":"Keep both features: a **flat aromatic ring** for π stacking and a **hydrogen-bond donor** (an N–H or O–H). **Tyrosine's phenol** supplies both. Asparagine donates but has no ring, valine has neither, and proline's ring is saturated with its N tied into the backbone.","how":{"ask":"Which side chain has both a flat aromatic ring and an H on N or O?","know":"• **π stacking** needs a flat aromatic ring (e.g. Phe, Tyr, His).\n• **H-bond donor** needs H on N or O (e.g. Ser, Thr, Tyr, Asn, Gln, His, Lys, Arg).\n• A **conservative substitution** keeps every feature the site uses: ring for ring, donor for donor.","steps":[{"text":"List what the pocket uses: stacking (a ring) plus one donated H-bond.","hl":"stacks on adenine"},{"text":"Valine: alkyl, no ring, no donor. Asparagine: amide N–H donor but no ring.","strike":["Valine","Asparagine"]},{"text":"Proline: its ring is saturated, neither flat nor aromatic, and its N sits in the backbone with no H to give.","strike":["Proline"]},{"text":"Tyrosine: its flat phenol ring stacks and its O–H donates.","calc":["ring ✓ + donor ✓ → both contacts kept"]},{"text":"Answer: tyrosine."}],"trap":"Picking proline because it has a ring; stacking needs a flat aromatic π system, which a saturated ring lacks."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"stacks on adenine","note":"needs a flat aromatic ring"},{"t":"donates an N–H hydrogen bond","note":"needs N–H or O–H"},{"t":"both contacts","note":"keep both"}]},{"text":"**List the jobs.** Stacking = flat aromatic ring. Donating = H on N or O."},{"text":"**Score each candidate**, one point per job; only a two-point side chain keeps binding.","calc":["Tyr 2, Asn 1, Pro 0, Val 0"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a conservative substitution keeps every feature the site uses: ring for ring, donor for donor, charge for charge."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-49a","s":"Amino Acids & Proteins","disc":"biochemistry","aamc":"5D","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:49#1"],"q":"An engineered enzyme has most of its surface serines and threonines replaced by valines and leucines. In water, the variant would most likely:","choices":["clump together in solution.","form extra disulfide bonds.","lose its catalytic activity.","bind more water molecules."],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"backwards","why":"Val and Leu cannot H-bond, so the variant holds less water, not more."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Disulfides need two cysteine –SH groups; valine and leucine contain no sulfur."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Residues scattered over the surface are not the active site; catalysis comes from a few positioned groups in a pocket."}],"rule":"Swapping polar surface residues for nonpolar ones makes the surface hydrophobic; molecules then hide those patches against each other.","exp":"Surface Ser and Thr **–OH groups hydrogen-bond with water**, building the hydration shell around the protein. Swapping them for **hydrophobic** Val and Leu removes those H-bonds and exposes oily patches, which stick to one another: the variant **aggregates**.","how":{"ask":"What do surface –OH groups do with water, and what happens when oily side chains replace them?","know":"• Ser and Thr carry polar, uncharged –OH groups at pH 7: they donate and accept H-bonds with water.\n• Those H-bonds build a **hydration (solvation) shell** around the protein.\n• Exposed hydrophobic side chains (Val, Leu, Ile, Phe) pull together to avoid water → aggregation.","steps":[{"text":"Note the swap: polar –OH side chains out, oily alkyl side chains in, all on the surface.","hl":"replaced by valines and leucines"},{"text":"No sulfur enters, so no new S–S bridges; and surface residues are not the catalytic pocket.","strike":["form extra disulfide bonds.","lose its catalytic activity."]},{"text":"Val and Leu cannot H-bond, so the variant holds less water, not more.","strike":["bind more water molecules."]},{"text":"Oily patches on neighboring molecules stick together (the hydrophobic effect).","calc":["fewer –OH···H₂O bonds → thinner water shell → molecules clump"]},{"text":"Answer: it clumps together in solution."}],"trap":"Assuming any big surface change must hit catalysis; the active site is a small pocket, while surface polarity governs how the protein mixes with water."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"surface serines and threonines","note":"polar –OH groups"},{"t":"valines and leucines","note":"oily, no H-bonding"}]},{"text":"**What the –OH does.** Each Ser or Thr –OH hydrogen-bonds with water molecules, adding to the protein's hydration shell."},{"text":"**Charge check.** Ser and Thr are uncharged at pH 7 (their –OH pKa is about 16), so the swap changes polarity, not charge."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** polar surface groups (–OH, amides, charges) hold the water shell that keeps proteins dispersed; exposed hydrophobic groups make them aggregate."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-50a","s":"Acids & Bases","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5A","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:50#2"],"q":"Each salt is dissolved in water at 0.20 M and 25 °C. Which solution is slightly acidic?","choices":["Na₂CO₃","NH₄NO₃","KNO₃","CH₃COONa"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"Carbonate is the conjugate base of a weak acid (HCO₃⁻), so it pulls H⁺ from water and makes the solution basic."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"K⁺ comes from the strong base KOH and NO₃⁻ from the strong acid HNO₃: neither ion reacts with water, so it stays neutral."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"Acetate is the conjugate base of the weak acetic acid, so it takes H⁺ from water and makes the solution basic."}],"rule":"Salt pH: ions from strong acids and bases are spectators; the conjugate acid of a weak base makes acid, the conjugate base of a weak acid makes base.","exp":"**NH₄⁺** is the conjugate acid of the weak base NH₃, so it hands H⁺ to water molecules (NH₄⁺ + H₂O ⇌ NH₃ + H₃O⁺) and the solution is **slightly acidic**; NO₃⁻ is a spectator. Carbonate and acetate are conjugate bases of weak acids (basic), and KNO₃ is neutral.","how":{"ask":"Classify each ion: from a strong acid or base (spectator), or the conjugate of a weak one (reacts with water).","know":"• **Conjugate acid of a weak base** (NH₄⁺) → acidic solution.\n• **Conjugate base of a weak acid** (CH₃COO⁻, CO₃²⁻) → basic.\n• Ions of strong acids and bases (Na⁺, K⁺, Cl⁻, NO₃⁻) are **neutral spectators**.","steps":[{"text":"Split each salt into its ions and tag them.","calc":["NH₄⁺: weak acid | NO₃⁻: spectator","K⁺, NO₃⁻: spectators","CO₃²⁻, CH₃COO⁻: weak bases"]},{"text":"Ammonium gives a proton to water, raising [H₃O⁺].","hl":"slightly acidic","calc":["NH₄⁺ + H₂O ⇌ NH₃ + H₃O⁺"]},{"text":"Carbonate and acetate make basic solutions; potassium nitrate is neutral.","strike":["Na₂CO₃","CH₃COONa","KNO₃"]},{"text":"Answer: NH₄NO₃."}],"trap":"Expecting the nitrate (from nitric acid) to make the solution acidic: an anion of a strong acid has no acidity left."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"slightly acidic","note":"a weak acid ion"},{"t":"0.20 M","note":"same for all"},{"t":"Each salt","note":"judge it by its ions"}]},{"text":"**Ask where each ion came from.** Partner of a strong acid or base: does nothing. Partner of a weak acid or base: reacts with water.","calc":["weak base's partner (NH₄⁺) → H₃O⁺","weak acid's partner (CH₃COO⁻) → OH⁻"]},{"text":"**Also:** the ammonium ion is a weak acid (Ka = Kw ÷ Kb(NH₃) ≈ 5.6 × 10⁻¹⁰), so any ammonium salt of a strong acid gives a slightly acidic solution."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** a weak base's conjugate acid (NH₄⁺) makes a salt solution acidic, a weak acid's conjugate base makes it basic, and strong-acid/strong-base ions are neutral."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-54a","s":"Thermodynamics & Kinetics","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5E","skill":"SIRS 4","from":["pe6-cp:54#2"],"q":"Compound R's phase diagram is shown. Its gas, held at a constant −25 °C, is compressed from 1 to 9 atm. Which changes occur, in order?","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Phase diagram of compound R (sketch)","x":{"label":"Temperature (°C)","min":-90,"max":50,"step":20},"y":{"label":"Pressure (atm)","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"series":[{"name":"Solid–gas","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[-90,0.3],[-70,0.7],[-50,1.2],[-30,2]]},{"name":"Solid–liquid","marker":"square","line":"straight","points":[[-30,2],[-25,6],[-20,10]]},{"name":"Liquid–gas","marker":"triangle","line":"straight","points":[[-30,2],[-10,3],[10,4.5],[30,6.5],[50,9]]}]},"choices":["Condensation, then freezing","Deposition only","Freezing, then condensation","Condensation only"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"A gas must condense into liquid before that liquid can freeze; going up in pressure meets the liquid–gas line first."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"That stops at the first line; the solid–liquid line sits at 6 atm, below 9 atm, so the path crosses it too."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"Deposition needs the solid–gas line, which lies only left of the triple point (−30 °C); a path at −25 °C never meets it."}],"rule":"Trace the path on the diagram: move straight along it at that temperature and name each boundary line you cross, in order.","exp":"Constant temperature = a **vertical** path at −25 °C. Going up, it first crosses the **liquid–gas line (≈ 2.3 atm): condensation**, then the **solid–liquid line (≈ 6 atm): freezing**, ending as a solid at 9 atm. It never touches the solid–gas line, which lies left of the triple point (−30 °C, 2 atm).","how":{"ask":"Draw the path at −25 °C from 1 to 9 atm: which lines does it cross, in order?","know":"• On a phase diagram, **pressure is up, temperature is across**; constant T = a vertical path, constant P = a horizontal path.\n• Each boundary crossed is one phase change: liquid–gas = vaporization/condensation; solid–liquid = melting/freezing; solid–gas = sublimation/deposition.\n• Name the change from the region you leave to the region you enter.","steps":[{"text":"Place the start: −25 °C and 1 atm lies in the gas region (low pressure, right of the triple point).","hl":"constant −25 °C"},{"text":"Move straight up. First boundary: liquid–gas, near 2.3 atm, so gas becomes liquid.","calc":["liquid–gas line at −25 °C: 2 + (5/20) × 1 ≈ 2.3 atm","gas → liquid = condensation"],"strike":["Deposition only"]},{"text":"Keep going: the solid–liquid line sits at 6 atm, below 9 atm, so the liquid becomes solid.","calc":["6 atm < 9 atm → freezing"],"strike":["Condensation only","Freezing, then condensation"]},{"text":"Answer: condensation, then freezing."}],"trap":"Choosing deposition because the path starts as gas and ends as solid; at −25 °C, warmer than the triple point, a liquid region lies between them."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"constant −25 °C","note":"a vertical path"},{"t":"compressed from 1 to 9 atm","note":"move up"},{"t":"phase diagram","note":"read regions and lines"}]},{"text":"**Read the map:** solid at upper left, liquid in the wedge, gas at lower right; the three lines meet at the triple point (−30 °C, 2 atm).","chart":{"type":"line","caption":"Phase diagram of compound R (sketch)","x":{"label":"Temperature (°C)","min":-90,"max":50,"step":20},"y":{"label":"Pressure (atm)","min":0,"max":10,"step":2},"series":[{"name":"Solid–gas","marker":"circle","line":"straight","points":[[-90,0.3],[-70,0.7],[-50,1.2],[-30,2]]},{"name":"Solid–liquid","marker":"square","line":"straight","points":[[-30,2],[-25,6],[-20,10]]},{"name":"Liquid–gas","marker":"triangle","line":"straight","points":[[-30,2],[-10,3],[10,4.5],[30,6.5],[50,9]]}]}},{"text":"**Draw the path and list each line it crosses**, in order, naming the region it leaves and the one it enters.","calc":["gas → liquid (condensation) → solid (freezing)"]},{"text":"**Also:** a path held below the triple-point pressure, such as warming at 1 atm from −80 °C, crosses only the solid–gas line: sublimation, with no liquid."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** follow the path (vertical for constant T, horizontal for constant P) and name each boundary it crosses in order; sublimation or deposition happens only below the triple-point pressure."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-56a","s":"Stoichiometry & Solutions","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 1","from":["pe6-cp:56#1","pe6-cp:56#2"],"q":"Baker's ammonia decomposes completely when heated: NH₄HCO₃(s) → NH₃(g) + CO₂(g) + H₂O(g). Water vapor makes up what mole fraction of the gas produced?","choices":["0.25","0.33","0.67","1.0"],"ans":1,"elim":[{"c":2,"tag":"backwards","why":"That is the share of the other two gases, the complement, not water's share."},{"c":3,"tag":"skipped","why":"A fraction of 1.0 would mean water is the only gas; the ammonia and carbon dioxide must go in the total too."},{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"That counts the solid in the total (1 of 4); only gases belong in a gas-phase mole fraction."}],"rule":"Mole fraction = moles of that gas ÷ moles of all gases; count gas coefficients only, never solids or liquids.","exp":"Count gas moles from the coefficients: **1 NH₃ + 1 CO₂ + 1 H₂O = 3 mol**; the solid is not part of the gas. **x(H₂O) = 1 ÷ 3 ≈ 0.33**.","how":{"ask":"Divide water's coefficient by the sum of the gas coefficients only.","know":"• **Mole fraction x_i = n_i ÷ n_total**, always between 0 and 1.\n• For a complete reaction, coefficients give mole ratios.\n• **Count only (g) species**: solids and liquids are not in the gas mixture.","steps":[{"text":"Pick out the gaseous products.","hl":"NH₃(g) + CO₂(g) + H₂O(g)","calc":["gas moles = 1 + 1 + 1 = 3"]},{"text":"Divide the water's share by the total.","calc":["x(H₂O) = 1 ÷ 3 ≈ **0.33**"]},{"text":"0.25 puts the solid in the total, 0.67 is the other two gases, and 1.0 forgets them entirely.","strike":["0.25","0.67","1.0"]},{"text":"Answer: 0.33."}],"trap":"Adding the solid reactant's coefficient to the total: a gas mole fraction counts gases only."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"NH₄HCO₃(s)","note":"solid: not in the gas"},{"t":"H₂O(g)","note":"1 mol of the gas asked"},{"t":"mole fraction","note":"part ÷ whole"}]},{"text":"**Coefficients are moles.** Per mole of solid that decomposes, the products are 1 mol each of NH₃, CO₂ and H₂O.","calc":["n(gas) = 1 + 1 + 1 = 3 mol"]},{"text":"**Part over whole.**","calc":["x(H₂O) = 1/3 ≈ 0.33","x(NH₃) + x(CO₂) = 2/3; all three sum to 1"]},{"text":"**Also:** leave every (s) and (l) out of the total; the phase labels decide what counts in a gas mixture."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** mole fraction of a gaseous product = its coefficient ÷ the sum of the gas coefficients, ignoring solids and liquids."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-58a","s":"Atomic & Nuclear Physics","disc":"physics","aamc":"4E","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:58#1"],"q":"Radon-222 (Z = 86), a gas that seeps into basements, is an alpha emitter. Given Po (Z = 84), At (Z = 85) and Ra (Z = 88), what is the daughter nucleus?","choices":["Astatine-218","Radium-226","Polonium-218","Polonium-220"],"ans":2,"elim":[{"c":1,"tag":"backwards","why":"Radium adds the alpha particle instead of removing it; emission makes the nucleus lighter."},{"c":0,"tag":"fact","why":"An alpha particle carries 2 protons, so Z drops by 2 (to 84), not by 1."},{"c":3,"tag":"fact","why":"An alpha particle has mass number 4, so A drops by 4; that subtracts only 2."}],"rule":"Alpha emission: A drops by 4 and Z drops by 2; then match the new Z to the element's name.","exp":"An **alpha particle is ⁴₂He**, so **A falls by 4 and Z by 2**: A = 222 − 4 = 218, Z = 86 − 2 = 84 = **polonium**. ²²²Rn → **²¹⁸Po** + ⁴He.","how":{"ask":"Subtract 4 from A and 2 from Z, then name the element from the new Z.","know":"• **Alpha decay** emits ⁴₂He: **A − 4, Z − 2**.\n• Z alone fixes the element's identity.\n• Check: mass numbers and charges balance on both sides.","steps":[{"text":"Read the parent.","hl":"Radon-222 (Z = 86)","calc":["A = 222, Z = 86"]},{"text":"Remove a helium-4 nucleus.","calc":["A: 222 − 4 = 218","Z: 86 − 2 = 84 → Po"]},{"text":"Balance check.","calc":["222 = 218 + 4 ✓; 86 = 84 + 2 ✓"]},{"text":"Astatine drops Z by only 1, radium adds the alpha, and polonium-220 subtracts 2 from A instead of 4.","strike":["Astatine-218","Radium-226","Polonium-220"]},{"text":"Answer: polonium-218."}],"trap":"Subtracting 2 from the mass number too: the alpha carries 2 protons but 4 nucleons."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find every number** and what it means.","marks":[{"t":"Radon-222","note":"A = 222"},{"t":"Z = 86","note":"protons"},{"t":"alpha emitter","note":"loses ⁴₂He"}]},{"text":"**What leaves.** An alpha particle is two protons and two neutrons.","calc":["ΔA = −4, ΔZ = −2"]},{"text":"**Name the daughter by Z.** The new proton count picks the element from the list.","calc":["Z = 84 → polonium; A = 218"]},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** alpha decay lowers A by 4 and Z by 2; the new Z names the daughter."}]},
  {"id":"cpt-pe6-59a","s":"Molecular Structure & Intermolecular Forces","disc":"gen_chem","aamc":"5B","skill":"SIRS 2","from":["pe6-cp:59#1","pe6-cp:59#2"],"q":"The nitrosonium ion, NO⁺, is drawn so that both atoms have a full octet. Which atom carries the positive formal charge?","choices":["Oxygen","Nitrogen","Both, half each","Neither atom"],"ans":0,"elim":[{"c":3,"tag":"impossible","why":"Formal charges must add up to the ion's overall charge (+); at least one atom has to carry a nonzero formal charge."},{"c":1,"tag":"fact","why":"With a triple bond and one lone pair, N owns 2 + 3 = 5 electrons, its own valence count, so its formal charge is 0."},{"c":2,"tag":"fact","why":"Formal charge counts whole electrons, so each atom gets an integer; no atom can carry +½."}],"rule":"FC = valence − lone-pair e⁻ − ½ bonding e⁻; they are whole numbers summing to the ion's charge. The + can sit on the more electronegative atom.","exp":"NO⁺ has 5 + 6 − 1 = **10 valence electrons**. Octets on both atoms need a **triple bond plus a lone pair on N and on O**. Formal charge = valence − nonbonding − ½ bonding: N = 5 − 2 − 3 = **0**; O = 6 − 2 − 3 = **+1**. The plus sits on oxygen, even though O is the more electronegative atom.","how":{"ask":"Count the valence electrons, draw the octet structure, then compute each atom's formal charge.","know":"• Valence electrons: add the atoms' group numbers, subtract one per + charge (add one per − charge).\n• Two atoms sharing **10 valence electrons** both reach octets only with a **triple bond** and one lone pair each.\n• **Formal charge = valence e⁻ − nonbonding e⁻ − ½(bonding e⁻)**; the formal charges add up to the overall charge.","steps":[{"text":"Count valence electrons, removing one for the + charge.","hl":"NO⁺","calc":["N 5 + O 6 − 1 = **10** e⁻"]},{"text":"Find the bond order that gives both atoms octets with 10 electrons.","hl":"have a full octet","calc":["single bond: 2 + 12 lone = 14 needed ✗","double bond: 4 + 8 lone = 12 needed ✗","triple bond: 6 + 4 lone = **10** ✓ → bond order 3"]},{"text":"Compute each formal charge. Nitrogen ends at 0; the formal charges must sum to the ion's +1, so they cannot both be 0, and formal charges are whole numbers, never halves.","calc":["FC(N) = 5 − 2 − 6/2 = **0**","FC(O) = 6 − 2 − 6/2 = **+1**","sum = +1 = ion charge ✓"],"strike":["Nitrogen","Neither atom","Both, half each"]},{"text":"Answer: oxygen."}],"trap":"Putting the + on nitrogen because it is less electronegative; that comes from a double-bond drawing in which nitrogen has only 6 electrons, which breaks the octet condition."},"teach":[{"text":"First, **find the key words**.","marks":[{"t":"NO⁺","note":"one electron removed"},{"t":"full octet","note":"8 e⁻ around each atom"},{"t":"positive formal charge","note":"the atom that owns fewer e⁻"}]},{"text":"**Count valence electrons**, then subtract one for the + charge.","calc":["5 + 6 − 1 = 10"]},{"text":"**Add bonds until both atoms have octets.** Shared electrons count for both atoms, so each extra bond saves 2 electrons.","calc":["two octets alone = 16 e⁻; each shared pair saves 2","16 − 10 = 6 shared e⁻ → triple bond"]},{"text":"**Compute formal charges** with the bookkeeping formula.","calc":["FC = valence − nonbonding − ½ bonding","N: 5 − 2 − 3 = 0","O: 6 − 2 − 3 = +1"]},{"text":"**Also:** every two-atom species with 10 valence electrons (N₂, CN⁻ and NO⁺) has this same octet structure: a triple bond and one lone pair per atom, bond order 3."},{"text":"**Also:** a neutral molecule can still have charged atoms whose formal charges cancel. In nitromethane, CH₃–NO₂, N has 5 − 0 − 4 = +1 and the single-bonded O has 6 − 6 − 1 = −1; they sum to 0."},{"text":"**Rule to reuse:** count valence e⁻ (adjusting for charge), add bonds until every atom has an octet, then FC = valence − nonbonding − ½ bonding for each atom."}]},
];
